window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Upper Urinary Development & Early Embryogenesis","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the three sequentially formed embryonic kidney systems is nonfunctional and undergoes complete regression by the end of the fourth week?","choices":{"A":"The mesonephros","B":"The metanephros","C":"The ureteric bud","D":"The pronephros","E":"The nephrogenic cord"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pronephros is represented by 7 to 10 solid cervical cell groups (nephrotomes) that form vestigial excretory units and regress before more caudal systems appear; all traces disappear by the end of the fourth week. The mesonephros forms functional excretory tubules that persist transiently into the fetal period. The metanephros becomes the permanent kidney. The ureteric bud is a duct outgrowth, not a kidney system, and the nephrogenic cord is the unsegmented intermediate mesoderm from which the mesonephros and metanephros arise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A classic trap is assuming the pronephros contributes tissue to the permanent kidney — it does not, it is entirely vestigial. COMLEX: Recognize the pronephros as a purely embryologic landmark with no adult clinical correlate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — sets up SDL 4 Obj 2's nephron/tubule segment histology, which begins once the metanephros has replaced the earlier kidney systems"},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic kidney system produces functional excretory tubules with glomeruli and Bowman capsules during the early fetal period, with a few caudal tubules and its duct later persisting in the male genital system?","choices":{"A":"The pronephros","B":"The metanephros","C":"The ureteric bud","D":"The nephrogenic cord","E":"The mesonephros"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The mesonephros forms S-shaped tubules with a glomerular tuft and Bowman capsule, functioning transiently before most of it degenerates by 8 weeks; in males, caudal tubules and the mesonephric (wolffian) duct persist to help form the genital duct system, while both disappear in females. The pronephros never becomes functional. The metanephros is the permanent kidney, and the ureteric bud and nephrogenic cord are not themselves excretory systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The mesonephric duct's persistence in males (forming the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicle) versus regression in females is a frequently tested sex-difference fact. COMLEX: Relevant when correlating congenital absence of the vas deferens with mesonephric duct anomalies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — sets up SDL 4 Obj 2's nephron/tubule segment histology, which begins once the metanephros has replaced the earlier kidney systems"},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the three embryonic kidney systems forms the permanent kidney and begins producing urine into the amniotic cavity around the twelfth week?","choices":{"A":"The pronephros","B":"The metanephros","C":"The mesonephros","D":"The ureteric bud","E":"The paramesonephric duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The metanephros, appearing in the fifth week, forms the permanent kidney; its glomerular capillaries begin differentiating by the tenth week and urine production starts around the twelfth week. The pronephros regresses without ever functioning, and the mesonephros functions only transiently before degenerating. The ureteric bud is the duct outgrowth that forms the collecting system, and the paramesonephric (mullerian) duct belongs to the genital rather than urinary system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fetal urine is the major contributor to amniotic fluid after mid-gestation — this is why bilateral renal agenesis or severe outflow obstruction causes oligohydramnios. COMLEX: Correlate absent fetal bladder filling on ultrasound with a metanephric or outflow-tract defect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — sets up SDL 4 Obj 2's nephron/tubule segment histology, which begins once the metanephros has replaced the earlier kidney systems"},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":1,"stem":"Which transcription factor, expressed by the metanephric mesenchyme, makes that tissue competent to respond to inductive signals from the ureteric bud?","choices":{"A":"WT1","B":"PAX2","C":"WNT4","D":"RET","E":"GDNF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"WT1 is the transcription factor expressed by the metanephric mesenchyme that confers competence to respond to ureteric bud induction; it also regulates the mesenchyme's own production of GDNF and HGF. PAX2 and WNT4 act downstream, driving epithelialization and tubule formation after WNT9B/WNT6 induction. RET is the receptor for GDNF expressed by the ureteric bud epithelium, not the mesenchyme, and GDNF is the ligand the mesenchyme secretes rather than the competence factor itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: WT1 mutations cause Wilms tumor and are the molecular basis of WAGR and Denys-Drash syndromes — a very high-yield gene-disease link. COMLEX: WT1's role in podocyte differentiation also connects it to congenital nephrotic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — WT1's mesenchymal induction role reappears in SDL 39 Obj 3's Wilms tumor genetics and SDL 31 Obj 1's podocyte slit-diaphragm biology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":1,"stem":"The metanephric mesenchyme secretes glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor, which binds which receptor tyrosine kinase on the ureteric bud epithelium to stimulate its growth and branching?","choices":{"A":"MET","B":"RET","C":"FGFR2","D":"BMPR1A","E":"FZD"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RET is the receptor tyrosine kinase for GDNF, expressed by the ureteric bud epithelium, and its activation drives bud growth and branching into the metanephric mesenchyme. MET is the receptor for HGF (a related but separate mesenchymal signal), FGFR2 and BMPR1A respond to other FGF and BMP signals in kidney development rather than to GDNF, and FZD (Frizzled) is the receptor family for the WNT9B/WNT6 signal, not for GDNF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease and MEN2, distinct from its role here in kidney induction — do not conflate the two RET-related conditions. COMLEX: GDNF-RET signaling failure is a mechanistic basis for renal hypoplasia/agenesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — WT1's mesenchymal induction role reappears in SDL 39 Obj 3's Wilms tumor genetics and SDL 31 Obj 1's podocyte slit-diaphragm biology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":1,"stem":"Ureteric bud-derived WNT9B and WNT6 upregulate both PAX2 and WNT4 in the metanephric mesenchyme. Which of these two induced genes causes the condensed mesenchyme to epithelialize and form renal tubules?","choices":{"A":"PAX2","B":"WT1","C":"WNT4","D":"RET","E":"GDNF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WNT4 causes the condensed metanephric mesenchyme to epithelialize and form the S-shaped tubules of the nephron. PAX2 acts just upstream, promoting condensation of the mesenchyme in preparation for this epithelialization but not driving the epithelial conversion itself. WT1 confers initial competence to the mesenchyme, and RET and GDNF are the receptor-ligand pair on the ureteric bud side of the induction, not downstream mesenchymal effectors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sequencing PAX2 (condensation) before WNT4 (epithelialization) is a common source of confusion — remember condensation must precede epithelial conversion. COMLEX: PAX2 mutations cause renal coloboma syndrome, pairing an eye finding with renal hypoplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — WT1's mesenchymal induction role reappears in SDL 39 Obj 3's Wilms tumor genetics and SDL 31 Obj 1's podocyte slit-diaphragm biology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":1,"stem":"Bilateral renal agenesis produces a characteristic fetal pattern of anuria, oligohydramnios, and pulmonary hypoplasia. What is this constellation of findings called?","choices":{"A":"Potter sequence","B":"WAGR syndrome","C":"Denys-Drash syndrome","D":"Meckel-Gruber syndrome","E":"Bardet-Biedl syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Potter sequence describes the downstream cascade from bilateral renal agenesis: absent urine production causes oligohydramnios, which mechanically compresses the fetus to produce pulmonary hypoplasia, flattened facies, and limb deformities. WAGR and Denys-Drash are WT1-related tumor syndromes, while Meckel-Gruber and Bardet-Biedl are ciliopathies that cause renal cysts rather than agenesis; none of the four describes the agenesis-to-oligohydramnios cascade itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Potter sequence\" is a sequence (one initiating defect causing a cascade), not a syndrome (multiple primary malformations) — a distinction commonly tested. COMLEX: Recognize oligohydramnios on prenatal ultrasound as the key trigger to evaluate fetal renal anatomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 3 — bilateral agenesis's high co-occurring anomaly rate previews SDL 3 Obj 2's duct-system disruptions, which share the same intermediate mesoderm origin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":1,"stem":"In bilateral renal agenesis, mechanical compression of the fetus by oligohydramnios produces a flattened facial appearance together with which limb finding?","choices":{"A":"Polydactyly of the digits","B":"Syndactyly of the digits","C":"Radial ray limb deficiency","D":"Talipes equinovarus","E":"Overlapping flexed fingers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) results directly from the physical compression of decreased amniotic fluid volume restricting normal fetal limb positioning, alongside the classic Potter facies. Polydactyly, syndactyly, and radial ray deficiency are primary limb-bud patterning defects, and overlapping flexed fingers is a posture seen in trisomy 18 rather than a product of oligohydramnios-related compression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clubfoot plus Potter facies on a stillbirth exam should immediately raise suspicion for bilateral renal agenesis. COMLEX: A useful physical exam correlation when counseling about a prenatal oligohydramnios finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 3 — bilateral agenesis's high co-occurring anomaly rate previews SDL 3 Obj 2's duct-system disruptions, which share the same intermediate mesoderm origin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":1,"stem":"In the majority of bilateral renal agenesis cases, additional anomalies of the vagina, uterus, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles are also present because these structures share which embryologic origin with the kidneys?","choices":{"A":"Lateral plate mesoderm","B":"Paraxial mesoderm","C":"Neural crest mesenchyme","D":"Extraembryonic mesoderm","E":"Intermediate mesoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The urinary and genital systems both arise from intermediate mesoderm, so a severe insult that prevents metanephric development can also disrupt the adjacent mesonephric and paramesonephric duct derivatives, explaining the high rate of co-occurring genital anomalies. Lateral plate mesoderm forms body wall and visceral structures, paraxial mesoderm forms somites and axial skeleton/musculature, neural crest forms structures like facial cartilage and peripheral neurons, and extraembryonic mesoderm contributes to placental and membrane structures, none of which give rise to the urogenital ridge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Urinary and genital systems share intermediate mesoderm\" is a foundational embryology fact tying multiple anomaly syndromes together. COMLEX: Explains why a patient with unilateral renal agenesis should also be screened for ipsilateral genital tract anomalies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 3 — bilateral agenesis's high co-occurring anomaly rate previews SDL 3 Obj 2's duct-system disruptions, which share the same intermediate mesoderm origin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anatomic structure prevents further ascent of a horseshoe kidney, fixing it at the level of the lower lumbar vertebrae?","choices":{"A":"The inferior mesenteric artery root","B":"The superior mesenteric artery root","C":"The celiac trunk origin at the aorta","D":"The common iliac artery bifurcation","E":"The aortic bifurcation itself"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The root of the inferior mesenteric artery lies at the level the fused kidney isthmus would need to cross during ascent, and it mechanically blocks further cranial migration, fixing the horseshoe kidney in the lower lumbar region. The superior mesenteric artery and celiac trunk originate higher on the aorta and are not the blocking structure, and the common iliac and aortic bifurcations lie caudal to where the isthmus actually becomes trapped.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This is a classic image-based question pairing a horseshoe kidney with the inferior mesenteric artery on cross-sectional imaging. COMLEX: Useful for interpreting abdominal CT/ultrasound findings of a low-lying fused kidney.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — abnormal kidney position and vasculature set up SDL 38 Obj 2's obstruction/hydronephrosis risk in horseshoe and ectopic kidneys","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"Fusion of the lower poles of the developing kidneys as they pass through the arterial fork of the umbilical arteries produces which anomaly?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic kidney","B":"Horseshoe kidney","C":"Duplicated ureter","D":"Renal ectopia","E":"Crossed fused ectopia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When the ascending kidneys are pushed close together while passing through the arterial fork of the umbilical arteries, their lower poles can fuse, producing a horseshoe kidney. A pelvic kidney results from a kidney simply failing to ascend at all rather than fusing with its counterpart, duplicated ureter results from early splitting of a single ureteric bud, and renal ectopia and crossed fused ectopia describe kidneys in an abnormal location rather than this specific lower-pole fusion event.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Horseshoe kidney occurs in about 1 in 600 people and is one of the most common renal fusion anomalies tested. COMLEX: Associated with increased risk of hydronephrosis and stone formation from impaired drainage across the isthmus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — abnormal kidney position and vasculature set up SDL 38 Obj 2's obstruction/hydronephrosis risk in horseshoe and ectopic kidneys","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"A kidney that fails to ascend through the arterial fork of the umbilical arteries and remains near the common iliac artery is termed what?","choices":{"A":"A horseshoe kidney","B":"A crossed ectopic kidney","C":"A pelvic kidney","D":"A duplex kidney","E":"An intrathoracic kidney"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pelvic kidney is a kidney that fails to ascend at all and remains in the pelvis close to the common iliac artery, retaining a pelvic branch of the aorta as its blood supply. A horseshoe kidney instead results from fusion of both kidneys' lower poles during ascent, a crossed ectopic kidney has migrated to the contralateral side, a duplex kidney has a duplicated collecting system rather than an abnormal final position, and an intrathoracic kidney has ascended too far rather than too little.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pelvic kidney can be mistaken for a pelvic mass on physical exam or imaging — know the vascular supply clue (a persistent pelvic aortic branch). COMLEX: Relevant to differentiating a pelvic kidney from ovarian or bowel masses in a pelvic exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — abnormal kidney position and vasculature set up SDL 38 Obj 2's obstruction/hydronephrosis risk in horseshoe and ectopic kidneys","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":2,"stem":"A 20-week fetal ultrasound reveals normally positioned, functioning kidneys on both sides with adequate amniotic fluid volume. At this gestational age, the absence of any functioning cervical or thoracolumbar nephric system reflects which expected developmental event?","choices":{"A":"Sequential regression of two transient excretory precursors","B":"Failure of ureteric bud induction into the metanephric mesenchyme","C":"Arrest of epithelial-mesenchymal signaling between the bud and mesenchyme","D":"Persistent fusion of the bilateral nephrogenic cords in the midline","E":"Incomplete caudal migration of the intermediate mesoderm layer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By 20 weeks, the pronephros and mesonephros have already regressed on schedule after each was superseded by the next kidney system, leaving only the functioning metanephros — an expected developmental milestone rather than a pathologic finding. Failed ureteric bud induction or arrested epithelial-mesenchymal signaling would instead produce renal agenesis or dysplasia, midline nephrogenic cord fusion is not a recognized developmental event, and incomplete caudal migration of intermediate mesoderm would affect kidney position, not the disappearance of earlier kidney systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognize that regression of the pronephros and mesonephros is the expected, not pathologic, developmental course once the metanephros has taken over. COMLEX: Reassures that the absence of the earlier nephric systems on a routine anatomy scan is expected and not a sign of disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — segment-specific nephron and collecting duct histology, once the metanephros has replaced the earlier kidney systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":2,"stem":"A stillborn fetus has complete bilateral absence of kidney tissue, with a preserved bladder and external genitalia. Which tissue, normally induced by the ureteric bud, failed to form nephrons in this case?","choices":{"A":"The pronephric nephrotomes","B":"The mesonephric tubules","C":"The ureteric bud epithelium","D":"The cloacal endoderm","E":"The metanephric mesenchyme"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the excretory units (nephrons) of the permanent kidney derive from the metanephric mesenchyme once it is induced by the ureteric bud, complete bilateral absence of kidney tissue with a preserved bladder points to failure of this mesenchymal induction rather than a bladder or cloacal defect. The pronephric nephrotomes and mesonephric tubules are earlier, already-regressed systems unrelated to the permanent kidney, and an intact bladder argues against a primary cloacal endoderm defect, while the ureteric bud itself forms only the collecting system, not the nephrons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A preserved bladder with absent kidneys localizes the defect to the metanephric mesenchyme/ureteric bud interaction rather than to bladder or cloacal development. COMLEX: This distinction guides which structures to examine on postmortem or prenatal imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — WT1 and other genetic drivers of pediatric nephroblastoma, arising from this same metanephric mesenchyme lineage","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the temporal and spatial progression of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros, and predict the systemic and phenotypic consequences of developmental failure at each stage. Compares pronephros (nonfunctional, regresses), mesonephros (transiently excretory), and metanephros (permanent kidney). Focuses on systemic consequences of early developmental arrest.","batch":2,"stem":"Histologic sections of a 5-week-old embryo show a large ovoid organ on each side of the midline, containing S-shaped tubules with glomeruli and Bowman capsules, forming a ridge together with the adjacent gonad. Which structure is being examined?","choices":{"A":"The metanephros","B":"The mesonephros","C":"The pronephros","D":"The gonadal ridge in isolation","E":"The paramesonephric duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At 5 to 6 weeks, the mesonephros forms a large ovoid organ containing S-shaped excretory tubules with glomeruli and Bowman capsules, and together with the adjacent gonad it forms the urogenital ridge. The metanephros appears around this time but has not yet formed mature S-shaped tubules with glomeruli, the pronephros has already regressed by this stage, the gonadal ridge alone would not contain excretory tubules, and the paramesonephric duct is a genital structure without glomeruli or Bowman capsules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The urogenital ridge (mesonephros plus gonad) is a key histology landmark for identifying 5- to 6-week embryonic sections. COMLEX: Useful for correlating embryology images with adult retroperitoneal anatomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the renal corpuscle and glomerular filtration barrier structure that this mesonephric tubule/glomerulus arrangement prefigures","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy presents with a large abdominal mass, aniridia, and intellectual disability. Genetic testing shows a microdeletion at chromosome 11p13 spanning two adjacent genes. In addition to WT1, which second gene is deleted in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"PAX2","B":"EYA1","C":"PAX6","D":"SALL1","E":"WNT4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous 11p13 microdeletion spanning both WT1, causing the Wilms tumor, and the neighboring PAX6 gene, causing the aniridia; the two genes are only about 700 kb apart. PAX2 mutations instead cause renal coloboma syndrome, EYA1 mutations cause branchiootorenal syndrome, SALL1 mutations cause Townes-Brocks syndrome, and WNT4 acts downstream in tubule epithelialization — none of these is the second gene in the WAGR deletion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: WAGR = Wilms tumor, Aniridia, Genitourinary anomalies, Range of developmental delay/intellectual disability — a classic mnemonic pairing two contiguous 11p13 genes. COMLEX: Aniridia in a child with an abdominal mass should trigger urgent renal ultrasound for Wilms tumor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — full genetics of WAGR, Denys-Drash, and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndromes as they relate to Wilms tumor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has ambiguous genitalia and is found on renal biopsy to have diffuse mesangial sclerosis, progressing to nephrotic-range proteinuria and renal failure within the first year of life. Genetic testing reveals a WT1 mutation distinct from the deletion pattern seen in WAGR syndrome. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":{"A":"WAGR syndrome","B":"Townes-Brocks syndrome","C":"Branchiootorenal syndrome","D":"Renal coloboma syndrome","E":"Denys-Drash syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Denys-Drash syndrome consists of the triad of ambiguous genitalia, early progressive renal failure from diffuse mesangial sclerosis, and Wilms tumor, arising from a distinct WT1 point mutation pattern rather than the contiguous 11p13 deletion of WAGR syndrome. WAGR syndrome instead presents with aniridia and a broader developmental delay picture without this specific nephropathy, and Townes-Brocks (SALL1), branchiootorenal (EYA1), and renal coloboma (PAX2) syndromes are caused by entirely different genes and do not produce this ambiguous genitalia/nephropathy combination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denys-Drash syndrome's early, severe nephropathy (versus WAGR's later, more variable renal involvement) is the key discriminating feature tested on boards. COMLEX: Ambiguous genitalia with early renal failure in a neonate should prompt WT1 testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — comparative genetics of the WT1-related Wilms tumor syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, and systematically map out how disruptions in molecular signaling (e.g., WT1, GDNF, RET, and WNT pathways) produce congenital anomalies of the kidney and collecting system. Integrates key molecular cascades (WT1 making metanephric mesenchyme competent to secrete GDNF, which binds RET receptors on the ureteric bud) with kidney dysplasias and developmental defects.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher blocks WNT9B signaling from the ureteric bud epithelium in a mouse metanephric explant culture. Which downstream event in the metanephric mesenchyme would most likely fail to occur?","choices":{"A":"Branching morphogenesis of the ureteric bud collecting duct system","B":"Epithelialization of the condensed mesenchyme into renal tubules","C":"Continued maintenance of WT1 expression in the mesenchyme cells","D":"Apoptosis inhibition mediated by the FGF2 and BMP7 growth factors","E":"Continued RET receptor synthesis by the ureteric bud epithelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"WNT9B (with WNT6) upregulates PAX2 and WNT4 in the metanephric mesenchyme, and it is specifically WNT4 that drives epithelialization of the condensed mesenchyme into tubules, so blocking WNT9B would prevent this step. Branching morphogenesis of the collecting ducts is driven by GDNF-RET signaling in the opposite direction, WT1 expression maintenance and apoptosis inhibition are sustained by the bud's FGF2/BMP7 signal rather than WNT9B, and RET synthesis is an intrinsic property of the ureteric bud epithelium unaffected by a mesenchymal WNT signal it receives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Keeping the reciprocal signals straight (GDNF/RET for bud branching versus WNT9B/WNT4 for mesenchymal epithelialization) is essential for this frequently tested pathway. COMLEX: Relevant background for understanding ciliopathy-related cystic kidney diseases, which disrupt this same signaling axis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — the ciliopathy paradigm disrupting this same mesenchymal signaling and epithelialization machinery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":2,"stem":"A pregnant woman at 22 weeks gestation is found on ultrasound to have severe oligohydramnios and bilateral absent fetal kidneys. Which additional fetal finding is most likely to be present?","choices":{"A":"Fetal polyhydramnios","B":"Fetal tracheoesophageal fistula","C":"Pulmonary hypoplasia","D":"Fetal omphalocele","E":"Fetal meningomyelocele"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe oligohydramnios from bilateral renal agenesis restricts fetal chest wall movement and thoracic cavity expansion, producing pulmonary hypoplasia as the classic downstream finding of Potter sequence. Polyhydramnios is the opposite fluid abnormality and would instead suggest a swallowing or upper gastrointestinal obstruction, and tracheoesophageal fistula, omphalocele, and meningomyelocele are distinct primary malformations not caused by decreased amniotic fluid volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary hypoplasia, not a primary lung malformation, is the lethal complication of Potter sequence responsible for most neonatal deaths. COMLEX: Oligohydramnios plus absent fetal kidneys on ultrasound should prompt counseling about a poor pulmonary prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — lower urinary tract histology relevant to distinguishing renal agenesis from an outflow obstruction as the oligohydramnios cause","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":2,"stem":"An infant born with Potter sequence has a flattened facial appearance and bilateral clubfoot deformities. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains these physical findings?","choices":{"A":"Direct teratogenic effect of the underlying renal gene mutation on facial and limb tissue","B":"Placental insufficiency reducing fetal nutrient delivery to developing tissues","C":"Increased amniotic fluid pressure from failed fetal swallowing","D":"Mechanical compression of the fetus from decreased amniotic fluid volume","E":"Abnormal neural crest cell migration affecting craniofacial structures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The flattened facies and clubfeet of Potter sequence result from external mechanical compression of the fetus once oligohydramnios removes the fluid cushion that normally allows free fetal movement and positioning. The causative genes (when identified) act on kidney development, not directly on facial or limb tissue, placental insufficiency and neural crest migration defects produce different anomaly patterns, and Potter sequence involves decreased rather than increased amniotic fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Sequence\" implies one initiating event causing a mechanical cascade — here, low fluid volume causing compression deformities, not a separate primary malformation of the face or limbs. COMLEX: Helps distinguish a deformation (Potter facies) from a true malformation on physical exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — bladder and lower urinary tract development relevant to ruling out obstructive causes of oligohydramnios","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Synthesize the clinical and developmental pathogenesis of bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT, tracing the precise embryologic failures that lead to the systemic downstream cascades of Potter sequence (including oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies). Traces the developmental pathophysiology of fluid dynamics where lack of metanephric urine production leads to oligohydramnios and subsequent physical compression defects.","batch":2,"stem":"A fetus with bilateral renal agenesis produces no urine into the amniotic cavity throughout the second and third trimesters. Which downstream consequence results primarily from the resulting reduction in amniotic fluid volume?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary hypoplasia from restricted fetal chest wall movement","B":"Cardiac septal defects from abnormal neural crest cell migration","C":"Cleft lip and palate from failed frontonasal prominence fusion","D":"Duodenal atresia from failed gut tube recanalization","E":"Tracheal atresia from abnormal foregut septation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Absent fetal urine production reduces amniotic fluid volume, and the resulting oligohydramnios restricts fetal chest wall and thoracic cavity movement, impairing lung development into pulmonary hypoplasia. Cardiac septal defects, cleft lip and palate, duodenal atresia, and tracheal atresia are all recognized congenital anomalies, but each arises from its own independent developmental failure rather than from reduced amniotic fluid volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary hypoplasia is the amniotic-fluid-volume-dependent consequence of renal agenesis that boards test repeatedly — trace the causal chain from no urine to no fluid to no lung expansion. COMLEX: Distinguishes fluid-volume-mediated complications from independently occurring congenital anomalies when reviewing a stillbirth case.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — clinical reasoning through congenital lower urinary tract anomalies and their secondary complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man undergoes abdominal CT for unrelated trauma, incidentally revealing two kidneys fused at their lower poles by an isthmus anterior to the aorta at the level of L4. Which embryologic event explains why this kidney failed to reach its normal adult position?","choices":{"A":"The metanephros failed to receive its initial pelvic blood supply","B":"The ureteric bud failed to penetrate the metanephric blastema","C":"The kidney rotated 90 degrees medially before completing its ascent","D":"Cranial ascent stopped at the inferior mesenteric artery","E":"The mesonephric duct failed to degenerate on the affected side"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A horseshoe kidney's isthmus forms when the lower poles fuse during ascent, and further cranial migration is then blocked once the isthmus reaches the level of the inferior mesenteric artery root, fixing the kidney in the lower lumbar region seen here. Failed pelvic blood supply and failed ureteric bud penetration would instead cause agenesis or dysplasia rather than a fused, low-lying kidney, medial rotation is a separate normal developmental step unrelated to the fixation itself, and mesonephric duct persistence is a genital rather than renal-position finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This vignette is a classic image-based pairing of horseshoe kidney with the inferior mesenteric artery as the blocking landmark. COMLEX: Horseshoe kidney is often an incidental imaging finding, as here, requiring recognition rather than symptom-driven workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — mechanical hydronephrosis risk from the impaired drainage across a horseshoe kidney's isthmus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"A routine prenatal ultrasound shows a fetal kidney located in the pelvis near the common iliac vessels that does not migrate to the renal fossa by the third trimester. Compared with a normally ascended kidney, which vascular finding is most expected?","choices":{"A":"Anomalous renal arteries arising from nearby low arteries","B":"A single renal artery arising at the L1-L2 level","C":"Arterial supply derived from the celiac trunk","D":"Arterial supply derived from the superior mesenteric artery","E":"Arterial supply derived from the inferior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pelvic kidney that fails to ascend keeps the low arterial supply of its early position, typically anomalous vessels from the distal aorta or iliac arteries, rather than gaining higher renal arteries during ascent. A single renal artery at L1-L2 is the normal ascended pattern, and the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries are not sources of renal supply, the inferior mesenteric artery instead being the blocking point for horseshoe kidneys.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pelvic kidney keeps low, often multiple anomalous arteries, a useful vascular clue distinguishing it from a normally ascended but ectopically rotated kidney. COMLEX: Important when planning pelvic surgery, since a pelvic kidney's anomalous vessels are easily mistaken for other pelvic vasculature.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — nephron vascular anatomy and the renal vasculature that this anomalous pelvic supply diverges from","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate the physical mechanics of kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular reorganization with characteristic clinical presentations, somatic structural relationships, and imaging findings in patients with pelvic, ectopic, or horseshoe kidneys. Links kidney migration from pelvic basin to the upper abdomen (and medial rotation) to mechanical barriers like the inferior mesenteric artery anchoring horseshoe kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with a horseshoe kidney undergoes imaging showing both ureters passing ventral to the isthmus of the fused kidney mass before entering the bladder. Which developmental event best explains this ureteral course?","choices":{"A":"Isthmus formation preceded ureteric bud positioning","B":"Medial rotation stopped before it was completed","C":"Ureteral migration occurred after the kidneys ascended","D":"Renal vein anomalies displaced the ureteral course","E":"Ureteric buds arose from the posterior kidney surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a horseshoe kidney the renal pelves stay rotated forward because the fused kidneys do not complete their normal medial rotation during ascent, so the ureters leave the anterior pelves and pass ventral to the isthmus. Isthmus formation does not alter ureteric bud position, ureters do not migrate after ascent, renal vein anomalies do not redirect the ureter, and a posterior bud origin would not produce a ventral course.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior ureteral course over a horseshoe kidney's isthmus is a key anatomic detail relevant to surgical approach and to distinguishing normal horseshoe anatomy from true obstruction. COMLEX: Useful for interpreting IVP/CT urogram findings in a patient with a known horseshoe kidney.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — how this anterior ureteral course over the isthmus predisposes to impaired drainage and hydronephrosis","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":2,"title":"SDL 2 — Lower Urinary Tract & Cloacal Partitioning","questions":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of structures results from division of the embryonic cloaca by the urorectal septum?","choices":{"A":"The bladder and the urogenital membrane","B":"The allantois and the hindgut proper","C":"The mesonephric duct and the metanephric mesenchyme","D":"The urogenital sinus and the anal canal","E":"The phallic urogenital sinus and the perineal body"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The urorectal septum grows caudally between the fourth and seventh weeks to split the single cloacal cavity into the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the anal canal posteriorly. The bladder and urogenital membrane are subsequent derivatives of the urogenital sinus rather than the products of the initial split, the allantois and hindgut are structures adjacent to the cloaca rather than its two resulting compartments, the mesonephric duct and metanephric mesenchyme belong to kidney development rather than cloacal partitioning, and the phallic urogenital sinus and perineal body are later, more specific derivatives rather than the immediate two-part division.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the urorectal septum's single defining act — anterior urogenital sinus versus posterior anal canal. COMLEX: This division is the anchor fact for reasoning through every anorectal malformation question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — the hindgut's own separate embryologic course once it is no longer sharing a cavity with the urinary tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the caudal tip of the urorectal septum form once it reaches the cloacal membrane?","choices":{"A":"The median umbilical ligament","B":"The genital tubercle","C":"The intraembryonic allantois","D":"The trigone of the bladder","E":"The perineal body"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Once the descending urorectal septum contacts the cloacal membrane, its tip differentiates into the perineal body, a fibromuscular node that becomes a site of insertion for several perineal muscles. The median umbilical ligament and urachus are unrelated allantoic derivatives, the genital tubercle is a separate external swelling that gives rise to the phallus or clitoris, and the trigone forms from incorporated mesonephric duct tissue rather than from the septum's tip.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The perineal body is the septum's terminal landmark, useful for visualizing why it is a common site of obstetric tearing. COMLEX: Anchors the muscular anatomy tested in pelvic floor questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the perineal body's proximity to sinovaginal bulb fusion in female genital tract development","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn boy is found to have an imperforate anus with meconium noted in the urine. Incomplete descent of which structure best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"The urorectal septum","B":"The genital tubercle","C":"The mesonephric duct","D":"The ureteric bud","E":"The paramesonephric duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meconium in the urine indicates a rectourinary fistula, which arises when the urorectal septum fails to descend completely and separate the anal canal from the urogenital sinus, leaving a persistent communication between the two. The genital tubercle governs external genital growth rather than the internal separation of gut from urinary tract, the mesonephric duct and ureteric bud are structures of the developing urinary collecting system rather than the septating tissue itself, and the paramesonephric duct belongs to female reproductive tract development.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meconium in the urine is the classic clue pointing straight to a rectourinary fistula from septal failure. COMLEX: A frequently tested newborn physical exam finding requiring urgent surgical evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — how the same urogenital-ridge origin links structural GU tract defects to renal anomalies","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":1,"stem":"The trigone of the bladder is initially lined by mesodermal tissue because it forms by incorporation of which paired embryonic structures?","choices":{"A":"The paired paramesonephric ducts","B":"The paired ureteric buds","C":"The paired allantoic stalks","D":"The urogenital sinus epithelium","E":"The caudal mesonephric ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As the cloaca differentiates, the caudal portions of the paired mesonephric ducts are absorbed directly into the posterior bladder wall, and because these ducts are themselves mesodermal, the resulting trigone mucosa starts out as mesoderm rather than the endoderm lining the rest of the bladder. The paramesonephric ducts belong to female reproductive development rather than trigone formation, the ureteric buds separate from the mesonephric ducts to enter the bladder independently rather than being incorporated as the trigone itself, the allantoic stalk gives rise to the urachus rather than the trigone, and the urogenital sinus epithelium is endodermal and lines the remaining bladder rather than the trigone initially.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trigone's mesodermal origin from the absorbed mesonephric ducts is a classic exception to the bladder's otherwise endodermal lining. COMLEX: A useful anchor fact for explaining why the trigone is described separately in gross anatomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — how this initial mesodermal trigone lining is later replaced by the endodermal urothelium seen on histology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":1,"stem":"Persistence of the portion of the allantoic lumen nearest the umbilicus, open at the umbilicus but closed toward the bladder, produces which anomaly?","choices":{"A":"A urachal fistula","B":"A urachal cyst","C":"A patent vitelline duct","D":"A urachal sinus","E":"A vesicoureteral fistula"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When only the segment of allantoic lumen nearest the umbilicus fails to obliterate, the result is a urachal sinus, a blind-ending tract that opens at the umbilicus. A urachal fistula instead reflects persistence of the entire allantoic lumen connecting bladder to umbilicus, a urachal cyst reflects an isolated mid-segment of persistent lumen with no connection to either end, a patent vitelline duct is an unrelated yolk-sac-to-gut remnant, and a vesicoureteral fistula is not a recognized allantoic anomaly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish the three urachal remnants by exactly which segment of the allantoic lumen persists. COMLEX: These anomalies are commonly tested by their differing connections (umbilicus versus bladder versus neither).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the same urachal remnant lining seen histologically as remnant urothelium along the median umbilical ligament","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":1,"stem":"In the adult, the obliterated allantois is represented by which fibrous structure?","choices":{"A":"The medial umbilical ligament","B":"The median umbilical ligament","C":"The lateral umbilical fold","D":"The urachal diverticulum remnant","E":"The perineal body attachment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once the allantoic lumen is obliterated, its fibrous cord persists as the single, midline median umbilical ligament connecting the bladder apex to the umbilicus. The paired medial umbilical ligaments instead derive from the obliterated umbilical arteries, the lateral umbilical folds cover the inferior epigastric vessels rather than any allantoic remnant, a urachal diverticulum is not the standard term for the obliterated structure, and the perineal body is an unrelated pelvic floor landmark.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A classic naming distinction — median (allantois) versus paired medial (umbilical arteries) ligaments of the anterior abdominal wall. COMLEX: Frequently tested as a direct terminology recall item.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the adjacent anterior abdominal wall vascular folds seen on the same peritoneal surface","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"Early splitting of a single ureteric bud before it contacts the metanephric mesenchyme most often produces which anomaly?","choices":{"A":"A duplicated collecting system","B":"A fused horseshoe kidney","C":"Bilateral renal agenesis","D":"A multicystic dysplastic kidney","E":"Obstructing posterior urethral valves"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the ureteric bud divides early, each branch can independently induce its own portion of the metanephric mesenchyme, yielding a partially or completely duplicated collecting system with two renal pelves and ureters. A horseshoe kidney instead results from abnormal fusion of the lower poles during ascent rather than bud splitting, bilateral renal agenesis results from failed induction rather than excess bud branching, a multicystic dysplastic kidney reflects abnormal differentiation of the metanephric tissue itself, and posterior urethral valves are an obstructing urethral membrane unrelated to ureteric bud number.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ureteric bud splitting is the direct mechanistic link to duplicated collecting systems, a frequently imaged finding. COMLEX: Useful for distinguishing duplication from other bud-related anomalies like agenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — how a duplicated system's abnormal drainage predisposes to obstruction and stasis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"In a complete ureteral duplication, the ectopic ureter draining the upper-pole moiety most often inserts at which position relative to the orthotopic lower-pole ureter?","choices":{"A":"Superior and lateral to it","B":"Inferior and medial to it","C":"At the identical point within the trigone","D":"Directly into the renal pelvis of the other moiety","E":"Superior and medial to it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the ectopic upper-pole ureteric bud arises higher on the mesonephric duct and is absorbed into the bladder base later, its final bladder insertion ends up inferior and medial to the orthotopic lower-pole ureter, which retains the normal, more superior and lateral trigonal position. The remaining options either invert this relationship, describe a shared insertion point that would not represent duplication, or describe an insertion into a renal pelvis rather than the bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This inferomedial/superolateral relationship is the Weigert-Meyer rule, a frequently tested pairing. COMLEX: Sets up why the two moieties in a duplex system have opposite complication patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — why this inferomedial upper-pole insertion is the anatomic setup for obstruction with ureterocele","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A young girl has continuous urinary dribbling between otherwise normal voids. Which ureteral anomaly best explains this specific pattern?","choices":{"A":"Vesicoureteral reflux occurring into an orthotopic ureter","B":"A ureterocele obstructing the bladder neck outlet region","C":"An ectopic ureter opening distal to the external sphincter","D":"Bilateral ureteropelvic junction obstruction from stenosis","E":"A patent urachus continuously draining fluid at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Continuous dribbling despite normal voiding indicates urine is bypassing the continence mechanism entirely, which occurs when an ectopic ureter opens distal to the external urethral sphincter (into the vagina, urethra, or vestibule) so that urine leaks constantly regardless of bladder filling or voiding. Reflux into an orthotopic ureter instead causes recurrent infections rather than constant leakage, a ureterocele and ureteropelvic junction obstruction both produce obstructive symptoms rather than continuous incontinence, and a patent urachus would leak from the umbilicus rather than cause urinary dribbling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Continuous incontinence in a girl with otherwise normal voiding is the classic vignette for an ectopic ureter distal to the sphincter. COMLEX: A key differential point separating ectopic ureter from primary nocturnal enuresis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the female urethra's short length and the sphincter anatomy this ectopic orifice bypasses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":1,"stem":"Bladder exstrophy is thought to result primarily from failure of which process?","choices":{"A":"Closure of the urorectal septum during the fifth week of gestation","B":"Fusion of the lateral body wall folds in the pelvic region","C":"Obliteration of the allantoic lumen and its umbilical stalk","D":"Caudal migration of the ureteric bud along the mesonephric duct","E":"Fusion of the paired paramesonephric ducts in the midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bladder exstrophy is attributed to failure of the paired lateral body wall folds to close in the midline over the pelvic region, leaving the bladder mucosa exposed on the abdominal surface. Urorectal septum closure failure instead produces rectourinary fistulas rather than exposed bladder mucosa, allantoic lumen persistence produces urachal remnant anomalies rather than a ventral wall defect, ureteric bud migration failure produces ectopic or duplicated ureters, and paramesonephric duct fusion is a female reproductive tract process unrelated to bladder exstrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bladder exstrophy is a ventral body wall folding defect, not a septation or duct-fusion problem — a key distinction from other GU anomalies in this SDL. COMLEX: Useful for separating folding defects from septation defects on exam vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the exposed urothelium seen histologically in bladder exstrophy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is described as a constant accompanying feature of bladder exstrophy?","choices":{"A":"Hypospadias","B":"Cryptorchidism","C":"Epispadias","D":"Imperforate anus","E":"Vaginal atresia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bladder exstrophy is consistently accompanied by epispadias, with the open urinary tract extending along the dorsal aspect of the penis from the bladder to the umbilicus. Hypospadias instead reflects a ventral, not dorsal, urethral opening defect unrelated to exstrophy, cryptorchidism and vaginal atresia are separate gonadal or duct anomalies, and imperforate anus reflects urorectal septum failure, which is a feature of the more severe cloacal exstrophy rather than isolated bladder exstrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epispadias is the fixed, always-present partner finding of bladder exstrophy — a high-yield pairing. COMLEX: A quick way to distinguish bladder exstrophy from other, non-constant genital anomalies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how epispadias fits among the other external genital malformations in that differential","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with bladder exstrophy, cloacal exstrophy is distinguished by which additional finding?","choices":{"A":"Anal canal malformation from a disrupted urorectal septum","B":"Isolated epispadias without any exposed bladder mucosa present","C":"Entirely normal and unaffected external genitalia bilaterally","D":"A fully intact and completely closed anterior abdominal wall","E":"Preserved anal sphincter tone with normal reflex function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloacal exstrophy involves a more severe ventral body wall defect than bladder exstrophy and additionally disrupts normal urorectal septum development, producing anal canal malformations and imperforate anus alongside the exposed viscera. Isolated epispadias without exposed mucosa describes a milder, unrelated anomaly, entirely normal genitalia and an intact abdominal wall are the opposite of what is seen in either exstrophy variant, and preserved sphincter tone contradicts the anal malformation that defines the added severity of cloacal exstrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cloacal exstrophy = bladder exstrophy's body wall defect PLUS a failed urorectal septum. COMLEX: A layered-severity concept commonly tested by asking what makes cloacal exstrophy worse.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — the same urorectal septum whose isolated failure instead produces rectourinary fistulas without a body wall defect","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn male has no visible anal opening and passes meconium through the tip of the penis. Which developmental process failed to occur normally?","choices":{"A":"Complete division of the cloaca by the urorectal septum","B":"Isolated failure of anal membrane canalization during week five","C":"Fusion of the paired genital swellings at the midline","D":"Closure of the lateral ventral body wall folds bilaterally","E":"Obliteration of the intraembryonic allantoic duct lumen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meconium exiting through the penile tip indicates a rectourethral fistula, which occurs when the urorectal septum fails to complete its caudal growth and fully separate the anal canal from the urogenital sinus, leaving persistent communication between gut and urinary tract. Isolated anal membrane canalization failure would instead cause a simple imperforate anus without a urinary fistula, genital swelling fusion governs external genitalia rather than the internal gut-urinary connection, lateral body wall fold closure failure produces exstrophy rather than a fistula, and allantoic duct obliteration failure produces urachal remnants rather than meconium in the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A rectourethral fistula in a boy is the signature finding of incomplete urorectal septum descent. COMLEX: Requires prompt recognition for surgical referral in the newborn nursery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how this same septal failure spectrum intersects with the broader differential of newborn genital and perineal malformations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":2,"stem":"A female infant has a single perineal opening through which both urine and stool exit, connecting to a shared internal channel with otherwise unremarkable external genitalia. This finding reflects failure of which structure to reach the cloacal membrane?","choices":{"A":"The genital tubercle","B":"The paramesonephric ducts","C":"The urorectal septum","D":"The labioscrotal swellings","E":"The urogenital folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A single perineal orifice draining both the urinary and gastrointestinal tracts describes a persistent cloaca, the most severe end of the urorectal septum failure spectrum, in which the septum never reaches the cloacal membrane to create separate openings. The genital tubercle governs clitoral/phallic formation rather than the shared internal channel, the paramesonephric ducts form the uterus and upper vagina rather than the perineal opening itself, and the labioscrotal swellings and urogenital folds shape the external vulva rather than the internal urorectal separation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent cloaca is the female-specific, most severe manifestation of urorectal septum failure and is frequently paired with other anorectal malformations. COMLEX: Recognize it as part of the broader anorectal malformation spectrum requiring multidisciplinary workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the shared urogenital-ridge origin explaining why such internal channel anomalies often co-occur with renal malformations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the step-wise partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum, and predict the complex fistulous and anatomical phenotypes that result from incomplete or disrupted septation. Examines the septation of the cloaca into the anterior urogenital sinus and posterior anal canal. Links septal failure to rectourinary fistulas and cloacal anomalies.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has a rectoperineal fistula, with the rectum opening onto the perineum just anterior to the normal anal site. Compared with a rectourethral fistula, this milder anomaly reflects which difference in the underlying defect?","choices":{"A":"A complete absence of the urorectal septum along its length","B":"A primary defect of neural crest cell migration pathways","C":"An isolated timing error during cloacal membrane rupture","D":"A primary defect of genital tubercle outgrowth patterning","E":"A more caudal and less extensive failure of septation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A rectoperineal fistula reflects a limited, low, and more caudal septation defect, in contrast to a rectourethral fistula, which reflects a higher, more extensive failure that leaves the rectum communicating with the urethra itself. Complete absence of the septum would produce a persistent cloaca rather than a mild perineal fistula, neural crest migration defects relate to enteric innervation rather than septal position, cloacal membrane rupture timing is a separate process from septal descent, and genital tubercle outgrowth defects affect external genitalia rather than the internal fistula location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anorectal malformation severity tracks with how caudally versus proximally the urorectal septum failure occurs. COMLEX: A useful framework for ranking anorectal malformations from mild to severe.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — how a far more extensive folding and septation failure instead produces cloacal exstrophy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has continuous clear fluid drainage from the umbilicus that increases with crying, and the fluid tests positive for urea. Which structure has failed to obliterate?","choices":{"A":"The bladder-adjacent allantoic segment","B":"The umbilicus-adjacent allantoic segment","C":"The entire intraembryonic allantois","D":"The vitelline duct","E":"The paired umbilical arteries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Continuous urine leakage at the umbilicus that worsens with increased intra-abdominal pressure describes a urachal fistula, which occurs when the entire intraembryonic allantoic lumen remains patent, creating an open connection from bladder to umbilicus. Persistence of only the bladder-adjacent segment instead produces a blind diverticulum off the bladder apex, persistence of only the umbilicus-adjacent segment produces a urachal sinus open at the umbilicus, the vitelline duct is an unrelated yolk-sac structure, and the umbilical arteries becoming the medial umbilical ligaments is unrelated to urine drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Umbilical drainage that tests positive for urea is the defining clue for a urachal fistula. COMLEX: A classic newborn umbilical discharge vignette requiring distinction from an omphalomesenteric duct remnant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the median umbilical ligament's normal adult anatomy on the anterior abdominal wall once this lumen properly obliterates","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":2,"stem":"Imaging in an infant reveals a cystic structure along the course of the median umbilical ligament, with no connection to either the bladder or the umbilicus. Which process explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Persistence of the entire allantoic lumen from end to end","B":"Persistence of a mid-segment portion of the allantoic lumen","C":"Persistence of just the bladder-adjacent allantoic segment","D":"Persistence of just the umbilicus-adjacent allantoic segment","E":"Failure of the mesonephric duct tissue to regress fully"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A cystic structure with no communication to the bladder or the umbilicus reflects a urachal cyst, formed when only an isolated, mid-portion segment of the allantoic lumen fails to obliterate while both ends close normally. Persistence of the entire lumen would instead produce an open fistula to both ends, persistence of only the bladder-adjacent or only the umbilicus-adjacent segment would each leave a tract open at one end rather than an isolated cyst, and mesonephric duct regression is unrelated to the allantoic remnant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A urachal cyst is defined by having no connection at either end, unlike a fistula (both ends) or a sinus (one end). COMLEX: Frequently imaged as a midline abdominal wall mass in infants or young adults.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the urothelial lining these urachal remnants retain on histologic sampling","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the dual embryonic origins of the bladder, trigone, urethra, and urachus, and deconstruct how failures in lumen obliteration or tissue integration manifest as patent urachus, urachal cysts, or urachal sinuses. Focuses on the integration of the mesodermal trigone into the endodermal bladder wall and the obliteration of the allantois into the median umbilical ligament.","batch":2,"stem":"A histology sample of the adult bladder trigone shows epithelium indistinguishable from the surrounding bladder wall. This finding reflects which normal developmental process?","choices":{"A":"Persistence of the original mesodermal lining without any change","B":"Replacement of endodermal epithelium instead by mesodermal connective tissue","C":"Failure of mesonephric duct incorporation into the full bladder wall","D":"Migration of paramesonephric epithelium fully into the bladder trigone","E":"Replacement of the mesodermal trigone lining by endodermal epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Although the trigone begins as mesoderm from the incorporated mesonephric ducts, this mesodermal lining is gradually replaced over development by the same endodermal epithelium lining the rest of the bladder, so the mature trigone is histologically indistinguishable from adjacent bladder mucosa. The original mesodermal lining persisting unchanged would instead make the trigone histologically distinct in the adult, endoderm being replaced by mesoderm reverses the actual direction of this process, mesonephric duct incorporation is the initial step that creates the trigone rather than something that fails here, and paramesonephric epithelium plays no role in bladder trigone development.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trigone's adult histology is a trick worth knowing — it looks just like the rest of the bladder despite its distinct mesodermal origin. COMLEX: A detail-oriented histology fact that can be tested as a \"which epithelium\" question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the transitional epithelium (urothelium) seen uniformly across the trigone and bladder body on light microscopy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has recurrent febrile urinary tract infections. Voiding cystourethrogram shows retrograde flow of contrast into a single, normally positioned ureter during voiding, with no ectopic orifice identified. Which anatomic mechanism best accounts for this pattern?","choices":{"A":"A short and incompetent intravesical ureteral tunnel","B":"A ureteral orifice located distal to the external sphincter","C":"Complete ureteral duplication with an ectopic upper-pole orifice","D":"A posterior urethral valve obstructing bladder outflow","E":"Failure of the mesonephric duct to be absorbed into the bladder wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reflux into a normally positioned, single ureter without any ectopic orifice points to vesicoureteral reflux from an incompetent, abnormally shortened intravesical ureteral tunnel, which normally acts as a one-way valve during voiding. An orifice distal to the external sphincter would instead cause continuous incontinence rather than reflux on voiding, complete duplication with an ectopic orifice would show two collecting systems rather than the single system described here, a posterior urethral valve produces outflow obstruction rather than reflux into an otherwise normal ureter, and mesonephric duct absorption failure would prevent normal ureteral incorporation altogether rather than produce isolated reflux.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reflux without duplication or ectopia points specifically to a short or incompetent intravesical tunnel. COMLEX: A key VCUG interpretation skill for recurrent pediatric UTI workups.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — how this same reflux mechanism raises long-term risk for pyelonephritis and renal scarring","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A duplex kidney is identified incidentally on prenatal ultrasound. Postnatal imaging shows the upper-pole moiety has hydronephrosis, while the lower-pole moiety shows reflux on voiding cystourethrogram. Which principle explains why the two moieties have opposite complications?","choices":{"A":"Ectopic, caudal insertion of the lower-pole ureter with a short upper-pole tunnel","B":"Ectopic, caudal insertion of the upper-pole ureter with a short lower-pole tunnel","C":"Both ureters sharing one identical trigonal insertion site under equal pressure","D":"The upper-pole ureter developing directly from the mesonephric duct tissue","E":"The lower-pole ureter arising from a second, independent ureteric bud"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a duplicated system, the upper-pole ureter is the ectopic one that arises from a second, higher bud and inserts caudally and medially, predisposing it to an obstructing, often stenotic distal insertion and hydronephrosis, while the lower-pole ureter keeps its normal, more superolateral trigonal position with a comparatively short or displaced intravesical tunnel that predisposes to reflux instead. Reversing which moiety is ectopic contradicts the established insertion pattern, a shared single insertion site would not represent two distinct complication patterns, ureters arise from the ureteric bud rather than directly from mesonephric duct tissue, and it is the upper-pole rather than the lower-pole ureter that comes from the second bud.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Upper pole obstructs, lower pole refluxes\" is a high-yield mnemonic pairing tested repeatedly in duplex-system vignettes. COMLEX: Useful for predicting which moiety needs surgical attention based on imaging alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — how this obstruction/reflux pairing determines which moiety is at greatest long-term risk for renal damage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate clinical vignettes and diagnostic imaging to differentiate congenital lower urinary tract anomalies, justifying the anatomical basis for their clinical presentations and secondary complications (e.g., urinary stasis, vesicoureteral reflux, recurrent infections). Requires active clinical reasoning to diagnose conditions (like ectopic ureters or VUR) based on structural anatomy and clinical presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl has continuous urinary leakage between otherwise normal voids. Imaging shows one kidney with two separate collecting systems, one ureter entering the bladder at a normal trigonal position and the other entering the vestibule. Which embryologic event produced the vestibular ureter?","choices":{"A":"Cranial migration of the ureteric bud past the metanephric mesenchyme layer","B":"Failure of the ureteric bud to induce the metanephric mesenchyme tissue","C":"Persistence of the mesonephric duct itself functioning as a ureter","D":"Caudal migration of an ectopic ureteric bud alongside the mesonephric duct","E":"Duplication of the metanephric mesenchyme without ureteric bud splitting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A vestibular ureteral opening arises when an ectopic second ureteric bud migrates caudally together with the regressing mesonephric duct instead of separating normally, ending its course far below the normal trigonal position and outside the continence mechanism, which explains the girl's continuous leakage. Cranial migration past the metanephric mesenchyme does not describe a recognized mechanism for ectopic ureteral position, failure of ureteric bud induction would cause renal agenesis rather than a functioning ectopic system, mesonephric duct persistence as a ureter is not how the vestibular ureter forms, and metanephric mesenchyme duplication without bud splitting would not explain two separate ureteral orifices.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ectopic bud's caudal co-migration with the mesonephric duct is the precise mechanism behind vestibular/vaginal ureteral openings. COMLEX: Ties directly into recognizing continuous incontinence as a red flag for ectopic ureter rather than primary enuresis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — the same mesonephric duct pathway this ectopic ureter follows during its caudal migration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has exposed, everted vesical mucosa on the lower abdominal wall, a widened pubic symphysis, and a dorsally displaced urethral groove, but a normally formed, patent anus. Which diagnosis best fits this combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Cloacal exstrophy with anal malformation","B":"Bladder exstrophy with epispadias","C":"Isolated epispadias without exstrophy","D":"A large, continuously draining patent urachus","E":"Persistent cloaca with a single perineal orifice"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Exposed bladder mucosa with a dorsal urethral defect but an otherwise normal, patent anus is the classic combination for bladder exstrophy with its constant epispadias, since the anorectal tract is unaffected in this milder variant. Cloacal exstrophy would additionally show an imperforate anus and disrupted genital swellings, isolated epispadias without exstrophy would not show exposed bladder mucosa, a patent urachus would drain from the umbilicus rather than expose the bladder itself, and persistent cloaca describes a single combined perineal orifice rather than exposed abdominal wall mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A patent anus is the key discriminator separating bladder exstrophy from cloacal exstrophy on physical exam. COMLEX: Use the anorectal exam as the fast triage step between these two diagnoses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — where epispadias also appears in the broader external genital malformation differential","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has a wide ventral abdominal wall defect exposing two separate bladder halves flanking a segment of exposed intestinal mucosa, an imperforate anus, and widely separated genital swellings. Which additional developmental failure, beyond the ventral body wall defect already seen in bladder exstrophy, explains the bowel finding?","choices":{"A":"Disrupted genital tubercle outgrowth","B":"Disrupted paramesonephric duct fusion","C":"Disrupted allantoic lumen obliteration","D":"Disrupted urorectal septum development","E":"Disrupted ureteric bud induction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The exposed bowel segment and imperforate anus seen in cloacal exstrophy reflect an additional, disrupted urorectal septum on top of the ventral body wall closure failure shared with bladder exstrophy, since normal septation would otherwise keep the gut and urinary tracts separately enclosed. Genital tubercle outgrowth failure affects phallic/clitoral formation rather than bowel exposure, paramesonephric duct fusion is a female reproductive tract process unrelated to this finding, allantoic obliteration failure produces urachal remnants rather than exposed bowel, and ureteric bud induction failure produces renal anomalies rather than an anorectal defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cloacal exstrophy = bladder exstrophy's wall defect plus additional urorectal septum failure, the more extensive end of one spectrum. COMLEX: A layered concept: identify both failures rather than treating cloacal exstrophy as just a bigger bladder exstrophy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — the same urorectal septum whose isolated failure alone instead produces rectourinary fistulas without any body wall defect","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the mechanical, structural, and lateral folding failures of the ventral body wall that culminate in bladder and cloacal exstrophy, distinguishing their clinical phenotypes and associated genitourinary defects. Contrasts bladder exstrophy (associated with epispadias) with more severe cloacal exstrophy resulting from abdominal wall and septation failures.","batch":2,"stem":"Both bladder exstrophy and cloacal exstrophy share a ventral body wall closure defect, but only cloacal exstrophy also disrupts hindgut and genital development. Which underlying difference in the primary defect accounts for this broader involvement?","choices":{"A":"A more extensive failure of lateral fold fusion during folding","B":"A defect confined to the genital tubercle region in scope","C":"A defect confined to the paramesonephric duct pair in scope","D":"A defect confined to the allantoic stalk and its surrounding tissue","E":"A defect confined to the mesonephric duct and its surrounding tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloacal exstrophy reflects a more extensive disruption of the same lateral body wall fold closure process that, in its milder form, produces isolated bladder exstrophy, and this greater degree of disruption is what additionally derails urorectal septation and separates the genital swellings widely. A defect confined only to the genital tubercle, paramesonephric ducts, allantois, or mesonephric duct would each explain an isolated anomaly in one system rather than the combined body-wall-plus-septation-plus-genital picture seen in cloacal exstrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Think of bladder and cloacal exstrophy as points on a single severity spectrum of lateral body wall folding failure, not as two unrelated diagnoses. COMLEX: A useful \"spectrum\" framework for ranking severity across related GU malformations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — the same lateral body wall folding process in its normal, successful form during embryonic folding","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":3,"title":"SDL 3 — Genital System, Duct Differentiation, & Sexual Development","questions":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene product functions as the master transcription factor that initiates the testis-determining cascade?","choices":{"A":"SOX9","B":"SF1","C":"SRY","D":"WNT4","E":"DAX1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SRY, encoded on the Y chromosome, is the testis-determining factor whose expression sets off the entire downstream cascade, including SOX9 and SF1 activation. SOX9 and SF1 act downstream of SRY rather than initiating the cascade themselves, and WNT4 and DAX1 belong to the opposing ovarian-determining pathway rather than the testis-determining one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SRY sits at the very top of the testis-determining cascade — know it as the trigger, not a downstream effector. COMLEX: A frequently tested single-gene recall item for sex determination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — the parallel transcription-factor logic of WT1 initiating the kidney-induction cascade","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene, once upregulated downstream of SRY, works together with SOX9 to elevate anti-Mullerian hormone production and drive Sertoli/Leydig cell differentiation?","choices":{"A":"SRY","B":"WNT4","C":"DAX1","D":"FGF9","E":"SF1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SF1 (steroidogenesis factor 1) is upregulated by SRY, directly or through SOX9, and then works alongside SOX9 to raise AMH levels and drive Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation, including testosterone-synthesizing enzyme expression in Leydig cells. SRY sits upstream of this step rather than being the factor described, WNT4 and DAX1 drive the opposing ovarian pathway, and FGF9 acts earlier to recruit mesonephric tubules into the gonadal ridge rather than to elevate AMH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SF1 is the hinge gene linking SRY/SOX9 to both AMH production and steroidogenic enzyme expression. COMLEX: Useful for tracing how one gene bridges duct regression and hormone synthesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — where GDNF/RET signaling plays an analogous downstream-effector role in kidney induction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene, upregulated by WNT4, inhibits SOX9 function and thereby supports ovarian rather than testicular gonadal development?","choices":{"A":"SRY","B":"SF1","C":"FGF9","D":"DAX1","E":"SOX9"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DAX1 is upregulated by WNT4, the ovary-determining gene, and inhibits SOX9 function, removing the testis-promoting signal and allowing ovarian differentiation to proceed. SRY and SF1 belong to the opposing testis-determining pathway, FGF9 acts to recruit mesonephric tubules during testis formation rather than to inhibit SOX9, and SOX9 itself is the target being inhibited rather than the inhibitor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DAX1 is WNT4's downstream tool for actively suppressing SOX9, not just a passive marker of ovarian fate. COMLEX: A key mechanistic detail distinguishing active suppression from simple absence of the male pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how disruption of this same WNT4/DAX1 axis contributes to 46,XX disorders of sex development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone, acting through intracellular receptors on the mesonephric duct itself, drives its differentiation into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicle?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrotestosterone reaching the duct tissue","B":"Testosterone binding its own androgen receptor","C":"Anti-Mullerian hormone suppressing the duct","D":"Estrogen binding receptors in the duct wall","E":"Follicle-stimulating hormone reaching the testis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Testosterone receptor complexes act directly on the mesonephric duct to mediate its differentiation into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicle. Dihydrotestosterone instead governs the external genitalia and prostate rather than the duct itself, anti-Mullerian hormone regresses the paramesonephric duct rather than developing the mesonephric duct, estrogen is not what directs mesonephric duct differentiation, and follicle-stimulating hormone acts on the seminiferous tubules rather than the duct system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone (internal duct) versus DHT (external genitalia) is one of the most frequently tested hormone-target pairings in reproductive embryology. COMLEX: Anchor this pairing before tackling any DSD vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — the caudal mesonephric duct tissue that is instead incorporated into the bladder trigone rather than becoming genital structures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":1,"stem":"Which substance, produced by Sertoli cells, causes regression of the paramesonephric ducts in the male embryo?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Mullerian hormone","B":"Testosterone from Leydig cells","C":"Dihydrotestosterone in tissue","D":"Luteinizing hormone released","E":"Insulin-like factor 3 signal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-Mullerian hormone (Mullerian-inhibiting substance), secreted by Sertoli cells, causes regression of the paramesonephric ducts in the male, leaving only a small cranial remnant, the appendix testis. Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone instead promote development of male duct and genital structures rather than causing duct regression, luteinizing hormone stimulates Leydig cell steroidogenesis rather than acting on the ducts directly, and insulin-like factor 3 contributes to testicular descent rather than duct regression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AMH from Sertoli cells is the single hormone responsible for eliminating the female duct system in males. COMLEX: A frequently tested cause-and-effect pairing for absent uterus/uterine tubes in a genetic male.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how isolated AMH deficiency produces persistent Mullerian duct remnants in an otherwise normally virilized male","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":1,"stem":"In the absence of testosterone and anti-Mullerian hormone, which duct system persists to become the main genital ducts of the female embryo?","choices":{"A":"The regressing mesonephric ducts","B":"The ureteric bud epithelium","C":"The paramesonephric ducts","D":"The sinovaginal bulb tissue","E":"The genital tubercle mesenchyme"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without testosterone to maintain them and without anti-Mullerian hormone to regress them, the paramesonephric ducts persist and develop into the uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina, while the mesonephric ducts degenerate for lack of testosterone support. The mesonephric ducts are the structures that regress in this scenario rather than persisting, the ureteric buds belong to kidney and collecting-system development, the sinovaginal bulbs contribute only to the lower vagina rather than the main genital ducts, and the genital tubercle forms external genitalia rather than internal ducts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"No testosterone, no AMH\" is the default female pathway — paramesonephric survives, mesonephric regresses. COMLEX: The baseline rule against which every DSD is compared.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — how these persisting paramesonephric ducts subsequently fuse to form the uterus and upper vagina","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"The upper portion of the vagina, including the fornices, derives from which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"The fused paramesonephric ducts","B":"The paired sinovaginal bulbs","C":"The pelvic urogenital sinus","D":"The regressing mesonephric ducts","E":"The developing genital tubercle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vaginal fornices and upper vaginal canal are of paramesonephric origin, formed once the fused caudal ends of the paramesonephric ducts canalize. The sinovaginal bulbs and urogenital sinus instead give rise to the lower portion of the vagina, the mesonephric ducts regress in the female rather than contributing to the vagina, and the genital tubercle forms the clitoris rather than the vaginal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The vagina's dual origin (upper = paramesonephric, lower = sinovaginal bulb/urogenital sinus) is a classic testable split. COMLEX: Useful for reasoning through which anomalies affect which vaginal segment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — how failure of sinovaginal bulb fusion instead produces a double or atretic lower vagina","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Failure of the paramesonephric ducts to fuse throughout their normal line of union produces which uterine anomaly in its most extreme, completely unfused form?","choices":{"A":"Uterus arcuatus","B":"Uterus didelphys","C":"Uterus bicornis","D":"Uterus bicornis unicollis","E":"Cervical atresia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When the paramesonephric ducts fail to fuse throughout their entire length, the result is a completely double uterus, uterus didelphys, the most extreme point on this spectrum of fusion failure. Uterus arcuatus reflects only slight midline indentation from a much milder, localized fusion defect, uterus bicornis reflects two horns entering a shared vagina rather than complete duplication, uterus bicornis unicollis reflects unilateral ductal atresia rather than bilateral non-fusion, and cervical atresia reflects bilateral atresia rather than a fusion failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rank uterine fusion anomalies along a single severity spectrum from mild indentation to complete duplication. COMLEX: A useful framework for imaging-based uterine anomaly questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — how one-sided rather than bilateral ductal atresia instead produces a rudimentary uterine horn","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Structural anomalies of the female genital tract are frequently accompanied by ipsilateral kidney anomalies because both systems share which embryonic origin?","choices":{"A":"The urogenital sinus","B":"The genital tubercle","C":"The sinovaginal bulb","D":"The allantois","E":"The urogenital ridge"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both the paramesonephric duct system and the metanephric/mesonephric kidney precursors arise from the same urogenital ridge, so a local developmental disruption there can derail both systems together and produce coincident renal and genital tract anomalies on the same side. The urogenital sinus gives rise to the bladder and lower vagina rather than the kidney, the genital tubercle forms external genitalia only, the sinovaginal bulb contributes only to the lower vagina, and the allantois is unrelated to either the genital ducts or the kidney precursors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal anomalies discovered alongside a Mullerian duct anomaly should prompt evaluation of the shared urogenital ridge origin. COMLEX: A frequently tested \"always check the kidneys\" clinical pearl for Mullerian anomalies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — the same ridge tissue whose disrupted induction instead produces bilateral renal agenesis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"Incomplete fusion of the urethral folds along the ventral aspect of the developing penis produces which anomaly?","choices":{"A":"Hypospadias","B":"Epispadias","C":"Micropenis","D":"A bifid penis","E":"Isolated chordee"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the paired urethral folds fail to fuse completely along the ventral surface, the urethral opening ends up somewhere along the ventral aspect of the penis rather than at the tip, defining hypospadias. Epispadias instead reflects a dorsal, not ventral, urethral opening most often linked to bladder exstrophy, micropenis reflects insufficient androgen-driven growth rather than a fusion defect, a bifid penis reflects splitting of the genital tubercle itself, and an isolated chordee without any urethral opening defect is a distinct, separate finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypospadias = ventral urethral opening from incomplete fold fusion; epispadias = dorsal opening tied to exstrophy — a frequently confused pair. COMLEX: Know the folds-fusion mechanism, not just the location, to avoid mixing these up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — the constant epispadias finding in bladder exstrophy, this anomaly's dorsal counterpart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"A 46,XY infant has ambiguous genitalia and is unable to convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone due to a missing enzyme. Which diagnosis fits this enzymatic defect?","choices":{"A":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","B":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","C":"Isolated anti-Mullerian hormone deficiency","D":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency","E":"Partial androgen insensitivity syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A missing 5-alpha-reductase enzyme prevents conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, so internal duct structures dependent on testosterone develop normally while the external genitalia, which depend on DHT, remain underdeveloped or ambiguous. Complete and partial androgen insensitivity syndromes instead reflect a receptor defect rather than an enzyme deficiency, congenital adrenal hyperplasia reflects an adrenal steroidogenesis defect rather than a peripheral conversion enzyme problem, and isolated AMH deficiency produces persistent Mullerian remnants rather than ambiguous external genitalia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-ARD is defined by a working androgen receptor but a missing conversion enzyme — internal male ducts intact, external genitalia ambiguous. COMLEX: Distinguish this enzyme defect from receptor-based AIS on labs and family history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — the same testosterone-versus-DHT hormone-target distinction that explains why internal ducts and external genitalia are affected differently here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme deficiency accounts for approximately 90% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia cases and is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait?","choices":{"A":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency","B":"21-hydroxylase deficiency","C":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","D":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency","E":"Cytochrome P450 oxidoreductase deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"21-hydroxylase deficiency accounts for roughly 90% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia cases and is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, causing masculinization of female external genitalia through excess androgen production. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency produces a similar but much rarer variant, 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency instead causes decreased sex steroid production at puberty rather than prenatal masculinization, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is a peripheral conversion enzyme defect unrelated to adrenal steroidogenesis, and cytochrome P450 oxidoreductase deficiency is a distinct, less common variant that can paradoxically cause either 46,XX or 46,XY DSD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 21-hydroxylase deficiency = the default answer for \"most common cause of CAH,\" at roughly 90% of cases. COMLEX: A high-yield percentage worth memorizing exactly for board-style recall questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — the same adrenal androgen excess pathway that can masculinize a 46,XX fetus despite an otherwise normal WNT4/DAX1 ovarian program","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":2,"stem":"A phenotypic female has a 46,XY karyotype, streak gonads, and no menstruation or secondary sexual characteristics at puberty. Genetic testing identifies a point mutation in a Y-chromosome gene. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"SOX9","B":"SF1","C":"WNT4","D":"DAX1","E":"SRY"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A point mutation or deletion of SRY produces XY gonadal dysgenesis (Swyer syndrome), in which the testis-determining cascade never begins, gonads remain streak-like, and a phenotypically female individual fails to develop secondary sexual characteristics at puberty because functioning ovaries never form either. SOX9 and SF1 act downstream of SRY and a defect there produces a milder or different phenotype depending on residual function, and WNT4 and DAX1 belong to the ovarian-determining pathway and are not Y-chromosome genes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Swyer syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis) is the classic clinical correlate of an SRY gene mutation. COMLEX: Distinguish this from androgen insensitivity syndrome, which has a normal SRY gene and functioning testes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how this contrasts with androgen insensitivity syndrome, where SRY and the testes are entirely normal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XX fetus has a normal WNT4 gene but is later found to have ambiguous genitalia at birth with mild virilization. Family history reveals maternal exposure to an androgenic medication in early pregnancy. Which statement best explains why virilization occurred despite an intact WNT4 pathway?","choices":{"A":"WNT4 itself must have been silenced despite normal testing","B":"SOX9 expression must have been present in this fetus","C":"DAX1 must have failed to inhibit SOX9 during gonadal development","D":"External genital tissue can respond to androgens from any source","E":"SRY expression must have occurred from an unidentified source"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The external genitalia contain androgen receptors and can virilize in response to circulating androgens from any source, maternal or fetal, independent of the internal WNT4/DAX1 ovarian program that governs the gonad itself, so a normal WNT4 pathway does not protect the external genitalia from an outside androgen source. WNT4 silencing despite normal testing would be an unsupported assumption rather than the explanation given intact WNT4 function, SOX9 expression would only occur if the testis-determining pathway itself were activated, DAX1 failing to inhibit SOX9 would require SOX9 activation in the first place, and unidentified SRY expression is not needed to explain external virilization from an external androgen source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: External genitalia virilization can occur independent of the internal gonadal determination pathway when androgens come from an outside source. COMLEX: A key concept for reasoning through maternal-androgen-exposure vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how this same external-genitalia androgen responsiveness underlies masculinization in 46,XX congenital adrenal hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the molecular master switches of genetic sex determination (including SRY, SOX9, SF1, WNT4, and DAX1), and predict the downstream gonadal phenotype when these genetic cascades are disrupted. Integrates molecular genetics (SRY upregulating SOX9/SF1 in males vs WNT4/DAX1 driving ovarian development in females) with gonadal phenotypes.","batch":2,"stem":"An embryology researcher knocks out a single gene in a genetically male (XY) mouse embryo. SRY and SOX9 are expressed normally and FGF9-mediated tubule recruitment occurs, but AMH and steroidogenic enzyme expression are markedly reduced in Sertoli and Leydig cells. Which gene was knocked out?","choices":{"A":"SOX9","B":"WNT4","C":"SF1","D":"DAX1","E":"FGF9"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SF1 is upregulated by SRY (directly or through SOX9) and works together with SOX9 to raise AMH and drive Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation, including steroidogenic enzyme expression, so its loss would reduce these outputs even with SRY, SOX9, and FGF9 all functioning normally. SOX9 is stated to be normal in this scenario, WNT4 and DAX1 belong to the opposing ovarian pathway, and FGF9 is likewise stated to be functioning normally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Use process of elimination in gene-cascade vignettes — SF1 is the node connecting the SRY/SOX9 signal to actual cell differentiation. COMLEX: A useful approach for any \"which gene is required for step X\" cascade question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — the analogous RET receptor node linking GDNF signaling to ureteric bud branching in kidney development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY infant has normal testes, normal testosterone and dihydrotestosterone levels, and normally formed external genitalia, but pelvic imaging incidentally reveals a uterus and uterine tubes. Which single hormone is most likely deficient?","choices":{"A":"Testosterone from the testes","B":"Anti-Mullerian hormone","C":"Dihydrotestosterone in tissue","D":"Luteinizing hormone release","E":"Estrogen from the ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Persistent Mullerian duct remnants (uterus and uterine tubes) in an otherwise normally virilized male with normal testosterone and DHT levels points specifically to isolated anti-Mullerian hormone deficiency, since AMH alone is responsible for paramesonephric duct regression. Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are both stated as normal and are responsible for external and internal male structures rather than duct regression, luteinizing hormone drives Leydig cell testosterone synthesis rather than duct regression, and estrogen is not part of the normal male hormonal profile at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent Mullerian remnants with otherwise normal male hormones and genitalia is the classic isolated AMH deficiency vignette. COMLEX: A useful pattern-recognition case distinguishing this from AIS, which instead shows undermasculinized genitalia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — how this contrasts with androgen insensitivity syndrome, where AMH is normal but the genitalia fail to virilize","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY infant has a small phallus with hypospadias, undescended testes, and normal Mullerian duct regression. Laboratory testing shows normal testosterone but low dihydrotestosterone, with a normal androgen receptor gene sequence. Which process is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cell synthesis of testosterone itself","B":"Sertoli cell secretion of anti-Mullerian hormone","C":"Androgen receptor binding of circulating testosterone","D":"Mesonephric duct responsiveness to circulating testosterone","E":"Peripheral conversion of testosterone to DHT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Normal testosterone with low dihydrotestosterone and an intact androgen receptor gene points to impaired peripheral conversion of testosterone to DHT by 5-alpha-reductase, which explains the ambiguous or undermasculinized external genitalia despite normally virilized internal duct structures that depend on testosterone rather than DHT. Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is intact given the normal testosterone level, Sertoli cell AMH secretion is intact given normal Mullerian regression, androgen receptor binding is intact given the normal receptor gene sequence, and mesonephric duct responsiveness to testosterone is preserved since internal duct virilization is not described as impaired here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal testosterone plus low DHT plus a normal receptor gene is the lab signature of 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. COMLEX: Distinguish this lab pattern from AIS, which instead shows normal or elevated testosterone with receptor-level unresponsiveness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — the full clinical diagnosis (5-alpha-reductase deficiency) that this specific hormonal pattern points to","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Synthesize the developmental timelines of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct systems, and predict the anatomical manifestations of disrupted hormone synthesis, receptor insensitivity, or signaling failure (specifically involving Testosterone, DHT, and Anti-Mullerian Hormone). Explores hormonal control over duct systems, detailing the effects of testosterone (Wolffian duct survival), DHT (external genitalia), and AMH (Mullerian regression).","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XX fetus with a normal ovarian program is examined at 10 weeks. Both the mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts are still present at this stage. Which statement correctly describes their expected fates by term?","choices":{"A":"The paramesonephric ducts persist and the mesonephric ducts regress","B":"The mesonephric ducts persist and the paramesonephric ducts regress","C":"Both duct systems persist and fuse into a single structure","D":"Both duct systems regress completely by term","E":"The paramesonephric ducts regress first and then the mesonephric ducts also regress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the female, the absence of testosterone causes the mesonephric ducts to degenerate, while the paramesonephric ducts, unopposed by anti-Mullerian hormone, persist and go on to form the uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina. Persistence of the mesonephric ducts with paramesonephric regression describes the male pattern rather than the female one, both ducts persisting and fusing does not reflect either sex's actual developmental outcome, both ducts regressing completely would leave no genital duct derivatives at all, and paramesonephric regression followed by mesonephric regression would leave the female without any functional duct derivatives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know both halves of the rule together — female development is not just \"absence of male development\" but an active, opposite outcome for each duct. COMLEX: A foundational fact for every subsequent duct-anomaly vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — what happens next as these persisting paramesonephric ducts fuse and canalize","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with primary infertility undergoes imaging that shows two separate uterine horns entering a single, shared vagina. Which developmental process was incomplete?","choices":{"A":"Canalization of the paired sinovaginal bulbs","B":"Regression of the paired mesonephric ducts","C":"Descent of the ovaries into the true pelvis","D":"Fusion of the caudal paramesonephric ducts","E":"Formation of the urorectal septum tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two uterine horns entering one shared vagina describes uterus bicornis, which results from incomplete fusion of the caudal portions of the paramesonephric ducts while the vagina, formed separately from the sinovaginal bulbs and lower duct segment, remains single. Sinovaginal bulb canalization failure would instead cause a vaginal rather than uterine anomaly, mesonephric duct regression is an unrelated, separate process in the female, ovarian descent is a distinct structure from the uterus itself, and the urorectal septum divides the cloaca rather than shaping paramesonephric duct fusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uterus bicornis = two horns, one vagina, from partial (not complete) paramesonephric fusion failure. COMLEX: A common imaging-based uterine anomaly question requiring precise fusion-defect localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — the same paramesonephric duct tissue whose persistence versus regression is governed by the WNT4/DAX1 pathway","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A teenager presents with progressively severe cyclic pelvic pain. Imaging reveals a single functioning uterine horn with a well-developed vagina, plus a separate, small, non-communicating rudimentary horn on the opposite side that fills with blood each cycle. Which developmental process explains the rudimentary horn?","choices":{"A":"Complete bilateral non-fusion of the paramesonephric ducts","B":"Partial atresia of one paramesonephric duct","C":"Failure of the sinovaginal bulbs to develop normally","D":"Complete regression of one paramesonephric duct","E":"Duplication of the paramesonephric duct on one side"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A small, non-communicating rudimentary horn alongside a well-developed side arises from partial atresia of one paramesonephric duct, leaving a blind, obstructed remnant that still accumulates menstrual blood each cycle. Complete bilateral non-fusion would instead produce two fully formed, communicating horns rather than one obstructed remnant, sinovaginal bulb failure would cause vaginal rather than uterine horn pathology, complete regression of one duct would leave no rudimentary horn to obstruct, and duplication of a duct is not a recognized cause of this anomaly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyclic pain without flow plus a rudimentary horn is the classic vignette for partial, one-sided paramesonephric duct atresia. COMLEX: A high-value pattern for recognizing obstructed Mullerian anomalies on the boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — screening for a coincident ipsilateral renal anomaly given the shared urogenital ridge origin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the physical fusion and canalization of the paramesonephric ducts and sinovaginal bulbs, correlate developmental failures with specific uterine and vaginal anomalies (e.g., uterus didelphys, uterus bicornis, vaginal atresia), and justify why these are frequently linked to ipsilateral renal abnormalities. Highlights the common embryologic origin from the urogenital ridge, explaining why structural female tract defects are strongly linked with renal abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with normal secondary sexual characteristics has primary amenorrhea and cyclic abdominal pain. Imaging shows a normal uterus but a short, blind-ending vaginal pouch with no connection to the external perineum. Which structure most likely failed to develop or canalize?","choices":{"A":"The sinovaginal bulbs","B":"The paramesonephric ducts","C":"The mesonephric ducts","D":"The greater vestibular glands","E":"The genital tubercle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A normal uterus with an obstructed, blind-ending lower vagina points to failure of the sinovaginal bulbs to develop or canalize, since these bulbs form the lower vagina independently of the paramesonephric-derived uterus and upper vagina. The paramesonephric ducts are functioning normally here given the normal uterus, the mesonephric ducts are unrelated to vaginal formation in the female, the greater vestibular glands are separate vestibular structures rather than part of the vaginal canal, and the genital tubercle forms the clitoris rather than the vaginal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal uterus plus an obstructed lower vagina localizes the defect to the sinovaginal bulbs, not the paramesonephric ducts. COMLEX: A key distinction for working through primary amenorrhea with cyclic pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — how the same bulbs failing to fuse (rather than failing to develop) instead produces a double vagina","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY infant has normal-appearing female external genitalia, a short blind-ending vagina, and no uterus or uterine tubes on imaging. Inguinal masses are palpated bilaterally. Hormone testing shows normal-to-elevated testosterone for a male infant. Which diagnosis best fits this combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"5-alpha-reductase enzyme deficiency","B":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","C":"Isolated anti-Mullerian hormone deficiency","D":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia variant","E":"46,XY gonadal dysgenesis syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal female-appearing external genitalia with palpable testes, an absent uterus (from intact AMH action), and normal-to-elevated testosterone that cannot act on tissue receptors together describe complete androgen insensitivity syndrome. 5-alpha-reductase deficiency instead produces ambiguous rather than fully female-appearing external genitalia, isolated AMH deficiency would leave a uterus present rather than absent, congenital adrenal hyperplasia causes virilization of a 46,XX fetus rather than undermasculinization of a 46,XY one, and 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis produces streak gonads rather than palpable descended-appearing testes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CAIS = normal female external genitalia, palpable testes (often inguinal), absent uterus, normal-to-high testosterone. COMLEX: A classic vignette often presenting as inguinal \"hernias\" in a phenotypic female.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — the intact AMH action and non-functional androgen receptor that together explain each individual finding here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with a 46,XX karyotype has an enlarged clitoris and partially fused labioscrotal folds. Laboratory studies show an elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone level. Which diagnosis is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"CAH caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency","B":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","C":"5-alpha-reductase enzyme deficiency","D":"Isolated anti-Mullerian hormone deficiency","E":"Ovotesticular disorder of sex development"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Virilized external genitalia in a 46,XX infant with an elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone level is the classic biochemical signature of 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the most common cause of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, in which the enzyme block shunts precursors toward excess androgen production. Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency both occur in 46,XY rather than 46,XX individuals, isolated AMH deficiency affects duct regression in males rather than causing virilization, and ovotesticular DSD would show ambiguous gonadal tissue on biopsy rather than the specific hormonal signature described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone in a virilized 46,XX newborn is the single highest-yield lab finding for 21-hydroxylase-deficient CAH. COMLEX: Know this exact lab marker, as it is commonly tested by name.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — how this adrenal androgen excess can virilize the external genitalia despite an entirely normal WNT4-driven ovarian program","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate clinical cases of Disorders of Sex Development (DSDs) and external genital malformations, including Hypospadias, Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS), Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH), and 5-alpha-Reductase Deficiency (5-ARD), by synthesizing genetic karyotypes, hormonal panels, and enzymatic pathways to formulate an accurate diagnosis. Demands synthesis of karyotype, hormone levels, and physical features to make precise differential diagnoses of complex intersex and congenital endocrine conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY adolescent raised as a female presents at puberty with primary amenorrhea. Examination reveals a small phallic structure that enlarged at puberty, previously undescended testes now palpable in the labioscrotal folds, and absent breast development. Which diagnosis best explains this pubertal change?","choices":{"A":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","B":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","C":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency","D":"Isolated anti-Mullerian hormone deficiency","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, ambiguous or female-appearing genitalia at birth can undergo unexpected virilization at puberty as rising testosterone levels partially compensate for the missing conversion enzyme, producing phallic growth and testicular descent alongside absent breast development, since estrogen is not the driving hormone here. Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome instead produces breast development at puberty from peripheral aromatization of testosterone with no virilization, congenital adrenal hyperplasia in a 46,XY individual would not present this way at puberty, isolated AMH deficiency does not affect pubertal virilization, and Klinefelter syndrome typically produces gynecomastia rather than virilization at puberty.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unexpected virilization at puberty in a child raised female is the signature presentation of 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. COMLEX: Contrast this directly with CAIS, which instead feminizes further at puberty via aromatization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — the testosterone-to-DHT conversion defect underlying this delayed, puberty-driven virilization","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Renal Histology","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"Which two structures together make up the renal corpuscle?","choices":{"A":"The glomerulus and the Bowman capsule","B":"The glomerulus and the proximal tubule","C":"The Bowman capsule and the collecting duct","D":"The afferent and efferent arterioles","E":"The mesangium and the distal tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The renal corpuscle consists of exactly two structures, the glomerulus (the capillary tuft that filters blood) and the surrounding Bowman capsule, which collects the resulting filtrate. The proximal tubule and collecting duct are downstream tubular segments rather than corpuscle components, the afferent and efferent arterioles supply and drain the glomerulus rather than forming the corpuscle itself, and the mesangium and distal tubule pair together two structures from different, non-corpuscle locations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The renal corpuscle = glomerulus + Bowman capsule, full stop — a foundational pairing worth memorizing exactly. COMLEX: The anchor fact underlying every subsequent glomerular filtration barrier question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — how disruption of this same corpuscle's filtration barrier produces proteinuria in nephrotic syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell type provides structural support for the glomerular capillary loops and can phagocytose filtered debris?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte cells","B":"Parietal epithelium","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Mesangial cells","E":"Principal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mesangial cells sit between the glomerular capillary loops, providing structural support through extracellular matrix production while also phagocytosing filtered debris and secreting cytokines. Podocytes form the outermost, visceral layer of the filtration barrier rather than sitting between capillary loops, parietal epithelial cells form the outer wall of Bowman capsule without a filtration or phagocytic role, endothelial cells line the capillary lumen itself rather than occupying the space between loops, and principal cells belong to the collecting duct rather than the glomerulus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mesangial cells are the glomerulus's support and cleanup crew — distinct from the filtration barrier's three true layers. COMLEX: A common distractor-trap cell type in filtration barrier questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — how mesangial cell proliferation contributes to certain glomerulonephritis patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the Bowman capsule is composed of simple squamous epithelial cells that provide structural support without contributing to filtration?","choices":{"A":"The visceral epithelial layer","B":"The parietal epithelial layer","C":"The endothelial layer of capillaries","D":"The basement membrane layer","E":"The mesangial matrix layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The outer, parietal layer of Bowman capsule consists of simple squamous epithelial cells that form a structural outer boundary around the urinary space but take no part in the actual filtration process. The visceral epithelial layer instead consists of podocytes, which are a functioning part of the filtration barrier, the endothelial layer belongs to the glomerular capillaries themselves rather than to Bowman capsule, the basement membrane is a distinct, separate structural layer of the filtration barrier, and the mesangial layer is not a component of Bowman capsule at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parietal (outer, structural only) versus visceral (inner, podocytes, functional) is a key Bowman capsule distinction. COMLEX: Useful for correctly labeling capsule layers on a histology image.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — how the visceral layer's podocytes instead form part of the three-layer filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature is characteristic of proximal convoluted tubule cells but absent from distal convoluted tubule cells?","choices":{"A":"A wide, clear tubular lumen space","B":"A simple cuboidal epithelial lining","C":"A brush border of apical microvilli","D":"Numerous cytoplasmic mitochondria","E":"Basolateral membrane infoldings"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Proximal convoluted tubule cells have a dense brush border of apical microvilli that markedly increases their absorptive surface area, a feature that distal convoluted tubule cells lack entirely, giving the DCT its comparatively clear, open lumen appearance. A wide, clear lumen is instead the DCT's own distinguishing feature rather than a PCT one, simple cuboidal epithelium and basolateral membrane infoldings are shared by both segments rather than distinguishing them, and numerous mitochondria are present in both PCT and DCT cells, just in greater density in the PCT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The brush border is the single feature that most reliably separates PCT from DCT on light microscopy. COMLEX: A classic histology-slide identification question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — how this brush border's expanded surface area supports bulk reabsorption of the glomerular filtrate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":1,"stem":"Which collecting duct cell type contains water channels that open in response to antidiuretic hormone?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Principal cells","C":"Proximal tubule cells","D":"Podocyte cells","E":"Mesangial cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Principal cells in the collecting duct express aquaporin water channels that insert into the apical membrane under antidiuretic hormone signaling, allowing variable water reabsorption, and these cells also respond to aldosterone by reabsorbing sodium and secreting potassium. Alpha-intercalated cells instead secrete hydrogen ions for acid-base regulation rather than responding to ADH, proximal tubule cells reabsorb water constitutively through a different, ADH-independent mechanism, podocytes belong to the filtration barrier rather than the collecting duct, and mesangial cells belong to the glomerulus rather than the collecting duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Principal cells = ADH-responsive aquaporins plus aldosterone-driven sodium reabsorption, a frequently paired dual function. COMLEX: A key cell type for reasoning through diabetes insipidus and diuretic mechanism questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — how this same ADH-aquaporin mechanism drives urine concentration physiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell type secretes net new hydrogen ions into the collecting duct lumen in response to acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Principal cells","C":"Proximal tubule cells","D":"Distal convoluted tubule cells","E":"Thick ascending limb cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha-intercalated cells in the collecting duct secrete hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen in response to acidosis and aldosterone, making them the primary acid-base regulating cell type of this segment. Principal cells instead regulate water and sodium/potassium balance rather than acid-base status, proximal tubule cells secrete hydrogen ions mainly to reclaim filtered bicarbonate rather than to add net new acid to the collecting duct lumen, distal convoluted tubule cells primarily reabsorb sodium, chloride, and calcium, and thick ascending limb cells reabsorb sodium, potassium, and chloride without this net acid-secreting function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-intercalated cells are the collecting duct's dedicated acid-secreting cell type — pair this with principal cells' water/electrolyte role. COMLEX: Useful for distal renal tubular acidosis reasoning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — how this acid secretion integrates into the kidney's overall acid-base regulatory physiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":1,"stem":"Which three layers together make up the wall of the ureter, bladder, and urethra?","choices":{"A":"Mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis","B":"Epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis","C":"Mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa","D":"Mucosa, muscularis, and mesothelium as one unit","E":"Serosa, muscularis, and endothelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urinary tract organs share a consistent three-layer wall organization: an inner mucosa (epithelium plus lamina propria), a muscularis of smooth muscle, and an outer layer that is either adventitia or serosa depending on whether the organ is retroperitoneal or intraperitoneal. A distinct submucosa is not a standard named layer of this system, listing epithelium and lamina propria as separate from mucosa splits a single layer into two rather than naming a third true layer, mesothelium is the specific lining of serosa rather than a separate third layer on its own, and endothelium lines blood vessels rather than the urinary tract wall itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mucosa/muscularis/adventitia-or-serosa is the universal three-layer template across the entire lower urinary tract. COMLEX: Apply this same template consistently across ureter, bladder, and urethra questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — the trigone's distinct mesodermal-then-endodermal mucosal origin within this same three-layer wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelial type lines the mucosa of the ureter, bladder, and the proximal urethra in both sexes?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","D":"Transitional epithelium","E":"Simple columnar epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Transitional epithelium, also called urothelium, is a specialized stratified epithelium impermeable to urine that lines the ureters, bladder, and proximal urethra, and it is uniquely able to adapt its shape to bladder distension. Simple cuboidal epithelium instead lines nephron tubular segments rather than the lower urinary tract, stratified squamous epithelium lines only the distal urethra near the external opening, pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the middle segments of the urethra rather than the ureters or bladder, and simple columnar epithelium is not a lining used anywhere in this system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Urothelium's defining trait is its ability to change shape (flatten) with distension — unique among epithelial types. COMLEX: The single most testable histology fact for identifying urinary tract tissue on a slide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — how chronic irritation of this same urothelium contributes to urothelial carcinoma risk","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with the male urethra, the female urethra differs in which structural respect?","choices":{"A":"Absence of transitional epithelium at the proximal end","B":"Presence of an additional prostatic segment","C":"A uniform stratified squamous epithelial lining","D":"Absence of an external urethral sphincter structure","E":"Absence of distinct anatomic segments along its course"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The female urethra is only 3 to 5 cm long, compared with roughly 20 cm in the male, and does not divide into the distinct preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, and penile segments seen in the male urethra, even though it shows a similar proximal-to-distal epithelial transition. The female urethra does begin with transitional epithelium proximally just like the male urethra, a prostatic segment is by definition a male-only structure that could not appear in a female comparison, the female urethra transitions through several epithelial types rather than a single uniform one, and the female urethra does have an external urethral sphincter where it crosses the urogenital diaphragm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Length and lack of named segments are the female urethra's defining differences from the male urethra, not epithelial type, which follows a similar transition pattern in both. COMLEX: A common exam trap is assuming an entirely different epithelial sequence rather than just a shorter, unsegmented tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — the shared urogenital sinus origin of both the male and female urethra","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"A kidney biopsy from a child with new-onset heavy proteinuria shows effacement (flattening) of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy, with an otherwise normal-appearing glomerular basement membrane and no significant electron-dense deposits. Which filtration barrier layer's dysfunction best explains the proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"The glomerular basement membrane layer","B":"The fenestrated endothelial layer","C":"The visceral epithelial layer","D":"The mesangial matrix","E":"The parietal epithelial layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Foot process effacement directly disrupts the podocyte slit diaphragms, the outermost layer of the filtration barrier facing the urinary space, so isolated podocyte injury without basement membrane or deposit changes points to podocyte-layer dysfunction as the cause of proteinuria, a pattern classic for minimal change disease. The glomerular basement membrane is described as normal-appearing here, ruling it out as the primary defect, the fenestrated endothelium mainly excludes cells rather than proteins and is not the layer implicated by foot process changes, the mesangial matrix provides structural support rather than functioning as a filtration barrier layer, and the parietal epithelial layer plays no role in filtration at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Foot process effacement with a normal GBM and no deposits is the classic minimal change disease pattern. COMLEX: A frequently tested electron microscopy finding in pediatric nephrotic syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — the full clinical and pathologic picture of minimal change disease that this isolated podocyte finding represents","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with heavy albuminuria is found to have loss of the normal negative charge along the glomerular basement membrane, with intact podocyte foot processes and normal capillary fenestrations. Which specific molecular feature of the basement membrane has most likely been lost?","choices":{"A":"Heparan sulfate proteoglycan levels","B":"Type IV collagen fibril content","C":"Laminin protein cross-link content","D":"Fenestral diaphragm protein makeup","E":"Slit diaphragm protein composition levels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The glomerular basement membrane's negative charge, which normally repels negatively charged plasma proteins such as albumin, comes from its heparan sulfate proteoglycan content, so loss of these specific molecules explains charge-selective albuminuria despite structurally intact podocytes and endothelium. Type IV collagen and laminin provide the membrane's structural scaffold rather than its negative charge, fenestral diaphragms are not a described feature of the endothelial fenestrations relevant to charge selectivity, and slit diaphragm proteins belong to the podocyte layer, which is explicitly stated to be intact here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heparan sulfate proteoglycans are the molecular basis of the GBM's charge barrier — loss of these, not structural collagen, explains a pure charge-selectivity defect. COMLEX: A detail-level fact worth knowing for distinguishing charge- versus size-selective proteinuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — how this same charge barrier works together with the size-selective fenestrations and slit diaphragms as one integrated filtration system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Renal Corpuscle and Glomerular Filtration Barrier: Identify the microanatomical components of the renal corpuscle, including the glomerulus (capillaries and mesangial cells), Bowman's capsule (visceral epithelial podocytes and parietal simple squamous cells), and the urinary space. Describe the three structural layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms) and explain how their physical and charge selectivity prevent protein loss in healthy kidneys.","batch":2,"stem":"Electron microscopy of a glomerulus shows normal podocyte foot processes and a normal basement membrane, but the capillary endothelial cells have lost their characteristic pores. Which consequence would be expected from this specific finding?","choices":{"A":"Loss of charge selectivity for albumin","B":"Loss of the basement membrane scaffold","C":"Disruption of slit diaphragm complexes","D":"Impaired bulk filtration of fluid","E":"Loss of mesangial phagocytic function"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The endothelial fenestrations exist specifically to allow bulk passage of fluid and small solutes out of the capillary lumen while still excluding blood cells, so their loss would primarily impair this basic filtration step rather than affecting charge selectivity or the other barrier layers, which are explicitly described as intact. Charge selectivity for albumin is mainly a basement membrane and podocyte property, with the endothelium contributing far less, the basement membrane's scaffold and the slit diaphragm complexes are both stated to be normal in this scenario, and mesangial phagocytic function is unrelated to the endothelial fenestrations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Match each filtration barrier layer to its specific job — fenestrations for bulk fluid/solute passage, GBM and podocytes for charge and size selectivity. COMLEX: A useful framework for isolating which layer a lab or imaging finding implicates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the full three-layer filtration barrier this single endothelial component contributes to","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops acute kidney injury after receiving intravenous contrast dye for a CT scan. Biopsy shows loss of the brush border and breakdown of the affected tubular cells, with tubular fluid leaking back into the peritubular capillaries. Which nephron segment is most directly affected?","choices":{"A":"The proximal tubule","B":"The thin descending limb of Henle","C":"The distal convoluted tubule","D":"The collecting duct","E":"The thick ascending limb of Henle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Contrast-induced acute tubular necrosis classically injures the proximal tubule, whose cells depend on a dense brush border and high mitochondrial density for their heavy reabsorptive workload, making them especially vulnerable to toxic and ischemic injury, with breakdown allowing backleak of tubular fluid. The thin descending limb is simple squamous epithelium without a brush border and is not the classic site of toxic injury described here, the distal convoluted tubule lacks a brush border to lose in the first place, the collecting duct is a relatively unspecialized, low-transport segment less vulnerable to this pattern of injury, and the thick ascending limb, while metabolically active, is not the segment defined by a brush border that could be lost.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Contrast dye and aminoglycosides are the classic causes of proximal-tubule-predominant acute tubular necrosis. COMLEX: Tie the brush border's high metabolic demand directly to this segment's vulnerability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — the proximal tubule's normal brush border and mitochondrial density that this injury pattern destroys","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient started on a diuretic develops hypercalcemia and hypocalciuria. Which nephron segment is the site of action of this diuretic class?","choices":{"A":"The proximal convoluted tubule","B":"The thick ascending limb","C":"The distal convoluted tubule","D":"The collecting duct segment","E":"The thin ascending limb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The distal convoluted tubule reabsorbs sodium and chloride along with calcium, and thiazide diuretics act at this segment, so blocking sodium/chloride reabsorption here paradoxically enhances calcium reabsorption, producing hypercalcemia with reduced urinary calcium loss. The proximal convoluted tubule handles bulk, largely unregulated reabsorption rather than this specific calcium-sparing effect, the thick ascending limb is the site of loop diuretic action and calcium handling there works oppositely, the collecting duct is targeted by potassium-sparing diuretics rather than agents with this calcium effect, and the thin ascending limb is a passive segment without a diuretic-targeted transport process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thiazide diuretics act on the DCT and are calcium-sparing, unlike loop diuretics acting on the thick ascending limb, which are calcium-wasting. COMLEX: A frequently tested diuretic-site-effect pairing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — how these same nephron segments contribute to the kidney's overall calcium and electrolyte regulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Tubular System Microanatomy: Differentiate the segment-specific epithelial linings of the nephron and collecting ducts under light microscopy, focusing on the prominent, eosinophilic brush border and high mitochondrial density of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) versus the clear, wider lumen of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Distinguish between water-permeable principal cells and acid-base regulating intercalated cells within the collecting ducts.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with central diabetes insipidus is given desmopressin, a synthetic ADH analog. Which nephron cell type must respond appropriately for this treatment to concentrate the urine?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-intercalated cells of the duct","B":"Principal cells of the duct","C":"Proximal tubule cells","D":"Distal convoluted tubule cells","E":"Thick ascending limb cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Desmopressin works by binding ADH receptors on principal cells, causing aquaporin water channels to insert into the apical membrane and increase water reabsorption from the collecting duct, which is the mechanism that concentrates the urine. Alpha-intercalated cells regulate acid-base balance rather than ADH-driven water reabsorption, proximal tubule cells reabsorb water constitutively regardless of ADH status, distal convoluted tubule cells reabsorb sodium, chloride, and calcium rather than mediating the ADH response, and thick ascending limb cells are impermeable to water and do not respond to ADH in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Desmopressin's target is specifically the principal cell's aquaporin channels, not any other collecting duct cell type. COMLEX: Central diabetes insipidus responds to desmopressin because the principal cells themselves are intact and can still respond.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — the full countercurrent multiplication system this ADH-driven water reabsorption depends on","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":2,"stem":"Cystoscopic biopsy of the bladder in a patient with a filling defect shows a stratified epithelium with dome-shaped superficial cells that appear flattened and stretched compared to a resting-state sample from the same patient. Which functional property of this epithelium does the flattened appearance demonstrate?","choices":{"A":"Its ability to secrete mucus for lubrication","B":"Its ability to transport sodium and water","C":"Its ability to regenerate after mechanical injury","D":"Its ability to change shape under distension","E":"Its ability to resist osmotic change via junctions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The flattened, stretched appearance of the superficial umbrella cells reflects transitional epithelium's hallmark ability to change shape and accommodate bladder filling while remaining an intact, impermeable barrier to urine. Mucus secretion is not the defining functional trait being demonstrated by this shape change, active sodium and water transport is not a recognized function of urothelium, rapid regeneration after injury is a separate reparative property rather than what a shape change under normal filling demonstrates, and while tight junctions do contribute to impermeability, the flattening itself specifically demonstrates shape adaptability rather than junctional resistance to osmotic change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The umbrella cell shape change under distension is urothelium's single most distinctive, testable functional trait. COMLEX: Recognize this on any bladder histology image showing full versus empty states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — this same urothelium's baseline dome-shaped appearance in the non-distended bladder","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":2,"stem":"A urethral biopsy taken from the segment surrounded by skeletal muscle of the deep perineal pouch, which provides voluntary control over urination, is most likely to show which epithelial lining?","choices":{"A":"Transitional epithelium of the urogenital sinus","B":"Stratified or pseudostratified columnar epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium of the penile glans","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium of the renal tubule","E":"Simple columnar epithelium of the collecting duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The segment described, surrounded by the skeletal muscle forming the external urethral sphincter, is the membranous urethra, which is lined by stratified or pseudostratified columnar epithelium rather than the transitional epithelium seen more proximally. Transitional epithelium instead lines the preprostatic and prostatic segments proximal to this point, stratified squamous epithelium lines only the distal, penile urethra near the external opening, simple cuboidal epithelium lines renal tubular segments rather than the urethra, and simple columnar epithelium is not part of the male urethral lining sequence at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The membranous urethra is defined anatomically by the external sphincter and histologically by its columnar (not transitional) lining. COMLEX: A useful landmark-to-histology pairing for urethral segment identification.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the transitional epithelium immediately proximal to this segment in the prostatic urethra","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lower Urinary Tract Histology and Epithelial Transitions: Describe the three-layer organization (mucosa, muscularis, and adventitia or serosa) of the ureters, bladder, and urethra. Explain how transitional epithelium (urothelium) and its superficial dome-shaped umbrella cells adapt to significant volume changes by flattening during bladder distension. Trace the mucosal epithelial transitions along the segments of the male urethra versus the shorter female urethra.","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy taken from the most distal segment of the penile urethra, immediately continuous with the skin of the glans, would be expected to show which epithelial lining?","choices":{"A":"Transitional epithelium","B":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","C":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","D":"Simple squamous epithelium","E":"Stratified squamous epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The most distal portion of the penile urethra transitions to stratified squamous epithelium as it becomes continuous with the keratinized skin of the glans penis, in contrast to the pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the proximal penile urethra. Transitional epithelium is found only in the more proximal preprostatic and prostatic segments, pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the proximal penile and membranous segments rather than this most distal point, simple cuboidal epithelium belongs to renal tubular segments, and simple squamous epithelium lines structures like capillaries and the thin loop of Henle limbs rather than any urethral segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Track the male urethra's full epithelial sequence proximal to distal — transitional, then columnar, then squamous — as one continuous progression. COMLEX: A complete, orderly sequence worth memorizing end to end rather than as isolated facts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the columnar epithelium of the more proximal membranous and penile urethral segments","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Renal Anatomy & Body Fluid Compartments","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron subtype has glomeruli located near the corticomedullary junction and long loops of Henle descending deep into the medulla?","choices":{"A":"Superficial cortical nephrons","B":"Midcortical nephrons","C":"Subcapsular nephrons","D":"Juxtamedullary nephrons","E":"Outer-cortical nephrons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Juxtamedullary nephrons make up roughly one-eighth of all nephrons and have glomeruli sited close to the corticomedullary junction, with long loops of Henle that plunge deep into the medulla and are essential for generating the medullary concentration gradient.","boardPrep":"A \"long loop of Henle reaching deep medulla\" descriptor on boards points to a juxtamedullary nephron, not a cortical one.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — this long-loop architecture is the anatomic prerequisite for the countercurrent multiplication mechanism developed in SDL 8","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron population makes up the majority of nephrons and has glomeruli located in the outer cortex with short loops of Henle confined to the outer medulla?","choices":{"A":"The deep juxtamedullary nephrons","B":"The mid-cortical nephrons","C":"The inner medullary nephrons","D":"The papillary duct nephrons","E":"Superficial cortical nephrons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Superficial cortical nephrons constitute the majority of the roughly one million nephrons per kidney, with glomeruli positioned in the outer cortex and short loops of Henle that dip only into the outer medulla.","boardPrep":"Majority + outer cortex + short loop = superficial cortical nephron.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the shorter loop length here is the anatomic reason cortical nephrons contribute less to the medullary osmotic gradient than juxtamedullary nephrons","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":1,"stem":"Which peritubular vascular specialization runs alongside the loop of Henle in juxtamedullary nephrons and supports countercurrent osmotic exchange?","choices":{"A":"Arcuate veins","B":"Vasa recta","C":"Interlobar veins","D":"Cortical veins","E":"Radial veins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In juxtamedullary nephrons, the peritubular capillary network takes the specialized form of the vasa recta, straight vessels that descend and ascend parallel to the loop of Henle and allow osmotic exchange without washing out the medullary gradient.","boardPrep":"Vasa recta = the peritubular capillary specialization unique to juxtamedullary (not cortical) nephrons.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — vasa recta blood flow rate is the physiologic variable manipulated in SDL 8's countercurrent exchanger models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":1,"stem":"Using the 60-40-20 rule, total body water is estimated at what percentage of body weight in an average adult?","choices":{"A":"60%","B":"40%","C":"20%","D":"75%","E":"50%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Total body water averages about 60% of body weight, though the exact percentage varies with gender and adiposity since fat contains relatively little water.","boardPrep":"The 60-40-20 rule: TBW = 60%, ICF = 40%, ECF = 20% of body weight — the numbers name the fractions in that fixed order.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — this 60% baseline is the reference value adjusted downward for adiposity and gender in the same objective's calculations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":1,"stem":"Under the 60-40-20 rule, which body fluid compartment corresponds to the \"40\" and represents two-thirds of total body water?","choices":{"A":"Extracellular fluid","B":"Plasma compartment","C":"Interstitial fluid","D":"Transcellular fluid","E":"Intracellular fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intracellular fluid is the largest single compartment, averaging 40% of body weight and accounting for about two-thirds of total body water, distinguishing it from the smaller extracellular compartment.","boardPrep":"ICF is two-thirds of TBW; ECF is the remaining one-third — a ratio worth memorizing outright.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — ICF's dominant ion composition is the subject of Objective 3 in this same SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":1,"stem":"A woman with a higher percentage of body fat than a man of equal body weight would be expected to have which change in total body water percentage?","choices":{"A":"A higher percentage, since adipose tissue is comparatively water-rich","B":"A lower percentage, since adipose tissue holds relatively little water","C":"No change, since biological sex has no bearing on fluid distribution patterns","D":"A lower percentage, but this pattern is restricted to elderly patients","E":"A higher percentage, but this pattern is restricted to pregnant patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adipose tissue contains comparatively little water, so individuals with a higher proportion of body fat, including women on average compared to men, have a lower total body water percentage relative to body weight.","boardPrep":"Higher adiposity always shifts total body water percentage downward, regardless of the reason for the increased fat proportion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — this adiposity adjustment is why pediatric and elderly patients also require age-specific TBW percentage corrections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":1,"stem":"Which ion is the dominant cation of intracellular fluid?","choices":{"A":"Potassium ion","B":"Sodium ion","C":"Calcium ion","D":"Magnesium ion","E":"Chloride ion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Potassium is the major intracellular cation, maintained at a high concentration inside cells by the Na+/K+-ATPase, in contrast to sodium's dominance in the extracellular fluid.","boardPrep":"ICF cation = potassium; ECF cation = sodium — this asymmetry is the basis of the resting membrane potential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — this potassium gradient is the specific driver of resting membrane potential covered mechanistically in SDL 7","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anions predominate in extracellular fluid?","choices":{"A":"Phosphate and protein","B":"Chloride and bicarbonate","C":"Sulfate and phosphate","D":"Chloride and phosphate","E":"Bicarbonate and sulfates"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extracellular fluid has chloride and bicarbonate as its major anions, whereas intracellular fluid instead relies on organic phosphates and proteins as its dominant anions.","boardPrep":"ECF anions: chloride and bicarbonate. ICF anions: protein and organic phosphate. Do not swap these pairs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — bicarbonate's ECF dominance here links directly to the acid-base buffering physiology explored in SDL 10","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":1,"stem":"The Gibbs-Donnan effect across the capillary wall results primarily from the presence of which large, impermeant molecule confined to the vascular compartment?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial collagen","B":"Red blood cell lipid","C":"Plasma protein","D":"Circulating urea","E":"Circulating glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Plasma proteins are too large to cross the capillary wall, and this impermeant negative charge is what generates the Gibbs-Donnan effect, altering the equilibrium distribution of small diffusible ions between plasma and interstitial fluid.","boardPrep":"The Gibbs-Donnan effect exists because plasma protein cannot cross the capillary wall — no protein impermeance, no Gibbs-Donnan effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — this same plasma-protein impermeance is the physical basis of oncotic pressure discussed in glomerular filtration physiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"Which marker is used in the dilution method to measure extracellular fluid volume?","choices":{"A":"Tritiated water","B":"Antipyrine","C":"Albumin","D":"Mannitol","E":"Evans blue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mannitol distributes only in the extracellular fluid because it cannot cross cell membranes, making its volume of distribution a direct measurement of ECF volume.","boardPrep":"Mannitol and inulin are the classic ECF-volume markers because both are confined to the extracellular space.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — this same ECF-restricted property is why mannitol is also used therapeutically as an osmotic diuretic","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"Which marker distributes throughout total body water and is used to measure its volume by the dilution method?","choices":{"A":"Deuterium oxide","B":"Mannitol solution","C":"Inulin solution","D":"Radioactive albumin","E":"Evans blue dye tracer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deuterium oxide, along with tritiated water and antipyrine, freely crosses all body fluid compartments, so its volume of distribution equals total body water.","boardPrep":"D2O/THO/antipyrine cross every compartment membrane, so they measure TBW rather than any single subcompartment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — TBW measured this way is the value used as the starting point for the ICF-by-subtraction calculation later in this objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"Total blood volume can be estimated indirectly from plasma volume using which additional measured value?","choices":{"A":"Osmolality","B":"Filtration rate","C":"Hematocrit","D":"Venous pressure","E":"Albumin level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because plasma constitutes the noncellular fraction of blood, total blood volume can be calculated indirectly from measured plasma volume once the hematocrit, the cellular fraction, is known.","boardPrep":"Blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − hematocrit) — hematocrit is the missing piece linking plasma volume to total blood volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — this hematocrit-based calculation is the indirect method contrasted with the direct dilution measurements earlier in the same objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":2,"stem":"During an episode of severe hypotension, the renal medulla suffers ischemic injury disproportionate to the renal cortex. Which structural feature of the medullary vasculature best explains this vulnerability?","choices":{"A":"Vasa recta blood flow is low relative to medullary oxygen demand","B":"Arcuate arteries lack smooth muscle and cannot autoregulate","C":"Medullary blood supply arises directly from the interlobar arteries","D":"Medullary capillaries have fenestrations that collapse under low pressure","E":"Interlobar arteries branch at right angles, creating turbulent flow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vasa recta supply the medulla with comparatively sluggish blood flow to preserve the countercurrent osmotic gradient, and this same low-flow design leaves the medulla operating near the edge of its oxygen supply, making it disproportionately susceptible to ischemic injury during hypotension.","boardPrep":"Medullary ischemia vulnerability on boards traces back to the vasa recta's intentionally low flow, not a lack of autoregulation or vessel abnormality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — this same low-flow vulnerability is the anatomic basis for acute tubular necrosis patterns discussed later in the renal pathology sequence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares urine-concentrating capacity between two isolated nephron populations. The population with the greater maximal concentrating ability also has glomeruli positioned nearest which structure?","choices":{"A":"The outer renal capsule surface","B":"The corticomedullary junction","C":"The interlobular arterial branches","D":"The minor renal calyx structure","E":"The outermost cortical surface region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nephrons with the greatest urine-concentrating capacity are the juxtamedullary nephrons, whose glomeruli sit near the corticomedullary junction and whose long loops of Henle descend deep into the medulla, generating the steep osmotic gradient needed for maximal urine concentration.","boardPrep":"Concentrating ability tracks directly with loop-of-Henle length, which tracks with how close the glomerulus sits to the corticomedullary junction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — this concentrating-capacity difference is what SDL 8 builds on when contrasting maximal urine osmolality across nephron populations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the microanatomic and vascular differences between superficial cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons, evaluating how their structural locations dictate their respective roles in urine concentration and susceptibility to ischemic injury.","batch":2,"stem":"Blood exits the glomerular capillaries of a cortical nephron through the efferent arteriole. Which vessel does it enter next under normal cortical (non-juxtamedullary) anatomy?","choices":{"A":"The specialized vasa recta capillaries descending into the medulla","B":"The arcuate vein without any capillary bed","C":"The renal vein without an intervening capillary bed","D":"Ordinary peritubular capillaries around the tubule","E":"The interlobular vein, with no intervening capillary bed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In superficial cortical nephrons, efferent arteriolar blood flows into an ordinary peritubular capillary network surrounding the cortical tubule segments, distinct from the specialized vasa recta that instead supply juxtamedullary nephrons.","boardPrep":"Cortical nephron efferent flow goes to ordinary peritubular capillaries; only juxtamedullary efferent flow goes to vasa recta.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — this second capillary bed is what makes the renal circulation a portal-like system, distinguishing it from single-capillary-bed organs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-kg lean, muscular man is compared with a 70-kg woman of the same age who has a higher percentage of body fat. Applying gender-adjusted 60-40-20 standards, which statement correctly describes their expected total body water values?","choices":{"A":"The man's total body water percentage is higher than the woman's","B":"Their total body water percentages are identical because their weights are equal","C":"The woman's total body water percentage is higher because estrogen promotes water retention","D":"Total body water cannot differ between two people of the same body weight","E":"The man's total body water percentage is lower because muscle contains less water than fat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because adipose tissue holds relatively little water while lean muscle mass holds proportionally more, a lean muscular man is expected to have a higher total body water percentage of body weight than a woman with greater adiposity at the same total weight, consistent with the gender-adjusted 60-40-20 standards.","boardPrep":"At equal body weight, more muscle and less fat always predicts a higher TBW percentage — this is a composition effect, not a sex-hormone effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — this composition-driven variability is why the 60-40-20 rule is described as a population average rather than a fixed constant","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-kg patient is estimated to have a total body water of 36 L using standard 60-40-20 percentages. Applying the same rule, what is this patient's expected extracellular fluid volume?","choices":{"A":"24 L","B":"18 L","C":"12 L","D":"9 L","E":"6 L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Extracellular fluid corresponds to the \"20\" in the 60-40-20 rule, or 20% of body weight; for a 60-kg patient this is 0.20 × 60 kg = 12 L, distinct from the intracellular fluid volume of 24 L (40% of body weight).","boardPrep":"Apply each percentage to body weight directly rather than to total body water — ECF is 20% of weight, not 20% of TBW.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — this same 12 L ECF figure is the quantity that subdivides further into plasma and interstitial fluid per the three-quarters/one-quarter split","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Utilize the gender-adjusted standards of the 60-40-20 rule to calculate total body water, extracellular fluid, and intracellular fluid volume, explaining how differences in biological sex and adiposity alter these volume percentages.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician recalls that extracellular fluid subdivides into two subcompartments in an approximate 3-to-1 ratio. Which subcompartment corresponds to the larger fraction?","choices":{"A":"Plasma compartment","B":"Interstitial fluid","C":"Intracellular fluid","D":"Transcellular fluid","E":"Lymphatic fluid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extracellular fluid is divided into interstitial fluid, comprising roughly three-quarters of the ECF, and plasma, comprising the remaining one-quarter, a ratio that is clinically important because interstitial fluid is not directly accessible for measurement.","boardPrep":"ECF splits roughly 3:1 into interstitial fluid : plasma — interstitial fluid is always the larger of the two.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — this 3:1 subdivision is exactly the relationship exploited in Objective 4's indirect calculation of interstitial fluid volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":2,"stem":"When plasma and interstitial fluid are compared across the capillary wall, the Gibbs-Donnan effect predicts which relationship for small permeant cations such as sodium?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial fluid has a slightly higher concentration of small cations than plasma","B":"Plasma and interstitial fluid maintain identical small-cation concentrations","C":"Plasma has a slightly higher concentration of small cations than interstitial fluid","D":"Small cations are entirely excluded from interstitial fluid","E":"Small cation concentration equalizes gradually over several hours of exchange"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because impermeant plasma protein carries a net negative charge, the Gibbs-Donnan effect draws additional small permeant cations into plasma to maintain electroneutrality, so plasma holds a slightly higher concentration of small cations than interstitial fluid.","boardPrep":"Protein's negative charge in plasma pulls extra small cations in — plasma cation concentration is always slightly higher than interstitial fluid's.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — this same electroneutrality requirement is mirrored, in the opposite direction, for small anions in the next concept covered by this objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":2,"stem":"Following the same Gibbs-Donnan logic applied to cations, what is the expected relationship between plasma and interstitial fluid concentrations of small diffusible anions such as chloride?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial fluid has a slightly lower concentration of small anions than plasma","B":"Small anions distribute identically between plasma and interstitial fluid","C":"Small anions cannot cross the capillary wall under any condition","D":"Interstitial fluid actively excludes small anions from its composition","E":"Plasma has a slightly lower concentration of small anions than interstitial fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because impermeant plasma protein's negative charge repels other anions to preserve electroneutrality, small diffusible anions such as chloride equilibrate at a slightly lower concentration in plasma than in interstitial fluid, the mirror image of the cation relationship.","boardPrep":"Protein's charge repels anions from plasma just as it attracts cations into plasma — opposite direction, same electroneutrality principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — together with the cation relationship, this anion asymmetry is the complete definition of the Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium at the capillary wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the characteristic ionic profile of intracellular fluid versus extracellular fluid, and analyze how the Gibbs-Donnan effect alters the equilibrium concentration of diffusible ions across the capillary wall.","batch":2,"stem":"A cell membrane separates intracellular fluid, rich in potassium and organic phosphate, from extracellular fluid, rich in sodium and chloride. This ionic asymmetry is maintained primarily by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Passive diffusion down existing concentration gradients","B":"Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium across the cell membrane","C":"Facilitated diffusion through aquaporins","D":"Active transport by the Na+/K+-ATPase","E":"Simple osmosis driven by protein concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The characteristic ionic asymmetry between intracellular and extracellular fluid, with potassium and organic phosphate predominating inside the cell and sodium and chloride predominating outside, is actively maintained against their concentration gradients by the Na+/K+-ATPase pump.","boardPrep":"ICF/ECF ionic asymmetry is actively maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase; the Gibbs-Donnan effect is only a small passive contributor here and mainly describes the plasma-interstitial fluid asymmetry across the capillary wall.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — this active-transport distinction is the key point separating cell-membrane ion handling from the passive Gibbs-Donnan phenomenon at the capillary wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient is injected with a known amount of a fluid-volume marker. After equilibration, a portion of the injected marker has been excreted in urine before the plasma concentration is measured. To calculate the correct volume of distribution, which value should be used in the numerator of the volume formula?","choices":{"A":"The full amount injected, without any correction","B":"The amount excreted in urine by itself","C":"The amount injected multiplied by the plasma concentration","D":"The amount injected divided by the equilibration time","E":"The dose given minus whatever the kidneys removed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dilution-method formula calculates volume as the amount of marker present in the body, meaning the amount originally injected minus any amount lost to urinary excretion during equilibration, divided by the measured plasma concentration.","boardPrep":"Volume of distribution = (amount injected − amount excreted) ÷ plasma concentration; forgetting the excretion correction is the most common calculation error.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — this same excretion-corrected formula is applied identically whether the marker used measures total body water or extracellular fluid volume","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher has already measured a patient's total body water using deuterium oxide and extracellular fluid volume using mannitol. Which compartment volume can now be calculated indirectly without an additional injected marker?","choices":{"A":"Total plasma volume measured","B":"Intracellular fluid volume","C":"Interstitial fluid volume","D":"Transcellular fluid volume","E":"Total circulating blood volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intracellular fluid volume cannot be measured directly with an injected marker, but once total body water and extracellular fluid volume are known, it is calculated indirectly as the difference between the two.","boardPrep":"ICF volume = TBW − ECF volume; this subtraction is the only way ICF is ever obtained since no marker distributes exclusively within intracellular fluid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — this same subtraction logic underlies why interstitial fluid volume is likewise calculated as ECF minus plasma volume rather than measured directly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of the indicator dilution method to calculate direct body fluid volumes (utilizing mannitol and radioactive albumin) and utilize indirect mathematical relationships to estimate interstitial fluid volume and total blood volume scaled by hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-kg man is injected with 100 mCi of tritiated water. During a 2-hour equilibration period he excretes 10% of the dose in urine, and the final plasma concentration is 0.213 mCi/100 mL. What is his approximate total body water, and how does this value compare with the normal range of 50 to 70% of body weight?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 42.3 L, about 65% of body weight, within the normal range","B":"Approximately 42.3 L, about 65% of body weight, above the normal range","C":"Approximately 90 L, about 138% of body weight, an impossible result","D":"Approximately 21.3 L, about 33% of body weight, below the normal range","E":"Approximately 100 L, equal to the full injected dose volume without correction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The corrected dose is 100 mCi minus 10% excreted, or 90 mCi, divided by the plasma concentration of 0.213 mCi/100 mL, yielding a volume of approximately 42.3 L; since 42.3 kg out of 65 kg body weight is about 65%, this falls squarely within the normal 50-70% range for total body water.","boardPrep":"Work the dilution formula step by step: correct the dose for excretion first, then divide by concentration, then convert the resulting volume to a percentage of body weight to check it against the normal range.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — this identical calculation, substituting mannitol for tritiated water, is how extracellular fluid volume is obtained in the companion worked example for this objective","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":6,"title":"SDL 6 — Fluid Shifts & Multi-Compartment Dynamics","questions":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":1,"stem":"In the steady state, how does intracellular fluid osmolarity compare to extracellular fluid osmolarity?","choices":{"A":"Intracellular osmolarity is characteristically higher","B":"Extracellular osmolarity is characteristically higher","C":"The two compartment osmolarities match precisely","D":"The two osmolarities differ by a fixed, constant amount","E":"They are equal during particular circadian phases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the steady state, intracellular fluid osmolarity equals extracellular fluid osmolarity throughout the body, because water shifts freely across cell membranes to eliminate any osmotic gradient.","boardPrep":"ICF and ECF osmolarity are equal at steady state — this equality is the reference point every fluid-shift disturbance question perturbs and then restores.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this steady-state equality is the baseline value that every disturbance in Objective 2 and 3 displaces and then re-establishes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":1,"stem":"A disturbance increases extracellular fluid osmolarity above intracellular fluid osmolarity. In which direction will water shift to restore equilibrium?","choices":{"A":"From intracellular fluid into extracellular fluid","B":"From extracellular fluid into intracellular fluid","C":"Water will not shift because membranes are impermeant to water","D":"Water shifts preferentially into the transcellular compartment","E":"Water shifts equally in both directions with no net change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When extracellular osmolarity rises above intracellular osmolarity, water moves down its osmotic gradient out of the cells and into the extracellular fluid until the two compartments reach a new, equal osmolarity.","boardPrep":"Water always shifts toward the compartment with the higher osmolarity — a rule that predicts the direction of every fluid shift in this SDL.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this exact direction-of-shift rule is what determines the ICF volume changes tabulated for each disturbance in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":1,"stem":"Which solute is described as confined to the extracellular fluid compartment because it does not readily cross cell membranes?","choices":{"A":"Urea molecule","B":"Mannitol sugar","C":"Ethanol molecule","D":"Water itself","E":"Carbon dioxide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Solutes such as NaCl, NaHCO3, and large sugars like mannitol are confined to the extracellular fluid because they cannot readily cross cell membranes, so any of these added to the body increases only ECF solute content.","boardPrep":"Membrane-impermeant solutes like mannitol and NaCl stay in the ECF; freely permeant solutes like urea and ethanol distribute across both ICF and ECF.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this same ECF-confinement property is exactly what makes mannitol a valid dilution-method marker for ECF volume in SDL 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with severe diarrhea loses a large volume of isosmotic gastrointestinal fluid. What happens to extracellular fluid osmolarity and intracellular fluid volume?","choices":{"A":"ECF osmolarity is unchanged, and ICF volume is unchanged, since isosmotic fluid carries no osmotic gradient into the ECF","B":"ECF osmolarity rises as free water is preferentially lost, pulling water out of cells and shrinking ICF volume","C":"Cells swell as ECF becomes more dilute than normal, so ICF volume expands while osmolarity falls","D":"ECF osmolarity stays the same, yet ICF volume still shrinks because isosmotic loss draws volume from each fluid compartment equally","E":"ECF osmolarity rises from the fluid loss itself, while ICF volume holds steady since no osmotic gradient forms"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the fluid lost in diarrhea is isosmotic to the ECF, ECF osmolarity does not change, so there is no osmotic gradient to drive a water shift, and intracellular fluid volume remains unchanged even though ECF volume decreases.","boardPrep":"Isosmotic volume contraction (diarrhea, burns): ECF volume down, ECF osmolarity unchanged, ICF volume unchanged — no water shift occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this same isosmotic pattern is why diarrhea appears in Objective 4's diagnostic table as increased hematocrit with unchanged osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":1,"stem":"A person lost in the desert loses hyposmotic sweat over several hours without replacing fluids. What happens to ECF osmolarity, and in which direction does water shift?","choices":{"A":"ECF osmolarity decreases, and water shifts from ECF into ICF","B":"ECF osmolarity is unchanged, and no water shift occurs","C":"ECF osmolarity increases, and ICF volume stays essentially the same because the fluid lost was confined to the vascular space","D":"ECF osmolarity rises briefly, then returns to its prior value with no lasting water shift","E":"ECF osmolarity increases, and water shifts from ICF into ECF"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sweat is hyposmotic relative to the ECF, so losing it removes proportionally more water than solute, raising ECF osmolarity; this transiently makes ECF osmolarity exceed ICF osmolarity, driving water from ICF into ECF until the two compartments re-equilibrate.","boardPrep":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction (sweating, fever, diabetes insipidus): losing hyposmotic fluid raises ECF osmolarity and pulls water out of cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this same water-shift-out-of-cells mechanism is why hyperosmotic contraction leaves hematocrit unchanged despite ECF volume loss, per Objective 4","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with adrenal insufficiency excretes excess NaCl in the urine due to aldosterone deficiency. What type of volume contraction results, and what happens to ICF volume?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction, with a decrease in ICF volume","B":"Hyposmotic volume contraction, with an increase in ICF volume","C":"Isosmotic volume contraction, with no change in ICF volume","D":"Hyposmotic volume contraction, with a decrease in ICF volume","E":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction, with an increase in ICF volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aldosterone deficiency causes excess renal NaCl loss, lowering ECF osmolarity below ICF osmolarity, so water shifts from ECF into ICF, producing hyposmotic volume contraction with a decreased ECF volume and an increased ICF volume.","boardPrep":"Hyposmotic volume contraction (adrenal insufficiency): ECF solute loss lowers ECF osmolarity, pulling water into cells and expanding ICF volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this same aldosterone-deficiency mechanism links directly to the mineralocorticoid physiology covered in the endocrine block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient receives an infusion of isotonic NaCl solution. What happens to plasma protein concentration and hematocrit?","choices":{"A":"Both increase due to hemoconcentration","B":"Plasma protein decreases but hematocrit is unchanged","C":"Plasma protein is unchanged but hematocrit decreases","D":"Both remain unchanged because the infusion is isotonic","E":"Both decrease together due to simple dilution"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isotonic NaCl is confined to the ECF and produces no osmolarity change, so there is no water shift between compartments, but the added ECF volume dilutes both plasma protein concentration and hematocrit, decreasing each.","boardPrep":"Isosmotic volume expansion (isotonic saline infusion): ECF volume up, osmolarity unchanged, both hematocrit and plasma protein decrease from dilution alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this dilutional decrease is the mirror image of diarrhea's dilutional increase in hematocrit and plasma protein from Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient ingests a large quantity of dry NaCl. What happens to ICF volume, and what happens to hematocrit?","choices":{"A":"ICF volume increases from osmotic water influx, and hematocrit increases from ECF concentration","B":"ICF volume decreases from osmotic water efflux, and hematocrit is unchanged from an offsetting cellular shift","C":"ICF volume increases from osmotic water influx, and hematocrit decreases from ECF dilution","D":"ICF volume decreases from osmotic water efflux, and hematocrit decreases from ECF dilution","E":"ICF volume decreases from osmotic water efflux, and hematocrit increases from ECF concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested NaCl raises ECF osmolarity above ICF osmolarity, drawing water out of cells into the ECF and decreasing ICF volume; the resulting ECF volume expansion dilutes the blood and, together with the water shift out of red blood cells, decreases hematocrit.","boardPrep":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion (high NaCl intake): water shifts out of cells, so ICF volume falls while ECF volume and dilution both push hematocrit down.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this same water-out-of-cells direction is the opposite shift from hyperosmotic volume contraction in Objective 2, useful for side-by-side comparison","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with SIADH retains excess water in proportion to existing compartment volumes. If an extra 3 L of water is retained, how is it distributed between ECF and ICF?","choices":{"A":"1.5 L to ECF and 1.5 L to ICF","B":"1 L to ECF and 2 L to ICF","C":"2.2 L to ECF and 0.8 L to ICF","D":"3 L to ECF and 0 L to ICF","E":"2.5 L to ECF and 0.5 L to ICF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retained water in SIADH distributes throughout total body water in direct proportion to each compartment's original volume; since ECF is one-third and ICF is two-thirds of total body water, of an extra 3 L, 1 L is added to ECF and 2 L to ICF.","boardPrep":"Water retained in SIADH splits by the same one-third/two-thirds ECF/ICF ratio as total body water itself — a direct application of the 60-40-20 rule's subdivisions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this proportional-distribution rule is the same one-third/two-thirds ECF/ICF split established for total body water in SDL 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"On a Darrow-Yannet diagram, which axis represents the extracellular fluid osmolarity of a given disturbance?","choices":{"A":"The vertical axis label","B":"The horizontal axis","C":"Neither axis; a separate labeled point","D":"Both axes simultaneously","E":"The diagonal axis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Darrow-Yannet diagram plots extracellular fluid osmolarity on the vertical axis against fluid compartment volume on the horizontal axis, allowing a single disturbance to be represented as a shift in both dimensions at once.","boardPrep":"Darrow-Yannet diagrams: vertical axis = osmolarity, horizontal axis = volume — knowing which axis is which lets you read any labeled diagram correctly under time pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — reading this diagram correctly is what lets Objective 4 map each of Objective 2 and 3's six disturbances onto a single visual framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"Which diagnostic indicator remains unchanged in both hyperosmotic volume contraction and hyposmotic volume expansion, despite opposite changes in ECF volume?","choices":{"A":"Plasma sodium level","B":"Plasma protein level","C":"ECF osmolarity","D":"Hematocrit level","E":"ICF volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction leaves hematocrit unchanged because the concentrating effect of ECF loss is offset by a decrease in individual red blood cell volume, and hyposmotic volume expansion likewise leaves hematocrit unchanged because dilution of red blood cell concentration is offset by an increase in individual red blood cell volume — hematocrit is therefore the one indicator unchanged in both of these otherwise opposite disturbances.","boardPrep":"Hematocrit is unchanged in exactly two of the six disturbances (hyperosmotic contraction and hyposmotic expansion) because two opposing effects on red blood cells cancel out — memorize which two.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — this hematocrit-cancellation logic is the same red-blood-cell concentration-versus-volume tradeoff explained for hyperosmotic contraction in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":1,"stem":"Which diagnostic indicator increases in isosmotic volume contraction but decreases in isosmotic volume expansion?","choices":{"A":"The extracellular fluid osmolarity value","B":"Plasma sodium concentration","C":"Plasma protein concentration","D":"ICF volume amount","E":"Plasma chloride concentration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Plasma protein concentration rises in isosmotic volume contraction because ECF volume loss concentrates the protein remaining in the vascular space, and falls in isosmotic volume expansion because added ECF volume dilutes it — while ECF osmolarity and plasma sodium concentration stay unchanged in both isosmotic states by definition.","boardPrep":"In BOTH isosmotic disturbances, osmolarity and plasma sodium stay flat by definition — only volume-dependent indicators like plasma protein and hematocrit move.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — this protein-concentration/dilution pairing is exactly what distinguishes diarrhea from isotonic saline infusion on the diagnostic table in Objectives 2 and 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-kg patient has a body fluid osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L and total body water of 39 L. A disturbance adds 780 mOsm of an ECF-confined solute with no change in total body water. What is the new total body water osmolarity?","choices":{"A":"300 mOsm/L","B":"320 mOsm/L","C":"340 mOsm/L","D":"310 mOsm/L","E":"360 mOsm/L"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Old total osmoles equal 300 mOsm/L times 39 L, or 11,700 mOsm; adding 780 mOsm gives 12,480 mOsm, and dividing by the unchanged 39 L total body water yields a new osmolarity of 320 mOsm/L, which will be the same throughout ECF and ICF once water redistributes.","boardPrep":"Work solute-addition problems by computing total osmoles before and after, then dividing by the (possibly changed) total body water — never skip straight to a compartment volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this same total-osmoles-then-divide method is applied specifically to ECF volume, rather than total body water, in the worked examples of Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":2,"stem":"A cell membrane separates two fluid compartments that are initially at equal osmolarity. A solute that cannot cross the membrane is then added only to the extracellular side. Once a new steady state is reached, which statement is correct?","choices":{"A":"ECF and ICF osmolarities remain unequal indefinitely","B":"ECF and ICF osmolarities are again equal, and ICF volume has returned to its original value","C":"ICF volume is unchanged because the solute cannot cross the membrane","D":"ECF volume decreases while ICF volume increases","E":"ECF and ICF osmolarities are again equal, but ICF volume has decreased"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adding an impermeant solute only to the ECF transiently raises ECF osmolarity above ICF osmolarity, so water shifts from ICF into ECF until the two osmolarities are equal again; because water, not the solute, moved across the membrane, ICF volume ends up decreased while ECF volume increases.","boardPrep":"An impermeant solute added to one compartment always re-equalizes osmolarity by moving water, never by moving the solute itself across the membrane.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this exact impermeant-solute logic is what produces the ICF volume decrease seen in hyperosmotic volume contraction and expansion alike","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the biophysical principles governing fluid distribution and osmotic equilibrium between the intracellular and extracellular compartments, predicting how water shifts across cell membranes to maintain osmotic equality in response to acute fluid and solute disturbances.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient's plasma sodium concentration is 145 mEq/L, plasma glucose is 90 mg/dL, and blood urea nitrogen is 14 mg/dL. Using the standard plasma osmolarity formula, what is the calculated plasma osmolarity?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 300 mOsm/L","B":"Approximately 250 mOsm/L","C":"Approximately 145 mOsm/L","D":"Approximately 350 mOsm/L","E":"Approximately 200 mOsm/L"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Plasma osmolarity equals 2 times plasma sodium plus glucose divided by 18 plus BUN divided by 2.8, which is (2 × 145) + (90 ÷ 18) + (14 ÷ 2.8) = 290 + 5 + 5 = 300 mOsm/L, the normal reference value for body fluid osmolarity.","boardPrep":"Calculated plasma osmolarity = 2×Na+ + Glucose/18 + BUN/2.8 — memorize the two conversion divisors (18 for glucose, 2.8 for BUN), not just the sodium doubling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — this calculated osmolarity formula is the clinical bedside estimate of the osmolarity that drives the compartment water shifts in SDL 5 Obj 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":2,"stem":"Before a marathon, a runner has a total body water of 36 L, an ECF volume of 12 L, and a body fluid osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L. She loses 3 L of sweat with an osmolarity of 150 mOsm/L and drinks no fluids. Assuming all lost solute came from ECF, what is her new ECF volume?","choices":{"A":"12.0 L","B":"9.0 L","C":"13.6 L","D":"10.0 L","E":"3.6 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"New total osmoles equal (300 × 36) − (150 × 3) = 10,800 − 450 = 10,350 mOsm, divided by the new total body water of 33 L for a new osmolarity of 313.6 mOsm/L; new ECF osmoles equal (300 × 12) − 450 = 3,150 mOsm, and dividing by 313.6 mOsm/L gives a new ECF volume of 10.0 L.","boardPrep":"Hyposmotic fluid loss (sweating) always leaves ECF volume higher than a naive subtraction would predict, because water shifting from ICF into ECF partially replaces the lost volume — here, 12 − 3 = 9 L naively, but the true answer is 10.0 L.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this worked marathon example is the fully solved version of the hyperosmotic volume contraction principle stated qualitatively in the quick-recall batch","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with severe diarrhea has an increased hematocrit and increased plasma protein concentration, but no change in ECF osmolarity. Which of the six body fluid disturbances best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Isosmotic volume contraction","B":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction","C":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","D":"Isosmotic volume expansion","E":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of isosmotic fluid from the ECF, as occurs in diarrhea, produces no change in ECF osmolarity and therefore no water shift between compartments, so the reduced ECF volume alone concentrates red blood cells and plasma protein, raising both hematocrit and plasma protein concentration — the defining pattern of isosmotic volume contraction.","boardPrep":"Increased hematocrit + increased plasma protein + unchanged osmolarity is the signature triad of isosmotic volume contraction, distinguishing it from the hyperosmotic and hyposmotic contraction states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this same triad is exactly what a Darrow-Yannet diagram displays as a leftward horizontal shift with no vertical movement, per Objective 4","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction (modeling clinical states such as severe diarrhea, profuse sweating, and adrenal insufficiency), tracing their specific impacts on extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes and osmolarity.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with hyperosmotic volume contraction has an increased plasma protein concentration but an unchanged hematocrit. What best explains the unchanged hematocrit despite the reduced ECF volume?","choices":{"A":"Red blood cell production increases sharply to fully compensate for the fluid loss from ECF contraction","B":"Red blood cells are completely unaffected by any changes in surrounding plasma osmolarity","C":"Water shifting out of red blood cells shrinks them and offsets the concentrating effect from ECF loss","D":"Plasma protein and red blood cells are concentrated to exactly the same proportional degree","E":"The kidney selectively retains red blood cells during states of volume contraction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of ECF volume alone would concentrate red blood cells and raise hematocrit, but the accompanying rise in ECF osmolarity draws water out of the red blood cells, shrinking their individual volume; these two opposing effects, increased concentration and decreased cell volume, offset each other and leave hematocrit unchanged.","boardPrep":"Whenever hematocrit is unchanged despite a clear ECF volume change, look for two opposing red-blood-cell effects (concentration versus cell volume) that cancel rather than assuming no disturbance occurred.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — this same two-opposing-effects logic reappears, in the opposite direction, to explain unchanged hematocrit in hyposmotic volume expansion (SIADH)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":2,"stem":"A woman with an ICF volume of 28 L, ECF volume of 14 L, and plasma osmolarity of 295 mOsm/L eats potato chips containing 300 mmol of NaCl (osmotic coefficient 2), all of which enters her ECF. What is her new ECF volume in the new steady state?","choices":{"A":"15.3 L","B":"14.0 L","C":"16.6 L","D":"12.7 L","E":"17.3 L"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Total body water is 42 L; ingested osmoles equal 300 mmol × 2 = 600 mOsm; new total osmoles equal (295 × 42) + 600 = 12,990 mOsm, giving a new osmolarity of 12,990 ÷ 42 = 309.3 mOsm/L; new ECF osmoles equal (295 × 14) + 600 = 4,730 mOsm, and dividing by 309.3 mOsm/L gives a new ECF volume of 15.3 L.","boardPrep":"Solute-ingestion problems: add the ingested osmoles to BOTH the total-body-water osmole count and the ECF osmole count (since the solute is ECF-confined), then divide each by its own new volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this worked potato-chip example is the fully solved version of the hyperosmotic volume expansion principle stated qualitatively in the quick-recall batch","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with SIADH has decreased plasma protein concentration but an unchanged hematocrit. What best explains the unchanged hematocrit despite the dilutional decrease in plasma protein?","choices":{"A":"Red blood cells are entirely and completely impermeant to the excess retained free water","B":"Antidiuretic hormone directly and permanently stabilizes red blood cell membrane integrity fully","C":"Dilution lowers red blood cell count per volume while inward water shift raises cell volume","D":"Plasma protein and red blood cells respond to the dilution at completely different rates","E":"The retained water remains confined entirely within the plasma compartment itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In SIADH, retained water dilutes the concentration of red blood cells in the blood, which would lower hematocrit, but the accompanying decrease in ECF osmolarity also draws water into the red blood cells, increasing their individual volume; these two offsetting effects leave hematocrit unchanged even though plasma protein concentration falls from dilution alone.","boardPrep":"SIADH's unchanged hematocrit mirrors hyperosmotic contraction's unchanged hematocrit — both involve two opposing red-blood-cell effects, just with the water shift running in opposite directions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this same offsetting-effects logic for hematocrit was first introduced for hyperosmotic volume contraction in Objective 2, just with the water shift reversed","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the pathophysiology of isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume expansion (modeling clinical states such as isotonic saline infusion, high sodium intake, and SIADH), deconstructing how unregulated water or solute retention alters compartment dynamics.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient receiving a large infusion of isotonic NaCl is compared to a patient with SIADH. Both have expanded ECF volume, but which distinguishing laboratory finding differs between the two conditions?","choices":{"A":"ECF volume increases with SIADH but stays fixed with isotonic saline","B":"ECF osmolarity is unchanged with isotonic saline but decreased with SIADH","C":"Plasma protein concentration increases with isotonic saline but decreases with SIADH","D":"Hematocrit level increases equally with both clinical conditions","E":"ICF volume decreases equally with both clinical conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isotonic saline infusion adds ECF-confined, isosmotic fluid, so ECF osmolarity does not change and ICF volume is unaffected, whereas SIADH causes retention of free water that is hyposmotic relative to the body fluids, lowering ECF osmolarity and drawing water into cells to expand ICF volume as well — the osmolarity change is the key distinguishing feature.","boardPrep":"Isosmotic expansion (isotonic saline) changes only ECF volume; hyposmotic expansion (SIADH) changes ECF osmolarity too and expands ICF volume as a consequence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — this osmolarity-based distinction is exactly what separates isosmotic and hyposmotic expansion on the Darrow-Yannet diagram in Objective 4","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"A Darrow-Yannet diagram shows a disturbance plotted as a horizontal shift to the left with no vertical movement. Which of the six disturbances does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction","B":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","C":"Isosmotic volume expansion","D":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion","E":"Isosmotic volume contraction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A purely horizontal leftward shift on a Darrow-Yannet diagram, with no vertical (osmolarity) movement, represents a pure decrease in ECF volume without any change in osmolarity, which is exactly the pattern of isosmotic volume contraction as seen in diarrhea.","boardPrep":"On a Darrow-Yannet diagram, purely horizontal movement means an isosmotic disturbance; any vertical movement signals a hyperosmotic or hyposmotic disturbance instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — reading this axis logic correctly is the visual equivalent of the isosmotic-contraction triad worked through numerically in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"Comparing isosmotic volume expansion to hyperosmotic volume expansion on the diagnostic indicator table, which finding is shared by both disturbances?","choices":{"A":"Both show increased ECF osmolarity","B":"Both show decreased hematocrit","C":"Both show decreased ICF volume","D":"Both show increased plasma protein concentration","E":"Both show unchanged ECF volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isosmotic volume expansion decreases hematocrit through simple dilution, while hyperosmotic volume expansion decreases hematocrit through both dilution and a water shift out of red blood cells, so hematocrit decreases in both disturbances even though their effects on ICF volume and ECF osmolarity differ.","boardPrep":"Both expansion states dilute the blood and lower hematocrit; they differ specifically in whether ECF osmolarity and ICF volume also change, which is what separates isosmotic from hyperosmotic mechanisms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — this shared hematocrit-decrease finding is what makes isosmotic and hyperosmotic expansion easy to confuse without also checking the plasma sodium/osmolarity column","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Model multi-compartment fluid shifts using Darrow-Yannet diagrams, analyzing how specific volume disturbances affect systemic diagnostic indicators, including plasma sodium concentration, plasma protein concentration, and hematocrit.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with decreased plasma sodium concentration, increased ECF volume, and increased ICF volume. Which disturbance and diagnostic pattern does this match?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion, as seen in high NaCl intake","B":"Isosmotic volume expansion, as seen in isotonic saline infusion","C":"Hyposmotic volume contraction, as seen in adrenal insufficiency","D":"Hyposmotic volume expansion, as seen in SIADH","E":"Isosmotic volume contraction, as seen in diarrhea"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Decreased plasma sodium concentration reflects decreased ECF osmolarity, and both ECF and ICF volumes are increased when excess retained water distributes across both compartments in proportion to their size, exactly the pattern produced by SIADH's hyposmotic volume expansion.","boardPrep":"Low plasma sodium plus increased volume in both compartments is a distinct signature of hyposmotic expansion (SIADH), not to be confused with the isolated ECF volume increase of isosmotic or hyperosmotic expansion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — this same low-plasma-sodium-plus-dual-compartment-expansion pattern reappears as the classic hyponatremia workup finding in the renal fluid-regulation SDLs ahead","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Renal Clearance Mechanics & Glomerular Markers","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the correct renal clearance equation for a substance x?","choices":{"A":"Cx = [P]x / ([U]x × urine flow rate)","B":"Cx = ([U]x + [P]x) × urine flow rate","C":"Cx = ([U]x × [P]x) × urine flow rate","D":"Cx = urine flow rate / ([U]x × [P]x)","E":"Cx = ([U]x × urine flow rate) / [P]x"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal clearance equals the urinary excretion rate of a substance, urine concentration times urine flow rate, divided by its plasma concentration, giving units of volume per unit time (e.g., mL/min).","boardPrep":"Clearance = ([U] × V̇) / [P] — the plasma concentration always sits alone in the denominator, never multiplied or added.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this exact formula, applied specifically to inulin, is what defines GFR itself in Objective 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"For a fixed plasma concentration of a substance, how does its renal clearance change as urinary excretion rate increases?","choices":{"A":"Clearance increases, but more slowly than excretion rate","B":"Clearance increases proportionally with excretion rate","C":"Clearance remains constant regardless of excretion rate","D":"Clearance becomes undefined at high excretion rates","E":"Clearance rises briefly, then plateaus at a fixed maximum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal clearance is the ratio of urinary excretion rate to plasma concentration, so for a fixed plasma concentration, clearance rises directly in proportion to any increase in urinary excretion rate.","boardPrep":"Clearance tracks excretion rate directly (for fixed plasma concentration) — more excreted per minute always means more plasma cleared per minute.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this proportionality is exactly what keeps inulin clearance (GFR) constant even as urine flow rate changes, per the worked hydration example","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"A person drinks a large volume of water, and urine flow rate rises from 1 mL/min to 5 mL/min. Urine inulin concentration falls proportionally by dilution. What happens to calculated GFR?","choices":{"A":"GFR stays unchanged since the formula adjusts for the concentration drop","B":"GFR increases fivefold, tracking the same fivefold rise seen in urine flow rate","C":"The diluted inulin concentration causes the formula to calculate a reduced GFR","D":"GFR becomes impossible to calculate without a corresponding plasma inulin sample","E":"GFR increases temporarily before returning to baseline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because urine inulin concentration falls by dilution in exact proportion to the rise in urine flow rate, the product of the two (urinary inulin excretion) stays the same, so calculated GFR (inulin clearance) remains constant despite the large change in urine flow rate.","boardPrep":"Increased water intake dilutes urine concentration proportionally to the flow-rate increase, leaving the calculated clearance of a purely filtered marker unchanged — a classic \"trick\" scenario on exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this same excretion-rate-times-concentration invariance is the mathematical reason clearance calculations are robust to hydration status","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio (Cx / Cinulin) of exactly 1.0. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and partially reabsorbed by the tubule","B":"It is filtered and partially secreted by the tubule","C":"It is too large to cross the glomerular filtration barrier","D":"It is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted","E":"It is filtered and secreted at a rate exceeding its reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A clearance ratio of exactly 1.0 means the substance's clearance equals inulin's clearance, which only occurs when the substance is freely filtered and then neither reabsorbed nor secreted by the renal tubule, making it a glomerular marker just like inulin.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio = 1.0 defines a glomerular marker: filtered, and nothing more happens to it in the tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this exact glomerular-marker property is what makes inulin (ratio always 1.0 by definition) the reference standard used throughout Objective 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio of 0.3. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and then partially secreted","B":"It is not filtered across the glomerular capillaries","C":"It is filtered and then partially reabsorbed","D":"It undergoes net secretion exceeding filtration","E":"It is neither filtered nor reabsorbed nor secreted"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A clearance ratio below 1.0 means the substance's clearance is lower than inulin's, which occurs when a filtered substance is subsequently reabsorbed by the tubule, removing some of the filtered load before it reaches the urine.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio < 1.0 signals net tubular reabsorption; the further below 1.0, the greater the fraction reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this reabsorption-indicating ratio is exactly the pattern seen for Na+, glucose, and amino acids in the worked clearance-ratio examples","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio of 4.5, higher than inulin's. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and then secreted by the tubule","B":"It is filtered and then reabsorbed by the tubule","C":"It is not filtered across the glomerular capillaries","D":"It is reabsorbed and secreted in exactly equal amounts","E":"It bypasses renal handling and undergoes hepatic clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A clearance ratio greater than 1.0 means the substance's clearance exceeds inulin's, which can only occur if the renal tubule adds additional substance to the urine beyond what was filtered, meaning the substance undergoes net tubular secretion.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio > 1.0 signals net tubular secretion — organic acids like PAH and organic bases are the classic examples with the highest ratios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this secretion-indicating ratio is the basis for using PAH clearance to estimate renal plasma flow, since it approaches complete single-pass extraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is inulin, rather than creatinine, considered the ideal exogenous marker for measuring GFR?","choices":{"A":"Inulin is produced endogenously through skeletal muscle metabolism processes","B":"Inulin undergoes no reabsorption or secretion after filtration","C":"Inulin measurements require timed urine samples and cannot be obtained from plasma","D":"Inulin persists in the plasma for a notably shorter duration","E":"Inulin undergoes substantial reabsorption after filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inulin is freely filtered and then neither reabsorbed nor secreted, making its clearance exactly equal to GFR, whereas creatinine is freely filtered but also secreted to a small extent by the renal tubule, causing creatinine clearance to slightly overestimate true GFR.","boardPrep":"Inulin clearance = GFR exactly; creatinine clearance slightly overestimates GFR because of its small secreted component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same small secretion-driven overestimate is the specific limitation clinicians accept in exchange for creatinine's practical convenience","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main practical advantage of using creatinine clearance over inulin clearance to estimate GFR in clinical practice?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine clearance is consistently more accurate than inulin clearance","B":"Creatinine is filtered but is not secreted by the tubule","C":"Creatinine measurement requires a specialized blood assay","D":"Creatinine clearance stays fixed despite changes in GFR","E":"Creatinine is endogenous and does not require infusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Creatinine is produced endogenously by muscle metabolism at a roughly constant rate, so its plasma concentration can be measured without infusing an exogenous substance, unlike inulin, which must be continuously infused to maintain a steady plasma level.","boardPrep":"The clinical tradeoff: inulin is the accurate but impractical exogenous marker; creatinine is the convenient endogenous marker with a small, accepted overestimate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same endogenous convenience is why creatinine, not inulin, is the marker used in Objective 4's BUN/creatinine ratio calculations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"Both BUN and serum creatinine are used clinically to estimate GFR. What property do they share that makes this possible?","choices":{"A":"Both are actively secreted by the proximal tubule","B":"Both are synthesized directly within the glomerulus","C":"Both freely cross cell membranes throughout the entire body","D":"Both are cleared from plasma through glomerular filtration","E":"Both are reabsorbed completely regardless of GFR"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BUN and serum creatinine can both be used to estimate GFR because both urea and creatinine are filtered across the glomerular capillaries, so a decrease in GFR reduces their filtration and causes both to accumulate in the plasma.","boardPrep":"BUN and creatinine both rise with falling GFR because both depend on glomerular filtration to be cleared — the difference between them lies in what happens after filtration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this shared filtration-dependence is the premise behind interpreting the BUN/creatinine ratio in Objective 4","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with volume contraction from severe vomiting has a BUN/creatinine ratio of 25:1. What type of azotemia does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic renal azotemia from parenchymal damage","B":"Prerenal azotemia from hypoperfusion","C":"Postrenal azotemia from urinary tract obstruction","D":"Combined prerenal and intrinsic renal azotemia","E":"No azotemia, since the ratio is within normal limits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio greater than 20:1, combined with a history of volume contraction, is the classic signature of prerenal azotemia, in which decreased renal perfusion drives increased proximal tubular reabsorption of urea disproportionately more than creatinine.","boardPrep":"BUN/creatinine ratio > 20:1 with a volume-contraction history = prerenal azotemia — one of the most frequently tested lab-ratio patterns on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this elevated ratio is the direct biochemical signature of the proximal urea reabsorption mechanism traced in this same objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic kidney disease has both an elevated BUN and an elevated serum creatinine, but the BUN/creatinine ratio remains around 12:1. What does this ratio pattern suggest?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic renal parenchymal dysfunction","B":"Prerenal azotemia from volume contraction","C":"Postrenal obstruction of the urinary tract","D":"A laboratory measurement error","E":"Resolving prerenal azotemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio below 15:1 despite elevations in both values reflects intrinsic renal parenchymal disease, where reduced GFR raises both urea and creatinine together without the disproportionate proximal urea reabsorption seen in volume-contraction states.","boardPrep":"BUN/creatinine ratio < 15:1 with both values elevated = intrinsic renal disease — the ratio distinguishes the mechanism, not just the presence, of azotemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — the absence of disproportionate urea reabsorption here is what separates intrinsic renal disease from prerenal azotemia's volume-driven mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"In prerenal azotemia from volume contraction, why does BUN increase more than serum creatinine, despite both being filtered at the glomerulus?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine is actively secreted during volume contraction, offsetting its filtered-load decrease","B":"Urea is produced in far larger quantities during states of volume contraction","C":"Proximal tubular reabsorption disproportionately affects urea relative to creatinine","D":"Creatinine is metabolized into a nontoxic byproduct during hypovolemia","E":"Urea filtration rises while creatinine filtration simultaneously falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volume contraction increases proximal tubular reabsorption of essentially all filtered solutes, and because urea (unlike creatinine) is substantially reabsorbed under normal conditions, this enhanced reabsorption raises BUN disproportionately more than serum creatinine, widening the ratio.","boardPrep":"The BUN/creatinine ratio widens in volume contraction specifically because urea, not creatinine, is subject to reabsorption that intensifies with hypovolemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same enhanced-proximal-reabsorption mechanism is the direct downstream consequence of the volume-contraction states studied in SDL 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient produces 2 mL/min of urine with a substance x concentration of 60 mg/mL, and the plasma concentration of x is 2 mg/mL. What is the renal clearance of substance x?","choices":{"A":"30 mL/min","B":"60 mL/min","C":"120 mL/min","D":"15 mL/min","E":"2 mL/min"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow rate, divided by plasma concentration: (60 mg/mL × 2 mL/min) / 2 mg/mL = 120 mg/min / 2 mg/mL = 60 mL/min.","boardPrep":"Always multiply urine concentration by flow rate FIRST to get the excretion rate in mg/min, then divide by plasma concentration — skipping this order is the most common calculation error.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this same calculation, applied specifically to inulin, becomes the GFR measurement developed further in Objective 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher measures albumin clearance as approximately zero and glucose clearance as also approximately zero in a healthy person. Do these two findings reflect the same underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Yes — both substances fail to cross the glomerular filtration barrier","B":"Yes — both substances undergo full reabsorption after filtration","C":"No — albumin undergoes full reabsorption while glucose fails filtration completely","D":"No — albumin fails filtration while glucose is filtered but fully reabsorbed","E":"No — both are secreted at rates that exactly cancel their filtration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albumin's near-zero clearance reflects the fact that it is normally not filtered across the glomerular capillaries at all, whereas glucose's near-zero clearance reflects a different mechanism: glucose is freely filtered but then completely reabsorbed by the proximal tubule under normal plasma concentrations.","boardPrep":"A clearance near zero does not always mean the same thing — always ask whether the substance failed to be filtered, or was filtered and then fully reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this same reabsorption mechanism for glucose is why glucosuria appears only once plasma glucose exceeds the tubular reabsorptive maximum, covered later in the renal physiology sequence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"In a 24-hour period, a patient produces 1.44 L of urine while receiving an inulin infusion. Urine inulin concentration is 150 mg/mL and plasma inulin concentration is 1 mg/mL. What is this patient's GFR?","choices":{"A":"144 mL/min","B":"100 mL/min","C":"120 mL/min","D":"1.5 mL/min","E":"150 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Urine flow rate is 1.44 L over 1440 minutes, or 1.0 mL/min; GFR equals inulin clearance, (150 mg/mL × 1.0 mL/min) / 1 mg/mL = 150 mL/min.","boardPrep":"Always convert a 24-hour urine volume to mL/min (divide by 1440 minutes) before plugging it into the clearance equation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this exact 150 mL/min inulin clearance value is the denominator used to calculate the Na+ clearance ratio worked out in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient's GFR measured by inulin clearance is 150 mL/min. Urine Na+ concentration is 200 mEq/L, plasma Na+ concentration is 140 mEq/L, and urine flow rate is 1.0 mL/min. What is the clearance ratio for Na+, and what does it indicate?","choices":{"A":"About 1.4, indicating that Na+ is secreted by the tubule","B":"About 0.01, indicating that 99% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed","C":"About 0.5, indicating that half of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed","D":"About 1.0, indicating that Na+ is handled as a pure glomerular marker","E":"About 0.01, indicating that just 1% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Na+ clearance is (200 mEq/L × 1.0 mL/min) / 140 mEq/L ≈ 1.43 mL/min; dividing by the inulin clearance of 150 mL/min gives a clearance ratio of about 0.01, meaning only 1% of the filtered Na+ load is excreted and 99% must have been reabsorbed by the tubule.","boardPrep":"A clearance ratio near 0.01 for a heavily reabsorbed solute like Na+ translates directly to \"about 99% reabsorbed\" — always subtract the excreted fraction from 100% to state the reabsorbed fraction correctly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this same 1% filtered-Na+-excretion figure is the quantitative basis for fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) used clinically to assess volume status","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A drug has a renal clearance approximately equal to that of PAH (para-aminohippuric acid) under the same conditions. What can be concluded about this drug's renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and secreted with near-complete extraction from plasma","B":"It is freely filtered with no significant tubular reabsorption or secretion","C":"It is filtered and reabsorbed to a similar degree as glucose","D":"It is metabolized within the renal tubule before excretion","E":"It bypasses filtration and is secreted directly by the collecting duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PAH is filtered and secreted so efficiently that it is nearly completely extracted from plasma in a single pass, so a drug whose clearance approaches that of PAH must also be filtered and secreted with near-complete extraction, and its clearance approximates renal plasma flow. A clearance equal to inulin's would indicate filtration without net tubular handling, reabsorption would lower clearance, and tubular metabolism or collecting duct secretion cannot produce a clearance near renal plasma flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAH is a near-maximal secretion benchmark whose clearance approximates renal plasma flow; a drug with a similar clearance is handled by both filtration and highly efficient secretion. COMLEX: Reinforces why PAH, not inulin, is used to estimate renal plasma flow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — PAH's near-complete single-pass extraction is exactly the property that makes its clearance a surrogate measure of renal plasma flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A new investigational solute is found to have a clearance ratio that changes from 0.4 to 0.7 as its plasma concentration rises, while remaining well below 1.0 throughout. What does this pattern most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"The solute switches from reabsorption to secretion as plasma concentration rises","B":"The solute stops being filtered once its plasma concentration rises past a threshold","C":"The solute undergoes reabsorption by a saturable process, less complete as concentration rises","D":"The solute's clearance ratio is being measured incorrectly, since ratios must remain perfectly constant","E":"The solute becomes a pure glomerular marker once its plasma concentration is high"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A clearance ratio that stays below 1.0 but rises toward 1.0 as plasma concentration increases is consistent with a saturable tubular reabsorption transporter that reabsorbs a smaller fraction of the filtered load as that load grows, similar in principle to the glucose transport system.","boardPrep":"A clearance ratio moving toward 1.0 with rising plasma concentration, while staying below 1.0, points to a saturable reabsorptive transporter, not a switch to secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this same saturable-transporter logic explains the glucose titration curve and the transport maximum concept covered alongside GFR marker physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician orders a creatinine clearance test instead of an inulin clearance test to estimate a patient's GFR. Which statement correctly describes the expected result and its clinical acceptability?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine clearance will slightly underestimate the true GFR value","B":"Creatinine clearance will slightly overestimate the true GFR value","C":"Creatinine clearance will grossly overestimate the true GFR value","D":"Creatinine clearance will exactly equal the true GFR value","E":"Creatinine clearance carries too much tubular secretion error to serve as a GFR estimate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because creatinine is filtered and also secreted to a small extent, creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR; this small, predictable error is accepted in clinical practice because creatinine does not require infusion, unlike inulin.","boardPrep":"Creatinine clearance overestimates GFR by a small, well-characterized margin — \"slightly\" and \"overestimate\" are the two words to anchor on when this concept is tested.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same creatinine-based estimate is what clinicians rely on when interpreting the BUN/creatinine ratio in prerenal versus intrinsic renal azotemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher proposes a new exogenous marker that is freely filtered but, unlike inulin, is partially reabsorbed by the proximal tubule. How would this marker's clearance compare to the true GFR value?","choices":{"A":"Its clearance would overestimate the true GFR value","B":"Its clearance would exactly equal the true GFR value","C":"Its clearance would be zero because reabsorption abolishes excretion","D":"Its clearance would underestimate the true GFR value","E":"Its clearance would be unpredictable and difficult to interpret"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A marker that is filtered and then partially reabsorbed will have a clearance lower than the true GFR, since some of the filtered load never reaches the urine; this makes it a less accurate glomerular marker than inulin, which is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted.","boardPrep":"Any reabsorption after filtration pulls a marker's clearance below true GFR — inulin remains the gold standard specifically because it lacks this reabsorptive error.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same reabsorption-causes-underestimation logic is why substances like Na+ and glucose have clearance ratios well below 1.0 in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient's muscle mass declines substantially over several months due to a chronic wasting illness, while true GFR remains stable. What is the expected effect on measured serum creatinine and creatinine clearance, assuming creatinine production falls in proportion to muscle mass?","choices":{"A":"Serum creatinine falls while creatinine clearance stays a similarly accurate GFR estimate","B":"Serum creatinine rises while less creatinine becomes available for filtration","C":"Serum creatinine and creatinine clearance both stay unchanged since GFR is stable","D":"Serum creatinine falls while creatinine clearance underestimates GFR because tubular secretion of creatinine drops","E":"Serum creatinine rises while creatinine clearance falls in direct proportion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because creatinine is produced by muscle at a roughly constant rate proportional to muscle mass, a substantial decline in muscle mass reduces creatinine production and lowers serum creatinine even though GFR itself is unchanged. Creatinine clearance, calculated from serum and urine creatinine, still tracks the unchanged GFR because urinary creatinine excretion falls in step with the serum value, and the secretory contribution is a small, stable fraction of excretion rather than something that changes with muscle mass. The clinically important change is therefore the low absolute serum creatinine, which can mask a real decline in renal function.","boardPrep":"Muscle mass, not just GFR, determines the absolute serum creatinine value — a low-muscle-mass patient can have a deceptively low or \"normal-looking\" creatinine even when GFR is only borderline, which can mask a real decline in renal function on future labs; this is a classic clinical pitfall in cirrhosis, malnutrition, and chronic wasting illness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this muscle-mass dependence is exactly why creatinine, unlike inulin, is not a universally reliable marker across all patient populations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents after several days of poor oral intake and diarrhea, with a BUN of 50 mg/dL and a serum creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL, giving a BUN/creatinine ratio near 28:1. What is the most likely underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic glomerular damage is filtering urea and creatinine at different rates","B":"A urinary tract obstruction is preventing both urea and creatinine excretion equally","C":"Increased dietary protein intake is independently raising both BUN and creatinine","D":"A defect in the countercurrent multiplication system is concentrating both solutes equally","E":"Decreased renal perfusion is driving disproportionate proximal urea reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The clinical history of poor intake and diarrhea points to volume contraction, and a BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1 reflects decreased renal perfusion driving increased proximal tubular reabsorption of urea disproportionately more than creatinine, the classic mechanism of prerenal azotemia.","boardPrep":"Whenever a vignette pairs a volume-contraction history (vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake, hemorrhage) with a BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1, the mechanism is prerenal, not intrinsic renal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same volume-contraction mechanism is the clinical endpoint of the hyperosmotic and hyposmotic contraction states studied quantitatively in SDL 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients both have a serum creatinine of 3.0 mg/dL. Patient A has a BUN of 65 mg/dL (ratio ≈ 22:1), and Patient B has a BUN of 33 mg/dL (ratio ≈ 11:1). Assuming no other confounding factors, which mechanism best distinguishes the two?","choices":{"A":"Patient A has superimposed volume contraction; Patient B has isolated intrinsic renal disease","B":"Patient A has isolated intrinsic renal disease; Patient B has superimposed volume contraction","C":"Both patients have identical underlying mechanisms since their creatinine values match","D":"Patient A has postrenal obstruction; Patient B has classic prerenal azotemia","E":"The ratio difference reflects measurement variability between the two labs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With identical elevated creatinine values reflecting a similar reduction in GFR, Patient A's markedly elevated BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1 suggests an additional volume-contraction component driving disproportionate urea reabsorption, whereas Patient B's ratio near 11:1 is consistent with intrinsic renal disease raising both values together without that disproportionate effect.","boardPrep":"When creatinine is equal between two patients, the BUN/creatinine ratio is what reveals whether volume contraction is also present — a key exam technique for layered renal pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this side-by-side ratio comparison is the clinical application of the same reabsorption-versus-filtration distinction established in Objective 2's clearance ratios","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with long-standing chronic kidney disease has a BUN of 80 mg/dL and a serum creatinine of 6.5 mg/dL, giving a ratio near 12:1. A family member asks whether aggressive intravenous fluids would be expected to normalize these values. What is the most accurate response?","choices":{"A":"Yes — the elevated lab values reflect volume contraction that fluids would fully reverse","B":"Yes — fluids would restore proximal urea reabsorption and lower the ratio","C":"No — intrinsic renal dysfunction explains this ratio and fluids would not correct it","D":"No — the ratio is already normal and requires no additional intervention","E":"No — fluids would paradoxically raise both BUN and creatinine further"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio near 12:1, within the normal-to-low range despite both values being markedly elevated, indicates intrinsic renal parenchymal disease rather than volume contraction, so intravenous fluids would not correct the underlying reduced GFR from chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"A low-normal BUN/creatinine ratio with elevated absolute values rules out a volume-contraction component — fluids treat prerenal azotemia, not intrinsic renal disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same ratio-based reasoning is the clinical decision point separating fluid resuscitation from renal-replacement-focused management in the nephrology block ahead","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Renal Blood Flow (RBF) & Hemodynamic Autoregulation","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net effect of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) on afferent arteriolar tone, efferent arteriolar tone, and overall renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"ANP constricts the afferent arteriole, dilates the efferent arteriole, and decreases renal blood flow overall","B":"ANP dilates both the afferent and efferent arterioles equally, leaving overall renal blood flow essentially unchanged","C":"ANP constricts both the afferent and efferent arterioles, decreasing overall renal blood flow substantially","D":"ANP dilates the afferent arteriole, constricts the efferent arteriole, and increases renal blood flow overall","E":"ANP dilates the efferent arteriole modestly, leaving afferent arteriolar tone essentially unaffected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ANP causes afferent arteriolar dilation and efferent arteriolar constriction; because the dilatory effect on the afferent vessel outweighs the constrictor effect on the efferent vessel, overall renal vascular resistance falls and renal blood flow rises.","boardPrep":"ANP = afferent dilation + efferent constriction, net resistance DOWN, RBF UP — the opposite arteriolar pairing from angiotensin II.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — this ANP-driven resistance pattern is why volume-expanded states can raise GFR even as renal vascular resistance falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extrinsic mediator constricts both the afferent and efferent arterioles, but has a preferential constrictor effect on the efferent arteriole at low circulating levels?","choices":{"A":"Atrial natriuretic peptide","B":"Prostaglandin E2","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Bradykinin","E":"Angiotensin II"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Angiotensin II constricts both arterioles, but the efferent arteriole is more sensitive to it; at low levels this preferential efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure and protects GFR even as renal blood flow falls.","boardPrep":"Low-dose angiotensin II = efferent-predominant constriction = GFR protected, RBF down; this is the basis for ACE-inhibitor/ARB effects on GFR in renal artery stenosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — this dose-dependent efferent selectivity is the physiologic basis for ACE-inhibitor-induced acute kidney injury in bilateral renal artery stenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"A low dose of dopamine is administered to a hemorrhaging patient specifically for its effect on which vascular beds?","choices":{"A":"Renal, cerebral, cardiac, and splanchnic arterioles are dilated","B":"Skeletal muscle and cutaneous arterioles are dilated","C":"Systemic arterioles throughout the body are constricted uniformly","D":"Renal arterioles are the single vascular bed affected, while other vascular beds remain unchanged","E":"Renal arterioles are constricted while cerebral arterioles are dilated"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At low levels, dopamine selectively dilates renal, cerebral, cardiac, and splanchnic arterioles while constricting skeletal muscle and cutaneous arterioles, which is why low-dose dopamine has historically been used to protect flow to critical organs during hemorrhage.","boardPrep":"Low-dose dopamine = selective dilation of renal/cerebral/cardiac/splanchnic beds — protects the organs that matter most during shock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — dopamine's selective renal vasodilation is a classic (though now largely abandoned in practice) example of extrinsic hemodynamic manipulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Locally produced renal prostaglandins such as PGE2 and PGI2 primarily protect renal blood flow during states of high sympathetic and angiotensin II activity by what mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Directly inhibiting angiotensin-converting enzyme activity in endothelial cells","B":"Vasodilating the afferent arteriole to counteract afferent vasoconstriction","C":"Increasing tubular sodium reabsorption to expand circulating plasma volume levels","D":"Suppressing renin release directly from the juxtaglomerular apparatus cells","E":"Stimulating aldosterone secretion directly from the adrenal cortex gland"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The same stimuli that activate the sympathetic nervous system and raise angiotensin II during hemorrhage constrict the afferent arteriole and also trigger local renal prostaglandin production; these prostaglandins vasodilate the afferent arteriole, attenuating that constriction and protecting glomerular blood flow from a profound, potentially injurious reduction.","boardPrep":"Prostaglandins are the afferent-arteriole counterweight to sympathetic/angiotensin II vasoconstriction — remove that counterweight (NSAIDs) and unopposed afferent constriction can precipitate renal failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this counterbalancing mechanism is exactly what NSAIDs disable, setting up the classic hypoperfusion-plus-NSAID acute kidney injury scenario","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"A hypovolemic patient with high circulating angiotensin II and sympathetic tone is given an NSAID. What is the expected renal consequence?","choices":{"A":"Renal blood flow rises because prostaglandin-mediated vasoconstriction is removed","B":"Renal blood flow is unaffected because prostaglandins play no meaningful role during hypovolemia","C":"Renal blood flow falls further, risking acute kidney injury","D":"Renal blood flow falls in the efferent arteriole while the afferent arteriole stays unaffected","E":"Renal blood flow briefly falls, then rebounds above baseline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In a hypovolemic patient, angiotensin II and sympathetic tone are already producing substantial renal vasoconstriction that is normally buffered by local prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation; NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis, removing this protective buffer and leaving the vasoconstriction unopposed, which can drop renal blood flow enough to precipitate acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"NSAID + volume depletion (or heart failure, cirrhosis, ACE-inhibitor/ARB use) = classic prerenal AKI setup — always ask about NSAID use in a volume-depleted patient with rising creatinine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this exact clinical scenario is the highest-yield teaching point in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism do NSAIDs interfere with the renal protective response to hemorrhage or volume depletion?","choices":{"A":"They directly block angiotensin II receptors, preventing arteriolar constriction","B":"They stimulate excess renin release, amplifying the renin-angiotensin system","C":"They increase sympathetic outflow, worsening arteriolar vasoconstriction","D":"They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing a vasodilatory buffer","E":"They inhibit aldosterone-mediated sodium reabsorption, reducing extracellular volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and therefore prostaglandin synthesis; because renal prostaglandins are the local vasodilatory buffer that normally offsets sympathetic- and angiotensin II-driven vasoconstriction during hemorrhage or volume depletion, their loss allows unopposed vasoconstriction to reduce renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"NSAID mechanism of renal harm = COX inhibition -> lost prostaglandin vasodilation -> unopposed vasoconstriction, not a direct arteriolar receptor effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this is the pharmacologic mechanism underlying the AKI risk tested throughout this objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"The myogenic hypothesis of renal autoregulation explains constant renal blood flow across a range of arterial pressures by what cellular mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Increased arterial pressure directly inhibits renin release, which substantially lowers renal vascular resistance","B":"Pressure-induced stretch opens calcium channels in afferent arteriolar smooth muscle, causing reflex contraction","C":"Stretch of the efferent arteriole triggers macula densa cells to release adenosine locally","D":"Baroreceptors located in the renal artery wall signal the medulla to adjust sympathetic outflow","E":"Increased arterial pressure reduces intracellular calcium concentration in vascular smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The myogenic hypothesis holds that increased arterial pressure stretches the afferent arteriolar wall, opening stretch-activated calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle; the resulting calcium influx produces reflex contraction, raising afferent arteriolar resistance to balance the pressure increase and keep renal blood flow constant.","boardPrep":"Myogenic mechanism = stretch -> calcium channels open -> smooth muscle contracts -> resistance rises to match the pressure rise, all within the afferent arteriole itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this stretch-activated calcium mechanism is a general vascular smooth muscle principle, not unique to the kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"In the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism, what ionic event at the macula densa directly precedes adenosine release?","choices":{"A":"Increased intracellular calcium following Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter-mediated depolarization","B":"Decreased intracellular chloride concentration following reduced Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter activity","C":"Direct calcium influx through stretch-activated channels in macula densa cell membranes","D":"Hyperpolarization of the basolateral membrane caused by potassium efflux through open channels","E":"Direct binding of filtered angiotensin II to receptors on macula densa cell membranes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased GFR raises Na+ and Cl− delivery to the macula densa, which are taken up via the Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter; the resulting rise in intracellular chloride depolarizes the basolateral membrane, opening calcium channels and raising intracellular calcium, which then triggers adenosine release and paracrine afferent arteriolar constriction.","boardPrep":"TGF sequence: increased delivered Na+/Cl− -> cotransporter uptake -> depolarization -> calcium channels open -> adenosine release -> afferent constriction -> GFR falls back to normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this exact signaling cascade is the mechanistic basis for SGLT2 inhibitors lowering GFR in diabetic hyperfiltration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"Loop diuretics blunt tubuloglomerular feedback primarily by inhibiting which transporter at the macula densa?","choices":{"A":"The Na+-glucose cotransporter protein","B":"The Na+/H+ exchanger protein","C":"The epithelial Na+ channel protein","D":"The Na+/K+-ATPase pump protein","E":"The Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The macula densa senses tubular Na+ and Cl− delivery through the same Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter targeted by loop diuretics; blocking this transporter prevents the macula densa from sensing increased delivered load, blunting the afferent arteriolar constriction that would otherwise occur.","boardPrep":"Loop diuretics blunt TGF by blocking the macula densa's own Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter sensor — the same target responsible for their diuretic action in the thick ascending limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this pharmacologic blockade of TGF sensing is a favorite integration point linking diuretic pharmacology to renal hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) makes it suitable for measuring effective renal plasma flow by the clearance method?","choices":{"A":"It is freely filtered across the glomerulus but undergoes neither secretion nor reabsorption","B":"It is almost completely extracted from renal arterial blood by filtration plus secretion","C":"It is reabsorbed extensively by the proximal tubule in direct proportion to plasma concentration","D":"It is metabolized extensively by renal tubular cells before urinary excretion","E":"It is bound almost entirely to circulating plasma proteins and therefore poorly filtered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PAH is neither synthesized nor metabolized by the kidney and is removed from renal arterial blood almost completely by the combined processes of glomerular filtration and proximal tubular secretion, leaving renal vein PAH concentration nearly zero; this near-complete extraction is what allows its clearance to approximate renal plasma flow.","boardPrep":"PAH clearance ≈ effective RPF only because PAH is almost completely extracted in one pass — a substance like glucose, with zero net extraction, cannot be used this way.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this near-complete single-pass extraction is the same property that makes PAH clearance a measure of renal plasma flow rather than GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Effective renal plasma flow, calculated from PAH clearance using only a peripheral venous plasma sample and a urine sample, underestimates true renal plasma flow by approximately how much?","choices":{"A":"10%","B":"1%","C":"25%","D":"50%","E":"75%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective RPF assumes renal vein PAH concentration is zero, but a small fraction of renal plasma flow perfuses tissue not involved in filtration or secretion of PAH, such as renal fat and the renal capsule, so renal vein PAH is nearly, but not exactly, zero; this makes effective RPF underestimate true RPF by roughly 10%.","boardPrep":"Effective RPF (from PAH clearance) ≈ true RPF, off by about 10%, because a small non-extracting fraction of renal blood flow never contacts filtering/secreting tissue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — remembering this 10% figure distinguishes \"effective\" from \"true\" RPF on exam questions that test this exact nuance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Once effective renal plasma flow is calculated from PAH clearance, what additional value is needed to calculate renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"The plasma protein concentration","B":"The glomerular filtration rate","C":"The patient's hematocrit","D":"The renal filtration fraction","E":"The mean arterial pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Renal blood flow equals renal plasma flow divided by the quantity one minus the hematocrit, since hematocrit represents the fraction of blood volume occupied by red blood cells and 1 minus hematocrit represents the plasma fraction that PAH clearance actually measures.","boardPrep":"RBF = RPF / (1 − Hct) — always convert plasma flow to blood flow by dividing out the red-cell fraction, never by multiplying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this hematocrit-scaling step is the final arithmetic bridge between the PAH-based plasma flow measurement and true whole-blood renal blood flow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with heart failure has markedly elevated circulating angiotensin II. According to the differential arteriolar sensitivity to angiotensin II, what happens to glomerular filtration rate at this high circulating level compared to a mild elevation?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, because a very high concentration of angiotensin II constricts both afferent and efferent arterioles substantially","B":"GFR rises further, because efferent arteriolar constriction continues to dominate the resistance response","C":"GFR stays completely unchanged, because afferent and efferent arteriolar sensitivity to angiotensin II does not vary with concentration","D":"GFR falls because the afferent arteriole becomes completely unresponsive while the efferent arteriole stays reactive","E":"GFR rises transiently after the initial change, then returns to baseline as renal autoregulation compensates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At low angiotensin II levels, the efferent arteriole's greater sensitivity means preferential efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure and protects or increases GFR; at high levels, both afferent and efferent arterioles are substantially constricted, and the resulting large rise in overall resistance drops GFR along with renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"Angiotensin II and GFR: low level protects/raises GFR (efferent-predominant), high level drops GFR (both arterioles constrict) — dose determines the direction of the GFR effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — this dose-dependent switch explains why severe heart failure or hemorrhage can still cause a falling GFR despite angiotensin II normally being GFR-protective at lower levels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient begins an ACE inhibitor for hypertension. Based on angiotensin II's arteriolar effects, what is the expected acute change in renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate?","choices":{"A":"Renal plasma flow falls sharply while glomerular filtration rate remains completely stable","B":"Renal plasma flow rises while glomerular filtration rate falls","C":"Both renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate rise together","D":"Both values decline together whenever systemic blood pressure falls low enough to reduce overall renal perfusion","E":"Renal plasma flow stays essentially unchanged while glomerular filtration rate falls sharply"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II production, removing its preferential efferent arteriolar constriction; efferent arteriolar dilation lowers overall renal vascular resistance and raises renal plasma flow, but it also lowers glomerular capillary pressure, which drops the glomerular filtration rate.","boardPrep":"ACE inhibitors/ARBs: efferent dilation -> RPF up, GFR down — this is why creatinine can rise modestly (an expected, tolerable change) after starting these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — a large or progressive creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor should prompt evaluation for bilateral renal artery stenosis rather than reassurance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in early hemorrhagic shock has elevated sympathetic tone activating renal alpha-1 receptors. Which mechanism explains why glomerular filtration rate falls together with renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-1 receptor density is greater on the efferent arteriole, so sympathetic activation preferentially raises efferent resistance","B":"Sympathetic activation dilates the afferent arteriole to preserve glomerular capillary pressure during hypovolemia","C":"Sympathetic nerves constrict the renal vein and raise glomerular capillary pressure through venous back-pressure","D":"Alpha-1 receptor density is greater on the afferent arteriole, so sympathetic activation preferentially raises afferent resistance","E":"Sympathetic nerves act directly on the glomerular capillary wall itself, reducing its overall filtering surface area"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because afferent arterioles carry more alpha-1 receptors than efferent arterioles, sympathetic activation during hemorrhage preferentially constricts the afferent vessel; this reduces glomerular capillary pressure along with renal blood flow, so both renal blood flow and GFR fall together, in contrast to the efferent-predominant pattern seen with low-level angiotensin II that protects GFR.","boardPrep":"Sympathetic activation = afferent-predominant constriction = both RBF and GFR fall together, unlike angiotensin II's efferent-predominant, GFR-protective pattern at low levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — contrasting the afferent-predominant sympathetic pattern with the efferent-predominant low-level angiotensin II pattern is a classic board distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"An elderly patient with chronic heart failure and reduced effective circulating volume takes ibuprofen daily for osteoarthritis and presents with a rising creatinine. What is the most likely mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Ibuprofen blocks protective prostaglandin vasodilation, allowing unopposed vasoconstriction to reduce renal blood flow","B":"Ibuprofen directly damages the glomerular basement membrane, causing significant protein loss and severely reduced filtration","C":"Ibuprofen stimulates excess renin release, raising circulating angiotensin II above its usual protective range","D":"Ibuprofen blocks tubular creatinine secretion directly, raising serum creatinine without any change in true GFR","E":"Ibuprofen causes afferent arteriolar dilation that raises glomerular capillary pressure excessively high"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a patient with reduced effective circulating volume, such as one with heart failure, renal perfusion depends heavily on prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation to counteract already-elevated angiotensin II and sympathetic tone; an NSAID like ibuprofen inhibits this prostaglandin synthesis, removing the protective buffer and allowing unopposed vasoconstriction to drop renal blood flow and precipitate a prerenal rise in creatinine.","boardPrep":"Heart failure + NSAID + rising creatinine = classic prostaglandin-buffer-loss prerenal AKI, one of the most frequently tested drug-induced kidney injury scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — heart failure, cirrhosis, and true volume depletion are the three classic settings where the prostaglandin buffer is already maximally recruited before an NSAID is even added","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A previously healthy, well-hydrated young adult takes a single dose of ibuprofen for a headache. Why is acute kidney injury unlikely in this scenario compared to a volume-depleted patient?","choices":{"A":"Ibuprofen does not inhibit prostaglandin synthesis at low single-dose exposure","B":"Young adults lack the renal prostaglandin receptors needed for this drug interaction","C":"Prostaglandin vasodilation is not heavily recruited when volume status is normal","D":"A single dose is metabolized and cleared before it ever reaches renal tissue","E":"Prostaglandins act on the efferent arteriole when volume status is well hydrated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In a euvolemic, well-perfused kidney, sympathetic and angiotensin II tone are not elevated, so the prostaglandin vasodilatory buffer is not being heavily called upon to protect renal blood flow; removing that buffer with an NSAID therefore has little hemodynamic consequence, whereas in a volume-depleted or hypoperfused state the buffer is essential and its loss is far more dangerous.","boardPrep":"NSAID nephrotoxicity risk tracks with how much the prostaglandin buffer is already being used — high-risk in volume depletion, heart failure, cirrhosis, or ACE-inhibitor use; low-risk in a euvolemic healthy patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this risk-stratification logic is exactly why NSAID warnings target volume-depleted and chronically ill patients rather than the general population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient on an ACE inhibitor for chronic kidney disease is started on an NSAID for pain and develops a sharp creatinine rise. How do the two drugs' mechanisms combine to produce this effect?","choices":{"A":"The ACE inhibitor and NSAID both act on the exact same vascular receptor type, producing an additive but mechanistically redundant effect","B":"The NSAID reverses the ACE inhibitor effect on angiotensin II production, fully restoring normal efferent arteriolar tone","C":"The ACE inhibitor affects tubular sodium handling while the NSAID affects glomerular capillary permeability","D":"The combination raises systemic blood pressure high enough to cause acute hypertensive glomerular capillary damage","E":"ACE inhibitor dilation of the efferent arteriole plus NSAID loss of afferent prostaglandins collapses glomerular pressure from both sides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ACE inhibitors lower glomerular capillary pressure by dilating the efferent arteriole (removing angiotensin II's constrictor effect), while NSAIDs remove the prostaglandin-mediated afferent vasodilation that normally sustains blood flow into the glomerulus; used together, efferent pressure support and afferent blood delivery are lost simultaneously, and glomerular filtration pressure can collapse far more than with either drug alone.","boardPrep":"ACE inhibitor + NSAID = a classic dual hit on glomerular hemodynamics from opposite arterioles — a well-known cause of acute kidney injury, especially with added volume depletion (the \"triple whammy\" with diuretics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — recognizing this two-drug (or three-drug, with a diuretic) combination is one of the most frequently tested acute kidney injury vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A patient is started on a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor for diabetes mellitus with early glomerular hyperfiltration. How does tubuloglomerular feedback mediate the drug's effect on GFR?","choices":{"A":"The drug directly constricts the efferent arteriole, acting completely independently of macula densa sensing mechanisms","B":"Blocking the cotransporter raises macula densa Na+ delivery, triggering afferent constriction that lowers GFR","C":"The drug blocks the Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter at the macula densa, the exact same target used by loop diuretics","D":"The drug increases proximal tubular reabsorption of Na+, substantially decreasing delivery to the macula densa","E":"The drug has no effect on tubuloglomerular feedback and lowers GFR through a completely separate mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SGLT2 inhibitors block Na+-glucose cotransport in the proximal convoluted tubule, increasing downstream Na+ delivery to the macula densa; through the normal tubuloglomerular feedback pathway, this increased delivered load triggers adenosine release and afferent arteriolar constriction, lowering GFR and offsetting the damaging hyperfiltration seen in diabetes mellitus.","boardPrep":"SGLT2 inhibitors lower GFR via intact tubuloglomerular feedback (more Na+ to macula densa -> afferent constriction), not via direct arteriolar action — this is the renoprotective mechanism in diabetic kidney disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this is one of the highest-yield clinical applications of tubuloglomerular feedback physiology tested on current board exams","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A denervated, transplanted kidney continues to autoregulate renal blood flow across a normal range of arterial pressures. What does this observation demonstrate about the underlying mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Renal autoregulation depends entirely on autonomic innervation for its stretch and feedback responses","B":"The transplanted kidney regenerates its sympathetic nerve supply within hours of transplantation","C":"Autoregulation in a transplanted kidney relies on circulating angiotensin II acting on both arterioles","D":"Renal autoregulation does not require intact autonomic innervation to operate normally","E":"Denervation abolishes tubuloglomerular feedback but spares the myogenic mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because a denervated kidney autoregulates renal blood flow just as well as an intact one, the autonomic nervous system cannot be responsible for autoregulation; instead, the myogenic mechanism (an intrinsic property of vascular smooth muscle) and tubuloglomerular feedback (a local paracrine mechanism at the macula densa) are entirely local, kidney-intrinsic processes that do not require nerve input.","boardPrep":"Autoregulation is intrinsic to the kidney itself — the classic evidence is that transplanted (denervated) kidneys autoregulate normally, ruling out a required autonomic component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this denervation evidence is a frequently quoted proof-of-concept for the intrinsic, local nature of both autoregulatory mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A patient eats a very high-protein meal. Through the tubuloglomerular feedback pathway, what is the expected effect on glomerular filtration rate?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, because increased amino acid delivery directly stimulates macula densa adenosine release","B":"GFR is unchanged, because dietary protein does not alter tubuloglomerular feedback sensitivity","C":"GFR rises, because increased proximal Na+ and Cl− reabsorption reduces delivery to the macula densa","D":"GFR falls, because a high-protein meal increases Na+ and Cl− delivery to the macula densa","E":"GFR rises transiently, then falls below baseline as feedback overcorrects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A high-protein diet increases proximal tubular Na+ and Cl− reabsorption, which reduces the amount of Na+ and Cl− delivered downstream to the macula densa; with less delivered load to sense, tubuloglomerular feedback generates less afferent arteriolar constriction, allowing GFR to rise above its usual set point.","boardPrep":"High-protein diet -> more proximal Na+/Cl− reabsorption -> less delivery to macula densa -> less TGF-mediated constriction -> GFR rises — a classic example of TGF sensitivity being altered by diet.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this dietary modulation of TGF sensitivity is a clinically relevant nuance distinguishing TGF from a fixed, unmodifiable set point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a urine flow rate of 1 mL/min, a plasma PAH concentration of 1 mg/100 mL, and a urine PAH concentration of 600 mg/100 mL. What is this patient's effective renal plasma flow?","choices":{"A":"600 mL/min","B":"60 mL/min","C":"1000 mL/min","D":"6 mL/min","E":"1200 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective RPF equals the clearance of PAH, calculated as urine PAH concentration times urine flow rate divided by plasma PAH concentration: (600 mg/100 mL × 1 mL/min) / (1 mg/100 mL) = 600 mL/min.","boardPrep":"Effective RPF = ([U]PAH × V̇) / [P]PAH — plug in urine concentration times flow rate, divide by plasma concentration, done.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this is the identical calculation structure used for any clearance calculation, just applied specifically to PAH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has an effective renal plasma flow of 600 mL/min and a hematocrit of 0.45. What is this patient's effective renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 270 mL/min","B":"Approximately 600 mL/min","C":"Approximately 833 mL/min","D":"Approximately 1333 mL/min","E":"Approximately 1091 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal blood flow equals renal plasma flow divided by one minus the hematocrit: 600 mL/min / (1 − 0.45) = 600 mL/min / 0.55 ≈ 1091 mL/min.","boardPrep":"RBF = RPF / (1 − Hct); with Hct 0.45, divide RPF by 0.55 — always larger than RPF itself, since blood includes red cells that plasma flow alone does not measure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — pairing this hematocrit-scaling step with the PAH clearance calculation is the complete two-step quantitative workflow tested in this objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"Why can true renal plasma flow not be calculated in a patient for whom only peripheral venous and urine PAH concentrations are available, without a direct renal vein sample?","choices":{"A":"True RPF requires a full 24-hour urine collection, and a single spot urine sample cannot substitute for that requirement","B":"True RPF needs concentration values from both the vessel entering the kidney and the vessel draining it afterward","C":"True RPF calculations require a separately measured hematocrit value, and no hematocrit value was reported anywhere in this workup","D":"A peripheral venous blood sample differs enough from the true arterial blood to invalidate any renal clearance calculation","E":"True RPF cannot be calculated for any substance that undergoes tubular secretion in addition to glomerular filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Fick principle formula for true RPF is urine PAH excretion divided by the difference between renal artery and renal vein PAH concentrations; because renal vein blood cannot practically be sampled in a patient, only the simplified effective RPF (which assumes renal vein PAH is zero and substitutes an easily obtained peripheral venous sample for the renal artery concentration) can actually be calculated in practice.","boardPrep":"True RPF needs an unobtainable renal vein sample; effective RPF is the practical, clinically calculable approximation, and it is what \"PAH clearance\" almost always refers to on exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — recognizing which formula (true vs. effective RPF) a question is actually asking for is the key discriminator in this objective's calculation questions","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Glomerular Filtration and Biophysics","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the glomerular capillary wall is considered the most significant barrier to filtration of plasma proteins based on its multilayered structure?","choices":{"A":"The basement membrane","B":"The fenestrated endothelium","C":"The podocyte foot processes","D":"The filtration slit diaphragms","E":"The mesangial matrix"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The basement membrane has three layers (lamina rara interna, lamina densa, and lamina rara externa) and does not permit filtration of plasma proteins, making it the most significant physical barrier of the glomerular capillary wall, even though the endothelium and epithelium also contribute size restriction.","boardPrep":"Three layers, one barrier hierarchy: endothelium (large pores, freely permeable) -> basement membrane (the main protein barrier) -> podocyte slit diaphragms (fine size restriction).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — this layered barrier structure is what a renal biopsy electron micrograph is showing when it demonstrates basement membrane thickening or podocyte foot process effacement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"The negatively charged heparan sulfate glycoproteins lining the glomerular filtration barrier primarily restrict the filtration of which type of solute?","choices":{"A":"Small cations such as Na+ and K+, too small for charge to matter","B":"Small anions such as Cl- and HCO3-, small enough to cross regardless of charge","C":"Large anionic plasma proteins such as albumin, restricted by both size and charge","D":"Water molecules, passing through the endothelial fenestrae unimpeded","E":"Neutral small solutes such as glucose, filtered without any size or charge restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small solutes such as Na+, K+, Cl-, and glucose are freely filtered regardless of charge because their tiny molecular diameter makes electrostatic repulsion irrelevant, but large solutes such as plasma proteins have a molecular diameter similar to the pore and slit sizes, so their net negative charge at physiologic pH causes electrostatic repulsion by the barrier's fixed negative charges, adding to size-based restriction.","boardPrep":"Charge selectivity only matters for large solutes whose size already puts them near the filtration cutoff — small ions and glucose are filtered freely no matter their charge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — losing this negative-charge barrier, without a change in the size-based barrier, is exactly the mechanism of minimal change disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"A pediatric patient develops selective proteinuria (loss of albumin without loss of larger plasma proteins) with normal-appearing glomeruli on light microscopy. What barrier defect best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Complete destruction of the basement membrane's size-restricting layers, with the charge barrier also compromised secondarily","B":"The filtration barrier's negative charges are gone, with its size-restricting structure remaining intact","C":"Formation of new large pores in the endothelium, with both size and charge selectivity lost","D":"Loss of podocyte foot processes, with the charge-based barrier remaining completely unaffected","E":"Deposition of immune complexes that mechanically obstruct the filtration slits, with the underlying charge barrier left entirely intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Minimal change disease classically presents with selective proteinuria and normal light microscopy because the defect is a loss of the barrier's fixed negative charges (detectable only by electron microscopy as podocyte foot process effacement), which selectively increases filtration of the negatively charged, moderately sized albumin while the size-based barrier remains largely intact, restricting larger proteins.","boardPrep":"Selective proteinuria (albumin only) with normal light microscopy = minimal change disease = charge-barrier loss, not a structural/size-barrier lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — contrast this with nonselective proteinuria (loss of both albumin and larger proteins), which points to a structural size-barrier lesion instead","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the oncotic pressure of Bowman space treated as zero in the Starling equation for glomerular filtration?","choices":{"A":"Because Bowman space has no fluid present under normal conditions","B":"Because Bowman space hydrostatic pressure equals capillary hydrostatic pressure","C":"Because the basement membrane actively pumps protein back into the capillary","D":"Because oncotic pressure develops in fluid compartments containing cells","E":"Because protein passage into Bowman space is normally negligible"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Oncotic pressure arises from protein concentration, and because the glomerular barrier normally filters only a negligible amount of protein into Bowman space, the fluid there has essentially no oncotic pressure, allowing this term to be dropped from the Starling equation for glomerular capillaries.","boardPrep":"Bowman space oncotic pressure = 0 only because normal filtrate is essentially protein-free — this assumption breaks down in proteinuric disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this same assumption is why an increase in filtered protein in glomerular disease can itself further alter the net ultrafiltration pressure calculation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"At the beginning of the glomerular capillary, hydrostatic pressure in the capillary is 45 mm Hg, hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space is 10 mm Hg, and oncotic pressure in the capillary is 19 mm Hg. What is the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point?","choices":{"A":"+35 mm Hg, favoring filtration","B":"+16 mm Hg, favoring filtration","C":"-16 mm Hg, favoring absorption","D":"0 mm Hg, at filtration equilibrium","E":"+26 mm Hg, favoring filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure: 45 − 10 − 19 = +16 mm Hg, a positive value that strongly favors filtration, consistent with the beginning of the glomerular capillary before oncotic pressure has risen.","boardPrep":"Net UF pressure = PGC − PBS − πGC; at the start of the glomerular capillary this is a strongly positive +16 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this exact arithmetic setup reappears in Objective 3's discussion of how this positive value falls to zero by the end of the capillary","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of properties of the glomerular capillary wall accounts for the fact that its filtration coefficient (Kf) is more than 100-fold that of typical systemic capillaries?","choices":{"A":"A much thinner basement membrane than found in systemic capillary walls","B":"A higher hydrostatic pressure driving fluid directly through the capillary wall","C":"Much higher water permeability paired with much greater surface area","D":"Active transport of water molecules across the entire endothelial layer","E":"A lower oncotic pressure present in the surrounding renal interstitium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kf reflects hydraulic conductance, which depends on both the water permeability per unit surface area and the total surface area available for filtration; glomerular capillaries have a combination of much higher intrinsic water permeability and much greater total surface area than systemic capillaries, together producing their dramatically higher Kf and correspondingly high GFR.","boardPrep":"Kf = permeability × surface area — glomerular capillaries win on both factors simultaneously, which is why GFR (180 L/day) dwarfs systemic capillary filtration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — a reduced Kf (from a lower functioning nephron mass or a diseased basement membrane) directly lowers GFR even when the net ultrafiltration pressure itself is unchanged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Starling pressure changes progressively along the length of the glomerular capillary to eventually produce filtration equilibrium?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, which rises as protein-free fluid is filtered out","B":"Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, which falls progressively along the capillary length","C":"Bowman space hydrostatic pressure, which rises steadily as filtrate accumulates downstream","D":"Bowman space oncotic pressure, which rises as protein leaks progressively into the filtrate","E":"Systemic arterial pressure, which falls steadily as blood traverses the capillary bed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike systemic capillaries, glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure remains essentially constant along the entire capillary length because efferent arteriolar resistance prevents its decline; instead, it is the glomerular capillary oncotic pressure that rises progressively as protein-free fluid is filtered out and protein is left behind in an ever-smaller plasma volume, eventually driving net ultrafiltration pressure to zero.","boardPrep":"Filtration equilibrium is caused by rising πGC, not falling PGC — the efferent arteriole is what keeps PGC constant along the glomerular capillary, unlike any systemic capillary bed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this same rising oncotic pressure becomes the peritubular capillary's high oncotic pressure that drives proximal tubular reabsorption immediately downstream","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"Under normal physiologic conditions, at what point along the glomerular capillary does filtration equilibrium typically occur?","choices":{"A":"Immediately at the very beginning of the glomerular capillary","B":"After blood has already entered the efferent arteriole","C":"It does not occur under normal conditions; true equilibrium requires a pathologic state","D":"Right at the very end of the glomerular capillary","E":"Precisely at the midpoint of the glomerular capillary length"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under normal conditions, net ultrafiltration pressure starts strongly positive at the beginning of the glomerular capillary and progressively falls as glomerular capillary oncotic pressure rises, reaching zero (filtration equilibrium) right at the end of the capillary, which conveniently means filtration occurs efficiently along virtually the entire capillary length before stopping.","boardPrep":"Normal filtration equilibrium occurs right at the capillary's end — filtration is essentially continuous along the whole glomerular capillary length under normal conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — a pathologic state that shifts equilibrium earlier along the capillary effectively shortens the functional filtering length and can lower GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"A reduction in renal plasma flow, without any change in the filtration coefficient, tends to shift the point of filtration equilibrium in which direction along the glomerular capillary?","choices":{"A":"Later along the capillary, since less fluid needs to be filtered to reach equilibrium","B":"Earlier along the capillary, since oncotic pressure rises more quickly per unit of blood flow","C":"The equilibrium point is unaffected by renal plasma flow under any condition","D":"Equilibrium is abolished entirely, and filtration becomes continuous throughout the capillary","E":"Earlier along the capillary, because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With reduced renal plasma flow, a smaller total volume of plasma passes through the glomerular capillary per unit time, so the same absolute amount of fluid filtration produces a proportionally larger rise in protein concentration and oncotic pressure sooner along the capillary length, shifting filtration equilibrium earlier and potentially reducing the effective filtering surface actually used.","boardPrep":"Low RPF states can shift filtration equilibrium earlier along the capillary, one mechanism by which low renal blood flow states can reduce GFR beyond what falling PGC alone would predict.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this RPF-dependence of the equilibrium point is part of why the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) is itself a hemodynamically meaningful ratio, not just an arbitrary quotient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"Constriction of the afferent arteriole produces what combined effect on renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, yet GFR actually climbs higher, pushing filtration fraction upward as well","B":"RPF unexpectedly climbs upward while GFR falls instead, dragging filtration fraction downward with it","C":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, and GFR falls even further in proportion, so filtration fraction actually drops","D":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, and GFR falls in proportion with it, so filtration fraction stays unchanged","E":"RPF stays essentially flat overall while GFR falls on its own accord, again lowering filtration fraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Afferent arteriolar constriction reduces blood flow into the glomerular capillary, decreasing both renal plasma flow and glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure; because GFR falls in the same proportion as RPF, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) remains unchanged.","boardPrep":"Afferent constriction: RPF down, GFR down, filtration fraction unchanged — both numerator and denominator fall together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — sympathetic activation and high-level angiotensin II both produce this afferent-predominant pattern discussed in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"Constriction of the efferent arteriole produces what combined effect on renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"RPF falls from reduced inflow instead, and GFR falls right along with it, leaving filtration fraction completely unchanged","B":"RPF unexpectedly climbs upward alongside GFR, so filtration fraction stays essentially flat","C":"RPF falls while GFR climbs much higher, yet filtration fraction still somehow ends up lower","D":"RPF stays essentially flat overall while GFR climbs on its own accord, still raising filtration fraction","E":"RPF falls from restricted outflow, yet GFR climbs because capillary pressure rises, so filtration fraction increases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Efferent arteriolar constriction restricts blood from leaving the glomerular capillary, decreasing renal plasma flow but raising glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and therefore increasing GFR; because GFR rises while RPF falls, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) increases.","boardPrep":"Efferent constriction: RPF down, GFR up, filtration fraction up — GFR and RPF move in opposite directions, the signature pattern of efferent-selective vasoconstriction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — low-level angiotensin II and ACE inhibitors both act through this efferent-selective pathway, just in opposite directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A decrease in plasma protein concentration, such as occurs in nephrotic syndrome from urinary protein loss, is expected to have what effect on GFR and filtration fraction, assuming RPF is unchanged?","choices":{"A":"Both GFR and filtration fraction increase together","B":"Both GFR and filtration fraction decline together","C":"GFR climbs even as filtration fraction actually falls","D":"GFR falls even as filtration fraction actually climbs","E":"Neither GFR nor filtration fraction shows any change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A decrease in plasma protein concentration lowers glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, which increases net ultrafiltration pressure and therefore increases GFR; since RPF is assumed unchanged, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) increases along with GFR.","boardPrep":"Low plasma protein (as in nephrotic syndrome or liver failure) -> low oncotic pressure -> higher net ultrafiltration pressure -> GFR and filtration fraction both rise, opposite to the effect of high plasma protein.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this oncotic-pressure-driven change in GFR is independent of the arteriolar resistance mechanisms and can be tested as a distractor against afferent/efferent effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher infuses a series of dextran molecules of identical molecular radius but differing net charge (cationic, neutral, anionic) and measures glomerular filtration rates for each. Which result would be expected if the glomerular barrier's charge selectivity is intact?","choices":{"A":"The three dextrans are filtered identically, since charge does not affect filtration at any molecular size","B":"Cationic dextran is filtered most readily, anionic dextran least readily, at the same molecular radius","C":"Anionic dextran is filtered most readily, cationic dextran least readily, at the same molecular radius","D":"Neutral dextran is filtered most readily, with both charged forms filtered equally less","E":"Cationic and anionic dextran are filtered identically, while neutral dextran is filtered least"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the glomerular barrier carries fixed negative charges, cationic (positively charged) solutes are electrostatically attracted and filtered more readily, while anionic (negatively charged) solutes of the same molecular radius are repelled and filtered less readily; this classic dextran experiment is the direct experimental demonstration of charge-selective filtration.","boardPrep":"Dextran sieving experiment: same size, different charge -> cationic filters best, anionic filters worst — the classic proof that the glomerular barrier discriminates by charge, not just size.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — this experimental principle is exactly why albumin (net negative at physiologic pH) is filtered far less than its size alone would predict","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with longstanding disease shows nonselective proteinuria (loss of both albumin and larger plasma proteins) along with structural changes visible on light microscopy. Compared to a patient with minimal change disease, what does this pattern suggest about the underlying barrier defect?","choices":{"A":"An isolated loss of fixed negative charge identical to minimal change disease","B":"A defect confined entirely to the endothelial fenestrae with the basement membrane unaffected","C":"An architectural, size-based barrier defect affecting the basement membrane or podocyte slit diaphragms","D":"No barrier defect whatsoever, since the protein loss reflects a purely tubular origin","E":"A defect that selectively targets the podocyte cell body while completely sparing the filtration diaphragms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective proteinuria, in which both albumin and larger plasma proteins are lost, indicates that the size-based barrier itself (the basement membrane and/or podocyte slit diaphragms) has been structurally disrupted, in contrast to minimal change disease, where the size barrier remains intact and only the charge-based restriction is lost, producing selective loss of the moderately sized, negatively charged albumin alone.","boardPrep":"Selective proteinuria (albumin-only) = charge-barrier loss (minimal change disease); nonselective proteinuria (albumin + larger proteins) = structural size-barrier disruption — a key clinical distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — visible light-microscopy changes are also a distinguishing feature, since minimal change disease is defined precisely by its normal light-microscopic appearance despite significant proteinuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"Why do red and white blood cells not appear in the glomerular filtrate under normal conditions, despite the relatively large 70- to 100-nm pores of the fenestrated endothelium?","choices":{"A":"Blood cells are actively pumped back into the capillary lumen by podocytes","B":"Blood cells carry a strong negative charge that repels them from the barrier","C":"Blood cells are destroyed by mechanical shear forces before reaching the barrier","D":"Blood cells are far larger than the largest openings in the filtration barrier","E":"Blood cells are retained by a separate cellular filter located upstream of the glomerulus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Although the endothelial fenestrae are relatively large at 70 to 100 nm, they are still far smaller than red or white blood cells, and the downstream basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits (25 to 60 nm) impose an even smaller effective size limit; blood cells are simply too large to pass through any layer of the barrier, with no active transport or charge-based mechanism required to exclude them.","boardPrep":"Blood cells are excluded by simple size, not charge or active transport — even the largest barrier pores are orders of magnitude smaller than a blood cell.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hematuria in glomerular disease therefore always implies a structural breach of the barrier severe enough to create passages far larger than the normal filtration slits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"At the end of the glomerular capillary, hydrostatic pressure in the capillary is 45 mm Hg, hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space is 10 mm Hg, and oncotic pressure in the capillary has risen to 35 mm Hg. What is the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point, and what physiologic state does it represent?","choices":{"A":"0 mm Hg, indicating complete filtration equilibrium has been reached","B":"+16 mm Hg, representing the same strong filtration drive as the start of the capillary","C":"-10 mm Hg, representing net fluid absorption into the capillary","D":"+35 mm Hg, representing maximal net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"-35 mm Hg, representing complete reversal of the normal filtration direction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure: 45 − 10 − 35 = 0 mm Hg; a value of zero means no further net filtration can occur, which is the definition of filtration equilibrium, and this normally occurs right at the end of the glomerular capillary.","boardPrep":"Net UF pressure falls from +16 mm Hg at the start to 0 mm Hg at the end of the glomerular capillary — entirely because oncotic pressure rises from 19 to 35 mm Hg as protein-free fluid is filtered out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — comparing this end-of-capillary value to the start-of-capillary value in Objective 2's earlier calculation directly demonstrates the filtration equilibrium concept in Objective 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"A ureteral stone completely obstructs one ureter, causing hydrostatic pressure in that kidney's nephrons to rise as far back as Bowman space. Using the Starling equation, what is the direct effect on net ultrafiltration pressure and GFR in the obstructed kidney?","choices":{"A":"Net ultrafiltration pressure increases because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure rises to compensate, and GFR increases","B":"Net ultrafiltration pressure is unaffected because Bowman space pressure is not a term in the Starling equation, and GFR is unchanged","C":"Net ultrafiltration pressure decreases because glomerular capillary oncotic pressure rises in response, and GFR decreases","D":"Net ultrafiltration pressure increases because Bowman space oncotic pressure falls to zero, and GFR increases","E":"Net ultrafiltration pressure decreases because Bowman space hydrostatic pressure rises, and GFR decreases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bowman space hydrostatic pressure is a term that opposes filtration in the Starling equation; when urine outflow is obstructed, pressure backs up as far as Bowman space, raising this opposing pressure, which directly lowers net ultrafiltration pressure and therefore decreases GFR in the obstructed kidney.","boardPrep":"Ureteral obstruction raises Bowman space pressure -> lowers net UF pressure -> lowers GFR, a mechanism distinct from any arteriolar resistance change and worth recognizing as its own Starling-force pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this is the classic postrenal mechanism of acute kidney injury, contrasted with the prerenal (hemodynamic) and intrinsic mechanisms discussed elsewhere in this block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a chronically reduced number of functioning nephrons from longstanding kidney disease, lowering the total glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf), while the net ultrafiltration pressure per remaining nephron stays normal. What is the expected effect on overall GFR?","choices":{"A":"Overall GFR stays normal, since net ultrafiltration pressure is the sole determinant of GFR","B":"Overall GFR rises, since the remaining nephrons compensate by increasing Kf individually","C":"Overall GFR falls whenever net ultrafiltration pressure also happens to fall","D":"Overall GFR falls, since GFR is the product of Kf and net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"Overall GFR is unaffected until fewer than half of the nephrons are lost"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GFR equals Kf multiplied by the net ultrafiltration pressure; because a chronic reduction in functioning nephron mass lowers the total Kf (the combined hydraulic conductance and surface area across all glomeruli) even when the net ultrafiltration pressure per remaining nephron is unchanged, overall GFR falls in direct proportion to the loss of functioning filtering surface area.","boardPrep":"GFR = Kf × net ultrafiltration pressure — losing functioning nephron mass lowers GFR through the Kf term, independent of any change in Starling pressures, which is exactly how chronic kidney disease progressively lowers GFR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this Kf-driven decline in GFR is the physiologic basis for using GFR itself as the primary staging criterion in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"In a state of significantly increased renal plasma flow (e.g., early pregnancy), how does the position of filtration equilibrium along the glomerular capillary typically shift, assuming Kf and arteriolar resistances are unchanged?","choices":{"A":"Earlier along the capillary, since more total protein is delivered to the capillary per minute","B":"Later along the capillary, or equilibrium may not be reached before the capillary ends","C":"The equilibrium position stays fixed regardless of renal plasma flow","D":"Earlier along the capillary, because increased renal plasma flow raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure enough to accelerate oncotic pressure buildup","E":"Later along the capillary, because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With increased renal plasma flow, a larger total volume of plasma passes through the glomerular capillary per unit time, so the same absolute amount of fluid filtered produces a smaller proportional rise in protein concentration and oncotic pressure at any given point along the capillary, shifting the point of filtration equilibrium later along the capillary or preventing it from being reached before the capillary ends, which increases the effective filtering surface used.","boardPrep":"High RPF states (like pregnancy) can push filtration equilibrium later or eliminate it entirely, contributing to the physiologic rise in GFR seen in those states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this is the opposite scenario from a low-RPF state, where equilibrium shifts earlier and can limit GFR below what Kf and pressures alone would predict","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"Why does glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure remain essentially constant along the length of the glomerular capillary, unlike the progressive decline seen along a typical systemic capillary?","choices":{"A":"The efferent arteriole adds a second downstream resistance that maintains glomerular pressure","B":"The glomerular capillary wall is rigid and cannot distend the way systemic capillary walls normally do","C":"Systemic capillaries generally lack any arteriolar resistance vessels controlling their flow","D":"Glomerular capillary blood flow is too slow for any pressure gradient to ever develop along its length","E":"The kidney actively regulates glomerular capillary pressure through neural reflexes acting on the vasculature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic capillaries have only a single upstream arteriole controlling inflow, so hydrostatic pressure falls progressively along the capillary as resistance is encountered downstream; glomerular capillaries are unique in having a second arteriole, the efferent arteriole, positioned downstream, whose resistance prevents the pressure decline that would otherwise occur as fluid is filtered out along the capillary length.","boardPrep":"The efferent arteriole is the anatomic feature unique to the glomerulus that keeps PGC constant along its length — no other capillary bed in the body has this two-arteriole arrangement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this same efferent arteriole is the site of action for angiotensin II's GFR-protective effect discussed in SDL 8","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"If filtration equilibrium is reached before blood reaches the efferent end, which statement correctly describes the blood leaving through the efferent arteriole?","choices":{"A":"Its oncotic pressure is lower than that of plasma entering the glomerulus","B":"Its oncotic pressure stays unchanged from the afferent arteriolar value","C":"Its oncotic pressure equals capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space pressure","D":"It contains measurable amounts of filtered protein that would not otherwise ever be present","E":"Its hydrostatic pressure is unchanged from the glomerular capillary value in the peritubular capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By definition, filtration equilibrium is reached once glomerular capillary oncotic pressure has risen enough to equal the net hydrostatic driving pressure, so net ultrafiltration pressure falls to zero. The blood leaving through the efferent arteriole therefore carries an oncotic pressure equal to capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space pressure, higher than that of afferent plasma because filtration concentrated the protein, and this elevated oncotic pressure becomes the driving force for peritubular capillary reabsorption downstream. A lower or unchanged oncotic pressure, filtered protein in the efferent blood, and an unchanged glomerular-level hydrostatic pressure downstream are all incompatible with this.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At filtration equilibrium, oncotic pressure equals PGC minus PBS; that value carries into the peritubular capillary as the driving force for reabsorption, the direct physiologic link between glomerular filtration and proximal tubular reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this handoff of oncotic pressure from glomerular to peritubular capillary blood is the central concept bridging this SDL to SDL 10's proximal tubule reabsorption discussion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with bilateral renal artery stenosis is started on an ACE inhibitor. Based on the hemodynamic consequences of efferent arteriolar effects, why can this precipitate acute kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Blocking angiotensin II causes disproportionate afferent arteriolar constriction that directly reduces renal blood flow while efferent tone stays unchanged","B":"Blocking angiotensin II removes the efferent constriction that was maintaining glomerular pressure despite low renal blood flow","C":"ACE inhibitors directly damage the glomerular basement membrane, independent of any hemodynamic effect on the arterioles","D":"ACE inhibitors cause systemic hypotension severe enough to trigger prerenal azotemia in most treated patients","E":"Blocking angiotensin II increases aldosterone secretion enough to cause dangerous systemic volume overload"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In bilateral renal artery stenosis, reduced renal perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin system, and the resulting angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole to maintain glomerular capillary pressure and GFR despite low renal blood flow; an ACE inhibitor removes this compensatory efferent constriction, causing glomerular capillary pressure and GFR to collapse even though renal blood flow itself may not change much further.","boardPrep":"Bilateral renal artery stenosis + ACE inhibitor/ARB = classic acute kidney injury because the kidney was relying on angiotensin II's efferent constriction just to maintain GFR at a low perfusion pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this scenario is the clinical endpoint of the same efferent-selective angiotensin II mechanism first introduced in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients each have a 20% fall in renal plasma flow from arteriolar constriction. Patient A has selective afferent arteriolar constriction and Patient B has selective efferent arteriolar constriction. Which patient has the higher filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"Patient B, since efferent constriction raises GFR while afferent constriction lowers it proportionally","B":"Patient A, because afferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure more than efferent constriction does","C":"Both patients have identical filtration fractions, since renal plasma flow is the same in each","D":"Patient A, because afferent constriction preserves a normal net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"Neither patient's filtration fraction can be determined without knowing absolute renal blood flow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Filtration fraction equals GFR divided by RPF; afferent constriction lowers both RPF and GFR proportionally, leaving filtration fraction unchanged, while efferent constriction lowers RPF but raises GFR by increasing glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, so Patient B's filtration fraction (GFR up, RPF down) is higher than Patient A's (both down proportionally).","boardPrep":"Same RPF change, different GFR direction: afferent constriction keeps filtration fraction flat, efferent constriction pushes filtration fraction up — a favorite conceptual comparison question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — recognizing this filtration-fraction divergence is the fastest way to distinguish an afferent-predominant from an efferent-predominant vasoconstrictor state on an exam vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in hemorrhagic shock has both elevated sympathetic tone (afferent-predominant) and elevated angiotensin II (efferent-predominant) acting simultaneously. What is the net expected effect on GFR compared to RPF?","choices":{"A":"RPF and GFR fall by exactly the same proportion, since the two mechanisms cancel out completely","B":"RPF is relatively protected while GFR falls substantially, the reverse of the expected pattern","C":"Both RPF and GFR rise, since the two mechanisms produce opposing effects that net to an increase","D":"GFR falls to zero immediately, since both arterioles are simultaneously maximally constricted","E":"RPF falls substantially while GFR is relatively protected, falling proportionally less"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic tone constricts the afferent arteriole (lowering both RPF and GFR), while angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole (lowering RPF but raising GFR); acting together, both mechanisms substantially reduce RPF, but the efferent-predominant angiotensin II effect partially offsets the afferent-driven fall in GFR, so GFR is relatively protected and falls proportionally less than RPF, raising the filtration fraction even as absolute GFR still declines somewhat.","boardPrep":"In shock, RPF always falls more than GFR because angiotensin II's efferent-predominant action partially protects GFR — this is why filtration fraction typically rises in hypovolemic states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this combined afferent-plus-efferent mechanism is the physiologic reason a patient can maintain a deceptively preserved GFR early in shock despite a large fall in renal blood flow","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Transport Kinetics & Saturable Carrier Curves","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"On the glucose titration curve, what does the term \"splay\" describe?","choices":{"A":"The portion of the curve where reabsorption nears saturation but is not yet fully saturated","B":"The portion of the curve where no glucose reabsorption occurs at any plasma concentration","C":"The specific plasma concentration at which glucose first begins appearing in the urine","D":"The maximum rate of glucose reabsorption reached once carriers become fully saturated","E":"The portion of the curve where glucose filtration exceeds glucose reabsorption at any concentration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Splay is the region of the glucose titration curve near the transport maximum where reabsorption is still increasing but has not yet reached its plateau; because of splay, glucose begins appearing in the urine (at threshold) at a plasma concentration lower than the one at which reabsorption actually levels off at Tm.","boardPrep":"Splay = the \"not-quite-saturated-yet\" zone near Tm — it explains why glucosuria threshold occurs below the true Tm value, not exactly at it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — splay has two explanations: low-affinity Na+-glucose cotransporters near Tm, and heterogeneity of Tm values across individual nephrons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the fundamental difference between the glucose titration curve and the para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) titration curve?","choices":{"A":"Glucose has no transport maximum, while PAH has a well-defined transport maximum","B":"Glucose is handled entirely by passive paracellular diffusion, while PAH is handled entirely by active transport","C":"Glucose illustrates a saturable reabsorptive pathway, while PAH illustrates a saturable secretory pathway","D":"Glucose excretion rises linearly with plasma concentration, while PAH excretion does not rise once Tm is reached","E":"Glucose is filtered but not reabsorbed, while PAH is reabsorbed but not secreted"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The glucose titration curve tracks a carrier-mediated reabsorptive pathway (Na+-glucose cotransport) that becomes saturated at Tm, causing excretion to rise sharply once Tm is exceeded, whereas the PAH titration curve tracks a carrier-mediated secretory pathway that becomes saturated at its own Tm, causing excretion to rise steeply below Tm (filtration plus secretion) and then less steeply above Tm (filtration alone, since secretion is already saturated).","boardPrep":"Same saturation-kinetics logic, opposite direction: glucose's Tm limits how much can be reabsorbed; PAH's Tm limits how much can be secreted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — recognizing which curve shape belongs to a reabsorptive versus a secretory pathway is the fastest way to identify an unlabeled titration curve on an exam","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"Above the transport maximum (Tm) for PAH, why does excretion continue to rise with increasing plasma PAH concentration, even though secretion has already saturated?","choices":{"A":"Because secretion capacity somehow increases automatically once Tm is exceeded","B":"Because PAH reabsorption begins immediately above Tm and adds to total excretion","C":"Because renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate both rise automatically at high plasma PAH concentrations","D":"Because excretion above Tm is entirely independent of plasma PAH concentration","E":"Because the filtration component of excretion keeps climbing linearly with plasma concentration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For a secreted substance, excretion equals the sum of filtration and secretion; once secretion saturates at Tm, only the filtration component (which is not saturable and rises linearly with plasma concentration) continues to increase excretion, producing the shallower slope seen above Tm on the PAH titration curve.","boardPrep":"Above Tm, PAH excretion still climbs, just more slowly, because filtration alone (a linear, non-saturable process) is carrying the increase once secretion maxes out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this same filtration-plus-saturable-transport logic applies to any secreted or reabsorbed substance with a defined Tm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"What creates the driving force for passive urea reabsorption along the proximal tubule, given that the initial filtrate has the same urea concentration as blood?","choices":{"A":"Urea is actively pumped out of the tubular fluid by a dedicated, energy-requiring transporter in the proximal tubule","B":"Proximal tubule cells synthesize additional urea that dilutes the luminal concentration substantially","C":"Urea is cotransported together with glucose out of the proximal tubule lumen","D":"Reabsorbing water without reabsorbing urea raises its tubular concentration above the filtered level","E":"Bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule directly drives parallel urea reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As water is reabsorbed along the proximal tubule, urea lags slightly behind, so its luminal concentration rises above that of blood, creating a concentration gradient that drives passive (diffusive) urea reabsorption; by the end of the proximal tubule, about 50% of the filtered urea has been reabsorbed this way.","boardPrep":"Urea reabsorption follows water reabsorption passively — no dedicated proximal tubule urea pump is needed, just the concentration gradient water reabsorption creates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this same principle (water reabsorption concentrates urea, driving further passive reabsorption) recurs at each downstream urea-permeable segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does the percentage of filtered urea remaining in the tubular lumen rise from 50% at the end of the proximal tubule to 110% at the bend of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"Urea is synthesized de novo directly by thin descending limb epithelial cells themselves","B":"Urea is secreted into the thin descending limb from the high-urea interstitial fluid of the inner medulla","C":"Water reabsorption in the thin descending limb concentrates urea there without any secretion ever occurring","D":"A calculation artifact causes the percentage to exceed 100% despite no net addition of urea occurring","E":"The thin ascending limb actively secretes urea into the descending limb fluid further upstream"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thin descending limb of the loop of Henle passes through the inner medulla, where a high interstitial urea concentration (established by other mechanisms) creates a gradient favoring diffusion of urea into the tubular lumen; this secretion adds more urea than was reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, so the amount present at the bend of the loop actually exceeds the original filtered load, reaching about 110%.","boardPrep":"Urea handling is not one-directional: reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (down to 50%), then secreted back in the thin descending limb (up to 110%) before ADH-dependent reabsorption downstream brings it back down.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this urea secretion into the descending limb is part of \"urea recycling,\" which contributes to the corticopapillary osmotic gradient discussed later in this block","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"In the presence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), what happens to urea handling specifically in the inner medullary collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"ADH upregulates UT1, increasing facilitated urea reabsorption down its gradient","B":"ADH inhibits UT1, preventing any further urea reabsorption at this segment","C":"ADH stimulates active, energy-requiring urea secretion into the collecting duct lumen","D":"ADH affects water permeability at this segment but leaves urea transport unchanged","E":"ADH converts urea transport in this segment from facilitated diffusion to active transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The inner medullary collecting duct expresses UT1, a facilitated urea transporter that is upregulated by ADH; in the presence of ADH, UT1 activity increases urea reabsorption down its concentration gradient into the inner medullary interstitium, ultimately leaving only about 40% of the original filtered urea to be excreted.","boardPrep":"ADH does double duty in the inner medullary collecting duct: it increases water permeability via aquaporins AND upregulates UT1 for urea reabsorption — both contribute to the corticopapillary gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this UT1 upregulation is a distinct ADH target separate from its aquaporin-2 water channel effect, even though both act in the same nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What percentage of the filtered load of uric acid is ultimately excreted in the urine under normal conditions, after the full sequence of proximal tubule reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 1%","B":"Approximately 25%","C":"Approximately 50%","D":"Approximately 90%","E":"Approximately 10%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uric acid is freely filtered (100% at Bowman space), then 99% is reabsorbed by URAT1 in the proximal convoluted tubule, then roughly half of the original filtered load is secreted back into the lumen (bringing the total present back up to about 50%), and finally most of that secreted uric acid is reabsorbed again by URAT1 in the proximal straight tubule, leaving approximately 10% of the original filtered load to be excreted.","boardPrep":"Uric acid's three-step dance (reabsorb ~99%, secrete back to ~50%, reabsorb again down to ~10%) nets out to about 10% excreted — memorize this final number, since drugs act on the individual steps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this final 10% figure is the baseline that probenecid and salicylates shift in opposite pharmacologic directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Probenecid inhibits both uric acid reabsorption and uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule. What is its net effect on uric acid excretion, and why?","choices":{"A":"Excretion increases, because probenecid blocks more reabsorption than secretion at baseline","B":"Excretion decreases, because probenecid blocks more secretion than reabsorption at baseline","C":"Excretion is unchanged, because the two inhibited processes cancel each other out exactly","D":"Excretion increases, because probenecid selectively stimulates tubular uric acid secretion","E":"Excretion decreases, because probenecid prevents uric acid from being filtered at the glomerulus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because baseline uric acid reabsorption (about 99% of the filtered load) is quantitatively much greater than baseline uric acid secretion, blocking both processes with probenecid removes proportionally more reabsorption than secretion, so the net effect is decreased reabsorption and increased uric acid excretion, making probenecid a uricosuric drug used to treat hyperuricemia and gout.","boardPrep":"Probenecid blocks both uric acid reabsorption (URAT1) and secretion (organic anion transporters), but since reabsorption is the dominant process at baseline, the net result is always more excretion; this is why probenecid treats gout.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — contrast this dual-blockade mechanism with salicylate's dose-dependent, single-direction-predominant mechanism in the next objective's teaching point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient takes a low dose of aspirin (salicylate) for cardioprotection. What is the expected effect on serum uric acid concentration?","choices":{"A":"Serum uric acid falls, because low-dose salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid reabsorption","B":"Serum uric acid rises, because low-dose salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid secretion","C":"Serum uric acid is unaffected, since salicylate has no effect on uric acid transport at any dose","D":"Serum uric acid rises, because low-dose salicylate directly stimulates hepatic uric acid production","E":"Serum uric acid falls, because low-dose salicylate increases the glomerular filtration of uric acid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At low doses, salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule; because secretion is blocked while reabsorption remains largely intact, net uric acid reabsorption increases, decreasing uric acid excretion and raising the blood concentration of uric acid, the opposite of the effect wanted in a patient being treated for gout.","boardPrep":"Low-dose aspirin RAISES uric acid (secretion-blocking, can precipitate gout); high-dose aspirin LOWERS uric acid (reabsorption-blocking, uricosuric) — a classic dose-dependent paradox worth memorizing exactly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this paradox is why low-dose aspirin is used cautiously in patients with a history of gout, while high-dose salicylates historically had the opposite, uricosuric effect","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"For a weak acid such as salicylate, which chemical form can diffuse across renal tubular cells by nonionic diffusion?","choices":{"A":"The charged, deprotonated A- form of the molecule","B":"Both the HA and A- forms diffuse equally well","C":"The uncharged, protonated HA form of the molecule","D":"Neither form can diffuse; salicylate requires active transport in both directions","E":"The charged form diffuses faster than the uncharged form"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonionic diffusion refers to the fact that only the uncharged species of a weak acid or base can cross the lipid bilayer of tubular cell membranes; for a weak acid, this is the protonated HA form, while the charged A- form cannot diffuse and remains trapped on whichever side of the membrane it currently occupies.","boardPrep":"\"Nonionic\" = uncharged = the only form that crosses the membrane — this single rule explains the entire pH-trapping phenomenon for both weak acids and weak bases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this exact rule, applied to ammonia (NH3) versus ammonium (NH4+), reappears in the acid-base physiology covered later in this block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does alkalinizing the urine increase the clearance of a weak acid such as salicylate?","choices":{"A":"Alkaline urine favors the uncharged HA form, which is actively secreted at a faster rate","B":"Alkaline urine directly increases glomerular filtration of salicylate independent of its charge state","C":"Alkaline urine denatures the salicylate molecule, preventing any tubular reabsorption","D":"Alkaline urine favors the charged A- form of salicylate, trapping it for excretion","E":"Alkaline urine decreases renal blood flow, concentrating salicylate in a smaller urine volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At alkaline urine pH, the equilibrium for a weak acid shifts toward the charged A- form, which cannot diffuse back across tubular cells into the blood; because this \"back-diffusion\" is minimized, more of the secreted and filtered salicylate remains trapped in the urine and is excreted, increasing its clearance.","boardPrep":"Alkalinize the urine to treat salicylate (aspirin) overdose — alkaline pH traps the charged A- form in the urine, exactly the same logic used for other weak acids like phenobarbital.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this is a direct clinical application tested as \"how do you treat aspirin overdose,\" with the mechanistic answer being urine alkalinization via nonionic diffusion/pH trapping","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"For a weak base such as morphine, how does the pH-trapping effect on excretion differ from that of a weak acid such as salicylate?","choices":{"A":"The relationship is identical: alkaline urine increases clearance of both weak acids and weak bases","B":"The relationship is mirrored: acidic urine traps the weak base for elimination","C":"Weak bases are unaffected by urine pH manipulation","D":"Acidic urine substantially decreases the overall renal clearance of weak bases","E":"Weak bases require alkaline urine specifically for effective pH-dependent trapping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For a weak base, the charged BH+ form predominates at acidic pH and cannot diffuse back into blood, so acidic urine traps the weak base for excretion, increasing its clearance; this is the mirror image of the weak acid case, where alkaline urine (which favors the charged A- form) increases clearance instead.","boardPrep":"Weak acids: alkalinize urine to trap and excrete. Weak bases: acidify urine to trap and excrete. Opposite pH manipulations for opposite molecule classes — a frequently tested distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this mirrored relationship is why amphetamine (a weak base) overdose is treated with urine acidification, the opposite intervention from salicylate overdose","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"A woman with polyuria and polydipsia is found to have glucosuria. Formal titration testing shows a normal Tm for glucose (375 mg/min) and a normal threshold. What is the most likely underlying cause of her glucosuria?","choices":{"A":"A congenitally low renal Tm for glucose that causes glucosuria even at normal plasma glucose levels","B":"An elevated plasma glucose concentration from insufficient insulin secretion","C":"A defect in the Na+-glucose cotransporter that lowers its affinity for glucose","D":"Complete absence of GLUT1 transporters in the proximal tubule basolateral membrane","E":"An acquired reduction in functioning nephron mass that lowers the whole-kidney Tm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because her Tm and threshold are both normal, the renal glucose transport machinery itself is intact; glucosuria in this setting therefore reflects a plasma glucose concentration high enough to exceed even a normal Tm and threshold, which points to insufficient insulin secretion (uncontrolled diabetes mellitus) as the cause rather than any renal defect, distinguishing this from a primary renal glucosuria in which the Tm itself would be abnormally low.","boardPrep":"Normal Tm + glucosuria = the problem is upstream (too much filtered glucose from hyperglycemia); low Tm + glucosuria at normal blood glucose = the problem is the renal transporter itself (primary renal glucosuria).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this exact reasoning (checking whether Tm itself is abnormal) is the standard way board vignettes distinguish diabetic glucosuria from primary renal glucosuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients both have glucosuria. Patient A has an elevated plasma glucose concentration with a normal renal Tm for glucose. Patient B has a normal plasma glucose concentration with an abnormally low renal Tm for glucose. Which patient's glucosuria reflects a primary renal transport disorder?","choices":{"A":"Patient A, because an elevated plasma glucose concentration indicates a renal transport disorder","B":"Both patients have a primary renal transport disorder, since both have glucosuria","C":"Neither patient has a renal transport disorder, since glucosuria is not inherently pathologic","D":"Patient B, since glucosuria occurs without any elevation in plasma glucose","E":"Patient A, because Tm values become clinically meaningful specifically when plasma glucose is also elevated"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patient B's glucosuria occurs at a normal plasma glucose concentration, which is only possible if the renal transport system itself has an abnormally low capacity (a low Tm), identifying a primary renal glucosuria; Patient A's glucosuria, in contrast, occurs because plasma glucose has risen high enough to exceed an otherwise normal Tm, identifying a prerenal (metabolic) cause such as diabetes mellitus rather than a transporter defect.","boardPrep":"The key discriminator is always plasma glucose at the time of glucosuria: normal plasma glucose + glucosuria = renal problem; elevated plasma glucose + glucosuria = a filtered-load problem, not a transporter problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — primary renal glucosuria is a benign, isolated proximal tubule transport abnormality and should not be mistaken for diabetes mellitus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"Why does the observed whole-kidney Tm for glucose reflect an average across many individual nephrons rather than a single fixed threshold shared by every nephron?","choices":{"A":"Because nephron-to-nephron heterogeneity in Tm values naturally contributes to the resulting splay","B":"Because glucose reabsorption capacity is randomly redistributed among nephrons on a frequent rotating basis","C":"Because glucose reabsorption is concentrated in a small, fixed subset of nephrons, with the remaining nephrons contributing very little","D":"Because whole-kidney Tm is calculated by simply summing each nephron's maximum urine flow rate","E":"Because nephron heterogeneity affects PAH secretion but has no analogous effect on glucose reabsorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Individual nephrons vary somewhat in their own individual Tm for glucose; because the measured whole-kidney Tm is effectively an average of many individual nephron Tm values, some nephrons begin spilling glucose into the urine before others reach their own Tm, which is the second explanation (alongside low cotransporter affinity near saturation) for why splay occurs on the whole-kidney titration curve.","boardPrep":"Splay has two causes: (1) low-affinity cotransporters near Tm, and (2) nephron-to-nephron heterogeneity in individual Tm values averaging out to a smeared whole-kidney curve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this nephron heterogeneity concept is a favorite \"why\" question following any glucose titration curve recall question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with central diabetes insipidus has essentially no circulating ADH. Compared to a healthy individual with normal ADH levels, what is the expected effect on the fraction of filtered urea excreted?","choices":{"A":"A smaller fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since ADH absence substantially increases proximal tubule urea reabsorption capacity","B":"The fraction of filtered urea excreted is unaffected by ADH, since urea transport is entirely ADH-independent","C":"A larger fraction of filtered urea is excreted, driven by disrupted proximal tubule reabsorption","D":"A larger fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since UT1-mediated reabsorption in the medullary collecting duct is reduced","E":"A smaller fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since thin descending limb secretion is abolished without ADH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Without ADH, UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct is not upregulated, so facilitated urea reabsorption at this final segment is markedly reduced compared to the ADH-present state; because less urea is reabsorbed at this last opportunity, a larger fraction of the originally filtered urea remains in the tubular fluid and is excreted, rather than the approximately 40% seen when ADH is present and UT1 is active.","boardPrep":"No ADH = no UT1 upregulation = less urea reabsorbed in the inner medullary collecting duct = more urea excreted, contributing (along with massive water loss) to the dilute urine of diabetes insipidus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this ADH-dependent urea handling is distinct from, but parallel to, ADH's water-permeability effect via aquaporin-2 in the same nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"The late distal tubule and the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts are impermeable to urea. What is the functional consequence of this impermeability when ADH is present and water is being reabsorbed in these segments?","choices":{"A":"Urea is actively degraded by these segments before reaching the inner medullary collecting duct","B":"Urea impermeability prevents any further change in urea concentration in these segments","C":"Urea becomes progressively more concentrated in the tubular fluid as its volume shrinks around it","D":"Urea continues diffusing passively across these segments regardless of the impermeability described","E":"Urea impermeability causes these segments to secrete additional urea into the lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because these segments are impermeable to urea but, in the presence of ADH, remain permeable to water (which is reabsorbed), urea is \"left behind\" as water leaves the tubular fluid, progressively concentrating the urea that remains in the lumen; this sets up a high luminal urea concentration entering the inner medullary collecting duct, where UT1 can then act on that already-concentrated urea.","boardPrep":"Urea-impermeable-but-water-permeable segments concentrate urea by exclusion, priming it for the UT1-mediated reabsorption that follows in the inner medullary collecting duct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this concentrating step is why UT1's downstream reabsorption can be so effective despite acting on only one nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"Urea reabsorbed via UT1 into the inner medullary interstitium is described as contributing to \"urea recycling.\" What does this term most directly refer to?","choices":{"A":"Urea is chemically converted into a different nitrogenous waste product and then re-excreted","B":"This urea moves from the medulla back through the thin descending limb to begin another loop pass","C":"Urea recycling refers to hepatic reuptake and reprocessing of urea excreted in the urine","D":"Urea recycling describes glomerular refiltration of urea that bypassed initial filtration","E":"Urea recycling refers to urea being reabsorbed and permanently retained without ever re-entering the nephron"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Urea reabsorbed by UT1 into the inner medullary interstitium raises the local interstitial urea concentration, which then drives urea back into the thin descending limb of the loop of Henle (which is permeable to urea via secretion), allowing the same urea molecules to re-enter the tubular fluid and be processed again; this cycling of urea between the interstitium and the descending limb is what \"urea recycling\" describes, and it contributes to the corticopapillary osmotic gradient.","boardPrep":"Urea recycling = interstitium to descending limb to lumen to eventual re-reabsorption downstream — a closed loop that helps build and maintain the medullary concentration gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this recycling loop is a key contributor to the countercurrent multiplication mechanism responsible for the kidney's ability to concentrate urine","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with gout is taking probenecid. High-dose aspirin is added for an unrelated indication. Based on the dose-dependent effect of salicylates on uric acid handling, what is aspirin's effect on uric acid excretion at this dose?","choices":{"A":"At this considerably higher dose than usual, aspirin also becomes uricosuric","B":"High-dose aspirin has no interaction with probenecid whatsoever, acting through an entirely separate URAT1 site","C":"High-dose aspirin blocks probenecid's access to URAT1, eliminating its uricosuric effect","D":"High-dose aspirin converts probenecid's effect from uricosuric to antiuricosuric","E":"High-dose aspirin interacts with probenecid specifically at low aspirin doses, not high ones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"High-dose salicylate independently inhibits uric acid reabsorption, so at this dose aspirin adds a uricosuric effect of its own, in contrast to low-dose aspirin, which is antiuricosuric because it blocks uric acid secretion more than reabsorption. The other options misstate the dose dependence or claim effects on probenecid that the question does not ask about.","boardPrep":"Dose matters enormously: low-dose aspirin raises uric acid by blocking secretion, while very high-dose aspirin is uricosuric and lowers uric acid; always ask what dose before predicting a salicylate-uric acid effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this dose-dependent, direction-flipping interaction is a favorite integration question combining pharmacology with the underlying URAT1 transport physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"Two investigational drugs each block one step of proximal tubule uric acid handling and leave the other steps intact: Drug X selectively blocks only URAT1-mediated reabsorption, while Drug Y selectively blocks only the secretory step. Which drug is expected to lower serum uric acid, and why?","choices":{"A":"Drug Y, since secretion contributes more to net uric acid handling than reabsorption does at baseline","B":"Both drugs lower serum uric acid to the same degree, since Drug X blocks reabsorption and Drug Y blocks secretion equally","C":"Drug X, since removing reabsorption eliminates the dominant of the two opposing transport processes","D":"Neither drug changes serum uric acid, because the remaining unblocked step fully compensates on its own","E":"Drug Y, because tubular secretion is quantitatively the larger baseline transport process"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Baseline uric acid reabsorption (about 99% of the filtered load) is quantitatively far greater than baseline secretion, so a drug that selectively blocks reabsorption (Drug X) removes the larger of the two opposing processes, sharply increasing the fraction of filtered uric acid that escapes reabsorption and is excreted; a drug that selectively blocks secretion instead (Drug Y) removes only the smaller process and would, if anything, tend to raise serum uric acid by leaving reabsorption unopposed, the same logic that makes low-dose salicylate antiuricosuric.","boardPrep":"Reabsorption is the dominant baseline process (~99%), so blocking it (Drug X, like probenecid) is uricosuric; blocking only secretion (Drug Y, like low-dose aspirin) leaves the dominant reabsorption process unopposed and raises uric acid instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this same reabsorption-dominates-secretion logic is what explains probenecid's net uricosuric effect despite blocking both transport directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"Why is uric acid's renal handling described as \"unusual\" compared to a typical reabsorbed or secreted solute such as glucose or PAH?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid is unique among nephron solutes in being neither filtered nor reabsorbed at any point","B":"Uric acid reabsorption is confined entirely to the distal convoluted tubule segment itself","C":"Uric acid secretion is confined entirely to the descending thin limb of the loop of Henle","D":"Uric acid bypasses glomerular filtration and enters the nephron tubule through secretion","E":"Uric acid undergoes both reabsorption and secretion within the same proximal tubule segment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most solutes are handled unidirectionally (glucose is only reabsorbed; PAH is only secreted), but uric acid undergoes reabsorption, then secretion, then reabsorption again, all within the proximal tubule, making its handling bidirectional and sequential within a single nephron segment, a pattern not seen with the other classic titration-curve examples.","boardPrep":"Uric acid is the exception to the \"one direction per solute\" rule — reabsorbed, then secreted, then reabsorbed again, all in the proximal tubule, netting out to about 10% excreted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — recognizing this bidirectional pattern is what makes uric acid pharmacology (probenecid, salicylates) more complex than glucose or PAH pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with a severe salicylate (aspirin) overdose. Based on the principle of nonionic diffusion, what is the appropriate emergency intervention to accelerate salicylate clearance, and why?","choices":{"A":"Administer intravenous ammonium chloride to acidify the urine, trapping the charged HA form for excretion","B":"Administer intravenous sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine, trapping the charged A- form for excretion","C":"Administer a loop diuretic, since increasing urine flow rate is sufficient regardless of urine pH","D":"Administer intravenous sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize the blood, since urine pH has no effect on salicylate clearance","E":"Administer intravenous calcium gluconate to precipitate salicylate directly in the renal tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salicylate is a weak acid, so alkalinizing the urine (with intravenous sodium bicarbonate) shifts the equilibrium toward the charged A- form, which cannot diffuse back into the blood across tubular cells and is therefore trapped in the urine for excretion, directly increasing salicylate clearance through the nonionic diffusion/pH-trapping mechanism.","boardPrep":"Salicylate overdose treatment: IV sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine — a classic emergency medicine application of the nonionic diffusion principle, distinct from bicarbonate's separate role in correcting any coexisting acidemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this is one of the most frequently tested direct clinical applications in this entire SDL, bridging basic transport physiology to emergency toxicology management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with a severe amphetamine overdose. Amphetamine is a weak base. Based on the mirrored logic of nonionic diffusion, what intervention would be expected to accelerate its renal clearance?","choices":{"A":"Acidifying the urine shifts amphetamine into its charged BH+ form, trapping it for excretion","B":"The same alkalinizing strategy used for salicylate overdose would be applied again","C":"Neither acidifying nor alkalinizing the urine changes amphetamine clearance","D":"Amphetamine trapping depends on blood pH, so systemic blood acidification would be the target intervention","E":"Forcing a maximal alkaline diuresis would trap the drug in the urine for excretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amphetamine, as a weak base, exists predominantly in its charged BH+ form at acidic pH; acidifying the urine therefore favors this charged form, which cannot diffuse back into the blood and is trapped for excretion, increasing amphetamine clearance, the mirror image of the alkalinization strategy used for the weak acid salicylate.","boardPrep":"Weak base overdose (amphetamine, PCP) -> acidify the urine; weak acid overdose (salicylate, phenobarbital) -> alkalinize the urine — memorize this pairing as a matched set, not two unrelated facts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — urine acidification for weak base overdose is generally not recommended clinically, unlike alkalinization for weak acids, but the underlying physiologic principle being tested is identical","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A weak acid drug is cleared mainly by secretion through the same proximal tubule organic acid transporter used by PAH. If a probenecid-like inhibitor blocked this secretory transporter, what would happen to the total amount of drug captured in the urine by pH trapping?","choices":{"A":"It would be entirely unaffected, since transporter blockade and pH trapping act via separate mechanisms","B":"It would rise, since transporter blockade increases the fraction of drug in the charged form","C":"It would reverse direction, so that acidic urine now trapped the drug for excretion","D":"It would depend on whether the inhibitor itself is a weak acid or a weak base","E":"It would fall, since less drug would reach the tubular fluid to be trapped in the first place"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonionic diffusion and pH trapping only matter for a drug that has already been secreted (or filtered) into the tubular fluid at a high enough concentration to create a gradient favoring back-diffusion in the first place; if the secretory transporter is blocked, less of the drug reaches the urine to begin with, so even a favorable urine pH has less total drug available to trap, diminishing the overall clearance benefit that pH manipulation alone could provide.","boardPrep":"pH trapping amplifies clearance of a drug that is already being secreted into the urine — it does not substitute for secretion, so blocking the secretory transporter undercuts the entire strategy from the start.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this integration point (secretion first, pH trapping second) is why urine alkalinization alone, without adequate urine flow and ongoing secretion, is not sufficient therapy for a severe overdose","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Proximal Tubular Transport & Isosmotic Reabsorption","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of the proximal tubule, [TF/P]Na+ remains approximately 1.0 even though roughly two-thirds of the filtered sodium has been reabsorbed by that point. What does this illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Sodium reabsorption occurs in exact, matched proportion to water reabsorption","B":"Sodium is not being reabsorbed in the proximal tubule even though filtered load is clearly being lost","C":"Water reabsorption is lagging well behind sodium reabsorption at this point in the nephron","D":"A [TF/P] ratio of 1.0 indicates that no filtration of sodium has yet occurred","E":"The measurement reflects a laboratory error, since reabsorbed solutes should lower the ratio"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A [TF/P]x ratio of 1.0 has two possible meanings: no transport has yet occurred, or solute and water are being reabsorbed in exact proportion (isosmotic reabsorption); for sodium in the proximal tubule, the second meaning applies, since water follows sodium reabsorption closely enough that the luminal sodium concentration stays equal to plasma even as the absolute amount of sodium falls.","boardPrep":"A [TF/P] ratio of 1.0 does not always mean \"nothing happened\" — for isosmotically handled solutes like proximal tubule sodium, it means solute and water left the lumen together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same isosmotic-reabsorption logic is why [TF/P]Na+ stays flat across the entire proximal tubule despite substantial sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"In the early proximal tubule, [TF/P]Cl- rises above 1.0 while [TF/P]HCO3- falls below 1.0. What does this divergence represent?","choices":{"A":"Chloride is being actively secreted into the lumen from the peritubular capillaries at this segment","B":"Bicarbonate is being secreted into the lumen while chloride is simultaneously reabsorbed","C":"Neither solute is undergoing any net transport, and the divergence simply reflects measurement variability","D":"Water reabsorption has stopped entirely at this point in the proximal tubule","E":"Bicarbonate is being reabsorbed preferentially relative to water at this point"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the early proximal tubule, sodium is reabsorbed preferentially with bicarbonate (via NHE3-driven bicarbonate reabsorption), so bicarbonate leaves the lumen faster than water does, dropping [TF/P]HCO3- well below 1.0; chloride, which is not preferentially reabsorbed in this segment, is left behind as water leaves, concentrating it in the lumen and raising [TF/P]Cl- above 1.0.","boardPrep":"Early proximal tubule: HCO3- ratio falls (reabsorbed faster than water), Cl- ratio rises (left behind) — this divergence is what builds the high luminal chloride concentration used downstream in the late proximal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same chloride-concentrating step is the direct setup for the late proximal tubule's NaCl paracellular reabsorption mechanism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Tubular fluid is sampled at a point along the nephron and [TF/P]inulin is measured at 3.0. What fraction of the originally filtered water has been reabsorbed at this point?","choices":{"A":"Approximately one-third (33%) of the filtered water","B":"Approximately three-quarters (75%) of the filtered water","C":"Approximately two-thirds (67%) of the filtered water","D":"Nearly the entire (over 99%) filtered water volume","E":"0% (no measurable amount) of the filtered water, since inulin is not reabsorbed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because inulin is freely filtered but never reabsorbed or secreted, its rising luminal concentration reflects only water removal; the fraction of filtered water reabsorbed equals 1 minus 1 divided by [TF/P]inulin, so at [TF/P]inulin = 3.0, the fraction reabsorbed is 1 - 1/3 = 2/3, or approximately 67%, consistent with the proximal tubule's characteristic isosmotic reabsorption of about two-thirds of the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Fraction of filtered water reabsorbed = 1 - 1/[TF/P]inulin — memorize this formula, since [TF/P]inulin = 3.0 at the end of a normal proximal tubule is a classic exam value.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same formula, applied to [TF/P]inulin = 125 at the end of the collecting duct during antidiuresis, is how overall urinary concentrating ability gets quantified","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the ultimate energy source that powers apical SGLT2-mediated glucose entry into early proximal tubule cells?","choices":{"A":"SGLT2 itself directly hydrolyzes ATP to power both sodium and glucose transport across the membrane","B":"Basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase because it maintains the sodium gradient that continuously drives SGLT2","C":"A separate apical H+-ATPase provides the energy for glucose entry completely independent of any sodium gradient","D":"Basolateral GLUT2 transporters actively pump glucose into the cell using hydrolyzed ATP directly","E":"Mitochondrial ATP synthase located within the brush border directly energizes SGLT2 without any pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SGLT2 itself performs no ATP hydrolysis; it is a secondary active transporter that uses the steep inward sodium electrochemical gradient established by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase (which primary-actively pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in) to move glucose into the cell against its own concentration gradient, coupling one glucose molecule to the downhill entry of one sodium ion.","boardPrep":"Every apical cotransporter in the early proximal tubule (SGLT2, Na+-amino acid, Na+-phosphate) is secondary active — the only primary active pump doing the actual ATP hydrolysis is the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this same basolateral-pump-as-ultimate-engine logic applies to every other proximal tubule apical cotransporter, not just SGLT2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"Why do the SGLT2 and Na+-amino acid cotransporters generate a lumen-negative transepithelial potential difference in the early proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"They cotransport chloride alongside sodium, making the lumen electronegative from chloride loss","B":"They pump negative ions into the lumen as a byproduct of glucose and amino acid reabsorption","C":"They bring net positive charge into the cell without any accompanying matched anion","D":"They are electroneutral exchangers that have no effect on transepithelial voltage","E":"They hyperpolarize the peritubular capillary blood while leaving the tubular lumen's potential unaffected"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters carry a sodium cation into the cell together with an uncharged organic solute (glucose or an amino acid), so no anion accompanies the sodium out of the lumen; this net movement of positive charge out of the lumen without a matched anion leaves the lumen relatively negative, generating the characteristic -4 mV lumen-negative potential difference of the early proximal tubule.","boardPrep":"Early proximal tubule = lumen-NEGATIVE (electrogenic Na+-organic solute cotransport); late proximal tubule = lumen-POSITIVE (Cl- diffusion potential) — a paired fact worth memorizing as opposites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this lumen-negative potential is the electrical mirror image of the lumen-positive potential generated downstream in the late proximal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"How does NHE3 (the apical Na+/H+ exchanger) in the early proximal tubule ultimately achieve bicarbonate reabsorption, given that NHE3 itself transports neither bicarbonate nor carbon dioxide?","choices":{"A":"Secreted H+ converts luminal bicarbonate to CO2 that diffuses into the cell and reforms bicarbonate","B":"NHE3 directly cotransports bicarbonate together with the hydrogen ion it secretes into the lumen","C":"NHE3 creates a lumen-positive potential that pulls bicarbonate across the tight junctions paracellularly","D":"NHE3 activates a separate basolateral bicarbonate channel that reabsorbs bicarbonate independently of any exchange","E":"NHE3 converts luminal bicarbonate directly into chloride via an intraluminal exchange reaction mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"NHE3 exchanges one luminal Na+ entry for one H+ secretion; the secreted H+ combines with filtered luminal HCO3- to form H2CO3, which brush-border carbonic anhydrase rapidly converts to CO2 and H2O that diffuse into the cell, where intracellular carbonic anhydrase reconstitutes H+ (recycled back out via NHE3) and HCO3- (which exits the basolateral membrane into blood); this indirect, CO2-shuttling mechanism is how an exchanger that moves no bicarbonate at all accounts for the bulk of proximal bicarbonate reabsorption.","boardPrep":"NHE3 reabsorbs bicarbonate indirectly: H+ out, combines with HCO3- to make CO2, CO2 diffuses in, gets remade into HCO3- inside the cell — the net effect is bicarbonate reabsorption without NHE3 ever touching a bicarbonate ion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this same CO2-shuttling logic is why carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., acetazolamide) block proximal bicarbonate reabsorption even though they target neither NHE3 nor a bicarbonate transporter directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does tubular fluid entering the late proximal tubule have an elevated chloride concentration of approximately 140 mEq/L, compared to about 105-108 mEq/L in peritubular blood?","choices":{"A":"Chloride is actively secreted into the tubular lumen throughout the early proximal tubule","B":"Preferential early proximal reabsorption of bicarbonate with sodium leaves chloride behind in the lumen","C":"The early proximal tubule actively synthesizes additional chloride ions from bicarbonate","D":"Chloride passes freely between lumen and interstitium and should equal plasma at any point along the tubule","E":"Aldosterone acts on the early proximal tubule to actively reabsorb bicarbonate in exchange for chloride secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because early proximal sodium reabsorption is preferentially coupled to bicarbonate, glucose, and amino acids rather than chloride, and water follows these reabsorbed solutes isosmotically, chloride is left behind and becomes progressively concentrated in the remaining tubular fluid; by the start of the late proximal tubule, this concentrating effect raises luminal chloride to roughly 140 mEq/L, well above the peritubular concentration.","boardPrep":"The high luminal chloride entering the late proximal tubule is not secreted — it is left behind as bicarbonate, glucose, and amino acids (and the water that follows them) exit the lumen upstream.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this concentrated luminal chloride is the entire driving force for both the paracellular and cellular NaCl reabsorption mechanisms that follow in the late proximal tubule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"What generates the lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference characteristic of the late proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"Passive paracellular diffusion of chloride down its concentration gradient out of the lumen","B":"Active secretion of sodium into the lumen through a dedicated basolateral sodium channel","C":"The same electrogenic Na+-glucose cotransport mechanism used in the early proximal tubule","D":"A dedicated apical proton pump that actively acidifies and electropositively charges the lumen","E":"Bicarbonate diffusing paracellularly out of the lumen, since very little remains by this point in the tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The high luminal chloride concentration established upstream drives passive paracellular diffusion of negatively charged chloride ions through the tight junctions into the peritubular interstitium; as negative charge leaves the lumen, the lumen becomes relatively electropositive, generating the characteristic +4 mV lumen-positive potential difference that then drives passive paracellular sodium reabsorption to preserve electroneutrality.","boardPrep":"Late proximal tubule lumen-positive potential comes from chloride LEAVING the lumen (not sodium entering it) — the resulting positive lumen then repels sodium out through the tight junctions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this same lumen-positive, chloride-diffusion-driven mechanism recurs in other nephron segments wherever a high luminal chloride gradient exists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"In the cellular (transcellular) pathway for late proximal NaCl reabsorption, what is the role of the apical Cl-/formate exchanger operating in parallel with NHE3?","choices":{"A":"It directly cotransports chloride together with sodium across the apical membrane in a single step","B":"It actively secretes formate into the blood to generate the electrochemical gradient for chloride entry","C":"It converts luminal chloride into bicarbonate before allowing its entry into the cell","D":"It allows luminal chloride to enter the cell in exchange for intracellular formate","E":"It operates independently of NHE3, using its own ATP-hydrolysis-driven mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical Cl-/formate (or oxalate) exchanger uses the steep luminal-to-cell chloride gradient to drive chloride entry into the cell in exchange for intracellular formate leaving into the lumen; the secreted formate combines with H+ (also secreted by the parallel NHE3) to form uncharged formic acid, which diffuses passively back into the cell to be recycled, so the net transcellular result is one Na+ (via NHE3) and one Cl- (via the anion exchanger) entering the cell together.","boardPrep":"The Cl-/formate exchanger and NHE3 work as a parallel pair: NHE3 brings in Na+/sends out H+, the anion exchanger brings in Cl-/sends out formate, and the secreted H+ and formate recombine and diffuse back in to keep the whole cycle going.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this same parallel-exchanger strategy (two countertransporters sharing a recycled neutral intermediate) is a recurring transport-physiology pattern worth recognizing in other epithelia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"In the proximal tubule, which event is the primary driver of isosmotic fluid reabsorption, with water movement following as a consequence?","choices":{"A":"Active pumping of water through luminal channels, followed passively by the reabsorption of sodium","B":"Hydrostatic pressure inside the tubular lumen, which forces fluid and solute across the epithelium together","C":"A standing osmotic gradient across the epithelium, which pulls water out before any solute follows","D":"Secretion of osmotically active particles into the lumen, which then draws solute and water together into the cells","E":"Active reabsorption of solute, mainly sodium with its accompanying anions, followed passively by water"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Solute reabsorption is the primary event in isosmotic reabsorption. Sodium enters the cell across the luminal membrane by several mechanisms, and because the luminal membrane is permeable to water, water follows the solute to maintain isosmolarity. The major cation is sodium, accompanied by bicarbonate in the early proximal tubule and chloride in the late proximal tubule. Water follows solute passively, so it cannot be the initiating step.","boardPrep":"In the proximal tubule, solute reabsorption is the primary event and water follows passively, which is why the reabsorbed fluid stays isosmotic to plasma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"The Na+-K+ ATPase that drives fluid reabsorption in the proximal tubule sits in which membrane, and what happens to fluid in the adjacent lateral intercellular spaces as the pump extrudes sodium?","choices":{"A":"Luminal membrane; sodium is pumped into the tubular fluid and water follows, so fluid is drained from the spaces","B":"Basolateral membrane; sodium is pumped into the cell and water follows, so fluid is removed from the spaces","C":"Tight junction; sodium is pumped across the junction and water follows, so fluid accumulates in the lumen","D":"Basolateral membrane; sodium is pumped out of the cell and water follows, so fluid accumulates in the spaces","E":"Capillary endothelium; sodium is pumped into the blood and water follows, so the spaces become hypotonic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Na+-K+ ATPase is located in the basolateral membranes, where basal refers to the membrane facing the peritubular capillary and lateral refers to the membrane facing the lateral intercellular spaces. As sodium is pumped out of the cell, water again follows passively, so isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces. Electron micrographs show these spaces widening when proximal tubule reabsorption increases. Pumping sodium into the cell or into the lumen would work against reabsorption.","boardPrep":"The proximal tubule Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral, and fluid collects in the lateral intercellular spaces before the peritubular capillary takes it up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Isosmotic fluid that accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces of the proximal tubule must still be reabsorbed into the circulation. Which force is the major driver of that uptake?","choices":{"A":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid from the spaces into the vessel","B":"The high oncotic pressure of the protein-rich peritubular capillary blood","C":"The lumen-positive transepithelial potential of the late proximal tubule","D":"Angiotensin II stimulation of apical sodium-hydrogen exchange in the cells","E":"Increased water permeability of the luminal membrane of the tubule cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The major Starling force driving uptake of isosmotic fluid from the lateral intercellular spaces is the high oncotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood. Glomerular filtration removes fluid but leaves protein behind, raising the protein concentration and oncotic pressure of blood that leaves the glomerulus and becomes peritubular capillary blood. Capillary hydrostatic pressure opposes reabsorption, while the transepithelial potential, angiotensin II, and luminal water permeability do not describe the force that moves the fluid into the vessel.","boardPrep":"Peritubular capillary oncotic pressure is the most important force pulling isosmotic fluid out of the proximal tubule spaces, and it is high because glomerular filtration concentrates plasma protein.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher measures [TF/P]x for an unknown solute x at the end of the proximal tubule and finds it equal to 0.4, while [TF/P]inulin at the same point is 3.0. What does the double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin reveal about solute x?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 40% of the filtered load of solute x remains in the tubular fluid, meaning about 60% has been reabsorbed","B":"Approximately 75% of the filtered load of solute x has been secreted into the tubular fluid beyond the filtered amount","C":"Approximately 13% of the filtered load of solute x remains in the tubular fluid, meaning about 87% has been reabsorbed","D":"Solute x is handled identically to inulin, since both ratios are being compared directly","E":"The double ratio cannot be calculated because the two ratios were measured at different absolute concentrations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin corrects the raw [TF/P]x for the effect of water reabsorption, directly yielding the fraction of the filtered load remaining in the tubular fluid; here that calculation gives 0.4 / 3.0 = 0.133, or about 13% remaining, meaning approximately 87% of the filtered load of solute x has been reabsorbed by this point in the nephron.","boardPrep":"The double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin directly gives the fraction of filtered load remaining — do not confuse the raw [TF/P]x value (0.4 here) with this corrected fraction (0.133 here); they are very different numbers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same double-ratio calculation, applied to sodium at the end of the proximal tubule, is what yields the classic 33%-remaining/67%-reabsorbed figure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"By the midpoint of the proximal tubule, essentially 100% of filtered glucose and amino acids have been reabsorbed, and their [TF/P] ratios approach zero. A student argues this means water reabsorption at this point must also have essentially stopped. Why is this reasoning flawed?","choices":{"A":"A [TF/P] ratio approaching zero is mathematically impossible unless water reabsorption has also stopped","B":"A [TF/P] ratio approaching zero reflects near-complete solute reabsorption, independent of the water reabsorption rate","C":"Glucose and amino acids are reabsorbed independently of any water movement throughout the nephron","D":"Water reabsorption in the proximal tubule waits until organic solute reabsorption is essentially complete before it begins","E":"The student is correct, since a falling [TF/P] ratio for any solute indicates falling water reabsorption as well"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The [TF/P] ratio for a given solute reflects the balance between that solute's own reabsorption rate and water's reabsorption rate; when a solute like glucose is reabsorbed essentially completely and very early (avidly, relative to water), its ratio falls toward zero, but water reabsorption continues steadily throughout the proximal tubule via isosmotic mechanisms tied primarily to sodium, entirely independent of whether glucose remains present in the lumen.","boardPrep":"A falling [TF/P] ratio for one solute (like glucose reaching near-zero) tells you about that solute's reabsorption relative to water — it does not by itself tell you whether water reabsorption sped up, slowed down, or stayed the same.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — contrast this with [TF/P]inulin, which is the one ratio that DOES directly and exclusively track water reabsorption, precisely because inulin itself is never transported","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A solute y is measured with [TF/P]y = 1.5 at a point in the nephron. Reabsorption of solute y lagging behind water reabsorption and net secretion of solute y would each produce this raw value. What additional piece of information would best distinguish these two scenarios?","choices":{"A":"The [TF/P]inulin measurement taken from the same tubular fluid sample","B":"The plasma concentration of solute y measured at the same time as the tubular fluid sample","C":"The total urine flow rate collected over a full 24-hour period","D":"The molecular weight of solute y compared with the molecular weight of inulin","E":"The distance in millimeters from Bowman's space to the sampling point"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A raw [TF/P]x value greater than 1.0 is ambiguous between two mechanisms (solute reabsorption lagging behind water reabsorption, or true net secretion); calculating the double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin resolves this ambiguity, because a double ratio greater than 1.0 specifically indicates net secretion (more solute is present than could be explained by water removal alone), while a double ratio at or below 1.0 with a raw ratio above 1.0 would instead indicate lagging reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Whenever a raw [TF/P] ratio above 1.0 could mean either \"lagging reabsorption\" or \"secretion,\" the double ratio (correcting for [TF/P]inulin) is the tool that tells them apart.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same double-ratio resolution is the standard method for confirming true tubular secretion of organic acids and bases at other nephron segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"A drug selectively and completely inhibits basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase in early proximal tubule cells, with no other nephron segment affected. What is the most direct expected effect on apical SGLT2-mediated glucose reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Glucose reabsorption is unaffected, because SGLT2 hydrolyzes its own ATP independent of the basolateral pump","B":"Glucose reabsorption increases, because intracellular sodium accumulation directly stimulates SGLT2 activity","C":"Glucose reabsorption shifts primarily to the basolateral membrane, with far less occurring apically","D":"Glucose reabsorption is unaffected, because GLUT2 is fully sufficient on its own to maintain normal apical glucose entry","E":"Glucose reabsorption falls markedly, since the sodium gradient powering SGLT2 dissipates without the pump"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SGLT2 is a secondary active transporter with no ATPase activity of its own; it depends entirely on the steep inward sodium gradient (low intracellular Na+, negative intracellular potential) that the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase continuously maintains, so blocking that pump collapses the sodium gradient and sharply reduces SGLT2-mediated glucose entry, even though SGLT2 itself is never directly inhibited.","boardPrep":"Any early proximal cotransporter (SGLT2, Na+-amino acid, Na+-phosphate) fails if the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase fails, because none of them can generate their own driving gradient — this is the defining feature of secondary active transport.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this same basolateral-pump-dependence logic explains why ouabain (a direct Na+/K+-ATPase inhibitor) impairs essentially all proximal tubule solute reabsorption, not just one transporter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a genetic defect that selectively eliminates NHE3 function in the early proximal tubule, with SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters unaffected. What is the most direct expected consequence for bicarbonate handling?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate reabsorption is unaffected, since SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters can fully compensate for NHE3 loss","B":"A substantial fraction of filtered bicarbonate escapes reabsorption, since NHE3 drives most of that pathway","C":"Bicarbonate reabsorption increases, since NHE3 normally opposes bicarbonate reabsorption","D":"Bicarbonate is instead secreted into the lumen in the absence of NHE3 activity","E":"Glucose and amino acid reabsorption are also lost, since early proximal transporters share a single underlying mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3-driven H+ secretion is the mechanism that converts luminal bicarbonate to CO2 and water for reabsorption, accounting for the great majority of early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption; because SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters operate through entirely separate, independent pathways that do not touch bicarbonate, they cannot compensate for NHE3 loss, and a substantial fraction of filtered bicarbonate would be expected to escape reabsorption and reach more distal segments.","boardPrep":"NHE3 loss causes bicarbonate wasting specifically, while leaving glucose and amino acid reabsorption intact, since those pathways are mechanistically independent of NHE3.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this selective bicarbonate-wasting pattern mirrors the bicarbonate loss seen in proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"Two early proximal tubule apical transporters, SGLT2 (a symporter) and NHE3 (an antiporter), both depend on the inward sodium electrochemical gradient. Why does SGLT2 generate a lumen-negative potential while NHE3 does not, even though both use the same sodium gradient?","choices":{"A":"SGLT2 uses a completely different sodium gradient than NHE3, generated by a separate basolateral pump","B":"NHE3 secretes a negative ion into the lumen that exactly cancels its own electrogenic sodium entry","C":"SGLT2 is confined to the earliest segment of the proximal tubule, before any electrical potential develops","D":"SGLT2 moves charge in unbalanced by any anion; NHE3 swaps one cation for another, staying electroneutral","E":"NHE3 is driven by an entirely different electrochemical gradient than the one that powers SGLT2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT2 is electrogenic because it moves one positively charged sodium ion into the cell together with one uncharged glucose molecule, with no anion entering the lumen to balance the charge loss, leaving the lumen relatively negative; NHE3, by contrast, exchanges one Na+ entering the cell for one H+ leaving the cell into the lumen, a one-for-one cation-for-cation swap that carries no net charge across the membrane and is therefore electroneutral, generating no transepithelial voltage on its own.","boardPrep":"Symport of an ion with an uncharged solute (SGLT2, Na+-amino acid) is electrogenic; antiport of a cation for another cation (NHE3) is electroneutral — the transport TYPE, not just the sodium gradient involved, determines whether a potential difference is generated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this electrogenic-versus-electroneutral distinction is exactly why the early proximal tubule is lumen-negative despite NHE3 handling the majority of bicarbonate reabsorption there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A drug selectively blocks early proximal NHE3 activity, preventing the normal preferential bicarbonate reabsorption that occurs there, while leaving all late proximal tubule transporters intact. What is the most direct expected effect on late proximal NaCl reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption is impaired, because the high luminal chloride gradient driving it fails to develop","B":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption increases, because more chloride remains available for the paracellular and cellular exchange mechanisms","C":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption is unaffected, because the late proximal transporters generate their own independent chloride gradient","D":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption shifts from a paracellular to a purely cellular mechanism","E":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption fails specifically through the cellular pathway, with the paracellular pathway remaining fully intact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The entire driving force for late proximal NaCl reabsorption, both paracellular (chloride diffusion and the resulting lumen-positive potential) and cellular (the Cl-/formate exchanger), depends on the high luminal chloride concentration established upstream by preferential early proximal bicarbonate (not chloride) reabsorption; if NHE3-driven bicarbonate reabsorption is blocked, chloride is not concentrated as it normally would be, and both late proximal NaCl reabsorption mechanisms lose their driving gradient together.","boardPrep":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption is entirely downstream-dependent on early proximal bicarbonate handling: impair the preferential bicarbonate reabsorption upstream, and you impair NaCl reabsorption late.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this early-to-late proximal tubule dependency is a favorite integration question linking Objective 2 and Objective 3 together","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"In the late proximal tubule, both a paracellular pathway and a cellular pathway contribute to net NaCl reabsorption. If the tight junctions between late proximal cells were made completely impermeable to chloride while the cellular Cl-/formate exchanger remained fully functional, what would be the most direct consequence?","choices":{"A":"NaCl reabsorption overall would stop completely because the two pathways are fully interdependent on each other","B":"The lumen-positive potential difference would become even larger from continued chloride accumulation","C":"The lumen-positive potential difference would be lost; transcellular NaCl reabsorption would continue","D":"The cellular Cl-/formate exchanger would compensate instead by secreting chloride paracellularly as well","E":"Sodium reabsorption would be completely unaffected because it depends entirely on the cellular pathway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference is generated specifically by passive paracellular chloride diffusion out of the lumen; if the tight junctions became impermeable to chloride, this paracellular diffusion (and the potential difference it generates, along with the passive paracellular sodium reabsorption that potential drives) would be lost, but the cellular pathway, which depends on the apical Cl-/formate exchanger and NHE3 rather than paracellular permeability, could continue to reabsorb some NaCl transcellularly.","boardPrep":"The paracellular and cellular NaCl reabsorption pathways in the late proximal tubule are mechanistically independent of each other — losing one does not automatically abolish the other, even though both are driven by the same underlying chloride gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — recognizing that these two pathways can be dissociated experimentally is what distinguishes a mechanistic understanding from simple memorization of \"NaCl is reabsorbed here\"","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"Why is the transcellular (cellular) pathway for late proximal NaCl reabsorption described as using \"parallel\" exchangers rather than a single combined transporter?","choices":{"A":"NHE3 and the Cl-/formate exchanger are physically fused into one transmembrane protein complex that moves both ions simultaneously","B":"NHE3 and the Cl-/formate exchanger function as two separate antiporters that happen to recombine byproducts","C":"The two exchangers alternate their activity in sequence, each becoming active while the other is quiet","D":"Just one of the two exchangers is active at any given moment, depending on the luminal pH","E":"The two exchangers require completely different basolateral pumps to sustain their respective gradients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3 and the apical Cl-/formate exchanger are distinct, independently operating transport proteins, each simply exchanging one luminal cation or anion for an intracellular counterpart; they are termed \"parallel\" because they function simultaneously and independently, and their combined net effect (one Na+ and one Cl- entering the cell) emerges only because their secreted byproducts, H+ and formate, happen to recombine in the lumen into neutral formic acid that diffuses back in to sustain both cycles, not because the two transporters are structurally linked.","boardPrep":"\"Parallel\" exchangers means two separate transporters running side by side, not one fused transporter — their apparent coordination comes entirely from the shared recycled formic acid intermediate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this parallel-exchanger-plus-recycled-intermediate strategy is a template physiologic mechanism worth recognizing whenever a \"coupled\" transport process turns out to be two independent exchangers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares electron micrographs of proximal tubule epithelium from a kidney under baseline conditions and under conditions of increased proximal reabsorption. Which change would be expected in the high-reabsorption specimen, and what does it reflect?","choices":{"A":"Narrowed lateral intercellular spaces, reflecting faster draining of fluid into the tubular lumen","B":"Collapsed tubular lumens, reflecting reduced filtration of fluid into Bowman space","C":"Thickened glomerular basement membranes, reflecting greater protein retention in the filtrate","D":"Flattened brush borders, reflecting reduced surface area for sodium entry from the lumen","E":"Widened lateral intercellular spaces, reflecting isosmotic fluid awaiting peritubular uptake"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As sodium is pumped out of the cell across the basolateral membrane and water follows, isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces. Electron micrographs show the spaces actually widening when proximal tubule reabsorption increases, because more fluid is in transit toward the peritubular capillary, where Starling forces take it up. Narrowed spaces would suggest less reabsorption, and the other findings do not describe the route that reabsorbed fluid takes.","boardPrep":"Widened lateral intercellular spaces on electron microscopy mark active isosmotic reabsorption in the proximal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — glomerulotubular balance acts on this same peritubular uptake step to keep reabsorption proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"In an isolated perfused proximal tubule, ouabain, an inhibitor of the Na+-K+ ATPase, is added to the bath. Net fluid reabsorption falls toward zero even though the luminal membrane remains freely permeable to water. Which statement best explains this result?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure rises sharply and overcomes the driving force for reabsorption of fluid","B":"The tight junctions seal completely, so fluid becomes trapped within the lateral intercellular spaces","C":"Sodium is secreted back into the tubular fluid, which draws water out of the cells and into the lumen","D":"Without the pump, solute reabsorption fails, and water movement stops because it follows solute","E":"The same ATPase pumps water directly, so its inhibition immediately halts the movement of water"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Solute reabsorption is the primary event in isosmotic reabsorption: sodium enters across the luminal membrane and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane by the Na+-K+ ATPase, and water follows passively to keep the fluid isosmotic. Blocking the pump removes the sodium gradient that supports luminal sodium entry, so solute reabsorption falls and water, which follows solute, stops with it even though the luminal membrane remains permeable. Water is not itself pumped, and an isolated perfused tubule has no peritubular blood in which oncotic pressure could change.","boardPrep":"If the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase is blocked, proximal fluid reabsorption stops because water depends on solute reabsorption, not the reverse.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — the sodium gradient maintained by this same pump also powers the apical cotransporters of the early proximal tubule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A micropuncture study shows that the proximal tubule reabsorbs about two-thirds of the filtered load of water and salt. Quantitatively, which solutes account for most of the solute reabsorption that the water follows?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride in the late segment and sodium bicarbonate in the early segment","B":"Glucose and amino acids taken up through sodium-coupled cotransporters at the luminal membrane","C":"Phosphate, lactate, and citrate taken up through sodium-coupled carriers at the luminal membrane","D":"Potassium chloride and urea moving down their concentration gradients across the epithelium","E":"Calcium and magnesium salts moving through the paracellular pathway between neighboring cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isosmotic reabsorption couples water to solute, and the major cation is sodium with bicarbonate as the main accompanying anion in the early proximal tubule and chloride in the late proximal tubule. Quantitatively, most of the solute reabsorbed by the proximal tubule is NaCl and NaHCO3. Glucose, amino acids, phosphate, lactate, and citrate are also reabsorbed but contribute far fewer osmoles.","boardPrep":"Most of the solute behind proximal isosmotic water reabsorption is NaCl and NaHCO3, with glucose, amino acids, and the minor anions contributing much less.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this bulk reabsorption of sodium and water is the quantity that glomerulotubular balance holds at a constant fraction","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Glomerulotubular Balance & ECF Volume Regulation","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"GFR spontaneously increases by 1% with no change in renal plasma flow (RPF). Why does the proximal tubule end up reabsorbing 67% of this larger filtered sodium load rather than the same absolute amount it reabsorbed before?","choices":{"A":"The rise in filtration fraction concentrates peritubular capillary blood, raising oncotic pressure and driving more reabsorption","B":"The proximal tubule cells directly sense the higher luminal sodium concentration and increase transporter expression within seconds","C":"Angiotensin II is released in direct proportion to GFR and stimulates proximal Na-H exchange independent of Starling forces","D":"A rise in GFR lowers peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly, without any change in filtration fraction","E":"The macula densa signals the proximal tubule directly through paracrine mediators to raise its reabsorptive rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With RPF unchanged, a 1% rise in GFR raises the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF), meaning a greater-than-usual fraction of plasma water leaves glomerular capillary blood; this concentrates the remaining plasma proteins, raising the oncotic pressure of the blood that becomes peritubular capillary blood, and because peritubular oncotic pressure is the dominant Starling force driving proximal reabsorption, the higher oncotic pressure increases fractional reabsorption to match the higher filtered load.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance works primarily through the filtration fraction and peritubular oncotic pressure rather than direct tubular sensing of luminal solute; memorize the chain: GFR up -> filtration fraction up -> peritubular oncotic pressure up -> reabsorption up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this filtration-fraction-to-oncotic-pressure chain is the entire mechanistic basis of glomerulotubular balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a GFR of 180 L/day and a plasma sodium concentration of 140 mEq/L. If glomerulotubular balance were completely absent (i.e., the proximal tubule reabsorbed a fixed amount rather than a fixed fraction) and GFR spontaneously rose by 1%, approximately how much additional sodium would be excreted per day?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 25 mEq/day","B":"Approximately 1960 mEq/day","C":"Approximately 2520 mEq/day","D":"Approximately 140 mEq/day","E":"Approximately 252 mEq/day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The filtered load of sodium equals GFR times plasma sodium concentration: 180 L/day x 140 mEq/L = 25,200 mEq/day; a 1% increase in this filtered load is 252 mEq/day, and without glomerulotubular balance correcting reabsorption to match, this entire extra amount would appear in the urine, which is a large loss relative to the body's total ECF sodium content of only about 1960 mEq.","boardPrep":"Filtered load = GFR x plasma concentration — this calculation is exactly why a seemingly tiny 1% GFR fluctuation would be dangerous without glomerulotubular balance, since it represents a meaningful fraction of total body sodium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this exact arithmetic illustrates why glomerulotubular balance is described as a protective mechanism rather than a minor physiologic curiosity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"Glomerulotubular balance maintains a constant fractional reabsorption of 67% in the proximal tubule. What is the key distinction between \"constant fraction\" and \"constant amount\" that makes this mechanism protective?","choices":{"A":"A constant amount would also scale the reabsorbed amount with whatever load is filtered, making the two descriptions functionally identical","B":"A constant fraction automatically scales the reabsorbed amount with whatever load is filtered","C":"A constant fraction means the reabsorbed amount stays exactly the same regardless of filtered load","D":"A constant fraction and a constant amount alternate roles depending on whether GFR is rising or falling","E":"Neither a constant fraction nor a constant amount can protect the reabsorbed amount against changes in filtered sodium load"},"correct":"B","explanation":"If the proximal tubule reabsorbed a fixed absolute amount of sodium regardless of filtered load, then any rise in GFR would send the entire extra filtered sodium straight to the urine, and any fall in GFR could even make reabsorption exceed the filtered load; because glomerulotubular balance instead keeps reabsorption at a constant fraction (67%) of whatever load is filtered, reabsorption automatically rises when the filtered load rises and falls when it falls, keeping the absolute amount delivered to more distal segments (and ultimately excreted) relatively stable despite spontaneous GFR fluctuations.","boardPrep":"The core protective logic of glomerulotubular balance: fixed fraction scales with load, fixed amount does not — this distinction is the entire conceptual point of the mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this same fixed-fraction logic is why proximal tubule reabsorption is described as \"load-dependent\" throughout renal physiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient receives a large infusion of isotonic saline. Which pair of peritubular capillary Starling force changes results, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Oncotic pressure rises because the saline preferentially dilutes red blood cells, leaving proximal tubule reabsorption unaffected","B":"The rise in intravascular volume raises glomerular capillary pressure, leaving reabsorption in the proximal tubule unaffected","C":"Both Starling forces shift to favor increased reabsorption in the proximal tubule, since expanded plasma volume raises oncotic pressure","D":"Oncotic pressure falls and hydrostatic pressure rises, together decreasing fractional reabsorption in the proximal tubule","E":"Neither Starling force changes, so reabsorption in the proximal tubule stays fixed since isotonic saline does not alter plasma protein concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isotonic saline dilutes plasma proteins without adding protein, lowering peritubular capillary oncotic pressure, and expands intravascular volume, raising peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure; because oncotic pressure normally favors reabsorption and hydrostatic pressure normally opposes it, a fall in the former together with a rise in the latter both push in the same direction, reducing the net force favoring reabsorption and decreasing fractional proximal reabsorption of isosmotic fluid.","boardPrep":"ECF volume expansion: peritubular oncotic pressure down, peritubular hydrostatic pressure up, both changes point the same direction — toward LESS reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this pair of Starling force changes is the direct mechanistic explanation for why isotonic saline infusion promotes natriuresis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"During ECF volume expansion, some of the isosmotic fluid that would normally be reabsorbed instead \"leaks back\" into the tubular lumen. Through which anatomic route does this back-leak occur?","choices":{"A":"Directly across the luminal apical brush border membrane of the proximal tubule cells","B":"Through the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase operating in a reversed transport direction","C":"Across the tight junctions between proximal tubule cells","D":"Through the glomerular capillary wall back into Bowman's space","E":"Through specialized aquaporin channels located in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces between proximal tubule cells before being taken up by peritubular capillaries; when peritubular Starling forces favor reabsorption less strongly (as in ECF volume expansion), a portion of this fluid instead moves backward across the tight junctions, which are not truly \"tight\" but are permeable to small solutes and water, returning fluid to the tubular lumen rather than to peritubular capillary blood.","boardPrep":"The tight junctions between proximal tubule cells are the anatomic route for back-leak — this is the same paracellular pathway that, run in the opposite direction, normally contributes to forward reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this paracellular back-leak is the direct anatomic explanation for the decreased fractional reabsorption produced by unfavorable peritubular Starling forces","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does an increase in filtration fraction have the opposite Starling-force effect on peritubular capillaries compared with a pure ECF volume expansion from isotonic saline infusion?","choices":{"A":"A rising filtration fraction and isotonic saline both concentrate peritubular blood proteins to the same degree","B":"A rising filtration fraction concentrates peritubular blood proteins, while isotonic saline dilutes them","C":"A rising filtration fraction affects glomerular capillaries, with no downstream effect on peritubular capillaries","D":"Isotonic saline directly increases the filtration fraction, making the two scenarios mechanistically identical","E":"Peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure is the single Starling force that changes in either scenario"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A rising filtration fraction means a greater share of glomerular capillary plasma water is filtered out, leaving the remaining plasma proteins more concentrated in the blood that becomes peritubular capillary blood, raising peritubular oncotic pressure and favoring reabsorption; isotonic saline infusion, by contrast, adds protein-free fluid directly to the plasma, diluting plasma protein concentration throughout the circulation including peritubular capillary blood, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure and opposing reabsorption — the two scenarios drive peritubular oncotic pressure in opposite directions.","boardPrep":"Filtration fraction changes and ECF volume changes both work through peritubular Starling forces, but they are separate levers that can push oncotic pressure in opposite directions — do not conflate them.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — recognizing filtration fraction and ECF volume status as two distinct, independently variable inputs to peritubular Starling forces is a common integration point with Objective 1","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with severe diarrhea has significant ECF volume contraction. Which pair of peritubular capillary Starling force changes results, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Oncotic pressure falls because diarrhea dilutes plasma proteins, an effect opposite to volume contraction, sparing reabsorption in the proximal tubule","B":"The fall in intravascular volume lowers glomerular capillary pressure, leaving reabsorption in the proximal tubule unaffected","C":"Both Starling forces shift to oppose reabsorption in the proximal tubule, since contracted plasma volume lowers oncotic pressure","D":"Oncotic pressure rises and hydrostatic pressure falls, together increasing fractional reabsorption in the proximal tubule","E":"Neither Starling force changes, so reabsorption in the proximal tubule stays fixed since diarrhea affects the intestines and not plasma composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ECF volume contraction concentrates the remaining plasma proteins (raising peritubular capillary oncotic pressure) and lowers intravascular volume and pressure (lowering peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure); because oncotic pressure favors reabsorption and hydrostatic pressure opposes it, a rise in the former together with a fall in the latter both push toward increased reabsorption, raising fractional proximal reabsorption of isosmotic fluid as a protective response to volume loss.","boardPrep":"ECF volume contraction: peritubular oncotic pressure up, peritubular hydrostatic pressure down, both changes point the same direction — toward MORE reabsorption. This is the mirror image of volume expansion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this pair of Starling force changes is the direct mechanistic explanation for why the kidney conserves sodium and water during volume depletion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the increased proximal tubule reabsorption seen during ECF volume contraction described as a \"protective\" response?","choices":{"A":"It prevents any further glomerular filtration from occurring until ECF volume is fully restored","B":"It shifts reabsorption preferentially toward potassium to protect against the specific losses caused by diarrhea","C":"It reabsorbs more of the filtered sodium and water that would otherwise have been excreted","D":"It reduces the oncotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood to compensate for the volume loss","E":"It shifts fluid from the intracellular compartment into the ECF compartment directly at the tubular level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When ECF volume is already reduced by an external loss such as diarrhea or vomiting, excreting additional sodium and water in the urine would worsen the volume deficit; by increasing the fraction of filtered sodium and water reabsorbed rather than excreted, the proximal tubule retains more of what remains in the body, working to restore ECF volume toward normal rather than to further deplete it.","boardPrep":"The \"protective\" label for increased proximal reabsorption during volume contraction simply means the kidney is conserving, not wasting, sodium and water at exactly the moment the body can least afford to lose more.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this protective conservation response is the proximal tubule component of the broader systemic response to volume depletion that also involves the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher artificially raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure in an experimental animal without changing peritubular oncotic pressure at all. What isolated effect on proximal tubule fractional reabsorption would this produce?","choices":{"A":"A decrease in fractional reabsorption, since elevated hydrostatic pressure by itself opposes reabsorption","B":"An increase in fractional reabsorption, since any rise in peritubular capillary pressure favors fluid uptake into the capillary","C":"No change in fractional reabsorption, since hydrostatic pressure has no independent effect apart from oncotic pressure","D":"A decrease in fractional reabsorption specifically if oncotic pressure is simultaneously lowered as well","E":"An increase in fractional reabsorption, restricted specifically to the late proximal tubule segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure and oncotic pressure act as independent, opposing Starling forces on isosmotic fluid reabsorption; hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out of the capillary (opposing reabsorption) while oncotic pressure pulls fluid into the capillary (favoring reabsorption), so isolating a rise in hydrostatic pressure alone, with oncotic pressure unchanged, would shift the net Starling balance toward less reabsorption, independent of whatever oncotic pressure happens to be doing.","boardPrep":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure and oncotic pressure are two separate, independently variable Starling forces — do not assume they always move together, since experimentally isolating one reveals its own independent effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — recognizing hydrostatic and oncotic pressure as independently manipulable variables is what allows this same Starling-force framework to explain both volume expansion and volume contraction with a single unified model","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to the Starling-force mechanism, what second, hormonally driven mechanism increases proximal tubule reabsorption during ECF volume contraction?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-glucose cotransport activity","B":"Antidiuretic hormone directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-H exchange independent of angiotensin II","C":"Atrial natriuretic peptide directly stimulates proximal tubule sodium reabsorption during volume loss","D":"Parathyroid hormone directly stimulates proximal tubule bicarbonate reabsorption during volume loss","E":"Angiotensin II directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-H exchange activity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ECF volume contraction lowers blood volume and arterial pressure, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system; angiotensin II acts directly on proximal tubule cells to stimulate the Na-H exchanger (NHE3), which in turn stimulates coupled reabsorption of Na+, HCO3-, and water, providing a second reabsorption-boosting mechanism that operates alongside (not instead of) the peritubular Starling force changes.","boardPrep":"Two separate mechanisms increase proximal reabsorption during volume contraction: peritubular Starling forces (oncotic pressure up, hydrostatic pressure down) and angiotensin II-stimulated NHE3 — the second one is what specifically drives bicarbonate along with sodium and water.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this angiotensin II-stimulated NHE3 mechanism is the direct causal link between volume contraction and the development of contraction alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does angiotensin II-stimulated proximal Na-H exchange during ECF volume contraction specifically produce a metabolic alkalosis rather than simply conserving sodium in an acid-base-neutral way?","choices":{"A":"Because angiotensin II directly secretes hydrogen ions into the urine through a mechanism entirely separate from NHE3","B":"Because NHE3 activity is coupled to bicarbonate reabsorption, preferentially retaining bicarbonate","C":"Because volume contraction directly increases dietary bicarbonate absorption in the gastrointestinal tract","D":"Because angiotensin II suppresses renal ammonia production, indirectly raising plasma bicarbonate","E":"Because NHE3 stimulation causes potassium wasting, which independently raises plasma pH through a separate pathway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3 is the same exchanger responsible for early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption (secreted H+ combines with luminal HCO3- to form CO2 and water that diffuse into the cell and are reconstituted into HCO3- that exits into blood); when angiotensin II stimulates NHE3 activity during volume contraction, it does not just conserve sodium and water in isolation, it also preferentially increases bicarbonate reabsorption above baseline, raising plasma bicarbonate and producing the metabolic alkalosis known as contraction alkalosis.","boardPrep":"\"Contraction alkalosis\" is not a coincidental label — it exists specifically because the same transporter (NHE3) that angiotensin II uses to conserve volume also happens to reabsorb bicarbonate, so volume conservation and alkalosis occur together as a package.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this NHE3-mediated coupling of sodium and bicarbonate reabsorption is exactly the same early proximal mechanism introduced with acid-base disturbances in the mixed acid-base disorder objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient receiving mannitol develops increased urinary excretion of both mannitol and sodium. What proximal tubule mechanism directly explains why unreabsorbed mannitol also causes increased sodium excretion?","choices":{"A":"Retained mannitol holds water in the lumen, diluting sodium and limiting later reabsorption","B":"Mannitol directly competes with sodium for the same luminal binding site on the Na-glucose cotransporter","C":"Mannitol is metabolized into an organic acid that directly inhibits the basolateral Na-K ATPase pump","D":"Mannitol raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly, independent of any luminal effect","E":"Mannitol binds directly to and blocks the apical Na-H exchanger throughout the proximal tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because a poorly reabsorbed solute like mannitol remains in the tubular lumen, it holds water in the lumen osmotically and limits water reabsorption; as sodium is reabsorbed, the retained water dilutes the luminal sodium concentration progressively along the tubule, weakening the gradient for further sodium reabsorption and promoting backleak, so less sodium (and the water that follows it) is reabsorbed later on and more of both sodium and mannitol appear in the final urine.","boardPrep":"Osmotic diuretics work through the isosmotic-reabsorption requirement, not through direct transporter blockade: an unreabsorbed solute holds water in the lumen, dilutes luminal sodium, and indirectly drags sodium reabsorption down.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this same isosmotic-requirement logic explains why untreated diabetes mellitus, with its high filtered glucose load exceeding proximal reabsorptive capacity, produces an osmotic diuresis and ECF volume contraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"In an experimental preparation, GFR is held artificially constant while renal plasma flow (RPF) is independently increased. What happens to the filtration fraction, and what downstream effect on proximal tubule reabsorption is expected?","choices":{"A":"The filtration fraction rises, raising the peritubular oncotic pressure and increasing fractional reabsorption","B":"The filtration fraction falls, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure and decreasing fractional reabsorption","C":"The filtration fraction is unaffected by changes in RPF as long as GFR itself remains constant","D":"The filtration fraction falls, but peritubular oncotic pressure rises as a compensatory response","E":"The filtration fraction rises, but fractional reabsorption is unaffected since GFR itself did not change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Filtration fraction equals GFR divided by RPF; if GFR is held constant while RPF rises, the filtration fraction must fall, meaning a smaller-than-usual fraction of the now-larger plasma flow is filtered out of glomerular capillary blood, so the remaining plasma proteins are diluted rather than concentrated, lowering peritubular capillary oncotic pressure and, by the same Starling-force logic used for GFR changes, decreasing fractional proximal reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Filtration fraction = GFR / RPF — changing either variable independently changes the filtration fraction and therefore peritubular oncotic pressure, even if the other variable stays fixed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this RPF-driven filtration fraction change demonstrates that glomerulotubular balance responds to the ratio GFR/RPF, not to GFR in isolation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A student claims that glomerulotubular balance means the proximal tubule \"senses\" GFR directly and adjusts its transporters accordingly. Based on the actual mechanism, why is this description inaccurate?","choices":{"A":"The tubule responds to peritubular oncotic pressure, which follows filtration fraction","B":"A dedicated GFR receptor on the proximal tubule cell reports GFR itself","C":"Glomerulotubular balance is a property of the glomerulus itself","D":"The proximal tubule senses angiotensin II levels, which are independent of GFR or filtration fraction","E":"The description is accurate, since peritubular oncotic pressure is mathematically identical to GFR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"There is no direct sensory link between GFR itself and the proximal tubule; instead, a change in GFR (relative to RPF) alters the filtration fraction, which alters the protein concentration and therefore the oncotic pressure of the blood that becomes peritubular capillary blood, and it is this indirect, physically mediated change in peritubular oncotic pressure (a Starling force acting on the tubule from the outside, not a tubular \"sensor\" of GFR) that ultimately adjusts fractional reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance is an indirect, Starling-force-mediated communication pathway, not a direct sensory mechanism: the proximal tubule responds mainly to peritubular Starling forces rather than sensing GFR itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this distinction between direct sensing and indirect Starling-force mediation is a common source of conceptual confusion worth explicitly correcting","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"If glomerulotubular balance functioned perfectly at all times with no delay, what would be the expected relationship between the filtered load of sodium and the amount of sodium delivered out of the proximal tubule to the loop of Henle, across a range of spontaneous GFR fluctuations?","choices":{"A":"A fixed absolute quantity would reach the loop of Henle no matter how the filtered load changes","B":"Downstream delivery would track changes in renal plasma flow, not glomerular filtration rate","C":"Delivery downstream would vanish whenever GFR rises and surge whenever GFR falls","D":"A constant 33% of the filtered load would reach the loop of Henle regardless of GFR","E":"The 67% reabsorbed figure would equally describe the fraction delivered onward at any GFR"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because glomerulotubular balance keeps proximal reabsorption at a constant 67% of the filtered load, the fraction NOT reabsorbed (and therefore delivered onward to the loop of Henle) is the remaining 33%, regardless of whether GFR happens to be higher or lower than usual at a given moment; this constant-fraction delivery is precisely what limits the swings in downstream nephron sodium and water delivery that would otherwise occur with every spontaneous GFR fluctuation.","boardPrep":"If proximal reabsorption is a constant 67% of filtered load, delivery to the loop of Henle is the complementary constant 33% — both numbers move together as fixed fractions, never as fixed absolute amounts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this constant-fraction delivery to the loop of Henle is exactly why downstream nephron segments can be tuned to handle a relatively predictable, stable sodium and water load despite upstream GFR variability","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient receives an infusion of concentrated albumin solution (raising, rather than diluting, plasma protein concentration) together with enough isotonic fluid to expand ECF volume equally. Compared with isotonic saline alone, how would the peritubular oncotic pressure response to this combined infusion differ, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would still fall, since ECF volume expansion lowers oncotic pressure regardless of what is infused","B":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would rise, blunting or reversing the expected decrease in fractional reabsorption","C":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would be unaffected by either infusion, since hydrostatic pressure is what responds to volume changes","D":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure would fall, canceling the entire volume expansion effect","E":"Fractional reabsorption would decrease even more sharply than with isotonic saline by itself, since two mechanisms now act together"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isotonic saline expands ECF volume while diluting plasma proteins, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure; concentrated albumin, by contrast, adds protein along with volume, raising rather than lowering plasma (and therefore peritubular) oncotic pressure even as ECF volume expands, so the oncotic pressure component of the Starling balance would move in the opposite direction from a pure saline infusion, blunting or potentially reversing the decrease in fractional reabsorption that saline alone would produce, even though both infusions expand ECF volume equally.","boardPrep":"ECF volume expansion and peritubular oncotic pressure changes are not automatically linked — what is infused (protein-free fluid versus protein-containing fluid) determines the oncotic pressure direction independent of the volume effect itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this dissociation between volume status and oncotic pressure is the same principle tested when comparing filtration fraction changes with pure volume changes in Objective 1","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"During ECF volume expansion, decreased fractional proximal reabsorption is described as \"one of several mechanisms\" that aids in excreting excess NaCl and water. What does this phrasing imply about the completeness of the proximal tubule's role in this response?","choices":{"A":"The proximal tubule effect is the sole and complete mechanism responsible for excreting the excess NaCl and water","B":"The phrasing indicates that the proximal tubule effect is a minor and physiologically insignificant contributor to the excretion","C":"The proximal tubule effect is one partial contributor among many to the excess NaCl and water excretion","D":"The phrasing implies that decreased fractional reabsorption occurs specifically in the late proximal tubule and not the early segment","E":"The phrasing means the proximal tubule mechanism reverses over time, eventually increasing reabsorption again"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Describing decreased proximal fractional reabsorption as \"one of several mechanisms\" explicitly signals that it is a real, meaningful contributor to the natriuretic response to volume expansion, but not the only one; other systemic mechanisms (such as suppression of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, natriuretic peptide release, and altered Starling forces or transport in more distal nephron segments) act in parallel, and the proximal tubule's Starling-force-driven decrease in reabsorption is best understood as one coordinated piece of a larger, multi-mechanism response rather than the entire explanation by itself.","boardPrep":"Renal responses to volume status changes are rarely explained by a single mechanism — recognizing the proximal tubule's Starling-force response as one contributor among several is important for not over-attributing the entire natriuretic response to this one pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this same multi-mechanism framing recurs whenever systemic sodium balance and natriuretic peptides are covered in later, more integrative SDLs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a chronic condition causing low plasma albumin (hypoalbuminemia) but normal ECF volume is evaluated. Based purely on the oncotic pressure component of peritubular Starling forces, what proximal tubule reabsorption effect would be expected from the low albumin alone, independent of any volume status change?","choices":{"A":"Decreased fractional reabsorption, since low plasma albumin lowers peritubular capillary oncotic pressure directly","B":"Increased fractional reabsorption, since low plasma albumin raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly","C":"No effect on fractional reabsorption, since plasma albumin concentration does not influence peritubular oncotic pressure","D":"Decreased fractional reabsorption, arising instead because low albumin directly raises peritubular hydrostatic pressure","E":"Increased fractional reabsorption, since the kidney compensates for low albumin by concentrating peritubular capillary blood"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peritubular capillary oncotic pressure depends directly on the protein concentration of the blood that reaches it, which in turn depends heavily on plasma albumin, the major plasma protein; a patient with chronically low plasma albumin will have chronically low peritubular capillary oncotic pressure regardless of ECF volume status, and because oncotic pressure is the major Starling force favoring reabsorption, this lower oncotic pressure by itself would be expected to decrease fractional proximal reabsorption, independent of whatever volume-status mechanisms might also be operating.","boardPrep":"Plasma protein concentration (driven mainly by albumin) is a direct determinant of peritubular oncotic pressure on its own, separate from volume-status-driven changes in filtration fraction or ECF volume — a chronic disease state can shift this baseline independent of acute volume changes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this independent albumin-driven oncotic pressure effect is the physiologic basis for the edema-forming states caused by hypoalbuminemia (such as nephrotic syndrome), covered further in later, more clinically integrated SDLs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with severe vomiting has both ECF volume contraction and ongoing loss of gastric hydrochloric acid. How do the Starling-force-driven and RAAS-driven mechanisms of increased proximal reabsorption interact in this specific clinical scenario to worsen, rather than merely accompany, the developing alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"The Starling-force mechanism directly removes hydrogen ions from the blood independent of any bicarbonate-related transporter","B":"RAAS activation during vomiting-induced volume contraction acts to lower plasma bicarbonate as a compensatory response to the ongoing acid loss","C":"Volume contraction from vomiting itself activates angiotensin II, which then stimulates NHE3 to reabsorb extra bicarbonate","D":"The two mechanisms act in opposing directions, so the net effect on plasma bicarbonate stays negligible despite both being active","E":"Angiotensin II during vomiting-induced volume contraction acts on the distal nephron and has no effect on proximal bicarbonate handling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vomiting causes both direct loss of gastric HCl (a primary metabolic alkalosis from acid loss) and ECF volume contraction from fluid loss; the resulting volume contraction independently activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and angiotensin II's stimulation of proximal NHE3 reabsorbs extra bicarbonate along with sodium and water, adding a second, RAAS-driven bicarbonate-retaining mechanism on top of the original acid-loss-driven alkalosis, so the two processes compound rather than cancel, worsening the net alkalosis beyond what gastric acid loss alone would produce.","boardPrep":"In vomiting, contraction alkalosis is not just \"gastric acid was lost\" — the resulting volume contraction independently triggers angiotensin II-driven bicarbonate retention, compounding the alkalosis through a second mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this Starling-force-plus-RAAS interaction is the direct bridge from this objective's volume-contraction mechanism into the contraction alkalosis pathophysiology covered next","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with ECF volume contraction from vomiting has an unchanged GFR and an unchanged renal plasma flow, so the filtration fraction is also unchanged. Does the glomerulotubular balance mechanism described for spontaneous GFR fluctuations explain the increase in fractional proximal reabsorption seen here?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because glomerulotubular balance operates identically whether GFR, RPF, or ECF volume is the variable that changes","B":"Yes, because any ECF volume change necessarily alters the filtration fraction even when GFR and RPF both stay fixed","C":"No, but with RAAS-driven NHE3 stimulation having fully replaced the Starling-force mechanism during this volume loss","D":"No, because the entire response comes from a rise in GFR itself despite RPF staying fixed","E":"No, this rise comes from ECF-volume-induced Starling-force changes, not from any filtration fraction change"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glomerulotubular balance in response to spontaneous GFR fluctuations works specifically through changes in the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF), which alters peritubular oncotic pressure; here, however, GFR and RPF are both explicitly unchanged, so the filtration fraction cannot be the driver, yet fractional reabsorption still increases because ECF volume contraction independently concentrates plasma proteins and lowers peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure through a route that has nothing to do with filtration fraction; this shows that GFR-driven and ECF-volume-driven inputs converge on the same peritubular Starling forces through two mechanistically separate pathways.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance (via filtration fraction) and the ECF-volume-contraction response (via direct plasma protein concentration and hydrostatic pressure changes) are two distinct routes to the same peritubular Starling forces — one does not require the other to operate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this dissociation between the filtration-fraction route and the direct-volume route is the same distinction tested for ECF volume expansion versus filtration fraction changes in Objective 2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"If a drug were developed that selectively blocked the rise in peritubular capillary oncotic pressure during ECF volume contraction, without affecting the fall in peritubular hydrostatic pressure, what would be the most direct expected consequence for the kidney's protective response to volume loss?","choices":{"A":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption would be blunted, since one of its two driving Starling forces would be removed","B":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption would be completely abolished, since oncotic pressure is the single Starling force involved","C":"The protective response would be unaffected, since hydrostatic pressure changes by themselves are sufficient to drive the entire response","D":"The protective response would instead reverse into a decrease in fractional reabsorption, worsening the volume contraction","E":"The drug would have no effect on proximal reabsorption but would instead alter angiotensin II release directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption during ECF volume contraction results from the combined effect of two Starling forces changing in the same direction: rising peritubular oncotic pressure and falling peritubular hydrostatic pressure, both favoring reabsorption; if a drug selectively blocked only the oncotic pressure rise while leaving the hydrostatic pressure fall intact, the hydrostatic pressure component would still favor reabsorption on its own, so the overall protective response would be reduced in magnitude (blunted) rather than completely abolished or reversed, since one of the two contributing forces would still be operating normally.","boardPrep":"Because two independent Starling forces (oncotic pressure up, hydrostatic pressure down) both contribute to the volume-contraction reabsorption response, blocking only one force blunts rather than eliminates the overall effect — a useful way to test whether a concept has been understood as additive rather than all-or-none.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this additive, two-force model is the same framework used to analyze isolated hydrostatic-pressure manipulations earlier in this objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with untreated diabetes mellitus develops both an osmotic diuresis and progressive ECF volume contraction. What causal chain connects the elevated filtered glucose load to the eventual volume contraction?","choices":{"A":"Elevated filtered glucose directly damages proximal tubule cells, causing them to secrete additional sodium and water into the urine","B":"Elevated filtered glucose directly suppresses angiotensin II release, independently causing excess sodium and water excretion","C":"Elevated filtered glucose is converted into an osmotically inactive form that has no effect on luminal water handling","D":"Elevated filtered glucose competes with sodium for reabsorption via SGLT2 until the transporter is fully saturated, after which no further effect occurs","E":"Filtered glucose exceeds proximal reabsorptive capacity, remains in the lumen, and limits sodium and water reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In untreated diabetes mellitus, the filtered glucose load exceeds the proximal tubule's maximal reabsorptive capacity for glucose, so unreabsorbed glucose remains in the tubular lumen, acting exactly like mannitol as a poorly reabsorbed osmotic agent; it holds water in the lumen and limits water reabsorption, the retained water dilutes the luminal sodium concentration and weakens the gradient for further sodium reabsorption, so both glucose and excess sodium and water are excreted, and the ongoing water and sodium losses progressively contract ECF volume.","boardPrep":"Diabetic glucosuria causing volume contraction follows exactly the same isosmotic-diuresis logic as mannitol administration — recognizing untreated diabetes as a clinical example of endogenous osmotic diuresis links Objective 4's two component mechanisms together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this same osmotic-diuresis-to-volume-contraction chain is the direct clinical link between the osmotic diuretic mechanism and the Starling-force/RAAS mechanisms covered earlier in this objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops ECF volume contraction from severe diuretic overuse (a loop diuretic acting on the thick ascending limb, not an osmotic diuretic). Does this scenario still activate the angiotensin II-driven contraction alkalosis mechanism described for volume contraction generally, even though the diuretic itself acts on a completely different nephron segment?","choices":{"A":"No, because osmotic diuretics acting specifically in the proximal tubule are what can trigger this volume-conservation mechanism","B":"Yes, because the resulting volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system regardless of cause","C":"No, because loop diuretics directly block angiotensin II synthesis regardless of the volume lost","D":"Yes, but requiring the loop diuretic to cause enough volume loss alongside an osmotic diuretic given at the same time","E":"No, because contraction alkalosis requires a primary bicarbonate loss driven by the diuretic's own site of action, separate from any volume change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The angiotensin II-driven proximal NHE3 stimulation mechanism is triggered by the systemic consequence of ECF volume contraction (decreased blood volume and arterial pressure activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system), not by the specific nephron segment or mechanism responsible for causing that volume contraction; a loop diuretic causes volume contraction by blocking NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, but the resulting drop in effective circulating volume activates RAAS just as readily as volume contraction from diarrhea, vomiting, or an osmotic diuretic would, so the same proximal angiotensin II/NHE3-driven contraction alkalosis mechanism is expected to contribute in this scenario as well (which is, in fact, why loop diuretic overuse is a recognized clinical cause of contraction alkalosis).","boardPrep":"Contraction alkalosis is triggered by the systemic fact of volume contraction and the RAAS activation it produces, not by the specific segment or drug causing the volume loss — this is why diverse causes (diarrhea, vomiting, loop diuretics, thiazide diuretics) all converge on the same contraction alkalosis mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — recognizing loop and thiazide diuretics as clinical causes of contraction alkalosis, despite acting on nephron segments far downstream of the proximal tubule, is a classic integration point tested across pharmacology and physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares two poorly reabsorbed luminal solutes: mannitol (which is completely inert and undergoes no cellular metabolism) and a hypothetical osmotic agent that is otherwise identical but is slowly metabolized by proximal tubule cells during transit. Would the slowly metabolized agent be expected to produce a weaker osmotic diuretic effect than mannitol, assuming both start at the same filtered concentration?","choices":{"A":"No, because osmotic diuretic potency depends on the filtered concentration and is unaffected by any metabolism occurring afterward","B":"No, because metabolism of a luminal solute increases its osmotic effect by generating additional osmotically active byproducts","C":"Yes, but specifically because metabolism directly blocks the isosmotic reabsorption requirement itself","D":"Yes, because partial metabolism would lower its effective luminal concentration versus unmetabolized mannitol","E":"Yes, but specifically because metabolized solutes are actively secreted back into the lumen, which mannitol is not"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The osmotic diuretic effect depends specifically on the concentration of poorly reabsorbed solute remaining in the tubular lumen as it raises luminal osmolarity and disrupts the isosmotic reabsorption of sodium; mannitol's complete metabolic inertness means its full filtered amount stays in the lumen throughout its transit (since it is neither reabsorbed nor broken down), maximizing and sustaining its luminal osmotic contribution, whereas a solute that is partially metabolized during transit would have progressively less of itself remaining in luminal form, lowering its effective osmotic contribution compared with mannitol at the same starting filtered concentration, and therefore producing a weaker osmotic diuretic effect overall.","boardPrep":"Mannitol's effectiveness as the prototypical osmotic diuretic depends on being both unreabsorbed and unmetabolized, so that its full filtered amount stays in the lumen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this same \"must stay in the lumen to work\" principle explains why glucose is a much less potent osmotic diuretic per filtered mole than mannitol whenever any glucose reabsorptive capacity remains intact","isHighYield":true}]}]},{"examNumber":2,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Loop of Henle & Countercurrent Multiplication","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of the thin descending limb makes it a concentrating segment of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"Active Na+ reabsorption raises the luminal solute concentration directly","B":"A dedicated urea transporter secretes urea into the lumen along the length of the segment","C":"High water permeability lets water leave the lumen as fluid moves toward the papilla","D":"Impermeability to NaCl traps solute within the lumen as fluid descends","E":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase drives active solute uptake from the interstitium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thin descending limb is highly permeable to water but has little active transport capacity. As tubular fluid descends into the increasingly hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, water passively exits, concentrating the remaining luminal fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin descending limb = water OUT, passive; thin ascending limb = NaCl OUT, passive, water-impermeable. Board vignettes test this permeability split directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"How does the thin ascending limb differ from the thin descending limb in its transport properties?","choices":{"A":"It is impermeable to both water and NaCl, functioning as a purely structural conduit","B":"It is impermeable to water but highly permeable to NaCl, which diffuses out passively","C":"It actively secretes NaCl into the lumen using a basolateral cotransporter","D":"It is permeable to water but impermeable to NaCl, mirroring the descending limb in reverse","E":"It reabsorbs water and NaCl together in a fixed isosmotic ratio"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thin ascending limb is a passive segment: it lacks water permeability but allows NaCl to diffuse out of the lumen down its concentration gradient, established as fluid concentrated on the way down the descending limb now dilutes on the way back up.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this passive NaCl exit (no ATP required) contrasts sharply with the thick ascending limb's active, energy-dependent NKCC2 transport tested in Objective 2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"A short loop of Henle nephron has an unusually short thin descending limb. What direct consequence follows for the tubular fluid reaching the bend of the loop?","choices":{"A":"The fluid is less concentrated than with more segment length available for water loss","B":"The fluid becomes more concentrated because less time allows less solute back-diffusion","C":"Urea secretion into the lumen increases to compensate for the shorter segment","D":"NaCl reabsorption in the segment increases to offset the reduced water loss","E":"The fluid osmolarity becomes identical to plasma regardless of segment length"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because concentration in the descending limb depends on progressive water loss into the increasingly hypertonic interstitium, a shorter segment gives water less distance and less residence time to exit, so the fluid at the bend is less concentrated than with a normal-length limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephron heterogeneity (short-looped vs. long-looped) is a classic distractor topic — length of the thin descending limb tracks directly with maximum achievable concentration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"What ions are transported into thick ascending limb cells by the luminal NKCC2 cotransporter?","choices":{"A":"One Na+ and one Cl- ion in a simple electroneutral pair","B":"Two Na+ ions and one Cl- ion in a fixed ratio","C":"One Na+, one K+, and two Cl- ions together","D":"One K+ ion and two Cl- ions, with Na+ entering separately","E":"One Na+, one K+, and one Cl- ion in a 1:1:1 ratio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NKCC2 (the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter) moves one sodium, one potassium, and two chloride ions together across the luminal membrane, powered by the low intracellular sodium concentration that basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase maintains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NKCC2 stoichiometry (1 Na+ : 1 K+ : 2 Cl-) is directly testable and is the molecular target of loop diuretics in Objective 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"What generates the +7 mV lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference across the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase directly pumps positive charge into the lumen","B":"Chloride exits the cell basolaterally faster than sodium is extruded","C":"A dedicated apical proton pump acidifies and electropositively charges the lumen","D":"K+ that entered via NKCC2 recycles back into the lumen through apical ROMK channels","E":"Paracellular calcium movement establishes the potential before any transport begins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A portion of the potassium that enters the cell via NKCC2 diffuses back out through apical ROMK channels into the lumen. Because this recycled K+ carries positive charge back into the tubular fluid without a matching anion, it establishes the lumen-positive potential.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this +7 mV potential is the driving force for paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption — losing it (loop diuretics) wastes both cations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"By what route are Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorbed across the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Paracellularly, driven by the lumen-positive potential repelling the positively charged cations","B":"Transcellularly through a dedicated apical calcium channel, mirroring divalent transport in the distal tubule","C":"By active cotransport with sodium across the apical NKCC2 transporter, using the same carrier that moves monovalent ions","D":"Through basolateral calcium-sensing receptors, pulling the ions directly from the lumen into the cell","E":"By facilitated diffusion coupled to urea movement, following the same channel used for urea recycling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lumen-positive potential generated by ROMK-mediated K+ recycling repels the positively charged divalent cations, pushing Ca2+ and Mg2+ through the paracellular tight-junction pathway into the interstitium and blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: paracellular, potential-driven divalent cation reabsorption explains why any drug or condition that abolishes the +7 mV potential wastes both calcium and magnesium together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"At what site on the NKCC2 cotransporter do loop diuretics such as furosemide bind?","choices":{"A":"The sodium-binding site of the cotransporter","B":"The chloride-binding site of the cotransporter","C":"An allosteric site on the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase pump","D":"The apical ROMK channel pore directly","E":"A regulatory site restricted to the potassium-binding domain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics bind the Cl- site of NKCC2, blocking the cotransporter and preventing Na+, K+, and Cl- entry together, which shuts down thick ascending limb reabsorption at its source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: furosemide, bumetanide, and ethacrynic acid all act at this same chloride site — a frequently tested pharmacology fact paired with their divalent-cation-wasting side effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on chronic furosemide therapy is found to have both hypercalciuria and hypermagnesuria. What single mechanism links both findings?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide directly blocks a shared calcium-magnesium cotransporter on the apical membrane","B":"Increased distal sodium delivery dilutes the calcium and magnesium concentration in the urine","C":"Furosemide upregulates parathyroid hormone secretion independent of any electrical effect","D":"Loss of the lumen-positive potential removes the driving force for paracellular cations","E":"The drug increases glomerular filtration of both cations without any tubular effect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because NKCC2 blockade eliminates the K+ recycling that generates the +7 mV lumen-positive potential, the paracellular driving force for both Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption is lost together, producing combined hypercalciuria and hypermagnesuria.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: loop diuretics wasting BOTH divalent cations (vs. thiazides sparing calcium) is a classic diuretic-comparison board question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the thick ascending limb referred to as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron?","choices":{"A":"It secretes additional free water into the lumen faster than any other segment","B":"It reabsorbs equal fractions of solute and water, keeping the fluid isosmotic along its length","C":"It is a segment whose reabsorption depends entirely on ADH levels","D":"It reabsorbs water preferentially over solute, concentrating the fluid as it moves along","E":"It reabsorbs a large fraction of filtered solute while remaining impermeable to water"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the thick ascending limb actively reabsorbs NaCl but cannot reabsorb water alongside it, the tubular fluid leaving the segment has a lower solute concentration and lower osmolarity than plasma, which is the definition of dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"diluting segment\" pairs with the early distal tubule (Obj 1 of SDL 14) as the two water-impermeable, solute-reabsorbing segments that generate dilute tubular fluid before the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the thick ascending limb especially vulnerable to ischemic acute tubular necrosis during renal hypoperfusion?","choices":{"A":"Its active NKCC2 transport creates high oxygen demand amid low medullary oxygen tension","B":"It lacks any local vasodilatory mechanism that could protect it during hypoperfusion","C":"It lacks mitochondria entirely, relying instead on anaerobic metabolism for its energy needs","D":"Its epithelium regenerates more slowly than most other nephron segments after injury","E":"It receives essentially no blood supply under normal physiologic conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Active Na+ reabsorption via NKCC2 requires substantial ATP, giving the thick ascending limb a high oxygen demand. Combined with the medulla's naturally low baseline oxygen tension, this makes the segment particularly susceptible to ischemic injury when renal perfusion falls.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TAL vulnerability to ischemic ATN is a recurring theme linking renal hemodynamics (SDL 8) to tubular injury — a classic two-objective bridge question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — this same oxygen-demand vulnerability is why the protective prostaglandin mechanism below is clinically essential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"How do local renal prostaglandins such as PGE2 and PGI2 protect the thick ascending limb during states of reduced renal perfusion?","choices":{"A":"They directly increase mitochondrial ATP production, supplying extra energy for transport","B":"They constrict the efferent arteriole, protecting filtration independent of tubular transport","C":"They stimulate additional Na+ reabsorption, which raises the segment's oxygen demand further","D":"They block ROMK channels, removing the paracellular pathway for divalent cations","E":"They inhibit NKCC2-mediated Na+ reabsorption, lowering the segment's own oxygen consumption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport, local prostaglandins reduce the amount of active transport the thick ascending limb must perform, which lowers its oxygen demand at a time when oxygen delivery is already compromised, protecting the segment from ischemic injury.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: prostaglandins also dilate afferent arterioles to protect GFR during hemorrhage — the same renal protective system operates at both the vascular and tubular level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — this same oxygen-demand vulnerability is why the protective prostaglandin mechanism below is clinically essential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Why are NSAIDs particularly dangerous in a patient with hypoperfusion from hemorrhage or dehydration?","choices":{"A":"They directly constrict the thick ascending limb epithelium and cause mechanical obstruction","B":"They block the prostaglandin synthesis that protects renal flow and lowers oxygen demand","C":"They stimulate excessive NKCC2 activity and worsen solute reabsorption beyond normal limits","D":"They increase renal blood flow beyond what the kidney can safely filter","E":"They block aldosterone receptors and cause acute hyperkalemia independent of perfusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a hypoperfused kidney, protective vasodilatory prostaglandins are maintaining renal blood flow and reducing the thick ascending limb's oxygen consumption. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing both protections at once and precipitating acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID-induced AKI in volume-depleted patients is one of the highest-yield pharmacology-physiology crossover questions on the exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — this same oxygen-demand vulnerability is why the protective prostaglandin mechanism below is clinically essential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes evaluation for a family history of chronic kidney disease. Imaging shows normal-appearing kidneys, and a research protocol measures tubular fluid osmolarity at sequential points along her loop of Henle. Fluid osmolarity rises steadily from the start of the descending limb to the bend of the loop, then falls steadily along the ascending limb back toward the cortex. Which single permeability difference between the two thin limbs best accounts for this pattern?","choices":{"A":"The descending limb actively pumps Na+ into the interstitium while the ascending limb does not","B":"Both limbs are equally permeable to water, with osmolarity changes driven entirely by urea secretion","C":"The descending limb is water-permeable while the ascending limb is water-impermeable but NaCl-permeable","D":"The ascending limb is more water-permeable than the descending limb, so osmolarity would fall on the way down and rise on the way up","E":"Osmolarity changes reflect purely glomerular filtration rate variation along the loop of Henle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water leaving the permeable descending limb concentrates the fluid on the way down; NaCl then leaving the impermeable-to-water but NaCl-permeable ascending limb dilutes the fluid on the way back up, producing exactly this rise-then-fall osmolarity profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this osmolarity profile along the loop is a classic figure-based board question — know which direction each solute/water moves in each thin limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — thin limb permeability underlies the corticopapillary gradient built further in SDL 19","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A comparative physiology study measures the maximum urine-concentrating ability of two mammalian species. Species A has proportionally much longer loops of Henle extending deep into the medulla than Species B. Assuming similar tubular transport machinery otherwise, which prediction about Species A follows directly from thin-limb physiology?","choices":{"A":"Species A will have a lower maximum urine osmolarity because of greater water loss along the descending limb","B":"Species A and Species B will have identical maximum urine osmolarity regardless of loop length","C":"Species A will secrete more urea in its ascending limb, offsetting any concentrating advantage","D":"Species A will require more ADH to achieve any given urine osmolarity than Species B","E":"Species A can achieve a higher maximum urine osmolarity than Species B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A longer descending limb allows tubular fluid more distance and time to lose water into an increasingly hypertonic medullary interstitium, producing more concentrated fluid at the bend of the loop and ultimately a higher achievable maximum urine osmolarity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: desert mammals (e.g., kangaroo rats) with very long loops of Henle achieve extraordinarily concentrated urine — a classic comparative-physiology teaching example.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — thin limb permeability underlies the corticopapillary gradient built further in SDL 19","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher genetically knocks out the water channels responsible for water permeability in the thin descending limb of an experimental animal, leaving all other transport pathways intact. What is the most direct expected consequence for fluid reaching the bend of the loop?","choices":{"A":"Tubular fluid at the bend would be far less concentrated than normal","B":"Tubular fluid at the bend would become more concentrated, since less water dilutes the remaining solute","C":"Tubular fluid osmolarity at the bend would be unaffected, since the descending limb contributes nothing to concentrating the fluid","D":"The animal would develop obligate glucosuria as a compensatory response","E":"NaCl reabsorption in the ascending limb would increase to fully compensate for the lost concentrating step"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without water permeability, the descending limb loses its ability to lose water into the hypertonic interstitium, so tubular fluid arriving at the bend would remain close to its original osmolarity instead of becoming progressively concentrated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this mirrors the physiology behind aquaporin-1 knockout models, which show markedly impaired urine-concentrating ability despite an intact ascending limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — thin limb permeability underlies the corticopapillary gradient built further in SDL 19","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with hypercalcemia from a parathyroid adenoma has a high serum calcium level that activates the calcium-sensing receptor in the thick ascending limb. Through what downstream mechanism would activation of this receptor be expected to reduce paracellular calcium reabsorption in this segment?","choices":{"A":"It directly opens a transcellular calcium channel that bypasses the paracellular pathway","B":"It reduces NKCC2 activity, weakening the potential for calcium movement across tight junctions","C":"It stimulates additional ROMK channel insertion, strengthening the lumen-positive potential further","D":"It increases basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity, indirectly raising calcium reabsorption","E":"It has no effect on thick ascending limb transport and instead acts in the distal tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Calcium-sensing receptor activation in the thick ascending limb inhibits NKCC2 activity, which weakens K+ recycling through ROMK and reduces the lumen-positive potential. Since paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption depends on that potential, its reduction lowers calcium reabsorption, providing a physiologic brake against worsening hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this calcium-sensing receptor feedback is the physiologic basis for why hypercalcemia itself limits further calcium reabsorption — a protective mechanism distinct from PTH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with Bartter syndrome, a genetic disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in NKCC2, presents with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypercalciuria despite normal dietary intake. Which mechanism directly explains her hypercalciuria?","choices":{"A":"NKCC2 loss increases distal tubule calcium channel expression and actively secretes calcium into the urine","B":"Chronic metabolic alkalosis directly increases intestinal calcium absorption that is then excreted unchanged","C":"A dysfunctional NKCC2 no longer sustains the potential that drives paracellular calcium reabsorption","D":"Hypokalemia independently stimulates parathyroid hormone release and causes bone calcium mobilization","E":"The mutation disrupts basolateral calcium-ATPase activity that is confined to the proximal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bartter syndrome is essentially a genetic loop-diuretic state: without functional NKCC2, K+ recycling through ROMK cannot generate the lumen-positive potential, so the paracellular driving force for calcium (and magnesium) reabsorption is lost, producing hypercalciuria that mirrors chronic loop diuretic use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bartter syndrome is essentially \"genetic furosemide\" — presenting with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypercalciuria; a frequently tested electrolyte-genetics vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"An investigational drug selectively blocks apical ROMK channels in the thick ascending limb without affecting NKCC2 or basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase. Compared to baseline, what would be the most direct effect on Na+ reabsorption and the lumen-positive potential in this segment?","choices":{"A":"Na+ reabsorption would increase to compensate for the blocked K+ channel, preserving the potential","B":"Na+ reabsorption would be unaffected, since NKCC2 does not depend on ROMK-mediated K+ recycling","C":"The lumen-positive potential would increase, since blocking K+ efflux traps more positive charge intracellularly","D":"Na+ reabsorption would fall from lost K+ recycling, and the +7 mV potential would be largely lost too","E":"Both Na+ reabsorption and the potential would be unaffected, since ROMK plays no role in either process"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NKCC2 requires a continuous supply of luminal K+ to keep transporting, and luminal K+ concentration is low relative to what NKCC2 needs, so ROMK-mediated recycling of K+ back into the lumen is what sustains ongoing cotransporter turnover. Blocking ROMK starves NKCC2 of luminal K+, reducing Na+ reabsorption and largely abolishing the lumen-positive potential that recycling itself generates.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ROMK is functionally coupled to NKCC2 turnover, not just potential generation — this is why ROMK loss-of-function mutations also cause a Bartter-like phenotype.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with heart failure and chronic diuretic resistance is switched from furosemide to a combination of furosemide plus a thiazide diuretic, achieving substantially greater diuresis than either drug alone. Which mechanism best explains this synergy?","choices":{"A":"The thiazide directly potentiates furosemide binding at the NKCC2 chloride site itself","B":"Blocking NKCC2 raises distal sodium delivery, which the thiazide then blocks from being reabsorbed","C":"Furosemide increases renal blood flow, which independently enhances thiazide delivery to the distal tubule","D":"Both drugs act at the identical transporter, producing an additive but mechanistically redundant effect","E":"The thiazide blocks aldosterone receptors, preventing any distal compensation for the lost TAL sodium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic loop diuretic use causes the distal nephron to hypertrophy and reabsorb more of the sodium that escapes the blocked thick ascending limb, blunting the diuretic effect over time. Adding a thiazide blocks that compensatory distal NCC-mediated reabsorption, unmasking the full natriuretic effect of the loop diuretic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sequential nephron blockade (loop + thiazide) is a classic clinical pharmacology teaching point for diuretic-resistant edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — this sequential-blockade principle depends on the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is prescribed a loop diuretic for resistant hypertension. Several weeks later, laboratory studies show a serum magnesium of 1.2 mg/dL (low) and a 24-hour urine calcium that is elevated above her pretreatment baseline. Which single upstream event best accounts for both findings occurring together?","choices":{"A":"NKCC2 blockade abolished the lumen-positive potential driving paracellular reabsorption of both cations","B":"The drug independently blocked a magnesium-specific channel and a separate calcium-specific channel","C":"Increased urine flow rate by itself diluted both cations without any change in their total reabsorption","D":"The drug stimulated parathyroid hormone release, which increased renal calcium reabsorption while wasting magnesium","E":"Reduced glomerular filtration from volume depletion decreased the filtered load of both cations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb share the same paracellular, potential-driven mechanism, a single upstream event — loss of the lumen-positive potential from NKCC2 blockade — explains both the hypomagnesemia and the hypercalciuria appearing together.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: combined hypomagnesemia and hypercalciuria on a loop diuretic should immediately suggest this shared TAL mechanism rather than two independent processes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — this sequential-blockade principle depends on the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with severe hyponatremia due to heart failure is treated with intravenous furosemide, producing substantial natriuresis. Why does the thick ascending limb's designation as the \"diluting segment\" explain why loop diuretics can worsen free-water clearance abnormalities if not carefully monitored?","choices":{"A":"Blocking NKCC2 causes the segment to actively secrete additional water into the lumen","B":"Blocking NKCC2 has no effect on urine dilution, since dilution occurs entirely in the collecting duct","C":"Blocking NKCC2 increases ADH secretion directly, independent of any nephron segment effect","D":"Blocking NKCC2 increases water permeability in the thick ascending limb itself","E":"Blocking NKCC2 stops the segment from forming hypotonic fluid needed for free-water excretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thick ascending limb normally dilutes tubular fluid by reabsorbing solute without water. When a loop diuretic blocks this reabsorption, the segment can no longer generate dilute fluid, which paradoxically impairs the kidney's capacity for free-water excretion even while the drug produces substantial natriuresis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics impair urine dilution and can blunt free-water clearance despite promoting sodium loss, whereas thiazides are the classic diuretic cause of hyponatremia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — the thick ascending limb's diluting function sets up the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with chronic NSAID use for osteoarthritis develops acute gastroenteritis with several days of vomiting and poor oral intake. He is admitted with a rise in creatinine from his baseline. Through what combined mechanism did his chronic NSAID use predispose him to this acute kidney injury during volume depletion?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs directly damaged the glomerular basement membrane independent of any hemodynamic effect","B":"NSAIDs increased renal blood flow beyond the kidney's autoregulatory capacity and caused barotrauma","C":"NSAIDs stimulated excessive aldosterone secretion, producing volume overload during the illness","D":"NSAIDs blocked the vasodilation and oxygen-sparing that protect the kidney during hypoperfusion","E":"NSAIDs blocked ADH release and prevented any renal water conservation response to the vomiting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During volume depletion, renal prostaglandins normally dilate afferent arterioles to preserve glomerular filtration and reduce thick ascending limb oxygen consumption to protect against ischemia. Chronic NSAID use had already suppressed this protective prostaglandin production, so when true volume depletion from vomiting occurred, the kidney had no reserve mechanism left to prevent acute injury.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this NSAID-plus-volume-depletion combination is one of the most frequently tested causes of prerenal-to-intrinsic AKI progression in clinical medicine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher measures oxygen consumption at multiple points along the nephron in an isolated perfused kidney model. The thick ascending limb shows disproportionately high oxygen consumption relative to its size compared to the proximal tubule. What best explains this finding given the segment's transport physiology?","choices":{"A":"The thick ascending limb has a larger absolute surface area than the proximal tubule that raises its total oxygen demand","B":"Active NKCC2-mediated Na+ reabsorption requires continuous ATP-dependent pump activity to sustain the gradient","C":"The thick ascending limb performs glucose reabsorption in addition to sodium transport that doubles its energy needs","D":"The thick ascending limb generates heat as its primary function, more than other reabsorptive segments","E":"The thick ascending limb has fewer mitochondria than the proximal tubule that forces inefficient metabolism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thick ascending limb reabsorbs a large fraction of filtered sodium entirely through active, ATP-dependent transport (NKCC2 driven by basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase), unlike the proximal tubule, which reabsorbs much of its sodium load passively alongside water and other cotransported solutes, giving the TAL a disproportionately high oxygen cost for the sodium it moves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing which nephron segments rely on active vs. passive transport directly predicts their relative ischemic vulnerability — a recurring integrative exam theme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with sepsis and hypotension is started on an NSAID for fever control before the renal risk is recognized, and the medication is stopped after one dose once renal function begins to decline. Which finding would most directly confirm that prostaglandin-dependent vasodilation had been transiently lost as the mechanism of this decline?","choices":{"A":"A measurable fall in blood flow and filtration rate linked to the dose, without glomerular structural damage","B":"A rise in serum creatinine occurring several weeks after the single dose, well outside the expected hemodynamic window","C":"Persistent proteinuria on urinalysis, consistent with glomerular basement membrane injury","D":"A finding of red blood cell casts on urine microscopy, suggesting an inflammatory glomerular process","E":"An elevated eosinophil count, suggesting an allergic interstitial nephritis reaction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A single-dose, temporally linked, purely hemodynamic decline in renal blood flow and GFR without structural markers of glomerular or interstitial injury is the expected signature of acutely losing protective prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation, distinguishing it from delayed or structural causes of NSAID-associated kidney injury.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: distinguishing hemodynamic (prostaglandin-mediated) NSAID nephrotoxicity from allergic interstitial nephritis (delayed, eosinophils present) is a common differentiation question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Distal Convoluted Tubule & Cortical Diluting Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"What ions does the NCC cotransporter move across the luminal membrane of the early distal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Two Na+ ions move together with one Cl-, producing an electrogenic current","B":"One Na+, one K+, and two Cl- ions move together, mirroring the thick ascending limb transporter stoichiometry","C":"One Na+ and one Cl- move together, making the process electroneutral","D":"A single Cl- ion crosses independently without any accompanying cation","E":"One Na+ moves independently through the channel, while Cl- follows a separate paracellular route"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The early distal tubule NCC cotransporter moves one Na+ and one Cl- together, making the process electroneutral; the thick ascending limb NKCC2 moves four ions (one Na+, one K+, and two Cl-) and is also electroneutral, with that segment's lumen-positive potential arising from ROMK-mediated K+ recycling rather than from the cotransporter itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NCC stoichiometry (1 Na+:1 Cl-) is the key distinguishing feature from NKCC2 (1 Na+:1 K+:2 Cl-); both are electroneutral, and the thick ascending limb's lumen-positive potential comes from ROMK K+ recycling. A frequently tested transporter-comparison point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the early distal convoluted tubule called the \"cortical diluting segment\"?","choices":{"A":"It reabsorbs NaCl while staying water-impermeable, lowering fluid osmolarity further","B":"It secretes free water into the lumen to actively dilute the tubular fluid","C":"It is located in the medulla, where the corticopapillary gradient dilutes the fluid passively","D":"It reabsorbs water in excess of solute, which paradoxically lowers osmolarity","E":"It has no active transport and dilution occurs purely by passive solute back-diffusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fluid entering the early distal tubule is already dilute from the thick ascending limb. Because the early distal tubule reabsorbs NaCl via NCC but stays water-impermeable, it dilutes the fluid further, earning its designation as the cortical diluting segment (cortical because distal tubules sit in the renal cortex).","boardPrep":"COMLEX: \"cortical\" distinguishes this segment's location from the medullary thick ascending limb, even though both are solute-reabsorbing, water-impermeable diluting segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"At which site do thiazide diuretics bind to inhibit the NCC cotransporter?","choices":{"A":"An extracellular Na+-binding regulatory domain, distinct from the transport pore itself","B":"An ATP-binding site, supplying the energy for active NCC cycling","C":"A basolateral receptor, triggering internalization of the transporter","D":"A calcium-binding regulatory loop, located on the intracellular tail of NCC","E":"The Cl- binding site, preventing NCC from completing its transport cycle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Like loop diuretics at NKCC2, thiazide diuretics are organic acid anions that bind the Cl- site of the NCC cotransporter, preventing it from cycling and thereby blocking NaCl reabsorption in the early distal tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides and loop diuretics share a Cl--site binding mechanism on their respective cotransporters — a useful mnemonic link between the two drug classes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"How does Na+ cross the luminal membrane of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"Via a Na+-Cl- cotransporter, identical to the one in the early distal tubule","B":"Via a Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, shared with the thick ascending limb","C":"Actively via a luminal Na+-ATPase, distinct from the basolateral pump","D":"Passively through ENaC channels, diffusing down its electrochemical gradient","E":"Via a Na+-glucose cotransporter, recycling residual filtered glucose"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unlike the coupled cotransporters of upstream segments, principal cells use ENaC, a dedicated Na+ channel, allowing Na+ to diffuse into the cell down its electrochemical gradient before being extruded basolaterally by Na+-K+-ATPase.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ENaC is a channel, not a cotransporter — recognizing this distinguishes principal cell Na+ entry from every upstream nephron segment discussed so far.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"Where does aldosterone bind after diffusing into a principal cell?","choices":{"A":"A luminal membrane receptor, coupled to a G protein and adenylyl cyclase","B":"An intracellular receptor, which then translocates to the nucleus","C":"A basolateral tyrosine kinase receptor, phosphorylating ENaC directly","D":"An extracellular binding protein, shuttling it to a cell-surface channel","E":"A cytoplasmic calcium-binding protein, activating protein kinase C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aldosterone, a steroid hormone, diffuses across the basolateral membrane and binds an intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor. The hormone-receptor complex then moves to the nucleus, where it directs transcription of new mRNAs and proteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid hormones act through intracellular/nuclear receptors and gene transcription, in contrast to peptide hormones like ADH, which use surface receptors and second messengers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"What proteins does aldosterone-directed transcription ultimately increase in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"ENaC channels and Na+-K+-ATPase pumps together with citrate synthase","B":"NKCC2 cotransporters and ROMK channels of the thick ascending limb","C":"Aquaporin-2 water channels, leaving Na+ transport proteins unaffected","D":"NCC cotransporters located in the early distal tubule, upstream of the principal cells","E":"V2 receptors for ADH located on the principal cell basolateral membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aldosterone-induced gene transcription increases synthesis of the luminal ENaC channel, the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, and mitochondrial citric acid cycle enzymes such as citrate synthase, which supply the ATP needed to sustain the increased pump activity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: citrate synthase induction is a less commonly recalled but board-relevant detail — aldosterone increases the metabolic machinery supporting transport, not just the transporters themselves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does spironolactone reduce Na+ reabsorption by principal cells?","choices":{"A":"It binds directly to ENaC channels, physically occluding the pore","B":"It inhibits basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity, independent of aldosterone levels","C":"It degrades circulating aldosterone, preventing it from reaching the principal cell","D":"It competitively blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, preventing hormone binding","E":"It blocks the V2 receptor for ADH, located on the basolateral membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone is a steroid structurally related to aldosterone that competitively occupies the mineralocorticoid receptor, blocking aldosterone from entering the nucleus and preventing the transcription-dependent increase in ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, and metabolic enzymes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spironolactone works upstream at the receptor/transcription level, while amiloride and triamterene work downstream at the channel itself — a key mechanistic distinction between K+-sparing diuretic subclasses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does amiloride reduce Na+ reabsorption by principal cells?","choices":{"A":"It competitively displaces aldosterone from the mineralocorticoid receptor","B":"It prevents aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal zona glomerulosa","C":"It blocks basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, indirectly reducing the driving force for ENaC entry","D":"It stimulates internalization of ENaC channels from the luminal membrane","E":"It binds luminal ENaC channels, without involving the mineralocorticoid receptor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Amiloride and triamterene act directly on the luminal ENaC channel, physically blocking Na+ entry regardless of aldosterone levels, in contrast to spironolactone, which works entirely through receptor antagonism and gene transcription.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: amiloride is useful even when aldosterone is already suppressed, since its action does not depend on the hormone-receptor pathway at all.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"Why are K+-sparing diuretics typically combined with loop or thiazide diuretics rather than used as monotherapy for diuresis?","choices":{"A":"They are rapidly inactivated by hepatic metabolism, reducing their overall effect","B":"They inhibit a modest fraction of Na+ reabsorption, limiting their effect","C":"They require a functioning thick ascending limb, delaying their onset of action","D":"They cause severe hypokalemia, unless combined with a second diuretic","E":"They have no natriuretic effect, acting purely on potassium transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the late distal tubule and collecting duct reabsorb only about 3% of filtered Na+, blocking that small fraction produces only mild diuresis. K+-sparing diuretics are combined with more potent loop or thiazide diuretics mainly to offset the kaliuresis those drugs cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each diuretic class to the fraction of filtered Na+ reabsorbed at its nephron site directly predicts its natriuretic potency — a recurring pharmacology integration point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What determines whether the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct are permeable to water?","choices":{"A":"The magnitude of the lumen-positive electrical potential, present in that segment","B":"The local concentration of Na+-Cl- cotransporter activity, occurring in that segment","C":"Circulating ADH levels, which control AQP2 channel insertion","D":"Fixed anatomic water channels, present regardless of hormonal status","E":"The rate of active solute reabsorption, occurring in the same segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike the ascending limb of Henle and early distal tubule, which are always water-impermeable regardless of hormonal state, principal cell water permeability is variable and controlled entirely by ADH-driven aquaporin-2 insertion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the physiologic basis for calling the ascending limb and early distal tubule \"fixed impermeable\" versus the late distal tubule/collecting duct \"variable, hormone-controlled\" permeability segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What second messenger is generated when ADH binds its basolateral V2 receptor on principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP, produced by Gs-protein-coupled activation of adenylyl cyclase","B":"Cyclic GMP, produced by activation of a membrane-bound guanylyl cyclase","C":"Inositol trisphosphate, produced by phospholipase C activation","D":"Diacylglycerol, produced alongside IP3 by phospholipase C","E":"Calcium released directly from intracellular stores by receptor activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ADH binding to the V2 receptor activates a stimulatory G protein (Gs), which activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP. cAMP then activates protein kinase A, which triggers phosphorylation events leading to AQP2 vesicle insertion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: V2 receptor signaling (Gs/cAMP/PKA) is a classic second-messenger pathway question, often paired with pseudohypoparathyroidism-style Gs-defect vignettes for other hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct functional consequence of protein kinase A activation in a principal cell exposed to high ADH levels?","choices":{"A":"Existing luminal Na+ channels are phosphorylated and directly gated open","B":"Vesicles containing AQP2 channels are shuttled into the luminal membrane","C":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase pumps are synthesized de novo within minutes","D":"Mineralocorticoid receptors are translocated into the nucleus immediately","E":"Cyclic AMP is converted back to ATP, terminating the signaling cascade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Protein kinase A phosphorylates intracellular structures involved in vesicle trafficking, causing AQP2-containing vesicles to be shuttled to and inserted into the luminal membrane, which is the step that directly increases the cell's water permeability.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: AQP2 trafficking (not new channel synthesis) is the acute mechanism; chronic ADH exposure additionally upregulates AQP2 gene expression, a distinct slower process sometimes tested separately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones is started on chlorthalidone. Over several weeks, her urinary calcium excretion falls substantially even though her Na+ excretion increases. Why does inhibiting NCC in the early distal tubule paradoxically increase Ca2+ reabsorption rather than decrease it?","choices":{"A":"Thiazides directly open a luminal calcium channel independent of any change in intracellular Na+","B":"Thiazides increase PTH secretion directly, which is the sole driver of the observed calcium effect","C":"Thiazides reduce glomerular filtration of calcium, lowering the filtered load reaching the tubule","D":"Lower intracellular Na+ from NCC blockade drives more Na+-Ca2+ exchange and luminal Ca2+ entry","E":"Thiazides upregulate a distal calcium-binding protein that has no relationship to sodium transport"},"correct":"D","explanation":"By inhibiting apical NCC, thiazides lower intracellular Na+ concentration in early distal tubule cells. This favors increased basolateral Na+-Ca2+ exchanger activity, which extrudes Ca2+ from the cell more efficiently and, by lowering intracellular Ca2+, favors greater passive Ca2+ entry across the luminal membrane, net increasing distal Ca2+ reabsorption despite the concurrent block of Na+ reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide-induced hypocalciuria (via enhanced distal Ca2+ reabsorption) is the mechanistic basis for using thiazides in idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium stone disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — this same distal calcium-handling mechanism is examined in depth in SDL 18's renal calcium objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with nephrogenic diabetes insipidus produces large volumes of dilute urine because the collecting duct principal cells fail to respond to ADH. Her physician prescribes a thiazide diuretic rather than an ADH analog. Which mechanism explains why a thiazide diuretic paradoxically reduces this patient's urine output?","choices":{"A":"The thiazide directly restores V2 receptor sensitivity to circulating ADH","B":"The thiazide increases aquaporin-2 insertion independent of any ADH signaling","C":"Volume contraction from NCC blockade increases proximal reabsorption and lowers distal delivery","D":"The thiazide blocks the countercurrent multiplier entirely, abolishing free-water clearance","E":"The thiazide converts nephrogenic diabetes insipidus into central diabetes insipidus pharmacologically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An ADH analog would be futile because the defect is in the principal cell response to ADH, not in ADH secretion. Thiazides work indirectly: by causing mild volume contraction, they increase proximal tubule Na+ and water reabsorption, which reduces the volume of fluid delivered to the water-impermeable diluting segments and ultimately lowers overall urine output.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: recognizing that thiazides treat nephrogenic DI through a volume-contraction, proximal-reabsorption mechanism (not by fixing the ADH defect) is a classic clinical pharmacology teaching point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — the fixed water-impermeability of the diluting segments discussed here connects directly to Obj 4's ADH-dependent segments below","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher perfuses an isolated early distal tubule segment and finds that adding a thiazide diuretic to the luminal perfusate abolishes NaCl reabsorption, while adding the same drug to the basolateral (blood) side has no effect. What does this finding indicate about NCC's drug-binding site?","choices":{"A":"The Cl- binding site targeted by thiazides is reachable from the lumen but not the basolateral membrane","B":"NCC has two independent Cl- binding sites that function identically regardless of luminal or basolateral location","C":"The thiazide is degraded before it can cross the basolateral membrane and produce any effect","D":"Thiazides bind the Na+ site of NCC, which faces the blood side of the cell","E":"The basolateral membrane lacks the ATP supply needed for thiazide binding to have any effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because NCC transports Na+ and Cl- from the lumen into the cell, the Cl- site that thiazides occupy to block cycling is only accessible from the luminal side, consistent with luminal but not basolateral drug application abolishing transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diuretics acting on luminal cotransporters (loop diuretics, thiazides) all require access from the tubular fluid side, which is why protein binding in the tubular lumen (as in nephrotic syndrome) impairs their delivery and effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — the Cl--site binding mechanism mirrors the loop diuretic pharmacology covered in SDL 13's NKCC2 objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a rare genetic mutation has principal cells with normal mineralocorticoid receptors but constitutively active ENaC channels that no longer require aldosterone stimulation. Which transport abnormality would be expected even if his serum aldosterone level is measured as very low?","choices":{"A":"Normal Na+ and K+ handling because ENaC activity would still require aldosterone-driven gene transcription to function","B":"Excessive renal Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion driven by unregulated ENaC activity","C":"Severe hyponatremia from excessive Na+ loss due to loss of aldosterone-driven ENaC synthesis","D":"Hyperkalemia from reduced K+ secretion because ENaC activity determines the driving force for ROMK","E":"No abnormality because basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity is what determines the transport rate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because this mutation makes ENaC constitutively active independent of aldosterone, Na+ reabsorption and the resulting lumen-negative potential that drives K+ secretion through ROMK proceed at a high rate regardless of aldosterone level, producing a picture resembling aldosterone excess (Na+ retention, K+ wasting) despite genuinely low aldosterone (this describes the physiology of Liddle syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome (constitutively active ENaC) produces hypertension and hypokalemia with LOW aldosterone and renin, distinguishing it from primary hyperaldosteronism on labs alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — the ENaC-ROMK electrical coupling described here is the same principle tested in SDL 17's principal cell secretion objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman is found to have a mutation that prevents aldosterone-bound mineralocorticoid receptor complexes from entering the nucleus, while receptor binding to aldosterone itself remains intact. What downstream consequence would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Normal Na+ reabsorption, since receptor binding is sufficient by itself to activate ENaC directly","B":"Increased Na+ reabsorption, since the trapped receptor complex acts as a constitutive activator in the cytoplasm","C":"Selective loss of ENaC transcription, with Na+-K+-ATPase and citrate synthase unaffected","D":"Compensatory upregulation of amiloride-sensitive channels independent of the mineralocorticoid pathway","E":"Failure of ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, and citrate synthase transcription despite normal hormone-receptor binding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone's genomic mechanism requires the hormone-receptor complex to translocate to the nucleus to direct mRNA synthesis. If nuclear translocation is blocked, none of the aldosterone-induced proteins — ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, or citrate synthase — are transcribed, functionally mimicking mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism even though the receptor itself binds hormone normally.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this vignette isolates the nuclear-translocation step of steroid hormone signaling, distinct from ligand binding — a mechanism-dissection style question favored on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — spironolactone (Obj 3, below) blocks this same pathway pharmacologically rather than through mutation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure on chronic loop diuretic therapy develops hyperaldosteronism secondary to activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Which combination of principal cell changes would be expected from the sustained elevation in aldosterone?","choices":{"A":"Coordinated increases in ENaC channels, Na+-K+-ATPase pumps, and citrate synthase","B":"Increased ENaC synthesis, with no change in Na+-K+-ATPase or citric acid cycle enzymes","C":"Increased Na+-K+-ATPase synthesis, since ENaC expression is aldosterone-independent","D":"Decreased ENaC synthesis, as a compensatory response to chronic diuretic-induced volume loss","E":"No change in principal cell protein synthesis, since genomic effects require weeks to develop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sustained aldosterone elevation drives coordinated transcription of the full set of aldosterone-induced proteins in principal cells — the luminal ENaC channel, the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, and citric acid cycle enzymes such as citrate synthase that supply the ATP needed to power the increased pump activity — not any single protein in isolation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary hyperaldosteronism in heart failure/diuretic therapy is a common clinical vignette; recognizing the coordinated, multi-protein nature of the genomic response is the key teaching point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — this same secondary hyperaldosteronism mechanism recurs in SDL 15's integrated sodium-balance objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with resistant hypertension and hyperaldosteronism from an aldosterone-producing adenoma is treated with spironolactone, but develops breast tenderness and gynecomastia over several months. His physician switches him to amiloride instead. Which property specific to spironolactone, absent with amiloride, best explains this side-effect profile?","choices":{"A":"Spironolactone causes a much greater degree of ENaC blockade than amiloride at equivalent doses","B":"Amiloride has no effect on K+ secretion, while spironolactone directly increases it","C":"Spironolactone, as a steroid, carries off-target antiandrogen activity that amiloride lacks","D":"Spironolactone is metabolized to a compound that independently raises H+ secretion in the kidney","E":"Amiloride works in the proximal tubule, so it cannot cause the same distal side effects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both drugs reduce distal K+ secretion by blunting Na+ reabsorption and the associated lumen-negative potential, so hyperkalemia risk is a class effect shared by both, not something specific to spironolactone. What distinguishes spironolactone as a steroid molecule is its off-target binding to androgen and progesterone receptors, producing side effects like gynecomastia that amiloride, a non-steroidal ENaC blocker, does not share. This structural difference, rather than any difference in K+-sparing potency, accounts for the side-effect profile.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spironolactone's antiandrogen activity (gynecomastia, menstrual irregularity) is a distinguishing clinical feature versus amiloride/triamterene, which lack steroid-receptor cross-reactivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — this receptor-versus-channel distinction is the same one tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 3 mechanism questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with cirrhosis and ascites is treated with furosemide plus spironolactone. In addition to enhancing diuresis, the combination limits a furosemide-induced side effect. Which furosemide-induced process does spironolactone most directly offset?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide-induced direct inhibition of the mineralocorticoid receptor itself","B":"Increased distal Na+ delivery driving greater ENaC-mediated reabsorption and K+ secretion","C":"Furosemide-induced destruction of principal cells requiring replacement by aldosterone-driven regeneration","D":"Furosemide-induced suppression of ADH secretion that spironolactone directly restores","E":"Furosemide-induced hypercalcemia that spironolactone corrects through calcium-sparing action"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics increase Na+ delivery to the distal nephron, where increased ENaC-mediated reabsorption in principal cells drives greater K+ secretion (kaliuresis). Spironolactone blunts this compensatory ENaC-driven Na+ reabsorption by blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor, thereby reducing the associated K+ loss while contributing natriuresis of its own.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combining loop/thiazide diuretics with K+-sparing agents specifically to offset kaliuresis is one of the most frequently tested diuretic-combination rationales.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — increased distal Na+ delivery from loop diuretic blockade is the same downstream effect discussed in SDL 13's sequential nephron blockade vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Bartter syndrome (nonfunctional NKCC2) has persistent hypokalemia despite adequate oral potassium supplementation. His physician considers adding a K+-sparing diuretic to reduce ongoing distal K+ secretion. Which agent would remain effective independent of circulating aldosterone levels, given that the primary defect is upstream in the thick ascending limb, not in ENaC itself?","choices":{"A":"Amiloride, since it blocks ENaC directly regardless of what caused the increased downstream Na+ delivery","B":"Furosemide, since it would directly correct the K+ wasting at its NKCC2 site of action","C":"A thiazide diuretic, since blocking NCC in the distal tubule would compensate for the nonfunctional NKCC2 upstream","D":"Spironolactone, since blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor would also restore NKCC2 function","E":"No K+-sparing diuretic would have any effect, since the primary defect lies upstream in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Regardless of the primary defect's location, amiloride acts locally on ENaC in the distal nephron and reduces K+ secretion driven by whatever Na+ delivery reaches that segment, making it effective even in a Bartter syndrome patient whose distal Na+ load is elevated because of an upstream NKCC2 defect. Spironolactone is also used clinically in Bartter syndrome, but it would not restore NKCC2 function, and the other options either worsen K+ wasting or misstate the effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: amiloride's site-specific mechanism (direct ENaC block) makes it broadly useful for distal K+ wasting regardless of the upstream cause; K+-sparing agents help by reducing distal K+ secretion, not by correcting the NKCC2 defect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Bartter syndrome's NKCC2 defect is the same genetic lesion introduced in SDL 13's deep-vignette Bartter question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with central diabetes insipidus is given desmopressin (a synthetic ADH analog) and his urine osmolarity rises substantially within an hour. Which cellular event most directly accounts for this rapid response, given that new AQP2 protein synthesis would take much longer to occur?","choices":{"A":"Desmopressin directly opens existing luminal AQP2 channels without any signaling cascade","B":"Desmopressin triggers immediate transcription of new AQP2 mRNA that is translated within minutes","C":"Desmopressin increases the permeability of the ascending limb of Henle to compensate acutely","D":"Desmopressin acts on V1 receptors to vasoconstrict the vasa recta, indirectly concentrating urine","E":"Existing AQP2 vesicles are shuttled to and fused with the luminal membrane once PKA is activated"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rapid rise in urine osmolarity reflects the fast, non-transcriptional arm of ADH action: V2-receptor-triggered cAMP/protein kinase A activation causes pre-formed AQP2-containing vesicles already present in the cytoplasm to translocate to and fuse with the luminal membrane within minutes, increasing water permeability immediately. Transcriptional upregulation of AQP2 expression is a separate, slower process that reinforces the effect over hours to days.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing the acute vesicle-trafficking mechanism of ADH action from its chronic transcriptional upregulation of AQP2 is a subtle but testable point on integrated renal physiology exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — this same vesicle-trafficking step is detailed mechanistically in Batch 1's Obj 4 protein kinase A question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares water permeability along the nephron in the presence of maximal circulating ADH. The thick ascending limb and early distal tubule remain impermeable to water throughout, while the late distal tubule and collecting duct become highly permeable. What structural difference between these segment types best explains this contrast?","choices":{"A":"The upstream segments express AQP2 constitutively but at a much lower density than principal cells","B":"Principal cells hold regulatable AQP2 channels; upstream segments carry no water channels under any condition","C":"ADH receptors are present throughout the nephron, but the upstream segments fail to couple to adenylyl cyclase","D":"The upstream segments actively pump water out of the lumen, masking any passive permeability that might exist","E":"The tight junctions of the upstream segments become more permeable to water under high ADH exposure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ascending limb of Henle and early distal tubule are impermeable to water because they lack water channels of any kind, regardless of ADH level — this impermeability is fixed and structural. Principal cells, by contrast, express AQP2 channels that are held in cytoplasmic vesicles until ADH-driven signaling triggers their insertion into the luminal membrane, making their permeability variable and hormone-dependent.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the fixed-versus-variable water permeability distinction along the nephron is essential for predicting how ADH changes urine concentration only at specific segments, not throughout the entire tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — this fixed-impermeability property of the thick ascending limb is the same one established in SDL 13's \"diluting segment\" objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with primary polydipsia develops chronically suppressed ADH secretion from persistent overhydration. Despite years of low ADH exposure, when she is later water-deprived, her principal cells still respond appropriately by inserting AQP2 and concentrating her urine. What does this preserved response indicate about the ADH-AQP2 signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"AQP2 channels had been permanently inserted in her luminal membrane throughout the period of low ADH","B":"Her principal cells developed a compensatory ADH-independent water permeability mechanism during the overhydration period","C":"Chronic low ADH exposure downregulated V2 receptors, and the observed response reflects a nonspecific stress reaction","D":"The V2 receptor-Gs-adenylyl cyclase complex stays intact and functional even after prolonged low ADH exposure","E":"Her collecting ducts secreted their own ADH locally, bypassing the need for pituitary-derived hormone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A preserved concentrating response after water deprivation demonstrates that the entire V2 receptor/Gs/adenylyl cyclase/PKA signaling cascade remains intact and functional; the pathway simply had not been actively engaged during the period of low circulating ADH, distinguishing this from an inherited defect such as nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, where the pathway itself is broken regardless of ADH level or history.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this vignette tests the conceptual difference between a pathway being unstimulated versus a pathway being broken — a distinction central to differentiating primary polydipsia from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus on water-deprivation testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — water-deprivation testing and the central-versus-nephrogenic DI distinction are developed further in SDL 19's urine concentration objective","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Systemic Sodium (Na+) Balance & Natriuretic Peptides","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"What is effective arterial blood volume (EABV)?","choices":{"A":"The total extracellular fluid volume measured across the entire body","B":"The plasma volume remaining after subtracting interstitial fluid volume","C":"The venous blood volume returning to the heart per minute","D":"The portion of ECF volume within arteries that perfuses tissues","E":"The fraction of cardiac output distributed toward the kidneys"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EABV refers specifically to the arterial portion of ECF volume that effectively perfuses tissues, a concept the kidneys sense and respond to by adjusting Na+ excretion, even when it diverges from total ECF volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EABV is a functional, perfusion-based concept distinct from measured ECF volume — the distinction becomes clinically important in edematous states covered later in this objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"How does increased sympathetic nerve activity change renal Na+ handling in response to a fall in arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"It dilates afferent arterioles and decreases proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","B":"It constricts afferent arterioles and increases proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","C":"It constricts efferent arterioles selectively, sparing the afferent arteriole from any change","D":"It has no direct renal effect, acting instead on cardiac output","E":"It increases distal tubule Na+ reabsorption without any vascular effect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Baroreceptor-triggered sympathetic activation constricts renal afferent arterioles and directly stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption, both actions that help restore arterial pressure at the expense of renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the same baroreceptor-sympathetic pathway tested in cardiovascular physiology, applied here to its renal Na+-conserving consequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"How do peritubular capillary Starling forces change when ECF volume expands?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure rises, which stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","B":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure falls, which stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","C":"Peritubular oncotic pressure falls, which inhibits proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","D":"Peritubular oncotic pressure remains fixed regardless of ECF volume changes","E":"Peritubular capillary flow reverses direction, pulling fluid back into the tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ECF volume expansion dilutes plasma proteins in peritubular capillary blood, lowering oncotic pressure and reducing the force favoring fluid uptake from the interstitium, which in turn inhibits proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption and promotes natriuresis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this Starling-force mechanism is the same one governing glomerulotubular balance, applied here to whole-body Na+ regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"What combination of vascular effects does ANP exert on the renal microcirculation?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriolar dilation together with efferent arteriolar constriction","B":"Afferent arteriolar constriction together with efferent arteriolar dilation","C":"Dilation of both afferent and efferent arterioles equally","D":"Constriction of both afferent and efferent arterioles equally","E":"No direct effect on either arteriole, with its action confined to the tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ANP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole, and because the dilatory afferent effect is proportionally larger, the net result is decreased renal vascular resistance, increased renal blood flow, and increased GFR.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ANP's afferent-dilation/efferent-constriction combination is a rare case where both changes push GFR in the same direction, unlike angiotensin II's more complex dose-dependent effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — ANP's hemodynamic and tubular actions built on cGMP signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"Where does ANP act directly to inhibit tubular Na+ reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"The proximal convoluted tubule","B":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","C":"The early distal convoluted tubule via NCC inhibition","D":"The thin descending limb of the loop of Henle","E":"The late distal tubule and collecting ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ANP directly decreases Na+ reabsorption in the late distal tubule and collecting ducts, acting on the same principal cells targeted by aldosterone but in the opposite direction, which is central to its natriuretic effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANP and aldosterone act on the same nephron segment with opposite effects on Na+ reabsorption, a useful pairing for board questions on collecting duct regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — ANP's hemodynamic and tubular actions built on cGMP signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"What second messenger mediates ANP's cellular actions after binding its receptor?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP, generated by a Gs-protein-coupled adenylyl cyclase","B":"Cyclic GMP, generated via intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity","C":"Inositol trisphosphate, generated by phospholipase C activation","D":"Calcium released directly from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","E":"Diacylglycerol, generated alongside IP3 by phospholipase C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ANP receptors possess intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity, so ligand binding directly generates cyclic GMP rather than requiring a separate G-protein/adenylyl cyclase step, distinguishing ANP signaling from the cAMP-based pathway used by ADH.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ANP's cGMP mechanism is shared with nitric oxide signaling and is a frequently tested contrast against ADH's cAMP-based V2 receptor pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — ANP's hemodynamic and tubular actions built on cGMP signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"What triggers ANP release in a patient with sustained mineralocorticoid excess?","choices":{"A":"A direct stimulatory effect of aldosterone on atrial ANP-secreting cells","B":"Hyperkalemia resulting from excessive principal cell K+ secretion","C":"The ECF volume expansion produced by aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","D":"A compensatory fall in renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus","E":"Direct stimulation of the atria by angiotensin II independent of volume status"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption, expanding ECF volume; this volume expansion itself, sensed by atrial stretch receptors, is what triggers ANP secretion, not a direct hormonal action of aldosterone on the atria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that ANP release is a volume-sensing response, not a direct aldosterone effect, is essential for understanding mineralocorticoid escape as a hemodynamic feedback loop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net effect of ANP release during mineralocorticoid escape on collecting duct Na+ handling?","choices":{"A":"It partially offsets aldosterone's Na+-retaining effect, limiting further ECF expansion","B":"It completely reverses aldosterone's Na+ retention, returning ECF volume to baseline","C":"It has no measurable effect on collecting duct Na+ transport during mineralocorticoid excess","D":"It potentiates aldosterone's Na+-retaining action, worsening ECF expansion further","E":"It acts on the proximal tubule instead, leaving the collecting duct unaffected"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ANP inhibits Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct, partially but not completely counteracting the ongoing Na+-retaining effect of excess aldosterone, which is why patients with primary hyperaldosteronism plateau at a new, higher ECF volume rather than developing progressively worsening edema.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mineralocorticoid escape explains why primary hyperaldosteronism classically presents with hypertension but not overt edema, unlike secondary hyperaldosteronism in heart failure or cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"Why does mineralocorticoid escape occur in primary hyperaldosteronism but classically fail to prevent edema in conditions like nephrotic syndrome or heart failure?","choices":{"A":"ANP secretion is blunted in nephrotic syndrome and heart failure compared with hyperaldosteronism","B":"The kidneys become entirely insensitive to ANP signaling once ECF volume is expanded","C":"Aldosterone levels are typically lower in nephrotic syndrome than in primary hyperaldosteronism","D":"Persistently low EABV keeps driving Na+ retention despite ongoing ANP release","E":"ANP receptors are downregulated permanently after the first exposure to volume expansion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In nephrotic syndrome and heart failure, EABV remains chronically low (from protein loss or pump failure) even as ECF volume rises, so the baroreceptor-sensed signal for Na+ retention persists and continues to override ANP's natriuretic effect, unlike in primary hyperaldosteronism, where EABV normalizes once ECF volume expands enough.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ECF-volume-versus-EABV distinction from Obj 1 is exactly what explains why escape succeeds in one condition and fails in another — a classic integrative board question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A healthy 25-year-old man switches from a low-Na+ to a high-Na+ diet over several days. Which combination of changes would be expected in his renin and sympathetic activity as his kidneys restore Na+ balance?","choices":{"A":"Both renin release and sympathetic activity rise as the kidneys attempt to conserve the excess Na+","B":"Renin release rises while sympathetic activity falls in an opposing pattern","C":"Renin release stays unchanged while sympathetic activity rises sharply","D":"Neither renin release nor sympathetic activity changes since Na+ balance is regulated entirely by ANP","E":"Both renin release and sympathetic activity fall as rising EABV is sensed by baroreceptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A high-Na+ diet expands ECF volume and EABV, which is sensed by baroreceptors and leads to coordinated suppression of both sympathetic outflow and renin release, reducing Na+-retaining signals so that excess Na+ can be excreted.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: renin and sympathetic activity move together (not in opposite directions) in response to volume changes, since both respond to the same baroreceptor-sensed EABV signal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — this same baroreceptor-driven suppression is the mirror image of the hemorrhage response covered in SDL 8's renal hemodynamics objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with cirrhosis and ascites has a measured ECF volume far above normal, yet his kidneys avidly retain Na+ as though he were volume-depleted. Which concept best resolves this apparent paradox?","choices":{"A":"His kidneys are responding appropriately to his elevated ECF volume, and the Na+ retention is an error in renal sensing","B":"His EABV is low despite an expanded ECF volume, because fluid has shifted into the peritoneal cavity","C":"ECF volume and EABV are, by definition, identical measurements of the same physiologic space","D":"His arterial pressure must be elevated, which would normally suppress Na+ retention","E":"Cirrhosis eliminates baroreceptor sensing entirely, leaving ANP to regulate Na+ retention independently"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cirrhosis with ascites, portal hypertension and reduced effective circulating volume cause fluid to sequester in the peritoneal cavity, so EABV falls even though total ECF volume rises. The kidneys respond to the low EABV they can sense, not the elevated total ECF volume, explaining the seemingly paradoxical Na+ retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhotic ascites is a classic example, alongside nephrotic syndrome and heart failure, of a dissociation between ECF volume and EABV driving inappropriate renal Na+ retention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — this same ECF-EABV dissociation is the mechanism tested directly in Batch 1's escape-versus-no-escape comparison question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure and reduced cardiac output is found to have elevated renin, elevated sympathetic activity, and significant peripheral edema. Why do the renin and sympathetic responses in this case actually worsen rather than resolve her edema?","choices":{"A":"Renin and sympathetic activity are elevated by direct cardiac injury signals independent of any hemodynamic sensing","B":"Her reduced cardiac output paradoxically produces a high EABV that the kidneys are misinterpreting","C":"The Na+ retention reflects a primary renal defect independent of any hemodynamic signal","D":"Her persistently low EABV keeps triggering Na+ retention even though her ECF volume has expanded","E":"Elevated renin and sympathetic activity in heart failure occur independent of any baroreceptor input"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In heart failure, reduced cardiac output lowers EABV even as total ECF volume and venous/interstitial fluid rise from Na+ retention. Baroreceptors continue to sense this low EABV as if the patient were volume-depleted, sustaining renin release and sympathetic activation that drive further Na+ retention and worsen the edema.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this same low-EABV-despite-high-ECF-volume mechanism links heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome as the three classic causes of edema driven by inappropriate renal Na+ retention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — the renin-sympathetic coupling described here connects directly to SDL 8's and SDL 15 Obj 3's hemodynamic feedback mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher administers a drug that selectively blocks the guanylyl cyclase activity of the ANP receptor without affecting receptor binding itself. Which downstream consequence would be expected?","choices":{"A":"ANP would no longer bind its receptor, since binding would require active guanylyl cyclase","B":"ANP's effects would be rerouted through a compensatory cAMP-based pathway","C":"ANP would still bind its receptor, but the intracellular cGMP rise would be lost","D":"The afferent arteriole would dilate normally, since that effect is independent of cGMP","E":"Aldosterone secretion would increase directly as a result of blocked guanylyl cyclase activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because ANP receptor binding and its intrinsic guanylyl cyclase catalytic function are separable properties, blocking only the catalytic activity leaves ligand binding intact but abolishes the cGMP generation that mediates all of ANP's downstream vascular and tubular effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing receptor binding from downstream catalytic signaling is a recurring theme across guanylyl cyclase-linked and enzyme-linked receptors on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — this same receptor-versus-catalytic-domain distinction is the basis of the cGMP mechanism question in Batch 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with acute decompensated heart failure is given a synthetic BNP analog. Within hours, his urine output increases and his GFR rises modestly. Which combined mechanism most directly explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Efferent arteriolar dilation raising GFR together with stimulation of collecting duct Na+ reabsorption","B":"Afferent dilation raising GFR together with direct inhibition of collecting duct Na+ reabsorption","C":"A pure aldosterone-antagonist effect at the mineralocorticoid receptor with no vascular component","D":"Increased proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchange combined with unchanged glomerular hemodynamics","E":"Direct diuretic action at the loop of Henle identical to a loop diuretic's mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BNP, like ANP, dilates the afferent arteriole (raising GFR) while constricting the efferent arteriole, and directly inhibits Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct, together producing both the increased GFR and the natriuresis/diuresis seen with BNP analog therapy in heart failure.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: synthetic BNP (nesiritide) exploits the same afferent-dilation/efferent-constriction plus tubular mechanism as endogenous ANP, making this a frequently tested pharmacology-physiology bridge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — nesiritide's mechanism mirrors the ANP hemodynamic and tubular actions established in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes an experimental infusion of urodilatin, a peptide secreted by the kidney itself rather than the atria. Which effect would be expected to differ least from ANP's known actions?","choices":{"A":"Its effects on GFR and Na+ reabsorption, since it shares ANP's receptor family","B":"Its site of synthesis, since urodilatin, like ANP, is produced by atrial myocytes","C":"Its second messenger, since urodilatin signals primarily through cAMP","D":"Its effect on the efferent arteriole, since urodilatin causes efferent dilation","E":"Its overall effect on GFR, since urodilatin decreases glomerular filtration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Urodilatin belongs to the same natriuretic peptide family as ANP and BNP and signals through the same cGMP-generating receptor mechanism, producing qualitatively similar afferent dilation, efferent constriction, increased GFR, and inhibited collecting duct Na+ reabsorption, even though it is synthesized locally by the kidney rather than the atria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urodilatin is a useful example of a locally acting (paracrine) member of a hormone family whose downstream signaling mechanism is otherwise identical to its circulating relatives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — urodilatin's shared receptor mechanism reinforces the cGMP signaling pathway tested directly in Batch 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with a confirmed aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma has hypertension and hypokalemia but no peripheral edema on examination. Her physician explains that this absence of edema is expected. Which physiologic principle best accounts for it?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone-secreting adenomas produce no net Na+ retention, since their effects are confined to K+ secretion","B":"Her renal Na+ channels have become permanently desensitized to aldosterone over time","C":"ANP release from ECF expansion has partially offset her aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","D":"Her low serum K+ has independently caused a compensatory diuresis that prevents edema","E":"The adrenal adenoma secretes ANP directly, bypassing the atria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary hyperaldosteronism classically produces hypertension and hypokalemia without significant edema because ANP-mediated mineralocorticoid escape allows Na+ excretion to catch up once ECF volume has expanded enough to normalize EABV, capping further fluid retention well before edema develops.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the classic absence of edema in Conn syndrome (versus its presence in secondary hyperaldosteronism from heart failure or cirrhosis) is explained entirely by this escape mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — this vignette is the direct clinical application of Batch 1's escape-mechanism and edema-comparison questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary hyperaldosteronism is found to have a persistently expanded ECF volume that has plateaued rather than continuing to rise, despite ongoing excess aldosterone secretion. Which sequence of events best explains this plateau?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone secretion spontaneously declines once ECF volume reaches a certain threshold","B":"The adrenal adenoma becomes progressively less sensitive to its own secreted aldosterone","C":"Renal Na+ channels are internalized permanently once a fixed cumulative Na+ load is reached","D":"Aldosterone raises ECF volume until EABV rises enough to trigger ANP release that caps retention","E":"ECF volume expansion directly suppresses adrenal aldosterone synthesis through a local feedback loop"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As aldosterone-driven Na+ retention expands ECF volume, EABV rises correspondingly and is sensed by atrial stretch receptors, triggering ANP release. ANP then partially inhibits collecting duct Na+ reabsorption, creating a new steady state where Na+ intake and excretion are balanced at a higher-than-normal ECF volume, without any change in aldosterone secretion itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the plateau in primary hyperaldosteronism reflects a new equilibrium between unchanged aldosterone secretion and rising ANP-mediated opposition, not any reduction in aldosterone itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — this plateau mechanism is the same escape process introduced conceptually in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism is treated preoperatively with spironolactone before her adrenal tumor is removed. Which change in her mineralocorticoid escape physiology would be expected on this medication?","choices":{"A":"Escape becomes largely unnecessary, since spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor directly","B":"Escape becomes more pronounced, since spironolactone directly stimulates additional ANP secretion","C":"Escape is abolished entirely, since spironolactone blocks ANP receptors along with mineralocorticoid receptors","D":"Escape physiology is unaffected, since spironolactone's relevant action is on potassium handling","E":"Escape reverses direction, causing progressive volume depletion without any plateau"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor directly, spironolactone reduces the excess Na+ reabsorption that made escape physiology necessary in the first place; with aldosterone's effect pharmacologically blunted, ECF volume and EABV move back toward normal without requiring ongoing ANP-mediated opposition.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spironolactone treats primary hyperaldosteronism at the same receptor step where mineralocorticoid escape naturally intervenes downstream, which is why the two mechanisms are often compared on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — spironolactone's mineralocorticoid receptor mechanism is the same one detailed in SDL 14's K+-sparing diuretic objective","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"SDL 16 — Potassium Homeostasis: Internal Trans-Cellular Shifts","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"What maintains the large concentration gradient between intracellular and extracellular K+?","choices":{"A":"Passive diffusion down a gradient established purely by dietary K+ intake","B":"A dedicated K+ pump expressed selectively in excitable tissues such as nerve and muscle","C":"Active transport by Na+-K+ ATPase present in the membrane of virtually any cell","D":"Facilitated diffusion through K+ channels that require no energy expenditure","E":"Selective renal reabsorption that concentrates K+ inside cells directly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Na+-K+ ATPase, present in virtually all cell membranes, actively pumps K+ into cells and Na+ out, maintaining intracellular K+ near 150 mEq/L against extracellular K+ near 4.5 mEq/L despite the constant passive leak that would otherwise dissipate this gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this Na+-K+ ATPase-maintained gradient is the same one that sets resting membrane potential, linking renal/fluid physiology directly to cardiac and neuromuscular excitability questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"Why can a relatively small shift of K+ out of cells produce a disproportionately large rise in blood K+ concentration?","choices":{"A":"Because extracellular K+ is normally near zero, so any efflux at any level produces a large percentage change","B":"Because about 98% of total body K+ is intracellular, a limited efflux is large relative to the smaller ECF pool","C":"Because the kidneys cannot excrete K+ quickly enough to prevent transient accumulation","D":"Because Na+-K+ ATPase reverses direction during any efflux, actively pumping additional K+ outward","E":"Because extracellular fluid has a much smaller volume of distribution than the intracellular compartment for most solutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With about 98% of total body K+ inside cells and only about 2% in the extracellular fluid, even a modest percentage shift out of the large intracellular pool represents a large absolute amount of K+ added to the comparatively small extracellular volume, producing a disproportionate rise in measured plasma K+.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this disproportion is why cell lysis syndromes (rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis) can cause severe, rapid hyperkalemia even when only a fraction of total body K+ is released.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"How does an acute rise in extracellular K+ concentration affect the resting membrane potential of excitable cells?","choices":{"A":"It depolarizes the resting membrane potential, moving it closer to threshold","B":"It hyperpolarizes the resting membrane potential, moving it further from threshold","C":"It has no effect on resting membrane potential, but does alter the action potential upstroke","D":"It shifts the resting membrane potential toward the Na+ equilibrium potential","E":"It abolishes the resting membrane potential entirely regardless of magnitude"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because resting membrane potential is set largely by the K+ concentration gradient, raising extracellular K+ reduces that gradient and depolarizes the resting membrane potential, altering the excitability of nerve, skeletal muscle, and cardiac tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia depolarizes resting membrane potential toward threshold, which paradoxically can inactivate Na+ channels and cause conduction abnormalities at severe levels — a classic ECG/physiology integration point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does insulin promote K+ uptake into cells?","choices":{"A":"It opens dedicated K+ channels that allow passive K+ entry down its gradient","B":"It stimulates a separate K+-glucose cotransporter distinct from Na+-K+ ATPase","C":"It increases cellular ATP production, indirectly and nonselectively raising K+ entry","D":"It increases the activity of Na+-K+ ATPase, moving more K+ across the membrane","E":"It inhibits K+ efflux channels without affecting any influx pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Insulin increases Na+-K+ ATPase activity, which actively drives more K+ into cells; this action, distinct from insulin's glucose-uptake effect, is what allows dietary K+ from a meal to be taken up into cells rather than remaining in the ECF and causing hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: IV insulin (with glucose to prevent hypoglycemia) is a first-line emergency treatment for hyperkalemia precisely because of this Na+-K+ ATPase-stimulating effect, independent of its metabolic actions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"What direction of K+ shift results from beta-2 adrenergic receptor activation?","choices":{"A":"Out of cells, producing a tendency toward hyperkalemia","B":"No net shift, since beta-2 receptors act mainly on vascular smooth muscle","C":"Into cells predominantly in cardiac tissue, with little effect in other organs","D":"Out of cells transiently, followed by a larger rebound shift inward","E":"Into cells, producing a tendency toward hypokalemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Beta-2 adrenergic agonists such as albuterol increase Na+-K+ ATPase activity, shifting K+ into cells and lowering plasma K+, which is why beta-2 agonists are used as an adjunct emergency treatment for hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta-2 agonists (K+ into cells) and beta-2 antagonists like propranolol (K+ out of cells) have opposite effects on K+ distribution — a frequently tested pharmacology pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"What direction of K+ shift results from alpha-adrenergic receptor activation?","choices":{"A":"Out of cells, producing a tendency toward hyperkalemia","B":"Into cells, producing a tendency toward hypokalemia","C":"No net shift, since alpha receptors regulate mainly vascular tone","D":"Out of cells predominantly in skeletal muscle, sparing cardiac and hepatic tissue","E":"Into cells transiently, followed by a larger rebound shift outward"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha-adrenergic receptor activation causes K+ to shift out of cells and can produce hyperkalemia, the opposite direction from beta-2 receptor activation, giving catecholamines a dual and receptor-specific effect on K+ distribution.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: alpha-adrenergic agonists shift K+ out of cells while alpha-adrenergic antagonists shift K+ into cells — the mirror image of the beta-2 agonist/antagonist pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"What is the physiologic rationale for H+-K+ exchange across cell membranes during acid-base disturbances?","choices":{"A":"It allows K+ to buffer H+ directly through a chemical acid-base reaction","B":"It is a byproduct of renal ammonia synthesis, with no role in cellular buffering","C":"It occurs to protect Na+-K+ ATPase from being inhibited by pH changes","D":"It moves H+ into cells to use buffering while preserving electroneutrality","E":"It is driven primarily by changes in cell volume, independent of charge balance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because H+ cannot cross cell membranes alone without disrupting electroneutrality, it is exchanged for another cation, typically K+, allowing H+ to reach the substantial buffering capacity inside cells while keeping the membrane electrically balanced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this electroneutrality requirement is the mechanistic reason acidemia and alkalemia predictably shift K+, and understanding it predicts which acid-base disorders will and will not cause a K+ shift.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"What K+ shift is expected in acidemia caused by excess inorganic mineral acid?","choices":{"A":"H+ leaves cells and K+ enters cells, producing hypokalemia","B":"H+ and K+ both leave cells simultaneously, producing hyperkalemia through two independent mechanisms","C":"H+ enters cells and K+ leaves cells, producing hyperkalemia","D":"Neither ion shifts, since inorganic acids do not require cellular buffering","E":"H+ enters cells while Na+, not K+, leaves to preserve electroneutrality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In acidemia from excess inorganic acid, elevated blood H+ drives H+ into cells in exchange for K+ leaving, producing hyperkalemia; this is the classic, predictable direction of the H+-K+ exchange during mineral (inorganic) acidosis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this predictable hyperkalemia is specifically for inorganic/mineral acidosis — recognizing when this rule does NOT apply (organic acidoses, respiratory disorders) is the higher-yield distinction tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"Why does respiratory acidosis typically fail to produce the same K+ shift seen in mineral metabolic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory acidosis does not meaningfully lower intracellular pH, so no buffering is required","B":"CO2 is lipid-soluble and crosses membranes freely, needing no exchange for K+","C":"The lungs compensate for respiratory acidosis too quickly for any K+ shift to develop","D":"CO2 is charged and requires an exchange with Na+ across the cell membrane","E":"Respiratory acidosis selectively affects extracellular buffering systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because CO2 is lipid-soluble, it diffuses freely across cell membranes without carrying a charge that would require a compensatory K+ exchange, so respiratory acid-base disturbances typically do not shift K+ across cell membranes the way mineral acid-driven metabolic disturbances do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this CO2-permeability exception, along with the organic-acid exception (lactic acidosis, DKA, salicylate poisoning), is exactly the kind of \"which acid-base disorder does NOT shift K+\" question boards favor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is extracted from a collapsed building after being trapped for 18 hours and is found to have dark urine and markedly elevated creatine kinase. Shortly after extrication, his serum K+ rises sharply. Which mechanism most directly explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"The prolonged immobilization by itself raised his insulin sensitivity, paradoxically shifting K+ out of cells","B":"His injury triggered a beta-2 adrenergic surge that shifted K+ out of cells over several hours","C":"Dehydration from the entrapment concentrated his existing extracellular K+ without any new release","D":"Crush injury caused widespread muscle cell lysis, releasing large amounts of K+ into the extracellular fluid","E":"His kidneys became acutely unable to excrete K+, causing accumulation from ordinary dietary intake"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crush injury causes rhabdomyolysis, in which extensive skeletal muscle cell membrane breakdown releases the large intracellular K+ pool directly into the extracellular fluid, producing rapid and often severe hyperkalemia independent of any change in renal excretion or hormonal signaling.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: crush syndrome hyperkalemia is a classic cell-lysis mechanism, mirroring tumor lysis syndrome and severe burns, and is an emergency requiring immediate K+-lowering treatment even before renal injury from myoglobin becomes apparent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — this same cell-lysis mechanism recurs in SDL 17's discussion of renal K+ handling during acute K+ loads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old previously healthy man completes an extremely intense sprint workout and has a mildly elevated serum K+ immediately afterward, which resolves within 15 minutes of rest. Which mechanism accounts for this transient rise?","choices":{"A":"Exercise directly inhibits Na+-K+ ATPase throughout the entire body for the duration of activity","B":"Lactic acid produced during the sprint caused a sustained organic acidosis that shifted K+ out of cells","C":"His skeletal muscle underwent mild rhabdomyolysis, releasing a small amount of intracellular K+","D":"Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic activation during exercise persists for hours after the activity ends","E":"ATP depletion in exercising muscle opens K+ channels, letting K+ move down its gradient out of the cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During intense exercise, cellular ATP depletion opens K+ channels in the muscle membrane, allowing K+ to leak out down its electrochemical gradient; this shift is usually small and self-limited, reversing quickly once ATP stores are replenished during rest, and does not require any cell injury or acid-base disturbance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: exercise-induced hyperkalemia is typically mild and rapidly reversible, unlike the exercise-associated hyperkalemia seen in patients on beta-blockers or with renal impairment, where the shift can become dangerous.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — this same exercise-released K+ also serves as a local vasodilator in exercising muscle, connecting to blood flow regulation covered in cardiovascular physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with severe hyperglycemia and a measured serum osmolarity well above normal, along with a serum K+ at the upper limit of normal despite clinically significant total-body K+ depletion from osmotic diuresis. Which mechanism explains why his measured K+ does not yet reflect his true deficit?","choices":{"A":"His pancreatic beta cells are secreting excess insulin, which is independently raising his measured K+","B":"His kidneys are actively secreting K+ into the tubular lumen faster than it can be measured in the blood","C":"Hyperosmolarity pulls water out of cells, dragging intracellular K+ out into the extracellular fluid with it","D":"The osmotic diuresis has concentrated his extracellular fluid without any actual K+ movement across cell membranes","E":"His acid-base status has shifted toward alkalemia, a change that would be expected to raise his measured K+"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe hyperosmolarity causes water to move from the intracellular to extracellular compartment along the osmotic gradient; as water leaves cells, intracellular K+ concentration rises and K+ is \"dragged\" out with the water flow into the ECF, temporarily masking an underlying total-body K+ deficit that will become apparent once the hyperosmolar state and osmotic diuresis are corrected.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this osmotic K+-shift phenomenon is why a \"normal\" or even high measured K+ in severe hyperglycemia/DKA can be dangerously misleading — total body K+ is typically depleted and will fall further with insulin treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — this same hyperosmolar mechanism is directly relevant to the insulin-treatment K+ shift tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus presents in diabetic ketoacidosis with a serum K+ of 6.2 mEq/L. His physician begins an insulin infusion. Which mechanism explains why his serum K+ is expected to fall over the following hours, even before his total body K+ deficit is corrected?","choices":{"A":"Insulin directly resolves his underlying organic acidosis via the H+-K+ exchange system","B":"Insulin stimulates Na+-K+ ATPase and moves K+ into cells independent of total body K+","C":"Insulin acutely increases renal K+ excretion and rapidly lowers his total body K+ content","D":"Insulin stimulates aldosterone release and rapidly increases distal nephron K+ secretion","E":"Insulin acutely expands plasma volume and dilutes his extracellular K+ concentration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Insulin's Na+-K+ ATPase-stimulating effect shifts K+ from the extracellular into the intracellular compartment directly, independent of any change in total body K+ content, renal excretion, or acid-base status, which is why serum K+ falls rapidly with insulin therapy even though the patient's underlying total body K+ deficit (from osmotic diuresis) has not yet been replaced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why insulin-treated DKA patients require careful K+ monitoring and supplementation — the transcellular shift can produce dangerous hypokalemia once the initially elevated (but often falsely reassuring) serum K+ falls.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — this insulin mechanism directly follows from Batch 1's Obj 2 question on Na+-K+ ATPase stimulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man on chronic propranolol for migraine prophylaxis performs unusually strenuous yard work on a hot afternoon and develops muscle weakness with a serum K+ of 6.8 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains why his hyperkalemia is more severe than would be expected from exercise alone?","choices":{"A":"Propranolol directly damages skeletal muscle cell membranes, independent of any receptor-level effect","B":"Propranolol increases renal K+ reabsorption acutely, adding to the exercise-induced efflux","C":"Propranolol blocks alpha-adrenergic receptors, which would otherwise have shifted K+ into cells","D":"Beta-2 blockade blocks the adrenergic K+-lowering response, leaving the K+ efflux unopposed","E":"Propranolol stimulates aldosterone release, further impairing renal K+ excretion during exercise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exercise normally triggers some counterbalancing beta-2 adrenergic activity that helps shift K+ back into cells; a nonselective beta-blocker like propranolol blocks this protective beta-2-mediated uptake, leaving the exercise-induced K+ efflux unopposed and producing more pronounced hyperkalemia than would occur without beta-blockade.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: nonselective beta-blockers plus strenuous exercise (or renal impairment) is a classic combination for clinically significant hyperkalemia, distinct from the usual mild, self-limited exercise-induced K+ rise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — this beta-2 blockade mechanism is the direct pharmacologic counterpart to Batch 1's beta-2 agonist question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man in hemorrhagic shock has markedly elevated circulating catecholamines, with predominant alpha-adrenergic activation as part of the compensatory response. Which K+ change would be expected from this adrenergic state alone, independent of any acid-base or renal effect?","choices":{"A":"A shift of K+ out of cells, tending to raise serum K+","B":"A shift of K+ into cells, tending to lower serum K+","C":"No K+ shift, since alpha-adrenergic receptors do not influence Na+-K+ ATPase directly","D":"A biphasic shift, first into cells and then out, canceling to a net-zero change","E":"A shift restricted mainly to cardiac myocytes, sparing skeletal muscle and hepatocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Predominant alpha-adrenergic activation, as occurs during the catecholamine surge of hemorrhagic shock, shifts K+ out of cells and tends to raise serum K+, an effect that can add to any concurrent hyperkalemia from tissue hypoperfusion or acidosis in severe shock states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this alpha-adrenergic K+-out effect is worth pairing mentally with beta-2's opposite K+-in effect, since catecholamine states often activate both receptor types simultaneously with competing net effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — this alpha-adrenergic mechanism completes the receptor-pairing framework introduced in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with severe salicylate poisoning has a significant metabolic acidosis but her serum K+ remains within the normal range. Which explanation best accounts for the absence of the hyperkalemia that might otherwise be expected with this degree of acidemia?","choices":{"A":"Salicylate poisoning does not actually lower blood pH despite laboratory measurements suggesting acidosis","B":"Her kidneys are excreting K+ at an accelerated rate that exactly offsets any cellular efflux","C":"Salicylate is an organic anion that enters cells with H+, preserving electroneutrality without K+ efflux","D":"Salicylate directly stimulates Na+-K+ ATPase, actively counteracting the expected K+ shift","E":"Organic acidoses typically produce hypokalemia through a different, non-electroneutrality mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because salicylate is an organic acid whose anion can accompany H+ into cells, electroneutrality is preserved without an exchange for K+, which is why organic metabolic acidoses like salicylate poisoning, lactic acidosis, and diabetic ketoacidosis often do not produce the hyperkalemia expected from an inorganic mineral acidosis of similar severity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: recognizing organic acidoses as an exception to the \"acidosis causes hyperkalemia\" rule is one of the most frequently tested nuances in acid-base/potassium physiology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — this organic-anion exception is the same principle tested directly in Batch 1's inorganic-acidosis and respiratory-acidosis questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man hyperventilates during a panic attack, producing acute respiratory alkalosis. His physician reassures him that clinically significant hypokalemia from this event alone would be unlikely. What is the physiologic basis for this reassurance?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory alkalosis produces far less intracellular pH shift than metabolic alkalosis does","B":"CO2 diffuses freely across cell membranes without needing to exchange with K+","C":"Panic-attack hyperventilation is too brief to allow any meaningful H+-K+ exchange","D":"Sympathetic activation during panic attacks reliably shifts enough K+ into cells to prevent any net shift","E":"CO2 is a charged molecule that requires exchange with Na+ to cross cell membranes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Respiratory alkalosis results from a primary change in CO2, a lipid-soluble gas that diffuses freely across cell membranes without needing to exchange with K+ to preserve electroneutrality, so respiratory acid-base disturbances typically produce little to no K+ shift compared with metabolic disturbances of similar pH magnitude.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this CO2-permeability principle applies symmetrically to both respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis, distinguishing both from their metabolic counterparts on K+-shift questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — this respiratory-disorder exception mirrors the respiratory acidosis mechanism established in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with chronic kidney disease develops a mineral (non-organic) metabolic acidosis from progressive renal failure, with a serum K+ that rises in parallel with his falling serum bicarbonate. Which sequence of events best explains this parallel change?","choices":{"A":"Rising H+ enters cells in exchange for K+ leaving, raising extracellular K+ as bicarbonate falls","B":"Falling bicarbonate directly stimulates renal K+ secretion, which is the sole cause of his rising serum K+","C":"His failing kidneys are unable to reabsorb K+, so excess dietary K+ remains unabsorbed in the gut","D":"Rising H+ stimulates aldosterone release, which is the primary driver of his observed hyperkalemia","E":"His acidosis has increased his glomerular filtration rate, paradoxically increasing K+ retention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In mineral (inorganic) metabolic acidosis, such as that from advanced renal failure with reduced acid excretion, rising extracellular H+ enters cells in direct exchange for K+, which leaves cells to preserve electroneutrality; this transcellular shift raises extracellular K+ in parallel with the falling bicarbonate, compounding whatever reduction in renal K+ excretion is also present from the kidney disease itself.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic kidney disease patients are especially vulnerable to acidosis-driven hyperkalemia because both the transcellular H+-K+ shift and reduced renal K+ excretion push serum K+ upward simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — the reduced renal excretion component of this vignette previews the external K+ balance mechanisms covered in SDL 17","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"SDL 17 — Potassium Homeostasis: External K+ Balance & Secretory Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of the filtered K+ load is reabsorbed by the proximal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"About 20%, reabsorbed actively via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter","B":"About 90%, reabsorbed through a dedicated apical K+-H+ exchanger","C":"About 67%, reabsorbed as part of isosmotic fluid reabsorption","D":"About 40%, reabsorbed by facilitated diffusion driven by insulin","E":"About 10%, reabsorbed passively behind an osmotic urea gradient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs roughly 67% of the filtered K+ load as an incidental part of isosmotic fluid and solute reabsorption, not through a K+-specific regulated transporter; the thick ascending limb accounts for another 20% via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, leaving the distal nephron to fine-tune the remainder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the K+ handling percentages (67% PCT, 20% TAL, then variable distal reabsorption or secretion) the same way you memorize Na+ and water handling by segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does the thick ascending limb reabsorb roughly 20% of the filtered K+ load?","choices":{"A":"K+ enters via a basolateral H+-K+ ATPase and exits through apical anion channels into the lumen","B":"K+ enters via the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter and exits basolateral K+ channels","C":"K+ enters via paracellular diffusion driven by a lumen-negative transepithelial potential","D":"K+ enters via facilitated diffusion through channels gated by ambient tubular flow rate","E":"K+ enters via active cotransport with glucose across the apical brush border membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"K+ enters thick ascending limb cells across the apical membrane via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) and can then either recycle back into the lumen through apical K+ channels, which generates the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular cation reabsorption, or exit across the basolateral membrane through K+ channels to be reabsorbed into blood.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this same NKCC2 transporter is the target of loop diuretics, which is why loop diuretics block TAL K+ reabsorption in addition to increasing distal K+ secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"Under a chronic low-K+ diet, which mechanism allows the kidney to further conserve K+ beyond fixed proximal and TAL reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Principal cells switch from secreting to reabsorbing K+ using the same apical ROMK channels","B":"The thick ascending limb doubles its Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport activity to reabsorb more K+","C":"The proximal tubule upregulates a dedicated basolateral K+ reabsorptive channel","D":"Alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ via an apical H+-K+ ATPase pumping K+ into the cell","E":"The collecting duct principal cells reverse their basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase to pull K+ inward"},"correct":"D","explanation":"On a low-K+ diet, alpha-intercalated cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct express an apical H+-K+ ATPase, similar to the one in gastric parietal cells, that actively pumps H+ into the lumen while pumping K+ from the lumen into the cell; the K+ then diffuses into blood, allowing urinary K+ excretion to fall as low as 1% of the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ and secrete H+ using the same H+-K+ ATPase, making this a favorite integration point between K+ balance and acid-base physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"What is the immediate source of the K+ that principal cells secrete into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"K+ filtered directly across the apical membrane from the tubular lumen","B":"K+ released from intracellular buffering stores during acidosis","C":"K+ absorbed from dietary intake through a dedicated luminal transporter","D":"K+ generated locally by cellular metabolism of ATP within the cell","E":"K+ pumped in from the blood by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Principal cells secrete K+ that was first pumped in from the blood by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, which maintains a high intracellular K+ concentration; this K+ then diffuses down its electrochemical gradient into the lumen through apical K+ channels rather than being recycled back across the basolateral membrane.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the same basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase used for Na+ reabsorption in principal cells, tying K+ secretion mechanistically to Na+ reabsorption and aldosterone action.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"What quantity most directly determines the magnitude of K+ secretion by principal cells at any given moment?","choices":{"A":"The K+ electrochemical gradient across the apical membrane","B":"The absolute plasma aldosterone concentration measured at that moment","C":"The total number of basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase pumps expressed by the cell","D":"The rate of glomerular filtration delivered to the distal nephron","E":"The plasma pH measured simultaneously in the systemic circulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aldosterone, dietary K+, acid-base status, and tubular flow rate largely regulate K+ secretion by changing the electrochemical gradient for K+ across the apical membrane (together with the availability of apical K+ channels); that gradient, not any single hormone or channel count in isolation, is the most direct determinant of secretion rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: frame every K+ secretion question around this one governing principle, then work out whether a given factor raises or lowers the apical K+ gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"How does an increase in tubular flow rate through the collecting duct raise K+ secretion?","choices":{"A":"Faster flow directly stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex","B":"Faster flow dilutes luminal K+ and steepens the apical K+ gradient","C":"Faster flow increases basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase expression within minutes","D":"Faster flow raises intracellular pH, indirectly opening more ROMK channels","E":"Faster flow increases paracellular Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When tubular flow rate increases, the secreted K+ is washed downstream faster, keeping luminal K+ concentration low; this widens the electrochemical gradient between the K+-rich cell and the diluted lumen, increasing the driving force for K+ exit through apical channels, which is why diuretics that raise distal flow also raise K+ excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this flow-dependence explains why any diuretic that raises distal flow (loop, thiazide, osmotic) tends to increase kaliuresis through this shared flow effect, whereas K+-sparing diuretics do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"How does intracellular magnesium regulate luminal ROMK channels in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Intracellular Mg2+ blocks ROMK, so Mg2+ depletion opens the channel","B":"Intracellular Mg2+ activates ROMK, so Mg2+ depletion closes the channel","C":"Intracellular Mg2+ regulates ROMK gene transcription over several days","D":"Intracellular Mg2+ competes with K+ for the same binding site on ROMK","E":"Intracellular Mg2+ has no direct effect on ROMK, acting through aldosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intracellular Mg2+ acts as a channel blocker on the luminal ROMK channel of principal cells, so a normal intracellular Mg2+ concentration keeps some channels closed; when intracellular Mg2+ is depleted, this inhibitory block is lost, ROMK channels open more readily, and K+ secretion rises.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this direct channel-blocking mechanism, not a genomic or hormonal one, is the specific detail boards test to distinguish Mg2+ from aldosterone-type regulators of K+ secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 3 — how intracellular Mg2+ gates ROMK channels and why hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"Why does hypomagnesemia tend to produce hypokalemia through the principal cell?","choices":{"A":"Reduced intracellular Mg2+ increases basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase activity directly","B":"Reduced intracellular Mg2+ stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal gland","C":"Reduced intracellular Mg2+ increases distal tubular flow rate through osmotic diuresis","D":"Reduced intracellular Mg2+ removes its ROMK block and increases K+ secretion","E":"Reduced intracellular Mg2+ activates the H+-K+ ATPase of alpha-intercalated cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because intracellular Mg2+ normally blocks luminal ROMK channels, hypomagnesemia removes that block, leaving more ROMK channels open and increasing K+ efflux into the lumen; this hypokalemia is a direct channel effect rather than a change in aldosterone or tubular flow.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: recognizing this as a direct channel effect rather than a hormonal one is the key distinction for predicting when Mg2+ repletion, not just K+ repletion, is required.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 3 — how intracellular Mg2+ gates ROMK channels and why hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"Why is magnesium repletion often necessary to correct hypokalemia caused by concurrent hypomagnesemia?","choices":{"A":"Oral potassium is poorly absorbed from the gut whenever magnesium is deficient","B":"Magnesium deficiency accelerates renal clearance of supplemented potassium directly","C":"Continued Mg2+ deficiency keeps ROMK unblocked despite ongoing K+ supplementation","D":"Magnesium deficiency shifts supplemented potassium into cells before it can act","E":"Magnesium deficiency blunts the intestinal signal that normally triggers insulin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Supplemented K+ raises plasma K+ only briefly if the underlying ROMK channel block from Mg2+ remains lost; without that block restored, principal cells continue secreting K+ at an elevated rate, so plasma K+ falls again unless the Mg2+ deficiency itself is corrected.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the mechanistic basis for the classic teaching that refractory hypokalemia should prompt a serum magnesium check before simply increasing the potassium dose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 3 — how intracellular Mg2+ gates ROMK channels and why hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman adheres to a strict low-potassium renal diet for months. Her 24-hour urine K+ excretion is measured at only 1% of her filtered K+ load. Which nephron segment accounts for this additional conservation beyond the fixed proximal and loop reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Principal cells in the late distal tubule, via reversal of their basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase","B":"The proximal convoluted tubule, via an inducible basolateral K+ reabsorptive channel","C":"The thick ascending limb, via a threefold increase in Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter density","D":"Alpha-intercalated cells in the late distal segments, via an apical H+-K+ ATPase","E":"The macula densa, via tubuloglomerular feedback that reduces filtered K+ delivery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Proximal and thick ascending limb K+ reabsorption stay essentially fixed regardless of dietary intake, so the additional conservation seen on a chronic low-K+ diet comes from the late distal tubule and collecting duct, where alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ through an apical H+-K+ ATPase, allowing urinary excretion to fall to as little as 1% of the filtered load.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this vignette is the clinical counterpart of Batch 1's Obj 1 question on the same H+-K+ ATPase mechanism, now applied to an extreme dietary conservation scenario.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — this same alpha-intercalated cell mechanism runs in parallel with the H+ secretion covered in acid-base balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man doubles his dietary potassium intake for two weeks as part of a nutrition experiment. Which statement correctly describes how his proximal tubule and thick ascending limb respond to this change?","choices":{"A":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption stays fixed while the distal nephron excretes the extra load","B":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption falls sharply to compensate for the added dietary K+ load","C":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption rises sharply to buffer the added dietary K+ load","D":"The thick ascending limb switches from reabsorbing to secreting K+ during the high-intake period","E":"The proximal tubule begins actively secreting K+ once dietary intake exceeds baseline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The proximal tubule and thick ascending limb reabsorb a relatively constant fraction of filtered K+ regardless of dietary intake, since their K+ handling is incidental to bulk fluid and NaCl transport rather than being independently regulated; all of the physiological adjustment to a higher K+ diet occurs in the distal tubule and collecting duct through increased principal cell secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this fixed-versus-variable segmental split is exactly why exam questions about dietary K+ adaptation always point to the distal nephron, never the proximal tubule or loop.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — this fixed-versus-variable segmental split is the same framework tested in Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is started on a loop diuretic and subsequently develops hypokalemia. Beyond the increased distal Na+ delivery that raises principal cell K+ secretion, which additional mechanism specific to the thick ascending limb contributes to his K+ loss?","choices":{"A":"The loop diuretic stimulates the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase to secrete additional K+","B":"The loop diuretic increases proximal tubular K+ secretion through an osmotic diuresis effect","C":"The loop diuretic directly opens ROMK channels in the thick ascending limb basolateral membrane","D":"The loop diuretic raises circulating insulin, shifting K+ from the ECF into distal tubular cells","E":"The loop diuretic blocks the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, inhibiting K+ reabsorption there"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick ascending limb, which both blocks the roughly 20% of filtered K+ that this segment normally reabsorbs and increases distal Na+ delivery to principal cells; both effects add together to produce the pronounced kaliuresis and hypokalemia seen with loop diuretic therapy.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this dual mechanism with K+-sparing diuretics, which act only on principal cells and therefore do not cause kaliuresis at all.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — this loop-diuretic mechanism connects directly to the diuretic-induced kaliuresis covered in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma has hypertension, plasma K+ of 2.1 mEq/L, and inappropriately high urinary K+ excretion. Which mechanism best explains why elevated aldosterone lowers his plasma K+?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone acts on alpha-intercalated cells to secrete K+ while leaving principal cells unchanged","B":"Aldosterone increases glomerular filtration of K+, raising the amount delivered to the distal nephron","C":"Aldosterone increases Na+-K+ ATPase activity, raising intracellular K+ and its secretion","D":"Aldosterone inhibits the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase, blocking compensatory K+ reabsorption","E":"Aldosterone lowers tubular flow rate, which paradoxically increases the luminal K+ gradient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of luminal Na+ channels and basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase in principal cells; increased Na+ entry and pumping raises intracellular K+ concentration, which increases the driving force for K+ secretion into the lumen, while aldosterone also separately increases the number of luminal K+ channels available to carry that secreted K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) is a classic vignette pairing hypertension from Na+ retention with hypokalemia from this same principal cell mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — this Na+ reabsorption/K+ secretion coupling is the same principal cell mechanism tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman on chronic thiazide therapy for hypertension develops a plasma K+ of 3.0 mEq/L. Which mechanism most directly explains how a diuretic acting on the distal convoluted tubule produces this hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Blocking NCC directly increases the number of luminal ROMK channels in principal cells","B":"Blocking NCC delivers more Na+ to principal cells, raising K+ secretion","C":"Blocking NCC raises plasma aldosterone independent of any change in Na+ delivery","D":"Blocking NCC reduces proximal tubular K+ reabsorption through a compensatory reflex","E":"Blocking NCC increases plasma pH, indirectly opening more luminal K+ channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazides inhibit the Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) in the early distal tubule, which is upstream of the principal cells; the resulting increase in Na+ delivery to principal cells raises Na+ entry and basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase activity, increasing intracellular K+ and the driving force for its secretion, producing the same downstream effect as the increased Na+ delivery seen with loop diuretics.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the same increased-Na+-delivery principle taught for high-Na+-diet kaliuresis, applied here to explain a common outpatient hypokalemia from thiazide therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — this increased-distal-Na+-delivery mechanism mirrors the loop-diuretic mechanism of Objective 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with protracted vomiting develops metabolic alkalosis and a plasma K+ of 2.8 mEq/L. Which mechanism links her alkalemia to this degree of hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Falling extracellular H+ directly inhibits the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase, reducing K+ reabsorption","B":"Falling extracellular H+ increases glomerular filtration of K+ by dilating afferent arterioles","C":"Falling extracellular H+ suppresses aldosterone release, paradoxically increasing K+ secretion","D":"Falling extracellular H+ drives H+ out of cells in exchange for K+, raising K+ secretion","E":"Falling extracellular H+ raises tubular flow rate independent of any volume contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alkalemia, the deficit of extracellular H+ drives H+ out of cells to help buffer the blood, and K+ enters cells in exchange to preserve electroneutrality; this raises intracellular K+ concentration in principal cells specifically, increasing the driving force for K+ secretion into the lumen and producing hypokalemia on top of any volume-contraction-driven aldosterone effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vomiting classically produces both volume contraction (raising aldosterone) and alkalemia (raising the H+-K+ exchange drive), so its hypokalemia is typically explained by two additive mechanisms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — this H+-K+ exchange across the principal cell basolateral membrane is the acid-base counterpart to Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man on chronic loop diuretic therapy has persistent hypokalemia that fails to improve despite two weeks of oral potassium supplementation. His plasma magnesium is found to be low. Which mechanism best explains why his hypokalemia has been refractory to potassium alone?","choices":{"A":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ increases intestinal potassium excretion, offsetting his oral supplementation","B":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ directly raises plasma aldosterone, worsening his ongoing K+ loss","C":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ leaves ROMK channels unblocked, so K+ secretion continues unabated","D":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ increases renal clearance of the supplemented potassium within hours","E":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ shifts the supplemented potassium into cells before it reaches the plasma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because intracellular Mg2+ normally blocks luminal ROMK channels, his ongoing Mg2+ deficiency leaves these channels open regardless of how much potassium he ingests, so principal cells keep secreting K+ at an elevated rate; his hypokalemia will remain refractory until his magnesium deficit is corrected alongside continued potassium supplementation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this same refractory-hypokalemia mechanism is why the Mg-ROMK link is tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — this same refractory-hypokalemia mechanism is why the Mg-ROMK link is tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man receiving cisplatin chemotherapy develops renal magnesium wasting and subsequent hypokalemia that does not respond to potassium chloride tablets alone. Which single intervention is most likely to correct his plasma potassium?","choices":{"A":"Increasing his oral potassium chloride dose substantially","B":"Repleting his Mg2+ deficit to restore the ROMK channel block","C":"Starting intravenous aldosterone-receptor antagonism empirically","D":"Discontinuing his other diuretic-like medications","E":"Giving intravenous insulin together with dextrose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cisplatin causes renal magnesium wasting, and the resulting intracellular Mg2+ deficiency removes the normal channel block on luminal ROMK, driving ongoing K+ secretion; because potassium loss will continue regardless of oral potassium dosing, only correcting the underlying magnesium deficiency restores the ROMK block and allows plasma potassium to normalize and stay normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cisplatin-induced renal Mg wasting is a frequently tested cause of refractory hypokalemia, distinct from the aldosterone- or flow-driven causes it superficially resembles.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — this cisplatin-induced Mg wasting is a classic clinical trigger for the same ROMK-gating mechanism introduced in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and poor oral intake presents with muscle weakness and a plasma K+ of 2.7 mEq/L that improves only transiently with intravenous potassium. His plasma magnesium is also low. Which explanation accounts for the transient response to potassium alone?","choices":{"A":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency keeps ROMK channels open, so the kidney keeps secreting the infused potassium","B":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency accelerates gastrointestinal loss of the infused potassium directly","C":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency increases insulin secretion, shifting the infused potassium into cells","D":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency raises plasma aldosterone, which independently worsens his ongoing potassium loss","E":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency reduces hepatic potassium storage, releasing it slowly back into plasma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic alcohol use commonly causes combined magnesium and potassium depletion through poor intake and renal wasting; as long as intracellular Mg2+ remains low, the ROMK channel block stays lost and principal cells continue secreting whatever potassium is infused, so plasma K+ rises only briefly unless magnesium is repleted alongside the potassium.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: combined hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia in a patient with alcohol use disorder is a classic exam pairing that tests whether the ROMK-gating mechanism is understood, not just memorized as a lab association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — this alcohol-use-disorder vignette combines the dietary depletion and Mg-ROMK mechanisms covered throughout this SDL","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"SDL 18 — Renal Handling of Calcium, Phosphate, & Magnesium","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of the filtered Ca2+ load is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"About 25%, matching the fraction reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb","B":"About 8%, matching the fraction reabsorbed in the distal tubule","C":"About 99%, matching the fraction reabsorbed across the whole nephron","D":"About 40%, matching the fraction of Ca2+ that is protein-bound","E":"About 67%, matching the fraction of Na+ reabsorbed there"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ca2+ reabsorption in the proximal tubule parallels Na+ reabsorption almost exactly, with about 67% of the filtered load reabsorbed passively, tightly coupled to Na+ transport; the thick ascending limb accounts for another 25%, and the distal tubule, where regulation actually occurs, reabsorbs only about 8%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize Ca2+ handling by segment the same way as Na+ handling, since the two ions are coupled everywhere except the distal tubule.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"A dedicated basolateral Ca2+-ATPase pump unique to the thick ascending limb","B":"Direct cotransport of Ca2+ with Na+ through the apical NKCC2 transporter","C":"A steep transcellular Ca2+ concentration gradient maintained by calbindin","D":"The lumen-positive potential generated by the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter","E":"PTH-stimulated active transport identical to that of the distal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb generates a lumen-positive transepithelial potential; because positive charge repels positive charge, this potential drives divalent cations such as Ca2+ (and Mg2+) through the paracellular pathway, not through any dedicated transcellular pump.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this shared lumen-positive driving force is exactly why loop diuretics, which abolish this potential, increase both Ca2+ and Mg2+ excretion together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What makes Ca2+ reabsorption in the distal tubule different from the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"It becomes entirely paracellular and remains tightly coupled to Na+ reabsorption","B":"It stops almost completely, since the distal tubule reabsorbs very little Ca2+","C":"It becomes actively transcellular and uncoupled from Na+ reabsorption","D":"It becomes coupled to phosphate reabsorption within the same nephron segment","E":"It becomes regulated primarily by aldosterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb, where Ca2+ reabsorption is passive and parallels Na+ reabsorption, the distal tubule reabsorbs Ca2+ through an active, transcellular, PTH-regulated pathway that is uncoupled from Na+ handling; this uncoupling is why thiazide diuretics increase Ca2+ reabsorption while decreasing Na+ reabsorption in the same segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the distal tubule is the one nephron segment where Ca2+ and Na+ reabsorption move in opposite directions under thiazide therapy — a frequently tested exception.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the majority of filtered phosphate get reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"The thick ascending limb, via a basolateral phosphate-chloride exchanger","B":"The distal tubule, via a PTH-independent active transport mechanism","C":"The collecting duct, via facilitated diffusion driven by ADH","D":"The proximal tubule, via an apical Na+-phosphate cotransporter","E":"The loop of Henle, via the same NKCC2 transporter used for Na+ reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 85% of the filtered phosphate load is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (convoluted and straight segments combined) via an apical Na+-phosphate cotransporter; little to no reabsorption occurs beyond the proximal tubule, which is why PTH acting there is sufficient to control urinary phosphate excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: because reabsorption beyond the proximal tubule is negligible, any phosphate PTH prevents from being reabsorbed there is excreted, with no downstream segment able to compensate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"Why does proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption show a Tm-limited pattern, similar to glucose?","choices":{"A":"Because it is filtered at a fixed rate regardless of the plasma phosphate concentration","B":"Because it is carried by a saturable Na+-phosphate cotransporter with finite capacity","C":"Because it depends entirely on the glomerular filtration rate and not on carrier-mediated transport","D":"Because it is reabsorbed once plasma phosphate exceeds a threshold value","E":"Because it is cotransported with glucose through the same luminal carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Like glucose, phosphate reabsorption depends on a specific carrier protein, the Na+-phosphate cotransporter, whose finite number of binding sites can become saturated; once transport maximum is reached, additional filtered phosphate cannot be reabsorbed and is excreted, producing the same Tm-limited kinetics seen with glucose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing shared Tm-limited kinetics between phosphate and glucose is a useful shortcut for predicting how any saturable proximal transporter will behave.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"By what intracellular pathway does PTH inhibit proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and activates adenylyl cyclase to generate cAMP","B":"PTH binds a luminal receptor and directly blocks the Na+-phosphate cotransporter pore","C":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and activates phospholipase C to generate IP3","D":"PTH binds a luminal receptor and triggers internalization of aquaporin channels","E":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and directly opens apical calcium channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor coupled via a Gs protein to adenylyl cyclase; the resulting cAMP activates protein kinases that phosphorylate luminal membrane components, ultimately inhibiting the Na+-phosphate cotransporter and producing phosphaturia, with excess cAMP itself excreted into the urine as a marker of PTH action.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion are the classic laboratory hallmarks of PTH bioactivity, both driven by this same Gs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP cascade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of filtered magnesium is reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"About 30%, making the proximal tubule the primary reabsorptive site","B":"About 95%, accounting for nearly complete filtered magnesium reabsorption","C":"About 60%, making it the primary site of Mg2+ reabsorption","D":"About 5%, a minor contribution compared with the proximal tubule","E":"About 80%, smaller than the distal tubule's larger contribution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike Na+ and Ca2+, whose primary reabsorptive site is the proximal tubule, Mg2+ handling is dominated by the thick ascending limb, which reabsorbs about 60% of the filtered load; the proximal tubule reabsorbs a comparatively small 30%, and the distal tubule reabsorbs only about 5%, together leaving roughly 5% for excretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thick ascending limb being the primary Mg2+ reabsorptive site, unlike its secondary role for Na+ and Ca2+, is the single most tested fact about renal Mg2+ handling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — why the thick ascending limb dominates Mg2+ reabsorption and why loop diuretics waste it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"What force drives paracellular Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"The lumen-positive potential generated by the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter","B":"An active basolateral Mg2+-ATPase pump unique to the thick ascending limb","C":"A steep transcellular Mg2+ gradient maintained by intracellular buffering","D":"Direct cotransport of Mg2+ with Na+ through the apical NKCC2 transporter","E":"PTH-stimulated active transport identical to that seen with calcium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As with Ca2+, Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb is entirely paracellular, driven by the lumen-positive potential generated by the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter; no dedicated transcellular Mg2+ transporter or hormonal regulator dominates this segment the way PTH does for Ca2+ in the distal tubule.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: since Ca2+ and Mg2+ share this same lumen-positive driving force in the thick ascending limb, any process that eliminates it affects both ions together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — why the thick ascending limb dominates Mg2+ reabsorption and why loop diuretics waste it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"Why do loop diuretics predictably cause hypomagnesemia?","choices":{"A":"They directly inhibit a basolateral Mg2+-ATPase pump in the thick ascending limb","B":"They block NKCC2, removing the potential that drives Mg2+ reabsorption","C":"They increase PTH secretion, which paradoxically suppresses Mg2+ reabsorption","D":"They stimulate a luminal Mg2+ secretory channel in the collecting duct","E":"They inhibit Mg2+ absorption directly at the level of the intestine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick ascending limb, which eliminates the lumen-positive potential that normally drives paracellular Mg2+ (and Ca2+) reabsorption in that segment; because the thick ascending limb is the primary site of Mg2+ reabsorption, this loss produces clinically significant hypomagnesemia with chronic use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair this with the parallel loop-diuretic effect on Ca2+ excretion, since both ions depend on the same lumen-positive potential in the same segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — why the thick ascending limb dominates Mg2+ reabsorption and why loop diuretics waste it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman receives a large IV saline bolus for volume resuscitation. Which additional urinary finding would be expected from this fluid load, beyond the increase in Na+ excretion?","choices":{"A":"Increased Ca2+ excretion, since proximal Ca2+ reabsorption tracks Na+ closely","B":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion, since ECF expansion independently stimulates PTH release","C":"No change in Ca2+ excretion, since Ca2+ handling is entirely independent of Na+ handling","D":"Increased phosphate reabsorption, since PTH secretion falls in response to volume expansion","E":"Decreased Mg2+ excretion, since thick ascending limb reabsorption rises with ECF expansion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because proximal tubular Ca2+ reabsorption is tightly coupled to Na+ reabsorption, any maneuver that inhibits proximal Na+ reabsorption, such as ECF volume expansion from a saline bolus, simultaneously inhibits Ca2+ reabsorption, producing parallel increases in both Na+ and Ca2+ excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this coupling explains why volume-expanded states (and volume-contracted states in the opposite direction) move urinary Ca2+ excretion in the same direction as Na+ excretion, outside of the distal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — this same proximal Na+-Ca2+ coupling is the mechanism tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has a markedly elevated serum Ca2+ from bony metastases. His urinary Ca2+ excretion is appropriately increased. Which receptor-mediated mechanism in the thick ascending limb explains this response?","choices":{"A":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and directly opens paracellular Ca2+ channels","B":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and inhibits NKCC2 directly","C":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and stimulates PTH release from the parathyroid","D":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and increases distal tubule Na+ reabsorption","E":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and blocks the apical H+-K+ ATPase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor in the thick ascending limb detects elevated plasma Ca2+ and inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter; this eliminates the lumen-positive potential that normally drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption, decreasing thick ascending limb Ca2+ reabsorption and appropriately increasing urinary Ca2+ excretion as a protective response to hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the same Ca2+-sensing receptor that is defective in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, where the protective increase in Ca2+ excretion fails to occur.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — this Ca2+-sensing receptor mechanism sets up familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old asymptomatic woman is found to have mild hypercalcemia and inappropriately low urinary Ca2+ excretion, a pattern present in several first-degree relatives. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the thick ascending limb calcium-sensing receptor. Which mechanism explains her hypocalciuria despite hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Her defective receptor over-senses hypercalcemia, paradoxically increasing Ca2+ excretion further","B":"Her defective receptor suppresses PTH secretion, which independently lowers Ca2+ reabsorption","C":"Her defective receptor fails to sense hypercalcemia, so Ca2+ reabsorption continues unchecked","D":"Her defective receptor blocks distal tubule Ca2+ reabsorption, redirecting it to the proximal tubule","E":"Her defective receptor stimulates additional Na+-phosphate cotransport in the proximal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia: a loss-of-function mutation in the thick ascending limb Ca2+-sensing receptor prevents hypercalcemia from inhibiting NKCC2, so the lumen-positive potential and paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption persist despite an elevated serum Ca2+, producing hypocalciuria (low urinary Ca2+) together with hypercalcemia, the two findings that give the syndrome its name.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the combination of hypercalcemia with inappropriately low urinary Ca2+ excretion should immediately suggest FHH rather than primary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — this receptor defect is the direct clinical counterpart to the normal Ca2+-sensing mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has short stature, short fourth metacarpals, and hypocalcemia with markedly elevated serum PTH. Urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion are both low despite the elevated PTH. Which defect best explains this combination?","choices":{"A":"A defect in PTH synthesis that produces a biologically inactive hormone despite high measured levels","B":"A defect in the renal PTH receptor-Gs protein-adenylyl cyclase complex causing PTH resistance","C":"A defect in the Na+-phosphate cotransporter that makes it constitutively resistant to inhibition","D":"A defect in renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase that lowers active vitamin D production selectively","E":"A defect in the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor that raises the PTH secretion setpoint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is pseudohypoparathyroidism: a defect in the renal PTH receptor, Gs protein, or adenylyl cyclase complex makes the kidney resistant to PTH despite elevated circulating hormone; because PTH signaling through this cAMP cascade never occurs, urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion remain low even though PTH itself is appropriately elevated in response to hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urinary cAMP despite high PTH is the key laboratory clue that localizes the defect to the renal receptor-signaling complex, not to PTH synthesis or secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — this receptor-signaling defect is the direct clinical counterpart to the normal cAMP cascade tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has elevated PTH and elevated serum phosphate despite the expected phosphaturic action of PTH. Which explanation accounts for this apparent mismatch?","choices":{"A":"His elevated PTH has become biologically inactive due to prolonged parathyroid gland stimulation","B":"His remaining nephrons have become completely resistant to any PTH-mediated phosphaturic effect","C":"His reduced nephron mass severely limits how much phosphate the kidneys can filter and excrete","D":"His elevated phosphate mainly reflects increased phosphate release from bone","E":"His Na+-phosphate cotransporter density has paradoxically increased in response to elevated PTH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PTH still inhibits the Na+-phosphate cotransporter in each remaining nephron, but with far fewer functioning nephrons in advanced chronic kidney disease, the total filtered phosphate load that can be excreted is severely limited; the resulting secondary hyperparathyroidism raises PTH further in an attempt to compensate, but nephron mass, not receptor function, is the limiting factor.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: secondary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease reflects an appropriate hormonal response overwhelmed by reduced nephron number, distinct from the receptor-level resistance seen in pseudohypoparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — this nephron-mass limitation contrasts directly with a receptor-signaling defect","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with hypocalcemia and low circulating PTH is given an infusion of exogenous PTH. Urinary cAMP excretion rises appropriately afterward. Which conclusion about her renal PTH signaling is best supported by this response?","choices":{"A":"Her basolateral PTH receptor is defective, but a separate luminal pathway compensates fully","B":"Her Na+-phosphate cotransporter is already maximally inhibited, independent of any cAMP signal","C":"Her renal tubules are entirely unresponsive to PTH, similar to pseudohypoparathyroidism","D":"Her basolateral PTH receptor and adenylyl cyclase pathway remain intact, functioning normally","E":"Her low baseline PTH mainly reflects ongoing bone-level hormone resistance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A normal rise in urinary cAMP after exogenous PTH administration demonstrates that the renal basolateral receptor, its coupled Gs protein, and adenylyl cyclase are all functioning normally; this response argues against pseudohypoparathyroidism and points instead toward deficient PTH secretion, that is hypoparathyroidism, as the cause of her hypocalcemia with low PTH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this cAMP-response test is the classic way to distinguish hypoparathyroidism (normal cAMP rise) from renal PTH resistance (blunted rise) at the bedside.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — this cAMP-response test directly probes the same receptor-Gs protein-adenylyl cyclase pathway introduced in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman on long-term furosemide for heart failure develops hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia that persists despite potassium supplementation. Which renal mechanism links her diuretic therapy to the magnesium loss specifically?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide blocks NKCC2, removing the potential that drives Mg2+ reabsorption","B":"Furosemide directly inhibits a basolateral Mg2+-ATPase pump located in the collecting duct","C":"Furosemide increases PTH secretion, which independently suppresses renal Mg2+ reabsorption","D":"Furosemide stimulates a luminal Mg2+ secretory channel newly expressed in the proximal tubule","E":"Furosemide reduces intestinal Mg2+ absorption through a separate gastrointestinal mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Furosemide inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, abolishing the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Mg2+ reabsorption in the nephron's primary Mg2+-reabsorptive segment; the resulting hypomagnesemia can then perpetuate her hypokalemia through the ROMK-gating mechanism, so her potassium will remain refractory until her magnesium is also repleted.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this vignette links directly back to SDL 17's magnesium-ROMK mechanism, showing how a single diuretic mechanism produces two seemingly separate electrolyte disturbances.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — this same thick ascending limb mechanism explains the refractory hypokalemia covered by the Mg2+-ROMK link in SDL 17","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving cisplatin develops progressive renal magnesium wasting that persists for months after treatment ends. Which segment-level explanation best accounts for this prolonged Mg2+ loss?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin damages the proximal tubule's Na+-phosphate cotransporter, secondarily impairing Mg2+ handling","B":"Cisplatin damages the distal tubule's PTH receptor, eliminating hypocalciuric Mg2+ reabsorption","C":"Cisplatin damages the collecting duct's principal cells, impairing aldosterone-driven Mg2+ secretion","D":"Cisplatin damages glomerular capillaries, reducing the filtered load of magnesium available for reabsorption","E":"Cisplatin damages tubular segments that reabsorb Mg2+, including the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cisplatin is directly nephrotoxic to renal tubules, including segments that reabsorb Mg2+ such as the thick ascending limb (roughly 60% of filtered Mg2+) and the distal convoluted tubule; this tubular injury can persist for months after treatment ends, so the resulting Mg2+ wasting is often prolonged well beyond the course of chemotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cisplatin-induced Mg2+ wasting reflects tubular injury including the thick ascending limb, the main site of Mg2+ reabsorption; this is distinct from the K+ wasting caused by intracellular Mg2+ deficiency releasing the ROMK block.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — this direct tubular injury contrasts with the ROMK-gating effects of Mg2+ deficiency covered in SDL 17","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman is switched from hydrochlorothiazide to furosemide for refractory edema. Which combined change in her urinary calcium and magnesium excretion would be expected after this switch?","choices":{"A":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide would affect the distal tubule directly","B":"Increased Ca2+ excretion and decreased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide selectively spares magnesium reabsorption","C":"No change in Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since calcium handling is unaffected by loop diuretics","D":"Increased Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide removes their shared driving force","E":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion and decreased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide mimics thiazide's distal action"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides act on the distal tubule and increase Ca2+ reabsorption while sparing the thick ascending limb, but furosemide acts on the thick ascending limb itself, eliminating the lumen-positive potential that drives both Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption there; switching from a thiazide to a loop diuretic therefore reverses the calcium effect (increasing rather than decreasing excretion) while also increasing magnesium excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this opposite effect on urinary Ca2+ is exactly why thiazides are used for idiopathic hypercalciuria while loop diuretics are used to treat hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — this side-by-side diuretic comparison integrates the thick ascending limb mechanism tested throughout Batch 1's Obj 1 and Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Urine Concentration & Dilution Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"What is the \"single effect\" in countercurrent multiplication?","choices":{"A":"Water reabsorption by the water-permeable descending limb concentrates the tubular fluid there","B":"NaCl reabsorption by the water-impermeable ascending limb dilutes its own tubular fluid","C":"Urea reabsorption by the inner medullary collecting duct concentrates the interstitium there","D":"Active Na+ secretion by the thin ascending limb dilutes the surrounding interstitial fluid","E":"Passive NaCl secretion by the descending limb concentrates the tubular fluid there"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The single effect refers specifically to NaCl reabsorption via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb; because this segment is impermeable to water, the reabsorbed NaCl leaves without water, diluting the ascending limb fluid while raising the osmolarity of the surrounding interstitium and the adjacent descending limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the single effect is a repeatable step, not the finished gradient — pair it with the flow of tubular fluid to understand how a small osmotic difference gets multiplied into the full corticopapillary gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"Why does water flow out of the descending limb during the single effect, even though the descending limb has no active transport?","choices":{"A":"The descending limb pumps water out via a dedicated aquaporin transporter independent of ADH","B":"The descending limb is impermeable to water, so water is trapped and concentrated by evaporation","C":"The descending limb actively cotransports water with the NaCl reabsorbed from the ascending limb","D":"The descending limb responds to a direct hormonal signal from the ascending limb's NaCl transport","E":"The descending limb is water-permeable and equilibrates with the nearby interstitial gradient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thin descending limb is passively permeable to water; as the ascending limb's single effect raises interstitial osmolarity, water simply diffuses out of the descending limb down this osmotic gradient until the descending limb fluid equilibrates with the surrounding interstitium, with no active transport or hormonal signal required.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this passive descending limb water permeability with the ascending limb's water impermeability, since mixing these two up is a common source of countercurrent multiplication errors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"What continuously renews the corticopapillary gradient beyond a single round of the \"single effect\"?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing secretion of ADH, which directly increases interstitial NaCl concentration","B":"Ongoing reabsorption of urea, which dominates the process at each step","C":"Ongoing flow of new fluid, pushing concentrated fluid toward the loop's bend","D":"Ongoing filtration at the glomerulus, which directly raises medullary osmolarity","E":"Ongoing vasa recta blood flow, which actively deposits additional NaCl each cycle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Countercurrent multiplication is a two-step repeating process: the single effect deposits NaCl into the interstitium, and then the ongoing flow of new tubular fluid through the loop pushes the newly concentrated descending limb fluid toward the bend, resetting the ascending limb for another round of the single effect; repeating these two steps is what multiplies a small osmotic difference into the full gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why countercurrent multiplication requires continuous glomerular filtration and tubular flow, not just the transporter activity of the thick ascending limb alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why does urea become concentrated in the tubular fluid of the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts?","choices":{"A":"ADH raises urea permeability there but not water permeability, so urea leaves while water remains","B":"ADH lowers water permeability there, concentrating both water and urea together","C":"ADH has no effect on this segment, and urea concentration simply reflects filtration rate","D":"ADH raises water permeability there but not urea permeability, so water leaves while urea remains","E":"ADH stimulates a dedicated urea pump that actively concentrates urea in this segment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts, ADH increases water permeability without increasing urea permeability, so water is reabsorbed while urea is left behind; this differential permeability effect concentrates urea in the tubular fluid, setting up the gradient that the inner medullary collecting duct will later use.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this differential water-versus-urea permeability is the setup step; the payoff comes once fluid reaches the inner medullary collecting duct, where urea is reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — how ADH-driven UT1 transporters recycle urea into the medulla to reinforce the gradient built by NaCl","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"What distinguishes the inner medullary collecting duct's handling of urea from the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts?","choices":{"A":"ADH increases both water permeability and the UT1 facilitated urea transporter there","B":"ADH increases water permeability there, exactly as in the more proximal segments","C":"ADH decreases urea permeability there, trapping urea for later excretion","D":"ADH has no effect on either water or urea permeability in this final segment","E":"ADH increases urea permeability there whenever tubular urea concentration falls below normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts, where ADH raises only water permeability, the inner medullary collecting duct responds to ADH by raising both water permeability and UT1 urea transporter activity; because upstream water reabsorption has already concentrated urea in the tubular fluid, this added urea permeability lets urea diffuse down its steep gradient into the interstitium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UT1 is specific to the inner medullary collecting duct, which is why urea recycling only becomes possible once tubular fluid reaches that final segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — how ADH-driven UT1 transporters recycle urea into the medulla to reinforce the gradient built by NaCl","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why is urea recycling described as ADH-dependent?","choices":{"A":"Urea transport requires ADH to activate an entirely separate hormonal cascade","B":"ADH is required to synthesize urea within the inner medullary collecting duct cells","C":"The differential permeability effects that concentrate and move urea require ADH","D":"Urea recycling occurs independent of ADH but is measured using ADH as a marker","E":"ADH is required to filter urea at the glomerulus before recycling can begin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Every step of urea recycling, from concentrating urea in the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts to transporting it via UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct, depends on ADH-driven changes in membrane permeability; without ADH, these differential permeability effects do not occur and urea is excreted rather than recycled.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the second mechanism by which ADH augments the corticopapillary gradient, alongside its enhancement of the single effect in the thick ascending limb.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — how ADH-driven UT1 transporters recycle urea into the medulla to reinforce the gradient built by NaCl","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why is countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta described as passive, unlike countercurrent multiplication?","choices":{"A":"It relies on ADH-stimulated active transport identical to the collecting duct mechanism","B":"It relies on diffusion of solutes and water down their existing gradients","C":"It relies on Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport identical to the thick ascending limb mechanism","D":"It relies on active urea transport through the UT1 transporter in the vasa recta wall","E":"It relies on active NaCl secretion by specialized cells lining the vasa recta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vasa recta behave like ordinary capillaries: they are freely and passively permeable to small solutes and water, with no active transporter of their own, so exchange between vasa recta blood and the interstitium occurs entirely by diffusion down existing concentration gradients, in contrast to the active NaCl transport that drives countercurrent multiplication in the loop of Henle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: countercurrent multiplication builds the gradient actively; countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta only helps preserve it passively — a frequently tested distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why does slow blood flow through the vasa recta help preserve the corticopapillary gradient?","choices":{"A":"It gives solutes more time to diffuse back out before the blood exits","B":"It reduces the total blood volume delivered to the renal medulla each minute","C":"It prevents any solute from entering the descending limb of the vasa recta","D":"It increases the hydrostatic pressure gradient driving glomerular filtration","E":"It stimulates additional ADH release from the posterior pituitary gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Slow blood flow through the hairpin-shaped vasa recta gives solutes and water more time to equilibrate at every level of the medulla; on the way up the ascending limb, this extra time allows most of the solute picked up on the way down to diffuse back out into the interstitium before the blood returns to the systemic circulation, limiting net solute loss.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: rapid medullary blood flow (as with aggressive volume expansion) shortens this equilibration time and can wash out the gradient, the clinical counterpart to this normal physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to blood osmolarity as it travels from the descending to the ascending limb of the vasa recta?","choices":{"A":"It rises to match the papilla and remains elevated permanently after exiting","B":"It falls throughout the entire hairpin loop, from entry to exit","C":"It stays constant throughout, since the vasa recta are impermeable to solutes","D":"It rises to match the papilla, then falls back near its entering value","E":"It rises briefly, then falls below its original entering value permanently"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Blood entering the descending limb of the vasa recta equilibrates with the progressively higher interstitial osmolarity, reaching about 1200 mOsm/L at the papilla; as it then travels up the ascending limb through progressively lower osmolarity, solutes diffuse back out and water diffuses back in, so the exiting blood osmolarity falls close to, though slightly above, its original entering value.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small residual rise in exiting osmolarity (about 325 mOsm/L versus 300 mOsm/L entering) represents the modest net solute loss that countercurrent multiplication and urea recycling continuously replace.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man is deprived of water for 24 hours. His plasma ADH rises markedly. Which change in his corticopapillary osmotic gradient would be expected as a direct result of this ADH rise?","choices":{"A":"An augmented gradient, since ADH increases Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter activity","B":"A diminished gradient, since ADH suppresses Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter activity to conserve energy","C":"No change in the gradient, since ADH primarily raises water permeability","D":"An augmented gradient, attributed entirely to a rise in glomerular filtration rate","E":"A diminished gradient, since dehydration reduces medullary blood flow below a critical threshold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ADH augments the corticopapillary gradient through two mechanisms: it enhances the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter and therefore the single effect in the thick ascending limb, and it separately promotes urea recycling; in water deprivation, rising ADH strengthens both mechanisms, producing a larger gradient than when ADH is low.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: dehydration and SIADH both raise ADH and augment the gradient, while water drinking and central diabetes insipidus both lower ADH and diminish it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — this ADH-enhanced single effect mechanism is the same one tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman with central diabetes insipidus has persistently low circulating ADH. Which change in her corticopapillary osmotic gradient would be expected as a direct consequence?","choices":{"A":"An augmented gradient, since low ADH removes an inhibitory brake on the single effect","B":"No change in the gradient, since ADH's role is limited to collecting duct water permeability","C":"A diminished gradient, caused entirely by increased vasa recta blood flow washing out solute","D":"A diminished gradient, since low ADH reduces Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter activity","E":"An augmented gradient, since low ADH increases proximal tubular NaCl reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because ADH normally enhances both the single effect and urea recycling, persistently low ADH in central diabetes insipidus removes both of these augmenting mechanisms rather than any inhibitory brake, so the corticopapillary gradient becomes diminished, contributing to the dilute urine characteristic of this condition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: central diabetes insipidus is the clinical mirror image of dehydration with high ADH, and both should be reasoned through the same ADH mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — this diminished-gradient mechanism is the direct clinical counterpart to Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"Long loops of Henle in species such as desert rodents produce much higher medullary osmolarity than the roughly 1200 mOsm/L seen at the human loop bend. Which explanation best accounts for this difference?","choices":{"A":"A longer loop contains a proportionally larger thick ascending limb Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter density","B":"A longer loop allows more repeating cycles of the single effect to multiply the gradient","C":"A longer loop increases glomerular filtration rate, directly raising interstitial osmolarity","D":"A longer loop increases vasa recta blood flow, delivering more solute to the papilla directly","E":"A longer loop bypasses the need for urea recycling entirely in these species"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Countercurrent multiplication works by repeating the single effect and fluid-flow steps many times along the length of the loop of Henle; a longer loop simply allows more repetitions of this two-step cycle before fluid reaches the bend, multiplying a small osmotic difference into a much larger gradient, which is why loop length itself (not transporter density) predicts maximal medullary osmolarity across species.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this loop-length-to-gradient relationship is the physiologic basis for why species built for extreme water conservation have disproportionately long loops of Henle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — this loop-length principle is the comparative-physiology extension of the multiplication mechanism tested throughout Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man on a chronically low-protein diet is noted to have a reduced maximal urine concentrating ability despite normal ADH secretion. Which explanation best accounts for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Reduced protein intake directly inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb","B":"Reduced protein intake decreases circulating ADH secretion despite normal osmotic stimulation","C":"Reduced protein intake increases vasa recta blood flow, washing out the medullary gradient","D":"Reduced urea production limits the substrate available for medullary urea recycling","E":"Reduced protein intake impairs water permeability in the inner medullary collecting duct directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because urea recycling contributes roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient and depends on urea generated from protein metabolism, a chronically low-protein diet reduces the amount of urea available to be recycled through UT1, limiting the urea-dependent portion of the gradient even when ADH secretion and signaling remain entirely normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced urine concentrating ability on a low-protein diet is a classic example of a substrate-limited rather than a hormone- or transporter-limited process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — this substrate-limitation on urea recycling is the mirror image of the ADH-dependence mechanism tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with SIADH has persistently elevated ADH and produces small volumes of highly concentrated urine despite hyponatremia. Which mechanism specifically involving urea contributes to his urine concentrating ability?","choices":{"A":"Persistently high ADH increases urea filtration at the glomerulus, directly raising tubular urea delivery","B":"Persistently high ADH keeps UT1 active in the inner medullary collecting duct, sustaining urea transport","C":"Persistently high ADH suppresses UT1 activity, redirecting urea toward the systemic circulation","D":"Persistently high ADH increases hepatic urea synthesis, raising the total urea available for excretion","E":"Persistently high ADH increases proximal tubular urea reabsorption independent of the medullary gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In SIADH, chronically elevated ADH continuously activates UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct, allowing urea to keep diffusing into the interstitium and reinforcing the corticopapillary gradient built by countercurrent multiplication; this sustained urea recycling is part of why SIADH produces persistently concentrated urine rather than an occasional or self-limited effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: SIADH is essentially the pathologic exaggeration of the same ADH-driven urea recycling mechanism seen physiologically during water deprivation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — this sustained UT1 activation is the pathologic exaggeration of the ADH-dependence mechanism tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man in a hypercatabolic state after a severe burn has markedly increased urea production. Assuming ADH secretion is appropriately elevated, which effect on his corticopapillary gradient would be expected from this increased urea production alone?","choices":{"A":"A smaller contribution from urea recycling, since excess urea saturates the UT1 transporter capacity","B":"No change in urea recycling, since ADH is presumed to be the single determinant of the process","C":"A larger contribution from urea recycling, since more substrate is available for UT1-mediated transport","D":"A larger contribution from countercurrent multiplication, since urea directly stimulates Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport","E":"A smaller contribution from countercurrent multiplication, since excess urea competes with NaCl for reabsorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because urea recycling is substrate-dependent, an increase in urea production, as occurs in the hypercatabolic state following a severe burn, provides more urea available to be concentrated in the collecting duct and transported via UT1, increasing the urea-driven contribution to the corticopapillary gradient as long as ADH remains available to permit the differential permeability effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the opposite scenario from a low-protein diet, and both should be reasoned through the same substrate-availability principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — this substrate-availability principle mirrors the low-protein-diet scenario","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man receives aggressive IV fluid resuscitation that substantially increases renal medullary blood flow. Which effect on his corticopapillary osmotic gradient would be expected from this increased vasa recta flow alone?","choices":{"A":"An augmented gradient, since faster flow delivers more NaCl directly to the thick ascending limb","B":"No change in the gradient, since vasa recta flow rate does not influence solute exchange efficiency","C":"A diminished gradient, caused by direct inhibition of the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter by increased flow","D":"An augmented gradient, since faster flow increases urea delivery to the inner medullary collecting duct","E":"A diminished gradient, since faster flow carries away more solute than exchange can return"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta depends on blood moving slowly enough for solutes to diffuse back out of the ascending limb before the blood exits the medulla; when medullary blood flow increases substantially, as with aggressive fluid resuscitation, there is less time for this equilibration, so more solute is carried away than can be returned, producing medullary washout and a diminished gradient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: medullary washout from rapid volume expansion is the clinical counterpart to the normal slow-flow physiology tested in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — this medullary washout is the clinical counterpart to the normal slow-flow mechanism tested in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"If the vasa recta ran as straight vessels through the medulla instead of hairpin loops, what would be the expected consequence for the corticopapillary gradient?","choices":{"A":"The gradient would be unaffected, since vessel geometry does not influence passive solute exchange","B":"The gradient would be augmented, since straight vessels would reduce turbulence and improve solute retention","C":"The gradient would be washed out, since straight vessels cannot return solute picked up on the way in","D":"The gradient would be augmented, since straight vessels deliver blood to the papilla more efficiently","E":"The gradient would be unaffected, since the vasa recta contribute no solute exchange under either geometry"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hairpin shape of the vasa recta is essential to countercurrent exchange because it lets the ascending limb recapture solute that the descending limb picked up from the interstitium on the way down; a straight vessel would carry that solute directly out of the medulla with no return path, rapidly washing out the corticopapillary gradient despite the vasa recta's passive permeability being unchanged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this thought experiment isolates why the hairpin geometry itself, not just passive permeability, is what makes the vasa recta effective at preserving rather than dissipating the gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — this hairpin-geometry principle is the structural basis for the passive exchange mechanism tested in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"Juxtamedullary nephrons, unlike superficial cortical nephrons, are associated with peritubular capillaries organized as vasa recta rather than ordinary capillaries. Which functional consequence follows most directly from this anatomic difference?","choices":{"A":"Their vasa recta specifically enable countercurrent exchange that preserves a deep medullary gradient","B":"Superficial cortical nephrons achieve the same countercurrent exchange using ordinary capillaries","C":"Juxtamedullary nephrons lose the ability to concentrate urine because of that hairpin capillary arrangement","D":"Superficial cortical nephrons generate a deeper medullary gradient despite lacking vasa recta","E":"Juxtamedullary nephrons rely on active NaCl pumping by the vasa recta to preserve the gradient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops of Henle that descend deep into the medulla, and their specialized peritubular capillaries, the vasa recta, follow this same hairpin course to passively preserve the deep gradient those long loops help build; superficial cortical nephrons, whose short loops barely enter the outer medulla, have no comparable need for this hairpin capillary arrangement.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pairing long-loop juxtamedullary nephrons with vasa recta and short-loop cortical nephrons with ordinary peritubular capillaries is a frequently tested anatomic correlation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — this nephron-type correlation ties the vasa recta mechanism to the anatomic basis for concentrating ability","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Clinical Water Disorders, Dysnatremias, & Neuro-Urology","questions":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"What is the core physiological defect in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"ADH is released mainly in response to volume depletion, not in response to osmolarity","B":"ADH receptors are upregulated, amplifying a normal osmotic stimulus excessively","C":"ADH is absent, so principal cells fail to insert aquaporin channels","D":"ADH is present but principal cells are structurally resistant to its V2 receptor","E":"ADH is released autonomously, independent of plasma osmolarity or blood volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In SIADH, ADH is secreted autonomously by an ectopic source or an abnormally sensitive pituitary, without regard to the usual osmotic or volume-based triggers; this unregulated secretion drives continuous water reabsorption regardless of the patient's actual osmotic or volume status, producing dilutional hyponatremia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the word \"inappropriate\" in SIADH refers specifically to ADH secretion occurring without its normal physiologic trigger, not to any structural or receptor-level abnormality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"Why does central diabetes insipidus produce dilute urine despite a strong osmotic stimulus?","choices":{"A":"The posterior pituitary secretes ADH normally, but principal cells fail to respond to it","B":"The hypothalamus overproduces ADH, paradoxically suppressing its own receptor sensitivity","C":"The collecting ducts secrete excess water directly, independent of any hormonal signal","D":"The posterior pituitary cannot secrete ADH, leaving collecting ducts impermeable to water","E":"The kidneys actively excrete ADH before it can reach the collecting duct principal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Central diabetes insipidus reflects a failure of the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary to synthesize, store, or release ADH; without circulating ADH, principal cells cannot insert aquaporin-2 channels, leaving the late distal tubule and collecting duct impermeable to water and producing large volumes of dilute urine despite rising plasma osmolarity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the ADH-deficiency half of diabetes insipidus, distinguished from nephrogenic DI's ADH-resistance defect by the response to exogenous dDAVP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"How does nephrogenic diabetes insipidus differ from central diabetes insipidus in terms of circulating ADH?","choices":{"A":"Circulating ADH is low or absent, identical to the defect in central diabetes insipidus","B":"Circulating ADH is normal, but osmoreceptors fail to detect rising plasma osmolarity","C":"Circulating ADH is normal or elevated, but principal cells fail to respond to it","D":"Circulating ADH is elevated, and principal cells respond normally but excessively","E":"Circulating ADH is absent because the kidneys destroy it before it reaches the nephron"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike central diabetes insipidus, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus involves normal or even elevated circulating ADH; the defect instead lies in the renal principal cell, whether from a V2 receptor mutation, aquaporin-2 mutation, or acquired causes such as lithium toxicity, so the kidney remains unresponsive despite abundant hormone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a rising ADH level in nephrogenic DI reflects appropriate compensatory pituitary secretion in response to persistently high plasma osmolarity, not a primary secretory abnormality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does demeclocycline treat SIADH?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits V2 signaling, inducing a controlled, reversible nephrogenic DI state","B":"It blocks ADH synthesis directly within the posterior pituitary gland itself","C":"It stimulates renal Na+ excretion directly, independent of any ADH-related pathway","D":"It increases aquaporin-2 degradation, without directly affecting V2 receptor signaling","E":"It competes with ADH for binding sites on the hypothalamic osmoreceptors directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Demeclocycline is a tetracycline derivative that interferes with V2 receptor-coupled Gs signaling and downstream cAMP generation in principal cells, deliberately inducing a state resembling nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; this blunts the excessive water reabsorption of SIADH and promotes solute-free water excretion, raising serum sodium.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: demeclocycline works at the receptor-signaling level in the kidney, not by reducing ADH secretion at the pituitary, which is why it remains effective even when the ectopic ADH source cannot be removed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism do vaptans raise serum sodium in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"They stimulate renal Na+ reabsorption, independent of any water excretion effect","B":"They competitively block ADH at the V2 receptor, preventing aquaporin-2 insertion","C":"They inhibit aquaporin-2 synthesis at the transcriptional level over several days","D":"They increase ADH secretion, paradoxically promoting solute-free water excretion","E":"They inhibit the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb directly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tolvaptan (a selective V2 receptor antagonist) and conivaptan (a mixed V1a/V2 antagonist) competitively block ADH binding on the basolateral membrane of principal cells, preventing aquaporin-2 channel translocation to the luminal membrane; this produces pure aquaresis, solute-free water excretion without a significant accompanying electrolyte loss, raising serum sodium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"aquaresis\" (water loss without solute loss) is the key term that distinguishes vaptans from a standard diuretic, which excretes both water and solute together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the strict upper limit for correcting severe hyponatremia within 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 15 to 20 mEq/L, with no meaningful hourly limit","B":"About 20 to 25 mEq/L, adjusted mainly for the patient's total body weight","C":"About 2 to 4 mEq/L, regardless of the patient's baseline risk factors","D":"About 8 to 10 mEq/L, monitored closely throughout the correction","E":"About 12 to 14 mEq/L, adjusted mainly for the patient's serum creatinine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Serum sodium correction in severe hyponatremia must not exceed about 8 to 10 mEq/L in any 24-hour period, because brain cells that have adapted to chronic hyponatremia by extruding organic osmolytes cannot re-accumulate them fast enough to keep pace with a more rapid rise in extracellular osmolarity; frequent monitoring during correction helps avoid exceeding this limit.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in high-risk patients (malnutrition, alcoholism, advanced liver disease), the target correction limit is tightened further, to less than 6 to 8 mEq/L in 24 hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"In SIADH, why does infusing isotonic 0.9% saline paradoxically worsen hyponatremia?","choices":{"A":"The saline directly stimulates additional ADH release from the posterior pituitary gland itself","B":"The saline is markedly hypotonic relative to plasma, diluting serum sodium immediately on contact","C":"The kidneys excrete the saline's entire solute load in a much larger urine volume than was infused","D":"The saline raises ECF volume substantially, triggering compensatory aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","E":"The fixed high urine osmolarity forces the infused solute into a smaller volume than infused"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because ADH is fixed and high in SIADH, the kidney can only excrete urine at its fixed, elevated osmolarity; the roughly 308 mOsm of solute in a liter of normal saline is therefore excreted in a urine volume smaller than one liter, leaving the difference behind as retained solute-free water that dilutes serum sodium further.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the direction of the effect flips entirely with 3% hypertonic saline, whose osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity, producing a net free water deficit instead.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"What general biophysical rule predicts whether an IV fluid will worsen or improve hyponatremia in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than plasma osmolarity by itself","B":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than the fixed urine osmolarity","C":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its total volume exceeds the patient's daily urine output","D":"Fluid improves hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than the fixed urine osmolarity","E":"Fluid improves hyponatremia whenever its total volume is less than one liter infused"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the SIADH kidney excretes urine at a fixed osmolarity regardless of intake, any infused fluid with an osmolarity below that fixed urine osmolarity requires more urine volume to excrete its solute than was infused, leaving free water behind; a fluid with a higher osmolarity than the fixed urine osmolarity does the opposite, producing a net water deficit.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this rule is why plain water, D5W, and even 0.9% saline can all worsen hyponatremia in SIADH, while only hypertonic saline reliably improves it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"Which intervention would be expected to raise serum sodium in a patient with SIADH and a fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L?","choices":{"A":"3% hypertonic saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","B":"0.9% normal saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","C":"5% dextrose in water, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","D":"3% hypertonic saline, since its osmolarity is lower than the fixed urine osmolarity","E":"0.45% half-normal saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only a fluid whose osmolarity exceeds the patient's fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L will raise serum sodium; 3% hypertonic saline has an osmolarity around 1026 mOsm/L, comfortably above this threshold, while normal saline (308 mOsm/L), D5W (effectively near 0), and half-normal saline (154 mOsm/L) all fall below it and would worsen hyponatremia instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the rule, not just the drug name — any hypotonic-relative-to-urine fluid worsens SIADH hyponatremia, and hypertonic saline is simply the fluid that reliably clears that bar.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net action of sympathetic innervation on the bladder during the storage phase?","choices":{"A":"It contracts the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and relaxes the internal sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","B":"It relaxes the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and tightens the internal sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","C":"It relaxes the detrusor via muscarinic receptors and constricts the sphincter via nicotinic receptors","D":"It contracts the detrusor via alpha-1 receptors and relaxes the sphincter via beta-3 receptors","E":"It has no direct effect on the detrusor and mainly modulates external sphincter tone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During storage, sympathetic fibers of the hypogastric nerve (lumbar outflow, L1-L3 per the SDL objective) release norepinephrine, which relaxes detrusor smooth muscle via beta-3 receptors (increasing bladder compliance) and simultaneously constricts the internal urethral sphincter via alpha-1 receptors, together promoting continence while the bladder fills.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pair beta-3 (detrusor relaxation) with alpha-1 (sphincter constriction) as the two sympathetic storage-phase effects, both driven by the same sympathetic L1-L3 outflow through the hypogastric nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net action of parasympathetic innervation on the bladder during voiding?","choices":{"A":"It contracts the detrusor via M3 receptors and relaxes the internal sphincter via nitric oxide","B":"It relaxes the detrusor via M3 receptors and contracts the internal sphincter via nitric oxide","C":"It contracts the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and relaxes the sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","D":"It relaxes the detrusor via alpha-1 receptors and contracts the sphincter via M3 receptors","E":"It has no effect on the internal sphincter and mainly acts on the external sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"During voiding, parasympathetic fibers from spinal segments S2 to S4 release acetylcholine, which drives powerful detrusor contraction via M3 muscarinic receptors while separately triggering nitric-oxide-mediated relaxation of the internal urethral sphincter, together coordinating effective bladder emptying.","boardPrep":"Step 1: M3 (detrusor contraction) and nitric oxide (sphincter relaxation) are both parasympathetic S2-S4 effects, the functional opposite of the sympathetic storage-phase pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve provides voluntary somatic control of the external urethral sphincter?","choices":{"A":"The pelvic splanchnic nerve, via muscarinic receptors on smooth sphincter muscle","B":"The hypogastric nerve, via alpha-1 receptors acting on smooth muscle","C":"The pudendal nerve, via nicotinic receptors on striated sphincter muscle","D":"The pudendal nerve, via muscarinic receptors on smooth sphincter muscle","E":"The obturator nerve, via nicotinic receptors on striated sphincter muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor fibers from the sacral spinal cord (S2-S4) to the striated external urethral sphincter, releasing acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors to provide tonic, voluntary contraction that maintains continence and can be voluntarily relaxed during voiding.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the external sphincter is the only striated, voluntarily controlled component of the continence mechanism; the internal sphincter is smooth muscle under autonomic control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with polyuria and excessive fluid intake undergoes a water deprivation test. Urine osmolarity rises to 900 mOsm/kg as plasma osmolarity increases. Which diagnosis is best supported by this result?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus, since urine remained dilute despite rising plasma osmolarity","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since urine remained persistently dilute despite rising plasma osmolarity","C":"SIADH, since urine osmolarity rose despite a falling plasma osmolarity","D":"Primary polydipsia, since intact ADH release concentrated the urine while plasma osmolarity climbed","E":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since dDAVP administration produced no further concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A urine osmolarity that concentrates appropriately (here, 900 mOsm/kg) during water deprivation shows that both ADH secretion and renal responsiveness are intact, which points to primary polydipsia as the cause of her polyuria rather than either form of diabetes insipidus, which would not concentrate urine to this degree under the same osmotic stimulus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the water deprivation test's first phase distinguishes primary polydipsia from both types of DI; the second phase, exogenous dDAVP, is what then distinguishes central from nephrogenic DI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — this water-deprivation response is the same diagnostic logic tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with polyuria has a urine osmolarity of 180 mOsm/kg despite water deprivation and a plasma osmolarity of 300 mOsm/kg. After receiving dDAVP, his urine osmolarity rises to 650 mOsm/kg. Which diagnosis is best supported by this response?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","C":"Primary polydipsia, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","D":"Central diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH produced no meaningful change","E":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH produced no meaningful change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His persistently dilute urine despite water deprivation first rules out primary polydipsia; the dramatic rise in urine osmolarity after exogenous dDAVP then shows that his principal cells are fully capable of responding to ADH once it is supplied, confirming that his own posterior pituitary was the source of the deficiency, the defining feature of central diabetes insipidus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a nephrogenic DI patient would show little to no response (typically less than 10-15% change) to the same dDAVP challenge, since the defect is intrinsic to the kidney.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — this dDAVP response test is the direct clinical application of the central-versus-nephrogenic distinction tested in Batch 1's Obj 1 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman on long-term lithium for bipolar disorder develops polyuria and polydipsia with dilute urine despite an elevated plasma ADH level. Which mechanism best explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Lithium markedly suppresses posterior pituitary ADH secretion, producing acquired central diabetes insipidus","B":"Lithium impairs principal cell responsiveness to ADH, producing acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Lithium directly stimulates thirst, producing primary polydipsia independent of ADH signaling","D":"Lithium destroys the corticopapillary osmotic gradient through direct medullary toxicity","E":"Lithium excessively increases aquaporin-2 synthesis and inappropriate water reabsorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lithium is a well-recognized cause of acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, impairing principal cell responsiveness to ADH at the receptor and post-receptor level; her elevated plasma ADH represents an appropriate compensatory pituitary response to the resulting polyuria and rising plasma osmolarity, not a primary secretory defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lithium-induced nephrogenic DI is managed by minimizing lithium where possible and using amiloride, which blocks lithium entry into principal cells through ENaC; thiazides treat other forms of nephrogenic DI but raise lithium levels, so they are used with caution here.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — this acquired nephrogenic DI mechanism sets up the nephrogenic DI treatment principles of Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and malnutrition has severe hyponatremia. Which correction target is most appropriate to minimize his risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 to 12 mEq/L over 24 hours, the limit typically used for uncomplicated hyponatremia","B":"Up to 15 to 20 mEq/L over 24 hours, appropriate for severe symptomatic hyponatremia cases","C":"Less than 1 to 2 mEq/L over 24 hours, an unnecessarily conservative correction target","D":"Up to 25 mEq/L over the first 24 hours whenever hypertonic saline is the chosen fluid","E":"Less than 6 to 8 mEq/L over 24 hours, given his additional risk factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Patients with malnutrition, alcohol use disorder, or advanced liver disease are at higher risk for osmotic demyelination syndrome at any given correction rate, so their target is tightened below the standard 8 to 10 mEq/L per 24 hours to a stricter limit of less than 6 to 8 mEq/L over the same period.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: always ask about alcohol use, malnutrition, and liver disease when setting a correction target, since these patients need a lower ceiling than the general 8-10 mEq/L/24h rule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — this high-risk correction limit is a direct application of the standard ODS limit tested in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with decompensated heart failure and hyponatremia is started on tolvaptan. Which mechanism accounts for the expected rise in his serum sodium?","choices":{"A":"Tolvaptan blocks V2 receptors, producing pure aquaresis without notable electrolyte loss","B":"Tolvaptan blocks aldosterone receptors, producing combined natriuresis and diuresis","C":"Tolvaptan inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, producing a distinct loop-diuretic-like effect","D":"Tolvaptan stimulates ADH release, paradoxically increasing free water excretion","E":"Tolvaptan blocks V1 receptors, reducing systemic vascular resistance directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tolvaptan's V2 receptor blockade prevents aquaporin-2 insertion in principal cells, producing solute-free water excretion (aquaresis) without a proportional loss of Na+ or K+; this raises serum sodium by removing excess free water, distinguishing it mechanistically from a conventional diuretic that excretes water together with solute.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vaptans are used for euvolemic (SIADH) and hypervolemic (heart failure) hyponatremia because they correct free water excess without worsening volume status the way a standard diuretic might; tolvaptan is avoided in underlying liver disease because of hepatotoxicity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — this V2-receptor-blockade mechanism is the same one tested directly in Batch 1's Obj 2 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with hereditary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from a V2 receptor mutation is started on hydrochlorothiazide and her urine output falls substantially. Which mechanism best explains this improvement?","choices":{"A":"Hydrochlorothiazide restores principal cell sensitivity to circulating ADH directly","B":"Mild ECF contraction increases proximal reabsorption, limiting distal delivery","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide increases collecting duct water permeability independent of ADH","D":"Hydrochlorothiazide corrects the receptor mutation responsible for her renal ADH resistance","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide suppresses thirst centrally, reducing her total fluid intake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazide-induced NCC inhibition in the early distal tubule causes mild sodium wasting and subclinical ECF volume contraction; this triggers compensatory proximal tubular Na+ and water reabsorption, reducing the volume of fluid delivered to the still-unresponsive collecting duct and lowering urine output, without directly restoring any ADH responsiveness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this proximal-compensation mechanism is the paradox used to treat hereditary nephrogenic DI with thiazides; in lithium-induced nephrogenic DI, amiloride is preferred because thiazides raise lithium levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — this thiazide-paradox mechanism is the direct clinical application of the nephrogenic DI mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with SIADH has a fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L and receives 1 liter of 0.9% normal saline (308 mOsm total solute). Approximately how much solute-free water would be expected to remain in his body after this infusion?","choices":{"A":"About 1.0 liter, since very little of the infused solute would be excreted in the urine","B":"About 0.3 liters, since roughly 0.7 liters of urine would be needed to excrete the infused solute","C":"Zero liters, since isotonic saline does not affect free water balance in SIADH","D":"About 0.5 liters, since roughly 0.5 liters of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","E":"About 0.7 liters, since roughly 0.3 liters of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dividing the infused solute load (308 mOsm) by the fixed urine osmolarity (600 mOsm/L) gives a required excretion volume of about 0.5 liters; subtracting that from the 1.0 liter infused leaves approximately 0.5 liters of solute-free water retained in the body, diluting serum sodium further.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the exact calculation to reproduce for any saline-trap question — solute load divided by fixed urine osmolarity gives required urine volume, and infused volume minus that gives retained free water.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — this is the worked calculation behind the general biophysical rule tested in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with SIADH has a fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L and receives 1 liter of 3% hypertonic saline (roughly 1000 mOsm total solute). Which outcome would be expected regarding his free water balance?","choices":{"A":"A net free water surplus, since less than a liter of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","B":"A net free water deficit, since more than a liter of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","C":"No net change in free water, since hypertonic saline behaves identically to isotonic saline","D":"A net free water surplus, since hypertonic saline directly suppresses ADH secretion","E":"A net free water deficit, driven primarily by centrally stimulated thirst"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing the larger solute load (about 1000 mOsm) by the same fixed urine osmolarity (600 mOsm/L) requires roughly 1.67 liters of urine to excrete it; because this exceeds the 1.0 liter infused, the patient ends up with a net free water deficit, raising serum sodium instead of lowering it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: comparing this result with the isotonic saline calculation (308 mOsm per liter) shows exactly why hypertonic, not isotonic, saline is required to correct hyponatremia in SIADH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — this contrasting calculation directly extends the isotonic-saline calculation for the same SIADH physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with SIADH and a fixed urine osmolarity of 500 mOsm/L receives an infusion of 5% dextrose in water (D5W, which is metabolized to free water with an effective osmolarity near zero). Which effect on his serum sodium would be expected?","choices":{"A":"A rise toward normal, since D5W provides free water and calories without any true osmotic effect","B":"No meaningful change occurs, since D5W behaves as essentially isotonic once administered intravenously","C":"A further decline, since the retained solute-free water lowers plasma tonicity further","D":"A further decline caused mainly by direct central suppression of endogenous ADH secretion","E":"A rise toward normal, since D5W's effective osmolarity substantially exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once metabolized, D5W behaves as nearly pure free water with an effective osmolarity close to zero, far below the fixed urine osmolarity of 500 mOsm/L; applying the same rule, essentially all of it is retained as free water, worsening hyponatremia even more severely than isotonic saline would, since D5W carries no solute at all to be excreted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: D5W is the most dangerous common IV fluid to give a SIADH patient by this rule, since it has essentially no solute to help drive its own excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — this D5W case is the most extreme application of the general biophysical rule tested in Batch 1's Obj 3 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man sustains a suprasacral spinal cord injury in a motor vehicle accident. Several weeks later, he develops involuntary bladder contractions against a simultaneously contracting external sphincter, with markedly elevated intravesical pressures. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the sacral reflex arc itself entirely abolishes detrusor contraction, producing a completely atonic bladder","B":"Loss of sympathetic innervation directly causes involuntary detrusor contraction to occur spontaneously","C":"Loss of pudendal nerve function eliminates voluntary external urethral sphincter tone","D":"Loss of parasympathetic innervation entirely prevents any detrusor contraction from occurring","E":"Loss of pontine coordination leaves the sacral reflex arc uninhibited, causing detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A suprasacral spinal cord injury interrupts the descending pathways from the pontine micturition center while leaving the sacral reflex arc (S2-S4) intact; the resulting spastic, hyperreflexic detrusor contracts involuntarily but without the normal coordinated relaxation of the external sphincter, producing detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia and dangerously high intravesical pressures.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this upper-motor-neuron picture with the lower-motor-neuron atonic bladder from a sacral or cauda equina lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — this spastic bladder mechanism is the direct clinical application of the parasympathetic pathway tested in Batch 1's Obj 4 questions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a cauda equina injury develops continuous urine dribbling with a massively distended, thin-walled bladder and no sensation of fullness. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Loss of parasympathetic detrusor innervation abolishes micturition, causing passive overdistention","B":"Loss of sympathetic innervation entirely abolishes detrusor relaxation, causing spastic overactivity","C":"Loss of pudendal nerve function chiefly abolishes the voluntary micturition reflex control","D":"Loss of descending pontine inhibition instead produces detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","E":"Loss of somatic innervation directly triggers involuntary detrusor contraction spontaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A cauda equina lesion damages the sacral nerve roots that carry parasympathetic fibers to the detrusor, abolishing the micturition reflex and interrupting the visceral sensory afferents that normally signal bladder fullness; the bladder then fills passively until pressure exceeds sphincter resistance, producing overflow incontinence with a hypotonic, overdistended bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this atonic, lower-motor-neuron bladder is the functional opposite of the spastic, upper-motor-neuron bladder from the suprasacral injury pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — this atonic bladder mechanism is the direct clinical counterpart to the spastic bladder vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia reports frequent dribbling of urine and a sensation of incomplete emptying, with a large post-void residual volume on bladder scan. Which type of incontinence best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Stress incontinence, caused by pelvic floor muscle weakness","B":"Urge incontinence, caused by uninhibited detrusor overactivity","C":"Overflow incontinence, caused by mechanical bladder outlet obstruction","D":"Overflow incontinence, caused by loss of parasympathetic detrusor innervation","E":"Urge incontinence, caused by mechanical bladder outlet obstruction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia mechanically obstructs the bladder outlet, preventing complete emptying and leading to an elevated post-void residual volume; as the bladder becomes chronically overdistended, urine dribbles out once intravesical pressure exceeds outlet resistance, the defining pattern of overflow incontinence from a mechanical rather than a neurogenic cause.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: BPH-related overflow incontinence is mechanical, not neurogenic, distinguishing it from the atonic neurogenic bladder that produces the same overflow pattern through nerve injury instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — this mechanical-obstruction overflow mechanism contrasts directly with the neurogenic overflow mechanism","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Acid-Base Foundations, Buffering, & Henderson-Hasselbalch Kinetics","questions":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"The normal arterial [H+] concentration of 40 nEq/L corresponds to a pH of 7.4. Which statement correctly describes the relationship between pH and [H+]?","choices":{"A":"The relationship between pH and [H+] is linear, so equal pH changes reflect equal changes in [H+]","B":"An increase in pH corresponds directly to a proportional increase in [H+] concentration","C":"The logarithmic relationship applies to intracellular fluid but not to arterial blood","D":"Equal changes in pH do not reflect equal changes in [H+], since the relationship is logarithmic","E":"A pH change from 7.4 to 7.2 reflects a smaller change in [H+] than a pH change from 7.4 to 7.6"},"correct":"D","explanation":"pH is defined as -log10[H+], a logarithmic function of [H+], not a linear one. Because of this, a decrease in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 reflects a larger absolute change in [H+] (an increase of about 23 nEq/L) than the change produced by an equal-magnitude increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (a decrease of only about 15 nEq/L).","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize that pH 7.4 = [H+] of exactly 40 nEq/L; this anchor value lets you quickly sanity-check an ABG. COMLEX: the same logarithmic principle explains why a seemingly small pH change on a blood gas can reflect a clinically significant swing in acid load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — pH math underlying anion gap acidosis severity"},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"A patient’s arterial pH decreases from 7.4 to 7.2, while a second patient’s pH increases from 7.4 to 7.6 — both are 0.2-unit pH shifts. Which statement is correct regarding the corresponding changes in [H+]?","choices":{"A":"The decrease to pH 7.2 produces a larger [H+] change than the increase to pH 7.6, about 23 vs. 15 nEq/L","B":"Both pH shifts reflect an identical absolute change in [H+] of approximately 19 nEq/L each","C":"The increase to pH 7.6 reflects a larger absolute change in [H+] than the decrease to pH 7.2","D":"Neither pH shift produces a measurable change in [H+], since both values remain compatible with life","E":"The relative magnitude of the two [H+] changes cannot be determined without knowing each patient’s Pco2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Over the physiologic range, a given change in pH in the acidic direction (below 7.4) reflects a larger change in [H+] than the same-magnitude change in the alkaline direction (above 7.4). Concretely, going from pH 7.4 to 7.2 raises [H+] by about 23 nEq/L, while going from pH 7.4 to 7.6 lowers [H+] by only about 15 nEq/L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this asymmetry is a favorite testing point; a pH drop on an ABG corresponds to a bigger absolute rise in [H+] than the same-sized pH rise would produce as a drop. COMLEX: useful for rapidly gauging acid load severity from an ABG pH alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — pH math underlying anion gap acidosis severity"},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following arterial pH values would be classified as alkalemia?","choices":{"A":"7.44","B":"7.38","C":"7.32","D":"7.28","E":"7.48"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The normal arterial pH range is 7.35 to 7.45. A value above 7.45, such as 7.48, is classified as alkalemia. Values of 7.44 and 7.38 fall within the normal range, while 7.32 and 7.28 are below 7.35 and represent acidemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the exact normal range (7.35-7.45) and the terms acidemia (<7.35) and alkalemia (>7.45) cold — they are used to define every acid-base disorder question. COMLEX: the range compatible with life is 6.8 to 8.0, a boundary occasionally tested directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — pH math underlying anion gap acidosis severity"},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following acids is classified as a \"volatile\" acid rather than a \"fixed\" acid?","choices":{"A":"Sulfuric acid formed from sulfur-containing amino acid catabolism, occurring continuously in protein metabolism","B":"CO2 formed from aerobic cellular respiration, occurring continuously throughout the body","C":"Phosphoric acid formed from phospholipid catabolism, occurring continuously in membrane turnover","D":"Lactic acid formed during strenuous exercise or tissue hypoxia, occurring via anaerobic glycolysis","E":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid formed during untreated diabetes mellitus, occurring via insulin deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CO2 is the volatile acid: it is produced by aerobic metabolism at 13,000-20,000 mmol/day, combines reversibly with H2O (via carbonic anhydrase) to form H2CO3, and is regenerated and expired by the lungs, making its buffering only a temporary problem for venous blood. Sulfuric acid, phosphoric acid, lactic acid, and ketoacids are all fixed (nonvolatile) acids that must be buffered until the kidneys can excrete them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"volatile acid\" = CO2, full stop — it is the only acid the lungs can eliminate. COMLEX: this distinction underlies why a primary respiratory problem is corrected by ventilation, while a primary metabolic (fixed-acid) problem requires renal or buffering correction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Catabolism of which class of molecules generates the approximately 50 mmol/day of fixed acid produced under normal metabolic conditions?","choices":{"A":"Carbohydrates metabolized via aerobic glycolysis in muscle and other tissues, generating pyruvic acid","B":"Triglycerides metabolized via beta-oxidation in the liver, generating ketoacids","C":"Nucleic acids metabolized via purine catabolism, generating uric acid as waste","D":"Glycogen metabolized via glycogenolysis in the liver, generating glucose-6-phosphate","E":"Proteins containing sulfur amino acids and phospholipids, generating sulfuric and phosphoric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Catabolism of proteins containing sulfur amino acids (methionine, cysteine, cystine) generates sulfuric acid, and catabolism of phospholipids generates phosphoric acid; together these normally produce about 50 mmol/day of fixed acid that must be buffered and then excreted by the kidneys. Ordinary carbohydrate, triglyceride, nucleic acid, and glycogen metabolism are not the source of this baseline fixed-acid load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the ~50 mmol/day fixed-acid figure and its protein/phospholipid origin — it is the baseline load the kidneys must handle even without any pathology. COMLEX: a high-protein diet increases this fixed acid load, relevant to CKD acid-base management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which fixed acids are characteristically overproduced in a patient with untreated diabetes mellitus?","choices":{"A":"Lactic acid, from anaerobic muscle metabolism during strenuous exercise","B":"Salicylic acid, from an intentional or accidental aspirin overdose ingestion","C":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid, from ketogenesis","D":"Formic acid, from accidental or intentional methanol ingestion","E":"Glycolic and oxalic acids, from accidental ethylene glycol ingestion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In untreated diabetes mellitus, fixed acids are overproduced in the form of the ketoacids beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid. Lactic acid instead accumulates with strenuous exercise or tissue hypoxia, while salicylic, formic, glycolic, and oxalic acids are ingested toxins rather than products of diabetic metabolism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ketoacids (beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acid) are the classic DKA fixed acids and drive the high anion gap seen on labs. COMLEX: distinguishing ketoacidosis from the toxic-ingestion causes of high anion gap acidosis is a frequently tested differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"The pK of the HPO4-2/H2PO4- buffer pair is 6.8. Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, what is the approximate ratio of the base form (HPO4-2) to the acid form (H2PO4-) at a blood pH of 7.4?","choices":{"A":"About 4 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-","B":"About 1 to 1, with equal concentrations of both forms","C":"About 1 to 4, with H2PO4- predominating over HPO4-2","D":"About 2 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-","E":"About 10 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Applying pH = pK + log([A-]/[HA]) with pH 7.4 and pK 6.8 gives 0.6 = log(HPO4-2/H2PO4-), so HPO4-2/H2PO4- = 10^0.6 ≈ 3.98, or approximately 4 to 1. This demonstrates that at pH 7.4 the base form of the phosphate buffer predominates over the acid form by roughly fourfold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: be able to run this exact Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation — it is a classic worked \"sample problem\" format. COMLEX: recognize that when pH equals a buffer’s pK, the two forms are present in equal (1:1) concentration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — Henderson-Hasselbalch application to respiratory acid-base disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation with a pK of 6.1 for the HCO3-/CO2 buffer, a plasma [HCO3-] of 24 mmol/L, and a Pco2 of 40 mm Hg (CO2 solubility 0.03 mmol/L per mm Hg), what is the calculated arterial pH?","choices":{"A":"7.1","B":"6.8","C":"7.4","D":"7.7","E":"6.1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"pH = pK + log([HCO3-]/(0.03 x Pco2)) = 6.1 + log(24/(0.03 x 40)) = 6.1 + log(24/1.2) = 6.1 + log20 = 6.1 + 1.3 = 7.4. This is the standard calculation used to derive the normal arterial pH from normal HCO3- and Pco2 values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this exact calculation (pK 6.1, HCO3- 24, Pco2 40 -> pH 7.4) is a foundational worked example — know how to substitute values into the equation under time pressure. COMLEX: this equation underlies ABG interpretation on nearly every acid-base vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — Henderson-Hasselbalch application to respiratory acid-base disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"On the acid-base map, isohydric lines connect combinations of Pco2 and [HCO3-] that yield the same pH. Which combination below would also yield a normal arterial pH of 7.4, despite both values being abnormal?","choices":{"A":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L","B":"Pco2 of 40 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 36 mEq/L","C":"Pco2 of 20 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L","D":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 12 mEq/L","E":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 36 mEq/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Substituting Pco2 60 and HCO3- 36 into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(36/(0.03x60)) = 6.1 + log(36/1.8) = 6.1 + log20 = 7.4, matching the normal value despite both variables being elevated. None of the other listed combinations satisfy the equation to yield pH 7.4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the classic teaching point behind chronic compensated respiratory acidosis — a markedly elevated Pco2 paired with a proportionally elevated HCO3- can still produce a normal or near-normal pH. COMLEX: always check whether the compensation is proportionate, not just whether pH looks normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — Henderson-Hasselbalch application to respiratory acid-base disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"A buffer’s titration curve is sigmoidal in shape. Within which pH range around its pK is the buffer most effective at resisting a change in pH?","choices":{"A":"Within 0.1 pH unit above or below the pK","B":"Precisely at the pK value, corresponding to a single specific pH","C":"Within 1.0 pH unit above or below the pK","D":"Within 2.0 to 3.0 pH units above or below the pK","E":"Buffering effectiveness is determined primarily by the total buffer concentration present"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The linear portion of a buffer’s sigmoidal titration curve, where small amounts of added or removed H+ produce only small pH changes, extends from about 1.0 pH unit below the pK to 1.0 pH unit above it. Outside this pK ± 1.0 range, the curve flattens and the same amount of H+ produces a much larger pH change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pK ± 1.0 rule defines the pH range over which a buffer is most effective, and phosphate (pK 6.8) sits inside it at pH 7.4; bicarbonate (pK 6.1) lies just outside it but is a major buffer because of its high concentration and its open system with CO2. COMLEX: apply the same rule when evaluating any buffer system in a lab or clinical context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Although the pK of the HPO4-2/H2PO4- buffer (6.8) is closer to blood pH (7.4) than the pK of the HCO3-/CO2 buffer (6.1), the HCO3-/CO2 system remains the more important extracellular buffer. Which two features explain this?","choices":{"A":"Phosphate has a much lower binding affinity for H+ than bicarbonate does, and CO2 is nonvolatile","B":"HCO3- circulates at a much higher concentration than phosphate, and its CO2 form is volatile","C":"Bicarbonate functions as an intracellular buffer, and phosphate cannot cross cell membranes","D":"The phosphate titration curve rises in a straight linear pattern across the full pH range","E":"Bicarbonate’s pK rises substantially with any increase in body temperature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCO3- circulates at about 24 mmol/L, far exceeding plasma phosphate’s 1-2 mmol/L, and its acid form (CO2) is volatile and continuously expired by the lungs, making the system an open one that can be regenerated rather than depleted. These two features outweigh phosphate’s theoretically more favorable pK for buffering at pH 7.4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: remember both reasons — concentration AND volatility — since USMLE distractors often supply only one to make an answer seem incomplete. COMLEX: this open-system property is exactly why bicarbonate buffering is reinforced by respiratory compensation, unlike phosphate buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"The imidazole group of histidine residues on intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin has a pK in which range, making it well suited for physiologic buffering?","choices":{"A":"3.5 to 4.5","B":"5.0 to 5.5","C":"9.0 to 10.0","D":"6.4 to 7.0","E":"1.0 to 2.0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The imidazole group of histidine has a pK of about 6.4 to 7.0, which lies within 1.0 pH unit of the physiologic pH of 7.4 and therefore falls in the effective buffering range. The alpha-amino groups on proteins have a somewhat higher pK of 7.4 to 7.9, also within the effective range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: histidine’s imidazole pK is why hemoglobin (rich in histidine) is such an effective intracellular buffer — this links directly to the deoxyhemoglobin buffering concept covered in this objective. COMLEX: know that not all amino acid side chains buffer equally well; the pK relative to 7.4 is what matters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"Deoxygenated hemoglobin (deoxyhemoglobin) is a more effective intracellular buffer than oxygenated hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin). Which pK values account for this difference?","choices":{"A":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 7.9, while deoxyhemoglobin has a lower pK of 6.7","B":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.7, while deoxyhemoglobin has a higher pK of 7.9","C":"Both forms share an identical pK of 6.7, but deoxyhemoglobin contains more histidine residues","D":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.1, matching that of the bicarbonate buffer system","E":"Deoxyhemoglobin’s superior buffering results from a pK of 9.5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.7, which is within the effective physiologic buffering range, but deoxyhemoglobin has a higher pK of 7.9, making it an even more effective buffer at physiologic pH. Both values come from conformational changes in hemoglobin’s histidine residues upon O2 release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the two pK values (oxyHb 6.7, deoxyHb 7.9) — this pairing is tested directly and also underlies why venous blood buffers its added CO2 so effectively. COMLEX: connect this to the timing of O2 release in the tissues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — transcellular H+-K+ exchange as an intracellular buffering-adjacent mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"As blood flows through systemic capillaries, which sequence of events allows hemoglobin to buffer the H+ generated from tissue-derived CO2 particularly effectively?","choices":{"A":"Oxyhemoglobin becomes deoxyhemoglobin as CO2 entering the cell generates H+, and deoxyhemoglobin buffers more effectively","B":"Deoxyhemoglobin is converted to oxyhemoglobin as CO2 enters the red blood cell, which coincides with the more effective buffering form","C":"Histidine residues in hemoglobin maintain a fixed pK value regardless of oxygenation state","D":"CO2 entering the red blood cell converts hemoglobin’s histidine residues to the imidazolium form independent of O2 binding","E":"Oxyhemoglobin buffers H+ more effectively than deoxyhemoglobin, which is why tissue O2 delivery enhances overall buffering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In systemic capillaries, hemoglobin releases O2 to the tissues and becomes deoxyhemoglobin at the same moment that CO2 diffusing into the red blood cell is converted to H2CO3 and then H+ and HCO3-. This timing is fortunate because deoxyhemoglobin, with its higher pK of 7.9, is the more effective buffering form, so the H+ is buffered just as it is generated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this coincidence of timing (O2 unloading and CO2 loading happening together) is exactly what keeps venous pH from falling much below arterial pH. COMLEX: this is a classic integrative physiology point linking respiratory gas exchange to acid-base buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — transcellular H+-K+ exchange as an intracellular buffering-adjacent mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"In respiratory alkalosis, plasma protein binding shifts in a way that lowers free ionized calcium. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"An excess of free H+ in alkalemia displaces Ca2+ from plasma proteins, increasing free ionized Ca2+","B":"Alkalemia directly precipitates calcium phosphate salts, thereby removing Ca2+ from the circulation","C":"Alkalemia increases parathyroid hormone secretion, which lowers free Ca2+ by promoting bone deposition","D":"A deficit of H+ in alkalemia lets more Ca2+ bind plasma proteins, decreasing free Ca2+","E":"Alkalemia has no effect on Ca2+-protein binding, since that binding is regulated by vitamin D"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Negatively charged groups on plasma proteins such as albumin can bind either H+ or Ca2+. In alkalemia, there is a deficit of H+ in the blood, so less H+ is bound to plasma proteins and more Ca2+ is bound instead, lowering free ionized Ca2+ concentration and producing the hypocalcemia-like symptoms seen with hyperventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this H+/Ca2+ competition for albumin binding sites explains the perioral tingling and tetany classically seen with acute hyperventilation, even though total serum calcium is unchanged. COMLEX: distinguish this functional (ionized) hypocalcemia from true hypocalcemia — total calcium is normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — transcellular H+-K+ exchange as an intracellular buffering-adjacent mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with severe, prolonged vomiting develops metabolic alkalosis, and her arterial pH rises from 7.4 to 7.6. Which of the following best approximates the resulting change in her plasma [H+] concentration?","choices":{"A":"A decrease of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 17 nEq/L","B":"A decrease of approximately 20 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 20 nEq/L","C":"An increase of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 55 nEq/L","D":"No meaningful change in [H+], since the pH remains within a range compatible with life","E":"A decrease of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 25 nEq/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (0.2 pH units in the alkaline direction) corresponds to a decrease in [H+] of about 15 nEq/L, from the baseline 40 nEq/L down to approximately 25 nEq/L. This is a smaller absolute change than the same-magnitude shift in the acidic direction would produce.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know both anchor values (pH 7.6 -> ~25 nEq/L and pH 7.2 -> ~63 nEq/L) so you can quickly estimate [H+] from either direction of pH change on an ABG.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — the logarithmic pH-[H+] relationship applied to metabolic alkalosis"},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe secretory diarrhea develops metabolic acidosis, and his arterial pH falls from 7.4 to 7.2. Which of the following best approximates the resulting change in his plasma [H+] concentration?","choices":{"A":"An increase of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 55 nEq/L","B":"An increase of approximately 30 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 70 nEq/L","C":"A decrease of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 17 nEq/L","D":"An increase of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 63 nEq/L","E":"No meaningful change in [H+], since a pH of 7.2 remains within the range compatible with life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A decrease in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 (0.2 pH units in the acidic direction) corresponds to an increase in [H+] of about 23 nEq/L, from the baseline 40 nEq/L up to approximately 63 nEq/L. This is a larger absolute change than the same-magnitude shift in the alkaline direction would produce.","boardPrep":"Step 1: for the same 0.2 pH change, the acidic shift produces the larger absolute change in [H+] (23 versus 15 nEq/L), a frequent testing point in interpreting an ABG pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — the logarithmic pH-[H+] relationship applied to metabolic acidosis"},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients each have a 0.2-unit change in arterial pH from the normal value of 7.4: patient A’s pH falls to 7.2, and patient B’s pH rises to 7.6. Which statement correctly compares the absolute changes in their plasma [H+] concentrations?","choices":{"A":"Patient B’s [H+] change is somewhat larger in absolute magnitude than patient A’s [H+] change","B":"Both patients have an identical absolute change in [H+] of approximately 19 nEq/L each","C":"Patient A’s [H+] change is larger in absolute magnitude than patient B’s, about 23 vs. 15 nEq/L","D":"Patient A’s [H+] decreases while patient B’s [H+] increases, but the two changes are equal in magnitude","E":"The absolute [H+] change cannot be compared without knowing each patient’s Pco2 and HCO3- values"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the pH-[H+] relationship is logarithmic, the same 0.2-unit pH shift produces a larger absolute [H+] change in the acidic direction (patient A, +23 nEq/L) than in the alkaline direction (patient B, -15 nEq/L), even though the pH shift itself is identical in magnitude for both patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this comparison format (equal pH shift, unequal [H+] shift) is a favorite way boards test whether students truly understand the logarithmic nature of pH, rather than just memorizing the normal range.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — comparing acidic-direction and alkaline-direction pH shifts of equal magnitude"},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents with polyuria, Kussmaul breathing, and a fruity breath odor after several days of missed insulin doses. Her serum bicarbonate is markedly low. Which category of acid is primarily responsible for her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Volatile acid, from CO2 retention secondary to hypoventilation of her lungs","B":"Fixed acid, from excess beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acid production","C":"Fixed acid, from excess catabolism of sulfur-containing amino acids in her diet","D":"Volatile acid, from decreased pulmonary CO2 excretion during her illness","E":"Fixed acid, from ingestion of salicylic acid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, insulin deficiency drives excessive production of the ketoacids beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid, both fixed (nonvolatile) acids that consume extracellular bicarbonate during buffering and must ultimately be excreted by the kidneys. Kussmaul breathing reflects hyperventilation (a respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis), not a primary volatile-acid problem, and her presentation is unrelated to protein catabolism or salicylate ingestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA is the prototypical fixed-acid, high anion gap metabolic acidosis — do not confuse the compensatory Kussmaul hyperventilation with a primary respiratory acid-base disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — fixed ketoacid production in diabetic ketoacidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department several hours after intentionally ingesting antifreeze. He is found to have a severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis with calcium oxalate crystals in his urine. Which fixed acid or acids are most likely responsible for his acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Formic acid, generated directly from methanol metabolism inside his liver cells","B":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid, generated from diabetic ketoacidosis","C":"Lactic acid, generated from severe tissue hypoxia during his illness","D":"Glycolic acid and oxalic acid, generated from ethylene glycol metabolism","E":"Sulfuric acid, generated from sulfur-amino-acid catabolism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antifreeze ingestion is a classic source of ethylene glycol, which is metabolized to the fixed acids glycolic acid and oxalic acid; oxalic acid combines with calcium to form the calcium oxalate crystals characteristically seen in the urine. Formic acid instead comes from methanol ingestion, and the remaining choices are unrelated to this specific poisoning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair the toxin with its fixed acid and clinical clue — ethylene glycol -> oxalic/glycolic acid -> calcium oxalate crystalluria; methanol -> formic acid -> visual disturbances/optic nerve injury. COMLEX: both are classic high anion gap, high osmolar gap toxic ingestions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — ingested fixed acids from ethylene glycol metabolism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with severe COPD and chronic CO2 retention has a compensated respiratory acidosis with an elevated Pco2 and a compensatorily elevated [HCO3-]. Which statement correctly characterizes the acid responsible for his primary acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"It is a volatile acid that is normally eliminated by the lungs","B":"It is a fixed acid derived from sulfur-containing amino acid catabolism in his liver","C":"It is a fixed acid that must be excreted as titratable acid or ammonium by his kidneys","D":"It is produced at a fixed rate of about 50 mmol per day under normal conditions","E":"It is buffered permanently by extracellular bicarbonate without contributing to any pulmonary elimination pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acid underlying his primary disturbance is CO2, the volatile acid, which is normally regenerated from H2CO3 and expired by the lungs rather than requiring renal buffering and excretion; his chronic CO2 retention is a failure of this pulmonary elimination pathway. Fixed acids like sulfuric acid, and the ~50 mmol/day baseline fixed-acid load, are unrelated to his primary respiratory problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic CO2 retention in COPD is a volatile-acid (respiratory) problem, compensated by renal HCO3- retention — do not mistake the compensatory renal response for the primary disturbance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — chronic volatile-acid retention in compensated respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"12 mmol/L of HCl is added to a volume of extracellular fluid with an initial [HCO3-] of 24 mmol/L and a Pco2 of 40 mm Hg (CO2 concentration 1.2 mmol/L). Assuming the additional CO2 generated by this buffering reaction cannot be expired by the lungs, what is the resulting pH (pK for HCO3-/CO2 = 6.1)?","choices":{"A":"About 7.32","B":"About 6.7","C":"About 5.5","D":"About 6.06","E":"About 7.1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adding 12 mmol/L of H+ converts 12 mmol/L of the original 24 mmol/L HCO3- into 12 mmol/L of new CO2, leaving [HCO3-] at 12 mmol/L and raising the CO2 concentration to 1.2 + 12 = 13.2 mmol/L. Substituting into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(12/13.2) = 6.1 - 0.04 = 6.06, a value that would be fatal without further compensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this worked example demonstrates why buffering alone is insufficient without respiratory compensation — adding respiratory compensation restores the pH to about 7.32.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation of pH before respiratory compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"After buffering an acute HCl load, a patient's [HCO3-] is 12 mmol/L. Respiratory compensation then drives the Pco2 down to 24 mm Hg (CO2 concentration 0.72 mmol/L). What is the calculated pH (pK 6.1)?","choices":{"A":"About 6.06","B":"About 7.4","C":"About 6.8","D":"About 7.6","E":"About 7.32"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Substituting the compensated values into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(12/0.72) = 6.1 + log(16.7) = 6.1 + 1.22 = 7.32. This is nearly normal despite both [HCO3-] and Pco2 being far below their normal values, illustrating how hyperventilation (respiratory compensation) protects pH after an acute fixed-acid load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: buffering alone (pH 6.06) is fatal, but adding respiratory compensation (pH 7.32) restores a survivable pH even though full correction still requires the kidneys to regenerate the consumed HCO3-.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation of pH after respiratory compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with severe, long-standing COPD has a chronic Pco2 of 60 mm Hg and a compensatorily elevated [HCO3-] of 36 mEq/L. His arterial pH is nearly normal. Which principle best explains why his pH is not markedly abnormal despite both values being far from their normal set points?","choices":{"A":"Multiple Pco2/[HCO3-] pairs satisfy the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, yielding the same pH","B":"His kidneys have reabsorbed filtered bicarbonate at a rate independent of his elevated Pco2","C":"The pK of the HCO3-/CO2 buffer decreases as Pco2 rises, falling to a value near 5.5","D":"His hemoglobin has fully converted to the oxyhemoglobin form, maximizing its buffering capacity","E":"The additional CO2 is entirely converted to lactic acid within his red blood cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because pH depends on the ratio of [HCO3-] to Pco2 rather than on their absolute values, a proportionate rise in both (here, [HCO3-] rising from 24 to 36 as Pco2 rises from 40 to 60) can preserve a near-normal pH, exactly as predicted by the isohydric lines on the acid-base map. This chronic renal compensation is distinct from the buffer’s pK, hemoglobin oxygenation state, or lactic acid production, none of which are the mechanism here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the isohydric-line illustration of compensated respiratory acidosis; real chronic compensation is usually somewhat less complete, so a bicarbonate this high can also signal a coexisting metabolic alkalosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — isohydric principle applied to chronic compensated respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A weak acid buffer has a pK of 5.5. Based on the principle that effective physiologic buffering occurs within 1.0 pH unit of a buffer’s pK, would this buffer contribute meaningfully to buffering at a blood pH of 7.4?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because pH 7.4 lies within its effective buffering range of 4.5 to 6.5","B":"No, because pH 7.4 lies outside its effective buffering range of 4.5 to 6.5","C":"Yes, because weak acid buffers are generally effective across the physiologic pH range","D":"No, because its effective range is 6.5 to 8.5, and 7.4 falls just outside it","E":"Yes, because a pK of 5.5 is closer to 7.4 than the pK of the bicarbonate buffer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The effective buffering range of a buffer with pK 5.5 extends from 4.5 to 6.5 (pK ± 1.0). Since 7.4 lies well outside this range, this buffer would contribute little to buffering blood at physiologic pH unless it were present at a very high concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: apply the pK ± 1.0 rule mechanically to any given pK value — this is a common way boards test the concept with an unfamiliar or hypothetical buffer rather than bicarbonate or phosphate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why organic phosphates such as ATP, ADP, and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate serve as effective intracellular buffers?","choices":{"A":"Their pKs range from about 6.0 to 7.5, which lies close to the physiologic pH range","B":"Their pKs range from 9.0 to 10.0, providing buffering at markedly alkaline pH","C":"They exist in a fully ionized, non-buffering form at physiologic pH","D":"They function as buffers after first converting entirely to inorganic phosphate","E":"Their intracellular concentration is too low to contribute meaningfully to buffering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organic phosphates such as ATP, ADP, AMP, glucose-1-phosphate, and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate have pKs ranging from about 6.0 to 7.5, placing them well within the pK ± 1.0 range needed for effective buffering at physiologic intracellular pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organic phosphates are a less commonly emphasized but real intracellular buffer class — know they buffer via the same pK-proximity principle as bicarbonate and phosphate, just intracellularly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A solution held at pH 7.4 contains two buffers at similar concentrations: one with a pK of 7.0, and another with a pK of 8.6. Which buffer would be more effective at this pH, and why?","choices":{"A":"The pK 8.6 buffer, because a higher pK confers superior buffering capacity regardless of solution pH","B":"Both buffers are equally effective, since effectiveness does not depend on the relationship between pH and pK","C":"The buffer with pK 7.0, because it lies within 1.0 pH unit of 7.4, while the pK 8.6 buffer does not","D":"The pK 8.6 buffer, because its titration curve is linear across the entire pH scale","E":"Neither buffer is effective, since neither pK exactly equals 7.4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The buffer with pK 7.0 is only 0.4 pH units from 7.4, placing it well within the pK ± 1.0 effective buffering range, while the pK 8.6 buffer is 1.2 pH units away, placing it just outside that range. The pK 7.0 buffer would therefore be the more effective one at pH 7.4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distance from the solution pH to the pK, not the absolute value of the pK, determines buffering effectiveness — a common trap is assuming a \"higher\" or \"lower\" pK is inherently better or worse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman having an acute panic attack hyperventilates and develops perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. Arterial blood gas shows a pH of 7.55. Which mechanism best explains her neuromuscular symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Increased free H+ displaces Ca2+ from plasma proteins, raising free ionized Ca2+ and increasing excitability","B":"Decreased free H+ lets more Ca2+ bind plasma proteins, lowering ionized Ca2+ and raising excitability","C":"Hyperventilation directly increases her renal Ca2+ excretion, producing hypocalcemia within minutes","D":"Respiratory alkalosis triggers a rapid decrease in bone calcium release independent of parathyroid hormone","E":"The low Pco2 directly binds free Ca2+ in her plasma, removing it from the ionized pool"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her acute hyperventilation produces respiratory alkalosis (pH 7.55); the resulting deficit of free H+ allows more Ca2+ to bind to plasma proteins such as albumin, lowering free ionized Ca2+ and making the threshold potential of nerve and muscle more negative, which increases excitability and produces perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. This is a functional, protein-binding-related hypocalcemia rather than a true loss of total body calcium through the kidneys or bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the classic mechanism behind acute hyperventilation-induced tetany — a rapid, reversible drop in ionized (not total) calcium from H+/Ca2+ competition for albumin binding sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — H+/Ca2+ plasma protein binding competition in acute respiratory alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a prolonged generalized seizure. His lactic acid level is markedly elevated. Which mechanism allows the H+ generated by this fixed acid to enter cells for intracellular buffering?","choices":{"A":"H+ freely diffuses into the cell unaccompanied, with K+ subsequently exiting to preserve electroneutrality","B":"Lactate is first converted to CO2, which then crosses the cell membrane independent of H+","C":"H+ enters the cell together with the lactate anion, preserving electroneutrality","D":"H+ exchanges directly for intracellular Na+ across the plasma membrane","E":"Lactic acid cannot enter cells and is instead buffered by extracellular bicarbonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When excess fixed acid is accompanied by an organic anion such as lactate, H+ and the anion enter the cell together, preserving electroneutrality without requiring an exchange for another intracellular cation. This differs from the H+/K+ exchange mechanism used when fixed acid accumulates without an accompanying organic anion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: contrast this organic-anion cotransport mechanism with the H+/K+ exchange mechanism used for acids like HCl — the presence of an organic anion (lactate, ketoacids) is why organic acidoses cause less hyperkalemia than inorganic mineral acidoses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — intracellular buffering of fixed acid accompanied by an organic anion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"Venous blood, which has picked up large amounts of CO2 from the tissues, has a pH of 7.37 — only 0.03 pH units more acidic than arterial blood (pH 7.4). Which of the following best explains why the pH decrease is so small despite the substantial CO2 addition?","choices":{"A":"Venous blood’s [H+] remains completely unchanged following the CO2 load delivered from the tissues","B":"The added CO2 is expired before it can be converted to H+ and HCO3-","C":"Venous blood’s lower temperature increases the solubility of CO2, preventing H+ generation","D":"Bicarbonate concentration in venous blood falls to zero, eliminating any further buffering need","E":"The H+ from the added CO2 is buffered by hemoglobin, now in its deoxygenated form"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As CO2 is added to systemic capillary blood, it is converted to H+ and HCO3-, but this occurs at the same time hemoglobin releases O2 and converts to deoxyhemoglobin, its more effective buffering form (pK 7.9 versus 6.7 for oxyhemoglobin). This fortunate timing buffers the added H+ so effectively that venous pH falls only 0.03 units below arterial pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this small arterial-to-venous pH difference (7.4 to 7.37) is a quantitative summary of the entire hemoglobin-buffering concept in this objective — know the actual numbers, not just the qualitative direction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — deoxyhemoglobin buffering explains the small arteriovenous pH difference","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Renal Acid-Base Mechanics: HCO3- Reabsorption & Titratable Acid","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Reabsorption of filtered HCO3- in the early proximal tubule begins with H+ secretion into the tubular lumen by which transporter?","choices":{"A":"The H+-ATPase on the luminal membrane","B":"The Na+-HCO3- cotransporter on the basolateral membrane","C":"The Cl--HCO3- exchanger on the basolateral membrane","D":"The H+-K+-ATPase on the luminal membrane","E":"The Na+-H+ exchanger NHE3 on the luminal membrane"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The luminal membrane of the early proximal tubule contains the Na+-H+ exchanger (NHE3), a secondary active transporter that moves Na+ into the cell down its electrochemical gradient while secreting H+ into the lumen against its gradient, and NHE3 accounts for most proximal H+ secretion. The H+-ATPase and H+-K+-ATPase are instead the main H+ secretors of collecting duct alpha-intercalated cells, and the basolateral transporters listed are responsible for HCO3- exit from the cell, not H+ entry into the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NHE3 is the workhorse of proximal HCO3- reclamation, and impaired proximal HCO3- reabsorption underlies proximal (Type 2) RTA. COMLEX: this transporter is distinct from the aldosterone-stimulated H+-ATPase of the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — proximal tubule glutamine metabolism also generates new HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"With a GFR of 180 L/day and a plasma [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L, the filtered load of HCO3- is 4320 mEq/day. If urinary HCO3- excretion is only 2 mEq/day, approximately what percentage of the filtered HCO3- is reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 95%","B":"Approximately 90%","C":"Approximately 80%","D":"More than 99.9%","E":"Approximately 50%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reabsorption is calculated as filtered load minus excretion: 4320 - 2 = 4318 mEq/day reabsorbed, which is 4318/4320 ≈ 99.95%, or more than 99.9% of the filtered load. This near-complete reabsorption ensures that the major extracellular buffer is conserved rather than lost in the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know this exact worked calculation cold — it is the standard way boards illustrate that HCO3- reabsorption, unlike many other filtered solutes, is essentially complete under normal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — proximal tubule glutamine metabolism also generates new HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, above approximately what plasma [HCO3-] concentration does the filtered load of HCO3- exceed the proximal tubule’s reabsorptive capacity, causing HCO3- to appear in the urine?","choices":{"A":"24 mEq/L","B":"30 mEq/L","C":"40 mEq/L","D":"55 mEq/L","E":"15 mEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When plasma [HCO3-] rises above about 40 mEq/L, the filtered load becomes high enough to saturate the proximal reabsorption mechanism, and any additional filtered HCO3- is excreted in the urine. This is the mechanism by which the kidney can correct metabolic alkalosis: as plasma HCO3- rises, filtered load eventually exceeds reabsorptive capacity and the excess HCO3- is excreted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this saturation threshold explains why severe metabolic alkalosis is partially self-limiting once plasma HCO3- climbs high enough. COMLEX: relevant to understanding why correcting the underlying cause (e.g., volume contraction) is still required for full resolution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — proximal tubule glutamine metabolism also generates new HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Excretion of H+ as titratable acid occurs primarily in which cell type, and which hormone stimulates the responsible luminal transporter?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-intercalated cells of the distal tubule and collecting duct, stimulated by aldosterone","B":"Principal cells of the collecting duct, stimulated directly by aldosterone","C":"Alpha-intercalated cells found within the proximal tubule, stimulated primarily by angiotensin II","D":"Beta-intercalated cells of the collecting duct, stimulated similarly by aldosterone","E":"Alpha-intercalated cells located specifically in the late distal tubule, stimulated by ADH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Titratable acid is excreted primarily by alpha-intercalated cells of the late distal tubule and collecting ducts, where aldosterone stimulates the luminal H+-ATPase (in addition to its actions on principal cells). Principal cells handle Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion rather than H+ secretion, and alpha-intercalated cells are not present in the proximal tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone has a dual renal action — stimulating principal cell Na+/K+ handling AND alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion — a frequently tested integrative point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"The H+ secreted into the collecting duct lumen by the H+-ATPase is generated inside the alpha-intercalated cell from which reaction?","choices":{"A":"Glutamine metabolism by mitochondrial glutaminase","B":"CO2 and H2O forming H2CO3 that dissociates into H+ and HCO3-","C":"Ammonia diffusing from the medullary interstitium into the cell","D":"Direct transport of H+ from the peritubular capillary blood","E":"Dissociation of filtered titratable acid within the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inside the alpha-intercalated cell, CO2 and H2O combine (via intracellular carbonic anhydrase) to form H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ (secreted into the lumen by the H+-ATPase) and HCO3- (reabsorbed into the blood via basolateral Cl--HCO3- exchange). Glutamine metabolism and ammonia handling instead underlie the separate NH4+ excretion pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the same CO2/H2O -> H2CO3 -> H+ + HCO3- reaction used in the proximal tubule, just paired with different luminal (H+-ATPase vs. NHE3) and basolateral (Cl--HCO3- exchange vs. Na+-HCO3- cotransport) transporters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"For each H+ excreted as titratable acid in the collecting duct, what happens to systemic HCO3- stores?","choices":{"A":"One HCO3- is filtered and excreted alongside the titratable acid","B":"HCO3- stores decrease further, since titratable acid excretion consumes additional HCO3-","C":"HCO3- stores remain unchanged, since titratable acid excretion does not involve HCO3-","D":"One new HCO3- is synthesized within the cell and reabsorbed into the blood","E":"Two new HCO3- molecules are synthesized per titratable acid molecule excreted"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For each H+ excreted as titratable acid, one new HCO3- is synthesized within the alpha-intercalated cell and reabsorbed into the blood, replenishing the HCO3- stores that were previously depleted while buffering fixed acid. This 1:1 relationship, not 2:1 or 0:1, is what allows ongoing titratable acid and NH4+ excretion to fully restore ECF HCO3- over time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 1:1 ratio of titratable acid H+ excreted to new HCO3- generated — this is what makes the kidney capable of fully replacing bicarbonate consumed by fixed-acid buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"The minimum achievable urine pH is 4.4. Approximately how many times higher is the [H+] concentration in this maximally acidified urine compared with blood at pH 7.4?","choices":{"A":"1000-fold higher","B":"10-fold higher","C":"100-fold higher","D":"10,000-fold higher","E":"3-fold higher"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A urine pH of 4.4 versus a blood pH of 7.4 represents a difference of 3.0 pH units, and because pH is a negative log10 scale, each whole pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in [H+]; 3 pH units therefore corresponds to a 10^3, or 1000-fold, higher [H+] concentration in the urine. This is the largest concentration gradient against which the H+-ATPase can secrete H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1000-fold figure is a direct application of the logarithmic pH scale — a good check that you understand both the acid-base foundations objective and this urine pH barrier concept together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients both have a urine pH of 4.4, but patient A has abundant urinary phosphate while patient B has very little. Which statement correctly compares their total H+ excretion as titratable acid?","choices":{"A":"Both patients excrete an identical amount of H+ as titratable acid, since their urine pH is the same","B":"Patient B excretes more H+ as titratable acid, since less buffer allows the lower pH to be reached faster","C":"Patient A excretes much more H+ as titratable acid, since he has more buffer available","D":"Neither patient excretes any H+ as titratable acid once urine pH reaches 4.4","E":"Patient A excretes less H+ as titratable acid, since more buffer slows the rate of H+ secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid depends on the amount of urinary buffer available, not on the final urine pH value itself. Patient A, with abundant phosphate buffer, can have large quantities of H+ secreted and buffered before urine pH falls to 4.4, while patient B, with little buffer, reaches the same pH after excreting far less total H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this distinction between the AMOUNT of H+ excreted and the urine pH VALUE is a classic conceptual trap — urine pH alone never tells you total acid excretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"The phosphate buffer (pK 6.8) has a titration curve with a linear portion extending from about pH 5.8 to 7.8. Within this linear range, what happens to tubular fluid pH as H+ is progressively secreted into it?","choices":{"A":"The pH decreases precipitously with even small amounts of secreted H+","B":"The pH falls gradually as the added H+ is buffered","C":"The pH remains completely constant regardless of how much H+ is secreted","D":"The pH increases, since H+ secretion converts H2PO4- back to HPO4-2","E":"The pH immediately falls to 4.4 as soon as H+ secretion begins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Within the linear portion of the phosphate titration curve (pH 5.8 to 7.8), added H+ converts HPO4-2 to H2PO4- while producing only a modest decrease in pH; only after most of the HPO4-2 has been converted does further H+ secretion cause the pH to fall precipitously toward the 4.4 minimum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the direct clinical application of the general titration-curve/pK ± 1.0 rule to phosphate specifically inside the nephron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with heart failure is treated with a loop diuretic and develops mild metabolic alkalosis. Which mechanism, triggered by her ECF volume contraction, directly increases proximal HCO3- reabsorption and contributes to this contraction alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone stimulation of H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Increased filtered load of HCO3- from a rising plasma concentration","C":"Decreased Pco2 reducing the intracellular CO2 supply for H+ generation","D":"Angiotensin II stimulation of the luminal Na+-H+ exchanger","E":"Direct inhibition of carbonic anhydrase by the loop diuretic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ECF volume contraction activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system, and angiotensin II directly stimulates the proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchanger, thereby increasing HCO3- reabsorption and raising blood HCO3- concentration — the mechanism underlying contraction alkalosis from loop or thiazide diuretics or vomiting. The alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase and Pco2-driven CO2 supply are separate mechanisms not primarily responsible for this diuretic-triggered effect, and loop diuretics do not act by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"contraction alkalosis\" is a named, testable entity — know both mechanisms (Starling-force-driven proximal reabsorption AND angiotensin II-driven NHE3 stimulation) and that it is corrected with isotonic NaCl.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — angiotensin II-driven proximal HCO3- reabsorption in contraction alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic CO2 retention and a compensatorily elevated plasma [HCO3-]. Which mechanism directly links his elevated Pco2 to increased renal HCO3- reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Increased Pco2 raises the CO2 supply for H+ generation by the Na+-H+ exchanger","B":"Increased Pco2 directly increases the number of Na+-HCO3- cotransporters on the basolateral membrane","C":"Increased Pco2 stimulates aldosterone release to increase proximal H+-ATPase activity","D":"Increased Pco2 raises the filtered load of HCO3- and mechanically increases its reabsorption","E":"Increased Pco2 inhibits angiotensin II release and reduces proximal Na+ reabsorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A chronically elevated Pco2 increases the supply of CO2 available to proximal tubule cells, providing more substrate to generate the H+ secreted by the Na+-H+ exchanger, which in turn increases HCO3- reabsorption and raises plasma [HCO3-] as a compensatory response. This is distinct from any direct change in transporter number, aldosterone-mediated signaling, filtered load, or angiotensin II suppression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this CO2-substrate mechanism is the physiologic basis for chronic renal compensation of respiratory acidosis, which takes days (unlike the immediate respiratory compensation for metabolic disorders).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — Pco2-driven proximal HCO3- reabsorption as renal compensation for chronic respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A patient is given acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, to treat altitude sickness. Which direct effect on proximal tubule HCO3- handling would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Increased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase inhibition preserves intracellular HCO3-","B":"Decreased HCO3- reabsorption, since brush border carbonic anhydrase converts luminal H2CO3 to CO2","C":"No change in HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase is limited to red blood cells","D":"Decreased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase directly transports HCO3- across the basolateral membrane","E":"Increased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase inhibition upregulates the Na+-H+ exchanger"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, including the brush border enzyme needed to convert luminal H2CO3 into CO2 and H2O so that they can diffuse into the proximal tubule cell; blocking this step interferes with HCO3- reabsorption and produces a mild diuresis with bicarbonaturia. Carbonic anhydrase does not transport HCO3- directly and is not confined to red blood cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide’s mechanism (brush border carbonic anhydrase inhibition -> decreased proximal HCO3- reabsorption) is a classic pharmacology-physiology integration question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — carbonic anhydrase inhibition blocking proximal HCO3- reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) has excess aldosterone secretion. Which acid-base disturbance would be expected from its effect on alpha-intercalated cells?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic acidosis, from aldosterone-induced suppression of the H+-ATPase","B":"Metabolic alkalosis, from aldosterone-induced inhibition of proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption","C":"Metabolic alkalosis, from aldosterone-stimulated overactivity of the luminal H+-ATPase","D":"Respiratory alkalosis, from aldosterone-stimulated hyperventilation","E":"No acid-base disturbance, since aldosterone action is restricted to principal cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess aldosterone in Conn syndrome overstimulates the H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells, increasing H+ secretion (and new HCO3- generation) beyond what is needed, producing metabolic alkalosis. Aldosterone does not suppress the H+-ATPase or inhibit proximal HCO3- reabsorption, has no direct respiratory effect, and acts on alpha-intercalated cells in addition to principal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism classically presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, AND metabolic alkalosis — this alpha-intercalated cell mechanism explains the alkalosis component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — aldosterone excess driving alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase overactivity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic CO2 retention. Which change in alpha-intercalated cell function contributes to his renal compensation?","choices":{"A":"Decreased Pco2 reduces intracellular CO2 supply, decreasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","B":"Increased Pco2 directly stimulates aldosterone release, entirely independent of CO2 supply","C":"Increased Pco2 inhibits H+-ATPase activity, substantially reducing titratable acid excretion","D":"Increased Pco2 raises intracellular CO2 supply, increasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","E":"Increased Pco2 has essentially no effect on alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In respiratory acidosis, the elevated Pco2 increases the CO2 supply available within alpha-intercalated cells, increasing H+ secretion and the accompanying synthesis and reabsorption of new HCO3-, which contributes to the compensatory rise in plasma [HCO3-]. This mirrors the same CO2-supply mechanism operating in proximal tubule cells for the same disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this parallels the proximal tubule Pco2 mechanism — both nephron segments increase HCO3- generation in response to chronic hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Pco2-driven alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion in renal compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) has aldosterone deficiency. Which effect on his alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion and acid-base status would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Increased H+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic alkalosis","B":"Decreased H+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic acidosis","C":"No change in H+-ATPase activity, since Pco2 is its sole regulator","D":"Decreased H+-K+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic alkalosis","E":"Increased Cl--HCO3- exchange, contributing to metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aldosterone deficiency in Addison disease removes a key stimulus for the alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase, decreasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis and contributing to a hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis (alongside the loss of aldosterone’s effect on principal cell K+ secretion). H+-ATPase activity is not regulated solely by Pco2, and the direction of the acid-base change is acidosis, not alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Addison disease produces hyperkalemia AND metabolic acidosis together — the mirror image of Conn syndrome’s hypokalemia and alkalosis, both explained by the same alpha-intercalated (and principal) cell aldosterone actions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — aldosterone deficiency reducing alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase activity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with severe protein-calorie malnutrition has markedly reduced dietary phosphate intake and a correspondingly low urinary phosphate concentration. Although his alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase function is normal, his capacity to excrete H+ as titratable acid is reduced. Which principle best explains this?","choices":{"A":"H+-ATPase activity is directly proportional to dietary phosphate intake","B":"Malnutrition directly impairs the Na+-H+ exchanger in the proximal tubule","C":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by urinary buffer supply despite intact transporters","D":"Reduced phosphate intake lowers the minimum achievable urine pH below 4.4","E":"Titratable acid excretion depends entirely on plasma aldosterone concentration, not on urinary buffer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Even with fully functional H+-ATPase, titratable acid excretion is limited by the amount of urinary buffer (chiefly phosphate) available to accept secreted H+; with markedly reduced urinary phosphate, urine pH falls to the 4.4 floor after far less total H+ has been buffered and excreted, reducing overall titratable acid excretion despite normal transporter function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is a clinically relevant application of the buffer-amount principle — malnourished or CKD patients have reduced titratable acid capacity even with intact tubular transporters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"Creatinine can also act as a urinary buffer, with a pK of 5.0 compared with phosphate’s pK of 6.8. Which statement correctly compares their effectiveness as urinary buffers, given a starting glomerular filtrate pH of 7.4?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine buffers a larger total amount of H+ before urine pH falls to 4.4, since its pK is closer to 4.4","B":"Both buffers have identical total buffering capacity, since total capacity does not depend on pK","C":"Creatinine cannot function as a urinary buffer at any physiologic pH","D":"Phosphate becomes completely ineffective once urine pH decreases below its pK of 6.8","E":"Phosphate buffers more total H+ before urine pH falls to 4.4, since its pK is within 1 pH unit of 7.4"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the glomerular filtrate starts at pH 7.4, phosphate (pK 6.8) sits within its pK ± 1.0 buffering range at the outset and strongly resists pH change, letting it absorb a large amount of H+ before tubular fluid pH falls to 4.4. Creatinine’s pK of 5.0 is more than 2 pH units from the starting pH of 7.4, well outside its own pK ± 1.0 buffering range, so it has little buffering capacity near pH 7.4 even though most of it starts out in the deprotonated form; its capacity only becomes substantial much later in the titration, closer to pH 5.0. This is a buffering-capacity effect, not simply which fraction of buffer is in the base form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: total buffering capacity depends on how much buffer is present in the correct form relative to the STARTING pH of the fluid, not simply on which pK is numerically closer to the final pH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"In a hypothetical patient with no urinary buffers whatsoever, what would happen as alpha-intercalated cells begin to secrete H+ into the tubular fluid?","choices":{"A":"Urine pH would rapidly fall to 4.4 after just a few H+ ions, halting further net secretion","B":"H+ secretion would continue indefinitely, since the H+-ATPase does not require any buffer to function","C":"Urine pH would remain at 7.4 indefinitely, since H+ cannot be secreted without a buffer present","D":"The kidney would compensate by secreting NH4+ instead, restoring normal titratable acid excretion","E":"Urine pH would gradually decline over several hours before reaching a stable value near 6.0"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without any urinary buffer to accept secreted H+, the first few H+ ions secreted would remain free in solution and rapidly drive the pH down to the 4.4 floor, at which point the H+-ATPase can no longer secrete against the resulting 1000-fold gradient and net H+ secretion ceases. This illustrates why urinary buffers, not transporter capacity alone, determine how much total H+ can be excreted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this thought experiment is exactly how Costanzo’s text itself illustrates the buffer-dependence principle — useful for building intuition before applying it to real clinical scenarios like malnutrition or CKD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Ammonium Excretion, NH3 Synthesis, & Intercalated Cell Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme metabolizes glutamine to glutamate and NH4+ in proximal tubule cells, initiating renal ammonium excretion?","choices":{"A":"Glutamate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells","B":"Carbonic anhydrase, a brush-border enzyme in proximal tubule cells","C":"Glutamine synthetase, a cytosolic enzyme found in hepatocytes","D":"Glutaminase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells","E":"Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mitochondrial enzyme glutaminase metabolizes glutamine to glutamate and NH4+ in proximal tubule cells; the glutamate is then further metabolized to alpha-ketoglutarate and ultimately to CO2, H2O, and new HCO3-. Glutamate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase act downstream of glutaminase rather than initiating the pathway, carbonic anhydrase handles CO2/H2CO3 interconversion rather than glutamine metabolism, and glutamine synthetase runs the reverse hepatic reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutaminase induction is the rate-limiting step for the adaptive rise in ammonium excretion seen in chronic acidosis. COMLEX: know this enzyme by name; its activity is enhanced by acidosis and hypokalemia and suppressed by hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"In proximal tubule cells, what happens to the NH4+ generated by glutamine metabolism before it appears in the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"It equilibrates with NH3 and H+, and lipid-soluble NH3 diffuses into the lumen while H+ is secreted separately","B":"It is transported intact across the luminal membrane by a dedicated NH4+-specific channel protein","C":"It equilibrates with NH3 and H+, and water-soluble H+ diffuses into the lumen while NH3 is secreted separately","D":"It is reabsorbed directly across the basolateral membrane before ever reaching the tubular lumen","E":"It combines with secreted HCO3- to form ammonium bicarbonate, which is excreted as one unit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intracellular NH4+ is in equilibrium with NH3 and H+; the lipid-soluble NH3 diffuses down its concentration gradient into the lumen, while H+ is secreted separately by the Na+-H+ exchanger (NHE3). Once both are in the lumen, they recombine into NH4+. NH4+ itself does not cross membranes via a dedicated channel, is not reabsorbed basolaterally at this step, and does not form an \"ammonium bicarbonate\" complex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this NH3/H+ splitting-and-recombining pattern in the proximal tubule is the same diffusion-trapping logic reused later in the collecting duct — recognizing the parallel saves time on paired-concept questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"For each NH4+ molecule generated from glutamine metabolism and ultimately excreted, how much new HCO3- is reabsorbed into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Two new HCO3- molecules, since each NH4+ ion carries two units of alkali","B":"One new HCO3-, matched to the NH4+ that appears in the urine","C":"Zero new HCO3-, since NH4+ excretion does not regenerate bicarbonate stores","D":"Half a new HCO3- molecule, since part of the glutamine carbon skeleton is lost as CO2","E":"Three new HCO3- molecules, produced by carbonic anhydrase acting on filtered CO2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Each glutamine molecule metabolized by glutaminase yields two NH4+ and, via downstream metabolism of the carbon skeleton to CO2 and H2O, two new HCO3- that are reabsorbed across the basolateral membrane via Na+-HCO3- cotransport, so each NH4+ excreted is matched by one new HCO3-. This 1:1 ratio per NH4+ mirrors the titratable acid mechanism and helps replenish HCO3- stores depleted by fixed-acid buffering; it is not a 2:1, 0:1, or fractional relationship per NH4+, and it originates from glutamine metabolism rather than filtered CO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair this 1:1 NH4+-to-new-HCO3- ratio with the 1:1 titratable-acid-to-new-HCO3- ratio from SDL 22 — both mechanisms replenish bicarbonate at the same fixed rate per H+ handled.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism is a portion of the secreted NH4+ reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"NH4+ is actively pumped by a dedicated basolateral NH4+-ATPase","B":"NH4+ diffuses passively through paracellular tight junctions","C":"NH4+ substitutes for K+ on the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter NKCC2","D":"NH4+ substitutes for Na+ on the apical Na+-H+ exchanger NHE3","E":"NH4+ is cotransported with Cl- on a separate NH4+-Cl- symporter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NH4+ delivered to the thick ascending limb is reabsorbed by substituting for K+ on the luminal Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2), the same transporter that drives countercurrent multiplication for NaCl. There is no dedicated NH4+-ATPase or NH4+-Cl- symporter here, paracellular diffusion is not the mechanism described for this segment, and NHE3 mediates H+ secretion in the proximal tubule rather than NH4+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the \"NH4+ mimics K+ on NKCC2\" fact directly explains why loop diuretics (which block NKCC2) can blunt the medullary ammonium gradient — a favorite pharmacology-physiology crossover question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure also disrupts urinary acidification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"What is the physiological consequence of NH4+ reabsorption by the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"NH4+ is immediately excreted unchanged into the urine before reaching the collecting duct","B":"NH4+ is converted directly into urea within hepatocytes for systemic excretion","C":"NH4+ diffuses back into the systemic circulation without ever entering the interstitium","D":"NH4+ is degraded by a dedicated thick ascending limb glutaminase into free ammonia gas","E":"NH4+ concentrates within the medullary interstitium via countercurrent multiplication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By substituting for K+ on NKCC2, NH4+ is reabsorbed alongside Na+ and Cl- and participates in countercurrent multiplication, becoming concentrated in the medullary interstitial fluid, where it equilibrates with NH3. This concentrated interstitial reservoir is what later supplies NH3 for diffusion trapping in the collecting duct; it is not excreted at this point, not converted to urea here, does not simply diffuse back into the circulation, and is not degraded by a thick-ascending-limb glutaminase reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this medullary NH3/NH4+ reservoir is analogous to the medullary NaCl gradient built by countercurrent multiplication; medullary urea additionally depends on ADH-driven urea recycling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure also disrupts urinary acidification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"In the collecting duct, why does only the NH3 form of the NH3/NH4+ buffer pair diffuse from the medullary interstitium into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"NH3 is lipid soluble and can cross cell membranes","B":"NH3 is transported by a dedicated carrier protein that excludes NH4+","C":"NH4+ is too large in molecular size to cross the same channels as NH3","D":"NH4+ is repelled electrically by the negative charge of the tubular lumen","E":"NH3 is actively secreted by a dedicated ATP-dependent transporter in the luminal membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"NH3 is lipid soluble and diffuses freely across cell membranes, while its charged conjugate NH4+ is water soluble and cannot cross the collecting duct cell membranes; this is why only NH3 moves from the medullary interstitium into the lumen, where it recombines with secreted H+ to re-form NH4+, which is then trapped and excreted. No dedicated carrier protein, size-exclusion effect, electrical repulsion, or active ATP-dependent transporter is required to explain this movement — it is passive diffusion governed by lipid solubility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"diffusion trapping\" is the exact term First Aid uses for this NH3-diffuses/NH4+-is-trapped pattern — expect it tested by name.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure also disrupts urinary acidification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"In a healthy person eating a typical high-protein diet, approximately how is the roughly 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ produced normally excreted?","choices":{"A":"About 35 mEq/day as titratable acid and 15 mEq/day as NH4+","B":"About 20 mEq/day as titratable acid and 30 mEq/day as NH4+","C":"About 45 mEq/day as titratable acid and 5 mEq/day as NH4+","D":"About 12 mEq/day as titratable acid and 38 mEq/day as NH4+","E":"About 28 mEq/day as titratable acid and 22 mEq/day as NH4+"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Of the roughly 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ produced from protein and phospholipid catabolism, about 40% (20 mEq/day) is normally excreted as titratable acid and about 60% (30 mEq/day) as NH4+, so that all of the daily fixed acid load is eliminated and the HCO3- consumed buffering it is fully replaced. The other proportions listed do not match these normal reference values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 20/30 split (40%/60%) for normal fixed acid excretion — it is the baseline every acidosis vignette compares against.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease also blunts overall renal acid excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, fixed H+ production can rise roughly tenfold. Which renal adaptation allows the kidneys to excrete this additional acid load?","choices":{"A":"An adaptive fall in NH3 synthesis, offset by a matching rise in titratable acid excretion","B":"A fixed, unchanging rate of NH4+ excretion regardless of the increased acid load","C":"A compensatory rise in filtered phosphate load produced directly by the liver","D":"An adaptive rise in NH3 synthesis, plus extra buffering from filtered ketoacid salts","E":"A shift of most fixed acid excretion away from NH4+ and toward respiratory CO2 elimination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, acidosis induces glutaminase and drives an adaptive rise in NH3 synthesis, increasing NH4+ excretion, while filtered ketoacid salts (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate) serve as additional urinary buffers that raise titratable acid excretion as well. NH3 synthesis rises rather than falls, NH4+ excretion is not fixed, the liver does not produce filtered phosphate, and acid excretion is not shifted toward respiratory CO2 elimination, which is a separate compensatory system entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ketoacid-salts-as-urinary-buffers detail explains why DKA patients can still mount a titratable acid response despite an unchanged filtered phosphate load — a classic integrative point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease also blunts overall renal acid excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease produces the usual 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ but excretes only about 15 mEq/day total (10 mEq/day as titratable acid, 5 mEq/day as NH4+). What best explains this reduced excretion?","choices":{"A":"Progressive nephron loss impairs NH3 synthesis while titratable acid excretion remains fully intact","B":"Progressive nephron loss impairs phosphate filtration while NH3 synthesis remains fully intact","C":"Nephron loss reduces both filtered phosphate buffering and NH3 synthesis for ammonium excretion","D":"Increased plasma aldosterone directly suppresses both excretion pathways to an equal degree","E":"Increased dietary phosphate intake overwhelms the kidneys’ titratable acid buffering capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In advanced chronic kidney disease, progressive nephron loss reduces the glomerular filtration and hence the filtered phosphate available for titratable acid buffering, and impairs NH3 synthesis in the remaining diseased nephrons, so both excretion pathways fall together; the resulting mismatch between the 50 mEq/day produced and only 15 mEq/day excreted is the source of the chronic metabolic acidosis of CKD. Neither pathway remains fully intact in isolation, aldosterone is not the mechanism here, and dietary phosphate intake is not implicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease acidosis is a failure of excretion, not overproduction — contrast with DKA, where acid production itself is what rises.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease also blunts overall renal acid excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and stage 3 chronic kidney disease is found to have a plasma [K+] of 6.2 mEq/L and a mild non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Which mechanism best explains how his hyperkalemia contributes to impaired renal acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"K+ enters proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, lowering intracellular pH and stimulating glutaminase","B":"K+ directly binds and inactivates the basolateral Na+-HCO3- cotransporter","C":"K+ competitively inhibits NH4+ binding to the Na+-H+ exchanger in the proximal tubule","D":"K+ enters proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, raising intracellular pH and inhibiting glutaminase","E":"K+ stimulates aldosterone release, which paradoxically suppresses collecting duct H+-ATPase activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hyperkalemia, K+ enters renal cells in exchange for H+ leaving; the resulting rise in intracellular and intramitochondrial pH inhibits glutaminase, reducing NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion, which is the mechanism underlying Type 4 renal tubular acidosis. Intracellular pH rises rather than falls, and K+ does not directly inactivate the Na+-HCO3- cotransporter, compete for the Na+-H+ exchanger, or act through an aldosterone-mediated suppression of H+-ATPase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia-induced Type 4 RTA is a top-tested cause of non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, classically seen in diabetic nephropathy with hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition causing Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with bulimia nervosa and chronic vomiting has a plasma [K+] of 2.8 mEq/L. Which effect on her renal ammoniagenesis would be expected from this potassium disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Increased NH3 synthesis, since intracellular H+ entry lowers intramitochondrial pH and stimulates glutaminase","B":"Decreased NH3 synthesis, since intracellular H+ entry directly raises intramitochondrial pH and inhibits glutaminase","C":"Increased NH3 synthesis, mediated through direct hypokalemia-induced upregulation of NKCC2","D":"No change in NH3 synthesis, since glutaminase activity is insensitive to intracellular pH shifts","E":"Decreased NH3 synthesis, mediated through direct hypokalemia-induced downregulation of NKCC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In hypokalemia, K+ leaves renal cells while H+ enters; the resulting fall in intracellular and intramitochondrial pH stimulates glutaminase, increasing NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion. Intracellular H+ entry lowers, rather than raises, intramitochondrial pH, glutaminase activity is directly sensitive to this intracellular pH shift, and the mechanism is a pH effect on glutaminase rather than an NKCC2-mediated effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hypokalemia from chronic vomiting or diuretic use classically produces a metabolic alkalosis, yet this same hypokalemia independently boosts ammoniagenesis — a nuance worth distinguishing from the acid-base disturbance itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition causing Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in chronic metabolic acidosis increases his NH4+ excretion from a baseline of 30 mEq/day to 150 mEq/day through adaptive glutaminase induction. Approximately how much additional new HCO3- would be expected to enter the blood as a result?","choices":{"A":"About 60 mEq/day, since half of the increased NH4+ excretion generates new HCO3-","B":"About 120 mEq/day, matching the increase in NH4+ excretion on a 1:1 basis","C":"About 240 mEq/day, since two new HCO3- molecules are generated per NH4+ excreted","D":"Essentially zero, since NH4+ excretion depletes existing HCO3- stores","E":"About 120 mEq/day, but limited to the initial 24 hours of adaptation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because each NH4+ excreted is generated alongside one new HCO3- reabsorbed into the blood, an increase in NH4+ excretion from 30 to 150 mEq/day (an increase of 120 mEq/day) is matched by an additional 120 mEq/day of new HCO3- entering the blood. The ratio is 1:1, not 1:2 or 2:1, NH4+ excretion generates rather than depletes HCO3-, and this relationship holds for as long as the adaptive glutaminase induction persists, not only for the first 24 hours.","boardPrep":"Step 1: quantitative 1:1-ratio problems like this are a favorite way to test whether the ammonium-HCO3- coupling is truly understood rather than just memorized as a phrase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition causing Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents with severe diarrhea and a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Urinalysis reveals a urine pH of 5.0. Which effect would this acidic urine have on ammonium excretion via diffusion trapping?","choices":{"A":"It would decrease NH4+ excretion, since low luminal NH3 concentration flattens the gradient favoring NH3 diffusion into the lumen","B":"It would have no measurable effect on NH4+ excretion, since diffusion trapping is independent of luminal urine pH","C":"It would increase NH4+ excretion, since low luminal NH3 concentration steepens the gradient favoring NH3 diffusion into the lumen","D":"It would increase NH4+ excretion, but through direct stimulation of glutaminase within the collecting duct cells","E":"It would decrease NH4+ excretion, since acidic urine directly inhibits collecting duct H+-ATPase activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A low tubular fluid pH converts more of the luminal NH3/NH4+ pool into NH4+, lowering the free luminal NH3 concentration; this steepens the concentration gradient favoring diffusion of NH3 from the medullary interstitium into the lumen, increasing NH4+ formation and excretion. The gradient is steepened, not flattened, diffusion trapping is directly dependent on luminal pH rather than independent of it, glutaminase is a proximal tubule enzyme rather than a collecting duct effect, and acidic urine does not inhibit the H+-ATPase — if anything, ongoing H+ secretion is what keeps the lumen acidic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why acidic urine and NH4+ excretion rise together in states like diarrhea — low urine pH is not just a marker of acidosis, it is part of the mechanism that boosts ammonium elimination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure disrupting the diffusion-trapping gradient for NH4+","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with primary hyperaldosteronism has increased collecting duct H+-ATPase activity. How would this be expected to affect ammonium excretion via diffusion trapping, independent of any change in NH3 synthesis?","choices":{"A":"Increased H+ secretion would leave local NH4+ formation unchanged, since NH3 diffusion is the true rate-limiting step","B":"Increased H+ secretion would have no effect on NH4+ formation, since NH3 diffusion is governed primarily by renal blood flow","C":"Increased H+ secretion would directly inhibit NH3 diffusion from the medullary interstitium","D":"Increased H+ secretion would form more NH4+ locally, retaining more of it within the tubular fluid","E":"Increased H+ secretion would convert trapped NH4+ back into diffusible NH3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"More luminal H+ combines with more diffused NH3 to form more NH4+, which remains trapped in the tubular fluid because it cannot diffuse back across the lipid membrane; increased H+-ATPase activity therefore increases local NH4+ formation and trapping. H+ secretion does not decrease NH4+ formation, is not unrelated to ammonium handling since it directly supplies the H+ half of the diffusion-trapping reaction, does not inhibit NH3 diffusion, and does not convert NH4+ back into NH3, which would require the opposite (alkaline) shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffusion trapping links titratable acid physiology and ammonium physiology through the same H+-ATPase — a single transporter effect can be tested from either angle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure disrupting the diffusion-trapping gradient for NH4+","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease and a markedly reduced number of functioning nephrons shows blunted ammonium excretion despite adequate systemic acidemia. Which mechanism best explains this, independent of any change in plasma potassium?","choices":{"A":"Fewer functioning nephrons increase total NH3 synthesis but impair its diffusion into the collecting duct lumen","B":"Fewer functioning nephrons have no effect on NH3 synthesis but eliminate luminal H+-ATPase activity","C":"Fewer functioning nephrons increase medullary NH3 concentration but impair thick ascending limb reabsorption","D":"Fewer functioning nephrons redirect NH4+ excretion toward the titratable acid pathway completely","E":"Fewer functioning nephrons reduce total NH3 synthesis and the medullary NH3 available for diffusion trapping"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With fewer functioning nephrons, total proximal tubule NH3 synthesis falls, which lowers the medullary interstitial NH3 concentration available to diffuse into the collecting duct lumen for diffusion trapping, blunting NH4+ excretion even when systemic acidemia is present to drive maximal stimulation. Total NH3 synthesis falls rather than rises, luminal H+-ATPase activity is not eliminated by nephron loss per se, medullary NH3 concentration falls rather than rises, and NH4+ excretion is not redirected into the titratable acid pathway, which depends on an entirely separate filtered phosphate buffer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the same nephron-mass principle from SDL 23 Objective 3 (fewer nephrons, less filtered phosphate and less NH3) applied specifically to the diffusion-trapping step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure disrupting the diffusion-trapping gradient for NH4+","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher notes that during chronic metabolic acidosis, ammonium excretion can increase several-fold above baseline, while titratable acid excretion increases only modestly. What best explains this difference in adaptive capacity?","choices":{"A":"Titratable acid excretion can be upregulated enzymatically, while NH3 synthesis is capped by fixed phosphate availability","B":"Both pathways are capped by fixed buffer availability, so neither can meaningfully increase during acidosis","C":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by fixed filtered phosphate, while NH3 synthesis is enzymatically upregulatable","D":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by aldosterone secretion, while NH3 synthesis is capped by ADH secretion","E":"Both pathways can be upregulated enzymatically to an equal, unlimited degree throughout chronic acidosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Titratable acid excretion is fundamentally limited by the fixed amount of filtered phosphate (and, in states like diabetic ketoacidosis, filtered ketoacid salts) available to serve as urinary buffer, whereas NH4+ excretion depends on glutaminase-driven NH3 synthesis, an enzymatic pathway that can be upregulated several-fold during chronic acidosis. The roles are not reversed, titratable acid excretion is not capped by aldosterone nor NH3 synthesis by ADH, and titratable acid excretion clearly does not scale enzymatically to the same degree as NH4+ excretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"fixed buffer amount vs. inducible enzyme\" is the single sentence that explains almost every titratable-acid-versus-ammonium comparison question on this topic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease blunting both titratable acid and ammonium excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients face an identical fixed-acid load: one from diabetic ketoacidosis with normal underlying renal function, the other from advanced chronic kidney disease. Which best describes their expected ammonium excretion responses?","choices":{"A":"The chronic kidney disease patient mounts a large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion, while the diabetic ketoacidosis patient cannot","B":"The diabetic ketoacidosis patient mounts a large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion, while the chronic kidney disease patient cannot","C":"Both patients mount an equally large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion, since their acid load is identical in magnitude","D":"Neither patient can increase NH4+ excretion, since this acid load exceeds the kidneys’ maximum capacity in both clinical cases","E":"Both patients fail to raise NH4+ excretion, each for a distinct underlying physiologic mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With intact nephron mass, the diabetic ketoacidosis patient can induce glutaminase and mount a large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion to help handle the increased acid load; the chronic kidney disease patient, with a markedly reduced number of functioning nephrons, cannot mount this same adaptive response regardless of how much acid is presented. The roles are not reversed, the two patients do not respond identically despite an identical acid load, and the diabetic ketoacidosis patient’s response is not against the kidneys’ maximum capacity in the way the chronic kidney disease patient’s is.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this side-by-side comparison is a common exam device for testing whether nephron mass, not just acid production, determines the adaptive ammonium response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease blunting both titratable acid and ammonium excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, titratable acid excretion rises above the normal 20 mEq/day baseline even though phosphate filtration is unchanged. What best accounts for this additional titratable acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"Filtered ketoacid salts such as beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate act as additional urinary buffers","B":"Increased glomerular filtration rate delivers more phosphate to the urine for buffering","C":"Increased aldosterone secretion directly raises phosphate reabsorption and urinary concentration","D":"Increased NH3 synthesis chemically converts into additional titratable acid in the lumen","E":"Increased H+-ATPase activity, independent of any buffer change, fully accounts for the extra titratable acid excretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, overproduced ketoacids such as beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid are filtered as their salts and act as additional urinary buffers analogous to phosphate, raising the total amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid even though phosphate filtration itself is unchanged. Glomerular filtration rate and aldosterone are not implicated in this increase, NH3 does not chemically convert into titratable acid, and increased H+-ATPase activity alone cannot generate titratable acid without additional buffer being present to accept the secreted H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ketoacid-salts-as-buffer detail is exactly why DKA patients can excrete more titratable acid than their phosphate load alone would predict — a frequently tested integrative point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — chronic kidney disease blunting both titratable acid and ammonium excretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Metabolic Acid-Base Disturbances & Plasma Anion Gap","questions":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has plasma Na+ 140 mEq/L, HCO3- 12 mEq/L, and Cl- 100 mEq/L. What is her plasma anion gap, and is it elevated above the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L?","choices":{"A":"12 mEq/L, essentially unchanged from her baseline","B":"16 mEq/L, marginally higher than her baseline","C":"28 mEq/L, a distinctly high calculated result","D":"40 mEq/L, a strikingly high calculated result","E":"8 mEq/L, near the lower boundary typically seen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Plasma anion gap = [Na+] - ([HCO3-] + [Cl-]) = 140 - (12 + 100) = 28 mEq/L, which exceeds the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L and indicates accumulation of unmeasured anions. None of the other listed values result from correctly applying this formula to the given electrolytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the anion gap formula cold — it is calculated identically on nearly every acid-base vignette regardless of which disorder is ultimately diagnosed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"The plasma anion gap reflects which underlying physiological principle?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic equilibrium between plasma and interstitial fluid","B":"Renal autoregulation of glomerular filtration rate","C":"Colloid osmotic pressure exerted by plasma proteins","D":"Acid-base buffering capacity of the bicarbonate system","E":"Electroneutrality of body fluid compartments"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plasma must remain electroneutral, so when the measured cation (Na+) exceeds the sum of the measured anions (HCO3- and Cl-), the difference reflects unmeasured anions such as plasma proteins, phosphate, citrate, and sulfate. The anion gap is not a measure of osmotic equilibrium, glomerular filtration rate, colloid osmotic pressure, or direct H+ titration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: understanding electroneutrality as the basis for the anion gap explains why an elevated gap means \"something is there that we did not measure,\" not \"something is wrong with sodium.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a classic cause of metabolic acidosis with an increased anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Diarrhea, from direct gastrointestinal loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal fluid","B":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis, from failure to reabsorb filtered HCO3-","C":"Infusion of large volumes of isotonic saline, from dilution of plasma HCO3-","D":"Diabetic ketoacidosis, from accumulation of beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acids","E":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis, from hypoaldosteronism-induced hyperkalemic suppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diabetic ketoacidosis accumulates the unmeasured organic anions beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate, producing a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, alongside lactic acidosis, salicylate poisoning, and methanol/ethylene glycol poisoning. Diarrhea and Types 2 and 4 RTA instead cause normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis, and isotonic saline infusion is not a classic cause of increased-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the MUDPILES mnemonic groups the high-anion-gap causes; RTAs and diarrhea belong on the opposite (normal-anion-gap) list.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with metabolic alkalosis from prolonged vomiting has persistent extracellular fluid volume contraction. Which combination of mechanisms best explains why her elevated HCO3- is not simply excreted in the urine?","choices":{"A":"Increased proximal HCO3- reabsorption from Starling forces, plus angiotensin II- and aldosterone-driven distal H+ secretion","B":"Beta-intercalated cells secreting new HCO3- into the lumen, overriding any proximal reabsorption change","C":"Increased proximal HCO3- reabsorption from Starling forces, plus angiotensin II- and aldosterone-driven distal HCO3- secretion","D":"A rise in glomerular filtration rate that delivers an overall smaller filtered HCO3- load to the tubule","E":"A drop in renin release that substantially blunts angiotensin II- and aldosterone-mediated H+ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Volume contraction increases proximal HCO3- reabsorption via Starling forces and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, so angiotensin II stimulates the Na+-H+ exchanger and aldosterone stimulates alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion; together these prevent the excess HCO3- from being excreted, maintaining the alkalosis (contraction alkalosis). Decreased reabsorption, decreased renin release, increased GFR, and distal HCO3- secretion all move in the wrong direction to explain this maintenance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this proximal-plus-distal triad is the textbook mechanism of \"contraction alkalosis\" and explains why saline, not diuretics, corrects it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism contributes to the hypokalemia commonly seen in vomiting-induced metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Volume contraction-induced secondary hypoaldosteronism substantially decreases principal cell K+ secretion","B":"Volume contraction-induced secondary hyperaldosteronism increases principal cell K+ secretion","C":"Direct loss of K+ in vomitus accounts for the majority of the observed hypokalemia","D":"Metabolic alkalosis shifts K+ out of cells in direct exchange for H+ entering the cells","E":"Increased glomerular filtration of K+ directly increases its urinary excretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Volume contraction from vomiting activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and the resulting secondary hyperaldosteronism increases principal cell K+ secretion, which is the most important contributor to hypokalemia; direct gastric K+ loss and the alkalosis-induced cellular H+/K+ shift (H+ leaves cells, K+ enters, lowering plasma K+) contribute more modestly. Hypoaldosteronism moves in the wrong direction, cellular shifts move K+ into rather than out of cells in alkalosis, and glomerular filtration of K+ is not the operative mechanism.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: rank the three contributors to vomiting-induced hypokalemia by importance — secondary hyperaldosteronism first, direct GI loss and cellular shifts second — this ranking is a favorite way to test genuine understanding versus rote memorization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which cause of metabolic alkalosis is classified as saline-unresponsive, meaning it is not corrected simply by giving intravenous saline?","choices":{"A":"Vomiting, since gastric H+ loss produces a classic volume-contraction alkalosis","B":"Loop diuretic use, since diuretic-induced natriuresis produces a volume-contraction alkalosis","C":"Thiazide diuretic use, since diuretic-induced natriuresis similarly produces a volume-contraction alkalosis","D":"Primary hyperaldosteronism, since it is not caused by extracellular volume contraction","E":"Nasogastric suction, since it removes gastric H+ much like vomiting does"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary hyperaldosteronism causes metabolic alkalosis independent of extracellular volume status (volume is often expanded, not contracted), so giving saline does not correct it; this is termed saline-unresponsive alkalosis, along with Cushing syndrome and excessive licorice intake. Vomiting, loop and thiazide diuretic use, and nasogastric suction are all saline-responsive causes driven by volume contraction, which saline directly corrects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"saline-responsive versus saline-unresponsive\" is a favorite classification question — volume-contraction causes respond to saline, mineralocorticoid-excess causes do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a plasma HCO3- concentration of 14 mEq/L, which is 10 mEq/L below the normal value of 24 mEq/L. Using the renal rule of a 1.2 mm Hg decrease in Pco2 per 1 mEq/L decrease in HCO3-, what Pco2 would indicate simple metabolic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"About 22 mm Hg","B":"About 34 mm Hg","C":"About 40 mm Hg","D":"About 16 mm Hg","E":"About 28 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The predicted decrease in Pco2 is 10 mEq/L x 1.2 mm Hg/mEq/L = 12 mm Hg, so the predicted Pco2 is 40 - 12 = 28 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 close to this value indicates simple metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation; the other values do not match this calculation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: practice this exact calculation until it is automatic — every metabolic acidosis vignette on boards expects you to check the predicted Pco2 before concluding \"simple\" versus \"mixed\" disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a plasma HCO3- concentration of 37 mEq/L, which is 13 mEq/L above the normal value of 24 mEq/L. Using the renal rule of a 0.7 mm Hg increase in Pco2 per 1 mEq/L increase in HCO3-, what Pco2 would indicate simple metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"About 40 mm Hg","B":"About 49 mm Hg","C":"About 53 mm Hg","D":"About 45 mm Hg","E":"About 58 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The predicted increase in Pco2 is 13 mEq/L x 0.7 mm Hg/mEq/L = 9.1 mm Hg, so the predicted Pco2 is 40 + 9.1 = 49.1 mm Hg, rounding to about 49 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 close to this value indicates simple metabolic alkalosis with appropriate respiratory compensation; the other values do not match this calculation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the metabolic alkalosis renal rule (0.7 mm Hg/mEq/L) is smaller than the metabolic acidosis rule (1.2 mm Hg/mEq/L) — mixing these up is a common test-taking error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a Pco2 of 70 mm Hg, which is 30 mm Hg above the normal value of 40 mm Hg, and an HCO3- of 33 mEq/L, which is 9 mEq/L above normal. Applying the acute rule (0.1 mEq/L HCO3- increase per 1 mm Hg Pco2 increase) and the chronic rule (0.3 mEq/L per 1 mm Hg), which best characterizes this patient?","choices":{"A":"Closer to the chronic respiratory acidosis prediction of a 9 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","B":"Closer to the acute respiratory acidosis prediction of a 3 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","C":"Closer to the chronic respiratory acidosis prediction of a 21 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","D":"Closer to the acute respiratory acidosis prediction of a 30 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","E":"Consistent with neither the acute nor the chronic prediction for respiratory acidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acute rule predicts a 30 mm Hg x 0.1 = 3 mEq/L increase in HCO3-, while the chronic rule predicts a 30 mm Hg x 0.3 = 9 mEq/L increase; the patient’s actual 9 mEq/L increase matches the chronic prediction closely, indicating renal compensation has had time to occur. The other values do not correctly apply either rule to this Pco2 change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute respiratory acidosis relies only on buffering (small HCO3- rise); chronic respiratory acidosis adds renal compensation over days (larger HCO3- rise) — this contrast is a recurring board theme.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"What defines a 'mixed' acid-base disorder, as opposed to a 'simple' one?","choices":{"A":"Exactly one primary disturbance is present, with an exaggerated compensatory response","B":"More than one primary acid-base disturbance is present and acting simultaneously","C":"A single disturbance affects both HCO3- and Pco2 through the same underlying mechanism","D":"A disturbance resolves spontaneously, with no compensatory response occurring","E":"A disturbance in which the pH stays within the normal range despite abnormal HCO3- and Pco2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A mixed acid-base disorder is defined by the presence of more than one primary disturbance occurring simultaneously, such as a metabolic acidosis coexisting with a respiratory alkalosis; a simple disorder has only one primary disturbance, with a single compensatory response. An exaggerated compensation, a single mechanism affecting both variables, spontaneous resolution, and a normal pH despite abnormal values are not what defines the mixed-versus-simple distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"mixed\" always means multiple PRIMARY disturbances, not an unusually large or small compensatory response to a single disturbance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"How is the acid-base map used to distinguish a simple from a mixed acid-base disorder?","choices":{"A":"Values falling inside one disorder’s shaded zone indicate that more than one disturbance is definitely present","B":"The acid-base map is used primarily to calculate the plasma anion gap value","C":"Inside a zone points to a lone disturbance; between zones points to more than one","D":"The acid-base map replaces the need for any arterial blood gas measurement at the bedside","E":"The acid-base map applies mainly to metabolic disorders, largely excluding respiratory ones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Each simple acid-base disorder occupies a defined range of pH, Pco2, and HCO3- values on the acid-base map; a patient’s values falling within one shaded area are consistent with only that disorder being present, while values falling outside any shaded area (e.g., between two areas) indicate a mixed disorder. The map does not calculate the anion gap, does not replace arterial blood gas measurement (it is built from those same values), and applies to both metabolic and respiratory disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the acid-base map is the visual complement to the renal rules calculation — both answer the same question, \"is this simple or mixed?\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term 'isohydric line' refer to on the acid-base map?","choices":{"A":"A line representing a constant ratio of HCO3- to dissolved CO2 concentration","B":"A line representing a constant plasma anion gap regardless of HCO3- or Pco2","C":"A line representing a constant Pco2 regardless of the HCO3- concentration","D":"A line representing a constant HCO3- concentration regardless of the Pco2","E":"A line representing the boundary between compensated and uncompensated disorders"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An isohydric line connects all combinations of HCO3- and dissolved CO2 that yield the same ratio, and therefore, by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, the same pH; this is why the acid-base map can display pH indirectly through HCO3- and Pco2 axes. It does not represent a constant anion gap, a constant Pco2 alone, a constant HCO3- alone, or a compensation boundary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing isohydric lines lets you read pH directly off the acid-base map without recalculating Henderson-Hasselbalch for every point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents with nausea, vomiting, and Kussmaul respirations. Labs: Na+ 132 mEq/L, Cl- 94 mEq/L, HCO3- 5 mEq/L. What is her plasma anion gap, and what acid-base category does her metabolic acidosis fall into?","choices":{"A":"33 mEq/L, consistent with normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","B":"33 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","C":"23 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","D":"13 mEq/L, consistent with normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","E":"43 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her anion gap is 132 - (94 + 5) = 33 mEq/L, well above the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L, so this is high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis and accumulation of beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate as unmeasured anions. The other numeric values do not correctly apply the anion gap formula to her given electrolytes, and 33 mEq/L cannot be classified as normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this exact DKA electrolyte panel (Na+ 132, Cl- 94, HCO3- 5) reproduces a classic teaching case — memorize the pattern of severe acidosis, dehydration, and a markedly elevated anion gap.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients each have a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis: one with two weeks of watery diarrhea, the other with confirmed distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis. Which additional finding would best distinguish the two at the bedside?","choices":{"A":"A urine pH that fluctuates unpredictably between acidic and alkaline values suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","B":"An elevated plasma anion gap suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","C":"A urine pH persistently above 5.5 despite systemic acidemia suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","D":"A normal serum potassium concentration reliably distinguishes the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","E":"Hyperchloremia present in one patient but not the other distinguishes the two conditions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase failure prevents urine pH from falling below about 5.5 even during systemic acidemia, whereas a patient with diarrhea and intact renal function can still acidify urine appropriately. Both conditions produce a normal anion gap and hyperchloremia, and serum potassium is not a reliable distinguishing feature since type 1 RTA, like diarrhea, commonly causes hypokalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"urine pH will not fall below 5.5 despite systemic acidemia\" is the single most quoted distinguishing fact for distal RTA on board exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease develops metabolic acidosis. Which statement about her anion gap status is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Her anion gap may be elevated, normal, or mixed, depending on retained organic anions versus chloride retention","B":"Her anion gap is necessarily elevated, since chronic kidney disease is a classic high-anion-gap cause","C":"Her anion gap is necessarily normal, since chronic kidney disease causes a purely hyperchloremic acidosis","D":"Her anion gap cannot reliably be assessed in chronic kidney disease, since creatinine directly interferes with the assay","E":"Her anion gap will be elevated early in disease and become normal after dialysis begins"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease can present with either an increased anion gap (from retained unmeasured organic anions as filtration falls) or a normal anion gap (from impaired NH4+ excretion causing hyperchloremic acidosis), and many patients show a mixed picture; the anion gap is not necessarily fixed to one category. Creatinine does not interfere with the anion gap calculation, and gap status is not reliably tied to dialysis initiation in the way described.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic kidney disease is a useful reminder that a single etiology can produce either anion-gap pattern — do not assume every listed \"cause\" maps to only one category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a pyloric ulcer and several days of vomiting has metabolic alkalosis with orthostatic hypotension. His vomiting has now stopped, but his metabolic alkalosis persists. What is the most effective treatment to correct his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Intravenous isotonic saline given specifically to directly neutralize the excess plasma HCO3-","B":"Acetazolamide to inhibit proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption regardless of volume status","C":"Intravenous isotonic saline to restore volume and permit renal HCO3- excretion","D":"Intravenous potassium chloride as the sole treatment, without any accompanying volume repletion","E":"Oral ammonium chloride to directly titrate the excess plasma HCO3-"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because his alkalosis is being maintained by extracellular volume contraction rather than ongoing gastric H+ loss, restoring volume with isotonic saline removes the stimulus for increased proximal and distal HCO3- reabsorption, allowing the kidneys to finally excrete the excess HCO3- and correct the alkalosis. Saline does not chemically neutralize HCO3-, acetazolamide is not the standard approach and does not address the underlying volume deficit, potassium alone does not correct the volume contraction driving the maintenance, and ammonium chloride is not standard first-line treatment here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"saline-responsive\" alkalosis literally means giving saline is the treatment — remember this pairs directly with the volume-contraction maintenance mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with several days of vomiting from a pyloric ulcer has metabolic alkalosis with orthostatic hypotension. Which specific renal transport effect of elevated angiotensin II directly contributes to maintaining his metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Stimulation of the proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchanger, increasing filtered HCO3- reabsorption","B":"Inhibition of the proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchanger, decreasing filtered HCO3- reabsorption","C":"Stimulation of the collecting duct H+-K+-ATPase, increasing K+ reabsorption","D":"Stimulation of the thick ascending limb Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, increasing NaCl reabsorption","E":"Inhibition of aldosterone release, decreasing distal H+ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated angiotensin II from volume contraction directly stimulates the proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchanger, increasing reabsorption of the filtered HCO3- load and helping maintain the elevated plasma HCO3-. Angiotensin II does not inhibit this exchanger, its primary maintenance effect here is proximal rather than through the collecting duct H+-K+-ATPase or thick ascending limb NKCC2, and it stimulates rather than inhibits aldosterone release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiotensin II (proximal NHE3) and aldosterone (distal H+-ATPase) are the two separate renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system effects that together maintain contraction alkalosis — know both, not just aldosterone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with acute opioid overdose has significant hypoventilation. Which acid-base and blood gas pattern would be expected as the direct consequence?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory alkalosis, from CO2 loss due to direct inhibition of the medullary respiratory center","B":"Respiratory acidosis, from CO2 retention due to peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation","C":"Respiratory alkalosis, from CO2 loss due to peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation","D":"Metabolic acidosis, from opioid-induced lactic acid accumulation","E":"Respiratory acidosis, from CO2 retention due to inhibition of the medullary respiratory center"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opioids directly inhibit the medullary respiratory center, causing hypoventilation, CO2 retention, and respiratory acidosis (decreased pH, increased Pco2). Hypoventilation causes CO2 retention rather than loss, the mechanism is central inhibition rather than peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation, and this scenario is a primary respiratory disturbance rather than a metabolic one from lactic acid accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: opioids, barbiturates, and anesthetics are the classic causes of respiratory acidosis from central respiratory center inhibition — a frequently tested pharmacology-physiology link.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis is explored further here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with metabolic acidosis has a plasma HCO3- concentration of 10 mEq/L. Using Winters' formula (predicted Pco2 = 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8, plus or minus 2), what is the predicted Pco2 range for appropriate respiratory compensation?","choices":{"A":"About 21 to 25 mm Hg","B":"About 16 to 20 mm Hg","C":"About 26 to 30 mm Hg","D":"About 33 to 37 mm Hg","E":"About 8 to 12 mm Hg"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Winters' formula gives predicted Pco2 = (1.5 x 10) + 8 = 23 mm Hg, plus or minus 2, for a range of about 21 to 25 mm Hg; a measured Pco2 within this range indicates appropriate respiratory compensation for a simple metabolic acidosis. The other ranges do not result from correctly applying this formula to an HCO3- of 10 mEq/L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Winters' formula and the renal-rule slope (1.2 mm Hg per mEq/L) are two commonly taught approximations for the same compensation — either gets you to a similar predicted Pco2 on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis has a decrease in plasma HCO3- of 10 mEq/L from normal, and an increase in anion gap of 20 mEq/L from normal. What does this delta-delta analysis suggest?","choices":{"A":"A second disorder is present that is independently decreasing HCO3-, such as a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","B":"A second disorder is present that is independently increasing HCO3-, such as a metabolic alkalosis","C":"No second disorder is present, since the decrease in HCO3- exactly mirrors the increase in anion gap","D":"A second disorder is present affecting the respiratory compensation, not the metabolic component","E":"The primary disorder must instead be reclassified altogether as an entirely different normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the HCO3- decreased by less (10 mEq/L) than the anion gap increased (20 mEq/L), an additional process is raising HCO3- beyond what the high-anion-gap acidosis alone would produce, such as a coexisting metabolic alkalosis. A fall in HCO3- roughly equal to the rise in anion gap would indicate no second disorder, and a fall in HCO3- greater than the rise in anion gap would instead suggest a coexisting normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis lowering HCO3- further; the respiratory compensation and the underlying anion-gap classification are not what this comparison addresses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta-delta analysis: HCO3- falls less than the anion gap rises = look for an additional alkalosis; HCO3- falls more than the anion gap rises = look for an additional normal-anion-gap acidosis; the two changes about equal = a simple high-anion-gap disorder by itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing, stable COPD has a Pco2 of 78 mm Hg, predicting a chronic HCO3- increase of about 11 mEq/L, but his actual HCO3- is only 26 mEq/L, an increase of just 2 mEq/L from normal. What does this discrepancy suggest?","choices":{"A":"A concurrent metabolic alkalosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation","B":"A concurrent metabolic acidosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much greater than predicted for chronic compensation","C":"Simple chronic respiratory acidosis is present, since some discrepancy from the predicted value is expected normally","D":"A concurrent metabolic acidosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation","E":"A concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The actual HCO3- rise (2 mEq/L) is far below the value predicted for chronic renal compensation (about 11 mEq/L), meaning an additional process is independently lowering HCO3-, most consistent with a concurrent metabolic acidosis such as lactic acidosis from poor tissue oxygenation. A concurrent metabolic alkalosis would raise, not lower, the observed HCO3-; the discrepancy is far too large to be normal variation around simple chronic respiratory acidosis; and a concurrent respiratory alkalosis would not directly explain an HCO3- lower than predicted for renal compensation of this respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the exact classic COPD-plus-lactic-acidosis teaching vignette — a smaller-than-predicted HCO3- rise in chronic respiratory acidosis should always prompt a search for hidden metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with salicylate overdose has direct respiratory center stimulation causing hyperventilation, plus salicylate-induced accumulation of organic acids. Which combination of primary acid-base disturbances would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Primary respiratory acidosis plus primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder","B":"Primary respiratory alkalosis plus primary normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder","C":"Just primary respiratory alkalosis, since salicylate's direct effect is limited to the respiratory center","D":"Just primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, since the respiratory center is not directly affected","E":"Primary respiratory alkalosis plus primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Salicylate directly stimulates the medullary respiratory center, producing a primary respiratory alkalosis, while its own accumulation as an unmeasured organic acid independently produces a primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis; together these constitute a double acid-base disorder rather than a single one. The disturbance is respiratory alkalosis rather than acidosis, the accompanying metabolic disorder has an increased rather than normal anion gap, and salicylate affects both the respiratory center and the metabolic (anion gap) component simultaneously rather than only one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate overdose is the single most commonly tested example of a double acid-base disorder — respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, occurring together rather than sequentially.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in cardiorespiratory arrest is found to have, at the same time, severe lactic acidosis from tissue hypoxia, respiratory acidosis from inadequate ventilation, and an iatrogenic metabolic alkalosis from large volumes of bicarbonate given during resuscitation. How many primary acid-base disturbances does this represent?","choices":{"A":"One, since each of these processes ultimately reflects the same underlying final common pathway of illness","B":"Two, since the metabolic alkalosis and lactic acidosis cancel each other into one net metabolic state","C":"Two, since respiratory acidosis and lactic acidosis are considered functionally the same clinical disturbance","D":"Three, since lactic acidosis, respiratory acidosis, and metabolic alkalosis each independently alter it","E":"Four, since lactic acidosis, respiratory acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, and tissue hypoxia are each separate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lactic acidosis (a metabolic acidosis from tissue hypoxia), respiratory acidosis (from inadequate ventilation), and iatrogenic metabolic alkalosis (from bicarbonate administration) each independently alter blood HCO3- or Pco2 through separate mechanisms, together constituting a triple acid-base disorder. These are not a single shared pathway, the alkalosis does not simply cancel the acidosis (they can coexist as distinct primary disturbances), respiratory and lactic acidosis are mechanistically distinct (Pco2-driven versus HCO3--driven), and tissue hypoxia itself is the trigger for the lactic acidosis rather than a separate primary acid-base disturbance.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cardiorespiratory arrest with bicarbonate resuscitation is a classic setup for a triple acid-base disorder — count each independent process rather than assuming they cancel out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with several days of sustained hyperventilation has arterial pH 7.50, Pco2 20 mm Hg, and HCO3- 15 mEq/L. Which combination of primary disturbance and compensation best explains this arterial blood gas profile?","choices":{"A":"Primary metabolic alkalosis with partial respiratory compensation raising Pco2","B":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with partial renal compensation lowering HCO3-","C":"Primary respiratory acidosis with partial renal compensation raising HCO3-","D":"Primary metabolic acidosis with partial respiratory compensation lowering Pco2","E":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with no renal compensation, since HCO3- remains normal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The alkalemic pH (7.50) is driven by the markedly low Pco2 (20 mm Hg), identifying this as a primary respiratory alkalosis; the low HCO3- (15 mEq/L) represents partial renal compensation, which decreases HCO3- reabsorption to bring the pH partway back toward normal. The other options either mismatch which variable is primary and which is compensatory, or state the compensatory HCO3- change in the wrong direction for respiratory alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: always identify the primary disturbance first by asking which single variable (Pco2 or HCO3-) explains the direction of the pH change, then check whether the other variable has moved in the expected compensatory direction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true}]}]},{"examNumber":3,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"SDL 25 — Renal Tubular Acidoses (RTA Type 1, Type 2, & Type 4)","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect underlies type 2 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete H+ into the collecting duct lumen","B":"Aldosterone deficiency that impairs distal Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion","C":"Loss of nephron mass that limits the total quantity of NH4+ that can be excreted","D":"Overproduction of ketoacids that exceeds the maximal renal capacity to excrete H+","E":"A lowered proximal reabsorptive threshold that lets filtered HCO3- escape"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In type 2 RTA the proximal tubule fails to reabsorb filtered HCO3- normally, which lowers the renal threshold for bicarbonate and produces renal HCO3- wasting with hyperchloremic, normal-anion-gap acidosis. Failed alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion describes type 1, aldosterone deficiency describes type 4, reduced nephron mass describes chronic kidney disease, and ketoacid overproduction describes a high-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RTA types are defined by where in the nephron acid-base handling fails; type 2 is the proximal HCO3- reabsorption defect. COMLEX: type 2 RTA is the RTA most often tied to a generalized proximal tubular disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the metabolic acidosis of untreated type 2 RTA typically self-limiting?","choices":{"A":"Filtered HCO3- is fully reabsorbed once plasma HCO3- falls below the lowered threshold","B":"Acidemia progressively raises proximal reabsorptive capacity until the normal threshold is restored","C":"Hyperventilation clears the acid load so the kidney no longer needs to excrete any H+","D":"Rising plasma K+ stimulates NH3 synthesis until enough new HCO3- is generated to normalize pH","E":"Alpha-intercalated cells upregulate H+ secretion to regenerate the HCO3- that was wasted"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the reabsorptive threshold is lowered, HCO3- is wasted only while plasma HCO3- remains above that threshold. As plasma HCO3- falls below it, the filtered load is once again reabsorbed completely, wasting stops, and plasma HCO3- stabilizes at a reduced steady-state level. The remaining options describe mechanisms that either do not occur or would not stop the bicarbonate wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in steady state the type 2 patient has a stable, mildly to moderately low HCO3- and can acidify urine below 5.5, unlike the type 1 patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Type 2 RTA is most classically associated with which generalized proximal tubular reabsorption disorder?","choices":{"A":"Liddle syndrome","B":"Fanconi syndrome","C":"Bartter syndrome","D":"Gitelman syndrome","E":"Apparent mineralocorticoid excess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fanconi syndrome is a generalized reabsorptive defect of the proximal tubule that wastes HCO3-, phosphate, glucose, and amino acids together, so proximal RTA is a defining feature. Liddle, Bartter, and Gitelman syndromes affect distal or loop transport and present with hypokalemic alkalosis rather than proximal RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fanconi syndrome equals proximal RTA plus phosphaturia, glucosuria, and aminoaciduria; hypophosphatemic rickets can follow in children. COMLEX: recall the generalized PCT reabsorption defect when a vignette lists several wasted solutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect underlies type 1, or distal, renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"A lowered proximal reabsorptive threshold, so filtered HCO3- is wasted into the urine","B":"Aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia that suppresses glutaminase activity","C":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion, so no new HCO3- is generated","D":"Loss of nephron mass with reduced total ammonium production","E":"Excess plasma organic anions that raise the anion gap"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+ normally, so titratable acid and NH4+ excretion fall, no new HCO3- is generated, and the urine cannot be acidified appropriately. A lowered proximal threshold describes type 2, aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia describes type 4, and the remaining options describe chronic kidney disease and high-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA means no new HCO3- is made, so acidosis persists and the urine stays too alkaline. COMLEX: think autoimmune disease, obstruction, and amphotericin B as causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"What urine pH is characteristic of type 1 RTA despite systemic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Persistently above 5.5, because distal H+ secretion cannot lower it further","B":"Persistently below 4.4, because distal H+ secretion is maximally stimulated","C":"Variable, rising above 5.5 after bicarbonate loading of the plasma","D":"Persistently near 7.4, because filtered HCO3- escapes reabsorption","E":"Persistently between 5.0 and 5.4, because ammonium buffering is intact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hallmark of type 1 RTA is an inability to acidify the urine below about pH 5.5 even in the face of systemic acidemia, because the alpha-intercalated cell H+ pump has failed. A minimum urine pH near 4.4 is the normal physiologic limit, and a variable pattern that depends on plasma HCO3- is the type 2 pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a urine pH persistently above 5.5 in a patient with acidosis and a normal anion gap points straight to distal RTA. COMLEX: this test is inexpensive and often the first clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory pattern is characteristic of type 1 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemia with low plasma renin and low aldosterone levels","B":"Hypokalemia with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","C":"Hypokalemia with a markedly increased plasma anion gap","D":"Normal serum K+ with glucosuria and phosphaturia","E":"Hypernatremia with dilute urine and marked polyuria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 1 RTA produces hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap, and hypokalemia is a recognized accompaniment. Hyperkalemia with low aldosterone describes type 4, and glucosuria with phosphaturia describes the generalized proximal wasting of Fanconi syndrome. An increased anion gap points to accumulated unmeasured anions, which RTAs do not cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: all RTAs share a normal anion gap; potassium classically separates type 4 (high) from types 1 and 2 (low), and urine pH then separates types 1 and 2. COMLEX: recall hypokalemia, hypocitraturia, and calcium phosphate stones as the distal RTA complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination defines type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone deficiency or resistance, with hyperkalemia and a normal anion gap","B":"Alpha-intercalated pump failure, with hypokalemia and a urine pH above 5.5","C":"Proximal HCO3- wasting, with hypokalemia and glucosuria from generalized tubular dysfunction","D":"Excess mineralocorticoid activity, with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","E":"Loss of nephron mass, with hyperkalemia and an elevated plasma anion gap"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Type 4 RTA is the hyperkalemic form, caused by hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, and hyperkalemia suppresses renal NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion so the acidosis has a normal anion gap. The remaining options describe type 1, type 2, primary mineralocorticoid excess, and advanced chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 is the classic hyperkalemic RTA; recall low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance as the trigger. COMLEX: think diabetic nephropathy, adrenal insufficiency, and RAAS blockade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"How does hyperkalemia impair renal H+ excretion in type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"K+ entry blocks luminal NHE3, which stops proximal HCO3- reabsorption directly","B":"Elevated plasma K+ raises aldosterone secretion, which suppresses collecting duct H+ ATPase","C":"Elevated plasma K+ inhibits carbonic anhydrase, which prevents the formation of titratable acid","D":"K+ entry stimulates phosphate reabsorption, which removes the urinary buffer for titratable acid","E":"K+ entry raises proximal cell pH, which inhibits glutaminase and lowers NH4+ synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In hyperkalemia, K+ enters renal cells and H+ leaves, so intracellular and intramitochondrial pH rise. The higher pH inhibits glutaminase, so less NH3 is synthesized from glutamine and less H+ can be excreted as NH4+. The other options describe transporters or enzymes that hyperkalemia does not directly inhibit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia causes intracellular alkalinization of proximal cells, whereas hypokalemia does the reverse and stimulates NH3 synthesis. COMLEX: this explains why treating hyperkalemia can improve the acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"Which medication classically causes type 4 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide","B":"Hydrochlorothiazide","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Spironolactone","E":"Amphotericin B"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, producing aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia and type 4 RTA. Acetazolamide causes a proximal, type 2 pattern, amphotericin B is a cause of type 1, and loop and thiazide diuretics waste K+ and tend to cause alkalosis rather than type 4 RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the SHARDS mnemonic lists spironolactone among the causes of normal-anion-gap acidosis; it is the potassium-sparing cause. COMLEX: review medication lists in any patient with unexplained hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis has a positive urinary anion gap. What does this suggest?","choices":{"A":"Brisk renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to diarrheal HCO3- loss","B":"A high plasma anion gap, pointing to accumulated organic acids","C":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to a renal tubular cause","D":"A lowered proximal HCO3- threshold, pointing to Fanconi syndrome","E":"Excess unmeasured urinary anions, pointing to ketoacid excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urinary NH4+ is excreted with Cl-, so a low urine Cl- relative to Na+ plus K+ produces a positive gap and signals that NH4+ excretion is low. This identifies a renal defect in acid excretion, as in type 1 or type 4 RTA. Brisk NH4+ excretion produces a negative gap and points to an extrarenal source such as diarrhea.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urinary anion gap is an indirect readout of urinary NH4+; positive means little NH4+ and a renal problem. COMLEX: use it to separate diarrhea from RTA when the history is unclear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"How is the urinary anion gap calculated?","choices":{"A":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ plus urine Cl-","B":"Urine K+ minus urine Na+ minus urine Cl-","C":"Urine Na+ minus urine Cl- minus urine HCO3-","D":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine Cl-","E":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine HCO3-"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The urinary anion gap equals urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine Cl-. NH4+ is the main unmeasured urinary cation and is excreted with Cl-, so brisk NH4+ excretion makes the gap negative. Using a sum of all three ions, altered signs, or HCO3- in place of Cl- does not produce this estimate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the formula as cations minus chloride; unlike the plasma anion gap, K+ is included and HCO3- is not. COMLEX: know both the formula and the interpretation of a positive versus negative result.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"Which pairing of serum K+ and urine pH best fits type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Low serum K+ with urine pH above 5.5","B":"High serum K+ with urine pH below 5.5","C":"Low serum K+ with urine pH that varies with plasma HCO3-","D":"High serum K+ with urine pH above 5.5 and a negative urinary anion gap","E":"Normal serum K+ with urine pH fixed at 7.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In type 4 RTA hyperkalemia suppresses NH4+ synthesis, but H+ secretion is largely preserved, so the urine can still be acidified below 5.5 and the urinary anion gap is positive. Low K+ with urine above 5.5 fits type 1, and low K+ with variable urine pH fits type 2. A fixed urine pH of 7.0, or hyperkalemia with a negative urinary anion gap, does not fit a recognized RTA pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the algorithm leans mainly on two bedside numbers, serum K+ and urine pH, to sort types 1, 2, and 4. COMLEX: hyperkalemia in a normal-anion-gap acidosis should trigger the aldosterone workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with multiple myeloma has fatigue. Labs: Na+ 140 mEq/L, K+ 3.1 mEq/L, Cl- 112 mEq/L, HCO3- 16 mEq/L, serum glucose 92 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows glucosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, and a urine pH of 5.2. Which of the following best explains his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Generalized proximal tubular reabsorptive failure that wastes filtered HCO3-","B":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete H+ against a steep luminal gradient","C":"Accumulation of unmeasured organic anions from excess fixed acid production","D":"Gastrointestinal loss of HCO3- from chronic secretory diarrhea","E":"Loss of nephron mass that limits total ammonium excretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 140 - 128 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, so this is a hyperchloremic acidosis. Glucosuria with normal serum glucose, phosphaturia, and aminoaciduria show a generalized proximal reabsorptive defect, which is Fanconi syndrome, and HCO3- wasting from the same defect produces type 2 RTA. An acidic urine pH argues against failed distal H+ secretion, and there is no organic acid load or diarrhea.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple myeloma light chains are a classic acquired cause of Fanconi syndrome; wasted glucose, phosphate, amino acids, and HCO3- together mean proximal tubule failure. COMLEX: normal serum glucose with glucosuria is the vignette clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — the proximal HCO3- reclamation machinery is what fails in this generalized defect","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with untreated type 2 RTA has plasma HCO3- of 14 mEq/L and an acidic morning urine with pH 5.0. He receives an intravenous bicarbonate infusion that raises plasma HCO3- toward 24 mEq/L. Which urinary change is most likely afterward?","choices":{"A":"Urine stays acidic, with pH below 5.5, as the proximal tubule reabsorbs the added HCO3- fully","B":"Urine turns alkaline, with pH above 5.5, as filtered HCO3- exceeds reabsorptive capacity","C":"Urine pH stays fixed above 5.5 before and after loading, from a distal acidification defect","D":"Urine HCO3- excretion stays negligible because the collecting duct reclaims the excess load","E":"Urine turns markedly acidic, with pH below 4.4, as distal H+ secretion rises in compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At a plasma HCO3- of 14 mEq/L the filtered load is at or below the lowered threshold, so HCO3- is fully reabsorbed and the urine can be acidified. When plasma HCO3- is raised toward normal, the filtered load exceeds the reduced proximal reabsorptive capacity, HCO3- spills into the urine, and the urine pH rises above 5.5. His baseline urine pH of 5.0 excludes a fixed distal defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the variable urine pH, acidic when plasma HCO3- is low and alkaline when the filtered load exceeds threshold, is the signature of type 2 RTA. COMLEX: this explains why bicarbonate therapy is difficult in proximal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — the filtered load saturation of proximal HCO3- reabsorption explains the pH shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman taking acetazolamide for glaucoma develops fatigue. Labs show Na+ 139 mEq/L, Cl- 111 mEq/L, HCO3- 17 mEq/L, K+ 3.4 mEq/L, and urine pH of 5.1. Which of the following best explains her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibited alpha-intercalated cell H+ ATPase that blocks titratable acid excretion","B":"Inhibited mineralocorticoid receptors that lower distal K+ and H+ secretion","C":"Inhibited proximal carbonic anhydrase that reduces HCO3- reabsorption","D":"Inhibited thick ascending limb NKCC2 that causes volume contraction with alkalosis","E":"Inhibited mitochondrial glutaminase that blunts proximal NH4+ synthesis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits brush border and intracellular carbonic anhydrase, which impairs conversion of luminal H2CO3 to CO2 and water and therefore blocks proximal HCO3- reabsorption, mimicking type 2 RTA with a normal-anion-gap, hypokalemic acidosis. Her acidic urine pH fits the steady-state proximal pattern rather than a distal acidification defect. The other options describe drug or disease effects that would give hyperkalemia, alkalosis, or a persistently alkaline urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"acid\"azolamide causes acidosis; carbonic anhydrase inhibition equals a drug-induced proximal RTA. COMLEX: watch for this in glaucoma and altitude-sickness patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — luminal carbonic anhydrase is the enzymatic step this drug inhibits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has muscle weakness. Labs: Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 2.9 mEq/L, Cl- 112 mEq/L, HCO3- 14 mEq/L. Urine pH is 6.6 despite the acidemia. Which of the following best explains her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Proximal HCO3- wasting that exceeds a lowered reabsorptive threshold","B":"Failed alpha-intercalated H+ ATPase that prevents urinary acidification","C":"Hypoaldosteronism with hyperkalemic suppression of ammonium synthesis","D":"Accumulation of lactate from tissue hypoperfusion that raises the plasma anion gap","E":"Gastrointestinal HCO3- loss from chronic diarrhea with volume depletion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her anion gap is 138 - 126 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, and the persistent urine pH of 6.6 despite an HCO3- of 14 mEq/L shows she cannot acidify her urine. With hypokalemia, this is type 1 RTA, and autoimmune disease such as lupus is a recognized cause. Hyperkalemia is absent, so type 4 is excluded, and the normal gap rules out lactate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA causes include autoimmune disease, amphotericin B, obstruction, and analgesic nephropathy. COMLEX: a urine pH above 5.5 in a patient with acidosis is the vignette clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — this is the alpha-intercalated cell pump the disease targets","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with chronic distal RTA develops recurrent flank pain, and imaging shows medullary calcifications. Which combination of urinary abnormalities best explains his risk of calcium phosphate deposition?","choices":{"A":"Acidic urine with low citrate excretion","B":"Acidic urine with high citrate excretion","C":"Alkaline urine with high citrate excretion","D":"Alkaline urine with low citrate excretion","E":"Acidic urine with normal citrate excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the urine stays persistently alkaline, and acidosis lowers urinary citrate, a natural inhibitor of calcium crystal formation. Calcium phosphate is insoluble at high urine pH, so the combination of alkaline urine and hypocitraturia promotes calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis. The other combinations either describe acidic urine, which is not the pattern of distal RTA since failure to acidify defines it, or preserve citrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA equals calcium phosphate stones, nephrocalcinosis, and hypokalemia. COMLEX: alkali therapy corrects acidosis and restores citrate excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — persistent failure of urinary acidification at the alpha-intercalated cell drives this stone risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman receiving amphotericin B develops normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia. Which pattern of urinary H+ excretion best describes her disorder?","choices":{"A":"Reduced excretion of H+ as NH4+ with normal titratable acid, and urine pH below 5.5","B":"Reduced excretion of H+ as titratable acid with an adaptive NH4+ rise, and urine pH below 4.4","C":"Normal excretion of both urinary buffers, with a lowered proximal HCO3- threshold","D":"Increased excretion of both urinary buffers, with urine pH near 4.4","E":"Reduced excretion of H+ as both titratable acid and NH4+, with urine pH above 5.5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Amphotericin B is a recognized cause of type 1 RTA. Because alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion fails, both titratable acid and NH4+ excretion fall, no new HCO3- is generated, and the urine pH stays above 5.5. A lowered proximal HCO3- threshold describes type 2 rather than the classic amphotericin B lesion, an NH4+-only defect with urine pH below 5.5 is type 4, and normal or increased excretion of both buffers would be the appropriate renal response to acidosis, not a cause of it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in type 1 RTA both urinary buffer routes fail, whereas in type 4 the defect is confined to ammonium. COMLEX: amphotericin B, lithium, and autoimmune disease are recurrent distal RTA triggers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — the titratable acid versus ammonium comparison in acidosis frames this pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 2 diabetes and mild CKD has Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 5.9 mEq/L, Cl- 110 mEq/L, and HCO3- 17 mEq/L. Renin and aldosterone are both low, and urine pH is 5.1. Which change in his renal NH4+ excretion is most likely, and why?","choices":{"A":"Decreased, because hyperkalemia raises proximal cell pH and inhibits glutaminase","B":"Decreased, because failed H+ secretion in alpha-intercalated cells blocks distal buffering","C":"Increased, because chronic acidosis induces glutaminase in proximal cells","D":"Increased, because hyperkalemia lowers proximal cell pH and stimulates NH3 synthesis","E":"Unchanged, because NH4+ excretion depends on titratable acid formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pattern of hyperkalemia, low aldosterone, and a normal anion gap of 138 - 127 = 11 mEq/L with an acidic urine identifies type 4 RTA. Hyperkalemia moves K+ into proximal cells and H+ out, raising cell pH and inhibiting glutaminase, so NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion fall. Urine pH remains 5.1, so the distal pump is intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism in diabetic kidney disease is the most common clinical setting of type 4 RTA. COMLEX: pair hyperkalemia with mild acidosis in a diabetic patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — plasma K+ regulates proximal NH3 synthesis through intracellular pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with type 4 RTA has a urine pH of 5.0 yet persistent metabolic acidosis. Which of the following best explains this dissociation?","choices":{"A":"Distal H+ secretion is absent, but proximal HCO3- reabsorption remains fully preserved","B":"Distal H+ secretion is intact, but bicarbonaturia from a lowered threshold offsets it","C":"Distal H+ secretion is intact, but low NH4+ buffering limits total H+ excretion","D":"Distal H+ secretion is excessive, but respiratory compensation has failed completely","E":"Titratable acid excretion is absent, but hyperkalemia markedly raises NH4+ excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urine pH reflects free H+ concentration, not the total quantity of H+ excreted. In type 4 RTA the alpha-intercalated cell H+ pump works, so urine can be acidified below 5.5, but suppressed NH3 synthesis leaves little NH4+ to buffer the H+ and total acid excretion falls. Hyperkalemia lowers rather than raises NH4+ excretion, and bicarbonaturia belongs to type 2 RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish urine pH from total acid excretion, since a low pH can coexist with a poor net acid load. COMLEX: this distinction is a standard trap in RTA vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — urine pH reflects free H+ while buffer availability governs total excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with type 4 RTA is started on a low-potassium diet and a loop diuretic, and his serum K+ falls from 5.8 to 4.2 mEq/L. Which change in renal ammonium metabolism is expected?","choices":{"A":"Falling NH3 synthesis, since the lower K+ removes the stimulus for glutaminase induction","B":"Unchanged NH3 synthesis, since glutaminase activity is set by aldosterone levels","C":"Rising NH3 synthesis, since loop diuretics directly induce mitochondrial glutaminase","D":"Rising NH3 synthesis, as correcting hyperkalemia reverses proximal cell alkalinization","E":"Falling NH3 synthesis, since volume loss raises proximal HCO3- reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correcting hyperkalemia lets H+ re-enter proximal cells as K+ leaves, so intracellular and intramitochondrial pH fall back toward normal and glutaminase inhibition is lifted, restoring NH3 and NH4+ production and improving acid excretion. Glutaminase responds to intracellular pH, which is shaped by plasma K+ and by acidosis, not directly by loop diuretics or aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: treating hyperkalemia often improves type 4 acidosis because ammonium synthesis recovers. COMLEX: loop diuretics and dietary K+ restriction are standard first steps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — hypokalemia stimulates and hyperkalemia inhibits the glutaminase pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has fatigue. Labs: Na+ 137 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Cl- 112 mEq/L, HCO3- 13 mEq/L. Urine pH is 6.5, and urine Na+ 40, K+ 30, and Cl- 35 mEq/L. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 renal tubular acidosis","B":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis","C":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","D":"Diarrheal bicarbonate loss","E":"Chronic kidney disease stage 4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Her plasma anion gap is 137 - 125 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, and her urinary anion gap is 40 + 30 - 35 = +35 mEq/L, which is positive and indicates poor NH4+ excretion. Hypokalemia with a persistently alkaline urine pH of 6.5 despite acidemia identifies type 1 RTA. Diarrhea would give a negative urinary gap, hyperkalemia would appear in type 4, and type 2 would typically acidify the urine at this level of acidemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: apply the three-step algorithm, normal anion gap, then serum K+, then urine pH and urinary anion gap. COMLEX: low K+ plus urine pH above 5.5 equals distal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — a normal-anion-gap acidosis is the entry point to the RTA algorithm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with serum HCO3- of 15 mEq/L. Urine Na+ is 20, K+ 25, and Cl- 70 mEq/L. Which interpretation of his urinary anion gap is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to distal renal tubular acidosis","B":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to hypoaldosteronism with hyperkalemia","C":"Excess unmeasured plasma anions, pointing to ketoacid accumulation","D":"Aldosterone excess, pointing to primary hyperaldosteronism with alkalosis","E":"Brisk renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to an extrarenal source of HCO3- loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His urinary anion gap is 20 + 25 - 70 = -25 mEq/L, which is negative. A negative gap indicates that abundant NH4+ is being excreted with Cl-, so the kidney is responding to the acid load appropriately and the HCO3- loss is extrarenal, as in diarrhea. A positive gap would suggest impaired NH4+ excretion from type 1 or type 4 RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a negative urinary gap means brisk NH4+ excretion and intact distal acidification, pointing away from type 1 or type 4 RTA; a positive gap means impaired NH4+ excretion. COMLEX: this is the standard way to tell diarrhea from distal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — diarrhea and RTA are the two major normal-anion-gap causes to be separated","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients have hypokalemic normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, one from type 1 RTA and the other from type 2 RTA. Which finding best separates them?","choices":{"A":"Generalized glucose and phosphate wasting in type 1 but not in type 2","B":"A urine pH above 5.5 in type 1 even when plasma HCO3- is markedly reduced","C":"A raised plasma anion gap in type 2 but a normal plasma anion gap in type 1","D":"Hyperchloremia in type 1 but a normal plasma Cl- concentration in type 2","E":"Preserved respiratory compensation in type 2 but absent compensation in type 1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the distal H+ pump fails, so the urine remains above pH 5.5 even at a very low plasma HCO3-. In type 2 RTA, once plasma HCO3- has fallen below the lowered proximal threshold, the urine can be acidified below 5.5. Both types cause hyperchloremic normal-anion-gap acidosis with intact respiratory compensation, and glucose and phosphate wasting belongs to the proximal disorder, not the distal one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the type 1 versus type 2 decision rests on whether the urine can be acidified at a low plasma HCO3-. COMLEX: a bicarbonate loading test can distinguish them further.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — the limits of urinary acidification distinguish distal from proximal defects","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":26,"title":"SDL 26 — Respiratory Acid-Base Disturbances & Acid-Base Map Integration","questions":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"What does each isohydric line on the acid-base map represent?","choices":{"A":"The set of Pco2 and HCO3- pairs that produce the same pH","B":"The set of Pco2 and HCO3- pairs that produce the same anion gap","C":"The set of pH and Pco2 pairs that produce the same plasma HCO3-","D":"The set of pH and HCO3- pairs that produce the same renal compensation","E":"The expected range of values for one simple acid-base disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isohydric lines radiate from the origin of the acid-base map, and each line gives all combinations of arterial Pco2 and HCO3- that yield the same H+ concentration and therefore the same pH. Shaded areas, not isohydric lines, show the range of values for a simple disorder, and the map does not plot anion gap or renal compensation as separate lines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a normal Pco2 of 40 mm Hg with HCO3- of 24 mEq/L sits on the pH 7.4 isohydric line. COMLEX: the same line also passes through Pco2 60 with HCO3- 36 and Pco2 20 with HCO3- 12, which is why compensated disorders can have a near-normal pH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"At a normal arterial pH of 7.4, what is the ratio of HCO3- concentration to dissolved CO2 concentration?","choices":{"A":"5 to 1","B":"10 to 1","C":"20 to 1","D":"15 to 1","E":"30 to 1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With a Pco2 of 40 mm Hg the dissolved CO2 is 0.03 x 40 = 1.2 mmol/L, and with HCO3- at 24 mmol/L the ratio is 24 to 1.2, or 20 to 1. The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation then gives pH = 6.1 + log 20 = 7.4, and every isohydric line on the acid-base map preserves this same ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize 20 to 1 as the HCO3- to CO2 ratio that defines pH 7.4; disorders shift the ratio, compensation restores it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"A patient's arterial blood values fall between the shaded areas of two different disorders on the acid-base map. What does this indicate?","choices":{"A":"A laboratory error because valid blood gases fall inside a shaded area","B":"A simple metabolic disorder fully compensated by the lungs","C":"A single simple disorder with complete renal compensation","D":"Two or more acid-base disorders are present at the same time","E":"Normal acid-base balance with abnormally low buffer stores"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When a patient's values fall inside a shaded area, a single simple disorder can be concluded. Values that fall outside the shaded areas, including values that fall between two areas, indicate a mixed disorder. A simple metabolic disorder with full respiratory compensation would still lie within its own shaded area, and complete renal compensation would place the values in the chronic shaded area.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the acid-base map and the renal rules answer the same question, whether the observed compensation matches a simple disorder. COMLEX: think mixed disorder whenever the numbers do not fit any shaded area.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"According to the renal rules, how much does HCO3- rise for each 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2 in acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"0.4 mEq/L","B":"0.1 mEq/L","C":"0.6 mEq/L","D":"1.0 mEq/L","E":"1.2 mEq/L"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In acute respiratory acidosis the renal compensation has not yet developed, so the small rise in HCO3- reflects mass action alone, about 0.1 mEq/L for every 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2. The values 0.6 and 1.2 belong to metabolic disorders, where they relate Pco2 to HCO3- in the opposite direction, and the chronic rules are larger than the acute rule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute respiratory rules are small because only mass action is operating; chronic rules are larger because the kidney has responded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal rule applies to chronic respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"HCO3- falls 0.4 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2","B":"Pco2 falls 1.2 mm Hg for each 1 mEq/L fall in HCO3-","C":"Pco2 rises 0.6 mm Hg for each 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-","D":"HCO3- rises 0.1 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2","E":"HCO3- falls 0.2 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The renal rule for chronic respiratory alkalosis predicts a 0.4 mEq/L fall in HCO3- for every 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2, larger than the acute prediction of 0.2 mEq/L because renal compensation has had time to reduce H+ excretion and new HCO3- reabsorption. The other options are the rules for metabolic acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, acute respiratory acidosis, and acute respiratory alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: for every respiratory disorder the chronic rule is larger than the acute rule. COMLEX: use these rules to decide whether a patient is acute or chronic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the compensation for a primary respiratory disorder renal rather than respiratory?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are unresponsive to changes in arterial Pco2","B":"The lungs cannot change Pco2 without a preceding change in renal HCO3- handling","C":"Intracellular buffers remove the change in Pco2 before the lungs can respond","D":"Respiration is itself the cause of the disorder and cannot correct it","E":"The medullary respiratory center is suppressed by any change in plasma pH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a respiratory disorder, ventilation is the cause of the abnormal Pco2, so changing ventilation cannot compensate. The kidney instead adjusts the HCO3- concentration to bring the HCO3- to CO2 ratio, and therefore the pH, back toward normal. Metabolic disorders, in contrast, are compensated immediately by ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic disorders are compensated by the lungs and respiratory disorders by the kidneys. COMLEX: use the rule of thumb that the compensating organ is generally not the organ causing the primary problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Where does buffering of excess CO2 occur in acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Primarily in extracellular fluid, through the HCO3-/CO2 buffer system","B":"Primarily in the urine, through excretion of titratable acid","C":"Primarily in plasma, through binding of H+ to albumin","D":"Primarily in the lungs, through increased alveolar ventilation","E":"Primarily in intracellular fluid, especially within red blood cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lipid-soluble CO2 diffuses into cells, especially red blood cells, where it is converted to H+ and HCO3-, and the H+ is buffered by hemoglobin, other proteins, and organic phosphates. The HCO3-/CO2 buffer cannot buffer a CO2 excess because CO2 is one of its own components, and urinary excretion and ventilation are not chemical buffering steps in acute respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the respiratory disorders are buffered in intracellular fluid, while metabolic disorders are first buffered by extracellular HCO3-. COMLEX: deoxyhemoglobin is an even more effective buffer than oxyhemoglobin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Why is arterial pH lower in acute respiratory acidosis than in chronic respiratory acidosis at the same Pco2?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory compensation is stronger during the chronic phase","B":"Renal compensation has yet to raise HCO3- in the acute phase","C":"Intracellular buffers become exhausted during the acute phase","D":"Pco2 is higher during the acute phase by definition","E":"Hypoventilation is corrected during the chronic phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The difference between acute and chronic respiratory acidosis lies in renal compensation. In the acute phase HCO3- rises only by mass action, so the HCO3-/CO2 ratio is low and pH is quite low. In the chronic phase the kidney excretes more H+ and adds new HCO3-, which raises HCO3-, restores the ratio toward 20 to 1, and moves pH toward normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: same Pco2, different HCO3-, different pH; the extra HCO3- is the renal compensation. COMLEX: an acutely worsening COPD patient can drop pH sharply from a compensated baseline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal changes underlie compensation for chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Greater excretion of H+ as titratable acid and NH4+ with new HCO3- reabsorption","B":"Increased HCO3- excretion in the urine with reduced NH3 synthesis in proximal cells","C":"Decreased alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion with greater glutaminase activity","D":"Increased proximal HCO3- secretion with decreased NHE3 activity in the tubule","E":"Increased urinary phosphate loss with decreased titratable acid formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal compensation for respiratory acidosis increases H+ excretion as titratable acid and NH4+ and increases synthesis and reabsorption of new HCO3-, which raises plasma HCO3- beyond the mass-action effect. The alternatives describe changes that would lower plasma HCO3- or that do not correspond to any known compensatory pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kidney compensates for acidemia by adding new HCO3- through titratable acid and ammonium excretion. COMLEX: the process takes several days to become complete.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Which arterial blood profile defines primary respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Low pH with low HCO3- and a compensatory fall in Pco2","B":"High pH with high HCO3- and a compensatory rise in Pco2","C":"Low pH with high Pco2 and a compensatory rise in HCO3-","D":"Normal pH with low Pco2 and an unexplained rise in HCO3-","E":"High pH with low Pco2 and a compensatory fall in HCO3-"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperventilation blows off CO2, so Pco2 falls first and pH rises, and HCO3- falls both by mass action and by renal compensation. The other profiles correspond to metabolic acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, respiratory acidosis, and a combination that no simple disorder produces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a respiratory disorder pH and Pco2 move in opposite directions, while HCO3- moves with Pco2 as compensation. COMLEX: in metabolic disorders pH and HCO3- move together, so ask which variable can explain the pH shift to find the primary disturbance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Why does alkalemia cause tingling, numbness, and tetany?","choices":{"A":"Albumin releases Ca2+, so ionized Ca2+ rises and prolongs membrane depolarization","B":"Less H+ binds albumin, so more Ca2+ binds and ionized Ca2+ falls","C":"Alkalemia raises PTH release, which lowers total serum Ca2+ through renal wasting","D":"Alkalemia lowers extracellular K+, which hyperpolarizes nerve and muscle membranes","E":"Alkalemia reduces intestinal Ca2+ absorption, which lowers total body Ca2+ within minutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Negatively charged groups on albumin bind either H+ or Ca2+. In alkalemia less H+ is bound, so more Ca2+ binds to albumin and the free ionized Ca2+ falls. Low extracellular Ca2+ makes the threshold potential of nerves and muscles more negative and increases excitability, producing tingling, numbness, and tetany.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pH changes ionized Ca2+ without changing total Ca2+; alkalemia lowers it and acidemia raises it. COMLEX: this is why anxious hyperventilating patients have perioral and acral paresthesias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a cause of respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Opioid overdose","B":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","C":"Salicylate poisoning","D":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","E":"Guillain-Barre syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salicylates directly stimulate the medullary respiratory center, producing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, in addition to a metabolic acidosis. Opioids depress the respiratory center, ALS and Guillain-Barre syndrome weaken respiratory muscles, and COPD impairs gas exchange, so all four cause respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: causes of respiratory alkalosis include hysterical hyperventilation, salicylate poisoning, gram-negative sepsis, hypoxemia, and mechanical ventilation. COMLEX: salicylate toxicity is the classic mixed respiratory alkalosis with metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with severe COPD has Pco2 60 mm Hg and HCO3- 36 mEq/L. Substitution into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives a pH of 7.4. Which statement best describes his acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"A near-normal pH despite abnormal values because HCO3- offsets the high Pco2","B":"Normal acid-base status because a normal pH excludes any acid-base disorder","C":"A laboratory error because Pco2 and HCO3- cannot both be elevated at a normal pH","D":"A metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation that fully normalized the pH","E":"An acute respiratory acidosis because the pH has not fallen below the normal range"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ratio of 36 to 0.03 x 60 = 1.8 is 20 to 1, so the pH is 7.4, but both Pco2 and HCO3- are clearly abnormal. This is the principle underlying compensation: abnormal combinations of Pco2 and HCO3- on the same isohydric line yield a normal pH. Deciding whether such a pattern is a pure chronic respiratory acidosis or a mixed disorder requires the compensation rules, not the pH. His HCO3- of 36 is higher than a simple chronic respiratory acidosis alone would predict, so a coexisting metabolic alkalosis is possible, but the isohydric principle holds either way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a normal pH does not exclude an acid-base disorder, since compensation can restore the ratio. COMLEX: check Pco2 and HCO3- separately before calling a blood gas normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — the acid-base map places compensated and mixed disorders on isohydric lines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has arterial values of pH 7.56, Pco2 23 mm Hg, and HCO3- 20 mEq/L after an episode of uncontrolled hyperventilation. Which pattern on the acid-base map is most consistent with her values?","choices":{"A":"The shaded area for chronic respiratory alkalosis","B":"The shaded area for simple metabolic alkalosis","C":"The shaded area for acute respiratory acidosis","D":"The shaded area for acute respiratory alkalosis","E":"A position between two shaded areas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Her high pH with low Pco2 and modestly low HCO3- identifies respiratory alkalosis. The HCO3- fall of only 4 mEq/L from mass action and minimal renal adjustment matches the acute rule of 0.2 mEq/L per mm Hg, since a 17 mm Hg fall predicts about 3.4 mEq/L. The chronic rule would predict a much larger fall of about 6.8 mEq/L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: compare the observed HCO3- change with the acute and chronic predictions to place a respiratory disorder on the map. COMLEX: a near-normal HCO3- with a very high pH means the disorder is acute.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — mixed versus simple disorders are separated by whether values fall in a shaded area","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has arterial pH 7.29, Pco2 70 mm Hg, and HCO3- 33 mEq/L. Which of the following best describes the primary disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic alkalosis, because HCO3- is elevated above the normal value of 24 mEq/L","B":"Respiratory acidosis, because the elevated Pco2 explains the acidemia","C":"Metabolic acidosis, because pH is below 7.4 and HCO3- is abnormal","D":"Respiratory alkalosis, because HCO3- is elevated after prolonged hyperventilation","E":"A primary disturbance cannot be identified without an anion gap measurement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pH of 7.29 indicates acidemia, and the disturbance that explains that direction is the elevated Pco2, so the primary disorder is respiratory acidosis. The elevated HCO3- is the compensatory response, not the primary disturbance, because a primary metabolic alkalosis would raise pH, not lower it. The anion gap is used to differentiate metabolic acidoses and is not needed here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the primary disturbance is the variable whose change would explain the direction of the pH shift, here a high Pco2 for a low pH. COMLEX: this step must come before any compensation calculation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — identifying the primary disturbance is the first step of any blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a long history of COPD has pH 7.29, Pco2 70 mm Hg, and HCO3- 33 mEq/L, a rise of 9 mEq/L above normal. The renal rules predict a rise of 3 mEq/L if the disorder is acute and a considerably larger rise if it has persisted for weeks. Which best describes his condition?","choices":{"A":"A new episode of CO2 retention with no renal compensation yet under way","B":"A primary metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation raising Pco2","C":"A primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation raising HCO3- further","D":"Long-standing CO2 retention with renal compensation already in place","E":"A primary metabolic acidosis with compensatory hypoventilation raising Pco2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"His HCO3- rise of 9 mEq/L is much greater than the 3 mEq/L predicted for acute respiratory acidosis and lies nearer the chronic prediction, indicating renal compensation from long-standing CO2 retention. Metabolic alkalosis would produce alkalemia, and metabolic acidosis would lower HCO3-, while respiratory alkalosis would lower Pco2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an HCO3- rise far above the acute prediction points to chronic respiratory acidosis, as in COPD. COMLEX: a chronic lung disease history in the vignette supports the chronic pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the delta rules are applied here to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with test anxiety has pH 7.56, Pco2 23 mm Hg, and HCO3- 20 mEq/L, a fall of 4 mEq/L from normal. A rule for acute disorders predicts a fall of 3.4 mEq/L for her Pco2 change, and a rule for chronic disorders predicts a fall of 6.8 mEq/L. Which best describes her disorder?","choices":{"A":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis, since the HCO3- fall approaches 6.8 mEq/L","B":"Acute respiratory alkalosis, since the HCO3- fall is close to 3.4 mEq/L","C":"Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation, since pH is high and HCO3- is below 24 mEq/L","D":"Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, since HCO3- is below the normal value","E":"Acute respiratory acidosis, since pH and HCO3- are both changed from normal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her Pco2 has fallen by 17 mm Hg, and her HCO3- has fallen by 4 mEq/L, which matches the acute prediction of 3.4 mEq/L far better than the chronic prediction of 6.8 mEq/L. The high pH with low Pco2 identifies a primary respiratory alkalosis. Metabolic disorders would move HCO3- and pH in the same direction, and acute respiratory acidosis would lower pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute respiratory alkalosis has a high pH and only a small HCO3- fall from mass action. COMLEX: anxiety-driven hyperventilation is the classic acute case.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied here to a primary respiratory disturbance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A mountaineer who has spent five days at high altitude has pH 7.48, Pco2 28 mm Hg, and HCO3- 20 mEq/L. Which best explains his HCO3- value?","choices":{"A":"Immediate mass action from lost CO2, the same change expected within minutes","B":"Respiratory compensation from the medullary center after the initial hyperventilation","C":"Intracellular buffering by hemoglobin, which lowers plasma HCO3- immediately","D":"Increased urinary excretion of titratable acid, which removes plasma HCO3- directly","E":"Renal compensation, with decreased H+ excretion and less new HCO3- reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hypoxemia at altitude stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. After several days the kidney decreases H+ excretion as titratable acid and NH4+ and reabsorbs less new HCO3-, so his HCO3- fall of 4 mEq/L is larger than the acute rule predicts for a 12 mm Hg fall in Pco2 and consistent with a chronic response. There is no respiratory compensation for a respiratory disorder, and excreting titratable acid would add HCO3-.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoxemia from altitude, pneumonia, or embolism stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors and produces respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: time course of exposure tells you whether the kidney has had time to compensate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the delta rules distinguish acute from chronic respiratory alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman in a severe asthma exacerbation has pH 7.21, Pco2 68 mm Hg, and HCO3- 26 mEq/L after two hours of shallow breathing. Which best explains her HCO3- concentration?","choices":{"A":"Mass action from CO2 hydration before renal compensation has begun","B":"Complete renal compensation from increased new HCO3- reabsorption over several hours","C":"Respiratory compensation from medullary stimulation that has raised her ventilation","D":"A coexisting metabolic alkalosis that balances the respiratory acidosis","E":"Extracellular buffering of CO2 by plasma proteins that releases HCO3-"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Retained CO2 is hydrated to H2CO3 and dissociates, so HCO3- rises slightly by mass action, but the kidney has not had time to add new HCO3-, so pH is quite low. There is no respiratory compensation for a respiratory disorder, complete renal compensation takes several days, and a coexisting metabolic alkalosis would push HCO3- much higher.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute respiratory acidosis HCO3- rises only slightly, and pH tracks the Pco2. COMLEX: a lower-than-expected pH in an acutely worsening asthmatic signals impending respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — CO2 buffering occurs within cells rather than by extracellular HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with chronic hypercapnia shows increased urinary NH4+ excretion and enhanced bicarbonate generation. Which effect of his elevated Pco2 on renal cells most directly supports this compensation?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition that lowers filtered HCO3- reclamation","B":"Luminal alkalinization that favors NH3 diffusion trapping in the collecting duct","C":"Greater intracellular CO2 supplies more H+ for secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","D":"Glutaminase suppression that lowers proximal NH4+ synthesis and its excretion","E":"Phosphate depletion in tubular cells that increases titratable acid excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Increased Pco2 raises the CO2 supply inside proximal and alpha-intercalated cells, which combines with water to generate more H+ for secretion and more HCO3- for reabsorption into blood, so more H+ is excreted as titratable acid and NH4+ and more new HCO3- is added. The other options describe effects that would either reduce HCO3- generation or that do not occur in hypercapnia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: arterial Pco2 is a direct determinant of renal H+ secretion, which is why chronic hypercapnia is compensated by the kidney. COMLEX: this is the mechanistic basis for the higher chronic rule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — arterial Pco2 modifies proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients have the same Pco2 of 70 mm Hg. Patient X developed the hypercapnia over 30 minutes, and patient Y has had it for several weeks. Which comparison of their blood gases is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Patient Y has the lower pH because prolonged CO2 retention exhausts intracellular buffers","B":"Patient X has the higher HCO3- because acute CO2 hydration is the larger effect","C":"Both patients have the same HCO3- and pH because their Pco2 values are equal","D":"Patient X has the pH closer to normal because immediate buffering is complete","E":"Patient Y has a higher HCO3- and a pH closer to normal than patient X"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Patient X is in the acute phase with almost no renal compensation, so HCO3- is only slightly elevated and pH is quite low. Patient Y has had weeks for the kidney to raise HCO3- through greater H+ excretion and new HCO3- generation, which restores the HCO3- to CO2 ratio partway, so pH is closer to normal. Equal Pco2 does not mean equal HCO3-.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic respiratory acidosis has a higher HCO3- and a less severe acidemia than acute respiratory acidosis at the same Pco2. COMLEX: this is why a chronic CO2 retainer tolerates a Pco2 that would be dangerous in an acute setting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the difference between acute and chronic rules is the renal compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old graduate student hyperventilates before an examination and develops light-headedness and tingling in her hands and feet. Arterial pH is 7.56, Pco2 is 23 mm Hg, and HCO3- is 20 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains the tingling?","choices":{"A":"Decreased ionized Ca2+ from greater albumin binding that raises excitability","B":"Decreased Pco2 that causes cerebral vasoconstriction and cerebral ischemia","C":"Decreased total body Ca2+ from renal calcium wasting after alkalemia","D":"Increased HCO3- excretion that produces hypokalemia and generalized weakness","E":"Decreased HCO3- that lowers plasma osmolality and causes neuronal swelling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alkalemia reduces H+ binding to albumin, so more Ca2+ is bound, ionized Ca2+ falls, the threshold potential of nerves becomes more negative, and excitability rises, producing tingling and numbness. Her light-headedness is explained separately by cerebral arteriolar vasoconstriction from the low Pco2. Total body Ca2+ is unchanged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish the two consequences of hypocapnia, cerebral vasoconstriction for light-headedness and low ionized Ca2+ for paresthesias. COMLEX: exam-anxiety hyperventilation is a classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — ionized Ca2+ and its effect on excitability are the link to this presentation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old student hyperventilating from anxiety before an exam has pH 7.56 and Pco2 23 mm Hg, with tingling around her mouth. She breathes into and out of a paper bag and quickly feels better. Which mechanism best explains her improvement?","choices":{"A":"Rebreathing raises plasma HCO3- through renal compensation within minutes","B":"Rebreathing increases H+ excretion, which raises albumin-bound Ca2+","C":"Rebreathing raises Pco2 to lower pH and restore ionized Ca2+","D":"Rebreathing supplies oxygen that corrects the hypoxemia driving her ventilation","E":"Rebreathing lowers Pco2, which relieves the cerebral vasoconstriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By rebreathing her own CO2 she raises Pco2 to normal, which restores the HCO3- to CO2 ratio and lowers the pH. Falling pH increases H+ binding to albumin, so less Ca2+ is bound and ionized Ca2+ rises, relieving the excitability. Renal compensation cannot act within minutes, her ventilation was driven by anxiety and not hypoxemia, and rebreathing raises rather than lowers Pco2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: treating the cause of hyperventilation, by restoring normal Pco2, resolves both the alkalemia and the low ionized Ca2+. COMLEX: reassurance and coached slow breathing or brief rebreathing are the textbook approach to uncomplicated anxiety hyperventilation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — normalizing pH restores ionized Ca2+ without changing total calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic hyperventilation from a brainstem lesion has a persistently low Pco2 and a low plasma HCO3- beyond what mass action predicts. Which mechanism explains the additional HCO3- loss?","choices":{"A":"A rising GFR filters more HCO3- than the proximal tubule can reabsorb","B":"Less CO2 in renal cells reduces H+ secretion and new HCO3- generation","C":"Aldosterone suppression blocks alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion and wastes HCO3- directly","D":"Increased proximal NHE3 activity raises H+ secretion and dilutes plasma HCO3-","E":"Stimulated glutaminase increases NH4+ excretion, which removes plasma HCO3-"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In respiratory alkalosis the low Pco2 leaves less CO2 available in renal cells to generate H+ for secretion, so H+ excretion as titratable acid and NH4+ falls and less new HCO3- is synthesized and reabsorbed. This decreased HCO3- reabsorption lowers plasma HCO3- beyond the mass-action effect and is the renal compensation. Stimulated glutaminase would add HCO3-, and none of the other listed mechanisms is the compensatory pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kidney compensates for alkalemia by wasting HCO3-, which is why chronic respiratory alkalosis has a larger HCO3- fall than the acute form. COMLEX: CNS disease, salicylates, and pregnancy are chronic hyperventilation causes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — arterial Pco2 governs proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"SDL 27 — Male Reproductive Architecture, Spermatogenesis, & Accessory Glands","questions":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"What proportion of the adult testis is composed of seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"About 20 percent of testicular volume","B":"About 80 percent of testicular volume","C":"About 50 percent of testicular volume","D":"About 65 percent of testicular volume","E":"About 95 percent of testicular volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 80 percent of the adult testis is seminiferous tubules, the site of sperm production, and the remaining 20 percent is connective tissue interspersed with Leydig cells that secrete testosterone. The other percentages do not match this compartment ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 80 to 20 split, seminiferous tubules to interstitium, since it anchors questions about which testicular function a lesion disrupts. COMLEX: the interstitial compartment is the smaller of the two but holds the endocrine cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular cell type synthesizes testosterone?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cell","B":"Spermatogonium","C":"Granulosa cell","D":"Primary spermatocyte","E":"Leydig cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leydig cells in the interstitial connective tissue synthesize and secrete testosterone in response to LH. Sertoli cells support developing sperm and secrete antimullerian hormone, androgen-binding protein, and inhibin, spermatogonia and spermatocytes are germ cells, and granulosa cells are ovarian.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leydig cells make testosterone and Sertoli cells make sperm-supporting products; LH acts on the former and FSH on the latter. COMLEX: link the two cell types to the two testicular compartments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell population serves as the stem cells of the seminiferous epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia","B":"Sertoli cells","C":"Round spermatids","D":"Leydig cells","E":"Mature spermatozoa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spermatogonia are the stem cells of the seminiferous epithelium and divide mitotically to generate the spermatocytes destined to become mature sperm. Sertoli cells are supporting cells, spermatids and spermatozoa are later products, and Leydig cells lie outside the tubules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lining of the seminiferous tubules holds three cell types, spermatogonia as stem cells, spermatocytes in transit, and Sertoli cells as support. COMLEX: cryptorchidism and heat injure this epithelium first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"What structure forms the blood-testis barrier?","choices":{"A":"Gap junctions between adjacent Leydig cells","B":"Desmosomes anchoring spermatogonia to the basement membrane","C":"The fibrous tunica albuginea surrounding the entire testis","D":"The fenestrated endothelium of interstitial capillaries","E":"Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sertoli cells form tight junctions with one another along the seminiferous epithelium, creating the blood-testis barrier that isolates developing sperm from the bloodstream. It admits allowable substances such as testosterone while excluding noxious substances that could damage the germ cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the blood-testis barrier is a Sertoli cell to Sertoli cell junction, not a vascular or capsular structure. COMLEX: it divides the tubule into a basal compartment and an adluminal compartment where meiosis and spermiogenesis proceed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"Which role does the blood-testis barrier serve for developing germ cells?","choices":{"A":"It shields haploid germ cells from immune recognition","B":"It carries testosterone out of the tubule and into the plasma","C":"It lets circulating antibodies reach meiotic germ cells freely","D":"It concentrates FSH within the adluminal compartment","E":"It converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone for germ cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Haploid germ cells appear after puberty and carry antigens the immune system has not learned to tolerate, so the barrier confers immune privilege by sequestering them from circulating immune cells and antibodies. The barrier is not a route for exporting testosterone, does not admit antibodies freely, and does not concentrate FSH or convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune privilege of the testis explains why antisperm antibodies follow testicular trauma or infection. COMLEX: breach of the barrier is the mechanism to remember for autoimmune infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"What is the function of androgen-binding protein in the seminiferous tubule?","choices":{"A":"It carries testosterone through the plasma to distant target tissues","B":"It converts testosterone to estradiol within the seminiferous tubule","C":"It inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary gland","D":"It keeps local testosterone levels high near developing sperm cells","E":"It suppresses antimullerian hormone release from fetal testes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete androgen-binding protein into the tubule lumen near developing sperm, where it binds testosterone and helps maintain a high local concentration, reinforcing FSH action on spermatogenesis. Transport in plasma is the role of sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin, and inhibin, not androgen-binding protein, feeds back on FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: androgen-binding protein acts locally in the tubule, whereas SHBG acts in the circulation. COMLEX: high intratubular testosterone is required for spermatogenesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell is produced when a primary spermatocyte completes meiosis I?","choices":{"A":"Type A spermatogonial cell","B":"Secondary spermatocyte","C":"Round spermatid","D":"Primary spermatocyte","E":"Mature spermatozoon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meiosis I is the reductional division that converts a diploid primary spermatocyte into two haploid secondary spermatocytes. Meiosis II then divides each secondary spermatocyte into spermatids, which mature into spermatozoa by spermiogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte marks meiosis I, and secondary spermatocyte to spermatid marks meiosis II. COMLEX: know which step halves the chromosome number.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits meiosis I in spermatogenesis?","choices":{"A":"An equational division that separates sister chromatids and keeps the diploid number","B":"A mitotic division that maintains ploidy while increasing spermatogonial numbers","C":"A reductional division that halves the chromosome number from diploid to haploid","D":"A remodeling step that converts round spermatids into flagellated sperm","E":"A division that begins after the acrosome has formed on the spermatid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, so the chromosome number falls from diploid to haploid, whereas meiosis II is the equational division that separates sister chromatids. Mitotic spermatogonial divisions maintain ploidy, and remodeling of spermatids is spermiogenesis, not a meiotic division.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meiosis I is reductional and meiosis II is equational, which explains why aneuploidy from nondisjunction arises at either step. COMLEX: relate the ploidy transitions to genetic counseling questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"What occurs during spermiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia divide mitotically to generate spermatocytes","B":"Primary spermatocytes undergo reduction division to produce haploid cells","C":"Secondary spermatocytes replicate DNA to restore the diploid number","D":"Spermatids lose cytoplasm and develop flagella to become mature sperm","E":"Sertoli cells transform into Leydig cells that secrete testosterone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spermiogenesis is the third phase of spermatogenesis, in which haploid spermatids are transformed into mature sperm through loss of cytoplasm and development of flagella, without further division. Mitosis of spermatogonia is the first phase and meiosis of spermatocytes is the second.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spermatogenesis has three phases, mitosis, meiosis, and spermiogenesis, and only the last involves no cell division. COMLEX: one full cycle takes about 64 days.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which accessory structure contributes a fluid rich in fructose, citrate, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen to the ejaculate?","choices":{"A":"Seminal vesicles","B":"Prostate gland","C":"Epididymal head","D":"Bulbourethral glands","E":"Rete testis tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The seminal vesicles empty into the ejaculatory ducts and contribute a nutritive fluid rich in fructose, citrate, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen. The prostate adds a milky fluid rich in citrate, calcium, and enzymes, and the epididymis and rete testis store and transport sperm rather than adding this fluid. The bulbourethral glands add only a small mucous pre-ejaculate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose in semen comes mainly from the seminal vesicles, and its absence suggests ejaculatory duct obstruction. COMLEX: the accessory glands together account for about 90 percent of semen volume.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits prostatic fluid?","choices":{"A":"An acidic viscous fluid containing fructose and prostaglandins","B":"A neutral watery fluid containing androgen-binding protein and inhibin","C":"A slightly alkaline milky fluid containing PSA and zinc","D":"A dense sperm-rich fluid stored for months and containing spermatozoa","E":"A clear mucous fluid containing fibrinogen and seminal coagulum proteins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prostatic secretion is a milky aqueous solution rich in citrate, calcium, and enzymes, with PSA and zinc, and it is slightly alkaline, which increases sperm motility and neutralizes acidic secretions. Fructose and prostaglandins come from the seminal vesicles, androgen-binding protein and inhibin from Sertoli cells, and stored sperm from the epididymis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PSA is a prostatic serine protease, and zinc is concentrated in prostatic secretion. COMLEX: know which gland contributes each component to interpret semen analysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which process occurs during sperm capacitation in the female reproductive tract?","choices":{"A":"Addition of prostatic enzymes to the sperm surface before ejaculation","B":"Removal of seminal inhibitory factors and membrane cholesterol","C":"Fusion of the acrosomal membrane with the oocyte plasma membrane","D":"Completion of meiosis II by the oocyte in the fallopian tube","E":"Conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone within the epididymis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Capacitation takes 4 to 6 hours in the female tract and washes inhibitory factors from the sperm, withdraws membrane cholesterol, redistributes surface proteins, and allows Ca2+ influx that increases whiplike motility. It also permits the acrosomal reaction. Completion of meiosis II is an oocyte event after fertilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: freshly ejaculated sperm cannot fertilize until capacitation has occurred. COMLEX: the acrosomal reaction is enabled by capacitation and releases enzymes that let sperm penetrate the ovum coverings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with infertility. Semen analysis shows a severely reduced sperm count, while serum testosterone is normal and libido is intact. Which pattern of testicular involvement is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired seminiferous tubules with preserved Leydig cell function","B":"Impaired interstitial cells with continued seminiferous tubule sperm production","C":"Equal impairment of both compartments from a shared blood supply","D":"Impaired interstitial cells with loss of androgen-binding protein secretion","E":"Impaired accessory glands with normal seminiferous tubule function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules, which make up about 80 percent of the testis, whereas testosterone is produced by Leydig cells in the interstitium. Normal testosterone and libido with a low sperm count indicate that the tubules are selectively affected while the Leydig cells work normally. Accessory gland disease alters semen volume and composition, not sperm production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: separate the two testicular functions, sperm production in the tubules and testosterone in the interstitium, because lesions can affect them independently. COMLEX: normal androgen effects with infertility point to a tubule problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Leydig cell steroidogenesis explains why testosterone remains normal in tubule disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with bilateral intra-abdominal undescended testes has azoospermia. Which mechanism best explains the failure of sperm production?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cells cannot synthesize testosterone in the abdominal environment","B":"The blood-testis barrier fails to form when the testis is not in the scrotum","C":"Sertoli cells stop secreting androgen-binding protein once the testis is intra-abdominal","D":"Core temperature exceeds the lower scrotal temperature that spermatogenesis needs","E":"Abdominal blood flow is too low to nourish the developing germ cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The scrotum is maintained 1 to 2 degrees C below body temperature by a countercurrent arrangement of testicular arteries and veins, and this lower temperature is essential for normal spermatogenesis. Undescended testes remain at core temperature, so germ cell maturation fails and azoospermia results. The other options describe cellular failures that are not established as the cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: temperature sensitivity of spermatogenesis explains why cryptorchidism, varicocele, and fever impair sperm production. COMLEX: orchiopexy is done early to protect germ cell function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — cryptorchidism produces progressive seminiferous tubule atrophy and heightened cancer risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A toxin selectively destroys Leydig cells but spares the seminiferous tubule epithelium. Which change is expected first?","choices":{"A":"Falling antimullerian hormone from loss of Sertoli cell secretion","B":"Falling testosterone with a rise in LH from loss of negative feedback","C":"Rising inhibin B from expansion of Sertoli cell activity","D":"Failure of blood-testis barrier formation from loss of tight junctions","E":"Rising testosterone from compensatory Sertoli cell steroidogenesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leydig cells synthesize testosterone, and when testosterone falls there is less negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, so GnRH and LH secretion increase. Sertoli cells are spared, so antimullerian hormone and inhibin are unaffected and barrier formation is not the first change, and Sertoli cells do not synthesize testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone loss raises LH, and Leydig failure therefore produces high LH with low testosterone. COMLEX: this pattern helps separate primary testicular failure from pituitary disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — testosterone negative feedback on hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man develops antisperm antibodies after severe testicular trauma. Which mechanism best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cell destruction removed testosterone-mediated tolerance of germ cell antigens","B":"Loss of androgen-binding protein allowed circulating antibodies into the interstitium","C":"Failure of FSH signaling in Sertoli cells permitted recognition of spermatogonia","D":"Damaged Sertoli tight junctions exposed haploid germ cell antigens to immune cells","E":"Elevated inhibin B suppressed regulatory immune cells within the seminiferous tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Haploid germ cells appear after puberty and are hidden from the immune system behind Sertoli cell tight junctions, which form the blood-testis barrier. Trauma that disrupts these junctions exposes their antigens to immune cells and provokes antisperm antibody formation. Androgen-binding protein, FSH signaling, Leydig cell function, and inhibin are not the primary determinants of this barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sequestered antigens behind the blood-testis barrier explain immune infertility after trauma, infection, or vasectomy. COMLEX: the same principle applies to sympathetic ophthalmia in the eye.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — testicular injury and its consequences for the seminiferous epithelium","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with infertility has selective Sertoli cell damage and intact Leydig cell function. Which hormone panel is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Elevated LH with low testosterone and a normal FSH level","B":"Low FSH with elevated inhibin B and a normal LH level","C":"Elevated FSH with normal LH and normal testosterone","D":"Low FSH and low LH with low testosterone levels","E":"Elevated FSH and LH with elevated testosterone levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, which selectively inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary. With Sertoli cell damage, inhibin falls and FSH rises, whereas Leydig cells continue to make normal testosterone, so LH and testosterone remain normal. Low inhibin, not high inhibin, accompanies Sertoli cell failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inhibin from Sertoli cells is the FSH-selective feedback signal, and a high isolated FSH marks tubule damage. COMLEX: use FSH as a marker of spermatogenic status in male infertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — inhibin B selectively inhibits pituitary FSH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"A mutation abolishes androgen-binding protein secretion by Sertoli cells, while plasma testosterone stays normal. Which consequence is expected?","choices":{"A":"Lower local testosterone near developing sperm that impairs sperm production","B":"Reduced antimullerian hormone in the fetus that leaves retained mullerian structures","C":"Reduced plasma SHBG that raises free testosterone in the circulation","D":"Impaired blood-testis barrier formation that exposes germ cells to plasma","E":"Impaired Leydig cell testosterone synthesis from lost LH receptor signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Androgen-binding protein is secreted into the tubule lumen, where it keeps local testosterone high near developing sperm. Without it, intratubular testosterone falls even though plasma levels are normal, so spermatogenesis is impaired. It does not affect antimullerian hormone, liver-derived plasma SHBG, tight junction formation, or Leydig cell signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish local testicular androgen concentration from plasma testosterone; spermatogenesis depends on the former. COMLEX: low intratubular testosterone impairs spermatogenesis even when plasma testosterone is normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — collapse of intratesticular testosterone underlies anabolic steroid azoospermia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A single primary spermatocyte completes both meiotic divisions and spermiogenesis. How many mature spermatozoa result, and what is their ploidy?","choices":{"A":"Two haploid spermatozoa","B":"Four haploid spermatozoa","C":"Four diploid spermatozoa","D":"Two diploid spermatozoa","E":"One haploid spermatozoon and three polar bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meiosis I produces two haploid secondary spermatocytes, and meiosis II divides each into two spermatids, giving four haploid spermatids that mature into four haploid spermatozoa by spermiogenesis. Polar bodies are a feature of oogenesis, not spermatogenesis, because the male divisions are symmetric.","boardPrep":"Step 1: one primary spermatocyte gives four sperm, whereas one primary oocyte gives one ovum. COMLEX: all four products are functional, unlike in oogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback reflects the state of spermatogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man has a mutation causing spermatogenic arrest at the primary spermatocyte stage. Which cell types are absent from his seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia, primary spermatocytes, and Sertoli cells","B":"Primary spermatocytes, spermatogonia, and secondary spermatocytes","C":"Spermatogonia, spermatids, and primary spermatocytes","D":"Secondary spermatocytes and Sertoli cells","E":"Secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and spermatozoa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Arrest at the primary spermatocyte stage means the cells cannot complete meiosis I, so all later stages, secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and spermatozoa, are absent. Spermatogonia and primary spermatocytes are present, Sertoli cells are supporting cells that persist, and Leydig cells lie in the interstitium outside the tubules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: order the stages of spermatogenesis, spermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, spermatozoon. COMLEX: a testicular biopsy showing maturation arrest identifies the stage of failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — primary testicular failure alters the hormonal panel through loss of feedback","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"About 2 million spermatogonia begin spermatogenesis each day, and each spermatogonium gives rise to 64 spermatozoa. Approximately how many sperm are produced daily?","choices":{"A":"128 million","B":"64 million","C":"256 million","D":"32 million","E":"512 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiplying 2 million spermatogonia by 64 spermatozoa each gives about 128 million sperm produced daily. Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty to senescence, and a full cycle takes about 64 days, with the spermatogenic wave ensuring continuous output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sperm production is continuous and about 64 days per cycle, in contrast to oogenesis, where the pool of oocytes only declines. COMLEX: these numbers explain why semen parameters reflect events about two months earlier.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback reflects sperm output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old infertile man has a low ejaculate volume with no fructose and no sperm. His testes are normal in size, and FSH, LH, and testosterone are normal. Which is the most likely site of the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Seminiferous tubules with complete germ cell arrest","B":"Prostate gland with loss of secretory epithelium","C":"Ejaculatory ducts that drain the seminal vesicles","D":"Bilateral obstruction of the epididymal tubules","E":"Leydig cells with loss of LH receptor signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fructose is contributed by the seminal vesicles, so its absence together with low volume points to obstruction of the ejaculatory ducts that deliver seminal vesicle fluid, which also blocks sperm. Germ cell arrest would raise FSH and leave volume and fructose normal, and epididymal obstruction would leave volume and fructose normal. Prostatic atrophy alters citrate, PSA, and zinc, and Leydig cell resistance affects testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absent fructose with low volume and normal testes localizes the lesion to the ejaculatory ducts or seminal vesicles. COMLEX: this is a classic obstructive azoospermia workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — accessory gland disease and semen composition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"Which contribution of seminal vesicle prostaglandins best assists fertilization?","choices":{"A":"Triggering capacitation by removing cholesterol from the sperm membrane","B":"Providing fructose as the principal energy source for ejaculated sperm","C":"Neutralizing the acidic secretions of the vagina and vas deferens","D":"Liquefying the semen coagulum through proteolytic activity","E":"Easing cervical mucus penetration and aiding tubal peristalsis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Seminal fluid prostaglandins react with cervical mucus to make it more penetrable by sperm and induce peristaltic contractions of the uterus and fallopian tubes that propel sperm up the tract. Fructose is the nutritive component, prostatic alkalinity neutralizes acid, and capacitation is a process within the female tract, not a prostaglandin action. Liquefaction of the coagulum is by prostatic proteolytic enzymes such as PSA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins from the seminal vesicles aid sperm transport, and fructose feeds the sperm. COMLEX: distinguish the nutrient, alkalinizing, and transport roles of accessory fluids.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — glandular secretions and their clinical correlates","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"Freshly ejaculated sperm are placed directly with a mature oocyte in vitro and fail to fertilize it. Which explains this result?","choices":{"A":"Sperm must first complete meiosis II inside the female tract","B":"Sperm have not yet been capacitated by time in the female tract","C":"The acrosome must be shed in the epididymis before ejaculation","D":"Prostatic fluid must first convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone","E":"Sertoli cells must complete spermiogenesis after ejaculation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ejaculated sperm cannot immediately fertilize an ovum. They must reside in the female reproductive tract for 4 to 6 hours for capacitation, which removes inhibitory seminal factors and cholesterol, redistributes surface proteins, and enables the acrosomal reaction that lets sperm penetrate the ovum coverings. Meiosis, acrosome loss, and spermiogenesis are not the missing steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: capacitation precedes the acrosomal reaction, and in vitro fertilization protocols must reproduce it. COMLEX: this is the practical reason sperm are incubated before insemination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — prostatic and seminal secretions modulate sperm function","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"SDL 28 — Testicular Steroidogenesis & the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Testicular Axis","questions":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme catalyzes the LH-stimulated first step of testicular steroidogenesis?","choices":{"A":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","B":"5-alpha-reductase type 2","C":"Cholesterol desmolase","D":"Steroid 11-beta-hydroxylase","E":"21-beta-hydroxylase enzyme"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LH stimulates Leydig cells to synthesize testosterone by increasing the activity of cholesterol desmolase, the first step of the steroidogenic pathway, which parallels the action of ACTH in the adrenal cortex. The remaining enzymes act later in the pathway or in other tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol desmolase catalyzes the first, conventionally rate-limiting step of steroidogenesis, stimulated by LH in Leydig cells and by ACTH in the adrenal cortex. COMLEX: think of LH as the testicular equivalent of ACTH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"What is the androgenic end product of the testes?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroepiandrosterone","B":"Androstenedione","C":"Dihydrotestosterone","D":"Testosterone","E":"Estradiol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The testes contain 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, which converts androstenedione to testosterone, so testosterone is the androgenic end product. Dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione are the androgenic end products of the adrenal cortex, dihydrotestosterone is formed in target tissues by 5-alpha-reductase, and estradiol is the major ovarian estrogen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testes end with testosterone, adrenals end with DHEA and androstenedione, and target tissues generate DHT. COMLEX: know which steroid each organ ultimately secretes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"Why can the testes not synthesize glucocorticoids or mineralocorticoids?","choices":{"A":"They lack the 21- and 11-beta-hydroxylases","B":"They lack cholesterol desmolase in Leydig cells","C":"They lack 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D":"They lack LH receptors on Leydig cells","E":"They lack aromatase for estrogen synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The testicular steroidogenic pathway resembles the adrenal pathway, but the testes lack 21-beta-hydroxylase and 11-beta-hydroxylase, so they cannot make glucocorticoids or mineralocorticoids. They do have cholesterol desmolase and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and Leydig cells express LH receptors. The testes do express aromatase, but estrogen synthesis is not what blocks corticoid production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the two differences between testis and adrenal are missing 21- and 11-hydroxylases and an added 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase. COMLEX: this is why 21-hydroxylase deficiency does not directly impair testicular testosterone synthesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which pattern of GnRH secretion drives normal FSH and LH release in men?","choices":{"A":"Continuous release","B":"Pulsatile release","C":"Release confined to sleep","D":"Tonic low-level release","E":"Single midcycle surge release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Throughout the reproductive period GnRH secretion is pulsatile, and it drives a parallel pulsatile secretion of FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary. Continuous exposure down-regulates GnRH receptors and suppresses gonadotropin secretion, and a single midcycle surge belongs to the female cycle. GnRH is pulsatile throughout, not confined to sleep or purely tonic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates gonadotropins, whereas continuous GnRH inhibits them. COMLEX: this principle underlies both puberty induction and GnRH agonist therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"What is the effect of continuous exposure to a GnRH agonist on pituitary gonadotrophs?","choices":{"A":"Receptor up-regulation that sustains LH and FSH secretion","B":"Receptor sensitization that amplifies pulsatile LH release","C":"Down-regulation of receptors that suppresses LH and FSH","D":"Receptor internalization that suppresses FSH but spares LH","E":"Receptor stabilization that shifts secretion toward FSH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Continuous high levels of GnRH down-regulate GnRH receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs and inhibit both FSH and LH secretion. This principle is used to suppress LH and testosterone in prostate cancer. Pulsatile, not continuous, stimulation up-regulates receptors and sustains gonadotropin release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: continuous GnRH agonists such as leuprolide cause an initial transient stimulation followed by profound suppression of LH, FSH, and testosterone. COMLEX: know that the therapeutic effect depends on desensitization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug exploits continuous GnRH receptor stimulation to lower testosterone in prostate cancer?","choices":{"A":"Leuprolide","B":"Finasteride","C":"Flutamide","D":"Ketoconazole","E":"Bicalutamide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leuprolide is a long-acting GnRH agonist that, given continuously, down-regulates pituitary GnRH receptors and lowers LH and testosterone. Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, flutamide and bicalutamide block the androgen receptor, and ketoconazole inhibits steroid synthesis, so none of them works through GnRH receptor desensitization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish central suppression by GnRH agonists from peripheral androgen receptor blockade. COMLEX: androgen deprivation therapy is a mainstay of advanced prostate cancer treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular hormone selectively inhibits FSH secretion by the anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"Testosterone secreted by Leydig cells","B":"Activin secreted by granulosa cells","C":"Androgen-binding protein secreted into the tubule lumen","D":"Antimullerian hormone secreted by fetal Sertoli cells","E":"Inhibin secreted by Sertoli cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, a glycoprotein of the transforming growth factor-beta superfamily, which serves as a feedback inhibitor of FSH secretion and acts as an indicator of spermatogenic activity. Testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH, activin increases FSH, and androgen-binding protein and antimullerian hormone are not pituitary feedback signals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone feeds back on GnRH and LH, whereas inhibin feeds back selectively on FSH. COMLEX: FSH tracks Sertoli cell and spermatogenic status.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"At which levels of the axis does testosterone exert negative feedback?","choices":{"A":"On the hypothalamus with no direct pituitary effect","B":"On the anterior pituitary with no hypothalamic effect","C":"On Sertoli cells to reduce inhibin secretion","D":"On both the hypothalamus and the anterior pituitary","E":"On the posterior pituitary to reduce oxytocin release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the first of two feedback paths, testosterone feeds back on both the hypothalamus and the anterior lobe, where it inhibits secretion of GnRH and LH. At the hypothalamic level it decreases both the frequency and amplitude of GnRH pulses. The second path is inhibin acting on FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone has two feedback targets, hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH, and inhibin has one target, FSH. COMLEX: high testosterone from any source suppresses LH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"What does testosterone decrease at the hypothalamus?","choices":{"A":"The number of GnRH neurons in the arcuate nucleus","B":"The frequency and amplitude of GnRH pulses","C":"The half-life of GnRH in portal blood","D":"The binding of GnRH to Sertoli cell receptors","E":"The delivery of GnRH to the posterior pituitary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Testosterone inhibits GnRH secretion by decreasing both the frequency and the amplitude of GnRH pulses. It does not reduce neuron number, alter portal blood half-life, or act through Sertoli cell GnRH receptors, and GnRH is delivered to the anterior lobe, not the posterior pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative feedback changes pulse frequency and amplitude, not the presence of GnRH neurons. COMLEX: removal of the testes increases both GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone panel defines primary hypogonadism?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with elevated LH and FSH","B":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","C":"Normal testosterone with elevated LH and low FSH","D":"High testosterone with low LH and FSH","E":"Low testosterone with elevated LH and low FSH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In primary hypogonadism the testes fail, so testosterone and inhibin fall and the loss of negative feedback raises LH and FSH, which is why it is called hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Low testosterone with low LH and FSH indicates hypothalamic or pituitary failure, which is secondary hypogonadism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: check the gonadotropins to localize the lesion, high in testicular failure and low in central failure. COMLEX: primary means the gonad, secondary means the pituitary or hypothalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect causes Kallmann syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cell failure to respond to LH stimulation","B":"Absent SRY gene product in the fetal gonad","C":"Absent androgen receptors in target tissues","D":"Loss of Sertoli cell inhibin secretion","E":"Failed GnRH neuron migration into the brain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kallmann syndrome is a form of secondary hypogonadotropic hypogonadism caused by failure of GnRH-secreting neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus, so GnRH, LH, FSH, and testosterone are all low. The other options describe Leydig cell resistance, sex determination failure, androgen insensitivity, and Sertoli cell dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kallmann syndrome combines hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia because olfactory and GnRH neurons migrate together. COMLEX: delayed puberty with no sense of smell is the clinical clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which additional feature is characteristic of Kallmann syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral early cataracts","B":"Absent sense of smell","C":"Macroglossia with hypotonia","D":"Galactorrhea with visual field loss","E":"Hypertension with hypokalemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because GnRH neurons and olfactory neurons migrate together during development, Kallmann syndrome features anosmia or hyposmia along with delayed or absent puberty. Cataracts, macroglossia, galactorrhea with visual field loss, and hypertension with hypokalemia point to unrelated disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia to recognize Kallmann syndrome. COMLEX: test smell in any adolescent with absent puberty and low gonadotropins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with an XX genotype has ambiguous genitalia, an enlarged clitoris, and ovaries because of a congenital lack of adrenal 21-beta-hydroxylase. Which mechanism explains the virilization?","choices":{"A":"Excess LH drives ovarian testosterone synthesis through 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","B":"Precursors accumulate behind the block and are shunted into adrenal androgen production","C":"Testes secrete testosterone because the enzyme block is absent in gonadal tissue","D":"Loss of aromatase activity raises adrenal cortisol and secondarily raises androgens","E":"Enhanced 5-alpha-reductase in the adrenal cortex converts cortisol to androgens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Without 21-beta-hydroxylase, steroid precursors accumulate behind the block and are directed toward adrenal androgens, dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione, which masculinize the external genitalia in utero. The child has ovaries and no testes, and low cortisol raises ACTH, causing adrenal hyperplasia. The other options invoke enzymes or organs that are not responsible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 21-hydroxylase deficiency shunts precursors to androgens, causing virilization in XX infants and adrenal hyperplasia from high ACTH. COMLEX: compare with the testes, which normally lack 21-hydroxylase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — external genital differentiation depends on androgen action and DHT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a pituitary tumor has lost LH secretion. Which testicular biochemical step falls first?","choices":{"A":"Reduced 5-alpha-reductase activity in Leydig cells that lowers DHT","B":"Reduced 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase expression that blocks androgen conversion","C":"Reduced aromatase activity in Sertoli cells that lowers estradiol","D":"Reduced hepatic sex hormone-binding globulin that lowers free testosterone","E":"Reduced cholesterol desmolase activity that lowers pregnenolone formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LH stimulates Leydig cells by increasing the activity of cholesterol desmolase, the first step of the pathway, so loss of LH decreases conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone and lowers testosterone synthesis. The other listed steps are downstream or occur in other tissues and are not the primary LH target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH increases cholesterol desmolase in Leydig cells just as ACTH does in the adrenal cortex. COMLEX: loss of LH from any cause therefore lowers testosterone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — testosterone actions follow from the synthesis reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"Which biochemical difference explains why the adrenal cortex and the testes have different androgenic end products?","choices":{"A":"The adrenal cortex cannot convert cholesterol to pregnenolone","B":"The adrenal cortex cannot convert progesterone to deoxycorticosterone","C":"The adrenal cortex cannot convert androstenedione to testosterone","D":"The adrenal cortex cannot convert cholesterol to androstenedione","E":"The adrenal cortex cannot convert pregnenolone to progesterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The adrenal cortex has little 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity, so its androgenic end products are dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione, whereas the testes, which have this enzyme, produce testosterone. The adrenal cortex does make pregnenolone, progesterone, deoxycorticosterone, and androstenedione.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the testes add 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase and delete 21- and 11-hydroxylase relative to the adrenal cortex. COMLEX: use this contrast to predict which hormones each organ secretes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — DHT and testosterone actions depend on these androgen products","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has delayed puberty from isolated GnRH deficiency. Which regimen most likely initiates puberty and establishes reproductive function?","choices":{"A":"A long-acting depot GnRH analog given at three-month intervals","B":"A continuous high-dose GnRH infusion over several weeks","C":"A daily oral FSH tablet given at bedtime","D":"Intermittent GnRH analog pulses that mimic normal release","E":"A GnRH receptor blocker given before each sleep period"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulsatility of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis is required for normal reproductive function, so intermittent pulses of a GnRH analog replicate the normal secretory pattern and initiate puberty. If a long-acting GnRH analog is given, puberty is not initiated because continuous exposure down-regulates receptors. A receptor blocker would suppress gonadotropins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates the axis and continuous GnRH shuts it down. COMLEX: therapy for GnRH deficiency must be delivered as a pulsatile pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — drug effects on the HPG axis depend on how the axis is stimulated or suppressed","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer receives depot leuprolide for several months. Which steady-state hormone profile is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low LH and testosterone from GnRH receptor down-regulation","B":"High LH and low testosterone from primary testicular failure","C":"High LH and high testosterone from pituitary receptor sensitization","D":"Low LH and high testosterone from increased Leydig cell sensitivity","E":"Normal LH and low testosterone from direct Leydig cell blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Continuous GnRH agonist exposure down-regulates pituitary GnRH receptors, so LH and FSH secretion fall and Leydig cells receive little stimulation, lowering testosterone. This profile is central, not testicular, so LH is low rather than high. An early transient rise in LH and testosterone can occur before down-regulation is established.","boardPrep":"Step 1: agonist therapy produces a central, secondary hypogonadal pattern, with low LH and low testosterone. COMLEX: the initial flare is the reason antiandrogens are sometimes given at the start.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — androgen deprivation therapy in prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A boy is entering puberty as pulsatile GnRH secretion begins. How does this pulsatile GnRH change the responsiveness of his anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"GnRH down-regulates its own receptors, which lowers FSH and LH release","B":"GnRH up-regulates its own receptors and raises FSH and LH output per dose","C":"GnRH inhibits gonadotroph LH synthesis so FSH becomes the dominant gonadotropin","D":"GnRH inhibits melatonin release, which directly raises testosterone secretion","E":"GnRH converts pituitary receptors to a tonic mode with constant FSH release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At puberty pulsatile GnRH up-regulates its own receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs, so a given concentration of GnRH produces greater FSH and LH secretion. The relative secretion also shifts so that LH exceeds FSH, the opposite of the childhood pattern. Down-regulation occurs with continuous, not pulsatile, exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH up-regulates its receptors and continuous GnRH down-regulates them. COMLEX: during childhood FSH exceeds LH, and after puberty LH exceeds FSH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — effects on the HPG axis depend on receptor regulation and pulsatile signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"A man undergoes bilateral orchiectomy for trauma. Which hypothalamic-pituitary change is expected afterward?","choices":{"A":"Greater GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude with rising LH and FSH","B":"Decreased frequency and amplitude of GnRH pulses with low LH and FSH","C":"Unchanged GnRH pulses with a fall in LH but a rise in FSH","D":"Increased GnRH pulse frequency with falling LH from receptor down-regulation","E":"Increased LH and FSH with no change in GnRH secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Removal of the testes eliminates testosterone and inhibin, so negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary is lost, and the frequency and amplitude of GnRH, FSH, and LH pulses all increase. Receptor down-regulation would require continuous GnRH exposure, and gonadotropin rises are driven by more GnRH, not unchanged GnRH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: castration raises GnRH, LH, and FSH because feedback inhibition by testosterone and inhibin is removed. COMLEX: this response mirrors primary testicular failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — exogenous androgens produce the reverse effect by exaggerating feedback inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"An infertile man has a low sperm count, normal LH and testosterone, an elevated FSH, and a low inhibin B level. What does the low inhibin B most directly reflect?","choices":{"A":"Reduced Leydig cell function that removes testosterone feedback on LH","B":"Reduced Sertoli cell function that removes selective feedback on FSH","C":"Reduced hypothalamic GnRH output that lowers gonadotropin secretion","D":"Increased pituitary gonadotroph sensitivity to circulating testosterone","E":"Increased androgen-binding protein secretion that binds inhibin in the tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibin is secreted by Sertoli cells and selectively inhibits FSH, serving as an indicator of spermatogenic activity. Low inhibin B with a high FSH reflects impaired Sertoli cell and spermatogenic function, whereas normal LH and testosterone show that Leydig cells and testosterone feedback are intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated rise in FSH with normal LH and testosterone identifies Sertoli cell or tubular damage. COMLEX: FSH and inhibin B are markers of spermatogenesis in infertility workups.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Sertoli cell functions and the blood-testis barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has an autonomous Leydig cell tumor secreting excess androgen. What gonadotropin pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low LH with high FSH because inhibin secretion is suppressed by the tumor","B":"High LH with low FSH because inhibin secretion is raised by the tumor","C":"Normal LH with low FSH because inhibin feedback remains intact","D":"High LH and FSH because the tumor stimulates autonomous hypothalamic GnRH release","E":"Low LH and FSH from testosterone feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Excess testosterone feeds back on the hypothalamus, decreasing GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude, and on the anterior pituitary, inhibiting LH, so both LH and FSH secretion fall. Testosterone does not stimulate GnRH release, and inhibin is not the driver of this pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an autonomous androgen source suppresses the axis, giving high testosterone with low LH and FSH. COMLEX: contrast this with androgen insensitivity, in which LH is elevated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — exogenous testosterone suppresses the axis through the same mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has absent pubertal development and cannot smell coffee. Testes are small, karyotype is 46,XY, and testosterone, LH, and FSH are all low. Which is the most likely defect?","choices":{"A":"Failed embryonic migration of GnRH-secreting neurons","B":"Testicular failure caused by an extra X chromosome","C":"Androgen receptor insensitivity with elevated LH","D":"Leydig cell hypoplasia from a mutated LH receptor","E":"Anterior pituitary destruction by a prolactin-secreting tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Absent puberty with anosmia and low testosterone, LH, and FSH is Kallmann syndrome, a secondary hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from failed migration of GnRH neurons with the olfactory neurons. An extra X chromosome and Leydig cell defects would produce high gonadotropins, and androgen insensitivity would raise LH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low testosterone with low gonadotropins localizes the defect above the testis, and anosmia identifies Kallmann syndrome. COMLEX: give pulsatile GnRH or gonadotropins to induce puberty and fertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH secretion is the hypothalamic driver of LH and FSH release","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with decreased libido, headaches, and bitemporal hemianopia has a large nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma. Which hormone panel is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with high LH and FSH","B":"High testosterone with high LH and FSH","C":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","D":"High testosterone with low LH and FSH","E":"Normal testosterone with high LH and low FSH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large pituitary mass compresses and destroys gonadotrophs, decreasing LH and FSH and therefore Leydig cell testosterone, producing secondary hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. High gonadotropins would imply testicular failure, and high testosterone would suppress LH and FSH but would not cause the libido loss. A gonadotroph mass does not raise LH in isolation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary hypogonadism shows low testosterone with low LH and FSH, occasionally inappropriately normal, and a pituitary source should be imaged. COMLEX: visual field loss points to a pituitary mass effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — central suppression of the axis lowers both gonadotropins and testosterone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with a female phenotype and breast development has primary amenorrhea and scant pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus, testes are found in the abdomen, and the karyotype is 46,XY. Which hormone panel is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","B":"Low testosterone with elevated LH and FSH","C":"Elevated estradiol with suppressed LH","D":"Mild testosterone excess with elevated LH","E":"Elevated testosterone with suppressed LH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is androgen insensitivity syndrome, in which androgen receptors are absent or defective. Because feedback through androgen receptors is impaired, LH is elevated and testosterone is mildly elevated, and the testes convert excess testosterone to estradiol, producing breast development. The panel differs from primary or secondary hypogonadism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: XY with a female phenotype, testes, no uterus, and high LH with mildly elevated testosterone identifies androgen insensitivity. COMLEX: the testes are removed because of tumor risk, followed by estrogen replacement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling is required for testosterone action and feedback","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":29,"title":"SDL 29 — Actions of Androgens, Target Tissues, & 5-alpha-Reductase Pathophysiology","questions":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which action of androgens is mediated directly by testosterone without conversion to dihydrotestosterone?","choices":{"A":"Differentiation of the prostate","B":"Male pattern baldness","C":"Differentiation of the scrotum","D":"Sebaceous gland activity","E":"Deepening of the voice"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Deepening of the voice, increased muscle mass, the pubertal growth spurt, spermatogenesis, and libido respond directly to testosterone. Differentiation of the prostate and scrotum, male hair patterns, male pattern baldness, and sebaceous gland activity require conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha-reductase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sort androgen actions into two lists, testosterone-mediated and DHT-mediated, since 5-alpha-reductase disorders and finasteride are tested on this split. COMLEX: muscle, voice, and libido are testosterone effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which androgen-dependent process requires conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone?","choices":{"A":"Increased muscle mass","B":"Spermatogenesis","C":"Growth of the prostate","D":"Deepening of the voice","E":"Closure of the epiphyseal plates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Growth of the prostate depends on dihydrotestosterone rather than testosterone, which is why 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors treat benign prostatic hyperplasia. Muscle mass, spermatogenesis, voice, and epiphyseal closure respond directly to testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DHT drives the prostate, external genitalia, hair pattern, baldness, and sebaceous glands. COMLEX: finasteride treats BPH and male pattern hair loss for this reason.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone drives differentiation of the fetal Wolffian ducts into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles?","choices":{"A":"DHT formed by peripheral 5-alpha-reductase in the developing ducts","B":"Antimullerian hormone secreted by Sertoli cells of the fetal testis","C":"Estradiol formed by aromatase within fetal Leydig cells of the testis","D":"Testosterone from the same-side testis acting without DHT conversion","E":"LH from the anterior pituitary stimulating the developing duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Testosterone from each testis acts on its own Wolffian duct on the same side, and it does not need to be converted to dihydrotestosterone for this action. Antimullerian hormone instead causes atrophy of the Mullerian ducts, and LH acts on Leydig cells rather than on the ducts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the internal male tract needs testosterone, and the external genitalia need DHT. COMLEX: this explains why 5-alpha-reductase deficiency spares internal structures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits the newborn presentation of classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency?","choices":{"A":"46,XX with virilized external genitalia and normal ovaries","B":"46,XY with ambiguous genitalia and normal Wolffian derivatives","C":"46,XY with female external genitalia and no Wolffian or Mullerian derivatives","D":"47,XXY with small testes and normal male external genitalia","E":"45,X with streak gonads and female external genitalia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Affected 46,XY individuals have testes and make testosterone, so the Wolffian ducts differentiate, and antimullerian hormone suppresses the Mullerian ducts. Without DHT, however, the external genitalia are ambiguous or appear female. Androgen insensitivity would leave no Wolffian derivatives, and the other options describe different karyotypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase deficiency gives ambiguous genitalia with a blind vaginal pouch, hypospadias, and male internal ducts. COMLEX: virilization at puberty occurs because high-normal testosterone can activate receptors without DHT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone profile is expected in classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency?","choices":{"A":"Normal testosterone with low DHT","B":"Low testosterone with normal DHT","C":"Low testosterone with low DHT","D":"Normal testosterone with high DHT","E":"High testosterone with high DHT"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The testes secrete testosterone normally, but tissues lacking 5-alpha-reductase cannot convert it, so testosterone is normal or high-normal and dihydrotestosterone is low. Low testosterone would reflect a defect in synthesis, and high DHT would not occur without the converting enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ratio of testosterone to DHT is elevated in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. COMLEX: contrast with androgen insensitivity, in which both androgens are normal or high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme is inhibited by finasteride?","choices":{"A":"Aromatase enzyme","B":"Cholesterol desmolase","C":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D":"21-beta-hydroxylase","E":"5-alpha-reductase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Finasteride blocks 5-alpha-reductase, which converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, and is therefore used in benign prostatic hyperplasia and male pattern hair loss. Aromatase, cholesterol desmolase, 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and 21-beta-hydroxylase are not its targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride blocks DHT formation, and its adverse effects derive from lowering DHT. COMLEX: treatment of BPH and alopecia are the two classic indications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the unliganded androgen receptor reside in a target cell?","choices":{"A":"In the cytoplasm bound to heat-shock proteins","B":"In the nucleus already bound to DNA androgen response elements","C":"On the plasma membrane as a receptor tyrosine kinase","D":"In the mitochondria bound to cytochrome oxidase","E":"In the extracellular fluid bound to SHBG"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are lipid-soluble and diffuse into cells, where the unliganded androgen receptor is held in the cytoplasm by heat-shock proteins. Ligand binding then releases the chaperones and allows the receptor to move to the nucleus. It is an intracellular receptor, not a membrane tyrosine kinase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid receptors are intracellular, with the ligand crossing the membrane. COMLEX: distinguish these from peptide hormone receptors at the cell surface.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"What does the ligand-bound receptor do inside the nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Binds cAMP response elements and activates protein kinase A","B":"Forms homodimers that bind androgen response elements","C":"Phosphorylates STAT proteins that return to the cell membrane","D":"Cleaves genomic DNA at splice sites to silence gene expression","E":"Binds cytoplasmic mRNA to block translation on ribosomes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After ligand binding, the receptor dissociates from heat-shock proteins, translocates to the nucleus, forms homodimers, and binds androgen response elements in DNA, initiating transcription of new mRNAs that are translated into proteins responsible for androgen actions. The other options describe unrelated signaling mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid hormone action is slow because it works through gene transcription. COMLEX: nuclear receptors act as ligand-activated transcription factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"What triggers release of heat-shock proteins from the androgen receptor?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis of DHT inside the nucleus of the cell","B":"Phosphorylation of the receptor by protein kinase A","C":"Release of mitochondrial calcium into the cytoplasm","D":"Binding of testosterone or DHT to the receptor","E":"Cleavage of the receptor by lysosomal proteases"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ligand binding, by testosterone or dihydrotestosterone, changes receptor conformation and releases the heat-shock proteins so the receptor can translocate to the nucleus. The other options are not steps in classical androgen receptor activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ligand binding is the trigger for chaperone release in the steroid receptor pathway. COMLEX: androgen insensitivity results from receptor defects that disrupt this pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"How do exogenous anabolic steroids suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis?","choices":{"A":"Negative feedback that inhibits GnRH and LH secretion","B":"Direct destruction of Leydig cells by androgen receptor activation","C":"Stimulation of inhibin secretion that blocks LH release","D":"Up-regulation of GnRH receptors that exhausts pituitary LH stores","E":"Conversion of the steroids into gonadotropins by the liver"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Androgens feed back on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary to inhibit GnRH and LH secretion, and the same feedback applies to synthetic androgens. With LH suppressed, Leydig cell stimulation and endogenous testosterone production fall. Inhibin acts selectively on FSH, not LH. Androgens do not destroy Leydig cells directly, and the liver does not convert steroids into gonadotropins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: supraphysiologic or exogenous androgen exposure suppresses the axis through negative feedback. COMLEX: this is why anabolic steroid users show low LH and FSH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to intratesticular testosterone in a man using high-dose anabolic steroids?","choices":{"A":"It rises because anabolic steroids concentrate within the testis","B":"It falls markedly because LH stimulation of Leydig cells declines","C":"It stays unchanged because Sertoli cells compensate for lost LH","D":"It rises because FSH secretion increases in response to feedback","E":"It stays unchanged because androgen-binding protein buffers the change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Suppressed LH removes the stimulus for Leydig cell testosterone synthesis, so intratesticular testosterone falls markedly even though serum androgen activity from the drug is high. Because spermatogenesis depends on high local testosterone reinforcing FSH action, sperm production is impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: serum androgens do not substitute for intratesticular testosterone. COMLEX: this is why testosterone therapy can cause infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular findings result from prolonged anabolic steroid abuse?","choices":{"A":"Testicular hypertrophy with increased sperm concentration","B":"Normal testicular size with increased sperm motility","C":"Testicular atrophy with azoospermia or severe oligospermia","D":"Testicular atrophy with high sperm concentration","E":"Testicular hypertrophy with normal intratesticular testosterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prolonged suppression of LH and FSH removes trophic support to Leydig cells and seminiferous tubules, so the testes shrink and spermatogenesis is markedly reduced, producing azoospermia or severe oligospermia. The other options describe changes that do not follow from gonadotropin suppression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic exogenous androgen use produces atrophy and infertility despite muscular development. COMLEX: fertility may recover after stopping the drug but can take months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man takes an agent that blocks the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone. Which outcome is expected?","choices":{"A":"Smaller prostate and slowed hair loss with preserved muscle mass","B":"Loss of muscle mass and voice deepening with normal prostate size","C":"Regression of the epididymis and vas deferens with shrinking seminal vesicles","D":"A rise in vocal pitch from loss of laryngeal growth","E":"Loss of spermatogenesis with testicular atrophy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prostate growth, hair pattern, and male pattern baldness depend on dihydrotestosterone, so blocking 5-alpha-reductase shrinks the prostate and slows hair loss. Muscle mass, voice, spermatogenesis, and the internal genital tract respond directly to testosterone, which remains available.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride mainly affects DHT-dependent tissues. COMLEX: expect prostate and hair benefit with limited effect on muscle and fertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT-dependent stromal and epithelial growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"At puberty a boy develops a growth spurt, voice deepening, and facial hair. Which statement correctly assigns the androgen responsible for each?","choices":{"A":"Growth spurt via testosterone; voice and facial hair via DHT","B":"Voice via testosterone; growth spurt and facial hair via DHT","C":"Each of the three via DHT after 5-alpha-reduction","D":"Each of the three via testosterone without DHT involvement","E":"Growth spurt and voice via testosterone; facial hair via DHT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pubertal growth spurt and deepening of the voice are mediated by testosterone itself, whereas facial and other male-pattern hair growth depends on dihydrotestosterone formed by 5-alpha-reductase in hair follicles. No single androgen accounts for all three.","boardPrep":"Step 1: split androgen actions by whether 5-alpha-reductase is required. COMLEX: patients with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency virilize in voice and muscle but lack facial hair.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — 5-alpha-reductase inhibition affects DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"A man has a high total testosterone level but no signs of androgen excess, and his SHBG level is markedly elevated. Which principle best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Bound testosterone is the active fraction and free hormone is biologically inert","B":"SHBG converts testosterone to DHT within the plasma compartment","C":"SHBG delivers testosterone directly into the nucleus of target cells","D":"Free testosterone is the active fraction and most hormone is protein-bound","E":"Total testosterone reflects the intracellular hormone concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 98 percent of circulating testosterone is bound to SHBG and albumin, and only free testosterone is biologically active, so SHBG acts as a reservoir. A high SHBG raises total testosterone while free testosterone stays normal or low. SHBG does not convert or deliver hormone, and total concentration does not reflect intracellular levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SHBG synthesis is stimulated by estrogens and inhibited by androgens. COMLEX: interpret total testosterone in light of SHBG binding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — steroid diffusion into the cell and binding to the intracellular androgen receptor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old raised as a girl has a deepening voice, increasing muscle mass, and an enlarging clitoris, with no breasts or menses. Examination shows testes, hypospadias, and a blind vaginal pouch. Karyotype is 46,XY, testosterone is high-normal, and DHT is low. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"5-alpha-reductase","B":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","C":"Androgen receptor protein","D":"21-beta-hydroxylase","E":"Aromatase enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 46,XY individual with testes, high-normal testosterone, and low DHT has 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. Internal male ducts formed from testosterone, but external genitalia lacked DHT, and at puberty testosterone-mediated changes such as voice deepening and muscle growth appear. Androgen receptor defects would give high androgens with little or no virilization, and the other enzymes are not consistent with this profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 46,XY with virilization at puberty and low DHT is the classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency picture. COMLEX: patients may be raised as girls and virilize at puberty.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT dependence of prostatic development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY patient with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency lacks DHT, yet at puberty her voice deepens and muscle mass increases. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"An alternative pathway supplies DHT that restores the missing androgen actions","B":"Estradiol from ovarian tissue drives the voice and muscle changes","C":"Those actions are mediated by testosterone itself and do not require DHT","D":"Excess LH directly stimulates laryngeal and skeletal muscle growth","E":"The androgen receptor becomes constitutively active without DHT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Voice deepening, muscle mass, the pubertal growth spurt, and spermatogenesis respond to testosterone directly, so they proceed despite the enzyme deficiency. Facial and body hair, sebaceous gland activity, and prostate growth require DHT and fail to develop. She has no ovaries, and LH does not act on these tissues. With DHT absent, no alternative pathway is needed for these actions, since testosterone acts directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the split between testosterone-mediated and DHT-mediated actions explains the mixed virilization. COMLEX: absent body hair with deep voice suggests DHT deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone secretion continues normally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia starts finasteride. Which changes are expected over several months?","choices":{"A":"Reduced prostate volume with an unchanged serum PSA level","B":"Reduced prostate volume with a fall in serum PSA","C":"Prostatic hypertrophy with a lower serum PSA level","D":"Reduced prostate volume with a fall in serum testosterone","E":"Unchanged prostate volume with reduced muscle mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"By blocking conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, finasteride reduces DHT-driven prostatic growth, so prostate volume decreases and PSA, which reflects prostatic tissue, falls. Serum testosterone is not lowered, and muscle mass, which depends on testosterone, is preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate and lower PSA, so PSA interpretation must account for therapy. COMLEX: sexual adverse effects stem from lowered DHT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — finasteride effects on DHT, PSA, and sexual adverse effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with a female phenotype, breast development, primary amenorrhea, and no uterus has testes and a 46,XY karyotype. Studies of genital skin fibroblasts show no binding of testosterone or DHT. Which defect explains this?","choices":{"A":"Absent or defective androgen receptors that prevent hormone action","B":"Absent 5-alpha-reductase that prevents formation of DHT","C":"Absent 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase that prevents testosterone synthesis","D":"Absent antimullerian hormone that leaves Mullerian ducts intact","E":"Absent SRY gene product that prevents testis formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Absent androgen binding in target cells indicates a defect in the androgen receptor, so testosterone and DHT cannot act and the external genitalia develop as female by default, while Wolffian ducts regress. The testes still make antimullerian hormone, so there is no uterus, and the presence of testes excludes an SRY defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: androgen insensitivity is a receptor disorder with normal or high androgens and elevated LH. COMLEX: the testes are removed because of tumor risk, and estrogen therapy follows.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling mediates feedback inhibition of LH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A drug prevents the hormone-bound receptor from entering the nucleus of a target cell. Which effect is expected?","choices":{"A":"Lower serum testosterone from inhibition of Leydig cell steroid synthesis","B":"Loss of androgen-responsive gene transcription despite high testosterone","C":"Blocked conversion of testosterone to DHT by 5-alpha-reductase in tissues","D":"Blocked release of LH from the anterior pituitary gonadotroph cells","E":"Lower SHBG-bound testosterone in the circulation after drug exposure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nuclear entry of the hormone-bound receptor is required for it to bind androgen response elements, so blocking translocation prevents transcription of androgen-responsive genes even when testosterone and DHT are abundant. The drug acts downstream of hormone synthesis, conversion, and release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral androgen receptor antagonists block target cell action without lowering testosterone. COMLEX: contrast this with GnRH agonists, which lower testosterone centrally.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — androgen receptor blockade in prostate cancer therapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a mutation in the DNA-binding domain of the steroid receptor. Which step of the signaling pathway fails?","choices":{"A":"Diffusion of testosterone across the plasma membrane","B":"Release of heat-shock proteins after ligand binding","C":"Receptor pair attachment to DNA response elements","D":"Formation of DHT from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase","E":"Movement of the ligand-bound receptor into the nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The DNA-binding domain mediates attachment of the receptor dimer to androgen response elements, so this step fails while ligand entry, chaperone release, DHT formation, and nuclear translocation can proceed. The result is androgen insensitivity despite normal hormone levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: different receptor mutations disrupt different steps but produce the same androgen insensitivity phenotype. COMLEX: normal or high testosterone with no androgen effect suggests a receptor problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — the receptor pathway underlies both target actions and feedback","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder using testosterone and other anabolic steroids has small soft testes and azoospermia. Which hormone profile is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High LH and FSH with low intratesticular testosterone","B":"Low LH and FSH with high intratesticular testosterone","C":"High LH with low FSH and high intratesticular testosterone","D":"Low LH and FSH with low intratesticular testosterone","E":"Normal LH and FSH with normal intratesticular testosterone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exogenous androgens inhibit GnRH and LH through negative feedback, so LH and FSH fall, Leydig cell stimulation is lost, and intratesticular testosterone collapses, causing azoospermia and testicular atrophy. High gonadotropins would indicate primary testicular failure, which is not the mechanism here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: suppressed gonadotropins with atrophic testes suggest exogenous androgen use. COMLEX: serum testosterone may be normal or high in these patients despite the atrophy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — testosterone negative feedback inhibits GnRH and LH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder who used anabolic steroids for years stops them and wants to restore fertility. Which treatment stimulates his testicular testosterone production directly?","choices":{"A":"Leuprolide given continuously to stimulate pituitary LH release","B":"Finasteride to raise intratesticular testosterone through DHT","C":"Testosterone gel to restore intratesticular androgen concentration","D":"Recombinant inhibin to raise FSH secretion from the pituitary","E":"Human chorionic gonadotropin acting on Leydig cell LH receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human chorionic gonadotropin belongs to the glycoprotein hormone family and shares an alpha subunit with LH, so it can activate Leydig cell LH receptors and stimulate testosterone synthesis. Continuous GnRH agonists suppress LH, exogenous testosterone maintains feedback suppression, finasteride blocks DHT formation, and inhibin lowers FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG is LH-like and stimulates Leydig cells directly. COMLEX: exogenous testosterone would further suppress the axis rather than restore fertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — LH-driven Leydig cell testosterone synthesis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man on prescribed testosterone injections has high serum testosterone, shrinking testes, and azoospermia. Which explains the azoospermia?","choices":{"A":"Direct Sertoli cell toxicity from androgen receptor overactivation","B":"Feedback inhibition of GnRH and LH lowers intratesticular testosterone","C":"Loss of inhibin feedback that raises FSH excessively","D":"Conversion of the injected testosterone to DHT that inhibits the tubule","E":"Closure of the blood-testis barrier by excess androgen-binding protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Injected testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH, so Leydig cells stop producing testosterone and intratesticular levels, which are needed to support spermatogenesis, collapse. High serum testosterone does not compensate. FSH is suppressed rather than raised, so loss of inhibin feedback does not explain the azoospermia, and androgen receptor overactivation or excess androgen-binding protein is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: exogenous testosterone works against fertility by suppressing LH and FSH. COMLEX: counsel men seeking fertility to avoid testosterone therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — androgen-binding protein maintains high local testosterone for spermatogenesis","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":30,"title":"SDL 30 — The BRS Active-Retrieval Assignment Guide (SDLs 05-29 Renal Physiology)","questions":[{"id":"sdl30_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of changes results from constriction of the efferent arteriole, as occurs with angiotensin II?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, RPF falls, and filtration fraction is unchanged","B":"GFR falls, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction falls","C":"GFR rises, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction rises","D":"GFR is unchanged, RPF rises, and filtration fraction falls","E":"GFR rises, RPF falls, and filtration fraction rises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, so GFR rises, while resistance to outflow lowers renal plasma flow. Because GFR rises as RPF falls, the filtration fraction increases. Afferent constriction instead lowers both GFR and RPF with no change in filtration fraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: efferent constriction gives up GFR, down RPF, up FF, and afferent constriction gives down GFR, down RPF, no change in FF. COMLEX: this explains why ACE inhibitors lower GFR by dilating efferent arterioles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar changes govern glomerular capillary pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"A ureteral stone raises Bowman space hydrostatic pressure. Which change in renal hemodynamics results?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, RPF falls, and filtration fraction is unchanged","B":"GFR is unchanged, RPF falls, and filtration fraction rises","C":"GFR rises, RPF rises, and filtration fraction is unchanged","D":"GFR falls, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction falls","E":"GFR is unchanged, RPF rises, and filtration fraction falls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Increased Bowman space pressure opposes filtration and reduces net ultrafiltration pressure, so GFR falls, but plasma flow through the glomerulus is not altered, so RPF is unchanged and the filtration fraction, GFR divided by RPF, decreases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: postrenal obstruction lowers GFR by raising PBS without changing RPF. COMLEX: think hydronephrosis with a fall in GFR after ureteral obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar changes govern glomerular capillary pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Which change would increase the net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Ureteral constriction","B":"Rising plasma protein concentration","C":"Dilation of the afferent arteriole","D":"Afferent arteriolar constriction","E":"Reduced glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Afferent dilation increases glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, which is the main force favoring filtration, and raises net ultrafiltration pressure and GFR. Ureteral obstruction raises Bowman space pressure, higher plasma protein raises capillary oncotic pressure, and afferent constriction and a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure all reduce net filtration pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PBS and capillary oncotic pressure oppose filtration, and only PGC favors it. COMLEX: use the Starling equation to predict the effect of any single change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar changes govern glomerular capillary pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Approximately what is the lower limit of mean arterial pressure below which renal blood flow autoregulation fails?","choices":{"A":"40 mm Hg","B":"60 mm Hg","C":"100 mm Hg","D":"120 mm Hg","E":"80 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal blood flow and GFR remain nearly constant when mean arterial pressure lies between about 80 mm Hg and the upper autoregulatory limit. Below about 80 mm Hg, autoregulation fails and both flow and filtration fall with pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation of RBF spans roughly 80 to 200 mm Hg, and hypotension below 80 mm Hg reduces GFR. COMLEX: severe hypotension can therefore cause acute tubular injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of intrinsic mechanisms accounts for autoregulation of renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II release and aldosterone secretion","B":"Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","C":"Sympathetic tone and vasopressin secretion","D":"Prostaglandin release and bradykinin generation","E":"Natriuretic peptide release and renin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The myogenic mechanism causes afferent arterioles to contract when stretched, and tubuloglomerular feedback causes afferent constriction when the macula densa senses increased NaCl delivery. Both are intrinsic to the kidney, whereas the other pairs are neurohumoral or paracrine mediators that modulate flow rather than autoregulate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation is intrinsic and works by changing renal vascular resistance. COMLEX: this is why renal blood flow remains stable with modest changes in systemic pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Macula densa cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus respond directly to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the renal arteriolar wall","B":"Plasma renin concentration in the peritubular blood","C":"Colloid osmotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood","D":"NaCl delivery to the early distal tubule","E":"Urine flow rate within the bladder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The macula densa senses NaCl delivery to the early distal tubule. Increased delivery signals afferent constriction through tubuloglomerular feedback, and reduced delivery promotes afferent dilation and renin release from the granular cells. Wall stretch is sensed by smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole in the myogenic response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the macula densa is the sensor and the afferent arteriole is the effector of tubuloglomerular feedback. COMLEX: loop diuretics blunt this sensing by blocking NKCC2 at the macula densa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain constant flow and filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"Which substance is freely filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid","B":"Glucose","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Potassium","E":"Inulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inulin is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so the amount excreted equals the amount filtered and its clearance equals GFR. PAH is filtered and secreted, and glucose, sodium, and urea are reabsorbed to varying degrees, so their clearances differ from GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: relative clearances run PAH greater than inulin, urea, sodium, and glucose. COMLEX: creatinine approximates inulin clinically, with slight secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"What does the clearance of PAH measure at low concentrations?","choices":{"A":"Effective renal plasma flow","B":"True glomerular filtration rate","C":"Total renal blood flow","D":"Filtration fraction","E":"Renal vascular resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PAH is filtered and secreted by the proximal tubule, so nearly all of the PAH reaching the kidney is cleared, and its clearance measures effective renal plasma flow, which underestimates true RPF by about 10 percent. GFR is measured by inulin, and blood flow requires correction for hematocrit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBF equals RPF divided by 1 minus the hematocrit. COMLEX: PAH clearance is valid only at plasma concentrations below its transport maximum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":1,"stem":"At approximately what plasma glucose concentration does glucose first appear in the urine?","choices":{"A":"About 100 mg/dL","B":"About 150 mg/dL","C":"About 350 mg/dL","D":"About 250 mg/dL","E":"About 500 mg/dL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose first appears in the urine at the renal threshold of about 250 mg/dL, before the carriers are fully saturated. The transport maximum is reached at about 350 mg/dL, and the region between the two is splay, caused by nephron heterogeneity and the low affinity of SGLT2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: threshold near 250 mg/dL and Tm near 350 mg/dL frame the glucose titration curve. COMLEX: glucosuria in diabetes reflects a filtered load beyond reabsorptive capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — saturable carrier kinetics define transport maximum, threshold, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which syndrome is caused by a generalized reabsorption defect of the proximal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Bartter syndrome","B":"Gitelman syndrome","C":"Fanconi syndrome","D":"Liddle syndrome","E":"Apparent mineralocorticoid excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fanconi syndrome is a generalized proximal tubule reabsorption defect that wastes amino acids, glucose, HCO3-, and phosphate. Bartter, Gitelman, and Liddle syndromes affect the thick ascending limb, distal convoluted tubule, and collecting tubule respectively, and apparent mineralocorticoid excess affects cortisol metabolism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the mnemonic order is Fanconi, Bartter, Gitelman, and Liddle, moving distally along the nephron. COMLEX: Fanconi syndrome causes proximal RTA and hypophosphatemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway is one of the transporters altered in these syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter is defective in Bartter syndrome?","choices":{"A":"NCC in the distal convoluted tubule","B":"ENaC in the collecting tubule","C":"SGLT2 in the proximal tubule","D":"Aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct","E":"NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bartter syndrome is a reabsorption defect of the thick ascending limb affecting the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, and it resembles chronic loop diuretic use with metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypokalemia, and hypercalciuria. NCC is affected in Gitelman syndrome and ENaC is overactive in Liddle syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bartter equals loop diuretic effect, and Gitelman equals thiazide effect. COMLEX: Bartter syndrome is autosomal recessive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway is one of the transporters altered in these syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits Gitelman syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Defective NCC that mimics chronic thiazide use","B":"Defective NKCC2 that mimics chronic loop diuretic use","C":"Overactive ENaC that mimics primary aldosteronism","D":"Defective SGLT2 that mimics chronic SGLT2 inhibitor use","E":"Defective aquaporin-2 that mimics vasopressin deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gitelman syndrome is a NaCl reabsorption defect in the distal convoluted tubule affecting the thiazide-sensitive NCC transporter, and it presents like chronic thiazide use with metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypomagnesemia, and hypokalemia. It is generally less severe than Bartter syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gitelman equals thiazide, and it is milder than Bartter. COMLEX: look for hypomagnesemia to point to Gitelman syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway is one of the transporters altered in these syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"Using Winter's formula, what Pco2 is expected in simple metabolic acidosis with a plasma HCO3- of 12 mEq/L?","choices":{"A":"20 mm Hg","B":"26 mm Hg","C":"32 mm Hg","D":"38 mm Hg","E":"44 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Winter's formula predicts an expected Pco2 of 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8, plus or minus 2, so for an HCO3- of 12 mEq/L the expected Pco2 is 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 above or below the expected range indicates a coexisting respiratory acidosis or alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: compare the measured Pco2 with the Winter's prediction to test whether compensation is appropriate. COMLEX: mixed disorders are found when the measured value falls outside the predicted range.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has plasma Na+ 138 mEq/L, Cl- 104 mEq/L, and HCO3- 14 mEq/L. What is the serum anion gap?","choices":{"A":"16 mEq/L","B":"24 mEq/L","C":"34 mEq/L","D":"20 mEq/L","E":"10 mEq/L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The serum anion gap equals Na+ minus the sum of Cl- and HCO3-, so 138 - (104 + 14) = 20 mEq/L, which exceeds the normal value of about 12 mEq/L and indicates unmeasured anions. The other values do not follow from the formula with the given electrolytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an elevated anion gap points to accumulation of unmeasured anions such as lactate, ketoacids, or toxic alcohol metabolites. COMLEX: calculate the gap on every metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with an elevated anion gap and acidosis has a fall in HCO3- of 12 mEq/L and a rise in the gap of 12 mEq/L from normal. What does the delta HCO3-/delta anion gap ratio of 1 indicate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap acidosis with no second metabolic disorder","B":"High-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting metabolic alkalosis","C":"High-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting normal-anion-gap acidosis","D":"High-anion-gap acidosis with HCO3- partly lowered by respiratory alkalosis","E":"Normal-anion-gap acidosis with a coexisting metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When a high-anion-gap acidosis is the only process changing HCO3-, the fall in HCO3- is mirrored by the rise in the anion gap, giving a ratio near 1. A ratio below 1 suggests a second process raising HCO3-, and a ratio above 1 suggests one lowering HCO3-. The ratio does not assess respiratory disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta HCO3- over delta anion gap near 1 means a single metabolic process. COMLEX: use the ratio to search for hidden second disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the renal rules and delta-delta ratio are applied to complete blood gas interpretation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"Which signaling sequence mediates the antidiuretic effect of ADH on principal cells?","choices":{"A":"V1 receptors raise IP3 and drive AQP2 insertion into the basolateral membrane","B":"V2 receptors raise cGMP and drive ENaC insertion into the luminal membrane","C":"V2 receptors raise cAMP and drive AQP2 insertion into the luminal membrane","D":"V2 receptors lower cAMP and drive AQP1 removal from the proximal tubule","E":"V1 receptors raise cAMP and drive urea channel removal from the medulla"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADH binds basolateral V2 receptors on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, increasing cAMP and triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the luminal membrane, which increases water permeability. V1 receptors mediate vasoconstriction through IP3, and ADH also stimulates the UT1 urea transporter rather than removing it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: V2 to cAMP to AQP2 is the antidiuretic pathway, and V1 to IP3 is the vasoconstrictor pathway. COMLEX: desmopressin acts on V2 receptors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has urine flow of 10 mL/min, urine osmolarity of 100 mOsm/L, and plasma osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L. What is the free-water clearance?","choices":{"A":"+6.7 mL/min","B":"+3.3 mL/min","C":"+10.0 mL/min","D":"0 mL/min","E":"-2.0 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free-water clearance equals urine flow minus osmolar clearance. Osmolar clearance is 100 x 10 / 300 = 3.3 mL/min, so free-water clearance is 10 - 3.3 = +6.7 mL/min. A positive value indicates that solute-free water is being excreted, as when ADH is absent or ineffective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: positive free-water clearance means dilute urine, and negative means concentrated urine. COMLEX: isosmotic urine gives a free-water clearance of zero.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":1,"stem":"Which condition is associated with a negative free-water clearance?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus from pituitary ADH deficiency","B":"SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion by a lung tumor","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from collecting duct ADH resistance","D":"Primary polydipsia from suppressed ADH release","E":"Loop diuretic treatment from loss of the medullary gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In SIADH excess ADH causes hyperosmotic urine with low urine flow, so free-water clearance is negative. Central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and primary polydipsia typically produce hyposmotic urine with a positive value, and loop diuretics produce isosmotic urine with a free-water clearance near zero.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high ADH gives negative free-water clearance, and low or ineffective ADH gives positive clearance. COMLEX: use urine osmolarity to separate these states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the corticopapillary gradient and ADH action determine urine osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve carries the parasympathetic efferents that contract the detrusor muscle during voiding?","choices":{"A":"Superior hypogastric plexus","B":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","C":"Pudendal nerve branches","D":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","E":"Genitofemoral nerve trunk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves and contract the detrusor while relaxing the internal sphincter to produce voiding. The hypogastric nerve carries sympathetic fibers that promote storage, and the pudendal nerve carries somatic fibers to the external sphincter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S2, S3, and S4 keep the bladder emptying, and sympathetic fibers promote storage. COMLEX: sacral lesions therefore impair detrusor contraction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic pathways govern storage and voiding","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure is under voluntary control through the somatic pudendal nerve?","choices":{"A":"Detrusor muscle of the bladder","B":"Internal urethral sphincter","C":"External urethral sphincter","D":"Trigone of the bladder base","E":"Smooth muscle of the ureter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor fibers from S2-S4 to the skeletal muscle of the external urethral sphincter, which allows voluntary control of continence. The detrusor, internal sphincter, trigone, and ureteral muscle are smooth muscle under autonomic control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external sphincter is the skeletal-muscle sphincter of the urethra, under somatic control. COMLEX: it provides voluntary continence via S2-S4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic pathways govern storage and voiding","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"Which effect does sympathetic stimulation have on the lower urinary tract during urine storage?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic contraction of the detrusor and relaxation of the internal sphincter","B":"Beta-3 relaxation of the detrusor and alpha-1 contraction of the sphincter","C":"Nicotinic relaxation of the external sphincter and contraction of the detrusor","D":"Beta-2 contraction of the trigone and relaxation of the ureters","E":"Alpha-2 relaxation of the internal sphincter and contraction of the detrusor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During storage the sympathetic hypogastric nerve relaxes the detrusor through beta-3 receptors and contracts the internal urethral sphincter through alpha-1 receptors, favoring urine retention. Muscarinic detrusor contraction with sphincter relaxation is the parasympathetic voiding pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic equals storage, parasympathetic equals voiding, somatic equals voluntary continence. COMLEX: alpha-1 blockers relax the internal sphincter and aid voiding in BPH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic pathways govern storage and voiding","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"Which autonomic pathway mediates penile erection?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Sympathetic hypogastric nerves from T11-L2","C":"Somatic pudendal nerves from S2-S4","D":"Sympathetic fibers from the celiac ganglion","E":"Parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Erection is a parasympathetic response mediated by the pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2-S4, which release nitric oxide and cause smooth muscle relaxation. Emission is sympathetic through the hypogastric nerve, and expulsion is somatic through the pudendal nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: point, squeeze, and shoot maps parasympathetic erection, sympathetic emission, and somatic expulsion. COMLEX: S2, S3, and S4 keep the penis off the floor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intracellular signaling effect of nitric oxide in penile erectile tissue?","choices":{"A":"Lowered cGMP that relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle","B":"Raised cGMP that relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle","C":"Raised cAMP that contracts cavernosal smooth muscle","D":"Raised intracellular Ca2+ that contracts arterial smooth muscle","E":"Lowered cAMP that relaxes venous smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP and activating protein kinase G, which relaxes smooth muscle in the corpora cavernosa, producing vasodilation and engorgement. Norepinephrine raising intracellular Ca2+ causes contraction and detumescence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the NO to cGMP to smooth muscle relaxation pathway is pro-erectile, and norepinephrine is anti-erectile. COMLEX: this is the pathway targeted by PDE5 inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the mechanism of action of PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil?","choices":{"A":"Increasing nitric oxide synthase expression in the corpora","B":"Blocking alpha-1 receptors in the penile arteries","C":"Slowing the breakdown of cGMP in cavernosal smooth muscle","D":"Stimulating norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerves","E":"Slowing the breakdown of cAMP in cavernosal smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PDE5 inhibitors block phosphodiesterase type 5, preventing breakdown of cGMP, so the effect of nitric oxide is prolonged and smooth muscle relaxation is enhanced when sexual stimulation triggers NO release. They do not create the erection without stimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sildenafil raises cGMP and is contraindicated with nitrates. COMLEX: priapism lasting more than four hours is an emergency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives the male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man in hemorrhagic shock has high sympathetic tone and moderately elevated angiotensin II levels. RPF falls markedly, yet GFR is relatively preserved. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Preferential afferent dilation from prostaglandins that lowers filtration fraction","B":"Marked afferent constriction that lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure","C":"Reduced plasma protein concentration that lowers oncotic pressure and raises RPF","D":"Efferent constriction that raises capillary pressure and filtration fraction","E":"Increased Bowman space pressure that raises net ultrafiltration pressure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low concentrations angiotensin II preferentially constricts efferent arterioles, which raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and protects GFR while reducing RPF, so filtration fraction rises. Afferent constriction would lower PGC and GFR, and raised Bowman space pressure would oppose filtration. Afferent dilation would raise RPF rather than lower it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in shock, angiotensin II maintains GFR at the cost of RPF, and NSAIDs or ACE inhibitors remove these protections. COMLEX: watch for acute kidney injury when these drugs are given during volume depletion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive control of afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with vomiting and volume depletion takes ibuprofen and develops a rising creatinine. Which mechanism best explains the fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Lost prostaglandin afferent dilation leaves constrictors lowering capillary pressure","B":"Direct toxicity to the podocyte foot processes that lowers the filtration coefficient","C":"Constriction of the efferent arteriole by prostaglandin E2 that lowers RPF","D":"Increased plasma oncotic pressure from prostaglandin inhibition that lowers GFR","E":"Obstruction of the ureter by drug crystals that raises Bowman space pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prostaglandins E2 and I2 dilate afferent arterioles and protect renal blood flow in high vasoconstrictor states, such as volume depletion with high sympathetic tone and angiotensin II. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing this protection, so afferent constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs cause prerenal azotemia in volume-depleted patients by removing afferent dilation. COMLEX: avoid NSAIDs in heart failure, cirrhosis, and volume depletion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — prostaglandins protect afferent flow and NSAIDs remove this buffer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"At the afferent end of a glomerular capillary, PGC is 45 mm Hg and pi GC is 27 mm Hg. A ureteral stone raises PBS from 10 to 25 mm Hg. What is the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point?","choices":{"A":"-13 mm Hg","B":"+8 mm Hg","C":"+18 mm Hg","D":"+20 mm Hg","E":"-7 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals PGC minus PBS minus pi GC, so 45 - 25 - 27 = -7 mm Hg, meaning the net force no longer favors filtration and GFR falls to near zero. The value +8 mm Hg is the normal result with a PBS of 10, and the other values result from omitting or mishandling one of the terms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: net pressure equals PGC minus PBS minus pi GC, with pi BS taken as zero. COMLEX: obstruction can abolish filtration when PBS rises enough.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — the Starling equation defines net ultrafiltration pressure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A patient's mean arterial pressure rises from 100 to 140 mm Hg during a hypertensive episode, yet renal blood flow stays constant. Which response best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Afferent constriction that raises renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","B":"Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","C":"Afferent arteriolar dilation that lowers renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","D":"Increased sympathetic activity that reduces renal vascular resistance in proportion to pressure","E":"Increased circulating ANP that lowers total renal resistance to hold flow constant"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoregulation maintains constant renal blood flow by changing renal vascular resistance in proportion to arterial pressure. Increased pressure stretches the afferent arteriole, causing myogenic constriction, and increases NaCl delivery to the macula densa, causing tubuloglomerular feedback constriction, so resistance increases to hold flow constant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoregulation changes resistance in the same direction as pressure to keep flow constant. COMLEX: this occurs at the afferent arteriole within the autoregulatory range.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — the intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with hemorrhage has low renal perfusion pressure. Which stimulus at the juxtaglomerular apparatus promotes renin release?","choices":{"A":"Rising afferent arteriolar wall stretch from higher pressure","B":"Angiotensin II feedback acting directly on the juxtaglomerular cells","C":"Decreased NaCl delivery sensed by the macula densa","D":"Beta-1 adrenergic blockade at the sympathetic nerve terminals","E":"Higher circulating ANP levels inhibiting renal cell signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Renin release is stimulated by reduced afferent arteriolar stretch, reduced NaCl delivery to the macula densa, and beta-1 sympathetic stimulation. Rising pressure, angiotensin II feedback, and ANP inhibit renin release, and beta-1 blockade would reduce it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renin is released by low perfusion pressure, low NaCl delivery, and sympathetic beta-1 stimulation. COMLEX: this is the entry point to the RAAS cascade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — juxtaglomerular apparatus signaling links flow sensing to renin release","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Autoregulation & JGA Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A man on a high-protein diet has an increase in GFR. Which mechanism best explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Greater macula densa NaCl delivery constricts the afferent arteriole to raise GFR","B":"Less macula densa NaCl delivery dilates the afferent arteriole through feedback","C":"Lower proximal NaCl reabsorption raises distal delivery and dilates the efferent arteriole","D":"Rising plasma protein concentration lowers capillary oncotic pressure and raises GFR","E":"Greater myogenic stretch of the afferent arteriole increases its diameter and GFR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A high-protein diet increases proximal Na+ and Cl- reabsorption, decreasing delivery to the macula densa. Tubuloglomerular feedback responds by dilating the afferent arteriole, which raises glomerular capillary pressure and GFR. Increased plasma protein would raise oncotic pressure and reduce GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced macula densa delivery dilates the afferent arteriole and raises GFR. COMLEX: this feedback loop matches filtration to tubular reabsorptive capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback adjusts afferent tone from distal delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A man with untreated diabetes has GFR 120 mL/min, plasma glucose 400 mg/dL, urine glucose 2500 mg/dL, and urine flow 4 mL/min. What is his glucose reabsorption rate?","choices":{"A":"480 mg/min","B":"100 mg/min","C":"580 mg/min","D":"380 mg/min","E":"280 mg/min"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The filtered load is GFR x plasma glucose, or 120 mL/min x 4 mg/mL = 480 mg/min. The excretion rate is urine concentration times flow, or 25 mg/mL x 4 mL/min = 100 mg/min. Reabsorption is the difference, 480 - 100 = 380 mg/min, which approximates the glucose transport maximum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reabsorption equals filtered load minus excretion, and it saturates at Tm. COMLEX: convert every concentration to consistent units before calculating.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — glucose titration curve, Tm, and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A freely filtered substance has plasma concentration 2 mg/mL, urine concentration 50 mg/mL, and urine flow 2 mL/min, while inulin clearance is 120 mL/min. Which statement is correct?","choices":{"A":"Clearance is 50 mL/min, so net secretion occurred","B":"Clearance is 50 mL/min, so net reabsorption occurred","C":"Clearance is 120 mL/min, so it is handled like inulin","D":"Clearance is 25 mL/min, so net reabsorption occurred","E":"Clearance is 200 mL/min, so net secretion occurred"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration, or 50 x 2 / 2 = 50 mL/min. Because this is lower than the inulin clearance of 120 mL/min, the substance was filtered and then net reabsorbed. A clearance greater than inulin would indicate net secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a clearance below inulin implies net reabsorption, and above inulin implies net secretion. COMLEX: use the clearance ratio to infer tubular handling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — clearance ratios relative to inulin reveal tubular handling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Clearance & Transport Maximum","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with plasma glucose of 300 mg/dL has glucosuria even though the average transport maximum has not been reached. Which explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"The transport maximum has been exceeded uniformly","B":"PAH is competitively inhibiting proximal glucose reabsorption","C":"Nephron heterogeneity and low SGLT2 affinity produce splay","D":"GFR has fallen enough to lower the filtered glucose load","E":"Glucose is secreted into the tubule by the distal nephron"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Between the threshold of about 250 mg/dL and the Tm of about 350 mg/dL glucose appears in the urine before all carriers are saturated, a region called splay. Splay is explained by heterogeneity among nephrons and the relatively low affinity of SGLT2. A falling GFR would lower, not raise, the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: splay lies between threshold and Tm, and threshold precedes Tm. COMLEX: this is why glucosuria occurs before maximal reabsorption is reached.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — carrier saturation kinetics and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old child has polyuria, growth restriction, metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypokalemia, and hypercalciuria. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Loss of NCC function in the early distal tubule","B":"Loss of NKCC2 function in the thick ascending limb","C":"Gain of ENaC function in the collecting tubule","D":"Generalized reabsorption failure in the proximal tubule","E":"Loss of aquaporin-2 function in the collecting duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metabolic alkalosis, hypochloremia, hypokalemia, and hypercalciuria in a child point to Bartter syndrome, a defect of the NKCC2 transporter in the thick ascending limb that resembles chronic loop diuretic use. Gitelman syndrome features hypomagnesemia and is milder, and Liddle syndrome features hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bartter equals loop diuretic and includes hypercalciuria. COMLEX: this is an autosomal recessive disorder that presents early.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — the thick ascending limb NKCC2 pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman has muscle cramps, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypochloremia, and metabolic alkalosis, with normal blood pressure. Her urinary calcium excretion is low. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Loss of NKCC2 function in the thick ascending limb","B":"Gain of ENaC function in the cortical collecting tubule","C":"Loss of SGLT2 function in the early proximal tubule","D":"Loss of NCC function in the distal convoluted tubule","E":"Excess aldosterone secretion from an adrenal adenoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with hypomagnesemia, normal blood pressure, and low urinary calcium identifies Gitelman syndrome, a defect of the thiazide-sensitive NaCl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule. Liddle syndrome and aldosteronism cause hypertension. Bartter syndrome causes hypercalciuria, whereas Gitelman syndrome causes hypocalciuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gitelman equals thiazide effect with hypomagnesemia. COMLEX: the presentation is later and milder than Bartter.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — NCC-mediated NaCl reabsorption in the early distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubular Transport & Congenital Channelopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with low renin and low aldosterone. Spironolactone is ineffective but amiloride corrects the abnormalities. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Excess aldosterone secretion from an adrenal adenoma","B":"Loss of NCC function in the early distal tubule","C":"Loss of NKCC2 function in the thick ascending limb","D":"Generalized proximal reabsorption failure with phosphate wasting","E":"Overactive ENaC from impaired channel degradation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hypertension with hypokalemic alkalosis and suppressed renin and aldosterone that responds to amiloride, an ENaC blocker, identifies Liddle syndrome, in which a gain-of-function mutation impairs ENaC degradation and increases Na+ reabsorption in the collecting tubule. An adrenal adenoma would raise aldosterone, and the other defects cause hypotension or proximal wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome mimics hyperaldosteronism but with low aldosterone. COMLEX: treat with amiloride or triamterene, not spironolactone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — collecting tubule ENaC and principal cell transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes has pH 7.22, Pco2 20 mm Hg, HCO3- 8 mEq/L, Na+ 132 mEq/L, and Cl- 96 mEq/L. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","B":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory acidosis","C":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed respiratory alkalosis","D":"Normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","E":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a superimposed metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 132 - (96 + 8) = 28 mEq/L, which is high. Winter's formula predicts an expected Pco2 of 1.5 x 8 + 8 = 20 mm Hg, matching the measured value, so compensation is appropriate. The delta HCO3- of 16 relative to the delta anion gap of 16 gives a ratio of 1, so there is no second metabolic disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: check the anion gap, then Winter's formula, then the delta ratio. COMLEX: DKA is the classic high-anion-gap acidosis with appropriate compensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — Winter's formula and the delta ratio applied to a full blood gas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with persistent vomiting has pH 7.52, Pco2 48 mm Hg, and HCO3- 38 mEq/L. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic alkalosis with a superimposed respiratory acidosis","B":"Metabolic alkalosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","C":"Metabolic alkalosis with a superimposed respiratory alkalosis","D":"Respiratory alkalosis with appropriate renal compensation","E":"Respiratory acidosis with appropriate renal compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vomiting causes loss of gastric H+ and a rise in HCO3-, the primary disturbance, producing alkalemia. The renal rule predicts a rise in Pco2 of about 0.7 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-, or about 10 mm Hg for a 14 mEq/L rise, giving about 50 mm Hg, which matches his measured 48 mm Hg. Compensation is therefore appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic alkalosis is compensated by hypoventilation, which raises Pco2. COMLEX: contraction alkalosis with hypokalemia and hypochloremia accompanies vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — metabolic alkalosis maintenance and respiratory compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Quantitative Acid-Base & Electrolyte Interpretation","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old who ingested aspirin tablets has pH 7.46, Pco2 22 mm Hg, HCO3- 15 mEq/L, Na+ 140 mEq/L, and Cl- 104 mEq/L. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with compensation matching the Winter's prediction","B":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory acidosis","C":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation","D":"Normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","E":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His anion gap is 140 - (104 + 15) = 21 mEq/L, so there is a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a delta ratio of 1, and no second metabolic disorder. Winter's formula predicts a Pco2 of 1.5 x 15 + 8 = 30.5 mm Hg, plus or minus 2, but the measured Pco2 of 22 mm Hg is lower than predicted, indicating an additional respiratory alkalosis. Salicylates stimulate the respiratory center and also cause metabolic acidosis. The high anion gap and a delta ratio of 1 show the HCO3- fall is metabolic, not renal compensation for a primary respiratory alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate poisoning is the classic mixed respiratory alkalosis and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: a Pco2 below the Winter's range signals a superimposed respiratory alkalosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — simple versus mixed disorders identified from blood gas values","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man on chronic lithium therapy has polyuria and serum osmolarity of 310 mOsm/L. Urine osmolarity is 90 mOsm/L before and 95 mOsm/L after desmopressin. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus","B":"Primary polydipsia","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","D":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion","E":"Osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperosmolar serum with persistently dilute urine that does not respond to desmopressin indicates that the collecting ducts are unresponsive to ADH, which is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, a known complication of lithium. In central diabetes insipidus, desmopressin would raise urine osmolarity, and primary polydipsia would produce low serum osmolarity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: central DI responds to desmopressin, and nephrogenic DI does not. COMLEX: lithium and demeclocycline are classic causes of nephrogenic DI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — ADH-dependent water permeability of the collecting duct","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old smoker with small cell lung cancer has serum Na+ 121 mEq/L, serum osmolarity 255 mOsm/L, and urine osmolarity 550 mOsm/L, and is euvolemic. Which combination of free-water clearance and urine flow is expected?","choices":{"A":"Positive free-water clearance with high urine flow","B":"Negative free-water clearance with low urine flow","C":"Zero free-water clearance with isosmotic urine","D":"Negative free-water clearance with high urine flow","E":"Positive free-water clearance with low urine flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion. Excess ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting ducts, producing hyperosmotic urine with low urine flow and a negative free-water clearance, and dilutional hyponatremia with low serum osmolarity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH gives hyponatremia, euvolemia, concentrated urine, and negative free-water clearance. COMLEX: small cell carcinoma is a classic ectopic ADH source.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — SIADH pathophysiology and its effect on urine concentration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Osmoregulation & Free-Water Clearance","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with schizophrenia drinks about 10 L of water daily. Serum Na+ is 128 mEq/L, serum osmolarity is 265 mOsm/L, and urine osmolarity is 60 mOsm/L, rising to 600 mOsm/L after water restriction. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus with low ADH and positive free-water clearance","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with ADH resistance and positive free-water clearance","C":"SIADH with excess ADH and negative free-water clearance","D":"Primary polydipsia with suppressed ADH and positive free-water clearance","E":"Adrenal insufficiency with volume-driven ADH release and negative free-water clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excess water intake suppresses ADH, producing dilute urine, positive free-water clearance, and low serum sodium and osmolarity. Because the kidney is normal, water restriction restores concentrating ability, which distinguishes primary polydipsia from both forms of diabetes insipidus, in which the urine stays dilute despite water restriction. A urine osmolarity of 60 is inconsistent with ADH excess.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low serum osmolarity with dilute urine points to water intake, and a response to water restriction confirms it. COMLEX: psychiatric patients are at risk for psychogenic polydipsia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — water deprivation testing and the ADH response","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man six months after a T6 spinal cord transection has involuntary bladder emptying at low volumes with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia. Which lesion pattern explains this?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron injury of the S2-S4 roots","B":"Isolated pudendal nerve injury at the pelvic floor","C":"Upper motor neuron injury above the sacral center","D":"Sympathetic chain injury below the L2 level","E":"Peripheral neuropathy of the pelvic splanchnic nerves"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After recovery from spinal shock, a lesion above the sacral micturition center leaves the sacral reflex arc intact but disconnected from pontine control, producing a spastic, hyperreflexic bladder with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia. Lower motor neuron lesions of S2-S4 produce a flaccid atonic bladder with overflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UMN lesions give a spastic bladder, and LMN lesions give a flaccid bladder. COMLEX: the level of the lesion determines the pattern of voiding dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — spastic versus flaccid neurogenic bladder types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a large midline disc herniation has saddle anesthesia, a distended bladder, and dribbling incontinence. Which best describes her bladder dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Atonic bladder from a lower motor neuron lesion","B":"Spastic hyperreflexic bladder from an upper motor neuron lesion","C":"Stress incontinence from weakened pelvic floor support","D":"Urge incontinence from detrusor overactivity","E":"Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia from a pontine lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cauda equina compression damages the S2-S4 roots, interrupting the sacral reflex arc and producing a flaccid, atonic bladder that fills without contracting, with overflow incontinence and loss of perineal sensation. Spastic bladder and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia result from upper motor neuron lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: saddle anesthesia with urinary retention points to cauda equina syndrome. COMLEX: this is a surgical emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — lower motor neuron lesions cause overflow incontinence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Neural Control of Micturition & Bladder Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old multiparous woman leaks urine when she coughs or laughs, with no urgency, and a normal postvoid residual. Which mechanism best explains her incontinence?","choices":{"A":"Detrusor overactivity with involuntary contractions during filling","B":"Chronic outlet obstruction with an overfilled bladder","C":"Loss of the sacral reflex arc from a lower motor neuron lesion","D":"Loss of pontine inhibition from an upper motor neuron lesion","E":"Weak urethral support against abdominal pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure and no urgency or retention is stress incontinence, caused by weakness of urethral sphincter or pelvic floor support so that abdominal pressure exceeds urethral closure pressure. Urge incontinence reflects detrusor overactivity, and overflow reflects outlet obstruction or an atonic bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stress incontinence equals leakage with cough, urge equals detrusor overactivity, overflow equals a full bladder with dribbling. COMLEX: pelvic floor exercises are first-line therapy for stress incontinence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — clinical types of incontinence and their mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man taking isosorbide for angina takes sildenafil and develops severe hypotension. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Sildenafil blocks nitrate conversion to nitric oxide and worsens angina","B":"Nitrates inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5 and lower cGMP","C":"Both drugs raise cGMP and cause additive vasodilation","D":"Sildenafil increases norepinephrine release and causes a hypertensive crisis","E":"Nitrates block alpha-1 receptors and potentiate sildenafil hypotension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nitrates release nitric oxide and raise cGMP in vascular smooth muscle, and sildenafil blocks cGMP breakdown by PDE5, so together they cause exaggerated vasodilation and severe hypotension. This is why PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with nitrates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NO to cGMP to vasodilation is common to both drugs. COMLEX: ask about nitrate use before prescribing a PDE5 inhibitor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — PDE5 inhibitor signaling and vascular dangers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"Which statement best describes the hormonal regulation of the male reproductive axis?","choices":{"A":"FSH stimulates Leydig testosterone synthesis, and LH supports Sertoli cell function","B":"Testosterone stimulates hypothalamic GnRH release through positive feedback","C":"Inhibin from Leydig cells suppresses pituitary LH release","D":"LH drives Leydig testosterone synthesis and FSH supports Sertoli cell function","E":"GnRH inhibits pituitary LH and FSH secretion in a pulsatile pattern"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulsatile hypothalamic GnRH stimulates anterior pituitary LH and FSH secretion. LH stimulates Leydig cells to synthesize testosterone, and FSH supports Sertoli cells and spermatogenesis. Testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH by negative feedback, and Sertoli cell inhibin selectively inhibits FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH acts on Sertoli cells. COMLEX: use the axis to interpret hormone panels in hypogonadism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — dual negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Male Reproductive Physiology & HPG Axis","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with a complete T4 spinal cord transection asks about sexual function. Which type of erection is most likely to persist?","choices":{"A":"Reflex erections through the sacral parasympathetic pathway","B":"Psychogenic erections through descending cortical input","C":"Erections mediated by sympathetic fibers from the celiac ganglion","D":"Nocturnal erections triggered by vagal input","E":"Erections mediated by the somatic pudendal motor neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reflex erections depend on the sacral S2-S4 parasympathetic arc, which remains intact below a high spinal lesion, so tactile stimulation can still produce erection. Psychogenic erections require descending input that is interrupted by the transection, and the pudendal nerve controls expulsion, not erection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: erection is parasympathetic through S2-S4 and can be reflexive without brain input. COMLEX: level of the cord lesion predicts which components of sexual function remain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — S2-S4 autonomic and somatic pathways as applied to bladder dysfunction after cord injury","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":31,"title":"SDL 31 — Glomerular Filtration Barrier Breakdown, Nephrotic Pathophysiology & Progressive Glomerular Injury","questions":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which three components make up the glomerular capillary wall filtration barrier?","choices":{"A":"Fenestrated endothelium, mesangial cells, and Bowman capsule epithelium","B":"Mesangial matrix, glomerular basement membrane, and parietal epithelial cells","C":"Fenestrated endothelium, glomerular basement membrane, and macula densa cells","D":"Fenestrated endothelium, glomerular basement membrane, and podocytes","E":"Podocytes, mesangial cells, and the tubular basement membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The glomerular capillary wall is composed of endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane, and podocytes, which together act as a size and charge barrier for the plasma filtrate. Mesangial cells and parietal epithelium are not layers of the filtration barrier, and the macula densa and tubular basement membrane lie outside it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to any of the three layers can produce proteinuria, and podocyte injury is the hallmark of primary nephrotic diseases. COMLEX: think of the barrier as size selective and charge selective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits highly selective proteinuria, as seen in minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Mostly albumin and other low-molecular-weight proteins","B":"Albumin together with abundant high-molecular-weight globulins","C":"Predominantly immunoglobulin light chains from plasma cells","D":"Predominantly red cell casts with mild albumin loss","E":"Predominantly tubular proteins with minimal albumin loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selectivity refers to the ratio of low to high molecular weight proteins in the urine. A highly selective proteinuria consists mostly of albumin and transferrin, whereas poorly selective proteinuria also contains higher-molecular-weight globulins, indicating a more damaged barrier. Minimal change disease characteristically has highly selective proteinuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: selective proteinuria predicts steroid responsiveness, as in minimal change disease, and nonselective proteinuria predicts steroid resistance. COMLEX: FSGS and membranous nephropathy are typically nonselective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which change initiates progressive glomerulosclerosis in the remaining glomeruli after loss of nephron mass?","choices":{"A":"Deposition of subepithelial immune complexes that activate complement","B":"Autoantibodies directed against the slit diaphragm protein nephrin","C":"Hypertrophy with raised capillary pressure and hyperfiltration","D":"Fibrin thrombi that occlude the lumens of glomerular capillaries","E":"Reduced glomerular blood flow with falling intraglomerular pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After loss of renal tissue the remaining glomeruli hypertrophy, and glomerular blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure increase, causing endothelial and podocyte injury that leads to segmental and eventually global sclerosis. The other options describe membranous nephropathy, a slit diaphragm injury, thrombotic microangiopathy, and a hemodynamic change opposite to the adaptive response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adaptive FSGS follows nephron loss, obesity, or reflux nephropathy through hyperfiltration and glomerular hypertension. COMLEX: this is why ACE inhibitors slow progression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define nephrotic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Hematuria, hypertension, oliguria, and mild proteinuria","B":"Heavy proteinuria, hematuria, hypertension, and azotemia","C":"Pyuria, fever, flank pain, and white blood cell casts","D":"Heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia","E":"Hypercalcemia, polyuria, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nephrotic syndrome consists of massive proteinuria of 3.5 g or more per day, hypoalbuminemia with plasma albumin below 3 g/dL, generalized edema, and hyperlipidemia with lipiduria. Hematuria, hypertension, and reduced GFR characterize nephritic syndrome, and the other combinations describe pyelonephritis and tubular disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tetrad is proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia, all following from glomerular protein loss. COMLEX: distinguish nephrotic from nephritic on the basis of the urinary sediment and blood pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — loss of the barrier's charge and size selectivity produces the heavy proteinuria that defines this syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"What best explains the generalized edema of nephrotic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure from failure of the right ventricle","B":"Obstruction of lymphatic drainage by interstitial protein deposition","C":"Elevated natriuretic peptide levels that widen the interstitial space","D":"Increased capillary permeability from circulating inflammatory mediators","E":"Low oncotic pressure with renal Na+ and water retention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heavy proteinuria lowers plasma albumin and therefore intravascular colloid osmotic pressure, causing fluid to leave the capillaries. Hypovolemia stimulates renin, aldosterone, and sympathetic activity, and primary renal sodium retention also contributes, so sodium and water retention aggravate the edema. The other mechanisms do not underlie nephrotic edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrotic edema is soft, pitting, and periorbital in children, and it is driven by low oncotic pressure plus salt retention. COMLEX: this differs from the hypertensive, hematuric edema of nephritic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — loss of the barrier's charge and size selectivity produces the albumin loss behind this edema","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Why are patients with nephrotic syndrome prone to thromboembolic events?","choices":{"A":"Immune destruction of platelets leading to compensatory thrombocytosis","B":"Urinary loss of endogenous anticoagulants such as antithrombin III","C":"Urinary loss of vitamin K needed for clotting factor synthesis","D":"Overproduction of erythropoietin that raises the hematocrit sharply","E":"Deposition of antiplatelet antibodies along the endothelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nephrotic patients lose antithrombin III and other endogenous anticoagulants in the urine, producing a hypercoagulable state that predisposes to renal vein thrombosis, particularly in membranous nephropathy. They also lose immunoglobulins and are vulnerable to staphylococcal and pneumococcal infections. Vitamin K loss would cause bleeding rather than thrombosis, and platelet destruction or antiplatelet antibodies are not features of nephrotic syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal vein thrombosis is usually a consequence, not a cause, of nephrotic syndrome. COMLEX: watch for flank pain and hematuria in a nephrotic patient as a clue to renal vein thrombosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the same barrier failure that leaks albumin also leaks low-molecular-weight anticoagulant proteins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which biopsy findings characterize minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial deposits on electron microscopy","B":"Segmental scarring of a subset of glomeruli with hyalinosis on light microscopy","C":"Normal light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement on electron microscopy","D":"Crescents filling Bowman space with linear IgG on immunofluorescence","E":"Subendothelial deposits with wire-loop lesions on light microscopy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Minimal change disease shows glomeruli that appear normal by light microscopy, with diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes and loss of slit diaphragms on electron microscopy, and no electron-dense deposits. Membranous nephropathy shows subepithelial deposits, FSGS shows segmental sclerosis, anti-GBM disease shows crescents with linear IgG, and lupus class IV shows wire loops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal light microscopy with effacement of foot processes on EM is the classic description of MCD. COMLEX: lipoid nephrosis refers to the lipid-laden proximal tubule cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is characteristic of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Sclerosis of part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli","B":"Uniform thickening of the capillary walls without hypercellularity","C":"Normal glomeruli with fine granular IgG over the podocytes","D":"Crescent formation in most glomeruli with linear IgG","E":"Mesangial IgA deposits with intermittent gross hematuria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FSGS is characterized by sclerosis of a portion of the capillary tuft, segmental, in a subset of glomeruli, focal, with collapse of capillary loops, matrix deposition, and hyalinosis. It is a leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults, particularly in Black and Hispanic patients, and responds poorly to corticosteroids. Uniform wall thickening, granular or linear IgG with crescents, and mesangial IgA describe membranous nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: focal means some glomeruli and segmental means part of the tuft. COMLEX: biopsy sampling can miss FSGS because only a minority of glomeruli are affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":1,"stem":"Which antigen is the most frequent autoantibody target in primary membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Nephrin of the slit diaphragm","B":"M-type phospholipase A2 receptor","C":"Alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","D":"Apolipoprotein L1 in podocytes","E":"Galactose-deficient IgA1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoantibodies to the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor on the surface of podocytes underlie about 60 percent of primary membranous nephropathy, leading to complement activation and subepithelial immune deposits. Nephrin defects cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, type IV collagen is involved in Alport syndrome, APOL1 variants confer FSGS risk, and galactose-deficient IgA1 underlies IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-PLA2R antibodies are a diagnostic and prognostic marker of primary membranous nephropathy. COMLEX: membranous nephropathy is a leading primary cause of nephrotic syndrome in older and white adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — podocyte slit diaphragm and basement membrane integrity determine which barrier layer is injured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with new-onset nephrotic syndrome has urine protein made up of albumin and transferrin with little IgG. Which mechanism best explains this selective albumin loss?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the fenestrated endothelium allows any plasma protein through","B":"Hypertrophy of podocyte foot processes widens the filtration slits","C":"Lost anionic charge removes repulsion of negatively charged albumin","D":"Increased Bowman space pressure forces albumin across the barrier","E":"Reduced tubular reabsorption allows more filtered albumin to escape"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anionic glycoproteins line the filtration barrier and repel negatively charged plasma proteins such as albumin. When these charges are lost, as in glomerular disease, albumin passes into the urine, producing proteinuria. Albumin loss is mainly a barrier defect, not a change in Bowman space pressure or tubular reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the charge barrier explains why albumin, an anionic protein, is lost early. COMLEX: this is the mechanism most emphasized in minimal change disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria starts lisinopril, and over the next year his proteinuria falls and his GFR decline slows. Which mechanism best explains this benefit?","choices":{"A":"They lower intraglomerular pressure and blunt angiotensin-driven fibrosis","B":"They raise glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure to increase filtration","C":"They remove circulating anti-nephrin antibodies from the plasma","D":"They replace lost podocytes through stimulation of progenitor cells","E":"They increase efferent arteriolar tone to maintain hyperfiltration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Angiotensin II constricts efferent arterioles, raising intraglomerular pressure, and has direct profibrotic effects. Inhibiting the renin-angiotensin system dilates the efferent arteriole, reduces glomerular hypertension, and blunts these effects, which ameliorates the progression of sclerosis in animal and human studies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are renoprotective in proteinuric and diabetic kidney disease. COMLEX: monitor potassium and creatinine after starting therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Progressive Injury Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with primary FSGS has biopsy evidence of segmental sclerosis with hyaline deposits. Which initial event best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial proliferation that occludes the capillary lumen","B":"Neutrophil infiltration of the mesangium with capillary wall rupture","C":"Subendothelial immune complex deposition with basement membrane duplication","D":"Fibrin deposition in afferent arterioles that blocks inflow","E":"Podocyte loss that exposes denuded segments of glomerular basement membrane"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In primary FSGS the podocytopathy leads to podocyte loss that exceeds the ability of surviving podocytes to compensate. Denuded areas of basement membrane become hyperpermeable, plasma proteins are entrapped causing hyalinosis, and the capillary collapses with matrix accumulation, producing segmental sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS is a primary podocytopathy with progressive podocyte loss. COMLEX: it lies on the same spectrum as minimal change disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — the layered structure and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier is what fails in this injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has periorbital edema, urine protein of 4 g/day, serum albumin of 2.1 g/dL, and hypercholesterolemia after a respiratory infection. Which sequence best explains his edema?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac failure raises venous hydrostatic pressure and drives fluid into tissues","B":"Inflammatory mediators increase capillary permeability and let albumin escape into interstitium","C":"Albumin loss lowers plasma oncotic pressure and the kidney retains Na+ and water","D":"Lymphatic obstruction traps protein in tissues and pulls water into the interstitium","E":"Excess natriuretic peptide release widens the interstitial compartment through vasodilation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Massive proteinuria depletes serum albumin faster than the liver can replace it, reducing colloid osmotic pressure so fluid leaves the vessels. The resulting hypovolemia raises renin, aldosterone, and sympathetic tone, and primary renal sodium retention also contributes, so sodium and water retention worsen the edema, which is soft and pitting and most marked periorbitally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a child with nephrotic syndrome after an infection most often has minimal change disease. COMLEX: periorbital swelling in the morning is a classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — renin, aldosterone, and natriuretic peptide regulation of sodium retention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with advanced CKD has serum phosphate 7.2 mg/dL, calcium 7.8 mg/dL, and a markedly elevated PTH. Which sequence best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Rising GFR clears phosphate and low PTH raises calcium reabsorption from bone","B":"High calcitriol increases gut Ca2+ absorption and suppresses PTH secretion","C":"Excess dietary calcium lowers phosphate and raises the PTH set point","D":"GFR decline retains phosphate while low calcitriol lowers Ca2+ absorption","E":"Primary parathyroid adenoma raises calcium and lowers phosphate excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As GFR falls the kidney retains phosphate, and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity declines so calcitriol falls. Hyperphosphatemia and low calcitriol both lower serum calcium, and the hypocalcemia stimulates PTH secretion, producing secondary hyperparathyroidism. A primary adenoma would cause hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD produces high phosphate, low calcium, low calcitriol, and high PTH. COMLEX: phosphate binders and vitamin D analogs treat this pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — PTH regulation of phosphate handling and its cellular mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Systemic Uremic Manifestations & Osteodystrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with long-standing CKD has bone pain and fractures. Alkaline phosphatase and PTH are high, and calcium is low. Which process best explains her bone disease?","choices":{"A":"Primary hyperparathyroidism from an adenoma with hypercalcemia and high turnover","B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism with high turnover and poor mineralization","C":"Excess calcitriol causing bone resorption with hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria","D":"Adynamic bone from suppressed osteoblast activity despite high circulating PTH","E":"Autoimmune destruction of osteoclasts causing dense sclerotic bone with low PTH"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal osteodystrophy results from secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases bone resorption, together with calcitriol deficiency and phosphate retention that impair mineralization of osteoid, producing osteomalacia features. High alkaline phosphatase reflects increased osteoblastic turnover, and primary hyperparathyroidism would raise calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal osteodystrophy combines osteitis fibrosa from high PTH and osteomalacia from low calcitriol. COMLEX: this explains bone pain and fractures in dialysis patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — calcium and phosphate handling and PTH action in bone and kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl develops edema after a viral illness. Urine protein is 4+, albumin is 1.9 g/dL, blood pressure is unremarkable, and there is no hematuria. Which biopsy and treatment response are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Normal-appearing glomeruli with foot process effacement and rapid steroid response","B":"Segmental sclerosis on light microscopy with a poor response to steroids","C":"Subepithelial deposits with spikes on silver stain and a variable steroid response","D":"Crescents and linear IgG deposits with rapidly declining renal function","E":"Diffuse mesangial IgA with episodic gross hematuria and preserved GFR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is minimal change disease, the most frequent cause of childhood nephrotic syndrome, with normal light microscopy, diffuse podocyte foot process effacement, and no deposits. Proteinuria is highly selective, renal function is stable, and more than 90 percent of children respond rapidly to corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric nephrotic syndrome with normal blood pressure and no hematuria is minimal change disease until proven otherwise. COMLEX: empiric steroids are given without biopsy in typical cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — podocyte foot process and slit diaphragm structure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old African American man with hypertension and HIV infection has nephrotic-range proteinuria and progressive azotemia. Biopsy shows collapse of the glomerular tuft with tubular microcysts. Which risk factor is most strongly associated?","choices":{"A":"Mutations in the COL4A5 gene","B":"High-risk APOL1 G1 or G2 alleles","C":"Anti-PLA2R autoantibodies","D":"Aberrant O-glycosylation of IgA1","E":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collapsing glomerulopathy, seen in HIV-associated nephropathy, is a severe FSGS variant, and high-risk APOL1 G1 and G2 alleles, common in people of African descent, greatly increase its risk. The other options describe Alport syndrome, membranous nephropathy, IgA nephropathy, and anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APOL1 risk alleles confer trypanosome resistance and increase risk of FSGS and CKD in people of African descent. COMLEX: HIVAN and collapsing FSGS have a poor prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — podocyte injury leads to segmental sclerosis and protein entrapment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Primary Nephrotic Podocytopathies","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with a lung mass has nephrotic syndrome. Biopsy shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls, electron microscopy shows subepithelial deposits, and silver stain shows spikes between them. Which diagnosis and next step are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Minimal change disease with immediate high-dose corticosteroids","B":"Primary FSGS with evaluation for an inherited slit diaphragm defect","C":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis with antibiotic therapy","D":"Membranous nephropathy with a search for an underlying cancer","E":"IgA nephropathy with evaluation for a recent pharyngeal infection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial deposits and basement membrane spikes are the hallmarks of membranous nephropathy. Because as many as 5 to 10 percent of adult cases are associated with malignancy, particularly lung and colon carcinoma, secondary causes must be excluded first, since treating the underlying cause can reverse the injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: membranous nephropathy is a leading primary cause of nephrotic syndrome in older and white adults, and it responds poorly to steroids. COMLEX: screen for cancer, drugs, infections, and lupus before calling it primary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — subepithelial deposits injure the podocyte layer of the barrier","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":32,"title":"SDL 32 — Inflammatory Glomerulonephritides & Rapidly Progressive Glomerular Injury","questions":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define nephritic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia","B":"Pyuria, fever, flank pain, and white blood cell casts","C":"Polyuria, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypotension","D":"Hematuria, hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and fever","E":"Hematuria with casts, oliguria, azotemia, and hypertension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nephritic syndrome results from inflammatory glomerular injury that damages capillary walls, letting blood into the urine, and lowers GFR, causing oliguria, fluid retention, and azotemia. Hypertension results from fluid retention and renin release from ischemic kidneys. The other combinations describe nephrotic syndrome, pyelonephritis, a tubular disorder, and a thrombotic microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephritic means hematuria, red cell casts, azotemia, oliguria, and hypertension, and the prototype is poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: compare with nephrotic syndrome, which is dominated by protein loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern characterizes acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","B":"Coarsely granular deposits of IgG and C3","C":"Dominant mesangial IgA deposits with C3","D":"Little or no immune deposition on staining","E":"Full-house granular IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Poststreptococcal GN is an immune complex disease, and immunofluorescence shows discrete coarsely granular deposits of IgG and C3 in a starry-sky pattern along the basement membrane and mesangium. Linear IgG is seen in anti-GBM disease, mesangial IgA in IgA nephropathy, and absent staining in pauci-immune GN. Full-house staining points to lupus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: granular means immune complexes, linear means anti-GBM, and pauci-immune means none. COMLEX: C3 is low because complement is consumed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding is characteristic of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial deposits forming wire loops","B":"Basket-weave splitting of the basement membrane","C":"Diffuse foot process effacement without deposits","D":"Discrete subepithelial electron-dense humps","E":"Tram-track duplication of the basement membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Discrete amorphous electron-dense deposits on the epithelial side of the basement membrane, appearing as humps, are the characteristic electron microscopic finding of poststreptococcal GN. Wire loops indicate lupus nephritis, basket-weave splitting suggests Alport syndrome, effacement without deposits suggests minimal change disease, and tram-tracks suggest membranoproliferative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subepithelial humps are the classic EM description of poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophils appear on light microscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — the same filtration barrier that leaks protein in nephrotic disease is inflamed and ruptured here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Crescents in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis are formed predominantly by proliferation of which cells?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial cells within the glomerular tuft","B":"Endothelial cells of the glomerular capillaries","C":"Epithelial cells lining Bowman capsule","D":"Granular cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus","E":"Proximal tubule epithelial cells of the cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crescents are produced predominantly by proliferation of the epithelial cells lining Bowman capsule, together with monocytes and macrophages that have migrated into the urinary space. Fibrin strands are frequently prominent, and the crescents may obliterate the space and compress the tuft.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crescent means proliferating parietal epithelium and macrophages in Bowman space. COMLEX: crescents in most glomeruli define crescentic, or rapidly progressive, glomerulonephritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — rupture of the filtration barrier lets fibrin and proliferating epithelium fill Bowman space","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern defines type I rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Granular IgG and complement deposits in capillary walls","B":"Linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane","C":"Dominant mesangial IgA with variable IgG and C3","D":"Little or no deposition of immune reactants","E":"Dominant C3 with ill-defined electron-dense deposits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type I RPGN is anti-GBM antibody-mediated disease, marked by smooth linear deposits of IgG, and often C3, along the basement membrane. Type II shows granular deposits, type III is pauci-immune with little or no deposition, and C3 glomerulonephritis shows C3-dominant staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type I is anti-GBM with linear IgG, type II is immune complex with granular deposits, and type III is pauci-immune. COMLEX: plasmapheresis is standard therapy for anti-GBM disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 2 — the collagen IV antigen targeted in anti-GBM disease is also mutated in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which autoantibody is characteristic of type III, pauci-immune, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"ANCA directed against PR3 or MPO","B":"Antibody to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","C":"Antibody to the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor","D":"Antibody to double-stranded DNA","E":"Antibody to streptococcal exotoxin B"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most patients with pauci-immune crescentic GN have circulating ANCA, either cytoplasmic PR3-ANCA or perinuclear MPO-ANCA. Anti-GBM antibodies target the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV, anti-PLA2R defines membranous nephropathy, and anti-dsDNA is a lupus marker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PR3-ANCA is c-ANCA and typical of granulomatosis with polyangiitis, while MPO-ANCA is p-ANCA and typical of microscopic polyangiitis. COMLEX: B-cell targeted therapy is important in treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — ANCA-mediated necrosis ruptures the filtration barrier without immune deposits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic class of lupus nephritis is the most common and most often associated with renal failure?","choices":{"A":"Minimal mesangial lupus nephritis","B":"Mesangial proliferative lupus nephritis","C":"Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","D":"Focal proliferative lupus nephritis","E":"Membranous lupus nephritis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Class IV, diffuse lupus nephritis, is the most common and severe form and involves more than 50 percent of glomeruli with proliferation of endothelial, mesangial, and epithelial cells, and crescents. Classes I, II, III, and V are less severe or have distinct patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class IV is diffuse, most common, and most severe. COMLEX: patients typically have hematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — immune deposits at different layers of the filtration barrier define the lupus nephritis classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"What do wire loop lesions in lupus nephritis represent?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial deposits thickening the capillary wall","B":"Subepithelial immune complex deposits with basement membrane spikes","C":"Mesangial IgA deposits with mesangial expansion","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of afferent arterioles with luminal thrombi","E":"Linear IgG deposits with crescents in Bowman space"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wire loops are circumferential thickening of the capillary wall caused by subendothelial immune complex deposits, seen on light microscopy in class IV lupus nephritis and confirmed by electron microscopy as dense subendothelial deposits. Subepithelial deposits with spikes describe membranous disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: wire loops are most typical of class IV lupus nephritis and reflect subendothelial deposits. COMLEX: immunofluorescence shows granular IgG with C3 and C1q.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — subendothelial immune deposits injure the endothelial layer of the filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature characterizes class V lupus nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Involvement of fewer than half of glomeruli with segmental necrosis","B":"Mesangial cell proliferation without capillary involvement","C":"Global sclerosis of most glomeruli with fibrosis","D":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening from subepithelial immune complexes","E":"Immune complex deposition seen by immunofluorescence without any light changes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Class V, membranous lupus nephritis, features diffuse thickening of the capillary walls due to subepithelial immune complexes, similar to idiopathic membranous nephropathy, and typically presents with severe proteinuria or nephrotic syndrome. Focal necrosis defines class III, mesangial proliferation class II, and global sclerosis class VI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class V equals membranous pattern and nephrotic syndrome. COMLEX: it can coexist with class III or IV lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — class V lupus nephritis mimics primary membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has cola-colored urine and periorbital edema two weeks after a sore throat. Blood pressure is 145/92 mm Hg, and urinalysis shows red cell casts. Which serologic pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Normal C3 with a positive ANCA test","B":"Low C3 with high antistreptococcal titers","C":"Low C3 with positive anti-GBM antibodies","D":"Normal C3 with an elevated serum IgA level","E":"Elevated C3 with positive cryoglobulin studies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, an immune complex disease that follows pharyngitis after a latent period compatible with antibody production. Complement is consumed, so serum C3 falls, and titers of antistreptococcal antibodies are elevated. ANCA, anti-GBM antibodies, and cryoglobulins point to other glomerular diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low C3 with recent strep infection and a nephritic picture is poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: C3 normalizes within about 8 weeks.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — glomerular capillary wall injury and altered filtration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"Biopsies of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis show early subendothelial deposits that later appear on the subepithelial side of the basement membrane. Which process is proposed to explain this shift?","choices":{"A":"Podocytes phagocytose subendothelial complexes and secrete them subepithelially","B":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV redirect deposits across the membrane","C":"Complexes dissociate and reassemble on the outer epithelial face of the membrane","D":"Neutrophils carry the deposits across the basement membrane into the urinary space","E":"Complement lysis of the basement membrane creates subepithelial spaces for deposits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antigens are initially planted in subendothelial locations, where they elicit an inflammatory response. Later, through poorly understood mechanisms, antigen-antibody complexes dissociate, migrate across the GBM, and re-form on the subepithelial side, producing the characteristic humps. The other options describe mechanisms that do not occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: early subendothelial deposits give way to subepithelial humps that later resolve. COMLEX: the exotoxin SpeB is a candidate nephritogenic antigen localized to humps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — the layered barrier through which immune deposits migrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nephritic Pathophysiology & Poststreptococcal GN","batch":2,"stem":"A pediatrician counsels the parents of a 6-year-old with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which statement about prognosis is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Most children progress to end-stage kidney disease within five years despite therapy","B":"Most children need long-term plasmapheresis to remove circulating antibodies","C":"Most children develop steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome after recovery","D":"Over 95 percent regain renal function with conservative supportive therapy","E":"Most children develop crescentic disease with rapid loss of renal function"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In children, more than 95 percent recover renal function with conservative therapy aimed at maintaining sodium and water balance. A small minority, perhaps less than 1 percent, develop a rapidly progressive form, and the disease is less benign in adults, of whom only about 60 percent of sporadic cases recover promptly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric poststreptococcal GN is usually self-limited. COMLEX: treatment is supportive, with salt restriction and antihypertensives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — maladaptive progressive glomerular injury contrasts with the self-limited course of acute nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old smoker has hemoptysis, hematuria, and a rapidly rising creatinine. Renal biopsy shows crescents with linear IgG along the basement membrane. Which mechanism and treatment are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Anti-alpha-3 collagen IV antibodies treated with plasmapheresis and immunosuppression","B":"PR3-ANCA vasculitis treated with B-cell targeted therapy and no plasmapheresis","C":"Streptococcal antigen complexes treated with antibiotics and salt restriction","D":"Deposition of galactose-deficient IgA1 treated with dietary sodium restriction","E":"Complement-mediated alternative pathway injury treated with plasmapheresis without immunosuppression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pulmonary hemorrhage with renal failure and linear IgG is Goodpasture syndrome, in which anti-GBM antibodies cross-react with the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV in the alveolar basement membrane. Plasmapheresis removes the pathogenic antibodies, and therapy to suppress the underlying immune response is added.","boardPrep":"Step 1: linear IgG with lung and kidney involvement is Goodpasture syndrome. COMLEX: smoking and hydrocarbon exposure can trigger alveolar exposure of the antigen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV also underlies Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic sinusitis, hemoptysis, and a rapidly rising creatinine has a positive c-ANCA. Biopsy shows segmental fibrinoid necrosis with crescents and no immune deposits. Which type of glomerulonephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Anti-GBM crescentic glomerulonephritis with linear IgG","B":"Immune complex crescentic glomerulonephritis with granular IgG","C":"C3 glomerulonephritis with alternative pathway dysregulation","D":"Pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis with PR3-ANCA","E":"Postinfectious glomerulonephritis with subepithelial humps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The absence of immune deposits with segmental necrosis and crescents defines pauci-immune crescentic GN, and PR3-ANCA, previously called c-ANCA, is characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which involves the upper respiratory tract and lungs. Anti-GBM disease shows linear IgG, and immune complex disease shows granular deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pauci-immune RPGN is the most common type of crescentic GN. COMLEX: c-ANCA points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis and p-ANCA to microscopic polyangiitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — classification of glomerular disease by immune pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Rapidly Progressive GN & Crescentic Histogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy from a patient with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis shows crescents containing fibrin strands. Which event initiates crescent formation?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte loss from adaptive hyperfiltration with segmental sclerosis","B":"GBM rupture letting proteins and leukocytes enter Bowman space","C":"Glucose-derived matrix accumulation in the mesangium with nodule formation","D":"Thrombosis of the afferent arteriole with patchy cortical infarction","E":"Cast obstruction of the proximal tubules with backpressure on the tuft"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regardless of the underlying type, electron microscopy may show ruptures in the GBM that allow leukocytes, plasma proteins such as coagulation factors and complement, and inflammatory mediators to reach the urinary space, where they trigger proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and macrophages, forming crescents rich in fibrin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibrin and GBM breaks in Bowman space drive crescent formation. COMLEX: early aggressive therapy can prevent scarring.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — structural failure of the glomerular capillary wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE has hematuria, proteinuria of 2.5 g/day, hypertension, and creatinine of 2.0 mg/dL. Biopsy shows proliferation in most glomeruli, wire loops, and granular immune deposits. Which class and immunofluorescence pattern are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Class II with granular mesangial IgA deposits and no C1q staining","B":"Class III with linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","C":"Class V with fine granular deposits over podocytes and no complement","D":"Class VI with global sclerosis and no immune deposits present","E":"Class IV with granular multi-immunoglobulin and C1q staining"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proliferation in more than 50 percent of glomeruli with wire loops is diffuse, class IV, lupus nephritis, the most common and severe form. Immune complexes are usually reactive for IgG, often accompanied by IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q, in a granular pattern. Linear IgG would indicate anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class IV with wire loops and a full immunofluorescence pattern is the classic severe lupus nephritis. COMLEX: induction immunosuppression is required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — immunofluorescence patterns distinguish glomerular diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with SLE has 7 g/day of proteinuria, edema, and a bland urinary sediment. Biopsy shows diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial deposits. Which class of lupus nephritis is this?","choices":{"A":"Class V membranous lupus nephritis pattern","B":"Class IV diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","C":"Class III focal proliferative lupus nephritis","D":"Class II mesangial expansion lupus nephritis","E":"Class VI advanced sclerosing lupus nephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial immune complexes and severe proteinuria or nephrotic syndrome is class V, membranous, lupus nephritis, resembling idiopathic membranous nephropathy. Classes III and IV are proliferative with nephritic features, class II is mesangial, and class VI has global sclerosis of 90 percent or more of glomeruli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class V lupus nephritis is nephrotic and looks like primary membranous nephropathy. COMLEX: it may coexist with class III or IV lesions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — membranous pattern injury of the podocyte side of the barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Lupus Nephritis Classifications","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with long-standing lupus nephritis has advanced CKD, and a repeat biopsy shows scarring of most glomeruli. Which class best describes this final histologic state?","choices":{"A":"Class I with immune deposits and no structural changes","B":"Class II with mesangial proliferation and deposits","C":"Class VI with 90 percent or more of glomeruli sclerosed","D":"Class III with involvement of fewer than half of glomeruli","E":"Class V with subepithelial deposits and capillary thickening"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Advanced sclerosing lupus nephritis, class VI, is characterized by global sclerosis of 90 percent or more of glomeruli. Lesions can evolve from one class to another over time, and active proliferative lesions in classes III and IV can heal with scarring that culminates in this state.","boardPrep":"Step 1: class VI is end-stage sclerosis and is not responsive to immunosuppression. COMLEX: class transitions determine renal survival, so early treatment of proliferative classes matters.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — progression to global glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":33,"title":"SDL 33 — Asymptomatic Hematuria & Inherited Basement Membrane Structural Defects","questions":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common type of glomerulonephritis worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Membranous nephropathy","B":"Minimal change disease","C":"Lupus nephritis","D":"Anti-GBM disease","E":"IgA nephropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IgA nephropathy, characterized by prominent mesangial IgA deposits and recurrent hematuria, is the most common type of glomerulonephritis worldwide. Membranous nephropathy, minimal change disease, lupus nephritis, and anti-GBM disease are less common causes of glomerular disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is also called Berger disease and is the leading cause of primary glomerulonephritis worldwide. COMLEX: think of young adults with recurrent gross hematuria after infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern characterizes IgA nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA deposits with C3 and properdin","B":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","C":"Coarsely granular IgG and C3 along the capillary walls","D":"Subepithelial IgG deposits separated by spikes","E":"Absent deposits with segmental fibrinoid necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The characteristic immunofluorescence finding in IgA nephropathy is mesangial deposition of IgA, often with C3 and properdin and lesser amounts of IgG or IgM. Early complement components are usually absent. Linear IgG indicates anti-GBM disease, granular capillary wall deposits indicate poststreptococcal GN, and subepithelial deposits indicate membranous nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mesangial IgA on immunofluorescence is characteristic, and diagnostic once secondary causes are excluded; light microscopy can be variable. COMLEX: electron microscopy shows dense deposits mainly in the mesangium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality is thought to initiate IgA nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV","B":"Aberrant O-glycosylation of the IgA1 hinge region","C":"Antibodies against the M-type phospholipase A2 receptor","D":"Mutations in the COL4A5 gene encoding collagen IV","E":"Excess IgG4 deposition along the subepithelial surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A key facet of IgA nephropathy is a hereditary or acquired defect in the formation or attachment of galactose-containing O-linked glycans to the hinge region of IgA1. This aberrantly glycosylated IgA is deposited or elicits IgG autoantibodies, forming mesangial immune complexes. The other options describe anti-GBM disease, membranous nephropathy, Alport syndrome, and the subepithelial IgG4 deposition of membranous disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is a multi-hit disease beginning with galactose-deficient IgA1. COMLEX: mucosal exposure to viruses, bacteria, or food proteins can drive production of abnormal IgA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — poststreptococcal GN is the classic contrast case, with a delayed onset and low C3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is mutated in the X-linked form of Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"COL4A3","B":"COL4A4","C":"COL4A5","D":"NPHS1","E":"TRPC6"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 85 percent of Alport syndrome is X-linked and results from mutations in COL4A5, which encodes the alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen. COL4A3 and COL4A4 are autosomal Alport genes, NPHS1 encodes nephrin, and TRPC6 encodes a podocyte calcium channel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COL4A5 on the X chromosome means males have full disease and female carriers have milder hematuria. COMLEX: autosomal forms involve COL4A3 and COL4A4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which electron microscopic finding is characteristic of Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Discrete electron-dense subepithelial humps","B":"Basket-weave splitting and lamination of the lamina densa","C":"Uniform thinning of the basement membrane to 150 to 225 nm","D":"Subendothelial deposits producing wire loops","E":"Diffuse foot process effacement over a normal basement membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fully developed Alport syndrome shows irregular thickening alternating with attenuation of the GBM and pronounced splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, producing a basket-weave appearance. Subepithelial humps indicate poststreptococcal GN, uniform thinning suggests thin basement membrane nephropathy, wire loops indicate lupus, and effacement over a normal membrane suggests minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: basket-weave GBM equals Alport syndrome. COMLEX: light microscopy is normal early, and antibodies to collagen IV chains fail to stain the membranes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of extrarenal features accompanies Alport syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Conductive hearing loss and retinal detachment","B":"Optic neuritis and vestibular schwannoma","C":"Cataracts and polycystic liver disease","D":"Sensorineural deafness and lens abnormalities","E":"Hearing loss and intracranial aneurysms"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Type IV collagen is crucial to the GBM, the lens of the eye, and the cochlea, so Alport syndrome causes sensorineural deafness and eye disorders such as lens dislocation, anterior lenticonus, posterior cataracts, and corneal dystrophy. Each other pair combines a feature Alport syndrome does not produce, such as retinal detachment, schwannoma, polycystic liver disease, or aneurysms, with conductive loss or an unrelated finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cannot see, cannot pee, cannot hear a bee, meaning eye, kidney, and ear involvement. COMLEX: audiometry may be needed because deafness can be subtle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV is also the target antigen in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts in the urine indicate which source of hematuria?","choices":{"A":"A glomerular source","B":"A lower urinary tract source","C":"A renal papillary source","D":"A ureteral stone source","E":"A bladder mucosal source"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dysmorphic red cells, distorted by passage through the damaged glomerular capillary wall, and red cell casts, molded in the tubules, point to a glomerular source of bleeding. Lower urinary tract bleeding typically produces normal-shaped red cells without casts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: red cell casts are highly specific for glomerular bleeding. COMLEX: a glomerular source prompts evaluation for nephritic disease, whereas non-glomerular bleeding prompts imaging and cystoscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 — dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts are the urinary signature of glomerular inflammation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding in a patient with IgA nephropathy predicts a greater risk of progression?","choices":{"A":"Recurrent gross hematuria after infections","B":"Onset in older childhood with normal blood pressure","C":"Heavy proteinuria with hypertension","D":"Isolated microscopic hematuria with no proteinuria","E":"Preserved renal function at initial presentation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Onset in old age, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and the extent of glomerulosclerosis on biopsy are clues to an increased risk of progression in IgA nephropathy. Recurrent gross hematuria, isolated microscopic hematuria, and childhood onset with normal pressure are associated with a more benign course. Preserved function at presentation is likewise a favorable feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteinuria and hypertension are important bedside predictors of progression. COMLEX: about 15 to 40 percent progress to chronic renal failure over 20 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — progression to glomerulosclerosis follows the same injury pathways described there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits the usual course of thin basement membrane nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Progressive renal failure by age 40 in most affected males","B":"Nephrotic syndrome with hyperlipidemia and lipiduria","C":"Rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis","D":"Familial hematuria with preserved renal function typically","E":"Recurrent gross hematuria with nephrotic-range proteinuria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is associated with mutations in the alpha-3 or alpha-4 chains of type IV collagen, usually heterozygous and autosomal, and typically causes familial hematuria with attenuation of the GBM to 150 to 225 nm and normal or only mildly affected renal function. Progressive failure characterizes classic X-linked Alport syndrome in males.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin basement membrane disease is the benign end of the type IV collagen spectrum. COMLEX: genetic counseling and follow-up are advised because relatives may have classic Alport syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — the collagen IV network is defective in thin basement membrane disease as in Alport syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man notes cola-colored urine within one to two days of each upper respiratory infection, which clears in several days. Serum C3 is normal. Which pathogenic sequence best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Galactose-deficient IgA1 forms complexes with IgG that deposit in the mesangium","B":"Streptococcal antigens form subepithelial complexes that activate the classical pathway","C":"Autoantibodies to the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV bind the glomerular basement membrane","D":"ANCA activate neutrophils that injure the glomerular capillary walls","E":"Anti-nephrin antibodies disrupt the slit diaphragm and efface foot processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recurrent gross hematuria coinciding with mucosal infection in a young adult with normal C3 fits IgA nephropathy. Aberrantly O-glycosylated IgA1 elicits IgG autoantibodies, and the resulting immune complexes deposit in the mesangium, activating mesangial cells. Poststreptococcal GN has a longer latent period and low C3, and the other mechanisms describe anti-GBM, ANCA, and minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hematuria during or within days of infection suggests IgA nephropathy, whereas one to four weeks later suggests poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: C3 is normal in IgA nephropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — comparison with poststreptococcal GN and its delayed onset","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A renal biopsy shows mesangial IgA and C3 deposits, but C1q and C4 are absent. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Immune complexes containing IgG activate complement through the classical pathway","B":"The findings indicate lupus nephritis because complement components are present","C":"The findings indicate anti-GBM disease because C3 is present","D":"The findings favor alternative-pathway complement activation by deposited IgA","E":"The findings indicate poststreptococcal GN because C3 is present"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deposited IgA and IgA-containing complexes may activate the alternative complement pathway, so C3 is present while C1q and C4 are absent, which is typical of IgA nephropathy. Lupus nephritis usually shows C1q as well, anti-GBM disease shows linear IgG, and poststreptococcal GN shows subepithelial humps with granular IgG and C3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C3 present with C1q and C4 absent points to alternative pathway activation, as in IgA nephropathy. COMLEX: lupus shows a full-house immunofluorescence pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — the immunofluorescence pattern of lupus nephritis differs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — IgA Immunopathology & Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with celiac disease develops recurrent episodes of gross hematuria, and biopsy shows mesangial IgA deposits. Which link between her conditions is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Gluten directly binds the glomerular basement membrane and triggers linear IgG staining","B":"Celiac disease deposits IgG4 in subepithelial locations through anti-PLA2R antibodies","C":"Intestinal mucosal defects increase exposure and production of aberrant IgA","D":"Malabsorption depletes vitamin D and leads to mesangial calcification","E":"Villous atrophy raises circulating antistreptolysin O titers with low complement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IgA nephropathy occurs with increased frequency in patients with gluten enteropathy, in whom intestinal mucosal defects are seen, and in liver disease, in which hepatobiliary clearance of IgA complexes is defective. Increased synthesis of abnormal IgA may occur in response to mucosal exposure to environmental agents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease and liver disease are recognized associations of secondary IgA nephropathy. COMLEX: Henoch-Schonlein purpura shows the same mesangial IgA deposits in children.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — immune complex nephritis and its infectious or mucosal triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has hematuria, high-frequency hearing loss, and a maternal uncle on dialysis. Electron microscopy shows basement membrane lamination. Which genetic defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"X-linked mutation in COL4A5 that impairs type IV collagen assembly","B":"Autosomal recessive mutation in NPHS1 that disrupts nephrin","C":"Autosomal dominant mutation in ACTN4 that disrupts podocyte actin","D":"X-linked mutation in the androgen receptor that blocks hormone action","E":"Autosomal dominant mutation in PKD1 that disrupts polycystin function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hematuria with sensorineural hearing loss and an affected maternal uncle, with a lamellated basement membrane, is X-linked Alport syndrome. Mutations in COL4A5 interfere with assembly of the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 collagen IV network, and large deletions are linked to earlier end-stage disease. Males express the full syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked inheritance explains affected males and hematuria in female carriers. COMLEX: most affected males reach ESRD in early to middle adulthood.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — the collagen IV network is the target in anti-GBM disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has persistent microscopic hematuria found on routine urinalysis. Her creatinine is normal, she has no hearing loss, and several relatives have the same finding. Electron microscopy shows diffuse GBM thinning to about 200 nm. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Classic X-linked Alport syndrome","B":"IgA nephropathy with mesangial deposits","C":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy","D":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","E":"Anti-GBM disease with crescents"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Familial hematuria with normal renal function and diffuse attenuation of the GBM to 150 to 225 nm, compared with 300 to 400 nm in healthy adults, is thin basement membrane nephropathy, associated with heterozygous alpha-3 or alpha-4 collagen IV mutations. Alport syndrome shows lamination, deafness, and progressive failure, and IgA nephropathy shows mesangial deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin GBM with familial hematuria and normal function is benign. COMLEX: follow blood pressure and proteinuria over time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — type IV collagen chains in health and disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hereditary Collagen Basement Membrane Mutations","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with suspected X-linked Alport syndrome cannot have electron microscopy performed. Which alternative finding would support the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining of the skin basement membrane","B":"Absent alpha-5 collagen IV staining in skin","C":"Mesangial IgA staining in a renal biopsy","D":"Anti-PLA2R antibodies in the patient serum","E":"Positive PR3-ANCA in the patient serum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immunohistochemistry can help in classic Alport syndrome, since antibodies to the alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 collagen chains fail to stain glomerular and tubular basement membranes in the classic X-linked form, and alpha-5 staining is also absent in skin biopsy specimens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an abnormal skin stain supports the diagnosis, and a normal stain does not exclude it. COMLEX: genetic testing is the definitive confirmation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — the glomerular basement membrane as part of the filtration barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has persistent microscopic hematuria with dysmorphic red cells, proteinuria of 1.5 g/day, hypertension, high-frequency hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus. Which conclusion best summarizes his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Low risk because isolated hematuria carries a benign long-term outlook","B":"Low risk because thin basement membrane disease seldom causes hearing loss","C":"Uncertain risk because a nonglomerular source of bleeding is more likely","D":"High risk because extrarenal Alport features accompany renal markers","E":"High risk because recurrent gross hematuria after infections predicts renal failure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensorineural hearing loss and ocular abnormalities point to Alport syndrome rather than thin basement membrane disease, and proteinuria and hypertension add to the risk. Dysmorphic red cells indicate a glomerular source, and in affected males the course is progressive, with renal failure in early to middle adulthood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrarenal features, proteinuria, hypertension, and reduced GFR raise the risk in hematuria. COMLEX: hearing loss and eye findings separate Alport from IgA nephropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — proteinuria and hypertension drive progression to glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has isolated microscopic hematuria with dysmorphic red cells on repeated testing, with normal blood pressure, no proteinuria, a normal GFR, and no family history. Which approach is most appropriate initially?","choices":{"A":"Immediate high-dose corticosteroids for presumed glomerulonephritis","B":"Periodic reassessment of renal risk markers over time","C":"Urgent plasmapheresis for suspected anti-GBM disease","D":"Cyclophosphamide induction for presumed lupus nephritis","E":"Immediate kidney biopsy to establish a tissue diagnosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A low-risk profile of isolated microscopic hematuria with preserved GFR, normal blood pressure, and no proteinuria supports monitoring for the appearance of proteinuria, hypertension, or falling GFR, which would signal progression. Aggressive immunosuppression or plasmapheresis is not indicated without evidence of active inflammatory disease, and biopsy is reserved for proteinuria, hypertension, or falling GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk stratify hematuria by proteinuria, blood pressure, GFR, and extrarenal features. COMLEX: persistent glomerular hematuria still warrants long-term follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — progressive glomerular injury is what proteinuria and GFR follow over time","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Microscopic Hematuria Prognostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy in a 55-year-old with IgA nephropathy is reviewed for prognostic features. Which finding most strongly indicates a risk of progression?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA deposition with C3","B":"Minor focal mesangial expansion","C":"Electron-dense mesangial deposits","D":"Preserved tubules without interstitial change","E":"Extensive glomerulosclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The extent of glomerulosclerosis on biopsy is a clue to increased risk of progression in IgA nephropathy, along with old age at onset, heavy proteinuria, and hypertension. Mesangial IgA deposition, minor mesangial expansion, and mesangial deposits are diagnostic or active features, whereas established sclerosis is irreversible and is a major indicator of progression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sclerosis and tubulointerstitial scarring indicate irreversible damage. COMLEX: active lesions may respond to treatment, but sclerotic glomeruli do not recover.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — secondary global glomerulosclerosis in progressive injury","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":34,"title":"SDL 34 — Infectious & Hypersensitivity Tubulointerstitial Nephropathies","questions":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common route by which bacteria reach the kidney in acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Hematogenous seeding from infective endocarditis","B":"Lymphatic spread from the intestinal tract","C":"Direct extension from the peritoneal cavity","D":"Retrograde spread from the seminal vesicles","E":"Ascent from the lower urinary tract"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending infection from the lower urinary tract is the most common cause of pyelonephritis, requiring colonization of the urethra, entry into the bladder, and movement up the ureter, usually in the presence of reflux or obstruction. The hematogenous route is less common and occurs with distant foci such as endocarditis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: most pyelonephritis is ascending from the bladder, so predisposing factors are reflux, obstruction, instrumentation, and pregnancy. COMLEX: infections are more frequent in females except in infancy and in older men with prostatic obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which organism causes the majority of urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D":"Candida albicans","E":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"More than 85 percent of urinary tract infections are caused by enteric gram-negative bacilli, and Escherichia coli is by far the most common, followed by Proteus, Klebsiella, and Enterobacter. Staphylococci and fungi are more often involved in hematogenous or immunosuppressed settings, and mycobacteria cause granulomatous disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli uses P-fimbriae to adhere to urothelial cells. COMLEX: Proteus produces urease and is associated with struvite stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic finding is the hallmark of acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomatous interstitial inflammation with central caseation","B":"Interstitial eosinophils and mast cells with lymphocytic tubulitis","C":"Coagulative necrosis of the papillary tips with preserved tubule outlines","D":"Patchy suppurative interstitial inflammation with tubular neutrophils","E":"Coarse polar corticomedullary scars with thyroidization of tubules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hallmarks of acute pyelonephritis are patchy interstitial suppurative inflammation, intratubular aggregates of neutrophils, neutrophilic tubulitis, and tubular injury, sometimes forming abscesses or wedge-shaped areas. Glomeruli are relatively resistant. Granulomas suggest fungal or mycobacterial disease, eosinophils suggest hypersensitivity nephritis, and scars with thyroidization indicate chronic pyelonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neutrophil casts and cortical microabscesses point to bacterial pyelonephritis. COMLEX: white blood cell casts indicate renal, not bladder, involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — bladder dysfunction and incomplete emptying predispose to ascending infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"Which gross finding characterizes chronic pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse symmetric fine granularity of the cortical surface","B":"Wedge-shaped pale infarcts with hemorrhagic borders","C":"Coarse corticomedullary scars overlying blunted calyces","D":"Bilaterally enlarged kidneys replaced by multiple cysts","E":"Small smooth kidneys with pinpoint surface hemorrhages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronic pyelonephritis produces coarse, discrete corticomedullary scars overlying dilated, blunted, or deformed calyces with flattening of the papillae, and if bilateral, the involvement is asymmetric. Most scars are in the upper and lower poles. Chronic glomerulonephritis instead scars both kidneys diffusely and symmetrically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvocalyceal damage with asymmetric scarring separates chronic pyelonephritis from glomerular disease. COMLEX: among the classic chronic tubulointerstitial diseases, chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy involve the calyces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"What is meant by thyroidization in chronic pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Follicular hyperplasia of lymphoid tissue within the interstitium","B":"Hyaline thickening of afferent arterioles with narrowed lumens","C":"Nodules of foamy macrophages mixed with plasma cells and giant cells","D":"Dilated atrophic tubules with colloid-like proteinaceous casts","E":"Lipid-laden proximal tubular cells with cytoplasmic vacuoles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thyroidization describes dilated tubules with flattened epithelium filled with proteinaceous casts that resemble thyroid colloid, a typical microscopic feature of chronic pyelonephritis. The other options describe lymphoid follicles, hyaline arteriolosclerosis, xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and lipoid change in minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thyroidization of tubules plus corticomedullary scars is chronic pyelonephritis. COMLEX: glomeruli are relatively spared until late, when periglomerular fibrosis and secondary FSGS appear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":1,"stem":"Why does scarring in chronic pyelonephritis favor the upper and lower renal regions?","choices":{"A":"Polar glomeruli carry a higher filtration fraction than midzone glomeruli","B":"Flattened or concave papillae there permit retrograde urine entry","C":"Polar arterioles lack collateral flow and infarct during infection","D":"Polar calyces are longer and hold more residual urine after voiding","E":"Polar cortex has a lower density of tubules and heals with scarring"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vesicoureteral reflux can propel infected urine deep into the parenchyma through open ducts at the papillary tips, called intrarenal reflux, and this is most common at the upper and lower poles where papillae tend to be flattened or concave rather than convex and pointed as in the midzone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary shape determines susceptibility to intrarenal reflux and hence polar scarring. COMLEX: voiding cystourethrography demonstrates reflux in a substantial minority of children with UTI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — loss of nephron mass drives adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"Which group of conditions predisposes to renal papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, obesity, and smoking","B":"Nephrotic syndrome, lupus, amyloidosis, and HIV infection","C":"Diabetes, sickle cell disease, obstruction, and analgesic abuse","D":"Polycystic kidney disease, tuberous sclerosis, and nephronophthisis","E":"Gout, hypercalcemia, hyperoxaluria, and cystinuria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Papillary necrosis is seen mainly in diabetics, patients with sickle cell disease or trait, those with urinary tract obstruction, and in analgesic nephropathy. In each of these settings ischemia from compression or obstruction of small medullary vessels is the final common pathway. The other groups describe risk factors for vascular, glomerular, cystic, and stone diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic causes of papillary necrosis are diabetes, sickle cell disease, analgesics, and obstruction. COMLEX: necrotic papillae may be passed and cause colic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"What is the pathogenic mechanism of papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Ischemia from small medullary vessel obstruction","B":"Direct bacterial invasion of the papillary tips","C":"Immune complex deposition in papillary capillaries","D":"Toxin-induced necrosis of the proximal tubule epithelium","E":"Thrombosis of the main renal artery with cortical infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Papillary necrosis is caused by ischemia resulting from compression or obstruction of small blood vessels in the medulla. Compression may result from interstitial edema, as in inflammation and obstruction, and vascular compromise from microvascular disease, as in diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the papillae are the most poorly perfused region of the kidney. COMLEX: necrosis is usually bilateral but may be unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":1,"stem":"Which microscopic finding characterizes renal papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells","B":"Ischemic coagulative necrosis with preserved outlines","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles with onion-skin thickening","D":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses","E":"Eosinophilic infiltrate with lymphocytic tubulitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"On microscopic examination, necrotic papillary tissue shows ischemic coagulative necrosis with preservation of the outlines of tubules, and the leukocytic response is limited to the junctions between preserved and destroyed tissue. The other options describe tuberculosis, malignant nephrosclerosis, acute pyelonephritis, and drug-induced interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: coagulative necrosis is the pattern of ischemic infarcts in solid organs. COMLEX: gross examination shows gray-white to yellow necrosis of the distal two-thirds of the pyramids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin buffers explain the vulnerability of the papillae","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has her second febrile urinary tract infection. A voiding cystourethrogram shows dye refluxing into both ureters and filling the calyces. Which anatomic defect most likely explains her repeated infections?","choices":{"A":"Absent ureteral peristalsis from a neurogenic bladder disorder","B":"Urethral stricture causing high-pressure bladder outflow obstruction","C":"Short intravesical ureter that is not compressed on micturition","D":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction with unilateral pelvic dilation","E":"A vesicovaginal fistula draining bladder urine into the vagina"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal ureteral insertion into the bladder acts as a one-way valve that is compressed when intravesical pressure rises during micturition. Reflux most often results from congenital absence or shortening of the intravesical portion of the ureter, allowing bladder urine to flow back into the ureters and carry bacteria to the renal pelvis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vesicoureteral reflux is the leading cause of pyelonephritis in children and it affects 1 to 2 percent of normal children. COMLEX: reflux is demonstrated by voiding cystourethrography.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — impaired emptying and reflux predispose to infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has fever, dysuria, and costovertebral angle tenderness. Which urinary finding best indicates that the infection involves the kidney rather than the bladder?","choices":{"A":"White blood cell casts","B":"Pyuria on microscopy","C":"Bacteriuria on culture","D":"A positive nitrite dipstick","E":"Microscopic hematuria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyuria does not differentiate upper from lower urinary tract infection, but leukocyte casts, typically rich in neutrophils, form only in the tubules and therefore indicate renal involvement. Bacteriuria, nitrite positivity, and hematuria can all occur with cystitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: casts form only in renal tubules, so any cast localizes disease to the kidney. COMLEX: white blood cell casts point to pyelonephritis or interstitial nephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — lower tract disease versus upper tract disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Acute Suppurative Pyelonephritis Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing diabetes develops recurrent pyelonephritis. Which factor most directly promotes ascent of bacteria in his case?","choices":{"A":"Increased antibacterial activity of prostatic fluid","B":"Neurogenic bladder with residual urine after voiding","C":"A longer urethra than in females that traps bacteria","D":"Hyperfiltration that flushes bacteria from the tubules","E":"Alkaline urine from glucosuria that inhibits growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes predisposes to infection through impaired host defenses, neurogenic bladder dysfunction that causes incomplete emptying and residual urine, and more frequent instrumentation. Stasis allows bacteria introduced into the bladder to multiply unhindered and ascend, particularly if reflux or obstruction is present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: residual urine favors bacterial growth in any obstruction or bladder atony. COMLEX: diabetic patients are also prone to papillary necrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — neurogenic bladder and incomplete emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with reflux and recurrent childhood urinary infections now has hypertension, asymmetrically small kidneys, and new proteinuria of 3 g/day despite no active infection. Which mechanism best explains the proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing suppurative infection that is destroying glomeruli","B":"Anti-GBM antibodies produced after scarring exposes new antigens","C":"Secondary FSGS from adaptive hyperfiltration after nephron loss","D":"Subepithelial immune complexes formed against bacterial antigens","E":"Mesangial IgA deposition triggered by recurrent mucosal infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Some individuals with pyelonephritic scars develop secondary FSGS with significant proteinuria, even in the nephrotic range, usually several years after scarring and often without ongoing infection or persistent reflux. The glomerulosclerosis reflects adaptive glomerular alterations secondary to loss of renal mass, and the onset of proteinuria is a poor prognostic sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reflux nephropathy is a classic cause of adaptive FSGS. COMLEX: proteinuria in a patient with scarred kidneys signals progression toward ESRD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — adaptive hyperfiltration and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist is told that a kidney specimen shows tubulointerstitial scarring with damage to the calyces and pelvis. Which pair of diagnoses best fits this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and minimal change disease","B":"Acute tubular injury and acute interstitial nephritis","C":"IgA nephropathy and membranous nephropathy","D":"Hypertensive nephrosclerosis and Alport syndrome","E":"Chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Although several diseases produce chronic tubulointerstitial alterations, chronic pyelonephritis and analgesic nephropathy are the classic pair that affect the calyces, making pelvocalyceal damage an important diagnostic clue. The other pairs include diseases that do not involve the calyces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calyceal involvement is the discriminating feature of these two diseases. COMLEX: analgesic nephropathy also causes papillary necrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — chronic tubulointerstitial damage accompanies progressive injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Chronic Pyelonephritis & Scarring Loops","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with reflux nephropathy has polyuria, nocturia, and hypertension. Which additional finding is the poorest prognostic sign for progression to end-stage disease?","choices":{"A":"Persistent leukocyturia","B":"Occasional flank discomfort","C":"A single small polar scar in one kidney","D":"New onset of proteinuria","E":"Bacteriuria on culture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pyelonephritis, proteinuria is usually mild, but in some patients secondary FSGS develops, and the onset of proteinuria is a poor prognostic sign since it indicates an increased likelihood of progression to ESRD. Leukocyturia, bacteriuria, flank discomfort, and a single scar are manifestations or markers of the disease but do not predict progression in the same way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteinuria signals secondary FSGS in scarred kidneys. COMLEX: loss of tubular concentrating ability produces polyuria and nocturia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — podocyte injury and secondary glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old diabetic woman with acute pyelonephritis develops gross hematuria, flank colic, and a sudden rise in creatinine. She passes gray-white tissue fragments in her urine. Which complication has occurred?","choices":{"A":"Pyonephrosis of the collecting system","B":"Renal papillary necrosis","C":"Perinephric abscess in the fat","D":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","E":"Renal vein thrombosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sloughed necrotic papillae can be excreted and cause gross hematuria or renal colic if they obstruct the ureter. Papillary necrosis is a complication of acute pyelonephritis in diabetics, and its superimposition may lead to acute renal failure. Pyonephrosis and perinephric abscess are suppurative complications, and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is a rare chronic form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sloughed papillae with hematuria and colic in a diabetic patient means papillary necrosis. COMLEX: it is usually bilateral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — medullary ischemia and susceptibility to injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with sickle cell trait has painless gross hematuria. Which explanation best accounts for the vulnerability of the renal medulla?","choices":{"A":"High oxygen tension drives free radical injury of medullary capillaries","B":"Rapid blood flow through the vasa recta damages sickled cells","C":"Alkaline medullary pH prolongs the survival of sickled red cells","D":"Hypoxia and hypertonicity promote sickling and vessel occlusion","E":"Dilute interstitial fluid causes red cell lysis within the vasa recta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medulla has low oxygen tension and a hypertonic environment, conditions that promote red cell sickling even in sickle cell trait, and sickled cells obstruct the slow-flowing small vessels of the medulla. This causes ischemia and papillary necrosis, which may present with hematuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the medulla is uniquely hypoxic and hypertonic, so sickle cell trait can cause hematuria and papillary necrosis. COMLEX: unlike other tissues, sickling in the medulla occurs even in the trait.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — low medullary oxygen tension and susceptibility to ischemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Papillary Necrosis & Vascular Vulnerability","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with years of daily use of an aspirin-containing analgesic has sterile pyuria and radiographs suggesting papillary necrosis. Which mechanism best links her drug exposure to the papillary injury?","choices":{"A":"Loss of vasodilatory prostaglandins worsens medullary ischemia","B":"Direct immune complex deposition in the papillary capillaries","C":"Gram-negative infection of the renal papillae from the bladder","D":"Uric acid crystal obstruction of the collecting ducts","E":"Osmotic swelling of proximal tubular cells that compresses vessels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Many nonsteroidal analgesics inhibit cyclooxygenase-dependent prostaglandin synthesis, which removes vasodilatory prostaglandins that protect medullary blood flow, and chronic analgesic use damages the papillae by ischemia. Papillary necrosis results from ischemia due to compression or obstruction of small medullary vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: analgesic nephropathy causes chronic interstitial nephritis and papillary necrosis, and it affects the calyces. COMLEX: sterile pyuria and hematuria are clues in chronic analgesic users.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — prostaglandins protect medullary flow and NSAIDs remove this buffer","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":35,"title":"SDL 35 — Hypersensitivity Nephritis & Acute Tubular Injury","questions":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which group of drugs most commonly causes acute interstitial nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Aminoglycosides, radiographic contrast, cisplatin, and amphotericin B","B":"Statins, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, and nitrates","C":"Heparin, warfarin, aspirin, and clopidogrel","D":"Synthetic penicillins, rifampin, thiazides, and NSAIDs","E":"Levothyroxine, insulin, metformin, and glipizide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute interstitial nephritis most frequently occurs with sulfonamides, synthetic penicillins such as methicillin and ampicillin, rifampin, thiazide diuretics, NSAIDs, and drugs such as allopurinol, cimetidine, proton pump inhibitors, and immune checkpoint inhibitors. Aminoglycosides, contrast agents, and cisplatin typically cause acute tubular injury instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: antibiotics, diuretics, and NSAIDs are the classic culprits, and they may present with rash, fever, and eosinophilia, although the full triad is seen in a minority. COMLEX: withdrawal of the drug is followed by recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"When does antibiotic-induced acute interstitial nephritis typically begin after drug exposure?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of the first dose","B":"Six to twelve hours after exposure","C":"Two to 40 days after exposure","D":"Two to three weeks after stopping the drug","E":"Two to three years after exposure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begins 2 to 40 days after drug exposure, a latent period consistent with a hypersensitivity reaction. Onset is not dose-related, and recurrence follows re-exposure to the same or a chemically related drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the latent period, rash, eosinophilia, and lack of dose relationship all point to hypersensitivity. COMLEX: the triad of fever, rash, and eosinophilia is present in a minority of patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most likely immunologic mechanism of drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis?","choices":{"A":"Drug binding modifies tubular antigens and provokes hypersensitivity","B":"Osmotic injury from drug crystals precipitating in the tubular lumen","C":"Complement lysis triggered by anti-GBM autoantibodies","D":"Bacterial invasion of the renal interstitium from the bladder","E":"Ischemia from sustained constriction of the afferent arteriole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The most likely sequence is that drugs bind to a plasma membrane or extracellular component of tubular cells and create modified self antigens that evoke an immune response, through IgE-mediated or T cell-mediated hypersensitivity directed at the tubular cells or their basement membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hapten mechanism means the drug alters self proteins so that the immune system attacks them. COMLEX: the response can be type I or type IV hypersensitivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — suppurative pyelonephritis is the infectious contrast to this hypersensitivity interstitial disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron segments are especially vulnerable to acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular capillary loops and Bowman capsule epithelium","B":"Proximal straight tubule and thick ascending limb","C":"Cortical collecting duct and the papillary tip urothelium","D":"Early distal convoluted tubule and macula densa cells","E":"Thin descending limb and the ascending vasa recta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The straight portion of the proximal tubule and the thick ascending limb in the outer medulla are especially vulnerable, because they have high metabolic and oxygen demands from active transport and receive limited oxygen delivery. Glomeruli and collecting ducts are relatively resistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high transport activity and low medullary oxygen tension make these segments the most susceptible. COMLEX: injury is patchy with skip areas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which morphologic pattern characterizes ischemic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Extensive necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule","B":"Uniform necrosis of the glomerular capillary loops","C":"Wedge-shaped cortical infarcts with preserved tubule outlines","D":"Patchy necrosis of short tubular segments with skip areas","E":"Diffuse tubular atrophy with interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ischemic ATI, tubular necrosis is patchy and affects relatively short lengths of tubule, with large skip areas, most often in the proximal straight segments and thick ascending limbs. Toxic ATI instead produces extensive necrosis along the proximal convoluted tubule with many toxins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic ATI is patchy and toxic ATI is diffuse in the PCT. COMLEX: toxic ATI typically spares the tubular basement membrane, whereas ischemic ATI can show focal basement membrane disruption, and both regenerate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is a well-recognized cause of nephrotoxic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Gentamicin","B":"Amlodipine","C":"Metoprolol","D":"Levothyroxine","E":"Ondansetron"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin, radiographic contrast agents, heavy metals, and organic solvents are classic causes of nephrotoxic ATI, in which proximal tubular cells are injured because they reabsorb and concentrate the toxin. The other agents are not recognized tubular toxins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aminoglycoside toxicity is dose dependent and shows muddy brown casts. COMLEX: monitor peak and trough levels and creatinine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in the thick ascending limb explains its susceptibility to ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which are the three clinical phases of classic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Prerenal, intrinsic, and postrenal stages","B":"Oliguric, anuric, and polyuric stages","C":"Acute, subacute, and chronic stages","D":"Injury, fibrosis, and scarring stages","E":"Initiation, maintenance, and recovery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The classic course of ATI is divided into an initiation phase of about 36 hours dominated by the inciting event, a maintenance phase with sustained oliguria and uremic complications, and a recovery phase heralded by a steady rise in urine volume. The other sets describe etiologic categories or unrelated staging systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the phases and the hazards of each. COMLEX: most survivors of the precipitating event recover substantial function, but some are left with chronic kidney disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"What urine output characterizes the maintenance phase of classic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Polyuria of 2 to 3 L per day","B":"Normal output of 1 to 1.5 L per day","C":"Oliguria of 40 to 400 mL per day","D":"Anuria of less than 10 mL per day","E":"Nocturia with 800 to 1000 mL per day"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The maintenance phase is characterized by sustained oliguria of 40 to 400 mL per day with salt and water overload, rising BUN, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and other manifestations of uremia. The recovery phase is marked by polyuria that can reach up to 3 L per day.","boardPrep":"Step 1: oliguria in the maintenance phase and polyuria in recovery. COMLEX: hyperkalemia is the most immediately dangerous complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism helps maintain a depressed GFR during the maintenance phase of acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Restored polarity of tubular cells increases proximal reabsorption","B":"Sloughed cells obstruct tubules and raise intratubular pressure","C":"Dilation of afferent arterioles reduces glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Increased plasma oncotic pressure raises the filtration coefficient","E":"Loss of ureteral peristalsis distends the renal pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Injured cells detach from their basement membranes and cause luminal obstruction, raising intratubular pressure and decreasing GFR, and filtrate can also leak back through damaged tubular epithelium into the interstitium, causing edema and ischemic damage. Tubuloglomerular feedback from increased distal sodium delivery adds afferent constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: casts, backleak, and vasoconstriction combine to sustain oliguria. COMLEX: muddy brown granular casts are the urinary hallmark.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback links tubular injury to the depressed GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man treated with ampicillin ten days ago develops fever, a rash, and a rising creatinine. Urinalysis shows eosinophiluria and mild proteinuria. Which biopsy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses and normal glomeruli","B":"Interstitial edema with mononuclear and eosinophil infiltrate","C":"Diffuse foot process effacement with completely normal interstitium","D":"Subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes on silver stain","E":"Granular capillary wall IgG and C3 with glomerular hypercellularity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fever, rash, and eosinophiluria beginning days after starting a synthetic penicillin indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. The interstitium shows pronounced edema and infiltration by lymphocytes and macrophages, with eosinophils, and lymphocytic infiltration of tubules called tubulitis. Glomeruli are usually normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils and tubulitis on biopsy point to a hypersensitivity interstitial nephritis. COMLEX: the treatment is to stop the drug, with steroids sometimes added.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — contrast with the neutrophilic suppurative inflammation of pyelonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has rising creatinine and pyuria. Which finding favors drug-induced interstitial nephritis over acute pyelonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophiluria with rash and sterile culture","B":"Neutrophil casts with a positive urine culture","C":"Costovertebral angle tenderness with bacteriuria","D":"Nitrite-positive urine with pus casts","E":"Fever with pyuria and gram-negative bacteremia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis is a hypersensitivity reaction with T cells and eosinophils, rash, fever, and eosinophiluria, and the urine culture is sterile because no infection is present. Pyelonephritis is a suppurative bacterial infection with neutrophil casts, bacteriuria, and a positive urine culture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sterile pyuria with eosinophils suggests interstitial nephritis. COMLEX: a positive culture with neutrophil casts and flank pain suggests pyelonephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — features of bacterial pyelonephritis for comparison","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Drug-Induced AIN Immunopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man taking an NSAID develops edema and nephrotic-range proteinuria, with foot process effacement on electron microscopy and normal glomeruli on light microscopy. Which glomerular lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"IgA nephropathy","B":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","C":"Minimal change disease","D":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","E":"Anti-GBM disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs can cause minimal change disease, with or without acute interstitial nephritis, a podocyte injury that appears as normal glomeruli on light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement, presenting as nephrotic syndrome. The other lesions do not have this histologic picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs cause AKI from prostaglandin loss, interstitial nephritis, minimal change disease, and rarely membranous nephropathy. COMLEX: stopping the NSAID is central to treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — minimal change disease is a primary nephrotic podocytopathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has prolonged hypotension during cardiac surgery. Which early ischemic change in tubular cells contributes to his falling GFR?","choices":{"A":"Loss of polarity with pump redistribution raising distal Na+ delivery","B":"Increased proximal Na+ reabsorption that lowers macula densa delivery","C":"Afferent arteriolar dilation from a surge of prostacyclin release","D":"Rapid hyperfiltration from a rising ultrafiltration coefficient","E":"Myogenic afferent dilation from lowered arterial pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"One early reversible result of ischemia is loss of cell polarity, with redistribution of Na+/K+-ATPase from the basolateral to the luminal surface, causing abnormal ion transport and increased sodium delivery to the distal tubule, which incites afferent vasoconstriction through tubuloglomerular feedback and lowers GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemia disrupts the tubular cytoskeleton and pump polarity. COMLEX: this early phase is reversible if perfusion is restored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — active transport, oxygen consumption, and ischemic susceptibility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man develops acute kidney injury after ingesting antifreeze. Which biopsy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Large acidophilic inclusions in proximal tubular cells that later calcify","B":"Neutrophil casts with cortical microabscesses and interstitial edema","C":"Wedge-shaped cortical infarcts with preservation of tubular outlines","D":"Eosinophils and lymphocytes infiltrating the interstitium with tubulitis","E":"Vacuolar degeneration of proximal tubules with oxalate crystals in lumens"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ethylene glycol poisoning produces marked ballooning and hydropic or vacuolar degeneration of proximal convoluted tubules, and calcium oxalate crystals are often found in the tubular lumens, an example of toxic acute tubular injury. Acidophilic inclusions occur with mercuric chloride, and the other options describe pyelonephritis, infarction, and interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ethylene glycol produces calcium oxalate crystals and a high anion gap, high osmolar gap metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: fomepizole is the antidote.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — proximal tubule brush border and high mitochondrial density","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Acute Tubular Injury Pathogenesis & Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops acute kidney injury after aminoglycoside therapy. Which set of proximal tubule properties best explains its vulnerability to this drug?","choices":{"A":"Small surface area with passive transport and rapid toxin excretion","B":"Absent transport proteins with low metabolism and high oxygen supply","C":"Thick basement membrane with low oxygen consumption and slow flow","D":"Large reabsorptive surface with active transport concentrating toxins","E":"Impermeability to solutes with high tonicity and abundant antioxidants"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Several factors predispose proximal tubular cells to toxic injury, including an increased surface area for tubular reabsorption, active transport systems for ions and organic acids, and the ability to absorb and concentrate toxins. High metabolic and oxygen demand also make them sensitive to ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal tubule is the primary target of nephrotoxins because it reabsorbs and concentrates them. COMLEX: hydration and dose adjustment reduce aminoglycoside toxicity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — high oxygen consumption in transporting segments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"On day 12 of acute tubular injury, a patient has urine output of 3 L per day, a falling BUN, serum Na+ of 128 mEq/L, and K+ of 3.0 mEq/L. Which hazard is most consistent with this phase?","choices":{"A":"Volume overload and hyperkalemia from persistent oliguria","B":"Volume depletion with hypokalemia from urinary loss of solutes and water","C":"Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis from persistently impaired acid excretion","D":"Worsening uremic pericarditis from a rapidly rising BUN and creatinine","E":"Severe hypertension from excessive renin release by ischemic cortex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The recovery phase brings a steady increase in urine volume, but the tubules are still damaged, so large amounts of water, sodium, and potassium are lost in the flood of urine, causing volume depletion, hyponatremia, and hypokalemia, and patients have a peculiar vulnerability to infection. Oliguria, hyperkalemia, and rising BUN belong to the maintenance phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyuria of recovery demands careful replacement of fluid and electrolytes. COMLEX: watch for infection during this phase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — tubular function and flow-dependent feedback recover slowly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in the maintenance phase of acute tubular injury has urine output of 250 mL per day. Which laboratory pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Falling BUN with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","B":"Falling creatinine with hypernatremia and dilute urine","C":"Rising BUN with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis","D":"Rising BUN with hypokalemia and respiratory alkalosis","E":"Stable creatinine with hyperkalemia and normal bicarbonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The maintenance phase is characterized by sustained oliguria with salt and water overload, rising BUN concentrations, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and other manifestations of uremia. Hypokalemia and dilute urine characterize the recovery phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced GFR retains potassium, acid, and nitrogenous waste. COMLEX: hyperkalemia may require urgent treatment or dialysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reduced filtration retains solutes and volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — ATI Maintenance Mechanics & Recovery Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"In a patient with ischemic acute tubular injury, which mechanism links tubular damage to a depressed GFR through tubuloglomerular feedback?","choices":{"A":"Injured tubules reabsorb more NaCl and low distal delivery dilates the afferent arteriole","B":"Injured tubules secrete renin directly and angiotensin II dilates the efferent arteriole","C":"Injured tubules release nitric oxide and vasodilation lowers glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Poor NaCl reabsorption raises distal delivery and constricts the afferent arteriole","E":"Injured tubules increase Bowman space pressure and this reduces the filtration coefficient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Defective sodium reabsorption in injured tubules increases the concentration of sodium reaching the macula densa, which causes constriction of the afferent arteriole through tubuloglomerular feedback and decreases GFR. This response lowers GFR, and cast obstruction and backleak add to the reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubuloglomerular feedback is a protective but maladaptive loop in ATI. COMLEX: loop diuretics do not reverse established ATI, but supportive care allows recovery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — tubuloglomerular feedback from macula densa sensing to afferent constriction","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":36,"title":"SDL 36 — Renovascular Pathology, Malignant Hypertension, Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Chronic Kidney Disease","questions":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"Which arteriolar lesion characterizes benign nephrosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","B":"Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles","C":"Cholesterol atheroemboli in arcuate arteries","D":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","E":"Transmural vasculitis of interlobular arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benign nephrosclerosis is characterized by thickening and hyalinization of arteriolar walls, hyaline arteriolosclerosis, with narrowed lumens, plus fibroelastic hyperplasia of larger arteries. The resulting ischemia causes glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial scarring. Onion-skin thickening and fibrinoid necrosis characterize malignant hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the lesion of benign hypertension and diabetes. COMLEX: uncomplicated nephrosclerosis is usually slowly progressive, and renal failure is more likely in African American patients and with severe hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of the kidneys in benign nephrosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Coarse polar scars overlying blunted dilated calyces","B":"Normal to moderately small kidneys with fine leathery cortex","C":"Wedge-shaped pale infarcts with hyperemic borders","D":"Markedly enlarged kidneys filled with multiple cysts","E":"Symmetrically small pale kidneys with smooth cortical surfaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In benign nephrosclerosis the kidneys are normal or moderately reduced in size, weighing 110 to 130 g, and the cortical surfaces have a fine, even granularity resembling grain leather from microscopic subcapsular scars. Coarse polar scars suggest chronic pyelonephritis, and wedge-shaped infarcts suggest embolic occlusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fine granular leathery cortex with hyaline arteriolosclerosis identifies benign nephrosclerosis. COMLEX: the loss of mass is mainly from cortical scarring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings define the renal lesion of malignant hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Onion-skin arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis","B":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis with subcapsular glomerular scars","C":"Atheromatous plaque at the origin of the renal artery","D":"Medial fibrous thickening with a string-of-beads appearance","E":"Glomerular crescents with linear IgG along the basement membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Malignant hypertension shows proliferation of intimal cells producing an onion-skin appearance, hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, along with fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and glomeruli, sometimes with intraglomerular microthrombi. It is defined by severe elevations of pressure, renal failure, and retinal hemorrhages with or without papilledema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: onion skin equals hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, and it is seen in malignant hypertension. COMLEX: the renal lesions overlap with thrombotic microangiopathy but the molecular defects differ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and renin regulate arteriolar tone and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"What is the usual trigger of typical, diarrhea-positive hemolytic uremic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies that inhibit ADAMTS13","B":"Mutation of complement factor H","C":"Antiphospholipid antibodies","D":"Heparin-dependent antibodies to platelet factor 4","E":"Shiga-like toxin from E. coli O157:H7"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Typical HUS follows intestinal infection with Shiga-like toxin-producing strains of E. coli, most commonly O157:H7, and less commonly Shigella dysenteriae. The toxin causes endothelial injury, platelet activation, and microvascular thrombosis. ADAMTS13 inhibitors cause TTP, and complement mutations and antiphospholipid antibodies are associated with atypical HUS or other secondary microangiopathies. Heparin-dependent antibodies cause platelet consumption and thrombosis, not a diarrhea-associated microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure in a child is typical HUS. COMLEX: antibiotics are avoided in suspected E. coli O157:H7 infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality underlies thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","choices":{"A":"Deficient ADAMTS13 protease activity","B":"Deficient complement regulatory factor H","C":"Deficient endogenous antithrombin III","D":"Deficient platelet glycoprotein Ib receptors","E":"Deficient plasma coagulation factor VIII"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TTP is characterized by inherited or acquired deficiency of ADAMTS13, the plasma metalloprotease that cleaves large multimers of von Willebrand factor. Ultralarge multimers accumulate and activate platelets spontaneously, forming microthrombi. Most cases are due to inhibitory autoantibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TTP means ADAMTS13 deficiency and platelet-rich microthrombi. COMLEX: plasma exchange removes autoantibodies and supplies functional enzyme.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination is characteristic of hemolytic uremic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Warm autoimmune hemolysis, leukocytosis, and acute hepatic failure","B":"Hemolytic anemia with target cells, neutropenia, and splenomegaly","C":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and kidney injury","D":"Iron deficiency anemia, thrombocytosis, and nephrotic syndrome with edema","E":"Macrocytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and a peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thrombotic microangiopathy leads to deposition of thrombi in the microvasculature with red cell hemolysis, tissue ischemia and organ dysfunction, and a consumptive thrombocytopenia. In HUS the kidney is the dominant organ affected, whereas in TTP central nervous system involvement is dominant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistocytes on smear indicate microangiopathic hemolysis. COMLEX: HUS causes renal failure and TTP causes neurologic symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"How is chronic kidney disease defined?","choices":{"A":"GFR below 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 1 month or transient proteinuria","B":"GFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for 3 months or persistent albuminuria","C":"GFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 6 months with a rising creatinine","D":"Serum creatinine above 1.0 mg/dL for at least 6 weeks with hematuria","E":"BUN above 20 mg/dL with hypertension lasting at least 3 months"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease is defined by a diminished GFR persistently less than 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for at least 3 months and, or, persistent albuminuria. It is the end result of a variety of renal diseases, most commonly diabetic and vascular kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duration of at least 3 months distinguishes CKD from acute kidney injury. COMLEX: CKD affects roughly 1 in 7 US adults by CDC estimates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"Which are the most common underlying causes of chronic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Polycystic kidney disease and Alport syndrome","B":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and pyelonephritis","C":"Obstructive uropathy and nephrolithiasis","D":"Diabetic and vascular kidney disease","E":"Lupus nephritis and IgA nephropathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease is the end result of a variety of renal diseases, most commonly diabetic kidney disease and vascular kidney disease, and it is particularly common in people over age 60. Inherited, infectious, obstructive, and autoimmune diseases account for a smaller share.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes and hypertension lead the list of causes of CKD and ESKD. COMLEX: screen with urine albumin and eGFR in these patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins' classic description of terminal uremia, what GFR defines end-stage kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Less than 50 percent of normal","B":"Less than 35 percent of normal","C":"Less than 5 percent of normal","D":"Less than 25 percent of normal","E":"Less than 60 percent of normal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In end-stage kidney disease the GFR is less than 5 percent of normal, the terminal stage of uremia, at which dialysis or transplantation is required to sustain life. The other thresholds correspond to earlier stages of chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins gives terminal ESKD as a GFR below 5 percent of normal, whereas KDIGO stage 5 is below 15 mL/min/1.73 m2 or dialysis. COMLEX: manifestations include anemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, and acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — hyperfiltration and podocyte injury drive progressive glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old African American man has blood pressure of 230/130 mm Hg, headache, retinal hemorrhages, papilledema, and a creatinine of 3.1 mg/dL. Which renal biopsy findings are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Onion-skin arterial thickening with fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles","B":"Hyaline thickening of arterioles with focal subcapsular glomerular scars","C":"Wedge-shaped coagulative infarcts with preserved tubule outlines","D":"Cholesterol clefts occluding arcuate arteries with giant cells","E":"Diffuse mesangial matrix expansion with nodular glomerulosclerosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe pressure elevations with renal failure and retinal hemorrhages with papilledema define malignant hypertension. Interlobular arteries show intimal proliferation producing an onion-skin appearance, hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, and arterioles and glomeruli may show fibrinoid necrosis. People of African descent and those with severe elevations are at increased risk of rapid renal failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: malignant hypertension shows hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis and fibrinoid necrosis, whereas benign hypertension shows hyaline arteriolosclerosis. COMLEX: it is a hypertensive emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — angiotensin II and vasoconstrictor tone in the renal arterioles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with severe hypertension has unilateral atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis. Which pair of findings in the two kidneys is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Small ischemic kidney with severe arteriolosclerosis and contralateral mild arteriolosclerosis","B":"Enlarged ischemic kidney with hydronephrosis and contralateral cortical atrophy","C":"Small ischemic kidney with fibrinoid necrosis and contralateral normal vessels","D":"Normal ischemic kidney with hyaline change and contralateral onion-skin lesions","E":"Small ischemic kidney with mild arteriolosclerosis and contralateral severe arteriolosclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ischemic kidney is reduced in size with diffuse atrophy, crowded glomeruli, and atrophic tubules, but its arterioles are usually protected from high pressure and show only mild arteriolosclerosis. The contralateral nonischemic kidney is exposed to the elevated pressure driven by renin from the ischemic side and may show more severe arteriolosclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis raises renin, angiotensin II, and blood pressure, which then damages the unprotected contralateral kidney. COMLEX: atherosclerosis causes 70 percent of stenosis and fibromuscular dysplasia most often affects young women.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — renin and angiotensin II as the cause of renovascular hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis & Renovascular Vicious Cycle","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with bilateral renal artery stenosis starts lisinopril, and his creatinine rises sharply. Which mechanism best explains the fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Afferent constriction from unopposed prostaglandin release lowers filtration pressure","B":"Increased Bowman space pressure from ureteral obstruction opposes filtration","C":"Efferent dilation removes angiotensin II support of glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Direct immune injury to podocytes reduces the filtration coefficient","E":"Volume expansion raises plasma oncotic pressure and reduces net filtration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With bilateral stenosis, perfusion pressure is low, and angiotensin II constricts efferent arterioles to preserve glomerular capillary pressure and GFR. ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole, abolishing this compensation, so GFR falls, which can precipitate acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated in bilateral renal artery stenosis. COMLEX: a creatinine rise above about 30 percent after starting an ACE inhibitor should prompt a search for renal artery stenosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — efferent constriction by angiotensin II and its inhibition by ACE inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy had bloody diarrhea five days ago and now has pallor, oliguria, schistocytes on the smear, platelets of 40,000, and a creatinine of 3.0 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains his kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies inhibit ADAMTS13 and ultralarge vWF multimers form","B":"An inherited factor H defect causes uncontrolled alternative pathway activation","C":"Immune complexes deposit in the mesangium and activate the classical pathway","D":"Shiga-like toxin injures endothelium and drives platelet microthrombi","E":"Antiphospholipid antibodies activate endothelium and promote thrombosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea followed by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure in a child is typical HUS. Shiga-like toxin activates endothelial cells, increases adhesion molecules and endothelin, decreases nitric oxide, and may cause endothelial apoptosis, leading to platelet activation and microvascular thrombi. ADAMTS13 inhibition, factor H defects, and antiphospholipid antibodies are the mechanisms of other TMAs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: typical HUS follows O157:H7 diarrhea, and recovery is expected in most children. COMLEX: supportive care with dialysis when necessary is the mainstay.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — severe hypertension also produces endothelial injury and microvascular disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has fever, confusion, schistocytes, platelets of 15,000, and a creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL. Activity of the von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease is below 10 percent. Which diagnosis and treatment are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"TTP from ADAMTS13 autoantibodies, treated with plasma exchange","B":"Typical HUS from Shiga-like toxin, treated with antibiotics","C":"Atypical HUS from factor H mutation, treated with platelet transfusion","D":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation, treated with heparin infusion","E":"Malignant hypertension, treated with intravenous vasodilators"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, neurologic symptoms, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction form the pentad of TTP, and severely deficient ADAMTS13 activity confirms it. Autoantibodies are the usual cause, and plasma exchange removes them and supplies functional enzyme, which is effective in most patients. Central nervous system involvement dominates while severe renal injury is uncommon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal ADAMTS13 levels distinguish atypical HUS from TTP. COMLEX: platelet transfusion is generally avoided in TTP because it can worsen thrombosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — thrombotic microangiopathy can also complicate lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thrombotic Microangiopathies & Microvascular Injury","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman without diarrhea has recurrent microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and renal failure, with normal ADAMTS13 activity and negative Shiga toxin testing. Which mechanism best explains her disease?","choices":{"A":"Shiga-like toxin binding to endothelial receptors in the glomerulus","B":"Alternative pathway overactivation from a defective regulator","C":"Inhibitory antibodies that block ADAMTS13 activity","D":"Immune complex deposition with consumption of C1q","E":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of collagen IV"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Atypical, diarrhea-negative HUS is most often complement-mediated TMA, in which mutations of regulatory proteins such as factor H, factor I, and CD46, or autoantibodies against them, permit uncontrolled alternative pathway activation and endothelial injury. Normal ADAMTS13 excludes TTP, and patients fare worse than in typical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: factor H protects cells by breaking down the alternative pathway C3 convertase. COMLEX: complement inhibitors treat complement-mediated TMA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — immune complexes activate complement in lupus nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with CKD has 3 g/day of proteinuria and progressive scarring of the interstitium. Which mechanism best links filtered protein to fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Filtered protein deposits collagen directly in the interstitium","B":"Filtered protein blocks the tubular lumens and causes hydronephrosis","C":"Filtered protein lowers oncotic pressure and causes cortical ischemia","D":"Tubular protein uptake activates inflammatory and TGF-beta pathways","E":"Filtered protein stimulates natriuretic peptide release and volume loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein that leaks through a damaged glomerular barrier is taken up by tubular cells, which activate inflammatory pathways and profibrotic mediators such as TGF-beta, driving interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy. Interstitial fibrosis correlates with progression to end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TGF-beta stimulates extracellular matrix production and is the central profibrotic cytokine in CKD. COMLEX: reduce proteinuria with RAAS blockade to slow progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — protein leakage and secondary tubulointerstitial injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with diabetic kidney disease and CKD stage 3 has surviving glomeruli under increased workload. Which change most directly drives further nephron loss?","choices":{"A":"Falling glomerular flow that lowers capillary pressure and filtration","B":"Hypertrophy with capillary hypertension that injures podocytes","C":"Regression of glomerular size with normal capillary pressure","D":"Increased podocyte number that thickens the filtration barrier","E":"Reduced single-nephron GFR that spares the remaining glomeruli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When the number of functional nephrons falls, surviving glomeruli hypertrophy and experience increased blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure. The resulting hyperfiltration injures podocytes, leading to glomerulosclerosis and further nephron loss in a vicious cycle toward end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperfiltration is the common pathway of progression in CKD. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors slow this process by lowering glomerular capillary pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — single-nephron capillary hypertension and podocyte injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Chronic Kidney Disease & The Common Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"Which sequence best describes the common pathway of progressive chronic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Nephron loss, hypoperfusion, tubular hypertrophy, podocyte hyperplasia, and regeneration","B":"Podocyte hypertrophy, mesangial atrophy, capillary dilation, filtration decline, and sclerosis","C":"Nephron loss, hyperfiltration, podocyte injury, glomerulosclerosis, and TGF-beta-mediated fibrosis","D":"Interstitial fibrosis, tubular hypertrophy, glomerular hyperplasia, hypertension, and nephron gain","E":"Hyperfiltration, nephron gain, podocyte proliferation, capillary regeneration, and reversal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progression begins with loss of nephrons, which causes compensatory hyperfiltration and capillary hypertension in surviving glomeruli. This injures podocytes, causing glomerulosclerosis, and TGF-beta-mediated interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy follow, driving the kidney toward end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the final common pathway is nonspecific and includes glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and arteriosclerosis. COMLEX: this explains why renal function can decline even after the original insult is removed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — maladaptive hyperfiltration and secondary global glomerulosclerosis","isHighYield":true}]}]},{"examNumber":4,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":37,"title":"SDL 37 — Genetic & Developmental Renal Cystic Disorders","questions":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure of tubular epithelial cells sits at the center of the favored hypothesis for cyst formation in polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"Brush border microvillus array","B":"Cilium-centrosome complex","C":"Tight junction protein complex","D":"Basolateral membrane infoldings","E":"Apical water channel vesicles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins states that the currently favored hypothesis places the cilia-centrosome complex of tubular epithelial cells at the center of polycystic disease. Tubular cells carry a single nonmotile primary cilium that projects into the lumen from the apical surface and arises from a basal body derived from the centriole. Ciliopathies include polycystic kidney disease and nephronophthisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polycystic kidney disease and nephronophthisis are ciliopathies, so think primary cilium when a cystic kidney disease is described. COMLEX: cystic kidney diseases are a cause of chronic kidney disease, and family history is the key clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"The nonmotile primary cilium of kidney tubular cells monitors changes in which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Luminal chloride concentration","B":"Interstitial oncotic pressure","C":"Fluid flow and shear stress","D":"Circulating hormone levels","E":"Tubular fluid acidity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The apical cilia of kidney tubule cells function as a mechanosensor that monitors changes in fluid flow and shear stress. In response, these sensors regulate ion flux and cellular behavior, including cell polarity and proliferation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cilium is a flow sensor, and loss of that sensing drives cystogenesis. COMLEX: cysts arise when mechanosensing, calcium flux, and signal transduction are defective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Bending of the primary cilium by tubular fluid flow normally regulates flux of which ion through the polycystin complex?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Potassium","C":"Chloride","D":"Calcium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 appear to form a complex that regulates intracellular calcium in response to fluid flow, perhaps because fluid moving through the tubules causes ciliary bending that opens calcium channels. Loss of the complex alters intracellular calcium, which affects proliferation, apoptosis, and secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polycystin-2 is a calcium-permeable cation channel, and disturbed calcium signaling is the proposed cyst mechanism. COMLEX: the decrease in calcium is thought to stimulate proliferation and secretion by the cyst-lining cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 1 — defective signaling in tubular epithelial cells that drives cyst development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"Mutations in which pair of genes cause almost all cases of adult polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"PKD1 and PKD2","B":"PKD1 and PKHD1","C":"PKD2 and NPHP1","D":"PKHD1 and NPHP1","E":"PKHD1 and COL4A5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Almost all cases of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease result from mutations in PKD1, which encodes polycystin-1 and accounts for about 85 percent of cases, or PKD2, which encodes polycystin-2. PKHD1 is the gene of the autosomal recessive childhood form, and the nephrocystin genes cause nephronophthisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PKD1 accounts for about 85 percent of ADPKD and PKD2 for the rest; First Aid places PKD1 on chromosome 16. COMLEX: PKD2 disease is less severe, with later renal failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"Most cases of autosomal recessive childhood polycystic kidney disease are caused by mutations in which gene?","choices":{"A":"PKD1","B":"PKD2","C":"PKHD1","D":"NPHP1","E":"COL4A5"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most cases of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease are caused by PKHD1, which encodes fibrocystin. Like the polycystins, fibrocystin localizes to the primary cilium of tubular cells. Most patients are compound heterozygotes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is PKHD1 and fibrocystin, and it is associated with congenital hepatic fibrosis. COMLEX: severe cases present at birth, and First Aid links in utero oliguric renal failure to Potter sequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, about what percentage of patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease have hepatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"10 percent","B":"40 percent","C":"25 percent","D":"65 percent","E":"90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 40 percent of patients with ADPKD have one to several cysts in the liver, called polycystic liver disease. The cysts are usually asymptomatic and derive from biliary epithelium. Cysts occur much less frequently in the spleen, pancreas, and lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD extrarenal manifestations include liver cysts, berry aneurysms, and mitral valve prolapse. COMLEX: the diagnosis is made by radiologic imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 3 — progressive cystic destruction of nephrons leads to chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"What is the usual renal prognosis of medullary sponge kidney?","choices":{"A":"Progression to end-stage disease within 5 to 10 years","B":"Renal failure beginning between 40 and 60 years of age","C":"Death in infancy or childhood from renal failure","D":"Renal failure if bilateral but surgically curable if unilateral","E":"Usually normal renal function with a benign course"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medullary sponge kidney is a relatively common and usually innocuous structural change of multiple cystic dilations of the medullary collecting ducts, usually found radiographically in adults. Renal function is usually normal, and Robbins lists the outcome as benign. Progression to end-stage disease in 5 to 10 years describes nephronophthisis, renal failure at 40 to 60 years describes adult polycystic disease, death in infancy or childhood describes the childhood polycystic form, and surgical cure of unilateral disease describes multicystic dysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: medullary sponge kidney is a benign cause of hematuria, urinary tract infection, and recurrent stones. COMLEX: it is discovered on excretory urography and needs no specific renal therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"With which manifestations do children with nephronophthisis present first?","choices":{"A":"Gross hematuria with recurrent flank pain","B":"Edema with heavy proteinuria and hyperlipidemia","C":"Oliguria with severe hypertension and hematuria","D":"Polyuria and polydipsia with sodium wasting","E":"Hematuria with hearing loss and lenticonus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Children with nephronophthisis present first with polyuria and polydipsia, reflecting a marked defect in the concentrating ability of renal tubules. Sodium wasting and tubular acidosis are also prominent, and the expected course is progression to end-stage kidney disease in 5 to 10 years.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephronophthisis is autosomal recessive with corticomedullary cysts and shrunken kidneys, and it is the most common genetic cause of end-stage kidney disease in children and young adults. COMLEX: consider it in unexplained chronic renal failure in a child with a positive family history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature characterizes multicystic renal dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Islands of undifferentiated mesenchyme with cartilage","B":"Basketweave lamination of the glomerular basement membrane","C":"Thickened tubular basement membranes with interstitial fibrosis","D":"Cysts lined by hyperplastic epithelium with oxalate crystals","E":"Radially arranged cylindrical dilations of collecting tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Although normal nephrons are present, the characteristic histologic feature of multicystic renal dysplasia is islands of undifferentiated mesenchyme, often with cartilage, and immature collecting ducts. Thickened tubular basement membranes with interstitial fibrosis describe nephronophthisis, oxalate-containing cysts describe acquired cystic disease, and radial collecting duct dilations describe the childhood polycystic form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cartilage in a cystic kidney means dysplasia, which is sporadic and has no inheritance pattern. COMLEX: most cases are associated with ureteropelvic obstruction, ureteral agenesis or atresia, and other lower urinary tract anomalies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 3 — how medullary cystic disorders and sporadic multicystic dysplasia differ in presentation and pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has hypertension, episodic gross hematuria, and palpable bilateral flank masses. His mother reached kidney failure in her fifties. CT shows both kidneys enlarged and replaced by innumerable cysts. Which cellular abnormality best explains enlargement of these cysts?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in tubular basement membranes with dilation","B":"Persistent embryonic mesenchyme that fails to form functional nephrons","C":"Lost flow-induced calcium signaling that drives cell proliferation","D":"Motile ciliary overactivity that increases luminal fluid secretion","E":"Ischemic tubular necrosis with regeneration of dilated tubular segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. Mutation of PKD1 or PKD2 leads to loss of the polycystin complex, which regulates intracellular calcium in response to flow sensed at the primary cilium. The decrease in calcium is thought to stimulate proliferation and secretion by cyst-lining epithelial cells, causing progressive cyst formation and enlargement. Tubular cilia are nonmotile, and the other options describe unrelated processes such as immune injury, dysplasia, and ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD is a ciliopathy in which altered calcium signaling drives proliferation and secretion by cyst epithelium. COMLEX: hypertension and hematuria are frequent early features, and progression is faster in males, in people of African descent, and with hypertension.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — cystic expansion destroys nephrons and feeds the chronic kidney disease common pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, pallor, and growth retardation, and his sister has similar disease. Ultrasonography shows small kidneys, and biopsy shows tubular basement membrane thickening with interstitial fibrosis. Genetic testing identifies a mutation in NPHP1. Where does the encoded protein normally localize?","choices":{"A":"Podocyte slit diaphragm","B":"Glomerular basement membrane","C":"Proximal tubule brush border","D":"Cilium and basal body","E":"Mitochondrial inner membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is familial juvenile nephronophthisis, the most common variant of the nephronophthisis complex. Mutations in the NPHP genes, which encode nephrocystins, affect proteins found in primary cilia, in the basal bodies attached to them, or in the centrosome from which basal bodies originate, so the disease is a ciliopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrocystins localize to the cilium-basal body-centrosome axis, which unites nephronophthisis with the polycystic diseases. COMLEX: the tubular concentrating defect causes polyuria and polydipsia, with salt wasting and tubular acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — nephrocystin proteins at the cilium and basal body in ciliopathy cyst pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — The Ciliopathy Paradigm & Molecular Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"Genetic testing in a child with nephronophthisis identifies a mutation in NPHP2, which encodes inversin. During embryogenesis this protein normally mediates which process?","choices":{"A":"Left-right patterning","B":"Ureteric bud branching","C":"Neural crest migration","D":"Cloacal septation","E":"Kidney ascent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The NPHP2 gene product is inversin, also called nephrocystin-2, which mediates left-right patterning during embryogenesis. Like the other nephrocystins, it is part of the cilium-centrosome system, which illustrates how ciliary defects can produce extrarenal and developmental phenotypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inversin links a ciliopathy to left-right patterning during embryogenesis. COMLEX: nephronophthisis includes a renal-retinal dysplasia variant in which kidney disease is accompanied by ocular lesions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — ciliary proteins such as inversin and their developmental roles in ciliopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has bilateral flank pain, hypertension, and enlarged kidneys with many cysts on ultrasonography. His father had the same disease. Pathology shows that cysts involve only a minority of nephrons even though every tubular cell carries the same inherited gene defect. Which best explains why only some tubules form cysts?","choices":{"A":"Dominant negative action of the mutant protein throughout the epithelium","B":"Somatic mutation that disables the second allele in an epithelial cell","C":"Mitochondrial heteroplasmy that varies between individual nephrons","D":"Random X inactivation that silences the normal allele in some cells","E":"Postnatal obstruction of individual tubules by interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Although susceptibility to ADPKD is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait, both alleles of the involved gene must be nonfunctional for cyst development. Each cyst is clonal: its cells carry one inherited mutation plus an acquired somatic mutation that inactivates the second allele. Because cysts initially involve only a minority of nephrons, renal function is retained until about the fourth or fifth decade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD follows a two-hit model, a germline hit plus a somatic hit in individual tubular cells. COMLEX: the disease is always bilateral, and apparent unilateral cases are probably multicystic dysplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — the two-hit model of inherited and somatic mutation in ADPKD cyst formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with bilateral enlarged kidneys and a family history of kidney failure develops a sudden severe headache with neck stiffness. CT shows blood in the subarachnoid space. Which vascular lesion is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm of penetrating arteries","B":"Cerebral arteriovenous malformation","C":"Berry aneurysm of the circle of Willis","D":"Mycotic aneurysm of a cerebral artery","E":"Dural venous sinus thrombosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease have intracranial berry aneurysms in the circle of Willis, presumably from altered polycystin expression in vascular smooth muscle. Subarachnoid hemorrhage from these aneurysms accounts for death in 4 to 10 percent of affected individuals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADPKD is associated with berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, and hepatic cysts. COMLEX: subarachnoid hemorrhage presents as a sudden worst headache of life with meningeal signs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — extrarenal manifestations of ADPKD, including intracranial aneurysms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Polycystic Kidney Disease Progression","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy has had smooth, enlarged kidneys since birth, and kidney biopsy showed radially arranged, cylindrical dilations of collecting tubules. He now has splenomegaly. Which hepatic lesion most likely explains the splenomegaly?","choices":{"A":"Macronodular cirrhosis from chronic viral hepatitis infection","B":"Hepatic vein thrombosis with centrilobular sinusoidal congestion","C":"Extensive steatosis with hepatocyte ballooning and inflammation","D":"Granulomatous inflammation surrounding the portal tracts","E":"Periportal fibrosis with biliary ductule proliferation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is autosomal recessive childhood polycystic kidney disease, in which the kidneys are enlarged and smooth and all collecting tubules show cylindrical dilation. Patients who survive infancy may develop congenital hepatic fibrosis, a bland periportal fibrosis with proliferation of well-differentiated biliary ductules. In older children the hepatic disease is the predominant concern, and portal hypertension with splenomegaly may develop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is PKHD1 with congenital hepatic fibrosis and portal hypertension. COMLEX: First Aid notes that significant oliguric renal failure in utero can lead to Potter sequence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — oligohydramnios and Potter sequence when fetal urine output fails","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has polyuria, polydipsia, pallor, and growth retardation. Ultrasonography shows small, contracted kidneys, and biopsy shows small cysts at the corticomedullary junction surrounded by chronic inflammatory cells. Which process is most responsible for his eventual renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Compression of parenchyma by expanding medullary cysts","B":"Immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries","C":"Ischemic necrosis of the renal papillae","D":"Cortical tubular atrophy with interstitial fibrosis","E":"Obstruction at the ureteropelvic junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is nephronophthisis. Although the medullary cysts are important, Robbins states that the cortical tubulointerstitial damage, with widespread tubular atrophy, thickened tubular basement membranes, and interstitial fibrosis, is the cause of the eventual renal insufficiency. Glomerular structure is generally preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis on biopsy in a child with unexplained renal failure and a family history suggests nephronophthisis. COMLEX: the expected course is end-stage kidney disease within 5 to 10 years.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — chronic tubulointerstitial scarring and tubular atrophy in the kidney","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had two episodes of kidney stones and a urinary tract infection, and her creatinine is normal. Excretory urography shows cystic dilation of the papillary ducts. Histologic examination of an involved papilla would most likely show cysts lined by which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Keratinizing squamous or ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Cuboidal or occasionally transitional epithelium","C":"Mucin-secreting columnar or goblet cell epithelium","D":"Pseudostratified columnar or simple squamous epithelium","E":"Atypical hobnail or clear cell epithelium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is medullary sponge kidney, which Robbins restricts to multiple cystic dilations of the collecting ducts in the medulla, usually discovered radiographically in adults with normal renal function. On gross inspection the papillary ducts in the medulla are dilated and small cysts may be present, and the cysts are lined by cuboidal epithelium or occasionally by transitional epithelium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recurrent stones plus medullary cysts on urography in an adult with normal renal function points to medullary sponge kidney. COMLEX: cortical scarring is absent unless pyelonephritis is superimposed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — medullary sponge kidney as a benign medullary cystic disorder, distinct from nephronophthisis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Medullary Cystic Diseases & Multicystic Renal Dysplasia","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old girl has a palpable left flank mass. A left nephrectomy specimen shows an enlarged, irregular kidney with cysts of variable size lined by flattened epithelium, with islands of cartilage and undifferentiated mesenchyme. The right kidney functions normally. Which additional anomaly is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ureteropelvic obstruction","B":"Congenital hepatic fibrosis","C":"Intracranial berry aneurysm","D":"Mitral valve prolapse","E":"Renal-retinal dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is multicystic renal dysplasia, a sporadic disorder that can be unilateral or bilateral. Most cases are associated with ureteropelvic obstruction, ureteral agenesis or atresia, and other anomalies of the lower urinary tract. Unilateral disease has an excellent prognosis after nephrectomy because the opposite kidney functions normally. Hepatic fibrosis, berry aneurysms, and valve prolapse belong to the polycystic diseases, and renal-retinal dysplasia is a variant of nephronophthisis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multicystic dysplasia is a sporadic developmental disorder with cartilage and immature ducts on histology. COMLEX: unilateral dysplasia may mimic a neoplasm and lead to surgical exploration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — reciprocal induction between the ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":38,"title":"SDL 38 — Urinary Tract Obstruction (Urolithiasis & Hydronephrosis)","questions":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, approximately what percentage of renal calculi are calcium oxalate or calcium oxalate mixed with calcium phosphate?","choices":{"A":"About 55 percent","B":"About 70 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 25 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins lists four main types of calculi, and calcium stones, composed largely of calcium oxalate or calcium oxalate mixed with calcium phosphate, make up about 70 percent. Struvite stones are 5 to 10 percent, uric acid stones 5 to 10 percent, and cystine stones 1 to 2 percent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium stones are the most common kidney stone, and calcium stones are radiopaque. COMLEX: most stones arise in the kidney, and a low urine volume favors supersaturation, so fluids are encouraged.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"A tendency to excrete urine of which pH predisposes to uric acid stones in patients who have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid?","choices":{"A":"A pH above 7.5","B":"A pH near 6.5","C":"A pH near 7.0","D":"A pH below 5.5","E":"A pH above 8.0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"More than half of patients with uric acid calculi have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid excretion. In this group a tendency to excrete urine with a pH below 5.5 is thought to predispose to stones, because uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine. In contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, uric acid stones are radiolucent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uric acid stones are radiolucent and form in acidic urine, and First Aid treats them with urine alkalinization and allopurinol. COMLEX: uric acid stones are also common in gout and in diseases with rapid cell turnover such as leukemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Cystine stones result from which underlying defect?","choices":{"A":"Defective tubular reabsorption of amino acids","B":"Excess intestinal absorption of dietary oxalate","C":"Bacterial conversion of urea to ammonia","D":"Overproduction of uric acid from purine turnover","E":"Reduced urinary excretion of citrate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cystine stones are caused by genetic defects in the renal reabsorption of amino acids, including cystine, which causes cystinuria. These stones form at low urinary pH and account for 1 to 2 percent of stones. Enteric oxalate absorption, urea-splitting infection, uric acid overproduction, and hypocitraturia are associated with other stone types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria is a hereditary transporter defect in which cystine is poorly soluble, and First Aid notes it can form staghorn calculi, although Robbins states that staghorn calculi are frequently a consequence of infection. COMLEX: cystine stones begin in childhood and run in families.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — renal stone types, urinary pH, and the metabolic or infectious triggers behind them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"Why does glomerular filtration persist for some time even with complete obstruction of urine outflow?","choices":{"A":"Tubular water secretion replaces glomerular filtration in the blocked kidney","B":"Pelvic pressure remains below Bowman space pressure during obstruction","C":"Loops of Henle absorb the filtrate before it can accumulate","D":"Filtrate diffuses back into the renal interstitium and perirenal spaces","E":"Efferent constriction sustains filtration pressure indefinitely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Even with complete obstruction, glomerular filtration persists for some time because the filtrate diffuses back into the renal interstitium and perirenal spaces, from where it returns to the lymphatic and venous systems. Because filtration continues, the calyces and pelvis become dilated, often markedly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydronephrosis is dilation of the pelvis and calyces with progressive atrophy due to outflow obstruction. COMLEX: relief of early obstruction leads to reversion toward normal function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the earliest functional alteration caused by urinary tract obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Falling glomerular filtration rate","B":"Heavy proteinuria with edema","C":"Impaired concentrating ability","D":"Renal salt retention with edema","E":"Hematuria with red cell casts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The high pelvic pressure is transmitted back through the collecting ducts into the cortex, and it also compresses the medullary renal vasculature, decreasing inner medullary blood flow. The initial functional alterations are therefore largely tubular, manifested primarily by impaired concentrating ability. Only later does the GFR begin to fall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in bilateral partial obstruction the earliest manifestation is polyuria and nocturia from the concentrating defect. COMLEX: some patients also develop distal tubular acidosis and renal salt wasting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of an advanced, long-standing hydronephrotic kidney?","choices":{"A":"Thin-walled cystic sac with obliterated pyramids","B":"Small kidney with coarse polar scars","C":"Shrunken kidney with fine granular cortex","D":"Enlarged kidney replaced by innumerable parenchymal cysts","E":"Pale wedge-shaped scar with a hyperemic border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In advanced hydronephrosis the kidney may be transformed into a thin-walled cystic structure up to 15 to 20 cm in diameter, with striking parenchymal atrophy, total obliteration of the pyramids, and thinning of the cortex. Earlier there is progressive blunting and then cupping of the pyramid apices, with cortical tubular atrophy and diffuse interstitial fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvicalyceal dilation with cortical thinning indicates chronic obstruction. COMLEX: the kidney may be slightly to massively enlarged depending on the degree and duration of obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — how outflow obstruction progresses from pelvic pressure to parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Renal colic is typically produced by which event?","choices":{"A":"Prostatic enlargement obstructing outflow","B":"A calculus lodged in the ureter","C":"Gradual dilation of the renal pelvis","D":"Formation of a simple cortical cyst","E":"Progressive cortical atrophy of the kidney"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to renal colic, which Robbins describes as spasms of severe pain, often with hematuria. Prostatic enlargement typically gives bladder symptoms, and slowly progressive dilation, cysts, and atrophy are typically silent or minimally painful.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an obstructing stone causes colicky flank pain with hematuria, and First Aid describes pain radiating to the groin. COMLEX: renal colic is a classic presentation of urolithiasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Most of the early symptoms of hydronephrosis are produced by which factor?","choices":{"A":"Uremia from a falling glomerular filtration rate","B":"Cortical scarring of the kidney","C":"The underlying cause of the obstruction","D":"The interstitial inflammatory reaction","E":"Hyperkalemia from tubular dysfunction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, but most of the early symptoms are produced by the underlying cause. Calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to renal colic, and prostatic enlargement gives rise to bladder symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the presenting symptoms of obstruction point to its cause, such as stones or prostatic enlargement. COMLEX: ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique for diagnosing obstructive uropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":1,"stem":"Why can unilateral complete or partial hydronephrosis remain silent for long periods?","choices":{"A":"The obstructed kidney raises its own filtration rate","B":"Pain fibers are absent from the renal capsule","C":"Filtration ceases so no distention can occur","D":"Collateral channels drain urine around the block","E":"The unaffected kidney maintains adequate function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unilateral complete or partial hydronephrosis may remain silent for long periods because the unaffected kidney can maintain adequate renal function, and it is sometimes first recognized on imaging. Filtration continues in the obstructed kidney, which is why the pelvis and calyces dilate, and Robbins attributes acute obstructive pain to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: unilateral obstruction may not raise creatinine, whereas bilateral obstruction does. COMLEX: in the first few weeks, relief of obstruction leads to reversion to normal function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 3 — clinical presentations of obstruction: pain quality, hematuria, and silent disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections has flank pain and fever. CT shows a branching stone occupying the renal pelvis and calyces, and urine culture grows Proteus mirabilis. Which mechanism best explains formation of this stone?","choices":{"A":"Insolubility of uric acid in persistently acidic urine","B":"Bacterial urea splitting with alkaline urine","C":"Excess absorption of dietary calcium from the intestine","D":"Inherited defect of renal tubular amino acid reabsorption","E":"Hyperoxaluria from intestinal oxalate overabsorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate, or struvite, stones form largely after infection by urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus and some staphylococci. These convert urea to ammonia, and the resulting alkaline urine precipitates magnesium ammonium phosphate. They form some of the largest stones, and staghorn calculi occupying large portions of the renal pelvis are frequently a consequence of infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urease-positive bacteria such as Proteus produce ammonia and alkaline urine, and First Aid treats struvite by eradicating infection and surgically removing the stone. COMLEX: stones both cause obstruction and predispose to superimposed infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — urinary stasis converts infection into ascending pyelonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with gout has colicky right flank pain and hematuria. A plain abdominal radiograph shows no radiopaque stone, and his urine pH is 5.2. Which best explains formation of his stone?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial urease splitting urea to ammonia","B":"Excess intestinal absorption of calcium","C":"Genetic defect in renal amino acid reabsorption","D":"Low solubility of uric acid in acidic fluid","E":"Enteric hyperabsorption of dietary oxalate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uric acid stones are common in patients with hyperuricemia such as gout, and uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine. In contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, uric acid stones are radiolucent, which explains the negative plain film. Urease-producing infection, calcium hyperabsorption, cystinuria, and oxalate hyperabsorption produce other stone types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: radiolucent stone plus gout or acidic urine points to uric acid, and First Aid treats it with urine alkalinization and allopurinol. COMLEX: uric acid stones also occur in diseases with rapid cell turnover such as leukemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — urine alkalinization and pH trapping of weak acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Calculus Chemistry & Crystal Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with gout has recurrent radiopaque stones, and a 24-hour urine collection shows hyperuricosuria. Which mechanism best explains his stone formation?","choices":{"A":"Urea-splitting infection that alkalinizes the urine","B":"Impaired tubular reabsorption of amino acids","C":"Nucleation of calcium oxalate by uric acid crystals","D":"Excess urinary pyrophosphate that inhibits crystal growth","E":"Intestinal overabsorption of dietary oxalate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Up to 20 percent of calcium oxalate stones are associated with increased uric acid secretion, called hyperuricosuric calcium nephrolithiasis, with or without hypercalciuria. The mechanism involves nucleation of calcium oxalate by uric acid crystals in the collecting ducts. Pyrophosphate is one of several inhibitors of crystal formation in urine, so an excess would not promote stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium oxalate stones are the most common type and are radiopaque. COMLEX: hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia is the most frequent metabolic association, so 24-hour urine chemistry guides prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — bidirectional renal handling of uric acid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with prostatic enlargement has had anuria for 36 hours from complete bilateral outflow obstruction. After urinary catheterization his urine output rises to 400 mL per hour. Which best characterizes the urine he is now excreting?","choices":{"A":"Large volume rich in sodium chloride","B":"Small volume with maximal sodium retention","C":"Large volume of sodium-free dilute urine","D":"Normal volume with nephrotic-range protein","E":"Small volume with red cell casts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Complete bilateral obstruction of rapid onset causes oliguria or anuria and is incompatible with survival unless relieved. Robbins notes that diuresis frequently occurs after relief of complete obstruction, and it can be massive, with the kidney excreting large amounts of urine rich in sodium chloride.","boardPrep":"Step 1: postobstructive diuresis can cause volume and electrolyte depletion, so monitor volume status after relief. COMLEX: prostatic enlargement is a common cause of bilateral outflow obstruction in older men.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — the diuretic recovery phase and its hazards","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has had left flank pain for 10 days from a stone in the left ureter, with left hydronephrosis on ultrasonography. The stone is removed. Which renal outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Permanent atrophy despite relief","B":"Progression to end-stage disease","C":"Persistent nephrotic syndrome","D":"Reversion to normal function","E":"Fixed tubular acidosis with salt wasting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the early stages of obstruction, perhaps the first few weeks, relief of the obstruction leads to reversion to normal function. Unrelieved obstruction almost always leads to permanent atrophy, and the medullary vascular defects are initially reversible. Progressive end-stage disease, fixed tubular defects, and nephrotic syndrome are not expected after early relief.","boardPrep":"Step 1: early relief of obstruction preserves renal function, whereas prolonged obstruction produces atrophy and interstitial fibrosis. COMLEX: prompt relief of obstruction is the priority.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — early versus prolonged obstruction and the fate of the hydronephrotic parenchyma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Mechanical Hydronephrosis Pathophysiology","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with years of untreated bladder outlet obstruction dies of an unrelated cause. Both kidneys show markedly dilated pelves, obliterated pyramids, and a thinned cortex. Which microscopic finding is expected in the cortex?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis with onion-skin arteriolar thickening","B":"Tubular atrophy with diffuse interstitial fibrosis","C":"Neutrophilic casts with cortical microabscesses","D":"Crescent formation within Bowman space","E":"Mesangial IgA deposition with hypercellularity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In chronic hydronephrosis the picture is one of cortical tubular atrophy with marked diffuse interstitial fibrosis, and obstruction triggers an interstitial inflammatory reaction even in the absence of infection. Onion-skin arteriolar lesions, microabscesses, crescents, and mesangial IgA deposits belong to malignant hypertension, acute pyelonephritis, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic obstruction ends in tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis with a thin cortex. COMLEX: in bilateral partial obstruction hypertension is common.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — obstruction and reflux causing chronic scarring of the kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has had 3 days of gross hematuria and excruciating flank pain that radiates toward the groin, without fever. CT shows a 6-mm calculus in the proximal ureter. Which best describes the pain pattern that distinguishes his symptom from the pain of a slowly dilating hydronephrotic kidney?","choices":{"A":"A dull, constant ache","B":"Pain limited to the bladder region","C":"Spasms of severe colicky pain","D":"No pain until renal failure develops","E":"Burning pain during voiding"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urolithiasis is manifested by spasms of severe pain, called renal colic, and hematuria, and calculi lodged in the ureters give rise to colic. In contrast, acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, unilateral hydronephrosis may remain silent for long periods, and prostatic enlargement gives rise to bladder symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute colicky flank pain with hematuria points to a ureteral stone. COMLEX: colic is intermittent and severe, in contrast to the dull constant ache of capsular distention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — colicky ureteral pain contrasted with the dull pain of hydronephrotic capsular distention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with prostatic enlargement develops acute urinary retention. Two days later he has a dull, constant bilateral flank ache. He is afebrile, and ultrasonography shows bilateral pelvicalyceal dilation with no stones or cysts. Which best explains the flank ache?","choices":{"A":"Acute bacterial invasion of the renal pelvis and ureter","B":"Hemorrhage into a simple cyst of the renal cortex","C":"Immune complex inflammation of glomerular capillaries","D":"Ischemic infarction of a segment of the renal cortex","E":"Stretch of the capsule and collecting system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute obstruction may provoke pain attributed to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule, so the dull constant flank ache after acute retention reflects stretch of these structures. Infection, cyst hemorrhage, glomerular inflammation, and cortical infarction are not supported by an afebrile patient with pelvicalyceal dilation and no cysts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive pain reflects stretch of the collecting system and capsule, and colicky pain suggests a stone lodged in the ureter. COMLEX: ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique to confirm obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — prostatic enlargement and urethral compression in benign hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Pain Pathways","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with known cervical carcinoma has flank fullness, and her creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 2.4 mg/dL. Which is the most appropriate initial test to evaluate for urinary tract obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Renal ultrasonography","B":"Percutaneous kidney biopsy","C":"Renal arteriography","D":"Retrograde pyelography","E":"Voiding cystourethrography"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ultrasonography is a useful noninvasive technique in the diagnosis of obstructive uropathy. Robbins lists carcinoma of the cervix or uterus among tumors that can obstruct the ureters. Biopsy, arteriography, retrograde pyelography, and catheter-based cystography are invasive procedures and are not the first test for suspected obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstruction is a postrenal cause of azotemia, and imaging identifies dilated pelvicalyceal systems. COMLEX: identify and relieve obstruction early, because early relief allows return of function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — ureteral obstruction and retroperitoneal compression","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":39,"title":"SDL 39 — Renal Neoplasms & Oncogenic Pathways","questions":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of one copy of which chromosomal region is found in nearly all clear cell renal cell carcinomas?","choices":{"A":"Chromosome 11p","B":"Chromosome 5q","C":"Chromosome 3p","D":"Chromosome 13q","E":"Chromosome 18q"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In 98% of clear cell renal cell carcinomas, whether sporadic, familial, or associated with VHL syndrome, one copy of chromosome 3 is deleted at band p25.3, the site of the VHL gene. In up to 80% the second copy is disabled by somatic mutation or silenced by hypermethylation, consistent with VHL acting as a tumor suppressor gene. The other regions are not the characteristic deletion of this tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC and clear cell histology point to chromosome 3 and VHL. COMLEX: clear cell carcinoma is the most common RCC subtype, accounting for 70% to 80% of renal cell cancers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"What is the normal function of the protein encoded by the VHL gene?","choices":{"A":"Phosphorylates HIF to enhance its transcription","B":"Targets HIF for ubiquitin-mediated degradation","C":"Binds VEGF to limit its receptor activation","D":"Deacetylates histones near angiogenic genes","E":"Cleaves MYC to reduce metabolic reprogramming"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The VHL protein is part of a ubiquitin ligase complex that targets the transcription factor hypoxia-inducible factor for degradation in an oxygen-dependent fashion. When VHL is inactive, HIF stays high even under normoxic conditions and drives genes such as VEGF and IGF-1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VHL loss produces pseudohypoxia with stabilized HIF, increased VEGF, and a highly vascular tumor. COMLEX: one-half to two-thirds of patients with VHL syndrome develop renal cysts and bilateral, often multiple, renal cell carcinomas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":1,"stem":"Gain-of-function mutation of which proto-oncogene causes familial papillary renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"KIT","B":"RET","C":"VHL","D":"WT1","E":"MET"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The familial form of papillary renal cell carcinoma is caused by gain-of-function mutations in MET, a proto-oncogene on chromosome 7. MET is also mutated in a small proportion of sporadic papillary carcinomas. VHL and WT1 are tumor suppressor genes, and KIT and RET are unrelated receptor tyrosine kinases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC shows MET activation and trisomy 7 with no 3p deletion, whereas clear cell RCC shows VHL loss. COMLEX: papillary carcinomas are frequently multifocal and can be bilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 — molecular drivers that separate clear cell from papillary renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone produced by the tumor accounts for erythrocytosis in some patients with clear cell renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Erythropoietin","B":"Parathyroid hormone","C":"Growth hormone","D":"Antidiuretic hormone","E":"Thyroid hormone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal cell carcinoma is a great mimic and produces several syndromes from abnormal hormone production, including polycythemia. The polycythemia results from ectopic erythropoietin production by the tumor, and First Aid lists ectopic EPO among the paraneoplastic mediators of renal cell carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC is associated with the paraneoplastic mediators PTHrP, ectopic EPO, ACTH, and renin. COMLEX: unexplained erythrocytosis or hypercalcemia in an adult with hematuria or a flank mass should prompt renal imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory abnormality can result from tumor production of PTHrP in renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphatemia","B":"Hypermagnesemia","C":"Hypouricemia","D":"Hypercalcemia","E":"Hyperchloremia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypercalcemia is among the syndromes of abnormal hormone production listed for renal cell carcinoma, and First Aid names PTHrP as the tumor-derived mediator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: paraneoplastic hypercalcemia with low PTH points to PTHrP secretion. COMLEX: RCC can also present with fever, weight loss, and hypertension without any renal symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":1,"stem":"Which benign renal tumor occurs in 25% to 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Papillary adenoma","B":"Metanephric adenoma","C":"Angiomyolipoma","D":"Renal oncocytoma","E":"Medullary fibroma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiomyolipomas consist of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat and are present in 25% to 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis, caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TSC1 or TSC2 tumor suppressor genes. Their clinical importance is largely due to susceptibility to spontaneous hemorrhage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tuberous sclerosis with seizures, intellectual disability, skin lesions, and renal angiomyolipomas. COMLEX: sudden flank pain and shock in a patient with tuberous sclerosis suggests hemorrhage into an angiomyolipoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 2 — paraneoplastic syndromes of renal cell carcinoma and benign renal tumor mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which tumor is the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, usually presenting between ages 2 and 4?","choices":{"A":"Angiomyolipoma","B":"Renal oncocytoma","C":"Chromophobe carcinoma","D":"Nephroblastoma","E":"Clear cell carcinoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nephroblastoma, also called Wilms tumor, is the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, ages 2 to 4, and contains embryonic glomerular structures. Robbins notes that it follows renal cell carcinoma as the second most common malignant renal tumor overall. Renal cell carcinoma is the dominant kidney cancer of adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a child with a large unilateral flank mass, hematuria, and possible hypertension is Wilms tumor until proven otherwise. COMLEX: examine for aniridia, hemihypertrophy, and genitourinary anomalies that signal a syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of which tumor suppressor gene, located on chromosome 11, causes Wilms tumor in the WAGR complex?","choices":{"A":"TP53","B":"WT1","C":"RB1","D":"APC","E":"NF2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"WAGR complex consists of Wilms tumor, Aniridia, Genitourinary malformations, and a Range of developmental delays, and is caused by deletion of WT1 on chromosome 11. TP53, RB1, APC, and NF2 are tumor suppressors not linked to this syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: WT1 deletion causes WAGR, and WT1 mutation causes Denys-Drash syndrome. COMLEX: Wilms tumor genes regulate normal renal development.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which syndrome combines Wilms tumor with macroglossia, organomegaly, and omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","B":"Denys-Drash syndrome","C":"Tuberous sclerosis complex","D":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis syndrome","E":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome features Wilms tumor, organomegaly, macroglossia, hemihyperplasia, and omphalocele, from an imprinting defect causing genetic overexpression, which First Aid associates with WT2 mutation. Denys-Drash syndrome combines Wilms tumor with diffuse mesangial sclerosis and gonadal dysgenesis, and WAGR complex features aniridia and genitourinary malformations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macroglossia plus omphalocele in a newborn suggests Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. COMLEX: recognize macroglossia and omphalocele at birth as a syndrome that carries a Wilms tumor risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes linked to pediatric renal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man who smokes cigarettes has intermittent gross hematuria. CT shows a 9-cm mass in the upper pole of the right kidney. Nephrectomy reveals a yellow-gray, well-circumscribed spherical tumor with foci of hemorrhage and necrosis. Histology shows nests of cells with abundant clear cytoplasm and delicate branching vasculature, and cytogenetics reveal deletion of one copy of a gene on chromosome 3p. Which of the following best explains the tumor's growth advantage and rich blood supply?","choices":{"A":"Activated hepatocyte growth factor receptor signaling through MET","B":"Overexpressed TFE3 transcription factor after an Xp11 translocation","C":"Sustained HIF activity with VEGF and IGF-1 expression","D":"Impaired folliculin function in a tumor suppressor pathway","E":"Loss of fumarate hydratase with disrupted Krebs cycle activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Clear cell carcinoma is defined by deletion of one copy of chromosome 3p25.3, the site of the VHL gene, and the second copy is disabled by mutation or hypermethylation in up to 80% of cases. Loss of VHL leaves HIF levels high even in normoxia, driving expression of VEGF, which promotes angiogenesis, and IGF-1, which stimulates growth. MET drives papillary carcinoma, TFE3 translocation defines a distinct subtype, and folliculin and fumarate hydratase loss underlie separate hereditary syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC in a smoker with a 3p deletion is clear cell carcinoma with VHL loss and HIF stabilization. COMLEX: smoking doubles the incidence of renal cell carcinoma, and the tumor is often silent until it exceeds 10 cm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — downstream systemic consequences of clear cell renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man whose father and paternal uncle had kidney cancer has multiple bilateral renal masses on CT. Biopsy of one lesion shows cuboidal cells in papillary formations with interstitial foam histiocytes in the papillary cores and low nuclear grade. Which of the following is the most likely genetic alteration?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic VHL loss with deletion of 3p","B":"Activating MET mutation with trisomy 7","C":"TFE3 overexpression with an Xp11 translocation","D":"Loss of TSC1 with unchecked mTOR signaling","E":"Multiple chromosome losses with extreme hypodiploidy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary papillary carcinoma is autosomal dominant and shows multiple bilateral tumors with papillary histology and usually low nuclear grade. It is caused by gain-of-function mutations in MET on chromosome 7, and trisomy 7 is the most common cytogenetic abnormality of the familial form. VHL loss with 3p deletion characterizes clear cell carcinoma, and extreme hypodiploidy characterizes chromophobe carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC equals MET, trisomy 7 and 17, and foamy macrophages in papillary cores, whereas clear cell RCC equals VHL and 3p deletion. COMLEX: papillary carcinomas account for 10% to 15% of renal cancers and are often multifocal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — MET activation in papillary carcinoma versus VHL loss in clear cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Cell Carcinoma Oncogenics","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has multiple renal cysts and a known germline heterozygous loss-of-function VHL mutation. Over the next decade he develops multiple clear cell carcinomas in both kidneys. Which of the following best explains the development of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"Somatic loss or silencing of the remaining VHL allele","B":"Gain-of-function change in the remaining VHL allele","C":"Somatic activating mutation of MET in each kidney","D":"Germline deletion of one HIF allele with reduced VEGF","E":"Loss of the Y chromosome in renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"VHL acts as a tumor suppressor gene. Individuals with VHL syndrome inherit one defective copy, and tumors develop when the second copy is disabled by an acquired somatic loss-of-function mutation or silenced by hypermethylation, the same event seen in up to 80% of sporadic clear cell cancers. One-half to two-thirds of patients develop renal cysts and bilateral, often multiple, carcinomas. MET activation and Y loss belong to papillary carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumor suppressor syndromes require loss of both alleles, the two-hit model. COMLEX: familial variants account for only 4% of renal cancers but have identified genes central to sporadic carcinogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — the two-hit model of an inherited germline mutation plus a somatic second hit, as in ADPKD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man who smokes has intermittent gross hematuria, a dull right flank ache, and a 10-kg weight loss. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 19.2 g/dL and serum calcium 12.4 mg/dL. CT shows a large solitary right renal mass with a tumor thrombus extending through the renal vein into the inferior vena cava. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","B":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","C":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","D":"Renal oncocytoma","E":"Urothelial carcinoma of the pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clear cell carcinoma usually occurs as a solitary unilateral lesion, and one of its striking characteristics is a tendency to invade the renal vein and grow as a solid column of cells up the inferior vena cava. Polycythemia and hypercalcemia are among its paraneoplastic syndromes. Papillary carcinomas tend to be multifocal, oncocytoma is benign, and pelvic urothelial carcinoma arises from the pelvic lining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCC with erythrocytosis and hypercalcemia is a paraneoplastic pattern from EPO and PTHrP. COMLEX: with renal vein invasion or extension into perinephric fat, 5-year survival falls to approximately 60%.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — flank pain and hematuria compared across renal masses, stones, and obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with epilepsy, intellectual disability, and facial skin lesions develops sudden severe left flank pain and lightheadedness. Blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg and heart rate is 122/min. CT shows a large perinephric hematoma surrounding a fat-containing left renal mass. Which of the following is the most likely underlying lesion?","choices":{"A":"Renal oncocytoma","B":"Papillary adenoma","C":"Collecting duct carcinoma","D":"Angiomyolipoma","E":"Clear cell carcinoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Angiomyolipomas are benign tumors of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat that occur in 25% to 50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis, a disease with cerebral cortical lesions causing epilepsy and intellectual disability and skin abnormalities. Their clinical importance is largely due to susceptibility to spontaneous hemorrhage, which explains the retroperitoneal bleeding and shock. Macroscopic fat is the defining component of angiomyolipoma, whereas the lipid in clear cell carcinoma is intracellular and does not form a fat-density mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tuberous sclerosis (TSC1 or TSC2) with renal angiomyolipomas that can bleed. COMLEX: spontaneous retroperitoneal hemorrhage in this setting is a surgical or interventional emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 3 — sudden severe flank pain and how its cause is separated from colic and obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Clinical Profiles, Paraneoplastics & Benign Mimics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has an incidental 6-cm mass in the kidney found on CT for abdominal pain. The nephrectomy specimen is a tan-to-mahogany-brown, well-circumscribed, homogeneous tumor with a central scar. Histology shows sheets of cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, small round nuclei with conspicuous nucleoli, and no perinuclear halos or prominent cell membranes. Electron microscopy shows numerous mitochondria. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Chromophobe carcinoma","B":"Clear cell carcinoma","C":"Papillary adenoma","D":"Angiomyolipoma","E":"Renal oncocytoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal oncocytoma is an epithelial neoplasm of eosinophilic cells with small, round, benign-appearing nuclei and numerous mitochondria, thought to arise from intercalated cells of collecting ducts. Grossly it is tan or mahogany brown and well circumscribed, with a central scar in one-third of cases, and it accounts for about 5% of renal neoplasms. Chromophobe carcinoma shows prominent cell membranes and perinuclear halos, although histologic distinction can be difficult.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mahogany brown mass with central scar and mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cells is oncocytoma. COMLEX: because it can mimic chromophobe carcinoma, resection is usually needed to exclude malignancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — how renal carcinoma subtypes are separated by histology and cytogenetics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought in with a large, palpable right flank mass and blood pressure above the 95th percentile. Examination shows absent irises bilaterally, an undescended left testis, and delayed developmental milestones. Which of the following genetic alterations most likely underlies his tumor?","choices":{"A":"Germline deletion of the WT1 gene","B":"Germline loss of the VHL gene","C":"Germline loss of the TSC2 gene","D":"Germline loss of the folliculin gene","E":"Germline activation of the MET gene"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The triad of aniridia, genitourinary malformations such as an undescended testis, and developmental delay with a renal mass in a child is WAGR complex, caused by deletion of WT1 on chromosome 11. Wilms tumor presents most often as a large, palpable unilateral flank mass with hematuria and possible hypertension. VHL, TSC2, folliculin, and MET alterations cause adult-type or unrelated renal syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aniridia plus a Wilms tumor points to WAGR and WT1 deletion. COMLEX: recognize the syndromic features, because a genetic syndrome changes the evaluation of a child with a renal mass.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — WT1 makes the metanephric mesenchyme competent to signal the ureteric bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-month-old child with ambiguous genitalia and gonadal dysgenesis has developed edema and heavy proteinuria. Renal biopsy shows diffuse mesangial sclerosis. Two months later an abdominal mass is found, and biopsy shows tumor containing primitive glomerular structures. Which of the following syndromes is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","B":"WAGR complex","C":"Alport syndrome","D":"Denys-Drash syndrome","E":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Denys-Drash syndrome combines Wilms tumor, diffuse mesangial sclerosis presenting as early-onset nephrotic syndrome, and gonadal dysgenesis, and it is caused by WT1 mutation. Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome features organomegaly, macroglossia, and omphalocele, and WAGR complex features aniridia. Alport syndrome and VHL syndrome do not carry a Wilms tumor risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wilms tumor with early nephrotic syndrome from mesangial sclerosis and gonadal dysgenesis is Denys-Drash, a WT1 mutation. COMLEX: nephrotic syndrome in an infant with abnormal genitalia warrants renal imaging and genetic evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 1 — WT1 signaling in induction of the metanephric blastema","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Pediatric Nephroblastoma Genetics","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has a markedly enlarged tongue, bowel herniated through the abdominal wall at the umbilicus, and enlargement of the right arm and leg compared with the left. Ultrasonography shows enlarged kidneys and liver. The child is at increased risk for a renal malignancy. Which of the following underlying mechanisms is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Contiguous deletion of chromosome 11 including WT1","B":"Germline missense mutation of WT1","C":"Imprinting defect causing gene overexpression","D":"Germline loss of function mutation of VHL","E":"Germline loss of function mutation of TSC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Macroglossia, omphalocele, organomegaly, and hemihyperplasia define Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, caused by an imprinting defect causing genetic overexpression, which First Aid associates with WT2 mutation, and it carries a risk of Wilms tumor. Contiguous chromosome 11 deletion with WT1 loss is WAGR complex, and WT1 mutation causes Denys-Drash syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beckwith-Wiedemann equals imprinting defect, macroglossia, omphalocele, hemihyperplasia, and Wilms tumor risk. COMLEX: hemihyperplasia and an abdominal wall herniation on newborn examination should prompt consideration of this syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome among the germline alterations that predispose to Wilms tumor","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":40,"title":"SDL 40 — Testicular & Penile Pathology, Vascular Emergencies & Neoplasms","questions":[{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which malformation places the urethral opening on the dorsal surface of the penis?","choices":{"A":"Hypospadias","B":"Phimosis","C":"Balanoposthitis","D":"Epispadias","E":"Peyronie disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Malformation of the urethral groove and canal creates an abnormal opening on the ventral surface of the penis in hypospadias or on the dorsal surface in epispadias. Hypospadias is the more common of the two and occurs in approximately 1 in 300 live male births. Phimosis is a small preputial orifice, balanoposthitis is infection of the glans and prepuce, and Peyronie disease is a fibrous penile plaque.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypospadias is ventral and more common, whereas epispadias is dorsal. COMLEX: both can accompany failure of testicular descent and urinary tract malformations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which complication is a recognized consequence of the often constricted abnormal urethral opening in hypospadias?","choices":{"A":"Cavernosal fibrosis with painful curvature","B":"Preputial scarring with balanoposthitis","C":"Testicular twisting with hemorrhagic infarction","D":"Penile plaque formation with squamous dysplasia","E":"Urinary obstruction with ascending infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The abnormal opening in hypospadias is often constricted, producing urinary tract obstruction and an increased risk of ascending infections. When the orifice lies near the base of the penis, normal ejaculation and insemination are hampered and may cause sterility. Cavernosal fibrosis describes Peyronie disease, and preputial scarring describes phimosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypospadias and epispadias can be associated with cryptorchidism and other urinary tract malformations. COMLEX: even isolated urethral defects carry clinical significance through obstruction and infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":1,"stem":"Which HPV type is most commonly associated with HPV-related penile intraepithelial neoplasia such as Bowen disease?","choices":{"A":"HPV 6","B":"HPV 16","C":"HPV 11","D":"HPV 1","E":"HPV 2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HPV-associated penile intraepithelial neoplasia, seen clinically as Bowen disease and bowenoid papulosis, is associated with high-risk HPV, most commonly HPV 16. High-risk types encode E6 and E7, which inactivate p53 and RB. Low-risk HPV 6 and, less often, HPV 11 cause condyloma acuminatum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high-risk HPV drives dysplasia and carcinoma, whereas HPV 6 and 11 cause benign genital warts. COMLEX: circumcision confers protection against penile cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 1 — congenital urethral malformations and HPV-associated precancerous lesions of the penis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Testicular torsion in adolescents and adults results from which anatomic defect?","choices":{"A":"Fusion of the two testes","B":"Dilated pampiniform venous plexus","C":"Bell-clapper abnormality","D":"Thickened tunica albuginea","E":"Failure of the inguinal ring to close"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In adults, torsion results from a bilateral anatomic defect that leads to increased mobility of the testes, termed the bell-clapper abnormality. Neonatal torsion occurs without an associated anatomic defect. Fusion of the testes is synorchism, a rare anomaly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bell-clapper deformity predisposes to torsion, presenting with sudden testicular pain, often without an inciting injury. COMLEX: torsion is one of the few true urologic emergencies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"What is the gross appearance of a testis in advanced torsion?","choices":{"A":"Swollen dark hemorrhagic and necrotic testis","B":"Firm gray-white lobulated mass without necrosis","C":"Small firm testis with hyalinized tubular cords","D":"Golden brown circumscribed nodule under 5 cm","E":"Enlarged pale testis with diffuse abscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Twisting of the spermatic cord cuts off venous drainage while the thick-walled arteries remain patent, producing intense vascular engorgement and then hemorrhagic infarction. In advanced stages the swollen testis consists entirely of soft, necrotic, hemorrhagic tissue. A homogeneous gray-white lobulated mass suggests seminoma, a small firm testis with hyalinized tubules suggests cryptorchid atrophy, and a golden brown nodule suggests a Leydig cell tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: venous outflow is blocked first, giving hemorrhagic rather than pale infarction. COMLEX: the dark discoloration reflects hemorrhage and infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":1,"stem":"Within approximately how long after the onset of torsion can manual untwisting spare the testis from orchiectomy?","choices":{"A":"About 12 hours","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 24 hours","D":"About 48 hours","E":"About 72 hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"If the testis is manually untwisted within approximately 6 hours of the onset of torsion, the affected testis may be spared an orchiectomy. Longer delays allow hemorrhagic infarction to progress toward irreversible necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: torsion is a surgical emergency with a salvage window of about 6 hours. COMLEX: First Aid lists absent cremasteric reflex and a high-riding testis as bedside findings, and orchiopexy is done bilaterally.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — torsion and cryptorchidism are contrasted by their mechanism of testicular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular neoplasm is characterized by Schiller-Duval bodies?","choices":{"A":"Yolk sac tumor","B":"Embryonal carcinoma","C":"Choriocarcinoma","D":"Seminoma","E":"Teratoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In approximately 50% of yolk sac tumors, structures resembling endodermal sinuses, called Schiller-Duval bodies, are seen. They consist of a mesodermal core with a central capillary and visceral and parietal layers of cells resembling primitive glomeruli. Yolk sac tumor cells express AFP, glypican-3, and cytokeratin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schiller-Duval bodies and AFP mark yolk sac tumor, the most common testicular tumor in children up to 3 years. COMLEX: serum AFP is used to diagnose and monitor these tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ cell tumor consists of syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts and produces markedly elevated serum hCG?","choices":{"A":"Embryonal carcinoma","B":"Yolk sac tumor","C":"Choriocarcinoma","D":"Seminoma","E":"Spermatocytic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choriocarcinoma is composed of two intimately juxtaposed cell types, syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts, and serum hCG is invariably elevated, often markedly. Hemorrhage and necrosis are extremely common, and widespread metastasis occurs early. About 15% of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts, but hCG is elevated only minimally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: choriocarcinoma equals hCG, hemorrhagic primary, and early hematogenous spread. COMLEX: pure choriocarcinoma has a poor prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic pattern characterizes classic seminoma?","choices":{"A":"Lace-like network with Schiller-Duval bodies","B":"Syncytiotrophoblasts admixed with cytotrophoblasts","C":"Anaplastic cells in alveolar or tubular patterns","D":"Clear glycogen-rich cells with lymphocytic septa","E":"Cartilage, neural tissue, and squamous epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Seminoma is composed of sheets of uniform round to polyhedral cells with distinct membranes, clear cytoplasm containing glycogen, and large central nuclei with prominent nucleoli, divided into lobules by delicate fibrous septa with a lymphocytic infiltrate. Lace-like reticular growth with Schiller-Duval bodies describes yolk sac tumor, trophoblastic cells describe choriocarcinoma, alveolar or tubular anaplastic cells describe embryonal carcinoma, and multiple germ layer tissues describe teratoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma is the most common germ cell tumor, radiosensitive, with an excellent prognosis and a gray-white homogeneous cut surface. COMLEX: seminoma cells are typically positive for KIT and OCT3/4 and negative for cytokeratin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — germ cell tumor subtypes are separated by their histology and by their characteristic serum markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old uncircumcised man has a solitary, thickened, gray-white plaque on the penile shaft that has been present for 8 months. Biopsy shows dysplastic squamous cells with large hyperchromatic nuclei, lack of orderly maturation, and numerous mitoses, all confined to the epidermis above an intact basement membrane. In situ hybridization detects high-risk HPV 16. Which of the following best explains how this lesion can progress to invasive carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Loss of VHL with stabilized HIF signaling","B":"Isochromosome 12p with KIT activation","C":"Viral E6 and E7 inactivation of p53 and RB","D":"Loss of fumarate hydratase with Krebs cycle disruption","E":"Gain-of-function MET activation by hepatocyte growth factor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is Bowen disease, a form of HPV-associated penile intraepithelial neoplasia, which gives rise to infiltrating squamous cell carcinoma in 10% of patients. High-risk HPV encodes E6 and E7, which inactivate the p53 and RB tumor suppressor proteins, leading to genomic instability and increased proliferation, the same role played in cervical cancer. The other alterations belong to renal or germ cell tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high-risk HPV E6 and E7 inactivate p53 and RB, causing genomic instability and proliferation. COMLEX: Bowen disease on the shaft appears as a solitary plaque, whereas on the glans it looks velvety red.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — carcinoma in situ progressing to invasive carcinoma in the lower urinary tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old sexually active man has multiple reddish-brown papules on the penile shaft for 3 months. Biopsy shows dysplastic squamous cells resembling carcinoma in situ, and high-risk HPV 16 is detected. At 6-month follow-up, several lesions have regressed spontaneously. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Bowen disease","B":"Condyloma acuminatum","C":"Verrucous carcinoma","D":"Basaloid carcinoma","E":"Bowenoid papulosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bowenoid papulosis occurs in sexually active adults and differs from Bowen disease by younger patient age and multiple rather than solitary reddish-brown papules. It is etiologically related to HPV 16 and histologically indistinguishable from Bowen disease, but it virtually never develops into invasive carcinoma and usually regresses spontaneously. Condyloma acuminatum, caused by HPV 6 and 11, keeps orderly maturation, and verrucous carcinoma is an HPV-independent well-differentiated subtype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple papules in a young adult with HPV 16 and spontaneous regression suggest bowenoid papulosis, while a solitary plaque in an older man suggests Bowen disease. COMLEX: both are HPV-associated precursor lesions of the penile intraepithelial neoplasia group.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — HPV-associated penile epithelial lesions and the clinical and histologic features that separate them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Penile Malformations & Epithelial Malignancies","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man reports 6 months of penile curvature to the left during erection and pain with intercourse. Examination reveals a firm palpable induration along the left side of the penile shaft, and the glans and foreskin are normal. Which of the following best describes the underlying lesion?","choices":{"A":"Squamous dysplasia above an intact basement membrane","B":"Collagen plaque between cavernosa and tunica albuginea","C":"Papillary epithelium with perinuclear vacuolization","D":"Candida infection of the glans and prepuce","E":"Scarring of the preputial ring after repeated infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peyronie disease is characterized by hard penile plaques from collagen deposition in the connective tissue between the corpora cavernosa and the tunica albuginea. It is reactive rather than neoplastic, and clinically causes penile curvature toward the side of the lesion and pain during intercourse. Squamous dysplasia describes penile intraepithelial neoplasia, koilocytosis describes condyloma, Candida causes balanoposthitis, and preputial scarring describes phimosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyronie disease results from microvascular trauma with sclerosing chronic inflammation, giving curvature toward the plaque. COMLEX: Robbins lists surgery and collagenase injection to lyse the fibrous plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — erectile physiology and the NO-cGMP pathway relevant to penile function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy is awakened from sleep by sudden severe left scrotal pain with vomiting. There was no trauma. The left testis is high-riding with a horizontal lie, and the cremasteric reflex is absent on the left. If the condition is not corrected, which of the following processes best explains the tissue damage that will develop?","choices":{"A":"Arterial occlusion with pale coagulative infarction","B":"Ascending bacterial infection with abscess formation","C":"Lymphatic obstruction with interstitial edema","D":"Venous occlusion with hemorrhagic infarction","E":"Immune vasculitis with fibrinoid necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Twisting of the spermatic cord typically cuts off venous drainage while the thick-walled arteries remain patent, producing intense vascular engorgement followed by hemorrhagic infarction. Torsion is one of the few true urologic emergencies, and adolescent torsion usually reflects the bilateral bell-clapper abnormality with increased testicular mobility. Infarction changes progress from congestion to widespread hemorrhage with duration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute severe scrotal pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex is torsion, and the treatment is immediate surgical exploration. COMLEX: manual untwisting within approximately 6 hours may spare the testis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — torsion as a time-critical testicular vascular emergency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old boy has an empty right hemiscrotum, and a testis is palpable in the right inguinal canal. He undergoes orchiopexy. His mother asks why the cancer risk remains higher after surgery and is also higher for his normally descended left testis. Which of the following best answers her question?","choices":{"A":"An intrinsic in utero defect in gonadal development","B":"Chronic heat injury that persists after repositioning","C":"Androgen excess from prominent Leydig cells","D":"Scarring of the inguinal canal after surgery","E":"Ascending infection along the spermatic cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cryptorchid testis carries a four- to 10-fold higher risk for testicular cancer, and repositioning does not eliminate the elevated risk. Because the contralateral, normally descended testis is also at higher risk, cryptorchidism and the associated risk for germ cell neoplasm is believed to be linked to an in utero defect in gonadal cell development, as in testicular dysgenesis syndrome, and not to the abnormal anatomic position.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cryptorchidism raises germ cell tumor risk, and medical therapy with orchiopexy reduces but does not eliminate the risk of cancer and sterility. COMLEX: histologic changes in a malpositioned testis begin as early as 2 years of age, so early repair is recommended.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — germ cell neoplasia in situ as the precursor lesion of testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Testicular Vascular Emergencies & Developmental Atrophy","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man evaluated for infertility has a history of untreated bilateral cryptorchidism. Testicular biopsy shows thickened tubular basement membranes, hyalinized tubular cords, and prominent interstitial cells. Which of the following cell populations is characteristically lost from the seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cells","B":"Spermatogonia","C":"Leydig cells","D":"Stromal cells","E":"Neoplastic germ cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the cryptorchid testis the basement membrane of the spermatic tubules thickens early, and subsequent loss of spermatogonia leaves tubules with only Sertoli cells. Scarred tubules may appear as dense cords of hyaline connective tissue with a concomitant increase in interstitial stroma. Leydig cells are spared and appear relatively prominent, which explains the interstitial cells on biopsy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cryptorchid atrophy features Sertoli-only tubules, thickened basement membranes, and spared Leydig cells. COMLEX: bilateral disease causes sterility, and repair does not fully restore spermatogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — primary testicular failure with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, distinguished from secondary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has painless enlargement of the right testis. Radical orchiectomy specimen shows a bulky, homogeneous, gray-white lobulated mass without hemorrhage or necrosis. Histology shows sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm in lobules separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes, and no other germ cell component is identified. Preoperative serum AFP is normal and hCG is mildly elevated. Which of the following best explains the hCG elevation?","choices":{"A":"Syncytiotrophoblasts within the tumor","B":"Yolk sac elements secreting hCG","C":"Hyaline globules that contain hCG","D":"Leydig cell hyperplasia that secretes hCG","E":"Teratoma elements secreting hCG"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is a seminoma, and approximately 15% of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts. In this subset serum hCG is elevated, but not to the extent seen in choriocarcinoma. Yolk sac tumors produce AFP, and the eosinophilic hyaline globules of yolk sac tumor contain AFP and alpha-1-antitrypsin, not hCG.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma may show mildly elevated hCG from syncytiotrophoblasts with a normal AFP. COMLEX: therapy and prognosis depend on stage and histologic type, and seminoma is radiosensitive and chemosensitive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — serum tumor markers and the histologic components that produce them in germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has a painless left scrotal mass. Radical orchiectomy shows a lace-like network of cuboidal cells with structures that have a mesodermal core, a central capillary, and surrounding cells resembling primitive glomeruli. Which of the following serum markers is most likely to be markedly elevated?","choices":{"A":"hCG","B":"PSA","C":"AFP","D":"CEA","E":"CA 125"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lace-like reticular pattern with Schiller-Duval bodies identifies a yolk sac tumor, the most common testicular tumor in infants and children up to 3 years. Eosinophilic hyaline globules containing AFP are typical, and marked elevation of serum AFP is produced by yolk sac tumor. Marked hCG elevation reflects syncytiotrophoblastic elements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFP marks yolk sac tumor and hCG marks choriocarcinoma. COMLEX: after orchiectomy, persistent AFP or hCG elevation indicates metastatic spread.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — histologic patterns that distinguish the non-seminomatous germ cell tumor subtypes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Testicular Germ Cell Neoplasms","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man underwent right radical orchiectomy for a mixed germ cell tumor with embryonal carcinoma and yolk sac elements. Preoperative serum AFP was markedly elevated. Eight weeks after surgery, repeat serum AFP measurements remain persistently elevated. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"A normal postoperative inflammatory response","B":"Normal AFP production by the remaining testis","C":"Production by a new seminoma in the left testis","D":"Metastatic spread beyond the testis","E":"Persistent effect of testicular dysgenesis syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the staging of testicular germ cell tumors, persistent elevation of hCG or AFP after orchiectomy indicates metastatic spread. Markedly elevated AFP or hCG is produced by yolk sac tumor and syncytiotrophoblastic elements, respectively, and both are elevated in more than 80% of nonseminomatous germ cell tumors at diagnosis. Retroperitoneal paraaortic nodes are generally the first involved, and nonseminomatous tumors tend to metastasize earlier and more often by the hematogenous route.","boardPrep":"Step 1: serum markers are used to evaluate a testicular mass, stage the tumor, assess burden, and monitor therapy. COMLEX: about 90% of patients with nonseminomatous tumors achieve complete remission with aggressive chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — serum tumor markers as tools for staging and monitoring germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":41,"title":"SDL 41 — Prostatic Diseases: Inflammatory, Hyperplastic & Malignant","questions":[{"id":"sdl41_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"In which region of the prostate does benign prostatic hyperplasia usually arise?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral zone","B":"Central zone","C":"Fibromuscular zone","D":"Transition zone","E":"Bladder trigone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins divides the prostate into peripheral, central, transition, and periurethral zones. Most hyperplasias arise in the transition zone, and the resulting nodules compress the urethra into a slit-like orifice and produce urinary obstruction. Most carcinomas instead originate in the peripheral zone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH is hyperplasia, not hypertrophy, of the transition zone, and it is not premalignant. COMLEX: the transition zone lies around the urethra, so enlargement causes obstructive voiding symptoms rather than a palpable posterior nodule.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"In benign prostatic hyperplasia, which cell population expresses the enzyme that generates dihydrotestosterone from testosterone in the prostate?","choices":{"A":"Prostatic secretory cells","B":"Prostatic basal cells","C":"Prostatic stromal cells","D":"Ejaculatory duct cells","E":"Prostatic neuroendocrine cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DHT is the main androgen in the prostate and is formed from testosterone by type 2 5-alpha-reductase. Robbins states that this enzyme is expressed primarily in stromal cells and is not expressed in prostatic epithelial cells. DHT then binds androgen receptors in stromal and epithelial cells and upregulates growth factors such as FGF and TGF-beta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors such as finasteride shrink the gland by decreasing DHT synthesis. COMLEX: the stromal location of the enzyme explains why DHT signaling drives stromal proliferation in BPH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which form of prostatitis occurs most commonly?","choices":{"A":"Acute prostatitis with fever and chills","B":"Chronic prostatitis with negative cultures","C":"Chronic prostatitis with recurrent same-organism UTIs","D":"Granulomatous prostatitis after BCG instillation","E":"Fungal granulomatous prostatitis in immunocompromised hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic abacterial prostatitis, often called chronic pelvic pain syndrome, is the most common form. It resembles chronic bacterial prostatitis in symptoms, but there is no history of recurrent urinary tract infection, expressed prostatic secretions contain more than 10 leukocytes per high-power field, and cultures are uniformly negative. The other forms are less frequent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostatitis presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and low back pain, and chronic prostatitis may be bacterial or nonbacterial. COMLEX: expressed prostatic secretions with leukocytes but sterile cultures point to the abacterial form.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — inflammatory and hyperplastic disorders of the prostate arise from distinct mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature is present in benign prostatic glands but missing from malignant glands?","choices":{"A":"Prominent nucleoli","B":"Perineural invasion","C":"Tightly packed cells","D":"Basal cell layer","E":"Amphophilic cytoplasm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benign prostatic glands have a basal cell layer beneath the secretory layer, and this layer is absent in adenocarcinoma. Immunohistochemical markers that stain basal cells can bring out the distinction. Malignant glands are also smaller, lined by a single uniform layer, tightly packed, without branching or papillary infoldings, and have enlarged nuclei with large nucleoli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of the basal cell layer is the key histologic marker of invasive prostate cancer, and AMACR is upregulated in most cases. COMLEX: high-grade PIN retains at least a partial basal layer and an intact basement membrane.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which alteration is the most common genetic event in prostate cancer?","choices":{"A":"BCR-ABL1 gene fusion","B":"COL1A1-PDGFB gene fusion","C":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene fusion","D":"PAX3-FOXO1 gene fusion","E":"EWSR1-FLI1 gene fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common genetic alteration in prostate cancer is a chromosomal rearrangement that places an ETS family gene, most commonly ERG or ETV1, next to the androgen-regulated TMPRSS2 promoter. This occurs in about half of prostate cancers and causes androgen-dependent overexpression of the ETS factor. The other fusions belong to other neoplasms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the androgen-regulated promoter explains why androgen deprivation therapy lowers expression of the driver. COMLEX: a firm posterior nodule on rectal examination or a raised PSA prompts needle biopsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":1,"stem":"Which lesion is considered a precursor of prostatic adenocarcinoma and shares its predominance in the peripheral zone?","choices":{"A":"High-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia","B":"Nodular hyperplasia of the periurethral and transition zones","C":"Nonspecific granulomatous prostatitis from ruptured ducts","D":"Prostatic infarct with adjacent squamous metaplasia","E":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis with leukocytes in secretions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"High-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia (HGPIN) is the proposed precursor. Both HGPIN and cancer predominate in the peripheral zone, HGPIN is found in about 80 percent of prostates removed for carcinoma, and many molecular changes of invasive cancer are also present in it. HGPIN glands retain at least a partial basal cell layer and have an intact basement membrane, unlike malignant glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PIN is architecturally benign acini lined by atypical cells with prominent nucleoli. COMLEX: BPH is not premalignant, which distinguishes it from HGPIN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — benign hyperplasia, adenocarcinoma, and its precursor lesions are separated by histologic and molecular features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, how is the combined Gleason score derived for a prostate tumor showing two distinct architectural arrangements of glands?","choices":{"A":"Highest grade seen in any single core","B":"Average of the pattern grades across cores","C":"Product of primary and secondary grades","D":"Proportion of tumor lacking gland formation","E":"Sum of primary and secondary pattern grades"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most tumors contain more than one pattern. A primary grade is assigned to the dominant pattern and a secondary grade to the second most frequent pattern, and the two numeric grades are added to give the combined Gleason score. A tumor with a single pattern is treated as having identical primary and secondary grades, so the number is doubled, and, according to Robbins, the score therefore ranges from 2 to 10.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gleason grade is based on glandular architecture and correlates closely with metastatic potential. COMLEX: scores are now collapsed into five grade groups that each carry a different prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"Which growth pattern characterizes the highest Gleason grade, grade 5?","choices":{"A":"Solid sheets and nests of infiltrating cells","B":"Uniform round glands in well-circumscribed nodules","C":"Individual discrete well-formed glands","D":"Branching glands with papillary infoldings","E":"Well-formed glands with lesser fused components"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In grade 5 tumors the cells infiltrate the stroma in cords, sheets, and solid nests, with no identifiable gland formation. Grade 1 is the opposite extreme, with uniform round glands packed into well-circumscribed nodules. Branching glands with papillary infoldings are a feature of benign glands, and the other options describe well-formed or mixed gland patterns of lower grade groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: higher grade means loss of gland formation and worse prognosis. COMLEX: Gleason scores of 8 through 10 tend to be advanced cancers that are less likely to be cured.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which skeletal site is most frequently involved by metastatic prostatic carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Proximal femur","B":"Lumbar spine","C":"Thoracic spine","D":"Pelvic bones","E":"Rib cage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hematogenous spread of prostate cancer occurs chiefly to bone, particularly the axial skeleton, and the metastases are typically osteoblastic. Robbins lists the most commonly involved bones in descending order as lumbar spine, proximal femur, pelvis, thoracic spine, and ribs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoblastic metastases in a male strongly suggest a prostatic primary, and spinal spread is often through the Batson vertebral venous plexus. COMLEX: low back pain with raised alkaline phosphatase and PSA suggests skeletal involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 3 — Gleason grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors, and prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to bone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has had 2 days of fever, chills, and dysuria. One week ago he underwent cystoscopy. His temperature is 39.2 C. Rectal examination shows an exquisitely tender, boggy prostate. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to establish the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Prostate needle biopsy","B":"Serum PSA level","C":"Repeat cystoscopy","D":"Urine culture","E":"Semen analysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, chills, dysuria, and an exquisitely tender, boggy prostate after urethral instrumentation indicate acute bacterial prostatitis, usually from E. coli, other gram-negative rods, enterococci, or staphylococci reaching the gland by reflux of contaminated urine. Robbins states the diagnosis is established by urine culture and clinical features. Biopsy of a gland with suspected acute prostatitis is contraindicated because it may lead to sepsis, and PSA is also raised by prostatitis so it does not confirm the cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli is the most common cause of acute bacterial prostatitis in older men, and in young men consider Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. COMLEX: avoid needle biopsy and vigorous manipulation of an acutely inflamed prostate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — ascending infection of the lower urinary tract through reflux or stasis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has nocturia, hesitancy, and a weak urinary stream. Rectal examination shows a smoothly enlarged, firm, nontender prostate. After 6 months of a new medication, his symptoms improve and ultrasonography shows a decrease in prostate volume. Which mechanism best explains the reduction in gland size?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of prostatic smooth muscle by alpha-1 blockade","B":"Blockade of androgen receptors in prostatic epithelium","C":"Blockade of estrogen receptors on prostatic stromal cells","D":"Inhibition of bacterial growth within residual urine","E":"Blocked testosterone conversion to dihydrotestosterone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The presentation is benign prostatic hyperplasia, in which DHT-driven growth factors increase stromal cell proliferation and decrease epithelial cell death. Robbins notes that 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors physically shrink the prostate by decreasing DHT synthesis. Alpha-adrenergic blockers relieve symptoms by lowering prostatic smooth muscle tone but do not reduce gland volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, and alpha-1 antagonists such as tamsulosin relax smooth muscle. COMLEX: BPH is not premalignant, and residual urine predisposes to infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride actions on DHT and PSA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Prostatitis & BPH Pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has had nocturia, difficulty starting his stream, and overflow dribbling for several years, with no episodes of acute retention. Ultrasonography shows a large post-void residual volume and a thickened bladder wall. Which change is most likely present in his bladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy with trabeculation","B":"Thinning of the muscular wall with smooth mucosa","C":"Transmural fibrosis producing a contracted bladder","D":"Yellow mucosal plaques of foamy macrophages","E":"Multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased resistance to urinary outflow from prostatic enlargement leads to bladder wall smooth muscle hypertrophy and distention with retained urine. As hypertrophy progresses the muscle bundles enlarge and produce trabeculation, and over time crypts form and may become diverticula. Marked thinning of the wall without trabeculae is described in some cases of acute obstruction or terminal disease, when the bladder becomes extremely dilated. The residual urine is a common site of infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH can lead to bladder hypertrophy, hydronephrosis, and urinary tract infections. COMLEX: sudden acute urinary retention in BPH requires emergency catheterization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — obstruction with dilation and progressive parenchymal atrophy of the upper urinary tract","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has an elevated serum PSA and a firm nodule on the posterior surface of the prostate on rectal examination. Needle biopsy confirms adenocarcinoma. Which histologic finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Large glands with papillary infoldings and basal cells","B":"Crowded small glands with absent basal cell layer","C":"Foamy macrophages containing laminated calcified bodies","D":"Noncaseating granulomas surrounding ruptured ducts","E":"Neutrophils filling glands with stromal edema"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most prostatic adenocarcinomas arise in the posterior peripheral zone and can be palpable on rectal examination. The glands are smaller than benign glands, tightly packed, lined by a single layer of cuboidal or low columnar cells, and lack the basal cell layer, with enlarged nuclei and large nucleoli. Large glands with papillary infoldings describe benign hyperplasia, and the granulomatous, histiocytic, and neutrophilic patterns describe inflammatory lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma arises most often in the posterior peripheral zone and is diagnosed by PSA and needle core biopsy. COMLEX: digital rectal examination detects some early carcinomas but has low sensitivity and specificity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — PSA and other secretory products of prostatic epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a transition zone nodule removed during transurethral resection. Histology shows nodules of glands lined by an inner columnar layer and an outer flattened basal layer, with papillary infoldings, separated by bland spindle-shaped stromal cells. Which statement about this lesion is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It usually arises in the peripheral zone","B":"It commonly evolves into high-grade PIN","C":"It carries no premalignant potential","D":"It is defined by absence of basal cells","E":"It is driven by loss of androgen signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The two-layered glands with papillary infoldings and stromal proliferation in the transition zone are benign prostatic hyperplasia, which Robbins states is not a premalignant lesion. It is driven by DHT signaling through androgen receptors, and it arises in the transition zone rather than the peripheral zone. The basal cell layer is retained, whereas its absence marks adenocarcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPH is hyperplasia of the transition zone and is not premalignant. COMLEX: PSA is organ specific but not cancer specific, and BPH, prostatitis, infarction, instrumentation, and ejaculation can all raise it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — DHT dependence of benign prostatic hyperplasia and its pharmacologic inhibition with finasteride","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Prostatic Adenocarcinoma Histopathology","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has prostate adenocarcinoma. Tumor sequencing shows overexpression of an ETS family transcription factor, and this expression falls after androgen deprivation therapy. Which genetic event most likely explains the androgen dependence of this expression?","choices":{"A":"Amplification of the MYC oncogene in tumor cells","B":"Deletion of the PTEN tumor suppressor gene","C":"Germline loss-of-function mutation in BRCA2","D":"Rearrangement placing ERG under the TMPRSS2 promoter","E":"Epigenetic silencing of the GSTP1 gene by methylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most common genetic alteration in prostate cancer is a rearrangement that juxtaposes the coding sequence of an ETS family gene, most commonly ERG or ETV1, with the androgen-regulated TMPRSS2 promoter. This produces androgen-dependent overexpression of the ETS factor, which explains why expression falls when androgens are withdrawn. MYC amplification, PTEN deletion, BRCA2 loss, and GSTP1 silencing are other recurrent alterations but do not place an ETS gene under androgen control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostate cancer cells depend on androgens acting through the androgen receptor, so castration or antiandrogens usually induce regression. COMLEX: most tumors eventually become resistant to androgen blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — androgen receptor binding to androgen response elements drives transcription of androgen-regulated genes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A prostate needle biopsy shows adenocarcinoma composed of predominantly poorly formed, fused, and cribriform glands with a lesser component of well-formed glands. The dominant pattern is grade 4 and the second most frequent pattern is grade 3. Which Gleason score and Grade Group apply?","choices":{"A":"Score 7, Grade Group 2","B":"Score 7, Grade Group 3","C":"Score 8, Grade Group 3","D":"Score 6, Grade Group 1","E":"Score 8, Grade Group 2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The primary grade of 4 and secondary grade of 3 add to a Gleason score of 7. In Robbins Table 21.7 a 4 plus 3 pattern, with predominantly poorly formed, fused, or cribriform glands and a lesser component of well-formed glands, is Grade Group 3. A 3 plus 4 tumor, with predominantly well-formed glands, is Grade Group 2, and a score of 6 or lower is Grade Group 1. A score of 8, reached by 4 plus 4, 3 plus 5, or 5 plus 3, is Grade Group 4, so neither score 8 option is correct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the primary pattern is the dominant one, so 4 plus 3 is worse than 3 plus 4 despite the same total. COMLEX: grade and stage are the most important prognostic factors and are combined to guide management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Gleason patterns are combined into grade groups that carry different prognoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has progressive low back pain. Serum alkaline phosphatase and PSA are elevated, and radiographs show dense sclerotic lesions in several lumbar spinal segments. Which route of spread best explains the pattern of spinal involvement?","choices":{"A":"Obturator and paraaortic lymphatics","B":"Hepatic portal venous drainage","C":"Batson vertebral venous plexus","D":"Direct extension into seminal vesicles","E":"Thoracic duct lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prostate cancer spreads hematogenously chiefly to the axial skeleton, and the metastases are typically osteoblastic, which in a male points strongly to a prostatic origin. First Aid notes that metastasis to the spine often occurs through the Batson vertebral venous plexus. Lymphatic spread goes to obturator and then paraaortic nodes, and local extension involves periprostatic tissue, seminal vesicles, and the bladder base, but none of these routes accounts for vertebral bone lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoblastic bone metastases in a man suggest prostate cancer, and lumbar spine is the most commonly involved site. COMLEX: low back pain with raised alkaline phosphatase and PSA should prompt evaluation for skeletal metastases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — hematogenous spread of prostate cancer to the axial skeleton with osteoblastic lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Gleason Grading & Skeletal Metastases","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with metastatic prostate adenocarcinoma is started on chronic leuprolide. Which mechanism explains its therapeutic effect?","choices":{"A":"Pituitary desensitization suppresses LH and testosterone","B":"Direct inhibition of 5-alpha-reductase in prostate stromal cells","C":"Competitive blockade of androgen receptors on tumor cells","D":"Inhibition of adrenal androgen synthesis by cytochrome blockade","E":"Enhanced inhibin B release that lowers pituitary FSH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Metastatic prostate cancer is treated with androgen deprivation, by orchiectomy or by LHRH analogs. Chronic administration of an LHRH agonist desensitizes pituitary cells expressing LHRH receptors, suppressing release of LH, which is required for testosterone production by Leydig cells. Tumor regression follows because prostate cancer cell growth and survival depend on androgens, although most tumors eventually become resistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates the pituitary whereas continuous GnRH agonist exposure downregulates its receptors. COMLEX: androgen deprivation induces remission, and resistance to testosterone withdrawal heralds disease progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — pulsatile versus continuous GnRH stimulation and pituitary receptor down-regulation by agonists such as leuprolide in prostate cancer therapy","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":42,"title":"SDL 42 — Lower Urinary Tract: Ureter, Bladder & Urothelial Neoplasms","questions":[{"id":"sdl42_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children?","choices":{"A":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","B":"Bifid ureter with duplicated pelvis","C":"Obstructing calcium oxalate calculi","D":"Ureteral diverticulum formation","E":"Extrinsic compression by a pelvic tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children. Cases presenting early in life preferentially affect males and are bilateral in 20 percent of cases. Ureteral duplication and diverticula are congenital anomalies that are often of little clinical significance, and calculi and pelvic tumors are causes of obstruction in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPJ obstruction is usually attributed to abnormal organization of smooth muscle bundles or excess collagen at the junction. COMLEX: in adults, UPJ obstruction is more common in females and is most often unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — obstructive ureteral lesions and inflammatory bladder lesions produce distinct clinical and morphologic pictures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which ureteral disorder features storiform scar tissue encasing the ureters, abundant IgG4-positive plasma cells, and an initial response to corticosteroids?","choices":{"A":"Ureteral duplication with reflux","B":"Primary urothelial carcinoma of the ureter","C":"Congenital ureteral diverticulum","D":"Sclerosing retroperitoneal fibrosis","E":"Obstructing ureteral calculus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sclerosing retroperitoneal fibrosis is a fibrotic proliferative inflammatory process that encases retroperitoneal structures and causes hydronephrosis. Microscopy shows storiform fibrous tissue with lymphocytes, plasma cells that are frequently IgG4-positive, and eosinophils. At least a subset is related to IgG4-related disease, initial treatment is corticosteroids, and resistant cases require stents or ureterolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: retroperitoneal fibrosis is a cause of hydronephrosis, and drug exposures such as ergot derivatives and beta-blockers are among its recognized associations. COMLEX: it occurs in middle to late age and is more common in males.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — obstructive ureteral lesions and inflammatory bladder lesions produce distinct clinical and morphologic pictures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which organism most commonly causes bacterial cystitis?","choices":{"A":"Proteus species","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Klebsiella species","D":"Enterobacter species","E":"Candida albicans"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most common etiologic agents of cystitis are the coliforms, with Escherichia coli first, followed by Proteus, Klebsiella, and Enterobacter. Females are more prone to cystitis because a shorter urethra gives bacteria from the intestinal tract easier access. Candida is a much less common cause, seen mainly in immunosuppressed patients or those on long-term antibiotics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystitis presents with frequency, suprapubic pain, and dysuria. COMLEX: cystitis can be a secondary complication of stasis from prostatic hypertrophy, cystocele, calculi, or bladder tumors, and these lesions must be corrected for it to resolve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 1 — obstructive ureteral lesions and inflammatory bladder lesions produce distinct clinical and morphologic pictures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"Which molecular changes characterize non-muscle-invasive papillary urothelial carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Inactivating TP53 and RB mutations","B":"MET proto-oncogene activation","C":"FGFR3 and RAS gain of function","D":"VHL loss with stabilized HIF-1 alpha","E":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene rearrangement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Non-muscle-invasive papillary cancers often have gain-of-function alterations that increase growth factor receptor signaling, such as amplification of the FGFR3 gene and activating mutations of RAS and PI 3-kinase. These tumors frequently recur but progress to muscle-invasive cancer in only about 20 percent of cases. TP53 and RB inactivation characterizes the flat CIS pathway to muscle-invasive disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary bladder tumors follow the FGFR3 and RAS pathway, whereas flat CIS and muscle-invasive tumors follow the TP53 and RB pathway. COMLEX: FGFR3 is also a target for antibody-drug conjugates in advanced disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"From which lesion do most muscle-invasive bladder cancers develop?","choices":{"A":"Papillary urothelial hyperplasia","B":"Urothelial carcinoma in situ","C":"Exophytic urothelial papilloma","D":"Inverted urothelial papilloma","E":"Cystitis cystica et glandularis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most muscle-invasive bladder cancers develop by progression from flat urothelial carcinoma in situ (CIS), and TP53 and RB mutations occur early in CIS. Papillary urothelial hyperplasia is the origin of the noninvasive papillary tumors, which more rarely progress. Inverted papillomas are completely benign, and cystitis cystica et glandularis is a metaplastic lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CIS is cytologically malignant but confined to the epithelium, with no basement membrane invasion. COMLEX: if untreated, 50 percent to 75 percent of CIS progresses to invasive cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":1,"stem":"A bladder tumor is staged T2. Which layer has it invaded?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Perivesical soft tissue","D":"Prostatic stroma","E":"Pelvic wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the AJCC staging system T2 means invasion of the muscularis propria, the detrusor muscle. T1 is invasion of the lamina propria, T3 is invasion of perivesical soft tissue, and T4 is extravesical invasion into structures such as prostatic stroma, seminal vesicles, uterus, vagina, or the pelvic wall. Invasion of the muscularis propria is the point at which survival falls most sharply, with a 30 percent 5-year mortality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: depth of invasion is the most important prognostic factor in bladder cancer. COMLEX: muscularis propria invasion is an indication for radical cystectomy or radiation therapy with chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 2 — urothelial carcinoma behavior is defined by its molecular pathway, precursor lesion, and depth of invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which organism, endemic in Egypt and Sudan, is an established risk factor for bladder cancer?","choices":{"A":"Wuchereria bancrofti","B":"Onchocerca volvulus","C":"Enterobius vermicularis","D":"Entamoeba histolytica","E":"Schistosoma haematobium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schistosoma haematobium infection in endemic areas such as Egypt and Sudan is an established risk factor for bladder cancer. The ova are deposited in the bladder wall and incite a chronic inflammatory response that induces progressive mucosal squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and in some instances neoplasia. The other organisms are not associated with bladder cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cystitis and Schistosoma haematobium are among the risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, along with smoking and chronic stones. COMLEX: painless hematuria in a patient from an endemic region warrants cystoscopic evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which histologic type accounts for about 70 percent of bladder cancers associated with Schistosoma haematobium?","choices":{"A":"Invasive urothelial carcinoma","B":"Adenocarcinoma of the bladder","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Small-cell carcinoma","E":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In schistosomal bladder cancer, chronic inflammation drives squamous metaplasia and dysplasia, and 70 percent of the cancers are squamous. The remainder are urothelial or glandular, and glandular tumors are the least common type. Squamous cell carcinoma makes up only 3 percent to 7 percent of bladder cancers in the United States, but it is much more frequent where urinary schistosomiasis is endemic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistosomiasis of the bladder produces squamous metaplasia that progresses to squamous cell carcinoma. COMLEX: pure squamous carcinomas are nearly always associated with chronic bladder irritation and infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which exposure is the most important risk factor for urothelial carcinoma of the bladder?","choices":{"A":"Cyclophosphamide therapy","B":"Chronic analgesic use","C":"Pelvic irradiation","D":"Cigarette smoking","E":"Aryl amine exposure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is clearly the most important influence, increasing risk three-fold to seven-fold, and 50 percent to 80 percent of bladder cancers in males are associated with cigarettes. Industrial exposure to aryl amines such as 2-naphthylamine, heavy long-term cyclophosphamide, long-term analgesic use, and pelvic irradiation are also recognized risk factors but contribute less.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for urothelial carcinoma are tobacco smoking, aromatic amines in dyes, and cyclophosphamide. COMLEX: painless hematuria is the most common presenting symptom of bladder cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 -> Obj 3 — different environmental exposures predispose the bladder to distinct histologic types of carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-month-old boy has a prenatal history of renal pelvic dilation. Ultrasonography now shows marked dilation of the right renal pelvis and calyces with a normal-caliber ureter and a normal bladder. Which mechanism is the most likely cause of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Sclerotic fibrous tissue encasing the retroperitoneal ureter","B":"Calculus lodged at the ureterovesical junction","C":"Neurogenic interruption of bladder innervation","D":"Disorganized smooth muscle at the ureteropelvic junction","E":"Papillary carcinoma growing within the ureteral lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in infants and children, and early presentation preferentially affects males. It is most often ascribed to abnormal organization of smooth muscle bundles or excess collagen deposition between them, and only rarely to extrinsic compression by abnormal renal vessels. Dilation limited to the right pelvis and calyces with a normal ureter and bladder localizes the obstruction to the junction, whereas retroperitoneal fibrosis and ureteral carcinoma are adult disorders and a distal calculus or neurogenic bladder would dilate the ureter or bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPJ obstruction is a congenital cause of hydronephrosis that may be bilateral in 20 percent of early cases. COMLEX: contralateral renal agenesis and other anomalies can coexist, so evaluate the other kidney.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — mechanical progression of hydronephrosis with calyceal dilation and parenchymal atrophy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman who received a renal transplant has recurrent E. coli urinary tract infections. Cystoscopy shows soft yellow raised mucosal plaques. Biopsy shows aggregates of large foamy histiocytes containing laminated calcified inclusions. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Mast cell accumulation in the bladder submucosa","B":"Downward growth of von Brunn nests into lamina propria","C":"Defective phagolysosome function in macrophages","D":"Lymphoid follicle formation within the bladder mucosa","E":"Implantation of renal tubular cells at eroded mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The yellow plaques and von Hansemann histiocytes with laminated calcified Michaelis-Gutmann bodies are diagnostic of malakoplakia. It arises in chronic bacterial infection, mostly by E. coli, and is more frequent with immunosuppression such as in renal transplant recipients. It appears to stem from acquired defects in phagocyte function, and the macrophages accumulate bacterial debris because of defective phagolysosome function. The other options describe interstitial cystitis, cystitis cystica, follicular cystitis, and nephrogenic adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: malakoplakia shows Michaelis-Gutmann bodies, which are calcified concretions in enlarged lysosomes, and von Hansemann histiocytes. COMLEX: it can mimic a bladder tumor clinically, so biopsy is needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — chronic bacterial infection of the urinary tract with E. coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Ureteral Anomalies & Inflammatory Bladder Pathologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with lupus nephritis is receiving high-dose cyclophosphamide and develops gross hematuria, frequency, and dysuria. Urine culture is negative and cystoscopy shows hyperemic, bleeding mucosa. With heavy long-term exposure to this drug, which neoplasm is she at increased risk of developing?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Bladder urothelial carcinoma","C":"Prostatic adenocarcinoma","D":"Penile squamous cell carcinoma","E":"Testicular germ cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cytotoxic agents such as cyclophosphamide may cause hemorrhagic cystitis, a noninfectious iatrogenic cystitis, and the negative culture supports this. Robbins states that heavy long-term exposure to cyclophosphamide induces hemorrhagic cystitis and increases the risk of bladder cancer. The other neoplasms are not linked to cyclophosphamide in this source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for urothelial carcinoma include smoking, aromatic amines, and cyclophosphamide. COMLEX: hematuria in a patient with prior cyclophosphamide exposure warrants cystoscopy rather than assuming persistent cystitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — chemical and drug exposures that injure the urothelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has painless gross hematuria. Cystoscopy shows patchy mucosal reddening and granularity without an intraluminal mass. Urine cytology shows dyscohesive malignant cells, and biopsy shows cytologically malignant cells within flat urothelium. Which genetic alteration most likely occurred early in the development of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"FGFR3 amplification with RAS activation","B":"MET proto-oncogene activation","C":"VHL loss with stabilized HIF-1 alpha","D":"WT1 deletion at chromosome 11p13","E":"Inactivating mutations of TP53 and RB"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Malignant cells confined to flat urothelium define carcinoma in situ, and the lack of cohesiveness explains shedding of cells into the urine. Most muscle-invasive bladder cancers arise from flat CIS, and mutations disrupting p53 and RB are prevalent in all muscle-invasive cancers but occur early in the development of CIS. FGFR3 and RAS alterations characterize the papillary pathway, and MET, VHL, and WT1 alterations belong to renal tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the flat CIS pathway is driven by TP53 and RB loss. COMLEX: CIS appears as mucosal reddening or granularity without a mass, is often multifocal, and 50 percent to 75 percent progress to invasive cancer if untreated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — divergence of the papillary and flat precursor pathways of urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes transurethral resection of a single papillary bladder tumor. Pathology shows a noninvasive low-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma with orderly architecture, and the tumor harbors a gain-of-function FGFR3 alteration. Which statement best describes his expected course?","choices":{"A":"Frequent recurrence with rare progression","B":"No recurrence after complete resection","C":"Progression to muscle invasion in most patients","D":"Early spread to the liver and lung","E":"Death from disease in about a quarter of patients"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancers have a high tendency to recur, up to 70 percent, and they need lifelong surveillance. Low-grade papillary tumors have a 98 percent 10-year survival rate regardless of the number of recurrences, and fewer than 10 percent progress to higher-grade lesions. Across non-muscle-invasive papillary cancers as a group, only about 20 percent progress to muscle-invasive disease, so progression is uncommon. Death in about 25 percent of cases is the figure for high-grade papillary carcinoma, not low-grade tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary tumors follow the FGFR3 and RAS pathway and recur but rarely progress. COMLEX: small localized low-grade papillary tumors need only diagnostic transurethral resection, whereas lifelong cystoscopic surveillance is required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — clinical behavior and surveillance needs of low-grade papillary urothelial tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Urothelial Carcinoma Pathways & Staging","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has painless hematuria. Transurethral resection shows high-grade urothelial carcinoma invading the muscularis propria, and staging imaging shows no distant metastases. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"BCG instillation","B":"Radical cystectomy","C":"Surveillance cystoscopy","D":"Repeat transurethral resection","E":"Intravesical chemotherapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stage determines treatment, and invasion of the muscularis propria is an indication for radical cystectomy, or radiation therapy with neoadjuvant or adjuvant chemotherapy, because of the significant potential for regional lymph node and systemic spread to liver and lung. Intravesical BCG is used for CIS and non-muscle-invasive papillary tumors that are large, high grade, multifocal, recurrent, or invade the lamina propria. Resection with surveillance is enough only for small, localized low-grade papillary tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2 invasion of the detrusor marks the shift from local therapy to radical management. COMLEX: metastatic bladder cancer responds poorly to chemotherapy, so a subset may benefit from immune checkpoint inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — depth of invasion into the bladder wall determines management of urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from an endemic region of Egypt has a long history of chronic urinary schistosomiasis and now has hematuria. Cystoscopy shows a fungating, ulcerated bladder mass, and biopsy shows an invasive keratin-producing carcinoma. Which sequence best explains this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Aromatic amine exposure with TP53 loss in flat urothelium","B":"FGFR3 amplification with papillary urothelial proliferation","C":"Egg-induced inflammation with squamous metaplasia","D":"Von Brunn nest downgrowth with intestinal metaplasia","E":"Renal tubular cell implantation at eroded mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Schistosoma haematobium ova are deposited in the bladder wall and incite a chronic inflammatory response that induces progressive mucosal squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and in some instances neoplasia. Seventy percent of these cancers are squamous, which explains the keratin-producing carcinoma. Aromatic amines and FGFR3 alterations promote urothelial carcinoma, intestinal metaplasia is a precursor of adenocarcinoma, and renal tubular cell implantation produces nephrogenic adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder include Schistosoma haematobium, chronic cystitis, smoking, and chronic stones. COMLEX: extensive multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia is a precursor to squamous dysplasia and carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — squamous metaplasia as the setting for squamous carcinoma of the bladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man worked for 25 years in dye manufacturing and smokes cigarettes. He has painless gross hematuria, and cystoscopy shows a large invasive bladder tumor. Which tumor type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Adenocarcinoma","C":"Small-cell carcinoma","D":"Urothelial carcinoma","E":"Leiomyosarcoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Urothelial neoplasms represent about 90 percent of bladder tumors. Cigarette smoking is the most important risk factor, and industrial exposure to aryl amines such as 2-naphthylamine is another, with cancer appearing 15 to 40 years after first exposure. Squamous carcinoma is associated with chronic irritation and schistosomiasis, adenocarcinoma is rare, small-cell carcinoma is an uncommon neuroendocrine variant, and leiomyosarcoma is the most common bladder sarcoma in adults but is rare.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urothelial carcinoma is associated with smoking, aromatic amines in dyes, and cyclophosphamide, and presents with painless hematuria. COMLEX: occupational history of dye or rubber industry exposure is a key element to elicit in hematuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — occupational aryl amine and tobacco exposures predispose to urothelial carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl42_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Metaplastic Transformations & Environmental Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with chronic bladder stones and recurrent cystitis undergoes cystoscopic biopsy. The urothelium is extensively replaced by multifocal keratin-producing stratified epithelium. Which interpretation of this finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A precursor to squamous dysplasia and carcinoma","B":"A precursor to bladder adenocarcinoma","C":"A mimic of cancer from implanted renal tubular cells","D":"A histiocytic reaction from defective phagolysosome function","E":"A normal glycogenated lining expected in adult men"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In response to chronic injury the urothelium is often replaced by a more durable squamous epithelium, and extensive multifocal keratinizing squamous metaplasia is a precursor to dysplasia and to in situ and invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Bladder schistosomiasis is the classic example of this sequence. Intestinal metaplasia, not squamous metaplasia, is the precursor of adenocarcinoma, nephrogenic adenoma is due to implanted tubular cells, and malakoplakia reflects defective phagocyte function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic irritation from stones or infection promotes squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and squamous cell carcinoma. COMLEX: glycogenated nonkeratinizing squamous epithelium is a normal finding at the trigone in females and should not be mistaken for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — chronic irritation and infection induce squamous metaplasia of the bladder mucosa","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":43,"title":"SDL 43 — Renal Pathology Review","questions":[{"id":"sdl43_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding defines minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Subepithelial deposits between basement membrane spikes","B":"Subendothelial deposits with duplicated basement membrane","C":"Diffuse foot process effacement without deposits","D":"Lamellated lamina densa with basketweave splitting","E":"Mesangial deposits with widened mesangial regions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In minimal change disease the glomeruli appear normal by light microscopy, and electron microscopy shows uniform, diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes with loss of the slit diaphragms and no electron-dense deposits. Effacement also occurs in other proteinuric states, but in those it usually appears later and with architectural distortion of the glomerulus, so the diagnosis requires normal glomeruli by light microscopy. Subepithelial deposits with spikes mark membranous nephropathy, subendothelial deposits with duplication mark membranoproliferative disease, basketweave lamination marks Alport syndrome, and mesangial deposits mark IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrotic syndrome in a young child with normal light microscopy and effacement on electron microscopy is minimal change disease, historically called lipoid nephrosis because tubular cells are lipid laden. COMLEX: more than 90 percent of children respond rapidly to corticosteroids, relapses are common, and in adults it can accompany Hodgkin lymphoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — nephrotic podocytopathies: minimal change disease, FSGS, and membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"Which antigen is targeted by autoantibodies in roughly 60 percent of membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-3 chain of glomerular type IV collagen","B":"Nephrin of the slit diaphragm","C":"Proteinase-3 of neutrophil granules","D":"Factor H of the alternative pathway","E":"Phospholipase A2 receptor of podocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The M-type phospholipase A2 receptor, a membrane protein on the basal surface of glomerular epithelial cells, is the antigen underlying about 60 percent of human membranous nephropathy. Autoantibody binding is followed by complement activation and shedding of immune aggregates that form subepithelial deposits. Nephrin is the antibody target proposed in minimal change disease, the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen is the target in anti-GBM disease, proteinase-3 is a common ANCA antigen in pauci-immune disease, and factor H is a complement regulator affected in atypical HUS and C3 glomerulopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: membranous nephropathy is an in situ immune complex disease with granular IgG and subepithelial deposits, and IgG4 is the main subclass deposited in many PLA2R cases. COMLEX: about 75 percent of cases are primary, so secondary causes such as drugs, malignancy, infection, and lupus should be excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — nephrotic podocytopathies: minimal change disease, FSGS, and membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which lesion is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly among people of Hispanic and African descent?","choices":{"A":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","B":"Primary membranous nephropathy","C":"Adult minimal change disease","D":"Fibrillary glomerulonephritis","E":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins identifies FSGS, primary and secondary forms together, as the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly in people of Hispanic and African descent. Idiopathic FSGS accounts for about 35 percent of adult nephrotic syndrome and 10 percent of childhood nephrotic syndrome, and Robbins notes that membranous nephropathy is the most common primary lesion in older adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS shows segmental sclerosis with hyalinosis, a poor steroid response, and progression to ESKD in at least 50 percent of patients within 10 years. COMLEX: minimal change disease is the leading cause in children, about 75 percent of childhood cases, and membranous nephropathy is the most common primary lesion in older adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — nephrotic podocytopathies: minimal change disease, FSGS, and membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern is characteristic of anti-GBM antibody disease?","choices":{"A":"Coarsely granular C3 with IgG in the capillary wall","B":"Continuous linear IgG along the basement membrane","C":"Little or no glomerular immune reactant deposition","D":"Mesangial IgA with C3 and properdin deposits","E":"Granular IgG with C1q along the capillary walls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-GBM antibodies bind intrinsic antigens along the entire length of the GBM, producing a diffuse linear pattern of IgG and often C3 by immunofluorescence. The antigen is a peptide in the noncollagenous portion of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. Coarsely granular C3 and IgG mark poststreptococcal disease, sparse or absent reactants mark pauci-immune disease, mesangial IgA marks IgA nephropathy, and granular IgG with C1q occurs in immune complex diseases such as lupus nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: linear IgG means anti-GBM disease, and cross-reactivity with the alveolar basement membrane produces Goodpasture syndrome. COMLEX: plasmapheresis to remove circulating antibodies plus immunosuppression is the mainstay of treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — immunofluorescence, serology, and electron microscopy patterns of acute nephritic and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"In pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis, which serologic finding is present in more than 90 percent of cases?","choices":{"A":"Circulating anti-GBM antibodies","B":"Elevated antistreptococcal antibody titers","C":"Circulating antiphospholipid antibodies","D":"Circulating antinuclear antibodies","E":"Circulating PR3-ANCA or MPO-ANCA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pauci-immune crescentic GN is defined by the lack of anti-GBM antibodies and a paucity of immune complexes, and more than 90 percent of cases have PR3-ANCA with a cytoplasmic pattern or MPO-ANCA with a perinuclear pattern in the serum. It makes up the majority of crescentic GN and may be a component of granulomatosis with polyangiitis or microscopic polyangiitis. Anti-GBM antibodies define type I disease, antistreptococcal antibodies accompany poststreptococcal disease, and antiphospholipid antibodies are linked to atypical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RPGN is divided into anti-GBM type I, immune complex type II, and pauci-immune type III, and ANCA is a highly sensitive marker for type III. COMLEX: B-cell targeting therapy has a critical role in ANCA-associated vasculitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — immunofluorescence, serology, and electron microscopy patterns of acute nephritic and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding is characteristic of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial deposits producing wire loops","B":"Ribbonlike dense material within the lamina densa","C":"Lamellated basement membrane with basketweave splitting","D":"Discrete subepithelial electron-dense humps","E":"Mesangial and paramesangial dense deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Poststreptococcal GN shows discrete, amorphous electron-dense deposits on the epithelial side of the GBM that appear as humps, together with granular IgG and C3 by immunofluorescence. Subendothelial deposits producing wire loops occur in class IV lupus nephritis, ribbonlike material in the lamina densa defines dense deposit disease, lamellation with basketweave splitting defines Alport syndrome, and mesangial deposits occur in IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subepithelial humps with low serum C3 in a child 1 to 4 weeks after pharyngitis or impetigo is poststreptococcal GN. COMLEX: more than 95 percent of affected children recover with conservative therapy, but the disease is less benign in adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — immunofluorescence, serology, and electron microscopy patterns of acute nephritic and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect underlies the pathogenesis of IgA nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Excess IgA1 production with normal glycosylation","B":"Deficient galactose on O-linked glycans of IgA1","C":"Mutated alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen","D":"Autoantibodies to the phospholipase A2 receptor","E":"Loss of complement factor H regulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IgA nephropathy is a multi-hit disease in which a hereditary or acquired defect in attaching galactose-containing O-linked glycans to the hinge region of IgA1 produces aberrantly glycosylated IgA. This IgA is deposited in the mesangium or elicits IgG autoantibodies that form mesangial immune complexes. Increased IgA production is not sufficient to cause the disease. Alpha-5 chain mutations cause Alport syndrome, PLA2R autoantibodies cause membranous nephropathy, and factor H regulatory defects relate to C3 glomerulopathy and atypical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mesangial IgA with C3 and absent C1q and C4 reflects alternative pathway activation, and IgA nephropathy is the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide. COMLEX: it is associated with celiac disease and liver disease, and 15 to 40 percent of patients progress to chronic renal failure over 20 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — IgA nephropathy pathogenesis and the inherited type IV collagen basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is mutated in the X-linked form of Alport syndrome, which accounts for about 85 percent of cases?","choices":{"A":"COL4A3","B":"COL4A4","C":"COL4A5","D":"NPHS1","E":"PKHD1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alport syndrome is X-linked in about 85 percent of cases and results from mutations in the collagen IV alpha-5 chain gene COL4A5, with large COL4A5 deletions associated with ESRD at an earlier age. COL4A3 and COL4A4 encode the autosomally encoded alpha-3 and alpha-4 chains, which are affected in autosomal forms, whereas NPHS1 and PKHD1 encode nephrin and fibrocystin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an X-linked type IV collagen defect with hematuria, sensorineural deafness, and lens dislocation is Alport syndrome. COMLEX: males express the full syndrome while heterozygous females typically present with hematuria, and about 90 percent of affected males reach ESRD by 40 years of age.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — IgA nephropathy pathogenesis and the inherited type IV collagen basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":1,"stem":"Which genetic pattern typifies thin basement membrane nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Heterozygous autosomal alpha-3 or alpha-4 mutations","B":"X-linked large deletions of the alpha-5 chain","C":"Autosomal recessive homozygous mutations in nephrin","D":"Autosomal dominant uromodulin gene mutations","E":"Sporadic autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is a related disorder associated with mutations in the genes encoding the alpha-3 or alpha-4 chains of type IV collagen. It most often has autosomal inheritance, and most patients are heterozygous for the defective gene. X-linked alpha-5 deletions cause classic Alport syndrome, nephrin mutations cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, uromodulin mutations cause autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease, and alpha-3 autoantibodies cause anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: benign familial hematuria with a diffusely thinned GBM points to the mild end of the type IV collagen spectrum, where a milder Alport-type presentation shows GBM widths of 150 to 225 nm compared with 300 to 400 nm in healthy adults. COMLEX: Robbins treats these as a spectrum requiring genetic counseling and clinical follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 1 and Obj 2 — IgA nephropathy pathogenesis and the inherited type IV collagen basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, how soon after drug exposure does drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begin?","choices":{"A":"3 to 12 hours","B":"3 to 6 months","C":"1 to 3 years","D":"2 to 40 days","E":"5 to 10 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begins 2 to 40 days after drug exposure and presents with fever, transient eosinophilia, rash in about 25 percent of patients, hematuria, mild proteinuria, and leukocyturia. Onset is not dose related and recurs on re-exposure to the same or a chemically related drug, consistent with hypersensitivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfonamides, synthetic penicillins, rifampin, thiazides, NSAIDs, allopurinol, cimetidine, and checkpoint inhibitors are classic triggers. COMLEX: withdrawing the offending drug is followed by recovery, although this can take several months and irreversible damage can occur.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — drug-induced interstitial nephritis, acute tubular injury susceptibility, and the phases of tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"What urine output characterizes the maintenance phase of acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"600 to 1200 mL per day","B":"40 to 400 mL per day","C":"1 to 2 L per day","D":"5 to 30 mL per day","E":"3 to 5 L per day"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The maintenance phase of ATI is marked by a sustained decrease in urine output to between 40 and 400 mL per day, with salt and water overload, rising BUN, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis. The initiation phase lasts about 36 hours, and the recovery phase brings urine volumes that may reach up to 3 L per day.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATI has initiation, maintenance, and recovery phases and is the most common cause of acute kidney injury, accounting for about 50 percent of cases in hospitalized patients. COMLEX: with supportive care, 95 percent of patients who survive the precipitating event recover.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — drug-induced interstitial nephritis, acute tubular injury susceptibility, and the phases of tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron segments are most vulnerable to ischemic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Distal convoluted tubule and cortical collecting duct","B":"Thin descending limb and inner medullary collecting duct","C":"Glomerular capillary tuft and Bowman capsule","D":"Macula densa and juxtaglomerular cells","E":"Proximal straight tubule and thick ascending limb"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ischemic ATI the necrosis is patchy, and the straight portion of the proximal tubule and the thick ascending limb of Henle in the outer medulla are most vulnerable because of high metabolic activity and reduced oxygen delivery from intrarenal vasoconstriction. Toxic ATI more prominently involves the proximal convoluted tubule. The glomerulus and juxtaglomerular apparatus are not the target segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic ATI is patchy with skip lesions, favoring the straight proximal tubule and thick ascending limb. COMLEX: mercury and other toxins produce proximal convoluted tubule necrosis, and ethylene glycol produces calcium oxalate crystals in tubular lumens.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — drug-induced interstitial nephritis, acute tubular injury susceptibility, and the phases of tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cause of renal artery stenosis, accounting for about 70 percent of cases?","choices":{"A":"Fibromuscular dysplasia of the medial layer","B":"Cholesterol atheroemboli in arcuate arteries","C":"Fibrinoid necrosis of interlobular arteries","D":"Atheromatous plaque at the vessel origin","E":"Tumor invasion of the arterial wall"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most common cause of renal artery stenosis, about 70 percent of cases, is narrowing at the origin of the renal artery by an atheromatous plaque, which is more frequent in males and increases with age and diabetes. Fibromuscular dysplasia is the second most frequent cause and occurs more often in younger women. Atheroemboli and fibrinoid necrosis affect intrarenal vessels and are not the usual cause of main renal artery stenosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis raises renin release from the ischemic kidney, causing angiotensin II mediated hypertension. COMLEX: unilateral stenosis causes 2 to 5 percent of hypertension and is potentially curable by surgery, with a cure rate of 70 to 80 percent in well-selected cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein is most commonly mutated in atypical, diarrhea-negative hemolytic uremic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"ADAMTS13 metalloprotease","B":"Von Willebrand factor multimers","C":"Complement factor H","D":"Antithrombin III","E":"Coagulation factor VIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"More than half of patients with atypical HUS have identifiable mutations of complement-regulatory proteins, most commonly factor H, which promotes breakdown of the alternative pathway C3 convertase and protects cells from uncontrolled complement activation. Mutations in factor I and CD46 also occur, and autoantibodies against regulators can produce the same disease. ADAMTS13 deficiency causes TTP, and von Willebrand factor, antithrombin III, and factor VIII are not the mutated proteins in atypical HUS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: atypical HUS lacks preceding diarrhea and reflects complement dysregulation, and it is separated from TTP by normal plasma ADAMTS13 levels. COMLEX: atypical HUS has a worse prognosis than typical HUS because the triggers may be genetic or chronic conditions that are difficult to treat.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"Once nephron loss lowers the GFR to roughly what fraction of normal does progression to end-stage kidney disease proceed independent of the original insult?","choices":{"A":"30 to 50 percent of normal","B":"5 to 10 percent of normal","C":"60 to 75 percent of normal","D":"80 to 90 percent of normal","E":"15 to 20 percent of normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once any renal disease has destroyed enough nephrons to reduce the GFR to about 30 to 50 percent of normal, progression to end-stage kidney disease proceeds at a steady rate independent of the original insult or the activity of the underlying disease. The two major histologic features of this progressive damage are glomerulosclerosis and tubulointerstitial fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after enough nephron loss, hyperfiltration of surviving glomeruli perpetuates the decline regardless of the original cause. COMLEX: the extent of tubulointerstitial damage often correlates better with functional decline than the severity of glomerular injury, and renin-angiotensin system inhibitors are the most successful intervention to slow sclerosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 -> Obj 1, Obj 2 and Obj 3 — renovascular disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, and the common pathway of chronic kidney disease progression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Mutations in which gene account for about 85 percent of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"PKD2","B":"PKHD1","C":"NPHP1","D":"PKD1","E":"UMOD"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Almost all autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is caused by mutations in PKD1, encoding polycystin-1, or PKD2, encoding polycystin-2. PKD1 mutations account for about 85 percent of cases and produce more severe disease, while PKD2 accounts for most of the remaining cases. PKHD1 encodes fibrocystin in the autosomal recessive form, NPHP1 encodes a nephrocystin in nephronophthisis, and UMOD encodes uromodulin in autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 localize to the primary cilium, and cyst formation requires loss of both alleles. COMLEX: the disease affects roughly 1 in 400 to 1000 live births and accounts for about 5 to 10 percent of ESKD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 and SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — polycystic kidney disease genetics and extrarenal features, and calculus chemistry with crystal morphology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Which urinary crystal shape is characteristic of magnesium ammonium phosphate stones?","choices":{"A":"Envelope","B":"Rhomboid","C":"Hexagonal","D":"Wedge-shaped prism","E":"Coffin lid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Struvite stones, composed of magnesium ammonium phosphate, form after infection with urease-producing bacteria and appear as coffin lid shaped crystals in urine. Calcium oxalate crystals are envelope shaped, uric acid crystals are rhomboid, cystine crystals are hexagonal, and calcium phosphate crystals are wedge-shaped prisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crystal shape identifies stone type on urine microscopy, and the coffin lid shape means struvite. COMLEX: struvite forms after urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus convert urea to ammonia, producing alkaline urine and staghorn calculi.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 and SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — polycystic kidney disease genetics and extrarenal features, and calculus chemistry with crystal morphology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":1,"stem":"Which liver lesion accompanies autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease in children who survive infancy?","choices":{"A":"Congenital hepatic fibrosis","B":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis","C":"Hepatocellular adenoma","D":"Portal vein thrombosis","E":"Alcoholic steatohepatitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patients with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease who survive infancy may develop congenital hepatic fibrosis, a bland periportal fibrosis with proliferation of well-differentiated biliary ductules. In older children the liver disease becomes the predominant concern and can cause portal hypertension with splenomegaly. In almost all cases the liver also has cysts with portal fibrosis and bile duct proliferation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARPKD is caused by PKHD1 mutations encoding fibrocystin, with enlarged smooth kidneys and radially arranged collecting duct cysts. COMLEX: in adult polycystic disease the liver cysts derive from biliary epithelium, occur in about 40 percent of patients, and are usually asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 -> Obj 2 and SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — polycystic kidney disease genetics and extrarenal features, and calculus chemistry with crystal morphology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal cell carcinoma subtype most often shows trisomy 7 and trisomy 17?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","C":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","D":"Collecting duct carcinoma","E":"Xp11 translocation renal cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma accounts for 10 to 15 percent of renal cancers and is not associated with chromosome 3p deletions. Its most common cytogenetic abnormalities are trisomies 7 and 17 with loss of the Y chromosome in sporadic male cases, and the familial form shows trisomy 7 with MET mutations. Clear cell tumors show 3p deletion, chromophobe tumors show multiple chromosome losses with hypodiploidy, and Xp11 tumors show TFE3 translocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary RCC is often multifocal, hemorrhagic, and cystic, with foamy macrophages in the papillary cores and sometimes psammoma bodies. COMLEX: papillary adenomas smaller than 1.5 cm share trisomies 7 and 17 and are found in 7 to 22 percent of autopsies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and Obj 3 — molecular drivers of renal cell carcinoma subtypes and germline genetics of pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"Familial papillary renal cell carcinoma is caused by gain-of-function mutations in which proto-oncogene?","choices":{"A":"RET","B":"KIT","C":"MET","D":"ALK","E":"ERBB2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Familial papillary carcinoma is caused by gain-of-function mutations in MET, a proto-oncogene on chromosome 7 that encodes the tyrosine kinase receptor for hepatocyte growth factor. MET is also mutated in a small proportion of sporadic papillary carcinomas. The other proto-oncogenes are not implicated in the hereditary papillary carcinoma described in Robbins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MET activation drives hepatocyte growth factor signaling, which stimulates growth, motility, and invasion. COMLEX: hereditary papillary carcinoma presents as multiple bilateral tumors with papillary histology and usually low nuclear grade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and Obj 3 — molecular drivers of renal cell carcinoma subtypes and germline genetics of pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":1,"stem":"A child with Wilms tumor also has macroglossia, organomegaly, and omphalocele. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","B":"Denys-Drash syndrome","C":"Tuberous sclerosis complex","D":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome","E":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome combines Wilms tumor with organomegaly, macroglossia, hemihyperplasia, and omphalocele, from an imprinting defect associated with WT2. Denys-Drash syndrome features diffuse mesangial sclerosis and gonadal dysgenesis with WT1 mutation. Tuberous sclerosis predisposes to angiomyolipoma, and von Hippel-Lindau disease and hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma syndrome predispose to renal carcinoma rather than to Wilms tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1 (First Aid): Wilms tumor is the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, ages 2 to 4, and presents with a large palpable unilateral flank mass, hematuria, and possible hypertension. COMLEX: renal cell carcinoma is the most common malignant kidney tumor overall, followed by Wilms tumor in children.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and Obj 3 — molecular drivers of renal cell carcinoma subtypes and germline genetics of pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy develops periorbital swelling 10 days after an upper respiratory infection. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood, serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL, and blood pressure is normal. A kidney biopsy shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, diffuse foot process effacement, and no electron-dense deposits. Which of the following best explains his proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies to phospholipase A2 receptor forming subepithelial complexes","B":"Planted streptococcal antigen forming subepithelial humps","C":"Autoantibodies to nephrin disrupting slit diaphragm cross-linking","D":"Galactose-deficient IgA1 deposited within the mesangium","E":"Antibodies to the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A young child with nephrotic syndrome, normal glomeruli by light microscopy, diffuse foot process effacement, and no deposits has minimal change disease. The current hypothesis is that circulating antibodies against nephrin, an essential slit diaphragm protein, interfere with nephrin cross-linking and cause rapid breakdown of the slit diaphragm. PLA2R antibodies, streptococcal antigens, and aberrantly glycosylated IgA1 all produce immune deposits, and anti-GBM antibodies cause linear GBM staining with crescentic disease, none of which fit normal light microscopy and absent deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: minimal change disease is the most frequent cause of nephrotic syndrome in children, with peak incidence at 2 to 6 years and onset sometimes after infection or immunization. COMLEX: expect a dramatic corticosteroid response in more than 90 percent of children, and the proteinuria is highly selective, mostly albumin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — matching light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy patterns to the primary nephrotic diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old African American man has 2 months of leg edema, blood pressure of 158/98 mm Hg, urine protein of 5 g/day, microscopic hematuria, and a creatinine of 1.9 mg/dL. HIV testing is negative and he takes no medications. A biopsy shows sclerosis and hyalinosis involving part of the capillary tuft in some glomeruli, with foot process effacement in the remaining glomeruli. Corticosteroids produce no response. Variants in which gene most likely increased his risk of this disease?","choices":{"A":"COL4A5","B":"PKD1","C":"PKHD1","D":"UMOD","E":"APOL1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sclerosis of part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli with hyalinosis defines FSGS, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States. Hypertension, hematuria, reduced GFR, and poor steroid response distinguish it from minimal change disease. Two sequence variants, G1 and G2, in APOL1 are strongly associated with chronic kidney disease and renal failure in individuals of African descent and with FSGS and collapsing glomerulopathy. COL4A5, PKD1, PKHD1, and UMOD are the genes of Alport syndrome, adult polycystic disease, childhood polycystic disease, and autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the APOL1 G1 and G2 risk alleles are common in people of African descent, apparently because they convey resistance to trypanosome infection. COMLEX: primary FSGS has a poor corticosteroid response, at least 50 percent of patients reach ESKD within 10 years, and it recurs in 25 to 50 percent of kidney transplants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — ancestry-linked and genetic risk factors in the primary nephrotic diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Glomerular Filtration & Podocytopathies Case Analysis","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man who smokes has 3 months of leg swelling. Urine protein is 6 g/day, serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL, and blood pressure is normal. A kidney biopsy shows diffusely thickened capillary walls with silver-staining spikes, granular IgG along the basement membrane, and subepithelial deposits on electron microscopy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Screen for underlying malignancy and other secondary causes","B":"Begin high-dose prednisone for presumed minimal change disease","C":"Start plasma exchange for anti-GBM antibody disease","D":"Give empiric penicillin for poststreptococcal disease","E":"Order genetic testing for mutations in the nephrin gene"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diffusely thickened capillary walls with silver-staining spikes, granular IgG, and subepithelial deposits define membranous nephropathy. Robbins stresses that secondary causes must be ruled out first, because treating the underlying condition or stopping the offending drug can reverse the injury, and as many as 5 to 10 percent of adults with membranous nephropathy have an underlying malignant tumor, particularly carcinoma of the lung or colon or melanoma. The proteinuria is nonselective and usually does not respond well to corticosteroids, plasma exchange treats anti-GBM disease, and poststreptococcal disease is a nephritic process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: membranous nephropathy shows subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes on silver stain and is a hypercoagulable state with a risk of renal vein thrombosis. COMLEX: about 75 percent of cases are primary, remissions occur in up to 40 percent of patients, and the disease recurs in up to 40 percent of transplants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 3 — primary nephrotic diseases and how their treatment responses differ","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy has cola-colored urine and periorbital edema 12 days after a sore throat. Blood pressure is 138/88 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic red cells, red cell casts, and 1+ protein. Which of the following serologic profiles is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low C3 with circulating PR3-ANCA","B":"Normal C3 with circulating anti-GBM antibodies","C":"Low C3 with rising antistreptococcal antibody titers","D":"Normal C3 with normal antistreptococcal antibody titers","E":"Normal C3 with elevated antinuclear antibody titers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cola-colored urine, hypertension, edema, and dysmorphic red cells with casts 1 to 2 weeks after pharyngitis indicate acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Serum C3 and other complement components fall from consumption, and antistreptococcal antibody titers are elevated in a great majority of patients. PR3-ANCA marks pauci-immune disease and anti-GBM antibodies mark anti-GBM disease, neither of which fits this history, and normal antistreptococcal titers would not support a poststreptococcal cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low C3 with elevated antistreptococcal titers after pharyngitis or impetigo is poststreptococcal GN, caused mostly by nephritogenic group A strains of M types 1, 4, and 12. COMLEX: management is conservative with sodium and water balance, and most children recover.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — acute nephritic syndrome and the serologic profile of poststreptococcal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has hematuria, proteinuria of 3.8 g/day, hypertension, and a rising creatinine. A kidney biopsy shows endocapillary proliferation in more than 50 percent of glomeruli, cellular crescents, and circumferential capillary wall thickening in a wire loop pattern. Which of the following best explains the wire loop appearance?","choices":{"A":"Subendothelial immune complex deposition","B":"Subepithelial deposits with matrix spikes","C":"Ribbonlike dense deposits within the lamina densa","D":"Mesangial deposits of IgA-containing complexes","E":"Linear IgG bound along the basement membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diffuse (class IV) lupus nephritis, the most common and severe class, involves more than 50 percent of glomeruli with proliferation of endothelial, mesangial, and epithelial cells and cellular crescents. Subendothelial immune complex deposits cause circumferential thickening of the capillary wall, seen as wire loops by light microscopy. Subepithelial deposits with matrix spikes characterize class V membranous lupus nephritis, ribbonlike dense deposits characterize dense deposit disease, mesangial IgA characterizes IgA nephropathy, and linear IgG marks anti-GBM disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: wire loops with granular IgG, often with IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q, point to lupus nephritis. COMLEX: class I is the least common and class IV the most common, lesions may evolve from one class to another over time, and up to 50 percent of patients with SLE develop clinically significant renal involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — lupus nephritis classes defined by immune complex location and proliferation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Nephritic & RPGN Diagnostic Differentiation","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has 3 weeks of fatigue and oliguria, and his creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 5.2 mg/dL. He has chronic sinus disease and pulmonary nodules on chest imaging. Urinalysis shows red cell casts and PR3-ANCA is positive. A kidney biopsy shows crescents in most glomeruli with segmental fibrinoid necrosis. Which additional finding is most likely on immunofluorescence and electron microscopy?","choices":{"A":"Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","B":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps","C":"Granular IgG and C1q with subendothelial deposits","D":"Mesangial IgA staining with mesangial deposits","E":"Scant immune deposits with GBM ruptures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sinus disease, pulmonary nodules, positive PR3-ANCA, and crescentic glomerulonephritis with segmental fibrinoid necrosis indicate pauci-immune crescentic GN as seen in granulomatosis with polyangiitis. By definition there is little or no immune reactant deposition by immunofluorescence, although electron microscopy shows GBM ruptures regardless of type, which allow leukocytes and plasma proteins into the urinary space to trigger crescent formation. Linear IgG, granular IgG with C3 and humps, granular IgG with C1q and subendothelial deposits, and mesangial IgA correspond to anti-GBM disease, poststreptococcal GN, lupus or MPGN, and IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: crescents are proliferating parietal epithelial cells with monocytes and macrophages in Bowman space, and pauci-immune disease is the majority of crescentic GN. COMLEX: about one-fifth of crescentic GN is anti-GBM disease and one-quarter is immune complex disease, and forms other than anti-GBM disease respond well to steroids and cytotoxic agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — comparing anti-GBM, immune complex, and pauci-immune RPGN by immunofluorescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has dark brown urine that began during a sore throat, lasted 3 days, and then cleared. He has had two similar episodes over the past year. Blood pressure and creatinine are normal, hearing is normal, and no relative has kidney disease. Urinalysis after the latest episode shows dysmorphic red cells and 1+ protein. Which set of biopsy findings is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Mesangial IgA and C3 with mesangial dense deposits","B":"Linear IgG along the basement membrane without deposits","C":"Granular IgG and C3 with subepithelial humps","D":"Granular IgG along capillary walls with subepithelial deposits","E":"Lamellated basement membrane with basketweave splitting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recurrent gross hematuria lasting several days after a respiratory infection, with normal hearing and no family history, is typical of IgA nephropathy. Immunofluorescence shows mesangial IgA, often with C3 and properdin, and electron microscopy shows electron-dense deposits predominantly in the mesangium. Linear IgG marks anti-GBM disease, subepithelial humps mark poststreptococcal GN, granular capillary wall IgG with subepithelial deposits marks membranous nephropathy, and basketweave lamellation marks Alport syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgA nephropathy is the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide, with mesangial IgA and C3 and usually no C1q or C4. COMLEX: 15 to 40 percent progress to chronic renal failure over 20 years, and older onset, heavy proteinuria, hypertension, and extensive glomerulosclerosis predict progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 1 — IgA deposition patterns and the recurrent gross hematuria timeline","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has recurrent hematuria with red cell casts and mild proteinuria. He has bilateral sensorineural hearing loss and lens dislocation on eye examination. His maternal uncle began dialysis at age 35, and his mother has microscopic hematuria with normal renal function. Which electron microscopic finding is most likely on kidney biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Uniform thinning to about 200 nm with intact lamina densa","B":"Subepithelial humps with neutrophils within the capillary lumen","C":"Irregular thickening and thinning with basketweave lamination","D":"Subendothelial deposits with tram-track duplication","E":"Ribbonlike dense material permeating the lamina densa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hematuria with red cell casts, sensorineural hearing loss, lens dislocation, and a family history consistent with X-linked inheritance indicate Alport syndrome. Electron microscopy shows irregular foci of GBM thickening alternating with attenuation and pronounced splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, producing a basket-weave appearance. Uniform attenuation without lamination fits thin basement membrane disease, subepithelial humps fit poststreptococcal GN, tram-track duplication with subendothelial deposits fits MPGN, and ribbonlike dense deposits fit dense deposit disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alport syndrome results from type IV collagen defects, about 85 percent X-linked, with hematuria, sensorineural deafness, and eye lesions. COMLEX: males develop overt renal failure between 20 and 50 years of age, whereas female heterozygotes typically present with hematuria and preserved function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — type IV collagen ultrastructure across inherited basement membrane disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Glomerular Hematuria Diagnostic Modeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has persistent microscopic hematuria found on a routine urinalysis. Her father and sister have the same finding. Blood pressure, creatinine, and hearing are normal, she has no ocular abnormalities, and urine protein is 150 mg/day. A biopsy shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, no immune deposits by immunofluorescence, and a diffusely narrow GBM of about 200 nm on electron microscopy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Immunoglobulin A nephropathy","B":"Thin basement membrane disease","C":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","D":"Dense deposit disease","E":"Lupus nephritis class II"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Persistent familial microscopic hematuria with normal hearing, no eye disease, minimal proteinuria, no immune deposits, and a diffusely attenuated GBM of about 200 nm, compared with 300 to 400 nm in healthy adults, indicates thin basement membrane disease. It is a related disorder associated with mutations in the alpha-3 or alpha-4 chains of type IV collagen, usually heterozygous and autosomal, and it sits within the Alport spectrum. IgA nephropathy requires mesangial IgA, poststreptococcal GN causes a nephritic syndrome with humps, dense deposit disease shows ribbonlike deposits within the lamina densa, and class II lupus nephritis shows mesangial immune deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial hematuria without hearing loss, eye disease, or immune deposits and with a diffusely thin GBM is thin basement membrane disease. COMLEX: Robbins considers these type IV collagen disorders a spectrum requiring genetic counseling and clinical follow-up, and hearing loss and ocular abnormalities help separate Alport syndrome from IgA nephropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — risk stratification of persistent microscopic hematuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man treated with a sulfonamide for a urinary tract infection develops fever, a maculopapular rash, and a creatinine rise from 0.9 to 2.8 mg/dL 12 days into therapy. Urinalysis shows hematuria, mild proteinuria, and leukocyturia, and urine culture grows no bacteria. Which histologic finding is most likely on kidney biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Edematous interstitium with eosinophils and lymphocytes and tubulitis","B":"Neutrophilic tubular casts and neutrophilic tubulitis with cortical microabscesses","C":"Sloughed tubular epithelium with skip areas of tubular necrosis","D":"Hyaline arteriolar thickening with subcapsular glomerular scars","E":"Coarse polar corticomedullary scars with thyroidization of tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, rash, hematuria, mild proteinuria, and leukocyturia beginning 2 to 40 days after a sulfonamide, with a sterile urine culture, indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. The interstitium shows edema and infiltration by lymphocytes and macrophages, with eosinophils and neutrophils often in clusters, plus tubulitis. Neutrophilic tubular casts with microabscesses define acute pyelonephritis, sloughed necrotic epithelium with skip areas defines ATI, hyaline arteriolosclerosis with subcapsular scars defines benign nephrosclerosis, and polar scars with thyroidization define chronic pyelonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs bind tubular components to create modified self antigens that evoke IgE or T cell mediated hypersensitivity. COMLEX: about 25 percent have rash and about 50 percent develop a rising creatinine, and 30 to 40 percent of acute interstitial nephritis has no identifiable drug.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — hypersensitivity markers that separate interstitial nephritis from suppurative infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man undergoes emergency repair of a ruptured aortic aneurysm with 45 minutes of hypotension. Over the next 9 days his urine output averages 250 mL/day while his BUN and potassium rise. On day 10 the urine volume climbs steadily to 2.8 L/day and his BUN begins to fall. Which of the following is the greatest hazard during this phase?","choices":{"A":"Salt and water overload from ongoing severe oliguria","B":"Water and electrolyte depletion from damaged tubules","C":"Life-threatening hyperkalemia from persistent severe oliguria","D":"Severe metabolic acidosis from failing renal acid excretion","E":"Permanent anuria from bilateral renal cortical necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The steadily rising urine volume with a falling BUN marks the recovery phase of ATI, when urine output may reach up to 3 L per day. The tubules are still damaged, so large amounts of water, sodium, and potassium are lost in the flood of urine, creating a risk of water and electrolyte depletion. Salt and water overload, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis are hazards of the oliguric maintenance phase, and cortical necrosis belongs to thrombotic microangiopathy or severe ischemia rather than to recovery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATI phases are initiation at about 36 hours, maintenance with 40 to 400 mL per day, and recovery with up to 3 L per day, and recovery depends on intact basement membranes and epithelial regeneration. COMLEX: monitor volume status and electrolytes in the polyuric phase, and remember the peculiar vulnerability to infection at this stage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — intrarenal feedback loops and the hazards of the recovery phase of tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Tubulointerstitial & Acute Kidney Injury Evaluation","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia has had nocturia for 8 months. Ultrasonography shows bilateral hydronephrosis. Which functional abnormality most likely appeared first as a result of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Marked fall in glomerular filtration rate","B":"Nephrotic-range proteinuria with lipiduria","C":"Red cell casts with dysmorphic hematuria","D":"Impaired urine concentrating ability","E":"Metabolic alkalosis with renal sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In bilateral partial obstruction the earliest manifestation is inability to concentrate urine, reflected by polyuria and nocturia. High pelvic pressure is transmitted back through the collecting ducts and compresses the medullary vasculature, reducing inner medullary blood flow, so the initial functional alterations are largely tubular and the GFR falls only later. Nephrotic-range proteinuria and red cell casts are glomerular findings, and Robbins lists distal tubular acidosis and renal salt wasting, not alkalosis with sodium retention, among the tubular defects that can develop in bilateral partial obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstruction first causes tubular concentrating defects, with GFR decline later, and interstitial inflammation leads eventually to fibrosis. COMLEX: relief of obstruction in the first few weeks restores normal function, and a massive sodium-rich diuresis can follow relief of complete obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — mechanical, vascular, and functional progression of hydronephrosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman has 2 days of fever, confusion, and petechiae. Hemoglobin is 7.4 g/dL with schistocytes on the smear, the platelet count is 16,000/microliter, and creatinine is 1.5 mg/dL. She has had no diarrhea, and plasma ADAMTS13 activity is markedly reduced. Which of the following best explains the thrombi in her small vessels?","choices":{"A":"Shiga-like toxin activating endothelial cells and platelets","B":"Unopposed alternative pathway C3 convertase activity","C":"Urinary loss of antithrombin III with hypercoagulability","D":"Immune complexes lodging in glomerular capillary walls","E":"Ultralarge von Willebrand multimers activating platelets"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, neurologic symptoms, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction form the classic pentad of TTP, in which central nervous system involvement dominates and severe renal injury is uncommon. ADAMTS13 deficiency, most often from inhibitory autoantibodies, lets very large von Willebrand factor multimers accumulate, bind platelet glycoproteins, and spontaneously activate platelets, producing microthrombi. Shiga-like toxin causes typical HUS after diarrhea, unopposed alternative pathway activity underlies complement-mediated atypical HUS with normal ADAMTS13, antithrombin III loss is a hypercoagulable feature of nephrotic syndrome, and glomerular immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis rather than microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schistocytes with severe thrombocytopenia, neurologic symptoms, and low ADAMTS13 indicate TTP. COMLEX: plasma exchange removes autoantibodies and supplies functional ADAMTS13, and it treats TTP successfully in more than 80 percent of patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — microthrombi, hemolysis, and thrombocytopenia across the thrombotic microangiopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman had a biopsy-proven glomerulonephritis 12 years ago that has been serologically quiescent for 8 years. Her GFR has now fallen to 40 percent of normal and continues to decline, with proteinuria and hypertension. A repeat biopsy shows global glomerulosclerosis, interstitial scarring, and tubular atrophy. Which of the following best explains the ongoing decline?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing antigen-driven immune complex deposition from the original disease","B":"Recurrent ascending bacterial infection with neutrophilic tubulitis","C":"Hyperfiltration of surviving nephrons driving sclerosis and fibrosis","D":"Compensatory nephron regeneration with falling glomerular capillary pressure","E":"Chronic obstruction from ureteral stones with progressive hydronephrosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once nephron loss reduces the GFR to about 30 to 50 percent of normal, progression to end-stage disease continues at a steady rate independent of the original insult. Surviving glomeruli undergo hypertrophy with increased blood flow, filtration, and transcapillary pressure, which injures podocytes and drives progressive glomerulosclerosis, and tubulointerstitial fibrosis follows and correlates closely with functional decline. Ongoing immune injury would show serologic and histologic activity, and infection, capillary hypotension, and obstruction are not supported by the findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the final common pathway of chronic kidney disease shows glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and arteriosclerosis, with TGF-beta central to matrix deposition. COMLEX: renin-angiotensin system inhibitors lower intraglomerular hypertension and are the most successful intervention to slow progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — the shared mechanism of progression across chronic kidney diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Renovascular & Chronic Kidney Disease Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has severe hypertension for 6 months, a bruit over the left flank, and elevated plasma renin activity. Imaging shows a small left kidney and a normal-sized right kidney. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the narrowing that led to her hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Atheroma at the origin of the main artery","B":"Cholesterol atheroembolism of arcuate arteries","C":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis of small arteries","D":"Fibromuscular thickening of the arterial wall","E":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Severe hypertension, a flank bruit, elevated renin, and a small ischemic kidney indicate renal artery stenosis. Fibromuscular dysplasia, fibrous or fibromuscular thickening that may involve the intima, media, or adventitia, is more common in females and in younger age groups, typically the third and fourth decades. Atheromatous plaque at the renal artery origin causes 70 percent of stenosis but occurs mostly in older men, atheroemboli occlude arcuate and interlobular arteries after aortic procedures, and hyperplastic and hyaline arteriolosclerosis are small-vessel lesions of malignant and benign hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal artery stenosis causes hypertension through renin release from the ischemic kidney and angiotensin II, and almost all patients show a fall in pressure with angiotensin II blockade. COMLEX: unilateral disease is potentially curable by surgery, with cure rates of 70 to 80 percent in well-selected cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — renovascular hypertension and the renin-mediated vicious cycle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has hypertension, flank pain, and hematuria. Imaging shows bilaterally enlarged kidneys filled with cysts and several liver cysts, and his mother had similar findings. He inherited one mutant copy of PKD1, yet cysts involve only a minority of his nephrons. Which mechanism best explains how individual cysts arise?","choices":{"A":"Acquired somatic loss of the second allele in a tubular cell","B":"Inherited biallelic loss throughout the tubular epithelium","C":"Mitochondrial heteroplasmy within tubular epithelium","D":"Random inactivation of the mutant X chromosome","E":"Dominant negative gain of function in the tubular epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is inherited as a dominant trait, but both alleles of the involved gene must be nonfunctional for cyst development. Each cyst is clonal, arising in a cell that carries the inherited mutation plus an acquired somatic mutation that disables the second allele, so cysts initially involve a minority of nephrons and renal function is retained until the fourth or fifth decade. Bilateral involvement and hepatic cysts derived from biliary epithelium fit the disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of polycystin-1 or polycystin-2 disrupts ciliary mechanosensing and calcium signaling, promoting tubular proliferation and fluid secretion. COMLEX: intracranial berry aneurysms arise in the circle of Willis, and subarachnoid hemorrhage accounts for death in 4 to 10 percent of patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — genetics and pathogenesis of adult and infantile polycystic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections has flank pain and fever. Urine pH is 8.0 and culture grows Proteus mirabilis. Imaging shows a large branching stone filling the right renal pelvis and calyces. Which mechanism best explains the stone formation?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalciuria from excessive intestinal calcium absorption","B":"Persistent urine acidity with uric acid supersaturation","C":"Defective renal reabsorption of cystine and other amino acids","D":"Urease-mediated hydrolysis of urea alkalinizing the urine","E":"Acquired enteric overabsorption of dietary oxalate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus and some staphylococci convert urea to ammonia, and the resulting alkaline urine precipitates magnesium ammonium phosphate salts. These form some of the largest stones, including staghorn calculi that cast the pelvis and calyces. Calcium oxalate stones relate to hypercalciuria and hyperoxaluria, uric acid stones to acidic urine, and cystine stones to defective amino acid reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urease-positive infection means alkaline urine, struvite stones, and staghorn calculi, with coffin lid crystals per First Aid. COMLEX: stones predispose to infection through obstruction and mucosal trauma, and struvite management requires both eradicating infection and removing the stone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — urinary pH, infectious triggers, and stone chemistry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Renal Cyst & Calculi Chemistry Case Resolution","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with acute leukemia is receiving chemotherapy. He now has severe colicky flank pain and hematuria. A plain abdominal radiograph shows no stone, but CT shows a 6-mm stone in the left ureter. Fresh specimen pH is 5.2. Which mechanism most likely produced the stone?","choices":{"A":"Ammonia release by urea-splitting infection","B":"Uric acid precipitation in persistently acidic urine","C":"Idiopathic fasting hypercalciuria with normal parathyroid function","D":"Inherited defect in proximal cystine reabsorption","E":"Hypocitraturia from chronic diarrhea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uric acid stones occur with hyperuricemia and rapid cell turnover such as leukemia, when chemotherapy kills tumor cells and nucleic acid breakdown raises uric acid production. Uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine, and a tendency to excrete urine of pH below 5.5 predisposes to precipitation. In contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, uric acid stones are radiolucent, explaining the normal plain film with a stone on CT. Urease-mediated stones need alkaline urine, cystinuria is an inherited reabsorption defect, and idiopathic hypercalciuria and hypocitraturia produce calcium stones, which are radiopaque and unrelated to chemotherapy for leukemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: radiolucent, acid-urine stones with gout or high cell turnover are uric acid stones, and rhomboid crystals are the classic urine finding per First Aid. COMLEX: uric acid stones are present in 22 percent of individuals with gout and 42 percent of those with secondary hyperuricemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — radiodensity and predisposing pH across the four major calculi types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who smokes has intermittent hematuria, flank pain, and a 6-kg weight loss. Hematocrit is 55 percent and serum calcium is 12.1 mg/dL. CT shows a solitary 9-cm heterogeneous mass in the upper pole of the right kidney with a tumor column extending through the renal vein toward the inferior vena cava. Which molecular abnormality most likely drives this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Gain-of-function MET mutation with trisomy 7","B":"TFE3 gene translocation at Xp11.2","C":"Fumarate hydratase loss with aggressive papillary histology","D":"Multiple chromosome losses with extreme hypodiploidy","E":"Biallelic VHL inactivation with stabilized HIF-1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A solitary mass in a smoker with a tumor column in the renal vein extending toward the inferior vena cava fits clear cell RCC, the most common subtype at 70 to 80 percent, which characteristically invades the renal vein. Polycythemia and hypercalcemia are among the hormone-related syndromes of renal cell carcinoma. In 98 percent of these tumors one copy of chromosome 3 is deleted at 3p25.3, the site of VHL, and the second copy is disabled in up to 80 percent, so the VHL ubiquitin ligase fails to degrade HIF-1 and its levels stay high even under normoxia, driving VEGF and IGF-1. MET gain and trisomy 7 characterize papillary carcinoma, TFE3 translocation defines Xp11 carcinoma, fumarate hydratase loss underlies hereditary leiomyomatosis and RCC, and hypodiploidy characterizes chromophobe carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clear cell RCC arises with chromosome 3 and VHL loss and has polygonal clear cells rich in lipid and glycogen, and First Aid lists PTHrP, ectopic EPO, ACTH, and renin as paraneoplastic products. COMLEX: tobacco doubles the incidence, and the lungs, over 50 percent, and bones, 33 percent, are the most common metastatic sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — molecular drivers and gross behavior of the renal cell carcinoma subtypes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with epilepsy, intellectual disability, and facial skin lesions has sudden severe flank pain and hypotension. CT shows a 7-cm fat-containing right renal mass with retroperitoneal hemorrhage. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Renal oncocytoma","B":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","C":"Renal papillary adenoma","D":"Renal angiomyolipoma","E":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Angiomyolipoma is a benign tumor of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat found in 25 to 50 percent of patients with tuberous sclerosis, which is caused by loss-of-function mutations in TSC1 or TSC2 and features epilepsy, intellectual disability, and skin abnormalities. Its clinical importance lies in its susceptibility to spontaneous hemorrhage, explaining the sudden flank pain and hypotension. Oncocytoma is composed of eosinophilic cells rich in mitochondria with a central scar in one-third of cases, papillary adenomas are small cortical nodules, and clear cell and chromophobe carcinomas are malignant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tuberous sclerosis from TSC1 or TSC2 tumor suppressor loss causes renal angiomyolipomas, whose vessel, smooth muscle, and fat composition is the hallmark. COMLEX: oncocytoma accounts for about 5 percent of renal neoplasms and is thought to arise from intercalated cells, and angiomyolipomas can bleed spontaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — differentiating malignant renal tumors from benign mimics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl43_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Renal Parenchymal & Pediatric Neoplasm Diagnosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has a large palpable flank mass, hematuria, and blood pressure above the 95th percentile. Examination shows absence of the iris, a genitourinary malformation, and developmental delay. Histologic examination of the resected mass shows a malignant embryonic renal tumor. Which gene deletion most likely underlies this constellation?","choices":{"A":"VHL","B":"WT1","C":"TSC2","D":"MET","E":"PKHD1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A large unilateral flank mass with hematuria and possible hypertension in a toddler indicates Wilms tumor, the most common renal malignancy of early childhood, ages 2 to 4. Absence of the iris, genitourinary malformations, and developmental delay define WAGR complex, which results from WT1 deletion. VHL loss underlies clear cell renal cell carcinoma, TSC2 loss underlies angiomyolipoma in tuberous sclerosis, MET activation underlies hereditary papillary carcinoma, and PKHD1 mutation causes autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1 (First Aid): WT1 loss also appears in Denys-Drash syndrome with diffuse mesangial sclerosis and gonadal dysgenesis. COMLEX: renal cell carcinoma is the most common malignant renal tumor overall, and Wilms tumor is the leading pediatric renal malignancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations in pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":44,"title":"SDL 44 — Diuretic Foundations, Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors & Osmotic Agents","questions":[{"id":"sdl44_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"Which portion of the nephron contains the organic acid secretory systems that deliver many diuretics into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"Medullary thick ascending limb","B":"Early distal convoluted tubule","C":"Cortical collecting duct","D":"Inner medullary collecting duct","E":"Proximal tubule S2 segment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Organic acid secretory systems are located in the middle third of the straight part of the proximal tubule, the S2 segment. They secrete organic acids such as uric acid, NSAIDs, diuretics, and antibiotics from the blood into the luminal fluid, which helps deliver most diuretics to the luminal side of the tubule where they act. Organic base systems for creatinine and choline lie in the S1 and S2 segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organic acid secretion in the proximal tubule delivers diuretics to the lumen, and NSAIDs and probenecid compete for weak acid secretion. COMLEX: a patient on a loop diuretic who starts an NSAID may respond less well to the diuretic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of plasma solutes accumulates almost in proportion to nephron loss because their excretion depends largely on glomerular filtration?","choices":{"A":"Sodium and chloride","B":"Creatinine and urea","C":"Phosphate and urate","D":"Glucose and amino acids","E":"Calcium and magnesium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Creatinine and urea depend largely on glomerular filtration for excretion and are not reabsorbed as avidly as electrolytes, so they accumulate almost in proportion to the number of nephrons lost. Plasma sodium and chloride stay nearly constant even with severe GFR reduction because tubular reabsorption falls, and phosphate, urate, and hydrogen ion stay near normal until GFR falls below 20 to 30 percent of normal and then rise less than proportionately.","boardPrep":"Step 1: at steady state creatinine excretion equals production, so plasma creatinine rises as GFR falls. COMLEX: rising creatinine and urea are the laboratory markers used to follow loss of kidney function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"By which process is intravenous mannitol mainly eliminated from the body?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular filtration","B":"Proximal tubular secretion","C":"Hepatic metabolism","D":"Biliary excretion","E":"Distal tubular secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mannitol is not metabolized and is excreted by glomerular filtration within 30 to 60 minutes, without any important tubular reabsorption or secretion. It is poorly absorbed from the gut, so systemic effects require intravenous administration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: because mannitol depends on filtration for excretion, a low GFR leaves it in the blood and risks volume overload. COMLEX: mannitol is used cautiously even in mild renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone released after diuretic-induced volume depletion increases K+ secretion by the collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Aldosterone","C":"Calcitonin","D":"Glucagon","E":"Thyroxine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diuretics cause total body volume depletion, which stimulates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to make more aldosterone. Aldosterone increases ENaC number, Na+/K+-ATPase activity, and K+ channel number in principal cells, so K+ is secreted and lost in the urine. Increased tubular flow adds to the K+ loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemia through higher distal flow and aldosterone. COMLEX: heart failure patients on loop diuretics need careful monitoring, because hypokalemia raises arrhythmia risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which diuretic class keeps water in the lumen with a nonreabsorbed solute, rather than by inhibiting one transporter?","choices":{"A":"Loop diuretics","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Osmotic diuretics","D":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","E":"Potassium-sparing diuretics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Osmotic diuretics are freely filtered but not reabsorbed, so they act as an effective osmole that keeps water in the tubule, with the strongest effect in the proximal tubule and the same mechanism throughout the nephron. Loop diuretics block NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter in the distal tubule, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act in the proximal tubule, and K+-sparing agents act in the collecting duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mannitol acts throughout the nephron with no single transporter target. COMLEX: mannitol is mainly used for elevated intracranial or intraocular pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which force drives K+ secretion across the luminal membrane of collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic gradient from tubular water reabsorption","B":"Proton gradient from intercalated cell H+ secretion","C":"Calcium gradient from paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption","D":"Lumen-negative potential from Na+ reabsorption","E":"Hydrostatic gradient from increased tubular flow rate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the collecting duct Na+ is reabsorbed and K+ is secreted. Na+ reabsorption generates an electrical gradient that makes the lumen negative with respect to the cell, and this drives K+ secretion. ENaC blockers reduce this gradient by making the lumen more positive, which lowers K+ secretion and spares K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lumen-negative potential from ENaC-mediated Na+ entry explains why K+-sparing diuretics conserve K+. COMLEX: K+-sparing diuretics are used as adjuncts to K+-wasting diuretics to avoid hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter couples Na+ entry to H+ secretion during proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter","B":"Apical Na+/Cl- symporter","C":"Basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase","D":"Apical H+/K+ ATPase","E":"Apical Na+/H+ antiporter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3- reabsorption begins with an Na+/H+ antiporter that pumps sodium into the proximal tubule cell and hydrogen ions into the lumen. Luminal H+ combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, carbonic anhydrase converts it to CO2 and water, and the CO2 diffuses into the cell. Because bicarbonate reabsorption is linked to Na+ reabsorption through this antiporter, blocking carbonic anhydrase reduces both.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide reduces Na+ and HCO3- reabsorption because the two are linked through the Na+/H+ antiporter. COMLEX: the effect is a mild diuresis, because other transporters compensate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal tubular disorder is mimicked by treatment with a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 RTA","B":"Type 4 RTA","C":"Type 2 RTA","D":"Gitelman syndrome","E":"Bartter syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower serum pH and produce a drug-induced proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis with hypokalemia, caused by the tubule inability to reabsorb filtered HCO3-. Urine becomes more alkaline than normal because of the extra bicarbonate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists proximal RTA (type 2) as an adverse effect of acetazolamide. COMLEX: acetazolamide causes acidosis, and its dose must be reduced in renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which electrolyte abnormality accompanies the metabolic acidosis produced by acetazolamide?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypernatremia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hypokalemia","E":"Hyperphosphatemia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors induce a metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, a drug-induced proximal type 2 RTA. Potassium is excreted along with the bicarbonate that the tubule cannot reabsorb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hypokalemia with proximal RTA among the adverse effects of acetazolamide. COMLEX: the alkaline urine can promote calcium phosphate stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism lowers intraocular pressure when a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor treats open-angle glaucoma?","choices":{"A":"Decreased aqueous humor formation by the ciliary body","B":"Increased aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork","C":"Increased aqueous outflow through the uveoscleral pathway","D":"Osmotic movement of water out of the vitreous body","E":"Constriction of the pupil to open the drainage angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ciliary body makes aqueous humor, a solution of mainly Na+ and HCO3-, using CO2 and intracellular carbonic anhydrase to form HCO3-. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease HCO3- production, which reduces aqueous humor production and lowers intraocular pressure. Topical agents such as dorzolamide and brinzolamide act locally.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide, dorzolamide, brinzolamide, and methazolamide all treat glaucoma by reducing aqueous humor production. COMLEX: glaucoma is the most common use of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors overall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure produces the fluid whose formation carbonic anhydrase inhibitors reduce in order to lower intracranial pressure?","choices":{"A":"Ciliary body","B":"Arachnoid granulations","C":"Trabecular meshwork","D":"Choroid plexus","E":"Subarachnoid space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The choroid plexus generates cerebrospinal fluid. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors inhibit intracellular carbonic anhydrase, reduce bicarbonate production, and so reduce CSF production and intracranial pressure in idiopathic intracranial hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide is used for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). COMLEX: this condition has raised intracranial pressure of unknown cause, with no tumor, infection, or structural defect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"By which mechanism does acetazolamide prevent altitude sickness?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate retention that dampens breathing","B":"Carbon dioxide retention that raises oxygen tension","C":"Metabolic acidosis that drives hyperventilation","D":"Pulmonary vasodilation that improves gas exchange","E":"Blood volume expansion that raises oxygen delivery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxemia at altitude causes hyperventilation, CO2 loss, and respiratory alkalosis, with headache and nausea. Acetazolamide blocks carbonic anhydrase in the kidney, increases bicarbonate excretion, and induces metabolic acidosis, which causes compensatory hyperventilation that increases arterial oxygen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists altitude sickness as an acetazolamide indication because it offsets respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: patients with a history of altitude sickness take the drug when they ascend to high elevation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main clinical indication for mannitol?","choices":{"A":"Chronic heart failure with edema","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Prevention of altitude sickness","D":"Recurrent calcium kidney stones","E":"Elevated intracranial pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The most important use of mannitol is to reduce elevated intracranial pressure from CNS infection, trauma, or brain tumors, by drawing water out of the brain into the blood. It is occasionally used for intraocular pressure in acute angle-closure glaucoma. It is contraindicated in congestive heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists elevated intracranial or intraocular pressure and drug overdose among the uses of mannitol. COMLEX: it is usually given in an intensive care setting with close monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"Which property allows mannitol to act as an osmotic diuretic?","choices":{"A":"Free filtration with poor tubular reabsorption","B":"Active secretion into the proximal tubule lumen","C":"Reabsorption with sodium in the proximal tubule","D":"Metabolism to an osmotically active product","E":"Binding to the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mannitol diffuses poorly across cell membranes but is filtered freely across the glomerulus and is not reabsorbed, so it raises tubular fluid osmolality and keeps water in the tubule. The increased osmotic pressure prevents filtered water from being reabsorbed, and sodium and other solutes follow water into the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic diuretics need filtration to reach the lumen, and with a severely reduced GFR the agent cannot be excreted into the urine. COMLEX: the effect is strongest in the proximal tubule but occurs throughout the nephron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"At which nephron segment is the effect of osmotic diuretics most marked?","choices":{"A":"Thick ascending limb","B":"Proximal tubule","C":"Distal convoluted tubule","D":"Cortical collecting duct","E":"Connecting tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The effect of osmotic diuretics is most marked in the proximal tubule, although the same process occurs throughout all segments of the nephron. Loop diuretics act at the thick ascending limb, thiazides at the distal tubule, and K+-sparing diuretics in the collecting duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mannitol acts at the proximal tubule and in the nephron generally, unlike transporter-specific diuretics. COMLEX: it is not a treatment for essential hypertension or heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2) takes oral acetazolamide at the standard dose. After two weeks he reports tingling in his fingers and ringing in his ears, and his serum bicarbonate is 14 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains his increased drug exposure?","choices":{"A":"Increased glomerular filtration of the drug","B":"Increased hepatic first-pass extraction of the drug","C":"Increased distal tubular reabsorption of the drug","D":"Reduced proximal tubular secretion of the drug","E":"Reduced intestinal absorption of the drug"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acetazolamide is excreted by secretion in the proximal tubule S2 segment, so dosing must be reduced in renal insufficiency, and failing secretion raises drug exposure. Paresthesias and tinnitus are recognized adverse effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. Acetazolamide also causes NaHCO3 excretion and metabolic acidosis, and it may accumulate in renal failure, leading to nervous system toxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs eliminated by tubular secretion accumulate when nephron mass is lost. COMLEX: reduce the dose of acetazolamide in renal insufficiency, because it may accumulate in renal failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — saturable carrier-mediated secretory pathways and the PAH titration curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has a GFR that has fallen from 120 to 30 mL/min. He receives a drug at an unchanged constant rate. The drug is freely filtered, is not metabolized, and is neither secreted nor reabsorbed by the tubules. Compared with baseline, what will its steady-state plasma concentration be?","choices":{"A":"Twofold higher","B":"Fourfold higher","C":"Eightfold higher","D":"Equal to baseline","E":"One-half of baseline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For a substance that is only filtered, the excretion rate equals GFR times plasma concentration, as for creatinine. At steady state the excretion rate must equal the input rate, so the plasma concentration rises until the reduced GFR again clears the same amount. A fall in GFR from 120 to 30 mL/min is a fourfold reduction, so the plasma concentration rises fourfold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: creatinine excretion equals GFR times plasma creatinine, and its plasma level rises until excretion again matches production. COMLEX: drugs handled mainly by filtration need dose reduction as GFR falls.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — the clearance equation and how plasma concentration and urine flow determine excretion of a filtered solute","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure and advanced chronic kidney disease (eGFR 15 mL/min/1.73 m2) needs a diuretic whose elimination depends least on renal function. Which drug best fits?","choices":{"A":"Torsemide","B":"Furosemide","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Mannitol","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Torsemide is mainly eliminated by the liver, whereas furosemide is mainly eliminated by the kidney through glomerular filtration and tubular secretion, and bumetanide is eliminated half by each. Acetazolamide is excreted by proximal tubular secretion, mannitol by glomerular filtration, and thiazides are secreted by the organic acid secretory system in the proximal tubule. Mannitol is also contraindicated in congestive heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: torsemide is the loop diuretic with mainly hepatic elimination. COMLEX: loop diuretic half-life depends on renal function, and loop diuretics should not be used in anuric patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure takes furosemide and now has a serum K+ of 3.0 mEq/L with metabolic alkalosis. Which mechanism best explains his potassium loss?","choices":{"A":"Direct blockade of K+ reabsorption in the proximal tubule","B":"Suppressed aldosterone release during volume contraction","C":"Higher distal flow and aldosterone increasing K+ secretion","D":"Inhibited H+/K+ ATPase in collecting duct intercalated cells","E":"Insulin release that shifts K+ from cells into plasma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loop diuretics reach the collecting duct with more sodium and water, and volume depletion stimulates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Both events increase K+ and H+ secretion in the collecting duct, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. The loop itself has little net K+ transport because K+ reabsorbed through NKCC2 is recycled by an apical K+ channel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: agents upstream of the collecting duct increase distal delivery, so they waste K+. COMLEX: hypokalemia greatly increases digoxin toxicity in heart failure patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — principal cell K+ secretion and the factors that regulate it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with hypertension takes hydrochlorothiazide, and his serum K+ is 3.1 mEq/L. His physician adds triamterene to the regimen. At which site does triamterene act to reduce his urinary K+ loss?","choices":{"A":"Cortical collecting duct","B":"Thick ascending limb","C":"Early distal tubule","D":"Proximal convoluted tubule","E":"Thin descending limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"K+-sparing diuretics act in the principal cells of the collecting duct, the final portion of the nephron. Triamterene blocks ENaC, which makes the lumen more positive and lowers K+ secretion. Hydrochlorothiazide acts earlier, in the distal convoluted tubule, and is combined with a K+-sparing agent so that the potassium loss is offset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides waste K+, while ENaC blockers and aldosterone antagonists conserve it at the collecting duct. COMLEX: fixed combinations of a thiazide with triamterene are used to limit hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — electrogenic K+ secretion through apical ROMK and BK channels and the systemic factors that regulate it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old volunteer receives an investigational diuretic. Urinary Na+ excretion rises, urinary K+ excretion falls, and the drug shows no binding to steroid hormone receptors. Which mechanism most likely accounts for these findings?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of the NKCC2 cotransporter in thick limb cells","B":"Blockade of the Na+/Cl- symporter in distal tubule cells","C":"Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule cells","D":"Retention of an unreabsorbed osmotic solute in the lumen","E":"Blockade of luminal Na+ channels in principal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A diuretic that raises Na+ excretion while lowering K+ excretion is K+-sparing. Because it does not act on hormone receptors, it must directly block ENaC in collecting duct principal cells, which prevents Na+ reabsorption and makes the lumen more positive, lowering K+ secretion. Loop and thiazide diuretics and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors raise urinary K+ excretion, and osmotic diuretics increase all urinary electrolytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: amiloride and triamterene block ENaC, and spironolactone and eplerenone block the aldosterone receptor. COMLEX: both K+-sparing subclasses can cause hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — RAAS and aldosterone effects on renal sodium retention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension started oral acetazolamide two days ago. Her blood pH is 7.31, serum HCO3- is 15 mEq/L, Na+ is 139 mEq/L, Cl- is 113 mEq/L, and urine pH is 7.2. Which mechanism best explains the alkaline urine despite her acidemia?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone excess driving distal H+ secretion","B":"Urease-producing bacteria generating urinary ammonia","C":"Ketoacid excretion that buffers urinary protons","D":"Filtered bicarbonate escaping proximal reabsorption","E":"Diarrheal bicarbonate loss with renal compensation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition prevents conversion of luminal H2CO3 to CO2 and water, so filtered HCO3- stays in the tubule and is excreted with Na+. This makes the urine more alkaline than normal while the plasma HCO3- falls, producing a type 2 (proximal) RTA-like acidosis. Her anion gap is 139 - 113 - 15 = 11 mEq/L, which is normal and consistent with bicarbonate loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide causes bicarbonaturia, alkaline urine, and a normal anion gap acidosis. COMLEX: use urine pH and serum K+ together to classify a renal tubular acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — urine pH and serum K+ in the differential of renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man taking acetazolamide for altitude prophylaxis has Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 3.2 mEq/L, Cl- 111 mEq/L, and HCO3- 15 mEq/L, and he has no diarrhea. Which best describes his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap acidosis from ketoacid accumulation","B":"High anion gap acidosis from lactate accumulation","C":"Normal anion gap acidosis from renal HCO3- loss","D":"Normal anion gap acidosis from diarrheal HCO3- loss","E":"High anion gap acidosis from toxic alcohol ingestion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"His anion gap is 138 - 111 - 15 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, so this is a hyperchloremic normal anion gap acidosis. Acetazolamide blocks proximal HCO3- reabsorption, so bicarbonate is lost in the urine along with K+, a drug-induced proximal type 2 RTA. High anion gap acidoses from ketoacids, lactate, or toxic alcohols would show a gap above normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RTA and diarrhea cause normal anion gap acidosis, whereas the MUDPILES causes raise the gap. COMLEX: acetazolamide is a reversible cause of hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — normal versus high anion gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman takes acetazolamide and has a serum K+ of 3.0 mEq/L with a serum HCO3- of 16 mEq/L. She has no vomiting or diarrhea. Which mechanism best explains her hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced K+ reabsorption by the thick ascending limb","B":"Urinary K+ loss with the unreabsorbed bicarbonate","C":"Reduced aldosterone release lowering K+ secretion","D":"Blocked apical K+ channels in principal cells","E":"Increased proximal K+ secretion through the Na+/H+ antiporter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause a proximal type 2 RTA with hypokalemia, and potassium is excreted along with the bicarbonate that cannot be reabsorbed. Volume contraction raises rather than lowers aldosterone, and enhanced K+ reabsorption would raise rather than lower the serum K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hypokalemia with proximal RTA among the adverse effects of acetazolamide. COMLEX: check serum K+ and bicarbonate in patients on long-term acetazolamide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — how tubular flow, luminal anions, and aldosterone regulate principal cell K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with obesity has daily headaches and bilateral papilledema. MRI is normal, and lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 36 cm H2O with normal CSF composition. Acetazolamide is started. Which effect underlies the expected benefit?","choices":{"A":"Greater CSF reabsorption by arachnoid granulations","B":"Osmotic movement of water out of brain tissue","C":"Constriction of cerebral arterioles to lower blood volume","D":"Diuresis that reduces total body water","E":"Less bicarbonate production and reduced CSF secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is elevated intracranial pressure of unknown cause. The choroid plexus generates CSF, and acetazolamide inhibits intracellular carbonic anhydrase, reducing bicarbonate production, CSF production, and intracranial pressure. The diuretic effect of acetazolamide is weak and is not the reason for its benefit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide is a treatment for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). COMLEX: the goal is to prevent permanent brain damage from persistently high intracranial pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — intracellular carbonic anhydrase and cellular bicarbonate transport mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma has an intraocular pressure of 32 mm Hg and starts oral acetazolamide. According to the brick, which pair of ions makes up most of the aqueous humor whose formation the drug reduces?","choices":{"A":"Sodium and bicarbonate","B":"Potassium and bicarbonate","C":"Sodium and phosphate","D":"Potassium and chloride","E":"Calcium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ciliary body makes aqueous humor, a hyperosmotic solution composed primarily of Na+ and HCO3-, using CO2 from plasma and intracellular carbonic anhydrase to form HCO3-. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease HCO3- production, which reduces aqueous humor production from the ciliary bodies and lowers intraocular pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous humor production. COMLEX: topical dorzolamide and brinzolamide avoid renal and systemic effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — intracellular carbonic anhydrase and cellular bicarbonate transport mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with prior high-altitude illness plans a rapid ascent to 4,000 m and takes acetazolamide beginning the day before. Which sequence best explains how the drug protects her?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate retention, metabolic alkalosis, then hyperventilation","B":"Bicarbonaturia, metabolic acidosis, then hyperventilation","C":"Bicarbonaturia, metabolic alkalosis, then hypoventilation","D":"Bicarbonate retention, metabolic acidosis, then hypoventilation","E":"Bicarbonate retention, metabolic acidosis, then hyperventilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypoxemia at altitude induces hyperventilation, respiratory alkalosis, headache, and nausea. Acetazolamide increases urinary bicarbonate excretion and induces metabolic acidosis, which causes compensatory hyperventilation. Hyperventilating ahead of time increases oxygenation, preventing hypoxemia and altitude sickness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the drug treats a respiratory alkalosis by creating a compensating metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: start acetazolamide before ascent in patients with a history of altitude sickness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with traumatic brain injury and oliguric acute kidney injury (urine output 100 mL/day, creatinine 5.8 mg/dL) receives intravenous mannitol for elevated intracranial pressure. Six hours later she has dyspnea, bibasilar crackles, and peripheral edema. Which mechanism best explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Mannitol-induced aldosterone release causing sodium retention","B":"Mannitol-induced ADH release causing free water retention","C":"Direct depression of myocardial contractility by mannitol","D":"Mannitol binding of plasma proteins lowering oncotic pressure","E":"Unexcreted mannitol draws cell water into the plasma volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mannitol depends on glomerular filtration to be excreted. With a severely reduced GFR it cannot be excreted into the urine, so it stays in the blood, and water is drawn from the intracellular regions into the extracellular fluid. The resulting volume overload and plasma volume expansion produce pulmonary or peripheral edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists pulmonary edema among the adverse effects of mannitol, and anuria is a contraindication. COMLEX: severe renal impairment with low urine output is a contraindication to osmotic diuretics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states and fluid shifts between compartments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with severe traumatic brain injury has received repeated intravenous mannitol doses for elevated intracranial pressure. He now has a heart rate of 122/min, blood pressure of 84/48 mm Hg, dry mucous membranes, and a urine output above 250 mL/h. Which complication best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Volume overload from retained unexcreted mannitol","B":"Pulmonary edema from acute cardiac decompensation","C":"Volume depletion from excessive osmotic diuresis","D":"Hyperkalemia from excessive distal potassium retention","E":"Metabolic acidosis from proximal bicarbonate wasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mannitol has powerful diuretic qualities, so patients are at risk for severe volume depletion, with tachycardia, hypotension, and signs of poor perfusion. Serum electrolytes may change, leading to hypokalemia or hyponatremia. Volume overload occurs in patients with poor kidney function who cannot excrete the drug, which does not fit his high urine output. Metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate wasting is an effect of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, not of mannitol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists dehydration and hypo- or hypernatremia among the adverse effects of mannitol. COMLEX: fluid status and electrolytes must be monitored closely during osmotic diuretic therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — clinical volume contraction states","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25 percent has a large intracranial mass with elevated intracranial pressure, and the team plans mannitol. Which complication is of greatest concern?","choices":{"A":"Ototoxicity from rapid intravenous bolus administration","B":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis from hyperkalemia","C":"Hyperuricemia with recurrent acute gout flares","D":"Pulmonary edema from intravascular volume expansion","E":"Calcium phosphate stones from alkaline urine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mannitol raises serum osmolality and expands the plasma volume, and in congestive heart failure the increased plasma volume can exacerbate pulmonary edema, so heart failure is a contraindication. Ototoxicity is a loop diuretic effect, type 4 RTA follows K+-sparing diuretics, hyperuricemia occurs with thiazide and loop diuretics, and alkaline-urine stones follow carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists heart failure and anuria as contraindications to mannitol. COMLEX: congestive heart failure, severe renal impairment with low urine output, and severe dehydration are the main contraindications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — integrated hemodynamic control of body sodium and volume","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":45,"title":"SDL 45 — Loop, Thiazide, & Potassium-Sparing Diuretics (Anatomical Transport & GFR Thresholds)","questions":[{"id":"sdl45_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"According to the loop diuretic brick, which diuretic is the alternative for patients with sulfa drug sensitivity?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide","B":"Ethacrynic acid","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Chlorthalidone","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The brick notes that furosemide, bumetanide, and torsemide are chemically similar because of their sulfonamide group, and it names ethacrynic acid as the alternative loop diuretic for patients with sulfa drug sensitivity. Katzung likewise describes ethacrynic acid as not a sulfonamide derivative, with allergic reactions much less common than with the other loop agents. The precise nature of sulfa allergies is described as controversial and murky.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ethacrynic acid is a nonsulfonamide loop diuretic, and First Aid describes it as more ototoxic than furosemide. COMLEX: use ethacrynic acid only in patients who cannot tolerate other loop diuretics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"Which transport event generates the lumen-positive potential that drives Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Na+ entry through apical ENaC channels","B":"Cl- entry through apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers","C":"K+ back-diffusion through apical channels","D":"H+ secretion through apical H+ pumps","E":"Ca2+ entry through apical calcium channels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter moves K+ into the cell, and K+ then leaves through an apical K+ channel into the lumen. This movement of K+ gives the lumen a positive charge that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption. Loop diuretics reduce this K+ gradient, so the lumen is less positive and Ca2+ and Mg2+ are lost in the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that loops lose Ca2+, and loop diuretics cause hypomagnesemia. COMLEX: hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia from loop diuretics increase the risk of cardiac arrhythmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"By which mechanism do NSAIDs reduce the efficacy of loop diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Blocked prostaglandin synthesis needed for vasodilation","B":"Induced NKCC2 expression that overrides the drug block","C":"Increased tubular reabsorption of the drug back into blood","D":"Direct binding of the drug within the tubular fluid","E":"Stimulated aldosterone release that retains sodium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loop diuretics increase prostaglandin synthesis in renal veins and can dilate afferent arterioles by limiting tubuloglomerular feedback, which raises renal blood flow and GFR. NSAIDs such as aspirin and ibuprofen inhibit the prostaglandins that mediate this vasodilatory effect, so they decrease the efficacy of loop diuretics and should not be used together. NSAIDs can also compete for weak acid secretion in the proximal tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links the loop diuretic effect on the afferent arteriole to PGE and notes that NSAIDs inhibit it. COMLEX: avoid combining NSAIDs with loop diuretics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter do thiazide diuretics inhibit?","choices":{"A":"Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter","B":"Epithelial Na+ channel","C":"Na+/H+ antiporter","D":"Na+/Cl- symporter","E":"Na+/K+ ATPase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter (NCC) in the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule. The Na+/K+-ATPase keeps intracellular sodium low, and this drives sodium entry through the symporter. Thiazides are only moderately potent because approximately 90 percent of filtered sodium has already been reabsorbed before the fluid reaches the DCT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes thiazides as inhibiting NaCl reabsorption in the early DCT. COMLEX: Gitelman syndrome, an inherited defect of the Na+/Cl- symporter, resembles thiazide use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"Why are thiazide diuretics useful for preventing calcium oxalate kidney stones?","choices":{"A":"Competitive binding of calcium within the tubular fluid","B":"Increased reabsorption of calcium from the tubular fluid","C":"Decreased plasma calcium concentration from diuresis","D":"Decreased glomerular filtration of calcium by the kidney","E":"Impaired transport of oxalate into the tubular fluid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazides increase calcium reabsorption in the renal tubule, which lowers urinary calcium and reduces the recurrence of calcium-containing stones. The effect arises partly from mild volume depletion, which enhances proximal sodium and passive calcium reabsorption, and partly from enhanced Na+/Ca2+ exchange in the DCT. Thiazides tend to raise rather than lower plasma calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists idiopathic hypercalciuria among thiazide uses and hypercalcemia among their adverse effects. COMLEX: thiazides may modestly reduce osteoporotic fracture risk but are not primary bone drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"According to Katzung, thiazide diuretics are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below which value?","choices":{"A":"Below 30 mL/min","B":"Below 10 mL/min","C":"Below 15 mL/min","D":"Below 20 mL/min","E":"Below 45 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung states that thiazide diuretics are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below 30 mL/min, although the exact GFR at which they lose benefit is still debated and may be lower than previously imagined. Thiazides can still lower the loop diuretic dose needed in patients with a GFR of 5 to 15 mL/min, for example metolazone combined with furosemide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that thiazides potentiate loop diuretics in refractory volume overload. COMLEX: as GFR declines, loop diuretics rather than thiazides are used for edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent directly blocks epithelial Na+ channels in collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Spironolactone","B":"Amiloride","C":"Eplerenone","D":"Acetazolamide","E":"Chlorthalidone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two K+-sparing subclasses are ENaC blockers, amiloride and triamterene, and aldosterone receptor antagonists, spironolactone and eplerenone. ENaC blockers directly block sodium reabsorption through the luminal channel, without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the ATPase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists amiloride and triamterene as Na+ channel blockers in the cortical collecting tubule, with amiloride used in nephrogenic DI. COMLEX: hyperkalemia is the main adverse effect of these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"How do ENaC blockers reduce K+ secretion in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Direct blockade of apical K+ channels in principal cells","B":"Blockade of the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase pump","C":"Competitive inhibition of the mineralocorticoid receptor","D":"Inhibition of H+ ATPase in intercalated cells","E":"Less Na+ entry weakens the lumen-negative potential"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ENaC blockers stop sodium reabsorption through the luminal channel. This makes the cell more negatively charged and the lumen more positively charged, which lowers K+ loss through the potassium channels. They act without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the Na+/K+-ATPase, which are the targets of the aldosterone antagonists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced Na+ entry lowers the lumen-negative potential, so K+ is spared. COMLEX: amiloride or triamterene is often combined with a thiazide to cancel its K+-wasting effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Why does eplerenone cause less gynecomastia than spironolactone?","choices":{"A":"Higher affinity for the androgen receptor","B":"Additional blockade of epithelial Na+ channels","C":"Inhibition of aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal gland","D":"Greater selectivity for the mineralocorticoid receptor","E":"Greater potency at the thiazide-sensitive symporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone has antiandrogen and estrogen-like effects because of its structural similarity to sex steroid hormones, and in men it can decrease libido, cause sexual dysfunction, and cause gynecomastia. Eplerenone is much more selective than spironolactone for the mineralocorticoid receptor and is virtually inactive on androgen or progesterone receptors, so these effects are less severe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists gynecomastia and antiandrogen effects among the adverse effects of spironolactone. COMLEX: switching from spironolactone to eplerenone is the option for a man with gynecomastia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of metabolic adverse effects is characteristic of thiazide diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and ototoxicity","B":"Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and gynecomastia","C":"Hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, and hyperuricemia","D":"Metabolic acidosis, paresthesias, and tinnitus","E":"Volume depletion, pulmonary edema, and kidney injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides are hyperGLUC: hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia. Hyperglycemia reflects reduced insulin secretion and altered glucose metabolism, and hyperuricemia reflects volume-related proximal uric acid reabsorption and competition for secretion. Loop diuretics cause hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and ototoxicity, K+-sparing agents cause hyperkalemia and acidosis with gynecomastia from spironolactone, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause acidosis, paresthesias, and tinnitus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia as thiazide adverse effects. COMLEX: thiazides should be avoided in patients with gout or metabolic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Which electrolyte abnormality do thiazides cause by limiting maximal urinary dilution?","choices":{"A":"Hyponatremia","B":"Hypocalcemia","C":"Hyperkalemia","D":"Hypermagnesemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazides block sodium reabsorption in the DCT diluting segment, so the kidney cannot make maximally dilute urine. A patient who drinks a lot of water and eats little food then retains water and becomes hyponatremic, often without looking volume depleted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides can cause hyponatremia yet treat the hypernatremia of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. COMLEX: poor food intake with high water intake increases the risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics suppresses proximal tubule synthesis of ammonia. Which acid-base disorder results?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 RTA","B":"Type 2 RTA","C":"Lactic acidosis","D":"Ketoacidosis","E":"Type 4 RTA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics may suppress proximal tubule synthesis of ammonia, the buffer needed to excrete acid. This produces a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with a high serum potassium, hyperkalemic type 4 RTA, most often seen with aldosterone antagonists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis as adverse effects of K+-sparing diuretics. COMLEX: potassium levels should be checked regularly in patients taking these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with gout takes probenecid. Furosemide 40 mg daily, which previously controlled his edema, now produces little diuresis. Which mechanism best explains the reduced response?","choices":{"A":"Competition for organic acid secretion lowers luminal drug delivery","B":"Inhibited NKCC2 expression from uric acid retention","C":"Enhanced proximal reabsorption of filtered furosemide","D":"Accelerated hepatic conversion of furosemide to inactive metabolites","E":"Blocked afferent arteriolar dilation by tubuloglomerular feedback"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loop diuretics act on the luminal side of the tubule, so their diuretic activity correlates with their secretion by the proximal tubule. Probenecid, like NSAIDs, competes for weak acid secretion in the proximal tubule, which reduces the amount of furosemide reaching NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics reach the lumen by proximal tubular organic acid secretion, and competing acids blunt their effect. COMLEX: reassess the diuretic dose when a patient starts probenecid or an NSAID.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — bidirectional proximal tubule handling of organic acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with heart failure and stage 5 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 12 mL/min/1.73 m2) still makes urine but has no diuretic response to oral furosemide 40 mg. Which adjustment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Convert furosemide to hydrochlorothiazide monotherapy","B":"Add spironolactone to potentiate diuresis","C":"Give intravenous mannitol to force diuresis","D":"Substantially raise the furosemide dose","E":"Switch to acetazolamide for bicarbonate diuresis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With severe renal insufficiency (GFR 5 to 15 mL/min), diuretics can still work, and high-dose loop diuretics, up to 500 mg/day of furosemide, may be needed. Loop diuretic activity depends on delivery to the lumen by proximal tubular secretion. Thiazides are thought to be ineffective below a GFR of 30 mL/min, potassium-sparing agents cause hyperkalemia, mannitol cannot be excreted when GFR is low, and acetazolamide can exacerbate acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics remain the agents for volume overload when GFR is low. COMLEX: loop diuretics should not be used in anuric patients, because they will not work and ototoxicity risk may increase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 1 — falling GFR limits drug clearance and tubular drug delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has a serum calcium of 13.8 mg/dL and is volume depleted. After saline repletion she receives intravenous furosemide, and her urinary calcium excretion rises. Which mechanism best explains the calciuresis?","choices":{"A":"Blocked PTH-sensitive Ca2+ channels in the distal tubule","B":"Lost lumen-positive potential driving paracellular Ca2+ uptake","C":"Suppressed calcitriol synthesis in the proximal tubule","D":"Stimulated calcitonin release that raises urinary calcium excretion","E":"Inhibited basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange in proximal cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2, so K+ movement into the lumen through the potassium channel falls and the lumen becomes less positive. There is then less drive for paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption, and both are lost in the urine. This is why loop diuretics are used together with normal saline to treat acute hypercalcemia in patients with malignancy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loops lose Ca2+, whereas thiazides increase Ca2+ reabsorption. COMLEX: loop diuretics with normal saline treat acute hypercalcemia, and hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia from loop use are usually mild.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — renal calcium handling along the nephron","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2) has peripheral edema and a blood pressure of 150/90 mm Hg. Using the GFR threshold for thiazide efficacy given in Katzung, which single diuretic is most likely to be effective?","choices":{"A":"Hydrochlorothiazide","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Furosemide","D":"Amiloride","E":"Mannitol"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Katzung states that thiazides are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below 30 mL/min, although the exact threshold remains debated, so by that threshold hydrochlorothiazide is unlikely to work at an eGFR of 22. Acetazolamide must usually be avoided in kidney disease because it causes NaHCO3 excretion and can exacerbate acidosis, potassium-sparing diuretics may cause hyperkalemia, and mannitol cannot be excreted when GFR is reduced. A loop diuretic is the appropriate agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide efficacy falls as GFR declines, whereas loop diuretics keep working at low GFR. COMLEX: excessive diuresis can impair renal function, and the consequences are more serious in patients with underlying renal disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — loss of functioning nephron mass in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus has polyuria of 6 L/day and a serum Na+ of 148 mEq/L. Her physician starts hydrochlorothiazide, and her daily urine output falls. Which mechanism best explains the benefit?","choices":{"A":"Restored ADH receptor signaling in collecting duct cells","B":"Hypovolemia increases proximal Na+ and water reabsorption","C":"Greater medullary interstitial osmolality from NKCC2 inhibition","D":"Direct insertion of aquaporin-2 channels into the DCT","E":"Blocked ADH release from the posterior pituitary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In nephrogenic DI the tubules are resistant to ADH. Thiazides induce relative hypovolemia, which activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and angiotensin II increases sodium and water reabsorption in the proximal tubule. Less water is delivered to the distal nephron, so polyuria and hypernatremia fall, with urine volume reduced by up to 50 percent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides can cause hyponatremia yet treat hypernatremia in nephrogenic DI, and First Aid lists nephrogenic DI as an indication. COMLEX: nephrogenic DI is usually due to chronic interstitial renal disease or drugs such as lithium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — central versus nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and the response to exogenous ADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with hypertension has recurrent calcium-containing kidney stones and hypercalciuria. He starts hydrochlorothiazide, and his urinary calcium falls. Which mechanisms best explain this effect?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced proximal Ca2+ uptake and distal Na+/Ca2+ exchange","B":"Blocked distal Na+/Ca2+ exchange with impaired proximal uptake","C":"Greater paracellular Ca2+ uptake in the thick ascending limb","D":"Reduced glomerular filtration of Ca2+ from volume expansion","E":"Direct binding of Ca2+ by the drug within the tubular fluid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazide-induced volume depletion enhances proximal sodium and passive calcium reabsorption. In the DCT, lower intracellular sodium from NCC blockade enhances Na+/Ca2+ exchange across the basolateral membrane, so overall calcium reabsorption rises. Thiazides are therefore used to prevent calcium-containing kidney stones caused by hypercalciuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides increase Ca2+ reabsorption and treat idiopathic hypercalciuria, whereas loop diuretics waste Ca2+. COMLEX: thiazides can unmask hypercalcemia from other causes such as primary hyperparathyroidism, carcinoma, or sarcoidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — calculus chemistry of calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate stones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis and ascites is treated with spironolactone. Which cellular effect in collecting duct principal cells produces his natriuresis?","choices":{"A":"Direct plugging of the epithelial sodium channel pore","B":"Blocked aldosterone signaling that lowers ENaC activity","C":"Inhibition of the NKCC2 cotransporter in the luminal membrane","D":"Increased number of apical K+ channels for secretion","E":"Stimulation of basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spironolactone competitively inhibits the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor, so aldosterone can no longer increase ENaC activity, Na+/K+-ATPase activity, or the number of K+ channels. Sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion are blocked, which produces a sodium diuresis that pulls excess fluid from the peritoneum. Direct channel blockers such as amiloride act at ENaC itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic ascites, heart failure, and hyperaldosteronism among the uses of spironolactone. COMLEX: patients with end-stage liver disease are often malnourished and prone to hypokalemia, so K+ sparing is useful.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — late distal principal cell transport and the kinetics of aldosterone action","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with hypertension takes hydrochlorothiazide, and his serum K+ is 3.1 mEq/L. Amiloride is added. Which pattern best describes the effect of amiloride on urinary electrolytes and blood pH?","choices":{"A":"Higher Na+ and K+ excretion with alkalosis","B":"Higher Na+ and HCO3- excretion with acidosis","C":"Higher Na+ and lower K+ excretion with acidosis","D":"Higher Na+, K+, and Cl- excretion with unchanged pH","E":"Higher Na+ and lower Ca2+ excretion with alkalosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sodium channel inhibitors raise urinary Na+ and lower urinary K+ and tend to cause acidosis, because they inhibit H+ secretion in parallel with K+ secretion, and chronic hyperkalemia may also suppress proximal tubule ammonia synthesis. Loop diuretics raise Na+, K+, and Ca2+ and cause alkalosis, thiazides raise Na+ and K+, lower Ca2+, and cause alkalosis, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors raise HCO3- and cause acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: K+-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis, whereas loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: a combined thiazide and ENaC blocker offsets the potassium loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — acid-base status and transcellular H+-K+ exchange","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients with edema receive different potassium-sparing diuretics, one taking amiloride and the other spironolactone. Which cellular effect do both drugs share in collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Reduced H+ ATPase activity on the apical membrane","B":"Reduced Na+/K+ ATPase activity on the basolateral membrane","C":"Reduced number of ENaCs in the basolateral membrane","D":"Increased number of ENaCs in the apical membrane","E":"Reduced Na+ influx from the tubular fluid into the cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both subclasses reduce sodium entry from the tubular fluid into principal cells, amiloride by blocking ENaC directly and spironolactone by blocking the aldosterone effect that increases ENaC number and activity. Amiloride acts without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the ATPase, whereas spironolactone also reduces Na+/K+-ATPase activity. ENaC is an apical channel, and neither drug increases its number.","boardPrep":"Step 1: both classes cause hyperkalemia by reducing K+ secretion at the collecting duct. COMLEX: both classes are weak diuretics because most sodium is already reabsorbed by the time fluid reaches the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy (eGFR 34 mL/min/1.73 m2) and hypertension takes lisinopril. Spironolactone is added, and 10 days later his serum K+ is 6.4, Na+ 135, Cl- 108, and HCO3- 17 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains his acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Inherited H+-ATPase defect in alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Impaired proximal HCO3- reabsorption from enzyme inhibition","C":"Gastrointestinal HCO3- loss from chronic diarrhea","D":"Suppressed proximal ammonia synthesis from hyperkalemia","E":"Accumulation of lactate from tissue hypoperfusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics, made worse by an ACE inhibitor and reduced GFR, may suppress proximal tubule ammonia synthesis, the buffer needed to excrete body acid. The result is a normal anion gap acidosis with high serum potassium, hyperkalemic type 4 RTA. His anion gap is 135 - 108 - 17 = 10 mEq/L, which is normal, so lactate is not the cause. Katzung also notes that K+-sparing diuretics can inhibit H+ secretion in parallel with K+ secretion, but the brick attributes the type 4 acidosis to hyperkalemia suppressing ammonia synthesis. An inherited H+-ATPase defect would produce a distal type 1 picture with hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia inhibits proximal ammoniagenesis and causes type 4 RTA. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and oral potassium supplements increase the hyperkalemia risk with potassium-sparing diuretics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — the pathophysiology of hyperkalemic (Type 4) RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman who eats very little and drinks large volumes of fluid started hydrochlorothiazide two weeks ago for hypertension. She is confused, appears euvolemic, and has a serum Na+ of 122 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains her hyponatremia?","choices":{"A":"Impaired urinary concentration from a lost medullary gradient","B":"Excess sodium loss from blocked thick limb transport","C":"Aldosterone-driven sodium retention with water dilution","D":"Autonomous ADH release from an occult lung tumor","E":"Impaired maximal urinary dilution with continued water intake"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Thiazides block sodium reabsorption in the DCT diluting segment, so the kidney cannot excrete fully diluted urine, and drinking a lot of water while eating little food leads to water retention and hyponatremia. Hypovolemia-induced ADH release and increased thirst also contribute, and these patients often do not look volume depleted. Elderly women are most prone to thiazide-induced hyponatremia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyponatremia among the adverse effects of thiazides. COMLEX: thiazide-induced hyponatremia can be prevented by reducing the dose or limiting water intake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — SIADH versus diabetes insipidus and free water handling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man takes hydrochlorothiazide and nifedipine, and his blood pressure is 162/92 mm Hg. His history includes hyperlipidemia with elevated LDL cholesterol, obesity with a BMI of 37, and a GFR of 38 mL/min/1.73 m2. Amiloride is considered. Which feature most argues against adding it?","choices":{"A":"Limited renal capacity to excrete potassium","B":"Obesity with a body mass index of 37","C":"Hyperlipidemia with elevated LDL cholesterol","D":"Concurrent nifedipine calcium channel blockade","E":"Concurrent hydrochlorothiazide therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Potassium-sparing diuretics can worsen underlying hyperkalemia, so they are contraindicated in significant acute or chronic renal impairment, and they should be used with caution in anyone prone to hyperkalemia such as patients with chronic kidney disease. Hydrochlorothiazide wastes potassium and is often paired with a potassium-sparing agent. Obesity, hyperlipidemia, and a calcium channel blocker are not listed contraindications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: K+-sparing diuretics carry a risk of hyperkalemia that rises when renal K+ excretion is impaired. COMLEX: significant renal impairment is a contraindication, and potassium should be monitored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — segmental potassium handling along the nephron","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":46,"title":"SDL 46 — Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) Inhibitors & Male Reproductive Pharmacology","questions":[{"id":"sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug directly inhibits renin?","choices":{"A":"Lisinopril","B":"Losartan","C":"Candesartan","D":"Benazepril","E":"Aliskiren"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aliskiren binds directly to renin and inhibits its binding to angiotensinogen, which reduces cleavage of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I and lowers all downstream RAAS effectors. It is the only renin inhibitor currently available in the United States. Lisinopril and benazepril are ACE inhibitors, and losartan and candesartan are angiotensin receptor blockers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren acts at the earliest RAAS step, blocking conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. COMLEX: it is indicated for hypertension when other agents have failed, and it should not be combined with an ACE inhibitor or an ARB.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Dilation of which vessel by ACE inhibitors and ARBs lowers glomerular hydrostatic pressure?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriole","B":"Interlobular artery","C":"Efferent arteriole","D":"Vasa recta","E":"Peritubular capillary"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Under normal circumstances angiotensin II raises GFR by constricting the efferent arteriole. Drugs that lower angiotensin II levels or block its effect dilate the efferent arteriole, which lowers glomerular hydrostatic pressure. This underlies both the early benefit in diabetic kidney disease and the risk of prerenal acute kidney injury in low-flow states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce intraglomerular pressure by dilating the efferent arteriole. COMLEX: expect an early rise in creatinine after starting these drugs, which is often acceptable if the GFR stabilizes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"A volume-depleted patient starts an ACE inhibitor, and the GFR falls within the first few days. What type of kidney injury is this?","choices":{"A":"Prerenal acute kidney injury","B":"Postrenal acute kidney injury","C":"Toxic acute tubular necrosis","D":"Acute interstitial nephritis","E":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Efferent arteriolar dilation acutely decreases GFR, especially in low-flow states such as dehydration or heart failure. Because the fall in GFR reflects reduced glomerular hydrostatic pressure rather than obstruction or tubular, interstitial, or glomerular injury, it is a form of prerenal acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAAS inhibitors plus low renal perfusion cause prerenal azotemia through loss of efferent tone. COMLEX: if the GFR keeps falling, monitor closely and reduce the dose; a small early decline may be acceptable given the benefit in CKD and heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Accumulation of which mediator is thought to cause the dry cough seen with ACE inhibitors?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II","B":"Aldosterone","C":"Bradykinin","D":"Vasopressin","E":"Angiotensinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, an inflammatory mediator that causes vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and bronchial smooth muscle contraction. ACE inhibition leads to bradykinin accumulation, which is thought to cause the dry, hacking cough. Angiotensin II and aldosterone fall with these drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cough and angioedema are both bradykinin-mediated ACE inhibitor effects. COMLEX: ARBs have a reduced incidence of cough, so they are the usual substitute when cough limits therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Which route mainly clears ACE inhibitors from the body?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic metabolism","B":"Biliary secretion","C":"Pulmonary exhalation","D":"Renal excretion","E":"Fecal excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ACE inhibitors are cleared by the kidney, so the dosage should be lowered in patients with reduced GFR. ARBs, in contrast, are cleared mostly by the liver, and renin inhibitors are also cleared by the kidney.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renally cleared drugs accumulate as GFR falls. COMLEX: dose adjustment of ACE inhibitors in reduced GFR is a standard prescribing safeguard.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Which rare complication of ACE inhibitors can swell the lips and tongue and obstruct the upper airway?","choices":{"A":"Angioedema","B":"Dry cough","C":"Hyperkalemia","D":"Prerenal AKI","E":"Fetal renal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE inhibitor-associated angioedema results from bradykinin accumulation and commonly affects the upper airways, face, lips, and tongue. Airway obstruction is the most feared complication and can be fatal. Cough does not obstruct the airway, and hyperkalemia, prerenal AKI, and fetal renal injury are other adverse effects without airway swelling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin-mediated and contraindicated in C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency. COMLEX: ARBs have a much lower risk of angioedema and cough than ACE inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug for male reproductive disorders inhibits both the type 1 and type 2 isoforms of 5-alpha-reductase?","choices":{"A":"Finasteride","B":"Dutasteride","C":"Tamsulosin","D":"Tadalafil","E":"Vardenafil"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dutasteride inhibits both isoforms and therefore suppresses DHT synthesis more completely than finasteride, which selectively inhibits the type 2 isoform. Serum DHT falls by roughly 70 percent with finasteride and by more than 90 percent with dutasteride. Tamsulosin is an alpha-1 blocker, and tadalafil and vardenafil are PDE5 inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT, and finasteride and dutasteride block that step upstream of the androgen receptor. COMLEX: both agents are approved for BPH, and finasteride at 1 mg is also used for androgenic alopecia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"By roughly what percentage do 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower serum PSA?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 35 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"These drugs lower PSA by approximately 50 percent, which must be accounted for when screening for prostate cancer. The value of about 70 percent describes the fall in serum DHT with finasteride, not the fall in PSA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors halve PSA, so a measured value is interpreted at roughly double. COMLEX: PSA screening for prostate cancer still matters while a patient takes these agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"Which adverse effect of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors can persist after the drug is stopped, a phenomenon termed post-finasteride syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Orthostatic hypotension","B":"Visual disturbances","C":"Nasal congestion","D":"Sexual dysfunction","E":"Prolonged erection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sexual dysfunction, including decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders, occurs in a minority of patients and may persist after discontinuation. Visual disturbances and nasal congestion are PDE5 inhibitor effects, and priapism is linked to alpha-1 blockade and pro-erectile agents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors cause decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders. COMLEX: both agents are teratogenic to male fetuses and contraindicated in pregnancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"PDE5 inhibitors prolong the action of which second messenger in penile smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Calcium ions","E":"Inositol trisphosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PDE5 is the enzyme that degrades cGMP in penile tissue. Sildenafil, tadalafil, and vardenafil competitively block it, prolonging cGMP activity and sustaining smooth muscle relaxation, which enhances and prolongs the physiologic response to sexual stimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP breakdown. COMLEX: they do not generate erections independently and depend on intact nitric oxide signaling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"Which mediator released by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings and endothelium in the corpus cavernosum activates guanylyl cyclase to start erection?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Endothelin","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Angiotensin II","E":"Vasopressin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sexual stimulation triggers release of nitric oxide from nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings and vascular endothelium. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, which converts GTP to cGMP, and elevated cGMP relaxes smooth muscle in penile arterioles, increasing blood inflow. Norepinephrine promotes detumescence rather than erection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the erection pathway runs NO, guanylyl cyclase, cGMP, then smooth muscle relaxation. COMLEX: parasympathetic input drives erection, while sympathetic norepinephrine drives detumescence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class is contraindicated with PDE5 inhibitors because of the risk of profound hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Statins","B":"Biguanides","C":"Anticoagulants","D":"Thyroid hormones","E":"Nitrates"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nitrates also elevate cGMP through nitric oxide-dependent pathways, so co-administration with a PDE5 inhibitor produces additive vasodilation and can cause life-threatening drops in blood pressure. The other classes do not act through the cGMP vasodilator pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a PDE5 inhibitor plus a nitrate is the classic contraindicated combination. COMLEX: ask every man with erectile dysfunction about nitrate use, including for angina, before prescribing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"A painful erection unrelated to sexual stimulation is termed priapism once it persists for how long?","choices":{"A":"Beyond 30 minutes","B":"Beyond 1 hour","C":"Beyond 2 hours","D":"Beyond 4 hours","E":"Beyond 6 hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Priapism is a prolonged, often painful erection persisting beyond four hours without sexual stimulation. It is a urologic emergency because sustained ischemia in the corpus cavernosum can cause irreversible fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism is a painful sustained erection lasting more than 4 hours. COMLEX: treatment is typically needed within 4 to 6 hours to prevent irreversible smooth muscle damage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"Which antidepressant with potent alpha-1 blocking activity is frequently cited as a cause of drug-induced priapism?","choices":{"A":"Trazodone","B":"Bupropion","C":"Fluoxetine","D":"Escitalopram","E":"Duloxetine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trazodone has potent alpha-1 adrenergic blocking activity and is one of the most frequently cited causes of drug-induced priapism. Blocking alpha-1 receptors removes the sympathetic norepinephrine signal that constricts cavernous smooth muscle and ends an erection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: trazodone is an antidepressant associated with priapism through alpha-1 blockade. COMLEX: review the medication list of any patient presenting with a prolonged erection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality underlies ischemic, or low-flow, priapism?","choices":{"A":"Excessive arterial inflow into the corpus cavernosum","B":"Impaired venous outflow from the corpus cavernosum","C":"Impaired lymphatic drainage of the corpus cavernosum","D":"Reduced nitric oxide release from cavernous nerves","E":"Increased sympathetic tone on cavernous smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most common form is ischemic, or low-flow, priapism, caused by impaired venous outflow from the corpus cavernosum. Blood becomes trapped, oxygen tension falls, and metabolic acidosis develops within the cavernous tissue. Reduced nitric oxide release and increased sympathetic tone would oppose an erection rather than sustain it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism is low-flow with trapped, deoxygenated blood, and sickled red blood cells can block venous drainage of the corpus cavernosum. COMLEX: it is a urologic emergency treated immediately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"THC suppresses GnRH pulsatility by activating which hypothalamic receptor?","choices":{"A":"Mu opioid receptor","B":"D2 dopamine receptor","C":"CB1 receptor","D":"GABA-B receptor","E":"5-HT2A receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"THC binds cannabinoid receptors expressed throughout the male reproductive axis. Activation of CB1 receptors in the hypothalamus suppresses GnRH pulsatility, lowering LH and FSH secretion and thereby decreasing testosterone production and impairing spermatogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: THC suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis through CB1 receptors. COMLEX: screen for recreational drug use in men with low libido, erectile dysfunction, or infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"According to the male reproductive pharmacology brick, which hormone pattern follows suppression of GnRH pulsatility by chronic marijuana use?","choices":{"A":"Low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone","B":"High LH, high FSH, and low testosterone","C":"Low LH, low FSH, and normal testosterone","D":"High LH, high FSH, and high testosterone","E":"Normal LH, normal FSH, and low testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Suppressed GnRH pulsatility reduces LH and FSH secretion, and the downstream consequence is decreased testosterone production and impaired spermatogenesis. A central mechanism therefore lowers all three hormones, whereas raised gonadotropins would indicate a testicular defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a central (hypogonadotropic) pattern has low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone. COMLEX: chronic users also show reduced sperm count, decreased motility, and abnormal morphology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which cytochrome P450 enzyme metabolizes sildenafil and also handles THC?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP3A4","C":"CYP1A2","D":"CYP2E1","E":"CYP2B6"},"correct":"B","explanation":"THC and cannabidiol are metabolized by hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, and PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil are also CYP3A4 substrates. Concurrent marijuana use may therefore alter sildenafil plasma concentrations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CYP3A4 substrates such as sildenafil are sensitive to other agents that share this pathway. COMLEX: marijuana can potentiate hypotension with PDE5 inhibitors, particularly in patients with cardiovascular disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has persistent microalbuminuria and a preserved GFR. His physician starts lisinopril to slow progression of his diabetic kidney disease. Which mechanism best explains the renal benefit of this drug?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriolar dilation that raises glomerular capillary pressure","B":"Reduced renal plasma flow that lowers the filtration fraction","C":"Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Direct restoration of negative charge on the basement membrane","E":"Blockade of tubular glucose reabsorption that lowers filtered glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In diabetic kidney disease, early use of ACE inhibitors reduces hydrostatic pressure in the glomerulus and decreases microalbuminuria. Angiotensin II normally constricts the efferent arteriole, so blocking its formation dilates the efferent arteriole and lowers glomerular capillary pressure, which is why ACE inhibitors and ARBs are used for chronic kidney disease with proteinuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs lower intraglomerular pressure and slow diabetic nephropathy. COMLEX: CKD with proteinuria and comorbid hypertension or heart failure favors an ACE inhibitor or ARB, whereas CKD without proteinuria does not specifically benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — how afferent versus efferent arteriolar resistance changes glomerular capillary pressure, plasma flow, GFR, and filtration fraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension and osteoarthritis has taken lisinopril for two years and started daily ibuprofen last week. She is mildly dehydrated from a viral illness, and her creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 1.9 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains her fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Unopposed afferent dilation from ibuprofen plus efferent constriction from lisinopril","B":"Obstruction of urine outflow that raises Bowman space hydrostatic pressure","C":"Immune complex deposition in the glomerulus with mesangial proliferation","D":"Loss of prostaglandin afferent dilation plus ACE inhibitor efferent dilation","E":"Direct tubular toxicity from ibuprofen that halts filtrate flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, and prostaglandins normally dilate the afferent arteriole. Blocking that afferent dilation while the ACE inhibitor dilates the efferent arteriole reduces glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR, especially in a patient already at risk such as one who is dehydrated. This is a form of prerenal acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID plus ACE inhibitor plus volume depletion is a classic hemodynamic cause of prerenal AKI. COMLEX: RAAS inhibitors should not be used with chronic NSAIDs because of the risk of prerenal AKI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — local prostaglandins protect the afferent arteriole, and NSAIDs remove that buffer in hypoperfused states","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with hypertension has not reached her blood pressure goal on several other antihypertensive agents. Her physician adds a drug that lowers plasma angiotensin I along with all downstream RAAS effectors. At which step does this drug act?","choices":{"A":"Blocking ACE from converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II","B":"Blocking AT1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","C":"Blocking mineralocorticoid receptors in principal cells","D":"Blocking aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal cortex","E":"Blocking renin from cleaving angiotensinogen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Direct renin inhibitors act at the earliest point in the RAAS: they bind renin and inhibit its binding to angiotensinogen, which reduces the formation of angiotensin I and every downstream effector. Aliskiren is indicated only for hypertension and only when other agents have failed. ACE inhibitors and ARBs act one and two steps further downstream.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren blocks conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. COMLEX: it should not be combined with ACE inhibitors or ARBs because of additive hyperkalemia and AKI without added benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — renin-angiotensin-aldosterone effectors in renal sodium balance and blood pressure control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who started ramipril three weeks ago presents with rapid swelling of the lips and tongue and difficulty swallowing. He has no urticaria or itching and no known allergies. Which mechanism best explains his condition?","choices":{"A":"Bradykinin buildup that increases vascular permeability","B":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation with histamine release","C":"Angiotensin II accumulation that constricts dermal venules","D":"Aldosterone excess that expands the interstitial fluid volume","E":"Complement activation by circulating immune complexes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, which causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. Inhibiting ACE lets bradykinin accumulate, producing angioedema of the face, lips, tongue, and upper airway. Airway obstruction is the most feared complication and can be fatal. Angiotensin II and aldosterone levels fall, rather than rise, with ACE inhibition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin-mediated, not allergic, and it is contraindicated in C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency. COMLEX: airway obstruction is the most feared complication, and ARBs carry a much lower risk of angioedema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators that adjust arteriolar tone in the kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with chronic kidney disease and an eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m2 is prescribed enalapril for heart failure. The pharmacist recommends a lower dose than usual. Which property of the drug best justifies this recommendation?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic clearance that falls when renal perfusion declines","B":"Renal elimination that causes accumulation at a low GFR","C":"Pulmonary metabolism that saturates when GFR is reduced","D":"Active biliary secretion that stops when GFR is reduced","E":"Renal tubular toxicity that becomes dose-dependent at low GFR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACE inhibitors are cleared by the kidney, so the dosage should be lowered in patients with reduced GFR to avoid accumulation. ARBs, by contrast, are cleared mostly by the liver. Reduced clearance of a renally eliminated drug raises its systemic exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renally eliminated drugs accumulate as GFR falls. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors and renin inhibitors are cleared by the kidney, whereas ARBs are largely hepatically cleared.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 1 — how falling GFR alters drug clearance and predicts systemic accumulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with heart failure takes enalapril and develops a persistent dry, hacking cough with a normal chest radiograph. His physician switches him to losartan, and the cough resolves within weeks. Which best explains the improvement?","choices":{"A":"Losartan inhibits renin so that less angiotensin I forms","B":"Losartan blocks bradykinin receptors on bronchial smooth muscle","C":"Receptor blockade leaves ACE-mediated bradykinin breakdown intact","D":"Losartan raises aldosterone, which relaxes bronchial smooth muscle","E":"Losartan is cleared by the kidney and accumulates less"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ARBs act one step downstream of ACE inhibitors by blocking angiotensin II receptors instead of inhibiting ACE, so bradykinin degradation by ACE is unaffected. Bradykinin therefore does not accumulate, and the dry cough is much less common with ARBs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARBs do not increase bradykinin, so they are used for patients who cannot tolerate an ACE inhibitor because of cough or angioedema. COMLEX: the effect on angiotensin II signaling and the benefit in heart failure are similar to an ACE inhibitor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — renin-angiotensin-aldosterone effectors in renal sodium balance and blood pressure control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia has taken finasteride for two years. His serum PSA is 2.2 ng/mL. Given the effect of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors on PSA, approximately what value would be expected without the drug?","choices":{"A":"1.1 ng/mL","B":"2.2 ng/mL","C":"3.3 ng/mL","D":"8.8 ng/mL","E":"4.4 ng/mL"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Five-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower PSA by approximately 50 percent, so the measured value of 2.2 ng/mL corresponds to about 4.4 ng/mL (2.2 divided by 0.5) without the drug. This adjustment must be made when screening for prostate cancer. A value of 1.1 would apply the reduction in the wrong direction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors halve PSA, so multiply the measured value by about 2. COMLEX: interpreting an apparently normal PSA in a treated man requires this correction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — how prostatic adenocarcinoma is distinguished from benign hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia has weak urinary stream. After six months of finasteride, imaging shows a smaller gland and his obstructive symptoms improve. Which effect best explains the response?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of androgen receptors in prostatic epithelium","B":"Lower intraprostatic DHT that shrinks the hyperplastic tissue","C":"Relaxation of prostatic smooth muscle through alpha-1 blockade","D":"Suppression of pituitary LH that removes testosterone drive","E":"Increased cGMP that relaxes the bladder neck musculature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, lowering intraprostatic DHT and shrinking prostate volume, which relieves urinary obstruction in BPH. Its action is upstream in the androgen signaling cascade: it does not block the androgen receptor but limits the availability of the most potent androgen ligand.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DHT drives prostate growth, and 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the gland over months. COMLEX: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower PSA by about 50 percent and are teratogenic to male fetuses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT-dependent transition zone hyperplasia and urethral compression in BPH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man with androgenic alopecia starts finasteride 1 mg daily. Which action underlies the expected benefit to his hair follicles?","choices":{"A":"Decreased conversion of testosterone to DHT in target tissue","B":"Increased aromatization of testosterone to estradiol in hair follicles","C":"Stimulation of testosterone secretion by testicular Leydig cells","D":"Direct vasodilation of scalp arterioles through cGMP accumulation","E":"Suppression of pituitary gonadotropin release by negative feedback"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated DHT is implicated in androgenic alopecia. DHT is formed when 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone within target tissues, and finasteride, approved at the lower dose of 1 mg for androgenic alopecia, selectively inhibits the type 2 isoform to limit DHT synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: male pattern hair loss is DHT-dependent, and finasteride blocks its formation. COMLEX: counsel about decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders, which can persist after discontinuation in a minority of patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — androgen actions that require 5-alpha-reductase conversion to DHT, including male pattern hair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes and erectile dysfunction is prescribed sildenafil. He asks why the drug will not produce an erection if he takes it without any sexual arousal. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It directly activates guanylyl cyclase in cavernous smooth muscle","B":"It stimulates testicular testosterone release that drives arousal","C":"It blocks alpha-1 receptors to relax cavernous smooth muscle","D":"It prolongs cGMP generated by arousal-driven nitric oxide release","E":"It releases nitric oxide from parasympathetic nerve endings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP degradation and so prolong cGMP-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, but they do not generate erections independently. Sexual stimulation must first release nitric oxide, which activates guanylyl cyclase to make cGMP, so these drugs enhance rather than replace the physiologic response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors depend on endogenous NO signaling and amplify the response to stimulation. COMLEX: endothelial dysfunction from diabetes lowers NO production, which is a common organic cause of erectile dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with stable angina takes isosorbide mononitrate daily and asks his physician for tadalafil for erectile dysfunction. What is the principal danger of taking both drugs?","choices":{"A":"Priapism from additive alpha-1 adrenergic blockade","B":"Hyperkalemia from reduced aldosterone secretion","C":"Severe hypotension from additive cGMP vasodilation","D":"Visual loss from PDE6 inhibition in retinal photoreceptors","E":"Myalgia from PDE11 inhibition in skeletal muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nitrates elevate cGMP through nitric oxide-dependent pathways, and PDE5 inhibitors block its breakdown, so co-administration causes additive vasodilation and can cause life-threatening drops in blood pressure. Visual disturbances relate to PDE6 inhibition by sildenafil, and myalgia relates to PDE11 inhibition by tadalafil; neither is the principal danger with a nitrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nitrates plus PDE5 inhibitors are absolutely contraindicated because both raise cGMP. COMLEX: screen every patient with erectile dysfunction for nitrate use before prescribing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"In a healthy 30-year-old man, sexual arousal activates nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings that release nitric oxide, which activates soluble guanylyl cyclase in cavernous smooth muscle and raises cGMP. Which kinase does cGMP activate to relax the smooth muscle and produce erection?","choices":{"A":"Protein kinase G","B":"Protein kinase A","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Janus kinase 2","E":"Src tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated cGMP activates protein kinase G, which opens potassium channels and acts on the myosin light chain kinase pathway, collectively producing smooth muscle relaxation, arterial dilation, and venous occlusion, the hemodynamic hallmarks of erection. PDE5 inhibitors act by prolonging this cGMP signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pathway is NO, soluble guanylyl cyclase, cGMP, PKG, then relaxation. COMLEX: this is the signaling axis that PDE5 inhibitors amplify and that NOS inhibition blunts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with schizophrenia started clozapine two weeks ago. He presents with a painful, rigid erection lasting six hours that is unrelated to sexual activity. He takes no erectile dysfunction medication. Which mechanism best explains this adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"PDE5 inhibition that prolongs cGMP-mediated relaxation","B":"Serotonin reuptake inhibition that raises spinal erectile drive","C":"Histamine H1 blockade that increases cavernous blood flow","D":"Elevated testosterone that sustains cavernous arterial inflow","E":"Alpha-1 blockade that impairs sympathetic detumescence"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Alpha-1 blockade is a shared property of many antipsychotics, including chlorpromazine and clozapine. Detumescence depends on sympathetic norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors of cavernous smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction and venous outflow, so blocking these receptors prevents the contraction needed to end an erection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 blockade, as with trazodone, antipsychotics, and alpha-1 blockers, is a classic mechanism of drug-induced priapism. COMLEX: this is a urologic emergency, and ischemic priapism needs immediate treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — sympathetic and parasympathetic control of erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who has trouble achieving erections self-injects alprostadil into the corpus cavernosum for the first time and develops a painful rigid erection lasting seven hours. Which outcome is most likely if the erection is not relieved?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction of the cavernous nerves with penile anesthesia","B":"Fibrous plaque formation within the tunica albuginea with penile curvature","C":"Fibrosis of cavernous smooth muscle with permanent erectile dysfunction","D":"Atherosclerotic occlusion of the internal pudendal arteries with impotence","E":"Testicular atrophy from hypothalamic suppression of gonadotropins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1 given by intracavernosal injection and carries a dose-dependent priapism risk. Prolonged ischemic priapism traps blood, lowers oxygen tension, and produces acidosis in the cavernous tissue; if not treated promptly, typically within 4 to 6 hours, irreversible smooth muscle damage and fibrosis occur, causing permanent erectile dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism lasting over 4 hours is a urologic emergency because sustained ischemia damages the corpus cavernosum. COMLEX: treatment is immediate, for example corporal aspiration, intracavernosal phenylephrine, or surgical decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — mechanisms of penile erection and detumescence in the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia takes lisinopril, metformin, atorvastatin, omeprazole, and prazosin. He presents with a painful erection lasting five hours that began without sexual stimulation. Which drug class most likely contributed?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","B":"Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist","C":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","D":"Biguanide antihyperglycemic","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alpha-1 blockers such as prazosin and tamsulosin can precipitate priapism by blocking the sympathetic detumescence pathway: norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors normally constricts cavernous smooth muscle and drives venous outflow. None of his other medications acts on that pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 blockers, trazodone, antipsychotics, PDE5 inhibitors, and intracavernosal alprostadil are the drug classes linked to priapism. COMLEX: review the full medication list of any patient with a prolonged erection, since polypharmacy raises the risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — sympathetic alpha-1 tone and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man who smokes marijuana daily presents with decreased libido and difficulty conceiving. His testosterone, LH, and FSH are all low, and semen analysis shows reduced sperm count and motility. He denies anabolic steroid use, and his testicular examination is normal. Which mechanism best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic CB1 activation that suppresses GnRH pulses","B":"Primary testicular failure with loss of negative feedback","C":"Androgen receptor blockade in peripheral target tissues","D":"Inhibition of 5-alpha-reductase in the prostate and skin","E":"Congenital Leydig cell steroidogenic enzyme deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"THC activates CB1 receptors in the hypothalamus and suppresses GnRH pulsatility, reducing LH and FSH secretion. The downstream consequence is decreased testosterone production and impaired spermatogenesis, with reduced sperm count, decreased motility, and morphologic abnormalities. Primary testicular or Leydig cell defects would raise LH and FSH rather than lower them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypogonadotropic hypogonadism has low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone. COMLEX: THC also impairs sperm capacitation and the acrosome reaction, so ask about recreational drug use in men with infertility or low libido.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — hormonal panels that separate primary from secondary hypogonadism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man takes sildenafil as needed for erectile dysfunction and smokes marijuana before using it. After taking both he reports lightheadedness, and his blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg. Which mechanisms are proposed to explain his hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Sildenafil-induced alpha-1 blockade that removes sympathetic vasoconstriction","B":"Additive vasodilation with altered sildenafil exposure through CYP3A4","C":"Cannabinoid-induced hyperkalemia that dilates systemic arterioles","D":"Increased eNOS activity that overwhelms sildenafil inhibition of PDE5","E":"Cannabinoid inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme in the lungs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Marijuana causes vasodilation and can lower blood pressure, which may potentiate the hypotensive effect of PDE5 inhibitors. In addition, THC is metabolized by CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 and sildenafil is a CYP3A4 substrate, so concurrent use may alter sildenafil plasma concentrations and increase adverse effects such as hypotension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: additive vasodilation and shared CYP3A4 metabolism make PDE5 inhibitor hypotension more likely. COMLEX: the risk is greatest in patients with underlying cardiovascular disease, so screen for recreational drug use.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man who smokes marijuana daily has erectile dysfunction that responds poorly to sildenafil. He has no diabetes or hypertension. Which proposed mechanism best explains the weak response?","choices":{"A":"Excess nitric oxide that exhausts cavernous cGMP stores","B":"Competitive antagonism of sildenafil by THC at the PDE5 active site","C":"Testosterone excess that desensitizes cavernous receptors","D":"Blunted eNOS activity that reduces nitric oxide bioavailability","E":"Enhanced sympathetic drive that shortens the erectile response"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The endocannabinoid system modulates nitric oxide signaling in vascular endothelium, and chronic THC exposure may blunt eNOS activity, reducing nitric oxide bioavailability. PDE5 inhibitors depend on intact NO-cGMP signaling and do not generate erections independently, so reduced nitric oxide can diminish their efficacy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors require endogenous nitric oxide, so conditions that lower NO bioavailability weaken the response. COMLEX: THC also lowers testosterone through hypothalamic suppression, so evaluate libido and screen for recreational drug use.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":47,"title":"SDL 47 — Pre-Clinical Comprehensive Revision & Board Review (Part 1: Basic Science, Physiology & High-Yield Foundations)","questions":[{"id":"sdl47_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"In which week of development does the metanephros, the permanent kidney, first appear?","choices":{"A":"Week 2","B":"Week 4","C":"Week 8","D":"Week 10","E":"Week 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The metanephros, or permanent kidney, appears in week 5 of development, after the nonfunctional pronephros regresses (its indications disappear by the end of week 4) and the mesonephros serves as an interim kidney.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the three kidney systems form in a cranial-to-caudal sequence, pronephros then mesonephros then metanephros, and only the metanephros is permanent. COMLEX: the mesonephric duct persists in males to form the epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the stepwise sequence of kidney development and the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling that builds the permanent kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic structure gives rise to the collecting ducts, calyces, renal pelvis, and ureter?","choices":{"A":"Metanephric blastema","B":"Ureteric bud","C":"Pronephric duct","D":"Urogenital sinus","E":"Allantois"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ureteric bud arises from the mesonephric duct and forms the collecting ducts, major and minor calyces, renal pelvis, and ureter. The metanephric blastema, or mesenchyme, instead forms the nephron from the glomerulus through the distal convoluted tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ureteric bud makes the collecting system, and the metanephric mesenchyme makes the nephron. COMLEX: failure of bud branching or of bud-mesenchyme interaction produces renal agenesis or multicystic dysplastic kidney.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the stepwise sequence of kidney development and the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling that builds the permanent kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"Which growth factor made by the metanephric mesenchyme binds the RET receptor on the ureteric bud?","choices":{"A":"FGF2","B":"BMP7","C":"GDNF","D":"WNT9B","E":"HGF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The mesenchyme, made competent by the transcription factor WT1, produces GDNF, which binds RET on the ureteric bud epithelium and stimulates bud branching and growth. HGF from the mesenchyme acts through a different receptor, MET. FGF2 and BMP7 are signals from the bud back to the mesenchyme, and WNT9B is produced by the bud to trigger nephron formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of GDNF or RET signaling stops bud branching and can produce renal agenesis. COMLEX: WT1 is a transcription factor in the mesenchyme, and WT1 deletion at 11p13 is linked to Wilms tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the stepwise sequence of kidney development and the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling that builds the permanent kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the renal filtration barrier contains the heparan sulfate that repels anionic albumin?","choices":{"A":"Fenestrated endothelium","B":"Glomerular basement membrane","C":"Podocyte slit diaphragm","D":"Mesangial cell layer","E":"Parietal epithelium of Bowman capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The glomerular basement membrane carries a negative charge from heparan sulfate proteoglycans, which repels negatively charged proteins such as albumin. The fenestrated endothelium lacks diaphragms and blocks blood cells by size, and the slit diaphragm adds size selectivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the three layers are fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes with slit diaphragms. COMLEX: loss of the charge barrier allows albumin into the urine in nephrotic states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the layers of the glomerular filtration barrier and segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Which microscopic feature is seen in the proximal convoluted tubule but absent in the distal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelial lining","B":"Transitional epithelial lining","C":"Macula densa chemoreceptor cells","D":"Brush border of microvilli","E":"Renin-containing granular cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Proximal tubule cells have numerous apical microvilli forming a brush border, whereas distal convoluted tubule cells lack it and show a clear, open lumen. Both segments are lined by simple cuboidal epithelium, the macula densa lies in the distal tubule, renin-containing granular cells sit in the afferent arteriole, and transitional epithelium lines the lower urinary tract rather than the nephron.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a prominent brush border and dense eosinophilic cytoplasm identify the proximal tubule. COMLEX: the proximal tubule reabsorbs about 67 percent of filtered water and sodium and all of the filtered glucose and amino acids under normal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the layers of the glomerular filtration barrier and segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Ischemic acute tubular necrosis after prolonged hypotension preferentially injures which nephron segment?","choices":{"A":"Cortical collecting duct","B":"Thin descending limb","C":"Proximal tubule","D":"Glomerulus","E":"Bowman capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs 67 percent of filtered water, sodium, and chloride and essentially all glucose and amino acids, so its high metabolic load makes it the primary site of ischemic tubular necrosis after hypotension or shock. Robbins adds that in the ischemic pattern the proximal straight tubule and the ascending limbs of the loop of Henle are the most vulnerable segments, whereas the lumens of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct contain casts rather than the main injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ischemic acute tubular injury the proximal straight tubule and the thick ascending limb are the most vulnerable segments, and toxic injury is most obvious in the proximal convoluted tubule. COMLEX: contrast dye and aminoglycosides are typical toxic triggers, and hypotension or shock is the typical ischemic trigger.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the layers of the glomerular filtration barrier and segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"Which substance is the gold standard glomerular marker for measuring GFR?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine","B":"Glucose","C":"Sodium","D":"Inulin","E":"Albumin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR. Creatinine is freely filtered but is also secreted to a small extent, so its clearance slightly overestimates GFR. Glucose and sodium are largely reabsorbed, and albumin is normally excluded by the filtration barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an ideal glomerular marker is freely filtered, is not reabsorbed or secreted, and does not alter GFR. COMLEX: creatinine is used clinically because it is endogenous, despite the small overestimate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3, SDL 8 -> Obj 4, and SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — clearance-based measures of renal function and how arteriolar resistance changes reshape glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"Clearance of para-aminohippuric acid is used to estimate which quantity?","choices":{"A":"Effective renal plasma flow","B":"Glomerular filtration rate","C":"Total renal blood flow","D":"Fractional sodium excretion","E":"Tubular transport maximum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PAH is filtered and secreted, and the kidneys extract most of it from renal arterial blood, so its clearance approximates effective renal plasma flow. Renal blood flow can then be calculated from that plasma flow and the hematocrit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAH clearance gives effective RPF, and RBF equals RPF divided by 1 minus the hematocrit. COMLEX: effective RPF underestimates true RPF by about 10 percent because some renal blood flow serves tissue that does not extract PAH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3, SDL 8 -> Obj 4, and SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — clearance-based measures of renal function and how arteriolar resistance changes reshape glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"What is the normal filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow, and it is normally about 0.20, or 20 percent. The other 80 percent of plasma flow is not filtered and leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arterioles to become peritubular capillary blood flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FF equals GFR over RPF and is normally about 20 percent. COMLEX: efferent constriction raises the filtration fraction, and afferent constriction leaves it about unchanged.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3, SDL 8 -> Obj 4, and SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — clearance-based measures of renal function and how arteriolar resistance changes reshape glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"Which fluid compartment keeps a normal volume in isosmotic volume contraction from diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Extracellular fluid","B":"Interstitial fluid","C":"Intracellular fluid","D":"Plasma volume","E":"Circulating blood volume"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isosmotic fluid lost from the ECF leaves ECF osmolarity unchanged, so there is no osmotic gradient and no water shift across cell membranes. The ECF volume falls, and with it the interstitial fluid, plasma volume, and blood volume, while the ICF volume is unchanged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea gives a lower ECF volume with unchanged ICF volume and unchanged osmolarity, plus a higher hematocrit and plasma protein concentration. COMLEX: this is the standard model of isosmotic volume contraction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 2 and 3 — isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction and expansion and the resulting water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"How does ICF volume change when sweat loss causes hyperosmotic volume contraction?","choices":{"A":"It falls as water shifts out to the more concentrated ECF","B":"It is unchanged because sweat is isosmotic to plasma","C":"It rises because hyposmotic sweat shifts water into cells","D":"It is unchanged because cells shrink to offset ECF loss","E":"It falls because cells lose solute to the ECF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sweat is hyposmotic relative to the ECF, so its loss lowers ECF volume and raises ECF osmolarity. The higher ECF osmolarity draws water out of cells, so ICF volume falls as well, and in the new steady state ECF and ICF osmolarities are equal and higher than normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sweating, fever, and diabetes insipidus produce hyperosmotic volume contraction with a lower ECF volume, a lower ICF volume, and a higher osmolarity. COMLEX: the hematocrit is unchanged because red cells shrink as fluid loss concentrates them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 2 and 3 — isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction and expansion and the resulting water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"Which volume disturbance follows an infusion of normal saline?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion","B":"Hyposmotic volume expansion","C":"Isosmotic volume contraction","D":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","E":"Isosmotic volume expansion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isotonic saline is added entirely to the ECF and does not change ECF osmolarity, so no water shifts between compartments. ECF volume increases while ICF volume and osmolarity stay unchanged, and hematocrit and plasma protein concentration fall by dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isotonic fluid stays in the ECF and expands it without changing osmolarity. COMLEX: hyposmotic expansion occurs with SIADH and hyperosmotic expansion with high salt intake.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 2 and 3 — isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction and expansion and the resulting water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"Winter's formula predicts PCO2 as 1.5 times serum bicarbonate plus which constant?","choices":{"A":"4 mm Hg","B":"8 mm Hg","C":"12 mm Hg","D":"24 mm Hg","E":"40 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Winter's formula gives expected PCO2 as 1.5 times the serum bicarbonate plus 8, with a range of plus or minus 2 mm Hg. It is used to judge whether respiratory compensation for a metabolic acidosis is appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a measured PCO2 above the predicted value means a concomitant respiratory acidosis, and a value below it means a concomitant respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: the formula applies to primary metabolic acidosis, not to metabolic alkalosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 and 3 — high versus normal anion gap acidoses and the formulas for expected compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"Which expression calculates the serum anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate","B":"Sodium plus chloride minus bicarbonate","C":"Chloride minus the sum of sodium and bicarbonate","D":"Bicarbonate minus the sum of sodium and chloride","E":"Sodium plus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anion gap equals the sodium concentration minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate concentrations. It represents unmeasured anions, so it rises when an organic anion such as a ketoacid or lactate replaces bicarbonate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an elevated gap points to ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, salicylate, methanol, ethylene glycol, or renal failure, while a normal gap points to bicarbonate loss. COMLEX: calculate the gap first in any metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 and 3 — high versus normal anion gap acidoses and the formulas for expected compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"Which condition produces a metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","B":"Methanol poisoning","C":"Profuse diarrhea","D":"Salicylate poisoning","E":"Lactic acidosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diarrhea causes gastrointestinal loss of bicarbonate, and chloride replaces it, giving a hyperchloremic acidosis with a normal anion gap. Diabetic ketoacidosis, methanol poisoning, salicylate poisoning, and lactic acidosis each add an unmeasured anion and raise the gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and renal tubular acidoses cause normal anion gap acidosis with hyperchloremia. COMLEX: use the anion gap to separate these from the causes of high gap acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 and 3 — high versus normal anion gap acidoses and the formulas for expected compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic fibers from spinal levels T10 to T11 supply which organs?","choices":{"A":"Heart and great vessels","B":"Stomach and gallbladder","C":"Prostate and lower gut","D":"Lungs and bronchi","E":"Kidney and upper ureter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The kidney and upper ureter receive sympathetic input from T10 to T11, which causes vasoconstriction and altered GFR. T1 to T4 corresponds to cardiac, T5 to T9 to the upper gastrointestinal organs (stomach, liver, gallbladder), and T12 to L2 to the prostate and lower gut.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic tone alters vascular resistance and GFR. COMLEX: somatic dysfunction at T10 to T11 supports a renal origin, so the level helps separate renal disease from other visceral sources.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — mapping autonomic innervation levels to the kidneys, ureters, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve provides the parasympathetic supply to the kidney and upper ureter?","choices":{"A":"Pudendal nerve","B":"Hypogastric nerve","C":"Phrenic nerve","D":"Vagus nerve","E":"Genitofemoral nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The parasympathetic supply of the kidney and upper ureter comes from the vagus nerve, cranial nerve X. The bladder, lower ureter, and prostate instead receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus supplies parasympathetic fibers to the kidney, whereas pelvic organs use S2 to S4. COMLEX: in the OPP convention of the review guide, the testes and ovaries also receive vagal parasympathetic input with T10 to T11 sympathetic input.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — mapping autonomic innervation levels to the kidneys, ureters, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerves provide the parasympathetic supply to the bladder?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Lumbar splanchnic nerves","C":"Greater splanchnic nerves","D":"Hypogastric nerves","E":"Sacral splanchnic nerves"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bladder and lower ureter receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4. The lumbar, greater, and sacral splanchnic nerves and the hypogastric nerves are sympathetic pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sacral parasympathetic outflow at S2 to S4 drives bladder contraction. COMLEX: the prostate also receives its parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — mapping autonomic innervation levels to the kidneys, ureters, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A term newborn has a flattened nose, low-set ears, a receding chin, and bilateral clubbed feet. He remains cyanotic and cannot be adequately ventilated despite intubation. Prenatal ultrasound showed severely reduced amniotic fluid and no visible bladder. Which mechanism best explains his respiratory failure?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant deficiency from delivery well before the due date","B":"Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid during a difficult delivery","C":"Tracheoesophageal fistula with aspiration of gastric contents","D":"Absent fetal urine that left no fluid to stent developing airways","E":"Ascending intrauterine infection with congenital pneumonia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral renal agenesis, ARPKD, or posterior urethral valves prevent fetal urine excretion, causing severe oligohydramnios. Fetal compression produces the characteristic facies and limb deformities, and without inhaled amniotic fluid to stent the airways, alveolar expansion halts, so pulmonary hypoplasia is the main cause of neonatal death.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Potter sequence is caused by oligohydramnios from absent fetal urine and is fatal because of pulmonary hypoplasia. COMLEX: fetal renal failure or obstruction, including maternal ACE inhibitor use, can trigger it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Potter sequence in bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections and a calcium oxalate stone has CT imaging that shows the lower poles of both kidneys joined across the midline, with both kidneys lying low in the abdomen. Which embryologic event best explains the position of her kidneys?","choices":{"A":"Fused kidney trapped beneath the inferior mesenteric artery on ascent","B":"Failure of the ureteric bud to induce the metanephric blastema","C":"Duplicated ureteric bud that inserted caudally on the urogenital sinus","D":"Loss of GDNF signaling that prevented branching of the ureteric bud","E":"Kidneys stalled in the pelvis beside the common iliac artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the kidneys are pushed close together as they pass through the arterial fork of the umbilical arteries, their lower poles fuse to form a horseshoe kidney. During ascent from the pelvis the isthmus is caught by the root of the inferior mesenteric artery, which prevents further ascent. The altered ureteral drainage predisposes to stasis, infection, and stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: horseshoe kidney is fused at the inferior poles and trapped under the inferior mesenteric artery, and it is more common in Turner syndrome and trisomies 13, 18, and 21. COMLEX: it is usually functional but predisposes to hydronephrosis, stones, and infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — how kidney ascent, rotation, and vascular relationships shape ectopic and fused kidneys","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has leaked urine continuously since toilet training, soaking her underwear all day, yet she also voids normally at regular intervals. Ultrasound shows a duplicated collecting system on one side. Which embryologic mechanism best explains her incontinence?","choices":{"A":"Failure of the allantoic lumen to obliterate into the median umbilical ligament","B":"Accessory ureteric bud carried caudally to open below the sphincter","C":"Incomplete urorectal septum partitioning of the cloaca","D":"Failure of ventral body wall closure exposing the bladder","E":"Loss of coordinated bladder emptying from a spinal cord lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A second ureteric bud can move caudally with the mesonephric duct and open at a low, abnormal site. In girls the ectopic ureter can drain into the urethra, vestibule, or vagina below the external urethral sphincter, so urine drips continuously while normal periodic voiding from the bladder persists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an ectopic ureter arises from a duplicated ureteric bud that inserts abnormally low, and the upper pole ureter is the one that is ectopic. COMLEX: continuous dribbling with normal voiding in a girl is the classic clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — congenital lower urinary tract anomalies and the anatomic basis of their presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy develops periorbital swelling two weeks after an upper respiratory infection. Urinalysis shows heavy proteinuria that is mostly albumin, his creatinine is normal, and a renal biopsy shows glomeruli that look normal by light microscopy. Which mechanism best explains the proteinuria?","choices":{"A":"Subepithelial immune complex deposition thickening the capillary wall","B":"Autoantibodies against type IV collagen in the basement membrane","C":"Podocyte injury with foot process effacement and slit diaphragm loss","D":"Mesangial deposition of IgA that expands the mesangial matrix","E":"Nodular accumulation of matrix that thickens the basement membrane"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Minimal change disease shows diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy with normal light microscopy. Podocyte injury, possibly through antibodies to nephrin, breaks down the slit diaphragm and alters the permeability and selectivity of the filter, and loss of glomerular polyanions may add a charge defect. It responds well to corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most frequent cause of nephrotic syndrome in children is minimal change disease, with normal light microscopy and foot process effacement. COMLEX: expect a highly selective, albumin-predominant proteinuria and a steroid response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — how loss of size and charge selectivity of the filtration barrier produces selective proteinuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A renal cortical biopsy after prolonged hypotension shows necrosis of tubule profiles with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm and sloughed cells, while adjacent profiles with lower cuboidal cells and open lumens are spared. Why are the injured cells especially vulnerable to ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Sparse mitochondria that limit oxidative capacity","B":"High ATP demand from bulk reabsorption of filtered solute","C":"Low reabsorptive workload that leaves reserves untrained","D":"Direct exposure to circulating antibodies in the lumen","E":"Absence of a brush border that exposes the apical membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The injured segment is the proximal tubule, packed with basal mitochondria that supply ATP for Na+/K+-ATPase as it reabsorbs 67 percent of filtered water, sodium, and chloride plus essentially all glucose and amino acids. Robbins notes that tubular epithelial cells are particularly sensitive to ischemia because of their high rate of metabolism and oxygen consumption, and the review guide names this high metabolic load as the reason the proximal tubule is the primary site of ischemic acute tubular necrosis. The spared distal convoluted tubule has lower cuboidal cells, lacks a brush border, and does less bulk reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proximal tubular epithelium is highly energy dependent and therefore highly sensitive to hypoxia. COMLEX: hypotension or shock is the typical ischemic trigger, and contrast dye or aminoglycosides are typical toxic triggers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — segment-specific epithelial features of the proximal versus distal convoluted tubule under light microscopy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with hemorrhage has reduced sodium and chloride delivery to the macula densa. Which cell type responds by releasing renin?","choices":{"A":"Macula densa cells","B":"Alpha-intercalated cells","C":"Principal cells","D":"Proximal tubule cells","E":"Juxtaglomerular cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The macula densa are chemoreceptor cells in the early distal tubule that sense luminal sodium and chloride. The juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that contain renin granules, so they are the source of renin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renin comes from juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole, and the macula densa is the sensor. COMLEX: the macula densa senses luminal sodium and chloride and signals the juxtaglomerular cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — the macula densa as a luminal solute sensor in tubuloglomerular feedback","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"After a large dose of an NSAID in a volume-depleted patient, selective constriction of the afferent arterioles occurs in both kidneys. Which set of changes is expected?","choices":{"A":"GFR increases, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction decreases","B":"GFR increases, RPF decreases, and filtration fraction increases","C":"GFR is unchanged, RPF increases, and filtration fraction decreases","D":"GFR decreases, RPF decreases, and filtration fraction is unchanged","E":"GFR decreases, RPF is unchanged, and filtration fraction is unchanged"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Afferent constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, so GFR falls, and it also reduces renal plasma flow. Because both fall proportionally, the filtration fraction is unchanged. Efferent constriction raises GFR and filtration fraction while lowering RPF, and a rise in plasma protein or ureteral obstruction lowers GFR with no change in RPF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: afferent constriction gives low GFR and low RPF with unchanged FF, efferent constriction gives high GFR, low RPF, and high FF. COMLEX: NSAIDs remove prostaglandin afferent dilation, which explains their AKI risk in hypoperfused states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction and its effects on GFR, RPF, and filtration fraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"A man has a urine flow rate of 1.5 mL/min, a plasma PAH concentration of 1.0 mg/dL, a urine PAH concentration of 400 mg/dL, and a hematocrit of 0.45. Assuming PAH clearance approximates renal plasma flow, what is his renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"270 mL/min","B":"330 mL/min","C":"1091 mL/min","D":"600 mL/min","E":"1333 mL/min"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Clearance of PAH equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration, or 400 times 1.5 divided by 1.0, which is 600 mL/min of effective renal plasma flow. Renal blood flow equals RPF divided by 1 minus the hematocrit, so 600 divided by 0.55 is about 1091 mL/min. Dividing by the hematocrit gives 1333, and multiplying gives 270 or 330.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBF equals RPF over 1 minus hematocrit, and RPF is estimated by PAH clearance. COMLEX: convert plasma flow to blood flow by dividing by the plasma fraction of blood, which is 1 minus the hematocrit; dividing by the hematocrit itself would give 1333 mL/min.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — PAH clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate renal plasma flow and renal blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"During an infusion, a man's plasma inulin concentration is 1 mg/mL. At a urine flow of 1 mL/min his urine inulin concentration is 120 mg/mL. After he drinks a large water load, urine flow rises to 2 mL/min and urine inulin concentration falls to 60 mg/mL. What is his inulin clearance during the water diuresis?","choices":{"A":"120 mL/min","B":"240 mL/min","C":"60 mL/min","D":"30 mL/min","E":"180 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration. Before the water load it is 120 times 1 divided by 1, and during diuresis it is 60 times 2 divided by 1, so both equal 120 mL/min. Urine flow doubles, but urine inulin concentration falls in proportion, so inulin clearance, which measures GFR, does not depend on urine flow rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diuresis changes urine flow and urine concentration in opposite directions, leaving clearance of a freely filtered marker unchanged. COMLEX: GFR is a property of the glomeruli, not of urine flow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — how changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect clearance of glomerular markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man collapses at the end of a marathon after losing a large volume of sweat. He is hypotensive and tachycardic, his plasma protein concentration and osmolarity are elevated, and his hematocrit is the same as before the race. Which mechanism best explains the unchanged hematocrit?","choices":{"A":"Isosmotic loss of ECF spares red cell concentration and volume","B":"Fluid loss concentrates red cells while high ECF osmolarity shrinks them","C":"Water entry into red cells from low ECF osmolarity offsets fluid loss","D":"Sweat is hyposmotic, so the resulting drop in ECF osmolarity dilutes red cells","E":"Red cell mass falls through hemolysis in proportion to fluid loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sweat is hyposmotic, so its loss lowers ECF volume and raises ECF osmolarity, which draws water out of cells. Loss of ECF fluid alone would raise the hematocrit, but the osmotic shift shrinks the red cells, and the two opposite effects offset each other so the hematocrit is unchanged while plasma protein concentration rises.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic volume contraction has a lower ECF volume, a lower ICF volume, a higher osmolarity, and an unchanged hematocrit. COMLEX: contrast this with diarrhea, in which the hematocrit rises.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — how isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic contraction alter ECF and ICF volume, plasma protein, and hematocrit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with psychogenic polydipsia drinks about 6 liters of water over a few hours and develops headache and confusion. Her plasma sodium concentration is 122 mEq/L. Which type of volume disturbance does she have?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion","B":"Isosmotic volume expansion","C":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","D":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction","E":"Hyposmotic volume expansion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure water added to the ECF lowers ECF osmolarity, so water shifts down its gradient into cells. Both ECF and ICF volumes increase, in proportion to their original sizes, and osmolarity falls in both compartments, which is hyposmotic volume expansion, as in SIADH. Plasma protein concentration falls by dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: water excess gives hyposmotic volume expansion with a higher ECF volume, a higher ICF volume, and a lower osmolarity. COMLEX: the same pattern occurs with SIADH and with excess D5W infusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states and their effects on ECF and ICF volume and osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A man has an ICF volume of 28 L, an ECF volume of 14 L, and a plasma osmolarity of 290 mOsm/L. He eats dry salt containing 200 mmol of NaCl, all of which enters the ECF. Assume the osmotic coefficient of NaCl is 2 and that he takes in no water and makes no urine during equilibration. What is his ECF volume in the new steady state?","choices":{"A":"14.0 L","B":"13.1 L","C":"15.4 L","D":"14.9 L","E":"16.4 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The added solute is 200 mmol times 2, or 400 mOsm. With no water taken in or excreted, total body water stays at 42 L, so new osmolarity is (290 times 42 plus 400) divided by 42, about 299.5 mOsm/L. New ECF volume is (290 times 14 plus 400) divided by 299.5, about 14.9 L, so roughly 0.9 L of water moved from the ICF to the ECF. This is hyperosmotic volume expansion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high NaCl intake raises ECF osmolarity, pulls water out of cells, and expands the ECF. COMLEX: solve these by conserving osmoles, using the new common osmolarity to find the new ECF volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — hyperosmotic volume expansion from high sodium intake and the osmole-based calculation of water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with type 1 diabetes is lethargic with shallow breathing. Glucose is 610 mg/dL and urine ketones are positive. Arterial pH is 7.12, PCO2 is 38 mm Hg, and serum HCO3 is 12 mEq/L. Using Winter's formula, which best describes his acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory acidosis","B":"Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","C":"Metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory alkalosis","D":"Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","E":"Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The low pH and low bicarbonate indicate metabolic acidosis from ketoacidosis. Winter's formula predicts PCO2 as 1.5 times 12 plus 8, which is 26 with a range of 24 to 28 mm Hg. His measured PCO2 of 38 is above the predicted range, so the lungs are not compensating adequately and a concomitant respiratory acidosis is present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: measured PCO2 above the Winter's prediction means a concomitant respiratory acidosis. COMLEX: inadequate ventilation in a severe metabolic acidosis is a warning sign of impending respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — applying Winter's formula to judge whether respiratory compensation is appropriate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has 3 days of vomiting and polyuria. Glucose is 550 mg/dL and ketones are positive. Serum Na is 142, Cl is 92, and HCO3 is 18 mEq/L, and her PCO2 matches the value predicted by Winter's formula. Using delta ratio = (anion gap minus 12) divided by (24 minus HCO3), which best describes her metabolic status?","choices":{"A":"Isolated high anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","B":"High anion gap acidosis with a concurrent normal anion gap acidosis","C":"High anion gap acidosis with a concurrent metabolic alkalosis","D":"Isolated normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","E":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her anion gap is 142 minus (92 plus 18), or 32 mEq/L, so she has a high anion gap acidosis. The delta ratio is (32 minus 12) divided by (24 minus 18), or 20 divided by 6, about 3.3. A ratio above 2 means the bicarbonate fell far less than the gap rose, indicating a coexisting metabolic alkalosis, here from vomiting; in Costanzo's terms the fall in HCO3 is less than the rise in the anion gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: with the delta ratio defined as in the stem, the review guide lists 1.0 to 2.0 as a pure high anion gap acidosis, above 2.0 as a coexisting metabolic alkalosis or a preexisting chronic respiratory acidosis, and a low ratio, given as about 0.4 to 0.8, as a combined high and normal gap acidosis. COMLEX: DKA with vomiting is the classic example of the high ratio.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — calculating expected compensation and the delta ratio to find a hidden second metabolic disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman presents with tinnitus, rapid breathing, and vomiting after ingesting a large quantity of aspirin. Arterial pH is 7.45, PCO2 is 24 mm Hg, and HCO3 is 16 mEq/L. Serum Na is 140 and Cl is 98 mEq/L. Which best describes her acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","B":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory acidosis","C":"Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory alkalosis","D":"Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation","E":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Her anion gap is 140 minus (98 plus 16), or 26 mEq/L, so this is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, consistent with salicylate. Winter's formula predicts PCO2 as 1.5 times 16 plus 8, or 32 with a range of 30 to 34 mm Hg. The measured PCO2 of 24 is below that range, so a concomitant respiratory alkalosis is present, which salicylate causes by stimulating respiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate poisoning classically produces a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: measured PCO2 below the Winter's prediction means a concomitant respiratory alkalosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — mixed acid-base disturbances such as salicylate overdose interpreted from arterial blood gases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has right flank discomfort. On osteopathic examination there is somatic dysfunction at T10 to T11, tenderness about 1 inch superior and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus, and a posterior tender point between the T12 and L1 spinous and transverse processes. Which organ is most likely the source of the viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Kidney","B":"Stomach","C":"Bladder","D":"Prostate","E":"Heart"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The kidney and upper ureter have sympathetic input at T10 to T11, an anterior Chapman point 1 inch superior and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus, and a posterior Chapman point between the T12 and L1 spinous and transverse processes. The stomach maps to T5 to T9 and the heart to T1 to T4, while the bladder and prostate have lower sympathetic levels and different Chapman points.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic tone alters vascular resistance and GFR. COMLEX: T10 to T11 dysfunction plus the kidney Chapman points supports a renal parenchymal source of the reflex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — mapping sympathetic levels and Chapman reflex points to the kidney, ureter, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with urinary hesitancy has somatic dysfunction at T12 to L2. Tenderness is found at the lateral margin of the pubic symphysis anteriorly and between the PSIS and the L5 transverse process posteriorly. Which organ is the most likely source of the viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Testis","B":"Prostate","C":"Stomach","D":"Urinary bladder","E":"Upper ureter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The prostate has sympathetic input from T12 to L2, parasympathetic input from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4, an anterior Chapman point at the lateral margin of the pubic symphysis, and a posterior point between the PSIS and the L5 transverse process. The kidney and testis are T10 to T11 organs, and the stomach maps to T5 to T9. The urinary bladder also has sympathetic input in the T11 to L2 range, but its Chapman points are periumbilical and at the superior border of the pubic symphysis anteriorly and at the upper edge of the L2 transverse process posteriorly, which do not match the findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvic organs use S2 to S4 parasympathetic outflow. COMLEX: matching the somatic level and the Chapman points to the organ is how these viscerosomatic questions are solved.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — matching somatic dysfunction levels and Chapman reflex points to the organ of origin, from kidney to prostate and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has flank discomfort. Palpation shows somatic dysfunction at T5 to T9 with no findings at T10 to T11. Which origin of his discomfort is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Renal parenchymal disease","B":"Upper ureteral obstruction","C":"Stomach or liver disease","D":"Testicular vascular disease","E":"Coronary artery disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"T5 to T9 represents the upper gastrointestinal organs, including the stomach, liver, and gallbladder. Findings at T10 to T11 must be present to confirm a renal parenchymal origin, and the upper ureter and testis share that level, whereas cardiac disease maps to T1 to T4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerosomatic levels distinguish organ systems, with T1 to T4 cardiac, T5 to T9 upper gastrointestinal, and T10 to T11 renal. COMLEX: absence of T10 to T11 findings argues against renal parenchymal origin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — using the level of somatic dysfunction to separate cardiac, upper gastrointestinal, renal, and pelvic sources","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":48,"title":"SDL 48 — Pre-Clinical Comprehensive Revision & Board Review (Part 2: Clinical Pathology, Pathophysiology & Therapeutics)","questions":[{"id":"sdl48_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature is more characteristic of primary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis than of minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Highly selective loss of albumin in the urine","B":"Poor response to corticosteroid therapy","C":"Normal glomeruli on light microscopy","D":"Remission in over 90 percent of treated children","E":"Absence of hypertension and hematuria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary FSGS is a diffuse podocytopathy in the same spectrum as minimal change disease, but sclerosis involves part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli. Compared with minimal change disease it has a higher incidence of hematuria, reduced GFR, and hypertension, more nonselective proteinuria, and a poor response to corticosteroids, and at least 50 percent of patients reach ESKD within 10 years. Minimal change disease shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, highly selective albuminuria, and a response to steroids in more than 90 percent of children.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly in patients of Hispanic and African descent, and high-risk APOL1 alleles are a recognized cause. COMLEX: NPHS2 podocin mutations are one cause of steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome of childhood.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 and SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and ultrastructure separate the primary nephrotic podocytopathies and the crescentic glomerulonephritides","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding characterizes membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes","B":"Discrete subepithelial humps with luminal neutrophils","C":"Basket-weave lamination of the lamina densa","D":"Electron-dense deposits confined to the mesangium","E":"Diffuse foot process effacement with no deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In membranous nephropathy electron microscopy shows irregular electron-dense immune complex deposits between the basement membrane and the overlying epithelial cells, and podocytes lay down basement membrane material between the deposits, which appears as spikes on silver stain. Discrete humps with neutrophils describe poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, basket-weave lamination describes Alport syndrome, mesangial deposits describe IgA nephropathy, and effacement without deposits describes minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PLA2R is the target antigen in about 60 percent of membranous nephropathy, and IgG4 is the principal immunoglobulin deposited. COMLEX: rule out secondary causes such as malignancy, infection, and SLE, and expect nonselective proteinuria that responds poorly to corticosteroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 and SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and ultrastructure separate the primary nephrotic podocytopathies and the crescentic glomerulonephritides","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunofluorescence pattern is characteristic of the crescentic glomerulonephritis seen in Goodpasture syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Coarsely granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls","B":"Dominant mesangial IgA with C3","C":"Continuous linear IgG along capillary walls","D":"Little or no immune deposition on staining","E":"Fine granular IgG over podocytes without complement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Goodpasture syndrome is anti-GBM antibody disease, in which antibodies bind the noncollagenous portion of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen shared by the GBM and alveolar basement membranes. Immunofluorescence shows linear deposits of IgG and often C3 along the GBM. Coarse granular deposits indicate immune complex disease, mesangial IgA indicates IgA nephropathy, little or no deposition indicates pauci-immune ANCA-associated disease, and subtle fine granular IgG over podocytes is seen in minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one-fifth of crescentic glomerulonephritis is anti-GBM disease, and the crescents are proliferating epithelial cells and macrophages in Bowman space. COMLEX: plasmapheresis to remove circulating antibodies is the mainstay of treatment, together with immunosuppression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 and SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and ultrastructure separate the primary nephrotic podocytopathies and the crescentic glomerulonephritides","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which nephron segments are most vulnerable to ischemic acute tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Proximal straight tubule and thick ascending limb","B":"Distal convoluted tubule and cortical collecting duct","C":"Thin descending limb and papillary collecting duct","D":"Glomerular capillary tuft and afferent arteriole","E":"Macula densa and juxtaglomerular granular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tubular epithelial cells are sensitive to ischemia because of their high metabolic rate and oxygen consumption. In ischemic injury the straight portion of the proximal tubule and the thick ascending limb in the outer medulla are most vulnerable, and reduced oxygen delivery to these segments is caused by intrarenal vasoconstriction. The lumens of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct contain casts rather than the main injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute tubular injury is the most common cause of acute kidney injury and produces granular, often muddy brown, casts. COMLEX: toxic injury is most obvious in the proximal convoluted tubule, so the pattern depends on the insult.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1 and 2 and SDL 34 -> Obj 3 — hypersensitivity interstitial disease, ischemic and toxic tubular injury, and the ischemic pathogenesis of papillary necrosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, when does drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis typically begin relative to drug exposure?","choices":{"A":"1 to 6 hours after exposure","B":"2 to 40 days after exposure","C":"3 to 6 months after exposure","D":"1 to 2 years after exposure","E":"10 to 12 weeks after exposure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins states that drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis begins 2 to 40 days after drug exposure. It is a hypersensitivity reaction, so onset is not dose related, and fever, eosinophilia, rash in about 25 percent of patients, and leukocyturia including eosinophils are typical. Recurrence follows re-exposure to the same or a chemically related drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the First Aid DRAINS mnemonic lists Diuretics, Rifampin, Antibiotics, Proton pump inhibitors, NSAIDs, and Sulfa drugs as causes. COMLEX: withdrawal of the offending drug is followed by recovery, although it may take several months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1 and 2 and SDL 34 -> Obj 3 — hypersensitivity interstitial disease, ischemic and toxic tubular injury, and the ischemic pathogenesis of papillary necrosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which systemic condition is associated with renal papillary necrosis?","choices":{"A":"Polycystic kidney disease","B":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","C":"Minimal change disease","D":"Sickle cell disease or trait","E":"Membranous nephropathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait, and urinary tract obstruction as the main associations, along with analgesic nephropathy and focal involvement in renal tuberculosis. In all cases papillary necrosis results from ischemia caused by compression or obstruction of small vessels in the medulla. The remaining conditions are not recognized causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid groups the associations as SAAD: Sickle cell, Acute pyelonephritis, Analgesics, and Diabetes. COMLEX: sloughed papillae can cause gross hematuria or renal colic from ureteric obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 -> Obj 1 and 2 and SDL 34 -> Obj 3 — hypersensitivity interstitial disease, ischemic and toxic tubular injury, and the ischemic pathogenesis of papillary necrosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination characterizes type 4 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Hypokalemia with a normal anion gap and urine pH above 5.5","B":"Hypokalemia with a normal anion gap and glucosuria","C":"Hyperkalemia with an elevated anion gap and urine pH above 5.5","D":"Hypokalemia with an elevated anion gap and urine pH below 5.5","E":"Hyperkalemia with a normal anion gap and urine pH below 5.5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Type 4 RTA results from hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance. Hyperkalemia suppresses renal ammonium production, giving a hyperchloremic acidosis with a normal anion gap, while the urine can still be acidified below 5.5. Hypokalemia with urine above 5.5 is type 1, hypokalemia with glucosuria is the proximal wasting of type 2, and RTAs classically have a normal anion gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 is the RTA classically associated with hyperkalemia, and diabetic nephropathy, ACE inhibitors, and potassium-sparing diuretics are classic settings. COMLEX: the urinary anion gap is positive because NH4+ excretion is low.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 to 4 — the defect mechanism, urine pH, and serum potassium separate the renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"Which urine pH finding is characteristic of untreated type 1 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Variable, depending on the plasma bicarbonate level","B":"Below 4.4 whenever plasma bicarbonate is low","C":"Between 4.4 and 5.0 with a positive urinary anion gap","D":"Above 5.5 even when plasma bicarbonate is low","E":"Near 7.4 with a negative urinary anion gap"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+ adequately, so the urine cannot be acidified below about 5.5 even during systemic acidemia. A variable pH that depends on plasma bicarbonate is the type 2 pattern, in which urine acidifies once plasma bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold. A pH near 4.4 is the physiologic minimum, and an acidic urine with a positive urinary anion gap fits type 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA is associated with amphotericin B, autoimmune disease such as SLE, and obstruction, and with calcium phosphate stones. COMLEX: hypokalemia and a normal anion gap accompany the persistently alkaline urine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 to 4 — the defect mechanism, urine pH, and serum potassium separate the renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis is a classic feature of which generalized proximal tubule disorder?","choices":{"A":"Fanconi syndrome","B":"Bartter syndrome","C":"Gitelman syndrome","D":"Liddle syndrome","E":"Apparent mineralocorticoid excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fanconi syndrome is a generalized reabsorptive defect of the proximal tubule that wastes HCO3-, phosphate, glucose, and amino acids together, so proximal type 2 RTA is a defining feature. Bartter, Gitelman, and Liddle syndromes and apparent mineralocorticoid excess involve loop, distal, or collecting duct transport and present with hypokalemic alkalosis rather than proximal RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists Fanconi syndrome, multiple myeloma, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as causes of type 2 RTA, with hypophosphatemic rickets as an association in Fanconi syndrome. COMLEX: the acidosis is self-limited because filtered bicarbonate is fully reabsorbed once plasma bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 to 4 — the defect mechanism, urine pH, and serum potassium separate the renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is most often associated with calcium oxalate stones?","choices":{"A":"Hyperuricemia with persistently acidic urine","B":"Hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia","C":"Genetic loss of renal amino acid reabsorption","D":"Elevated urinary citrate with low urinary oxalate","E":"Persistently dilute urine with a high daily volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 55 percent of calcium oxalate stones are associated with hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia, from absorptive hypercalciuria, renal hypercalciuria, or idiopathic fasting hypercalciuria with normal parathyroid function. Hypercalcemia from hyperparathyroidism and similar states accounts for about 5 percent. Hyperuricemia with acidic urine favors uric acid stones and inherited amino acid loss produces cystine stones, while citrate and a high urine volume are protective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most important determinant of stone formation is supersaturation of urine with the stone constituents, and low urine volume promotes it. COMLEX: First Aid describes calcium oxalate crystals as envelope-shaped or dumbbell-shaped, and stones are radiopaque.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — crystal chemistry, urinary pH, and radiodensity define the major renal stone types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism leads to formation of struvite stones?","choices":{"A":"Insolubility of uric acid in persistently acidic urine","B":"Genetic loss of renal cystine reabsorption in the tubules","C":"Intestinal overabsorption of oxalate in enteric disease","D":"Hyperparathyroidism with hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria","E":"Bacteria that split urea to ammonia and alkalinize urine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate, or struvite, stones form after infection with urea-splitting bacteria such as Proteus and some staphylococci that convert urea to ammonia. The resulting alkaline urine precipitates magnesium ammonium phosphate salts, and these stones can be very large, forming staghorn calculi. The other mechanisms describe uric acid, cystine, and calcium oxalate stone formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: staghorn calculi in an alkaline urine after urinary infection suggest struvite. COMLEX: First Aid lists coffin lid crystals and treatment by eradicating the infection and removing the stone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — crystal chemistry, urinary pH, and radiodensity define the major renal stone types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"Which urine pH predisposes to uric acid stone formation?","choices":{"A":"Persistently above 7.5","B":"Persistently between 7.0 and 7.4","C":"Persistently below 5.5","D":"Persistently between 6.5 and 6.9","E":"Persistently above 8.0"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Uric acid is insoluble in acidic urine, and a tendency to excrete urine with a pH below 5.5 predisposes to uric acid stones. More than half of patients with uric acid calculi have neither hyperuricemia nor increased urinary uric acid excretion, although hyperuricemia in gout and rapid cell turnover in leukemia are also risk factors. Uric acid stones are radiolucent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uric acid stones are radiolucent, in contrast to radiopaque calcium stones, and are seen in gout and in leukemia with rapid cell turnover. COMLEX: First Aid lists rhomboid crystals and treatment with urinary alkalinization and allopurinol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — crystal chemistry, urinary pH, and radiodensity define the major renal stone types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"Which cytogenetic abnormality is typical of clear cell renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Deletion of chromosome 5q","B":"Trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17","C":"Isochromosome 12p","D":"Translocation involving Xp11.2","E":"Deletion of chromosome 3p"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In about 98 percent of clear cell carcinomas, whether sporadic, familial, or associated with VHL syndrome, one copy of chromosome 3 is deleted at band p25.3, the site of the VHL gene. Trisomies 7 and 17 characterize papillary carcinoma, isochromosome 12p is found in invasive testicular germ cell tumors, and Xp11.2 translocations define TFE3-rearranged renal cell carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that RCC has 3 letters, chromosome 3, and VHL also has 3 letters. COMLEX: clear cell carcinoma accounts for 70 to 80 percent of renal cell carcinomas and tends to invade the renal vein.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — molecular drivers distinguish renal cell carcinoma subtypes, and histologic patterns distinguish testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"What is the molecular consequence of VHL inactivation in clear cell renal cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Gain-of-function of the MET receptor for hepatocyte growth factor","B":"Persistent HIF-1 that drives VEGF and IGF-1 expression","C":"Loss of the Krebs cycle enzyme fumarate hydratase","D":"Overexpression of the TFE3 transcription factor","E":"Loss of the tumor suppressor folliculin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VHL encodes part of a ubiquitin ligase complex that targets hypoxia-inducible factor-1 for degradation in an oxygen-dependent manner. When VHL is inactive, HIF-1 levels stay high even under normoxic conditions, inducing genes that promote angiogenesis such as VEGF and growth-promoting genes such as IGF-1. MET activation causes papillary carcinoma, fumarate hydratase loss causes hereditary leiomyomatosis and RCC, TFE3 overexpression defines Xp11 translocation carcinoma, and folliculin loss causes Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudohypoxic HIF signaling explains the highly vascular tumor and its response to VEGF inhibitors. COMLEX: drugs that inhibit VEGF and tyrosine kinases, and immunotherapy, are used for metastatic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — molecular drivers distinguish renal cell carcinoma subtypes, and histologic patterns distinguish testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular germ cell neoplasm contains Schiller-Duval bodies?","choices":{"A":"Spermatocytic tumor","B":"Choriocarcinoma","C":"Yolk sac tumor","D":"Embryonal carcinoma","E":"Postpubertal teratoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yolk sac tumors are composed of a lace-like network of cells, and in about 50 percent of tumors structures resembling endodermal sinuses, called Schiller-Duval bodies, are seen, with a mesodermal core and central capillary. Their cells contain hyaline globules with alpha-fetoprotein and alpha-1 antitrypsin. Prepubertal yolk sac tumor is the most common testicular tumor in infants and children up to 3 years of age.","boardPrep":"Step 1: marked AFP elevation points to a yolk sac component, whereas marked hCG points to syncytiotrophoblastic elements as in choriocarcinoma. COMLEX: persistent elevation of hCG or AFP after orchiectomy indicates metastatic spread.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 -> Obj 1 and SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — molecular drivers distinguish renal cell carcinoma subtypes, and histologic patterns distinguish testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter is the site of action of loop diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Na+-Cl- symporter of the distal convoluted tubule","B":"Epithelial sodium channel of the collecting duct","C":"Carbonic anhydrase of the proximal tubule","D":"Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the ascending limb","E":"Mineralocorticoid receptor of the principal cell"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the luminal membrane of the thick ascending limb. Blocking it also reduces the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption, so both are lost in the urine. Thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter, amiloride and triamterene block the epithelial sodium channel, acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, and spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics are first-line for pulmonary edema and severe volume overload, and cause hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, ototoxicity, and hypocalcemia. COMLEX: NSAIDs blunt their effect by inhibiting the prostaglandins that mediate renal vasodilation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 1 and 3 and SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — the site and mechanism of action of each diuretic class and of the androgen pathway inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug inhibits both type 1 and type 2 isoforms of 5-alpha reductase?","choices":{"A":"Dutasteride","B":"Finasteride","C":"Tamsulosin","D":"Tadalafil","E":"Spironolactone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dutasteride inhibits both isoforms of 5-alpha reductase, whereas finasteride selectively inhibits the type 2 isoform. Finasteride reduces serum DHT by about 70 percent and dutasteride by more than 90 percent. Tamsulosin is an alpha-1 blocker, tadalafil is a PDE5 inhibitor, and spironolactone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist with antiandrogen effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate in BPH by lowering intraprostatic DHT and lower PSA by about 50 percent, which must be accounted for in prostate cancer screening. COMLEX: adverse effects include decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders, and both agents are teratogenic to male fetuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 1 and 3 and SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — the site and mechanism of action of each diuretic class and of the androgen pathway inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic directly blocks the epithelial sodium channel?","choices":{"A":"Spironolactone","B":"Eplerenone","C":"Amiloride","D":"Acetazolamide","E":"Furosemide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Potassium-sparing diuretics act on principal cells of the collecting duct and fall into two classes: aldosterone receptor antagonists, spironolactone and eplerenone, and epithelial sodium channel blockers, amiloride and triamterene. Blocking the channel reduces sodium reabsorption and makes the cell more electronegative, which inhibits K+ loss. Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase and furosemide blocks the loop cotransporter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: potassium-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalemia and type 4 RTA, and spironolactone has antiandrogen effects including gynecomastia. COMLEX: combining them with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or potassium supplements increases the risk of hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 1 and 3 and SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — the site and mechanism of action of each diuretic class and of the androgen pathway inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy has cola-colored urine, swelling around both eyes, and blood pressure of 140/90 mm Hg. He had a sore throat 2 weeks ago. Urinalysis shows red cell casts and 1+ protein, and serum C3 is low. Which electron microscopy finding is most likely on renal biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Basket-weave lamination of the lamina densa","B":"Discrete subepithelial electron-dense humps","C":"Electron-dense deposits confined to the mesangium","D":"Diffuse foot process effacement with no deposits","E":"Spikes projecting from a uniformly thickened basement membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cola-colored urine, periorbital edema, hypertension, red cell casts, and low C3 one to two weeks after pharyngitis indicate acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. Electron microscopy shows discrete amorphous electron-dense subepithelial humps, and immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3. Basket-weave lamination is Alport syndrome, mesangial deposits are IgA nephropathy, effacement without deposits is minimal change disease, and spikes with diffuse thickening are membranous nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: poststreptococcal disease follows nephritogenic group A streptococcal strains, and more than 90 percent of cases are traced to M types 1, 4, and 12. COMLEX: more than 95 percent of affected children recover with conservative therapy aimed at maintaining sodium and water balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — complement consumption and immune complex localization in acute nephritic syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has dark brown urine that began 2 days after the onset of a viral upper respiratory infection. He had similar episodes twice in the past year, each resolving within a few days. Blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg and creatinine is normal. He has no hearing loss and no family history of kidney disease. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic red cells and 1+ protein. Which immunofluorescence finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous linear IgG along capillary walls","B":"Coarsely granular C3 along capillary walls","C":"Predominantly mesangial IgA deposition","D":"Subtle fine granular IgG over podocytes","E":"Scant immune deposits with segmental necrosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recurrent gross hematuria that begins within days of a mucosal infection and resolves, in a young patient with mild proteinuria, is typical of IgA nephropathy, the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide. Immunofluorescence shows mesangial IgA, often with C3 and lesser IgG or IgM, and early complement components are usually absent. Linear IgG indicates anti-GBM disease, coarse granular C3 suggests poststreptococcal disease that follows infection by weeks, fine granular IgG over podocytes fits minimal change disease, and scant deposits fit pauci-immune disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aberrant O-glycosylation of the IgA1 hinge region and immune complexes in the mesangium underlie the disease, and it is associated with celiac disease and liver disease. COMLEX: progression to chronic renal failure occurs in 15 to 40 percent of cases over 20 years, and heavy proteinuria and hypertension mark higher risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 1 — multi-hit immunopathogenesis of episodic post-infectious hematuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has recurrent hematuria and sensorineural hearing loss. His maternal uncle developed kidney failure in his thirties, and slit-lamp examination shows lens dislocation. Which molecular abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","B":"Circulating autoantibodies against nephrin","C":"Defective galactosylation of IgA1 hinge glycans","D":"Mutation of the alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen","E":"Autoantibodies against the phospholipase A2 receptor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hematuria with sensorineural deafness, lens dislocation, and an affected maternal uncle indicates X-linked Alport syndrome, present in about 85 percent of cases. Mutations affect the collagen IV alpha chains, and the X-linked form involves COL4A5, which impairs assembly of the GBM, lens, and cochlear collagen network. Alpha-3 antibodies cause anti-GBM disease, anti-nephrin antibodies cause some minimal change disease, abnormal IgA1 glycosylation causes IgA nephropathy, and PLA2R antibodies cause membranous nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: electron microscopy shows GBM thickening with splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, a basket-weave appearance. COMLEX: about 90 percent of affected males reach ESRD by age 40, while female heterozygotes typically present with hematuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — type IV collagen defects and their renal, ocular, and cochlear phenotypes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man is on postoperative day 2 after emergency repair of a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm complicated by 40 minutes of intraoperative hypotension. Urine output has fallen to 15 mL/h, and creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 3.4 mg/dL with a BUN of 38 mg/dL. Urine sodium is 58 mEq/L, FENa is 3 percent, and urine osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg. Urinalysis shows muddy brown granular casts. Which mechanism most directly sustains his reduced GFR?","choices":{"A":"Sloughed tubular cells obstruct lumens while filtrate leaks into the interstitium","B":"Immune complexes in the mesangium that activate complement and recruit neutrophils","C":"Fibrin thrombi in glomerular capillaries from endothelial injury","D":"Ischemic necrosis of papillary tips from compression of medullary vessels","E":"Hapten-modified tubular antigens that provoke an eosinophil-rich infiltrate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postoperative hypotension followed by oliguria, muddy brown casts, high urine sodium, FENa above 2 percent, and urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg is ischemic acute tubular injury. Injured cells detach from the basement membranes and obstruct lumens, raising intratubular pressure, and glomerular filtrate leaks back into the interstitium, causing edema and further ischemia; increased distal sodium delivery also constricts the afferent arteriole through tubuloglomerular feedback. The other options describe glomerulonephritis, thrombotic microangiopathy, papillary necrosis, and interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ATN First Aid gives urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg, urine sodium above 40 mEq/L, and FENa above 2 percent, compared with prerenal azotemia. COMLEX: initiation lasts about 36 hours, maintenance urine output is 40 to 400 mL per day, and recovery can reach 3 L per day with electrolyte loss and susceptibility to infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — intrarenal loops that maintain a depressed filtration rate after tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman started ampicillin 12 days ago for a skin infection. She now has fever, a diffuse maculopapular rash, and a creatinine that has risen from 0.9 to 2.6 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows leukocytes with eosinophils, a few red cells, and mild proteinuria, without red cell casts. Which mechanism best explains her kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Direct dose-dependent cytotoxicity to proximal tubular epithelium","B":"Prolonged hypoperfusion that causes ischemic tubular necrosis","C":"Subepithelial immune complex deposition that activates complement","D":"Compression of medullary vessels that causes papillary infarction","E":"Drug-modified tubular antigens that provoke an immune response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, rash, eosinophiluria, and rising creatinine days to weeks after starting a synthetic penicillin indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. Drugs bind to tubular cell membrane or extracellular components to create modified self antigens that evoke an IgE-mediated or T cell-mediated response against tubular cells and their basement membranes. Onset is not dose related, unlike direct toxic injury, and glomeruli are normal, which excludes immune complex glomerulonephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: synthetic penicillins such as methicillin and ampicillin, rifampin, thiazides, NSAIDs, and allopurinol are recognized causes, and a rash occurs in about 25 percent of patients. COMLEX: rising creatinine or oliguria develops in about 50 percent, and stopping the drug is followed by recovery that may take months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — drug-associated interstitial inflammation and its clinical markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes has flank pain and gross hematuria. He passes a fragment of tan tissue in his urine, and pathology shows coagulative necrosis with preserved tubule outlines. Which mechanism best explains the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Direct dose-dependent toxicity to proximal convoluted tubular cells","B":"Ischemia from compression or obstruction of medullary blood vessels","C":"Immune complex deposition in the papillary interstitium with complement activation","D":"Hypersensitivity reaction with eosinophils in the medullary interstitium","E":"Supersaturation and crystal precipitation within the papillary collecting ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Passage of necrotic papillae with gross hematuria in a diabetic patient indicates renal papillary necrosis, which shows ischemic coagulative necrosis with preserved outlines of tubules. In all cases it is caused by ischemia from compression or obstruction of small vessels in the medulla, and in diabetes vascular compromise arises from microvascular disease. Direct toxicity, immune complexes, hypersensitivity infiltrates, and crystal precipitation do not produce this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary necrosis is associated with diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait, urinary tract obstruction, analgesic nephropathy, and pyelonephritis. COMLEX: necrotic papillae can obstruct the ureter and cause renal colic, and interstitial edema in obstruction or inflammation compresses medullary vessels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 3 — medullary pyramid susceptibility in metabolic, hematologic, and obstructive disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with multiple myeloma has fatigue. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 3.0 mEq/L, Cl- 113 mEq/L, HCO3- 15 mEq/L, and serum glucose 90 mg/dL. Urine pH is 5.0, and urinalysis shows glucosuria and phosphaturia. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 renal tubular acidosis","B":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","C":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","D":"Diarrhea-related bicarbonate loss","E":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Her anion gap is 138 - 128 = 10 mEq/L, which is normal, so this is a hyperchloremic acidosis. Glucosuria with a normal serum glucose and phosphaturia show generalized proximal tubular dysfunction, Fanconi syndrome, which wastes HCO3- and causes type 2 RTA, and multiple myeloma is a recognized cause. An acidic urine pH argues against type 1, hypokalemia argues against type 4, the normal gap excludes ketoacidosis, and diarrhea would not cause glucosuria or phosphaturia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acidification of the urine below 5.5 once plasma bicarbonate has fallen distinguishes proximal from distal RTA. COMLEX: First Aid lists hypophosphatemic rickets as an association of the proximal form in Fanconi syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — using urine pH, serum potassium, and the urinary anion gap to separate the renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy takes lisinopril and spironolactone. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 5.9 mEq/L, Cl- 110 mEq/L, and HCO3- 17 mEq/L. Urine pH is 5.1. Which mechanism best explains his impaired renal acid excretion despite this urine pH?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemia that suppresses proximal ammonium production","B":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete protons into the lumen","C":"A lowered proximal threshold that wastes filtered bicarbonate","D":"Overproduction of organic acids that raises the anion gap","E":"Excess mineralocorticoid effect that drives urinary potassium loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 138 - 127 = 11 mEq/L, which is normal, and hyperkalemia with an acidic urine in a patient with diabetic nephropathy taking lisinopril and spironolactone identifies type 4 RTA. Hyperkalemia moves K+ into proximal cells and H+ out, raising intracellular pH and inhibiting glutaminase, so NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion fall. Urine pH of 5.1 shows the urine can still be acidified below 5.5, which argues against type 1, so the limit is the ammonium available to carry acid rather than proton secretion, and bicarbonate wasting describes type 2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetic nephropathy with hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism, ACE inhibitors, and potassium-sparing diuretics are common causes of type 4 RTA. COMLEX: correcting the hyperkalemia lifts the glutaminase inhibition and improves acid excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — how potassium balance and aldosterone status shape renal acid excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has muscle weakness. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Cl- 113 mEq/L, and HCO3- 13 mEq/L. Urine pH is 6.8 despite the acidemia. Which long-term complication is she at greatest risk for if the disorder is untreated?","choices":{"A":"Hypophosphatemic rickets","B":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest","C":"Medullary nephrocalcinosis","D":"Uric acid nephrolithiasis","E":"Cystine nephrolithiasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypokalemia, a normal anion gap of 138 - 126 = 12 mEq/L, and an alkaline urine despite acidemia identify type 1 RTA, and autoimmune disease such as SLE is a recognized cause. The persistently alkaline urine and acidosis-related loss of citrate favor calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis. Hypophosphatemic rickets is linked to Fanconi syndrome, hyperkalemia is not a feature, and uric acid and cystine stones form in acidic urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid associates type 1 RTA with calcium phosphate kidney stones, and amphotericin B, obstruction, and analgesic nephropathy are also causes. COMLEX: alkali therapy replaces the unexcreted acid load and reduces stone risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell function and its secondary complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has colicky right flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a 5 mm distal ureteral stone, and a plain abdominal radiograph shows a radiopaque density. Serum calcium and PTH are normal, urinalysis shows no bacteria, and 24-hour urinary calcium excretion is elevated. Which mechanism most likely explains his stone?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial urease activity that alkalinizes the urine","B":"Hypercalciuria that causes supersaturation of the urine","C":"Acidic urine that reduces uric acid solubility","D":"Inherited failure of tubular amino acid reabsorption","E":"Rapid nucleic acid turnover that raises uric acid production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A radiopaque stone with normal serum calcium and PTH but elevated urinary calcium reflects calcium oxalate stone disease from hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia, which accounts for about 55 percent of such stones. The key determinant of stone formation is supersaturation of the urine with stone constituents. Urease activity produces struvite stones, acidic urine and rapid cell turnover produce uric acid stones, and inherited amino acid loss produces cystine stones; uric acid stones are also radiolucent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium stones are radiopaque, and First Aid lists thiazides, citrate, and a low-sodium diet as prevention. COMLEX: causes of calcium oxalate stones include hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia, and hyperuricosuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — supersaturation, urinary calcium handling, and calcium stone chemistry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has had four episodes of kidney stones since age 8, and his brother has had similar episodes. Urine testing shows markedly increased cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine. Which defect is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"Excessive intestinal absorption of dietary calcium","B":"Overproduction of uric acid from rapid cell turnover","C":"Inherited loss of a proximal tubular transporter","D":"Chronic infection with urease-producing bacteria","E":"Deficient urinary citrate excretion from chronic diarrhea"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Childhood onset, a similarly affected sibling, and excess urinary cystine with the dibasic amino acids ornithine, lysine, and arginine indicate cystinuria, an inherited defect of the proximal tubular transporter that reabsorbs these amino acids. Cystine is poorly soluble in urine, so stones form, and they also form at low urinary pH. The other options describe the mechanisms of calcium, uric acid, struvite, and calcium stone formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria is autosomal recessive and the mnemonic is COLA for Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, and Arginine. COMLEX: First Aid describes hexagonal crystals and a positive sodium cyanide-nitroprusside test, with treatment by hydration, urinary alkalinization, and chelators if refractory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — inherited tubular transport defects as a cause of stone formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a long-term indwelling bladder catheter after a spinal cord injury has recurrent urinary infections, flank pain, and fever. Urine pH is 8.2. Imaging shows a large branching stone filling the renal pelvis and calyces. Which organism is most likely to be cultured?","choices":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Enterococcus faecalis","C":"Streptococcus agalactiae","D":"Proteus mirabilis","E":"Bacteroides fragilis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A staghorn stone in alkaline urine after recurrent infection is a magnesium ammonium phosphate, or struvite, stone. Robbins names Proteus and some staphylococci as the urea-splitting bacteria that convert urea to ammonia, alkalinize the urine, and precipitate magnesium ammonium phosphate salts, forming some of the largest stones. The other organisms are not the urea-splitting organisms named in the source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists urease-positive organisms such as Proteus mirabilis, and coffin lid crystals. COMLEX: treatment requires eradicating the infection and surgically removing the stone, since stones predispose to superimposed infection and obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — infectious triggers and urinary pH in stone formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man who smokes has 3 months of intermittent gross hematuria, left flank discomfort, and an 8 kg weight loss. Hemoglobin is 18.2 g/dL and serum calcium is 12.4 mg/dL. CT shows a 10 cm left renal mass with tumor extending into the renal vein and inferior vena cava. Which pair of tumor-derived products best explains his laboratory abnormalities?","choices":{"A":"EPO and PTHrP","B":"ACTH and renin","C":"hCG and calcitonin","D":"TSH and ADH","E":"GH and prolactin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A large renal mass with renal vein extension and inferior vena cava involvement is clear cell renal cell carcinoma, which Robbins calls one of the great mimics because of hormone-related syndromes including polycythemia and hypercalcemia. Erythropoietin explains the erythrocytosis and parathyroid hormone-related peptide explains the hypercalcemia. ACTH and renin can also be produced by renal cell carcinoma but would cause Cushing syndrome and hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the paraneoplastic mnemonic PEAR, for PTHrP, EPO, ACTH, and Renin. COMLEX: the most reliable clue is hematuria, and the tumor is often silent until large, with the lungs and bones the most common metastatic sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — clear cell RCC paraneoplastic syndromes and renal vein invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has a large, nontender left flank mass found by her mother during a bath, along with hypertension and microscopic hematuria. Examination shows absent irises bilaterally, global developmental delay, and a genitourinary malformation. Which gene is most likely deleted?","choices":{"A":"VHL","B":"MET","C":"WT1","D":"FH","E":"BHD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large unilateral flank mass with hematuria and hypertension in a 3-year-old is Wilms tumor, the most common renal malignancy of early childhood. Aniridia, genitourinary malformations, and developmental delay define the WAGR complex, which results from deletion of WT1. VHL, MET, FH, and BHD are the genes of familial renal cell carcinoma syndromes in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links WT1 mutations to Denys-Drash syndrome and WT2 to Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and describes embryonic glomerular structures in the tumor. COMLEX: Wilms tumor presents most often at ages 2 to 4 with a palpable flank mass.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 3 — germline genetic alterations and syndromes in pediatric nephroblastoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man has a painless, firm right testicular mass. Radical orchiectomy shows a homogeneous, gray-white, lobulated mass without hemorrhage or necrosis. Microscopy shows sheets of uniform round cells with clear cytoplasm, distinct borders, and prominent central nucleoli, divided by fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes. Serum AFP is normal and hCG is minimally elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Embryonal carcinoma","B":"Classic seminoma","C":"Yolk sac tumor","D":"Choriocarcinoma","E":"Postpubertal teratoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A homogeneous gray-white mass without hemorrhage or necrosis, with uniform cells, clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm, prominent nucleoli, and lymphocytic septa, is seminoma, the most common germ cell tumor. About 15 percent of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts and cause a minimal rise in hCG. Embryonal carcinoma is variegated and hemorrhagic, yolk sac tumor raises AFP, choriocarcinoma causes marked hCG elevation, and teratoma has mixed tissue elements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma cells are positive for KIT, OCT3/4, and podoplanin and negative for cytokeratin. COMLEX: seminoma is radiosensitive and chemosensitive with the best prognosis, and more than 95 percent of stage I and II patients are cured.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — histologic patterns and serum markers that separate germ cell tumor types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man who previously developed altitude sickness plans to climb to a high elevation and is prescribed acetazolamide. Which mechanism best explains its preventive effect?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic removal of cerebral water by a nonreabsorbable solute","B":"Blockade of NKCC2 with increased urinary free water loss","C":"Antagonism of aldosterone with retention of hydrogen ions","D":"Dilation of pulmonary arterioles that improves oxygen delivery","E":"Bicarbonaturia that drives compensatory hyperventilation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, blocking reabsorption of filtered bicarbonate, so bicarbonate and sodium are lost in the urine. The resulting metabolic acidosis induces compensatory hyperventilation, which increases oxygenation and prevents hypoxemia at altitude. Osmotic removal of water describes mannitol, NKCC2 blockade describes loop diuretics, and aldosterone antagonism describes spironolactone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause a proximal type 2 RTA with hypokalemia, alkaline urine, and calcium phosphate stones, along with paresthesias and tinnitus. COMLEX: they are also used for open-angle glaucoma and idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and are avoided in sulfa allergy and cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 4 — extrarenal indications of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and their physiologic rationale","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old postmenopausal woman has hypertension, osteopenia, and recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones with high 24-hour urinary calcium excretion. Which diuretic would best address both her blood pressure and stone recurrence?","choices":{"A":"Ethacrynic acid","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Spironolactone","D":"Chlorthalidone","E":"Bumetanide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides such as chlorthalidone block the Na+/Cl- symporter in the distal convoluted tubule, lower blood pressure, and increase renal calcium reabsorption, which lowers urinary calcium, reduces stone recurrence, and may help maintain bone density. Loop diuretics such as ethacrynic acid and bumetanide increase calcium and magnesium excretion, acetazolamide alkalinizes the urine and raises the risk of calcium phosphate stones, and spironolactone acts on the collecting duct and is not used to prevent calcium stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides cause the hyperGLUC profile, for hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia, and can cause hyponatremia and hypokalemic alkalosis. COMLEX: they are sulfonamides, should be avoided in gout, and are less potent than loop diuretics for volume overload.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — diuretic effects on urinary calcium and glomerular filtration thresholds","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man takes trazodone for depression and sildenafil for erectile dysfunction. He presents with a painful erection lasting 5 hours that is unrelated to sexual stimulation. Which mechanistic combination best explains the persistent erection?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-1 blockade plus prolonged cGMP-mediated relaxation","B":"Serotonin reuptake inhibition plus increased testosterone synthesis","C":"Dopamine receptor blockade plus reduced nitric oxide synthase activity","D":"Beta-2 stimulation plus inhibition of 5-alpha reductase","E":"Nitric oxide synthase inhibition plus increased venous outflow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Detumescence depends on sympathetic norepinephrine acting at alpha-1 receptors on cavernous smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction and venous outflow. Trazodone has potent alpha-1 blocking activity, and sildenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor, prolongs cGMP-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, so the combination overwhelms detumescence and produces ischemic priapism. The other pairings describe mechanisms that would reduce erectile function or do not act on penile smooth muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with nitrates because both raise cGMP and cause profound hypotension. COMLEX: ischemic priapism is a urologic emergency requiring treatment within 4 to 6 hours to prevent fibrosis, and alpha-1 blockers such as prazosin and tamsulosin are also implicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — how sympathetic tone and cGMP signaling regulate detumescence","isHighYield":true}]}]}]};